/* Gamedev Postmortem Atlas data: one entry per game, its decisions in the order they were made. */
window.PCA_DATA = {"games":[{"k":"13sentinelsaegisrim","n":"13 Sentinels: Aegis Rim","dv":"Vanillaware","y":"2019","sc":"AA","se":"Port 2022","lk":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/13-sentinels-aegis-rim-switch/","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"13sentinelsaegisrim.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Story & world","t":"Chose 1980s Japan as the setting and built the backgrounds in 3D","s":"Vanillaware could no longer reuse backgrounds the way it had in its fantasy games. Background art became extremely time-consuming, and scenes were written around a fixed set of locations.","w":"George Kamitani drew the first key visual as a New Year's card in 2013 and set the story in Japan in 1984 and 1985, the school years he remembered. It was Vanillaware's first game with 3D backgrounds for parallax, and that work was extremely time-consuming. A real street crossing could not be reused the way Muramasa's forests were, so scenes were written for fixed sets. A character first planned around a temple was changed because the temple art was too much work. After launch, the team described all of this to Famitsu in December 2019.","r":"Background art took far longer and scenes were tied to fixed sets.","re":"The team staged scenes like a play, with a limited set of movements.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you lock the setting, count how many locations can share background art, and budget the art for every one that cannot.","i":"Players accept a reused forest as a new place, but they spot a reused street crossing at once. A real setting gives each location its own art bill. The squeeze is worst for a small art team; generic rooms such as classrooms still reuse.","a":"How many of our locations can share background art?","q":"“Our resources are limited as our company doesn’t have a lot of staff; hence, up till now we’ve been making do by reusing backgrounds in different scenes wherever possible, and not being able to do so for this project was a struggle.”","qa":"Koichi Maenou, designer at Vanillaware","st":"13 Sentinels: Aegis Rim Double Helix – Developers’ Talk 1","su":"https://garmtranslations.wordpress.com/2021/08/11/13-sentinels-aegis-rim-double-helix-developers-talk-1/#:~:text=Our%20resources%20are%20limited%20as,this%20project%20was%20a%20struggle","sd":"Dec 2019, translated Aug 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"80days","n":"80 Days","dv":"inkle","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2015","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/381780/","ls":"Steam","cv":"80days.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned a round-the-world race game as a 3-month side project","s":"Making every neighboring city reachable multiplied the writing. The build took 9 months, and the writer's share grew from 20 or 30 cities to over 100.","w":"inkle started 80 Days as a 3-month side project next to its Sorcery! series, picturing a few routes around the world. The more the team played, the more it looked like a board game: from every city, players expected to reach every neighboring city. Each new link meant more journeys, and long journeys were split by more cities, until Europe became a maze. The build took 9 months, and the iOS version shipped on 31 July 2014. In October 2015 the directors said the Americas felt thin at launch because the team was burned out.","r":"The schedule tripled and the writing grew past 100 cities.","re":"inkle later filled the Americas with free updates, including 30 new cities.","co":"6 extra months; months of work without holiday.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock the schedule, count how many new journeys each added city creates, and price the writing per journey.","i":"When every node on a map should link to its neighbors, content grows with the links, not with the cities. The trap is specific to connected structures; a linear story grows a chapter at a time.","a":"How many new journeys does each extra city add to our map?","q":"“What was slated to be a quick three-month build became a nine-month slog, with our writer's contribution rising from \"20 or 30 cities worth of content\" to over a hundred.”","qa":"Jon Ingold and Joseph Humfrey, creative director and development director of inkle","st":"Postmortem: Inkle's 80 Days","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-inkle-s-i-80-days-i-#:~:text=What%20was%20slated%20to%20be,to%20over%20a%20hundred","sd":"Oct 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Left the conversation game undefined, with no mechanic or player benefit","s":"The first prototypes came late and none worked. A strategic mini-game was pulled less than 3 weeks before launch, and the shipped system often gives unhelpful lines.","w":"The original design said only that the player would talk to people to fill the time between stops. Nobody had decided what the player gained or how the mechanic worked. inkle first prototyped it about 75% of the way through development, and nothing worked. Simple passive exchanges felt pointless, and a strategic mini-game of collecting facts was removed less than 3 weeks before the July 2014 launch because no one enjoyed it. In October 2015 the directors wrote that the final quiz-like system often gives obtuse lines.","r":"A strategic mini-game was cut less than 3 weeks before launch.","re":"The team shipped a simpler, quiz-like conversation system.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Prototype every core interaction, and name what the player gains from it, before you write content for it.","i":"A mechanic with no stated player benefit cannot be judged on paper, so it survives until a prototype exposes it. Found late, there is no time left to iterate, and the fallback ships.","a":"Have we played a prototype of every core interaction yet?","q":"“And when we came to prototype it, three-quarters of the way through development, nothing we tried worked.”","qa":"Jon Ingold and Joseph Humfrey, creative director and development director of inkle","st":"Postmortem: Inkle's 80 Days","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-inkle-s-i-80-days-i-#:~:text=And%20when%20we%20came%20to%20prototype,nothing%20we%20tried%20worked","sd":"Oct 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the game on the inklewriter engine already scaled up for Sorcery! 2","s":"The team never had to worry about the core engine. The writer got a proven scripting language, compiler and text engine, and could just write.","w":"Just before 80 Days, inkle had finished Sorcery! 2, a 350,000-word interactive story set in a single city. Scaling inklewriter for it took real work, including streaming story content on demand instead of loading the whole script up front. By the time 80 Days started, the scripting language, compiler and text engine were stable. inkle handed them to lead writer Meg Jayanth and let her write. The iOS game shipped on 31 July 2014, and the October 2015 postmortem listed the engine under what went right.","r":"The team had no engine worries as the script grew.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you plan a big script, prove your authoring tools on a shipped project of similar size.","i":"Sorcery! 2 had already paid for the engine work, such as streaming content, so 80 Days started without it. The saving covers authoring only: a proven engine says nothing about platform reach, which later cost inkle separate ports.","a":"Have our writing tools ever handled a script this size?","q":"“We knew we were writing a big game, but we had no concerns about the core engine - we'd just finished Sorcery! 2, a 350k word sprawling interactive story set within a single city.”","qa":"Jon Ingold and Joseph Humfrey, creative director and development director of inkle","st":"Postmortem: Inkle's 80 Days","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-inkle-s-i-80-days-i-#:~:text=We%20knew%20we%20were%20writing%20a,set%20within%20a%20single%20city","sd":"Oct 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Story & world","t":"Agreed on a handful of stats as the only state shared between cities","s":"Each city and journey stood on its own. Writing could happen out of order and in parallel, and the map could grow in any direction.","w":"At the start of writing, inkle fixed what the game would track from city to city: money, health, time and 3 character traits. Nothing else carried over. The plan was for lead writer Meg Jayanth to finish a single journey around the world first, then branch out. That route took several months, and by then other routes already covered the globe. Because each scene stood alone, Jayanth wrote out of order while the 2 directors added content of their own. The iOS game shipped on 31 July 2014.","r":"The writer and both directors wrote in parallel without clashing.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before writers start, fix the short list of variables shared across all content, and keep everything else local to each scene.","i":"Shared state forces writers to coordinate, because every variable that crosses scenes is a dependency. With 6 tracked values, scenes could be written in any order. That kept writing parallel; it did not stop the journey count from growing.","a":"Which variables must every writer track across the whole game?","q":"“But the episodic nature of the design meant we could scale up in whichever direction we wanted, Meg could write scenes entirely out of order, and we were even able to jump in and provide additional content as well, all without stepping on each other's toes.”","qa":"Jon Ingold and Joseph Humfrey, creative director and development director of inkle","st":"Postmortem: Inkle's 80 Days","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-inkle-s-i-80-days-i-#:~:text=But%20the%20episodic%20nature%20of,stepping%20on%20each%20other%27s%20toes","sd":"Oct 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Wrote the iOS game in Objective-C on existing Apple-centric code","s":"A launch on every platform at once was impossible. Android and PC versions came out months apart, and each had to fight for visibility again.","w":"80 Days was written in Objective-C on top of existing, Apple-centric code. The iOS version shipped on 31 July 2014. Freelancer Iain Merrick ported it to Android from scratch in 3 months, using an emulation layer he had built for Sorcery!, and it came out on 16 December 2014. The PC and Mac version followed on 29 September 2015. In October 2015 the directors wrote that they had faced the visibility problem 3 times, and the Steam version still had no Metacritic page.","r":"iOS, Android and PC launched separately over 14 months.","re":"inkle planned to release its next games on all platforms at once.","co":"A 3-month Android port; 3 separate launches to win visibility.","nf":false,"l":"Before you build on code tied to a single platform, price the port to every platform in your plan.","i":"Code tied to a single platform turns every later platform into its own project and its own launch. For a small studio each launch must win visibility again, a cost the engineering estimate never shows.","a":"Which platforms can our current code base ship on at launch?","q":"“A simultaneous release across all platforms would have been much better for us, though there's no way we could have done, as the original game was built in Objective-C - and built on existing, Apple-centric code.”","qa":"Jon Ingold and Joseph Humfrey, creative director and development director of inkle","st":"Postmortem: Inkle's 80 Days","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-inkle-s-i-80-days-i-#:~:text=A%20simultaneous%20release%20across%20all%20platforms,existing%2C%20Apple%2Dcentric%20code","sd":"Oct 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"aboyandhisblob","n":"A Boy and His Blob","dv":"WayForward Technologies","y":"2009","sc":"AA","se":"Port 2016","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/281200/","ls":"Steam","cv":"aboyandhisblob.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built 80 levels on a new engine, collision system and art pipeline","s":"Later changes to gameplay and collision broke finished puzzles and forced rework. The team ran short of time to polish every level.","w":"WayForward pitched Blob to Majesco at E3 2008 as a small WiiWare title, then turned it into its first boxed Wii game. The 2D engine, collision system and art pipelines were all untested. The team built levels and Blob transformations on them anyway, against a schedule of best-guess dates. Collision was tweaked until late in production, and gameplay changes left elaborate puzzles in ruins. DS-era tools had to be rewritten. The game shipped on 13 October 2009 after 11 months with 35 developers. Polish time ran short, so the team focused on the first 10 and final 10 levels.","r":"Repeated level rework, and too little time left to polish every level.","re":"The team focused polish on the first and final 10 levels.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Lock collision, core mechanics and the art pipeline in one small test area before you build levels in bulk.","i":"Every level is built against the current physics and abilities, so each later change multiplies across all 80 levels. An 11-month schedule left no slack to redo them, so collision fixes became hacks that made the game rigid.","a":"Are collision and core mechanics locked before we build more levels?","q":"“Levels were being built around gameplay that was not finalized, and then the gameplay was tweaked further, leaving the elaborately-constructed puzzles in ruins.”","qa":"Sean Velasco, game designer and director at WayForward","st":"Postmortem: WayForward's A Boy and His Blob","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-wayforward-s-i-a-boy-and-his-blob-i-#:~:text=Levels%20were%20being%20built%20around,the%20elaborately%2Dconstructed%20puzzles%20in%20ruins","sd":"Feb 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"ahighlandsong","n":"A Highland Song","dv":"inkle","y":"2023","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1240060/","ls":"Steam","cv":"ahighlandsong.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Made the music sections full rhythm-action instead of background music","s":"Every jump had to land on the beat, so timing had to be exact in authoring, terrain fitting and frame rate. Humfrey said he could list maybe 20 things he wrote just to make the basics work.","w":"A Highland Song grew from Joseph Humfrey's side prototype and the folk band Talisk, who play at about 120 beats per minute. At one point the music was going to sit in the background. inkle chose full rhythm-action instead. The terrain is semi-procedural: the game reads the next bars of music and inserts terrain pieces so the jumps land on the beat. A few milliseconds off is audible, and a low frame rate breaks the timing. In May 2022 Humfrey said he could list maybe 20 things he wrote for the basics alone. The game shipped in December 2023.","r":"Exact timing became a requirement at every layer of the game.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Maybe 20 things to write just to get the basics working.","nf":false,"l":"Before you commit to a rhythm mechanic, prototype the music, terrain fitting and frame rate together, and measure timing error in milliseconds.","i":"The ear catches errors of a few milliseconds, finer than one frame at 30 fps. Authoring, terrain fitting and frame rate can each drift, and any one of them breaks the beat. Mechanics tied to music pay this cost; looser genres hide the same drift.","a":"Have we measured our timing error in milliseconds on target hardware?","q":"“I think we were inspired by some of this music, but it wasn't necessarily going to be rhythm-action, because rhythm-action is notoriously difficult, technically speaking--which, as we've discovered, it is!”","qa":"Joseph Humfrey, co-founder of inkle","st":"Developing to 120 beats-per-minute: A Highland Song Q&A","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/programming-to-120-beats-per-minute-a-highland-song-q-a#:~:text=I%20think%20we%20were%20inspired%20by,as%20we%27ve%20discovered%2C%20it%20is","sd":"May 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"ashorthike","n":"A Short Hike","dv":"Adam Robinson-Yu","y":"2019","sc":"Solo","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1055540/","ls":"Steam","cv":"ashorthike.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Committed a solo project to a 3-month deadline with a tight core and stretch goals","s":"The core shipped on time as a Humble Original in April 2019. Stretch goals that missed the first release went into the Steam and itch.io version in July 2019, which beat Robinson-Yu's expectations.","w":"In 2017 Adam Robinson-Yu quit his software job to make a big RPG. After a year he had no clear vision and the game felt years from done. In December 2018 he set it aside, built a tiny prototype, shared it on Twitter and got funding for a small project. He committed to release in 3 months, with milestones a month apart, a core scope he could finish, and the rest as stretch goals. He re-estimated tasks weekly and daily and reused tools and RPG assets. The game shipped in Humble Monthly in April 2019 and on Steam in July.","r":"First release met the 3-month deadline; some stretch goals waited for Steam.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"3 months to first release; a year-plus of RPG work set aside.","nf":false,"l":"Split the plan into a core you can finish early and stretch goals, then re-estimate weekly so scope tracks real velocity.","i":"A fixed external date forces finishing over perfecting, and a flexible scope lets the project shrink to the hours available instead of slipping. It worked because tools, assets and art style came from earlier projects; a first game on new tools has no such shortcuts.","a":"Which features are core, and which can miss the first release?","q":"“So most of the art direction work was done in December 2018 but after I got funding to make it into a tiny project I committed to releasing the game in just three months.”","qa":"Adam Robinson-Yu, independent developer of A Short Hike","st":"Crafting A Tiny Open World: A Short Hike Postmortem","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZW8gWgpptI8&t=544s","sd":"Mar 2020","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZW8gWgpptI8&t=541s"}]},{"k":"aaaaa","n":"Aaaaa! – A Reckless Disregard for Gravity","dv":"Dejobaan Games","y":"2009","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/15520/","ls":"Steam","cv":"aaaaa.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Wrote the design document once and set no review dates for it","s":"By mid-development the game no longer matched the plan, and the team worked day to day. Missing art pieces, a weak demo and repetitive levels surfaced late.","w":"Dejobaan built Aaaaa! in 9 months with 2 full-time developers, 1 part-timer and 2 interns. After the initial planning, nobody substantially updated the design document. There was no set interval to revisit it and no procedure to rewrite it. By mid-development the game looked very different from the plan, so the team threw the plans out and improvised. Nobody spotted the need for new architectural pieces, and level designers spent 20 hours reshuffling the same 3 pieces. The game shipped in September 2009. A year later, the postmortem said the planning nearly killed the project.","r":"Problems were found late, some only after the game shipped.","re":"On the next game, the team met weekly and reviewed the document regularly.","co":"Level designers spent 20 hours making the same 3 pieces play differently.","nf":false,"l":"Put design document reviews on the calendar from day one, with a set interval and a set way to rewrite it.","i":"Without a fixed review date, the document stops matching the game, and the team plans only for today. A 9-month game with a handful of people survived that; Dejobaan itself said a bigger project would not be so lucky.","a":"When is the next scheduled review of our design document?","q":"“Stop us if you've heard this one: after the initial planning stages, we never substantially updated our design document.”","qa":"Leo Jaitley, marketing and strategy at Dejobaan Games","st":"Postmortem: Dejobaan Games' Aaaaa! -- A Reckless Disregard for Gravity","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-dejobaan-games-i-aaaaa----a-reckless-disregard-for-gravity-i-#:~:text=Stop%20us%20if%20you%27ve%20heard,substantially%20updated%20our%20design%20document","sd":"Sep 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"acecombat7","n":"Ace Combat 7","dv":"Bandai Namco Studios","y":"2019","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/502500/","ls":"Steam","cv":"acecombat7.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Moved the series to Unreal Engine 4 and carried the old flight models across","s":"The aircraft handling code came over easily and gave the team a base with the series' feel. Engineers read the engine source and fixed bugs faster.","w":"Ace Combat 7 was the first game in the series on Unreal Engine 4 and the first mainline entry in 12 years. The team brought over the flight models from earlier games, the code that decides how each aircraft behaves, and they imported easily. Engineers read the UE4 source code directly and fixed bugs more efficiently. The new skies, very large maps and free flight among many NPCs still took a long time to tune. The game shipped in January 2019, and that August producer Kazutoki Kono called the switch the correct choice.","r":"Flight models came over easily and gave the team a working base.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you switch engines, port the code that defines your core feel first, and play it against the old game.","i":"The flight model is the series' core feel, and the team already had it as working code. Porting it gave UE4 work a proven base. The saving stops at what you carry over: the new skies and huge maps still took long tuning.","a":"Have we ported our core feel code to the new engine yet?","q":"“Flight models from previous ACE COMBAT games, which determine the behavior of aircrafts, were easily imported into the engine.”","qa":"Kazutoki Kono, producer of Ace Combat 7 at Bandai Namco","st":"ACE COMBAT 7 soars high with UE4 to become franchise's best installment","su":"https://www.unrealengine.com/developer-interviews/ace-combat-7-soars-high-with-ue4-to-become-franchise-s-best-installment#:~:text=Flight%20models%20from%20previous%20ACE,easily%20imported%20into%20the%20engine","sd":"Aug 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"againstthestorm","n":"Against the Storm","dv":"Eremite Games","y":"2023","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2022","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1336490/","ls":"Steam","cv":"againstthestorm.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Scoped the game to comparable city builders' budgets and demanded a 25% safety net","s":"The cushion also screened out publishers who would not accept it. The game sold 700,000 copies in its first year of Steam Early Access and passed 1 million by March 2024.","w":"The team worked on the game after hours for almost 2 years before founding Eremite Games in March 2021. Before pitching publishers, lead designer Michał Ogłoziński built a spreadsheet of other city builders' budgets and business models, benchmarked the plan against Kingdoms and Castles, and released a demo to check the core idea. Every publishing deal had to carry a 25% safety net for delays; many publishers were stunned. Hooded Horse signed in May 2022. Steam Early Access opened on 1 November 2022, 1.0 followed on 8 December 2023, and Steam sales passed 1 million copies in March 2024.","r":"Scope stayed in check; Steam Early Access to 1.0 took 13 months.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"A 25% cushion in the publishing budget; 10% lost on each US cash transfer.","nf":false,"l":"Before you pitch, benchmark your scope against shipped games in your genre and write a 25% delay cushion into the deal.","i":"A team that has not shipped at this scale cannot estimate the knock-on cost of early choices, so comparable games are the only hard data. The cushion covers what the estimate misses. It works when comps exist at your size; a genre first has none.","a":"Which shipped game is our budget and scope benchmarked against?","q":"“We can't deliver more than they did.”","qa":"Michał Ogłoziński, lead designer at Eremite Games","st":"How the small team behind Against the Storm kept scope in check","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/production/here-s-how-the-small-team-behind-against-the-storm-kept-project-scope-in-check#:~:text=We%20can%27t%20deliver%20more,than%20they%20did","sd":"Apr 2024","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"ageofempires","n":"Age of Empires","dv":"Ensemble","y":"1997","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2019","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1017900/","ls":"Steam","cv":"ageofempires.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Measured multiplayer bandwidth before the computer AI was finished, using human players' traffic","s":"The numbers missed that computer players send commands in bursts. The gap reached players and had to be fixed in the game's first patch.","w":"Ensemble started the multiplayer code in early 1996, with a synchronous model: every machine runs the whole game and only commands travel. To size bandwidth, the team metered human players, who sent a command every 1.5 to 2 seconds and 3 to 4 per second in heated battles. This happened before the computer AI was complete, and the metering came out too early to re-check against final code. Computer players sometimes sent large bursts of commands. The game shipped in 1997, and the first patch fixed the oversight. Graphics programmer Matt Pritchard listed it in the March 1998 postmortem.","r":"The bandwidth budget missed AI command bursts until the first patch.","re":"The team later advised keeping metering in until the final code.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Measure network traffic again once the AI and final content are in, not only on early human playtests.","i":"Human players issue commands at a human pace, while an AI can issue many at once, so traffic measured without it undercounts the peaks. A number taken before every command source exists is a floor, not a budget.","a":"Have we measured network traffic with the finished AI playing?","q":"“Load metering, the process of determining how much bandwidth the game updates required, was done before the computer AI was completed, and was based on the data flow model taken from human players.”","qa":"Matt Pritchard, graphics engine programmer at Ensemble Studios","st":"The Game Developer Archives: 'Postmortem: Ensemble's Age of Empires'","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/the-game-developer-archives-postmortem-ensemble-s-age-of-empires-#:~:text=Load%20metering%2C%20the%20process%20of,taken%20from%20human%20players","sd":"Mar 1998, reprinted Aug 2009","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"ageofempiresii","n":"Age of Empires II","dv":"Ensemble","y":"1999","sc":"AAA","se":"Remaster 2019","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/813780/","ls":"Steam","cv":"ageofempiresii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned a 1-year sequel on reused code while adding a giant feature wish list","s":"Within months the design had lost focus and a 1-year finish was out of reach. Ensemble took a second year and built the Rise of Rome expansion for Microsoft's Christmas 1998 slot.","w":"Ensemble had a sequel contract with Microsoft before Age of Empires shipped in 1997. Starting Age of Kings, the team assumed its existing code and tools would make the sequel easier, and planned 1 year. It also pulled together a giant wish list: off-map trade, renewable resources, combat facings, diplomacy and religion. Several months in, the design was pulling in contradictory directions, and a 1-year finish was impossible. Ensemble took another year and made the Rise of Rome expansion for Christmas 1998. Age of Kings shipped in October 1999 after 24 months, with a team of 40.","r":"The 1-year plan became 24 months under firm new deadlines.","re":"Ensemble reset units to an Age of Empires baseline and hired more staff.","co":"1 extra year; extra programmers, artists and designers; a stopgap expansion.","nf":false,"l":"Estimate new features separately from reused code, and cut the wish list before you give the publisher a sequel date.","i":"Reused code makes the old game cheap to rebuild, not the new features on top of it. The sequel's schedule is set by the wish list, and every added system also pulls the design away from its core.","a":"Have we estimated each new feature without counting on reused code?","q":"“Filled with these optimistic thoughts, we concluded that we could develop AoK in a single year.”","qa":"Matthew Pritchard, programmer at Ensemble Studios","st":"Postmortem: Ensemble Studio's Age of Empires II: Age of Kings","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-ensemble-studio-s-age-of-empires-ii-age-of-kings#:~:text=Filled%20with%20these%20optimistic%20thoughts,AoK%20in%20a%20single%20year","sd":"Mar 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"ageofmythology","n":"Age of Mythology","dv":"Ensemble","y":"2002","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/266840/","ls":"Steam","cv":"ageofmythology.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Spent early programmer time on data-driven tools so designers could add content","s":"Designers built new God Powers and other content through data without waiting on programmers. Near the end, they had the tools but not the time to enter every change.","w":"Ensemble started Age of Mythology on a new engine with no shared code from its earlier games. Early on, it put a lot of programmer time into data-driven tools while everyone wanted to start playing. That paid back when designers added content alone: once programmers built one God Power that switched units, designers could turn enemy soldiers into pigs through data. Some tools were never finished because people were pulled to other problems, so designers hacked content in place. Near the end, designers had the tools but no time to enter changes. The game shipped in October 2002.","r":"Designers authored content alone, and design time became the late bottleneck.","re":"The team planned to protect tool development time on future projects.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"For every content tool you plan, budget the designer hours to enter content with it, not only the programmer hours to build it.","i":"Tools move work from programmers to designers; they do not remove it. Without planned designer hours, the bottleneck moves to the end of the project. The trade works best for content built on shared mechanics, like God Powers that reuse one effect.","a":"Have we budgeted designer hours to enter content with our tools?","q":"“It took a lot of programmer time away at the start of the project, when we were all anxious to begin playing the game, but the resource hit paid for itself when designers could implement new content without having to wake up the programmers all the time.”","qa":"Greg Street and Ian Fischer, designer and lead designer at Ensemble Studios","st":"Classic Postmortem: Ensemble Studio's classic RTS Age of Mythology","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-ensemble-studio-s-classic-rts-i-age-of-mythology-i-#:~:text=It%20took%20a%20lot%20of%20programmer,the%20programmers%20all%20the%20time","sd":"Feb 2003, reprinted Oct 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"alanwake","n":"Alan Wake","dv":"Remedy Entertainment","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"Port 2012","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/108710/","ls":"Steam","cv":"alanwake.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Designed a story-driven thriller as an open-world sandbox on a huge map","s":"The sandbox prototype showed that free roaming broke the story's pacing, and Remedy switched to a linear structure. Häkkinen later named the sandbox as the biggest mistake behind the 5-year development.","w":"Remedy first built Alan Wake as an open world: a huge map with forests, a lake, power plants and a Twin Peaks-style town. A research team brought back 60,000 reference photos, and the studio built procedural world tools. While building the sandbox prototype, the team found that freedom to go anywhere robbed the story of its pacing. Remedy switched to a linear, episodic thriller and kept the large environments for landmarks and foreshadowing. The game shipped on Xbox 360 in May 2010 after 5 years of development. In 2020 Oskari Häkkinen called the sandbox design the biggest mistake.","r":"The open-world design was dropped after the prototype, adding to a 5-year schedule.","re":"Remedy kept the open-world tech and used its large spaces for landmarks.","co":"Part of a 5-year development, with about 55 staff at peak.","nf":false,"l":"Prototype the game's structure against the story's pacing before you build the world for it.","i":"A thriller needs build-ups the designer controls, and a sandbox hands that control to the player. The lesson fits story-led games; an open world built around systems and exploration does not need authored pacing.","a":"Does our structure let us control the pacing this story needs?","q":"“We just weren't getting this in a sandbox design because all the game-istic things you need were detracting from the story being the focal point.”","qa":"Oskari Häkkinen, head of franchise development at Remedy Entertainment","st":"Alan Wake turns 10: Remedy's game took five years to emerge from the darkness of development, the studio explains how it finally saw the light","su":"https://www.gamesradar.com/the-making-of-alan-wake/#:~:text=We%20just%20weren't%20getting%20this,the%20story%20being%20the%20focal%20point","sd":"May 2020","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"alanwake2","n":"Alan Wake 2","dv":"Remedy","y":"2023","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.epicgames.com/en-US/p/alan-wake-2","ls":"Epic","cv":"alanwake2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Pitched a multiplatform remaster together with the sequel once the rights came back","s":"Epic was excited by the package, and the sequel Remedy had tried to fund for years went ahead with Epic Games Publishing.","w":"Sam Lake had wanted an Alan Wake sequel for years, but the funding was not there. Before Control, he and Mikael Kasurinen wrote a less linear, action-heavy sequel concept; it did not feel like Alan Wake, and its design became Control. Remedy also got the publishing rights to the original game back. The original was only on Xbox 360 and PC, so a multiplatform remaster was the first idea. Remedy met publishers with a remaster plus a sequel, and Epic was excited. Alan Wake 2 shipped in October 2023 under Epic Games Publishing.","r":"The sequel found a publisher after years without funding.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When a sequel will not get funded alone, list the rights and existing games you can bundle into the pitch.","i":"A remaster on new platforms gives a publisher an earlier release and a bigger audience for the sequel. Lake names other factors too, a concept in hand and Control's crossover episode, so the bundle alone did not win the deal.","a":"What rights or older games could we bundle into this pitch?","q":"“That’s the package that we started meeting publishers with, and when we went to Epic with this idea to create a remaster for the original game and then a sequel they were excited!”","qa":"Sam Lake, creative director at Remedy Entertainment","st":"Alan Wake 2 interview with Sam Lake and Kyle Rowley","su":"https://stevivor.com/features/interviews/alan-wake-2-interview-with-sam-lake-and-kyle-rowley/#:~:text=That%E2%80%99s%20the%20package%20that%20we%20started,a%20sequel%20they%20were%20excited","sd":"Jun 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"alienhominid","n":"Alien Hominid","dv":"The Behemoth","y":"2004","sc":"Indie","se":"Web original 2002","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1285360/","ls":"Steam","cv":"alienhominid.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Funded development from a house mortgage sized to a rough 9-month estimate","s":"The game kept growing and took about twice as long. Key programmers worked far below market rate, Tom Fulp put in his own money, and the studio sold merchandise to stay afloat.","w":"Knowing a 2D console game would be hard to fund, the team paid for it with a mortgage on John Baez's house. The loan was meant to cover the whole game, roughly estimated at 9 months. Console work began on 1 April 2003, and as the game grew richer the founders let it keep growing, with several content lockdowns along the way. Programmers Josh Barth, Scott Fadick and Chip Burwell took wages far below market rate, and Fulp kept adding money. The game shipped on 17 November 2004.","r":"Development took about twice the 9-month estimate.","re":"Fulp added money and the team sold its own t-shirts and figurines.","co":"Roughly double the 9-month plan; programmers paid far below market rate.","nf":false,"l":"Size your funding to a schedule with slack, and agree in advance who pays if the game grows.","i":"Self-funding removes the outside deadline, so a team that loves adding features keeps adding them while the loan runs down. The founders chose the extra time on purpose; the price landed on wages and on the founders' own money.","a":"Who pays if this game takes twice as long?","q":"“We thought this initial loan would cover the complete development of the game, which we roughly estimated at nine months.”","qa":"John Baez and Tom Fulp, co-founders of The Behemoth","st":"Classic Postmortem: The Behemoth's Alien Hominid (2004)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/classic-postmortem-the-behemoth-s-i-alien-hominid-i-#:~:text=We%20thought%20this%20initial%20loan,roughly%20estimated%20at%20nine%20months","sd":"May 2005, reprinted Nov 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Staffing","t":"Kept an 8-person core team of former colleagues on off-the-shelf content tools","s":"With no new software to learn and nobody waiting on meetings, the team shipped a multi-console game of a kind that usually takes 25 or more people.","w":"When Gratuitous Games was closing, several of its developers decided to make a console Alien Hominid together. Console work began on 1 April 2003 with 8 full-time developers and a few part-time friends. All but Tom Fulp had worked in game production before, so there was little new software to learn. The engine was built around Flash and Photoshop, since the team knew it could not make its own content tools. The game shipped on PlayStation 2 and GameCube on 17 November 2004. In May 2005 the founders called their shared history the number one reason they succeeded.","r":"A core team of 8 shipped a multi-console title.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Each person did double or triple duty, with more responsibility than was healthy.","nf":true,"l":"Build the core team from people who have shipped together, and pick content tools they already know.","i":"People who know each other's habits and tools need fewer meetings and no ramp-up. The advantage belongs to this group, not to small teams in general: the same studio wasted time and money once it added remote contractors.","a":"Has our core team shipped together, on tools it already knows?","q":"“This, combined with the fact that we had all worked together at Gratuitous is probably the number one reason we succeeded.”","qa":"John Baez and Tom Fulp, co-founders of The Behemoth","st":"Classic Postmortem: The Behemoth's Alien Hominid (2004)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/classic-postmortem-the-behemoth-s-i-alien-hominid-i-#:~:text=This%2C%20combined%20with%20the%20fact,number%20one%20reason%20we%20succeeded","sd":"May 2005, reprinted Nov 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"alienisolation","n":"Alien: Isolation","dv":"Creative Assembly","y":"2014","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/214490/","ls":"Steam","cv":"alienisolation.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built a short playable mood-piece demo to pitch Alien: Isolation to Sega","s":"The demo spread inside Sega and won the greenlight for a studio known for Total War. It fixed the game's rules early: the Alien, no guns, nowhere to run. Over 1 million sold by March 2015.","w":"Sega acquired the Alien rights in 2006. At Creative Assembly, Alistair Hope and Jude Bond formed a small team to prototype a survival horror take on the series. In 4 weeks they had a technical demo: a corridor, a recreated medical lab from the first film, an area torn apart by the Alien, and a final confrontation with no guns. Hope says the demo went a bit viral within Sega. The pitch meeting ended in silence, then a thumbs up. Fox opened 3 terabytes of film archive to the team. The game passed 1 million sales by March 2015.","r":"The greenlight came with the game's core rules already fixed.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"4 weeks of a small team for the demo.","nf":false,"l":"Pitch with a short playable demo that already has your hardest design rules in it.","i":"A playable mood piece let Sega feel the no-guns, nowhere-to-run idea instead of reading about it, and it travelled on its own inside the publisher. It works when the feeling is the pitch; a systems-heavy game needs a different proof.","a":"Can we show our pitch's core feeling in a 4-week playable demo?","q":"“You had the sense there was nowhere to run, there's no guns, you've got to respect this thing.”","qa":"Alistair Hope, creative lead on Alien: Isolation, Creative Assembly","st":"Horror in the making: The Alien: Isolation gamble","su":"https://mcvuk.com/?p=70758#:~:text=You%20had%20the%20sense%20there,respect%20this%20thing.","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"alienscolonialmarines","n":"Aliens: Colonial Marines","dv":"Gearbox Software","y":"2013","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/49540/","ls":"Steam","cv":"alienscolonialmarines.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Announced the licensed shooter when pre-production had just started, then sidelined it for 4 years","s":"Kotaku reported that Gearbox did little work on it until 2010, handed it to TimeGate without a locked script, then rebuilt much of it in 9 months. It shipped in February 2013 and drew a class action over its demos.","w":"Sega announced its Aliens licence on 11 December 2006; the first game was not expected before 2009. Kotaku reported in February 2013 that pre-production had just started and that Gearbox did little work on it between 2007 and 2010, focusing on Borderlands and Duke Nukem Forever. Around November 2010 TimeGate took over development with no final script locked. In mid-2012 Gearbox took it back and changed everything; staff felt they could not ask Sega for another extension after 7 years. The game shipped in February 2013. In 2014 Gearbox said its own unpaid contributions had totalled millions.","r":"After 7 years, most of the shipped game was built in about 9 months.","re":"Gearbox took the project back from TimeGate in mid-2012 and reworked it.","co":"7 years elapsed; Gearbox said millions of its own money went in, unrepaid.","nf":false,"l":"Do not announce a licensed game at the start of pre-production unless the team that will build it is free to start now.","i":"An announcement starts the clock on a licence, but it does not start the work. Kotaku's sources say the team was busy elsewhere from 2007 to 2010, so the deadline arrived before the design did. With a free team, an early announcement costs little.","a":"If we announce now, who actually starts building it this month?","q":"“The game feels like it was made in nine months, and that's because it was.”","qa":"Unnamed source familiar with the project, quoted anonymously by Kotaku","st":"From Dream To Disaster: The Story Of Aliens: Colonial Marines","su":"https://kotaku.com/from-dream-to-disaster-the-story-of-aliens-colonial-m-5986694#:~:text=The%20game%20feels%20like%20it,because%20it%20was","sd":"Feb 2013","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Outsourced most of the game to TimeGate while Gearbox focused on Borderlands 2","s":"TimeGate threw out whole scenes and levels while the story kept changing. Gearbox later told a court it spent millions of its own money to finish the game.","w":"Sega announced the shooter with Gearbox in December 2006, when pre-production had just started. Three sources told Kotaku that Gearbox put little work into it from 2007 to 2010 while it made Duke Nukem Forever and Borderlands. After Borderlands took off in 2009, Gearbox outsourced the bulk of development to TimeGate, the main developer from the end of 2010. With no locked script, TimeGate discarded entire scenes and levels in 2011. In mid-2012 Gearbox took the project back and changed most of it, partly because it could not run on PS3. The game shipped in February 2013 and was panned.","r":"A source told Kotaku the shipped game was made in 9 months.","re":"Gearbox took the game back in mid-2012 and changed most of it.","co":"Over 6 years from announcement; Gearbox says it added millions of its own.","nf":false,"l":"Lock the script and the content plan before you hand a game to an outside studio.","i":"An outside team builds what it is told, so a moving story turns its levels into waste. With 3 companies approving work, feedback slowed further. A partner can execute a locked plan; it cannot settle the vision for you.","a":"Is our script locked before the partner studio starts building levels?","q":"“The game feels like it was made in nine months, and that’s because it was,”","qa":"Unnamed source connected to the game, quoted by Kotaku","st":"From Dream To Disaster: The Story Of Aliens: Colonial Marines","su":"https://kotaku.com/from-dream-to-disaster-the-story-of-aliens-colonial-m-5986694#:~:text=The%20game%20feels%20like%20it,because%20it%20was","sd":"Feb 2013","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Showed trade-show demos that ran on a high-end PC, over console budgets","s":"Buyers sued over the gap between the demos and the final game. Sega paid $1.25 million to settle the class action without admitting wrongdoing.","w":"From June 2011 until release, Gearbox and Sega promoted the game with trade-show demos, trailers and previews, the class-action complaint says. Kotaku's sources said TimeGate built the demo with animation help from Gearbox, and it ran in real time on a high-end PC far over the console performance budget. For the February 2013 release, lighting, texture and shader detail were cut. Buyers sued in April 2013, alleging a separate demo engine, which Gearbox denied in court. Sega settled for $1.25 million in 2014, and in May 2015 the court refused to certify the class against Gearbox.","r":"The final game shipped with lower-res textures and simpler shaders than the demo.","re":"Sega settled in 2014; Gearbox fought on, and the court refused class certification.","co":"$1.25 million settlement paid by Sega, plus 2 years of litigation.","nf":false,"l":"Build every public demo inside the target console's performance budget, on the build you will ship.","i":"A demo built over the performance budget promises detail the consoles cannot run, and every cut later shows. Work in progress watermarks on the trailers did not stop buyers from suing.","a":"Does our next public demo run within the console's performance budget?","q":"“[Gearbox made] big changes to lighting, texture and shader complexity,”","qa":"Unnamed source familiar with later builds, quoted by Kotaku","st":"From Dream To Disaster: The Story Of Aliens: Colonial Marines","su":"https://kotaku.com/from-dream-to-disaster-the-story-of-aliens-colonial-m-5986694#:~:text=big%20changes%20to%20lighting%2C%20texture,and%20shader%20complexity","sd":"Feb 2013","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"aloneinthedark","n":"Alone in the Dark","dv":"Infogrames","y":"1992","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/548090/","ls":"Steam","cv":"aloneinthedark.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Built a playable proof of concept before Infogrames approved Alone in the Dark","s":"Approval in September 1991 gave Raynal a team of 6. Three days of story meetings set every task, and the game shipped in 1992 with some mechanics left rough.","w":"Raynal built his own 3D editor and scripting language at Infogrames. By September 1991 he had a working proof of concept: one polygonal character under keyboard control and a couple of mansion rooms. Infogrames approved it and gave him a 3D artist, a 2D artist and a programmer. With writer Hubert Chardot the team of 6 planned the whole story in 3 days of meetings, so everyone knew their tasks. The game shipped in 1992. In 2012 Raynal confessed that by October 1992 he hated it: rushed pieces like the thrown-object arc were never fixed.","r":"A team of 6 shipped a genre-founding game about a year after approval.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Team of 6, about 1 year from approval to release.","nf":false,"l":"Before asking for a team, build one character in two rooms that plays, and show that instead of a document.","i":"The prototype settled the hard technical questions before the team grew, so the team could plan the whole game in 3 days. The speed had a price: mechanics built quickly for the prototype stayed rough in the shipped game.","a":"Can we show the publisher one playable room before we ask for headcount?","q":"“And actually Infogrames didn't really believe in it at this time.”","qa":"Frederick Raynal, creator of Alone in the Dark at Infogrames","st":"GDC 2012: Inside the making of Alone in the Dark","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/gdc-2012-inside-the-making-of-i-alone-in-the-dark-i-#:~:text=I%20have%20a%20confession,I%20hated%20this%20game","sd":"Mar 2012","sy":"Press report","w8":4,"qu":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c2lgEyNaop4&t=495s"}]},{"k":"altosadventure","n":"Alto's Adventure","dv":"Snowman","y":"2015","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/altos-adventure/id950812012?uo=4","ls":"App Store","cv":"altosadventure.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Let the hired artist try Unity after the first technical approach stalled","s":"Things that had been hard came together within days. The artist became the game's full-time programmer too, and the game was playable a third of the way into production.","w":"In early 2013, Snowman's founders asked illustrator Harry Nesbitt to do the art for a simple 2D snowboarding game. Working out how to build it was the hardest part, and the first technical approach proved difficult. Nesbitt spent a couple of days in Unity, his first time with it, and found things coming together. A few months later he was full-time programmer and artist. The game was playable about a third of the way into production, and the rest went into polish. It shipped on iOS in February 2015, after 2 years of work.","r":"One person owned art and code, with a playable build early.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When the build approach stalls, give a team member a few days to test another engine before you hire around it.","i":"Unity let an artist with no programming background build the game himself, so art and code stayed in one head. That fits a simple 2D game; a system-heavy game still needs dedicated programmers.","a":"Has anyone spent a few days testing another engine for this?","q":"“Fast forward a few months and I had -- I graduated to full-time programmer and artist.”","qa":"Harry Nesbitt, artist and developer on Team Alto","st":"Alto's Adventure","su":"https://forums.autodesk.com/t5/community-blog-m-e-english/alto-s-adventure/ba-p/13896268#:~:text=Fast%20forward%20a%20few%20months,programmer%20and%20artist","sd":"Sep 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"altosodyssey","n":"Alto's Odyssey","dv":"Team Alto","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/altos-odyssey/id1182456409","ls":"App Store","cv":"altosodyssey.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Built the desert world from procedural rules and a small asset set","s":"The small team shipped a game that Nesbitt says could stand next to ones with much larger budgets. Pacing the hand-made sequences inside random runs took time.","w":"Procedural generation was part of the series from Alto's Adventure, Harry Nesbitt's first game. The desert sequel was an idea before that game was finished. For Alto's Odyssey the team kept refining one core set of rules that builds a large world from a small number of assets, all in Unity. Hand-crafted sequences were mixed in for memorable moments, but kept rare so they would not grow tiresome. That rhythm took time to get right. The game was on the App Store by February 2018, and in January 2019 Nesbitt credited the procedural approach for letting the small team compete.","r":"A small asset set produced a world that felt large.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"On a small team, spend effort on rules that turn a small asset set into many runs, then tune how often set pieces appear.","i":"Rules scale where hand-made content cannot: each refined rule improves every run. That suits an endless, replayable structure like Alto's. A story game that needs authored levels gets less from it, and generated runs still need hand tuning for pacing.","a":"Which of our content could rules generate instead of artists?","q":"“The procedural aspect of the series is ultimately what allowed us to ship a game that could even hope to stand alongside others with much larger budgets or more experienced teams.”","qa":"Harry Nesbitt, lead artist and programmer at Team Alto","st":"Road to the IGF: Team Alto's Alto's Odyssey","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/road-to-the-igf-team-alto-s-i-alto-s-odyssey-i-#:~:text=The%20procedural%20aspect%20of%20the%20series,budgets%20or%20more%20experienced%20teams","sd":"Jan 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"amazongamestudiosportfolio","n":"Amazon Game Studios portfolio (Breakaway, Crucible, New World)","dv":"Amazon Game Studios","y":"","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Required internal game teams to build on Lumberyard while the engine was brand new","s":"Developers said building Breakaway felt like driving a train while the track was still being built. Breakaway was cancelled in 2018, and the mandate was later eased.","w":"Amazon launched Lumberyard, built from CryEngine, in February 2016, and its general manager Eric Schenk called the internal studios its first customers. In September 2016 Amazon Game Studios announced Breakaway, its first game on the engine. Breakaway was cancelled in March 2018. In June 2019 the Wall Street Journal reported that Lumberyard was not built for the multiplayer games Amazon wanted, and that the engine team struggled to adapt it. Bloomberg reported in January 2021 that Christoph Hartmann, who joined in 2018, had eased the mandate. New World shipped in September 2021.","r":"Breakaway, the first game on the engine, was cancelled in March 2018.","re":"Christoph Hartmann, who joined in 2018, eased the Lumberyard mandate.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you mandate an in-house engine, check that it already runs the core features of the game you plan.","i":"A team building a game on an engine that is still being built carries both schedules, and every engine gap lands on the game. The lesson covers unfinished engines pushed onto genres they were not built for, not in-house engines in general.","a":"Does our engine already run this game's core features today?","q":"“Yes, the Amazon Game Studios are using the technology we've constructed internally.”","qa":"Eric Schenk, general manager of Amazon Lumberyard","st":"Inside Amazon’s decision to make a video game engine","su":"https://gamesbeat.com/inside-amazons-decision-to-make-a-video-game-engine/#:~:text=Yes%2C%20the%20Amazon%20Game%20Studios,are%20using%20the%20technology","sd":"Feb 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"amnesiaamachineforpigs","n":"Amnesia: A Machine for Pigs","dv":"The Chinese Room","y":"2013","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/239200/","ls":"Steam","cv":"amnesiaamachineforpigs.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Built a near-complete Cellar level as the first milestone instead of grey-boxing the whole game","s":"The Cellar was later rebuilt as an almost entirely different level, many areas were reworked 2, 3 or more times, and a 12-month plan became 24 months.","w":"The Chinese Room pitched the game to Frictional Games as a 2-to-3 hour experience called We are the Pig, planned for 12 months. Frictional's first deliverable was a near-complete Cellar level, so the team never grey-boxed the full game. 6 to 7 months in, core mechanics such as the infection system were still not signed off. The Cellar became an almost entirely different level, the Tunnels shrank to about a quarter of their size, and the Boiler and Fattening levels were cut. The game shipped on 10 September 2013 after 24 months; Peter Howell's postmortem followed in May 2014.","r":"Development took 24 months instead of the planned 12.","re":"Levels were cut or shrunk, and further delays were refused.","co":"12 extra months of development; Boiler and Fattening levels cut.","nf":false,"l":"Make the first milestone a grey-box of the whole game, not a polished first level.","i":"Polishing one level before the whole game exists means later decisions are made without seeing the full sequence, and conflicting feedback on each deliverable turns into rework. This only works if the partner accepts a grey-box as a milestone.","a":"Is our first milestone a grey-box of the whole game?","q":"“Meeting this first milestone prevented TCR from grey-boxing the full game at this critical early stage.”","qa":"Peter Howell, co-designer and lead events scripter, The Chinese Room","st":"Postmortem: The Chinese Room's Amnesia: A Machine for Pigs","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-the-chinese-room-s-i-amnesia-a-machine-for-pigs-i-#:~:text=Meeting%20this%20first%20milestone%20prevented,this%20critical%20early%20stage","sd":"May 2014","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Started a first co-development with the franchise owner taking a hands-off role","s":"The Chinese Room got wide creative freedom, but notes on its deliverables often contradicted each other, and it sometimes gave in on its own design.","w":"Frictional Games, the Amnesia series creator, and The Chinese Room made the sequel together, and neither had collaborated with another company before. Frictional stayed hands-off in early development, and the team tried many plot, puzzle and enemy ideas. As deliverables came in, the feedback was often different and conflicting. Many areas were reworked two, three or more times. Some came out stronger; in others the team gave way when it should have defended its design. Planned at 12 months, development took 24, and the game shipped in September 2013. Co-designer Peter Howell wrote the postmortem in May 2014.","r":"Many areas were reworked two, three or more times.","re":"No fix reported; the postmortem says the team should have pushed back.","co":"Many areas reworked two, three or more times.","nf":false,"l":"Before a partner starts work, agree who signs off each deliverable and how conflicting notes get resolved.","i":"Freedom early meant the owner's view arrived only at deliverables, where notes from different angles could conflict. The author says the hands-off approach may have caused this, and also blames his own team for not defending its design.","a":"Who has the final say when partner notes conflict?","q":"“Many areas of the game were reworked two, three, or even more times based on different and often conflicting feedback on deliverables.”","qa":"Peter Howell, co-designer and lead events scripter at The Chinese Room","st":"Postmortem: The Chinese Room's Amnesia: A Machine for Pigs","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-the-chinese-room-s-i-amnesia-a-machine-for-pigs-i-#:~:text=Many%20areas%20of%20the%20game%20were%20reworked,often%20conflicting%20feedback%20on%20deliverables","sd":"May 2014","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"amnesiathedarkdescent","n":"Amnesia: The Dark Descent","dv":"Frictional Games","y":"2010","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/57300/","ls":"Steam","cv":"amnesiathedarkdescent.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Kept adding features to a prototype that did not feel engaging","s":"Weeks went into combat that was then scrapped, and the hub structure was later thrown out. Cutting the leftover features took longer than expected.","w":"In December 2008, Frictional Games finished the prototype it had pitched to the Nordic Game Program. The content pipeline and most basic gameplay worked, but the game did not feel engaging. The team convinced itself that more features would fix it. In spring 2009 it added weapons, spent a few weeks tuning combat, then scrapped it. In June 2009, after a Penumbra sale on Steam brought in money, the team dropped the Mario 64-like hub design for a linear game closer to Penumbra. Shedding the leftover features took longer than expected. Amnesia shipped on 8 September 2010.","r":"Combat and the hub design were built, then scrapped.","re":"The team refocused on a linear game closer to Penumbra.","co":"A few weeks on combat, plus the scrapped hub design.","nf":false,"l":"If a prototype does not feel engaging, fix the core loop before you add any new feature.","i":"Time already spent biases a team toward adding to what it has rather than questioning it. Money troubles and a lucky Steam sale also shaped the 2009 change of direction, so not all the rework traces back to this choice.","a":"Is our prototype engaging without the features we plan to add?","q":"“While this raised some worries, we convinced ourselves that adding more features would fix the problem.”","qa":"Thomas Grip, co-founder and creative director of Frictional Games","st":"Amnesia: Post-Mortem","su":"https://frictionalgames.com/2011-07-amnesia-post-mortem/#:~:text=While%20this%20raised%20some%20worries,more%20features%20would%20fix%20the%20problem","sd":"Jul 2011","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"amongus2","n":"Among Us","dv":"Innersloth","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/945360/","ls":"Steam","cv":"amongus2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Cancelled the announced sequel and reworked the old codebase instead","s":"All the content planned for Among Us 2 went into the original game. The new map arrived about 6 months later, accounts after 9 months and roles after 14.","w":"On 18 August 2020 Innersloth confirmed Among Us 2, with 70,000 to 110,000 players an hour on a game whose code was so fragile that each extra map had taken 6 months. Five weeks later, on 23 September 2020, the developers cancelled the sequel and chose to pour all planned content into Among Us 1, calling it the more difficult choice because it meant reworking the core code. The Airship map shipped on 31 March 2021, with a post explaining why things took longer than they wanted. Accounts followed in June 2021 and roles in November 2021.","r":"The team had to rework the game's core code while running the live game.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Core code rework; the first new map took about 6 months.","nf":false,"l":"Before starting a sequel to escape technical debt, cost the rework of the live game against building a second product.","i":"A sequel splits a tiny team and its audience, and the live game's players are the asset; reworking old code is slower per feature but keeps everyone on one product. True for a small team with a live hit; a larger team can do both.","a":"Is a sequel cheaper than fixing the code we already ship?","q":"“We have decided to cancel Among Us 2 and instead put all our focus into improving Among Us 1.”","qa":"PuffballsUnited, Innersloth developer","st":"The Future of Among Us","su":"https://www.innersloth.com/the-future-of-among-us/#:~:text=We%20have%20decided%20to%20cancel,improving%20Among%20Us%201.","sd":"Sep 2020","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"anachronox","n":"Anachronox","dv":"Ion Storm","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/242940/","ls":"Steam","cv":"anachronox.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned Anachronox around a story its writer later called too epic","s":"To get the game done, the team cut about 40% of the story at a natural break. The rest of the story is still unreleased.","w":"Tom Hall, a co-founder of Ion Storm, wrote the story for Anachronox. To finish the game, the team cut about 40% of that story at a natural place. Anachronox came out in 2001 with the rest of the story left out. Asked in a September 2025 interview how big the narrative was meant to be, Hall took the blame himself. He said he still plans to get the rest of the story out somehow, and that the battle system could have been better.","r":"About 40% of the planned story was cut to ship.","re":"Hall says he will still get the rest of the story out.","co":"About 40% of the story cut.","nf":false,"l":"Before production, cost the full story in scenes and levels, and plan a natural ending for a shorter version.","i":"Story size sets the content bill for every scene, so a story too large for the schedule gets cut late, at whatever break exists. Planning a shorter natural ending early keeps that cut clean.","a":"If we had to cut 40% of our story, where would it end?","q":"“We cut about 40% of the story at a natural place because we had to get DONE.”","qa":"Tom Hall, co-founder of Ion Storm","st":"Back to the past. Part 4.2. id Software. Dark prophecies. Interview with Tom Hall","su":"https://medium.com/@unkndoomer/back-to-the-past-part-4-2-id-software-dark-prophecies-interview-with-tom-hall-d21b908a6842#:~:text=We%20cut%20about%2040%25%20of%20the,we%20had%20to%20get%20DONE","sd":"Sep 2025","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"angrybirds2","n":"Angry Birds 2","dv":"Rovio","y":"2015","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/angry-birds-2/id880047117","ls":"App Store","cv":"angrybirds2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Kept the launch build bare, assuming 3 billion downloads meant players knew the game","s":"At the July 2015 launch, retention and revenue fell short. Data showed players were bad at throwing birds and did not use bird abilities, so Rovio rebuilt the game around characters and more features.","w":"In pre-production, the slingshot games had passed 3 billion downloads, and the team assumed players knew how to play. Product lead Mans Wide kept the start screen clean and unlocked features as late as possible. Angry Birds 2 launched at the end of July 2015 and did not retain players or earn as planned. Data showed players were bad at throwing birds and did not use abilities. Rovio added an arena, daily challenges, hats and more; by 2019 the start screen had 25 buttons. At GDC 2019, Wide said the game had lost a lot by launching without them.","r":"Launch retention and revenue came in below plan.","re":"Rovio refocused the game on the birds and added many features.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Test the core action with players new to your franchise before launch; brand downloads do not prove they can play.","i":"Download counts measure reach, not skill. The team read 3 billion downloads as proof players could play, while holding back features left weak players with little to do. The trap is specific to sequels and franchise spin-offs.","a":"Have players new to our franchise played the core action before launch?","q":"“Obviously we assumed that since we have all these downloads people know how to play these games.”","qa":"Mans Wide, product lead of Angry Birds 2 at Rovio","st":"Top 3 Mistakes of 'Angry Birds 2'","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RzZSyjag7cQ&t=675s","sd":"2019","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RzZSyjag7cQ&t=689s"}]},{"k":"animalcrossing","n":"Animal Crossing","dv":"Nintendo","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/n01/n64/software/nus_p_nafj/index.html","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"animalcrossing.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Wrote the spec around the 64DD's large save storage and built-in clock","s":"When the 64DD fell away, the game moved to a cartridge with 1 megabit of flash. Four season islands with dungeons became one small village, and the adventure was dropped.","w":"Katsuya Eguchi's 2-page proposal used the 64DD's large save data so family members could share one world at different times. The spec planned 4 islands, one per season, with dungeons, and used the drive's clock. The team had briefed designers when the 64DD's situation kept changing, and the game was moved to a standard N64 cartridge. With 1 megabit of flash, content had to be cut drastically. The team kept the shared field, shrank it into one village and dropped the adventure. Doubutsu no Mori shipped on 14 April 2001; Eguchi and Hisashi Nogami told the story in December 2008.","r":"The design was cut to one village, keeping only shared play.","re":"The team rebuilt the game around one small village that players share.","co":"Drastic content cuts; 4 planned islands cut to 1 village.","nf":false,"l":"If your design depends on one hardware feature, name the core idea that must survive without it and test that on the fallback.","i":"The spec was sized to the 64DD's storage, so losing the drive removed most of the design at once. The team recovered because it knew its core: several players sharing one save. A design without a named core has nothing to keep.","a":"What survives of our design if that hardware feature disappears?","q":"“カセットだとバックアップメモリが大容量じゃなくなっちゃうので、内容を大幅に削らないといけなくなったんです。” (On a cartridge the backup memory would no longer be large, so we had to cut the content drastically.)","qa":"Katsuya Eguchi, producer of Animal Crossing at Nintendo","st":"社長が訊く ゲームセミナー２００８～『どうぶつの森』ができるまで～","su":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/etc/seminar2008/doubutsu/02.html#:~:text=%E3%82%AB%E3%82%BB%E3%83%83%E3%83%88%E3%81%A0%E3%81%A8%E3%83%90%E3%83%83%E3%82%AF%E3%82%A2%E3%83%83%E3%83%97%E3%83%A1%E3%83%A2%E3%83%AA%E3%81%8C%E5%A4%A7%E5%AE%B9%E9%87%8F%E3%81%98%E3%82%83%E3%81%AA%E3%81%8F%E3%81%AA%E3%81%A3%E3%81%A1%E3%82%83%E3%81%86%E3%81%AE%E3%81%A7,%E5%86%85%E5%AE%B9%E3%82%92%E5%A4%A7%E5%B9%85%E3%81%AB%E5%89%8A%E3%82%89%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84%E3%81%A8%E3%81%84%E3%81%91%E3%81%AA%E3%81%8F%E3%81%AA%E3%81%A3%E3%81%9F%E3%82%93%E3%81%A7%E3%81%99","sd":"Dec 2008","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"animalwell","n":"Animal Well","dv":"Shared Memory","y":"2024","sc":"Solo","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/813230/","ls":"Steam","cv":"animalwell.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Wrote his own C++ engine and tools instead of using Unity or Unreal","s":"A general-purpose 3D engine with no game behind it ate 3 years of nights and weekends and was written off in 2017. The 2D restart on a new bespoke engine took about 7 years solo.","w":"Billy Basso made a quick Metroidvania prototype in 2012 and in 2014 began coding a general-purpose 3D engine in his off hours to avoid the bloat and lag of the engines at his day job. By 2017 he judged it had solved problems in advance with no game direction, wrote it off as a learning experience and restarted with a 2D game on a new engine of his own. For years people told him he would be much farther along in Unity. The engine had no cutscene or dialogue tools, so the game has none. Animal Well launched in 2024.","r":"3 years of engine work written off; a 7-year solo project followed.","re":"Restarted in 2D with a genre and design goals set before the engine.","co":"3 years of nights and weekends written off; about 7 years solo after that.","nf":false,"l":"If you build your own engine, give it a specific game to serve from day one, not problems you expect later.","i":"An engine with no game has no way to decide what to leave out, so the work expands into problems that never arrive. Basso still recommends engine first, but as a solo side project with no deadline; a funded team pays those years in salaries.","a":"Which game is our engine work serving this month?","q":"“Animal Well was this sort of pure side project where I could implement things in what I felt was the ideal way,”","qa":"Billy Basso, developer of Animal Well, Shared Memory","st":"Why Animal Well's home-brewed engine was key to its success","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/why-animal-well-s-home-brewed-engine-was-key-to-its-success#:~:text=Animal%20Well%20was%20this%20sort,the%20ideal%20way","sd":"Nov 2024","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"anothereden","n":"Another Eden","dv":"Wright Flyer Studios","y":"2017","sc":"AA","se":"Port 2021","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1252600/","ls":"Steam","cv":"anothereden.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"External commitments","t":"Handed the battle system choice to a livestream poll about a year in","s":"61% of viewers chose turn-based combat over the real-time system the team had nearly finished. The vote triggered the third of 5 battle rebuilds in about 1.5 years.","w":"Wright Flyer Studios started Another Eden with about 10 people and a 1-month prototype that set the look of a pop-up picture book world. About 6 months in, the reveal at Tokyo Game Show 2015 drew a cold reaction, and the producer handed decisions to viewers. At Toukaigi 2016, about a year in, a Niconico poll asked turn-based or real-time. 61% chose turn-based, so the nearly finished real-time battle was rebuilt. The game shipped in April 2017. At CEDEC 2017, lead engineers Satoshi Abe and Masaru Ida described 5 battle rebuilds in 1.5 years.","r":"A nearly finished real-time battle system was thrown away and rebuilt.","re":"The team rebuilt combat as a classic command-based turn battle.","co":"The third of 5 battle system rebuilds in about 1.5 years.","nf":false,"l":"Settle core systems like battle type before you build them; if players will vote, hold the vote first.","i":"A public vote is binding in practice, so when it lands after a system is nearly built, the build is wasted. The team also lacked code assets and know-how, which caused other rebuilds: the vote explains one of the 5.","a":"Is any core system we have built still open to a player vote?","q":"“じゃあ、決めてください”","qa":"Producer of Another Eden at Wright Flyer Studios (GREE), as recounted at CEDEC 2017","st":"【CEDEC 2017】開発ノウハウのなさからバトルシステム完成まで5回も作り直し―「アナザーエデン」が完成するまでの意外な裏話とは？","su":"https://www.gamer.ne.jp/news/201709010043/#:~:text=%E3%81%9D%E3%81%93%E3%81%A7%E3%83%97%E3%83%AD%E3%83%87%E3%83%A5%E3%83%BC%E3%82%B5%E3%83%BC%E3%81%8B%E3%82%89,%E4%B8%B8%E6%8A%95%E3%81%92%E3%81%8C%E8%A1%8C%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8C%E3%81%9F%E3%81%A8%E3%81%84%E3%81%86","sd":"Sep 2017","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"anthem","n":"Anthem","dv":"BioWare","y":"2019","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built BioWare's first online-only action game on the Frostbite engine","s":"Kotaku's 19-source report says a save-load system, a third-person camera and other basics did not exist in the engine, lighting bakes took 24 hours and small bug fixes took days of back-and-forth.","w":"Aaryn Flynn, BioWare's general manager, said he chose Frostbite in late 2011 because the studio's Eclipse engine could not do open worlds, strong rendering or multiplayer. By the time Anthem started, Frostbite was BioWare's engine for every project. Kotaku's April 2019 report, based on 19 interviews, says the engine had no save-load system or third-person camera, so the team rebuilt a large part of its technology from scratch, with 24-hour lighting bakes and bug fixes that took days. In 2016 EA moved BioWare's Frostbite-experienced programmers to FIFA. DICE later sent a strike team. Anthem launched in February 2019.","r":"Basic systems had to be built from scratch and iteration slowed to days per fix.","re":"DICE sent a strike team to work out Frostbite problems and improve visuals.","co":"Lighting bakes of up to 24 hours; bug fixes taking days instead of minutes.","nf":false,"l":"If the engine is inherited or set from above, budget its missing features as pre-production work with its own schedule.","i":"Kotaku's sources say Frostbite was built for Battlefield-style shooters, so every basic system an online action RPG needed had to be built or hacked, and support depended on EA's central team. Flynn still defends the 2011 choice; the gap was between engine and game.","a":"Which features does our engine lack for this game, and who builds them?","q":"“Frostbite is full of razor blades.”","qa":"Unnamed former BioWare employee, quoted by Kotaku","st":"How BioWare's Anthem Went Wrong","su":"https://kotaku.com/how-biowares-anthem-went-wrong-1833731964#:~:text=Frostbite%20is%20full%20of%20razor%20blades","sd":"Apr 2019","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Started Anthem's tech from scratch on Frostbite instead of reusing Inquisition and Andromeda systems","s":"Anthem spent about 5% of its programming budget on tools, against about 30% on Inquisition, by Mark Darrah's account. UI work ran far slower than expected, and the gap surfaced only in 2018.","w":"EA moved its studios onto its in-house Frostbite engine, and BioWare first used it for Dragon Age: Inquisition in 2011. From the start, Anthem's leaders chose to build much of its technology anew, because the game was online and older systems might not hold up. Kotaku's sources described sluggish tools, 24-hour lighting bakes and bug fixes that took days. In 2016 EA moved BioWare's Frostbite programmers to FIFA. In 2018 executive producer Mark Darrah found the UI team blocked by missing tools. In 2026 he said Anthem spent about 5% of its tech budget on tools.","r":"UI work ran orders of magnitude slower than leads expected.","re":"Darrah found the gap in 2018, too late to fix it before launch.","co":"Tools got about 5% of the tech budget; UI ran orders of magnitude slower.","nf":false,"l":"When you rebuild systems the engine lacks, set the tools budget as a share of programming before production and track it each milestone.","i":"A shared engine covers only part of a game, and each team rebuilt the rest, here three times across BioWare. Thin tools slow every content team. Darrah's own boundary: a content-heavy game needs more tools work than a mostly handcrafted one.","a":"What share of our programming budget goes to tools?","q":"“The part of the technology that wasn't included in Frostbite had basically been rebuilt three times, which to me is complete insanity.”","qa":"Mark Darrah, former executive producer of Anthem at BioWare","st":"The Truth About What Happened on Anthem - Complete (2011-2026)","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CxwrCRdqZb8&t=4943s","sd":"Jan 2026","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CxwrCRdqZb8&t=4976s"},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Kept Anthem in pre-production for about four years without locking its core systems","s":"Production was due in June 2017 but did not start. The core loop, story and missions were built in the last 12 to 16 months before the February 2019 launch.","w":"Casey Hudson started the project in late 2012 and left BioWare in 2014. Mark Darrah later said the team refused to name comparisons such as Destiny, and decisions were often not made at all. Flight was cut and re-added several times, and vertical levels were built and then flattened for combat. By the end of 2016 Anthem had been in pre-production for roughly four years. Production was due in June 2017 but did not start. Darrah took over as executive producer in October 2017. The game shipped in February 2019, and its Metacritic score settled at 55.","r":"Production slipped past June 2017 and was squeezed into about 18 months.","re":"BioWare made Mark Darrah executive producer and moved Dragon Age 4 staff onto Anthem.","co":"About 4 years in pre-production; production only in the final 18 months.","nf":false,"l":"Name the shipped game each core system starts from, so the team iterates from a known point.","i":"Without a named starting point, each system began from a blank page and took years of iteration to reach the obvious answer. A reference only gives the start; someone still has to make the final call.","a":"Which shipped game does each of our core systems start from?","q":"“And the resistance to drawing those comparisons prevented a foundation from being built below the game in this time period.”","qa":"Mark Darrah, executive producer at BioWare (took over Anthem in October 2017)","st":"How BioWare's Anthem Went Wrong","su":"https://kotaku.com/how-biowares-anthem-went-wrong-1833731964#:~:text=They%20had%20been%20in%20idea,making%20and%20make%20it","sd":"Apr 2019","sy":"Press report","w8":4,"qu":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9_QY8F8z_4c&t=1421s"},{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Announced a fall 2018 date at E3 2017 with barely one mission implemented","s":"Kotaku's sources say the demo was mostly guesswork and the date was never realistic. EA then held the team to its fiscal year, and Anthem shipped in February 2019 with all missions built in the last 12 to 16 months.","w":"At Christmas 2016 BioWare showed EA's Patrick Söderlund a build; three people told Kotaku he called it unacceptable. Over the next 6 weeks the team crunched on a new demo and re-added flying over a weekend, unsure whether it was for real. At E3 2017 BioWare announced Anthem for fall 2018, although the game was still in pre-production with barely one mission implemented; developers called the demo mostly guesswork. EA would not allow a delay past its fiscal year end in March 2019. Anthem shipped in February 2019, its missions made in the final 12 to 16 months.","r":"The public date locked a schedule the team never considered realistic.","re":"EA held the launch inside its fiscal year; Anthem shipped in February 2019.","co":"Fall 2018 promise slipped to February 2019; all missions built in 12 to 16 months.","nf":false,"l":"Do not announce a date or show a public demo until a playable slice exists that the team can build out.","i":"Kotaku's sources describe a demo hacked together to show what was possible, which then set public expectations and the schedule before production had started. A show demo is a budget line: the work to make the real game match it is rework, not marketing.","a":"Does our show demo use only systems that exist in the build?","q":"“The demo was not actually built properly—a lot of it was fake, like most E3 demos.”","qa":"Unnamed Anthem developer, quoted by Kotaku","st":"How BioWare's Anthem Went Wrong","su":"https://kotaku.com/how-biowares-anthem-went-wrong-1833731964#:~:text=The%20demo%20was%20not%20actually,like%20most%20E3%20demos","sd":"Apr 2019","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"apballpointsbulletin","n":"APB: All Points Bulletin","dv":"Realtime Worlds","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"Relaunch 2011","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/113400/","ls":"Steam","cv":"apballpointsbulletin.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Designed APB around constant PvP for 100 fully customised players with vehicles","s":"APB launched in June 2010 to a Metacritic score of 58 and weak sales. Realtime Worlds went into administration that August, and the original game shut down months after launch.","w":"Realtime Worlds conceived APB in 2005 as an open-world online game where players fought each other. The client had to run 100 fully customised players with vehicles. The company unwound its Webzen publishing deal in April 2008 at a cash cost of $10.4 million and raised over $100 million, including $21 million in January 2010. The planned 2009 launch slipped to June 2010. APB scored 58 on Metacritic and sold 9,181 units in the US by the end of July. Realtime Worlds entered administration in August 2010. In 2013, Dave Jones said the game was too focused on PvP.","r":"Weak sales, and the studio entered administration within 7 weeks of launch.","re":"The game was shut down and later relaunched as free-to-play APB Reloaded.","co":"Over $100M raised from investors; 242 jobs lost.","nf":false,"l":"If the core mode is PvP, build and test a PvE activity players can retreat to after a losing streak.","i":"When every activity is a fight against other players, a losing streak leaves nothing else to do. Jones also named the high client spec and a wrong guess about the market as causes, so PvP alone did not sink APB.","a":"What can a player do here after losing five PvP matches in a row?","q":"“It was definitely too focused on PvP, which is really hard.”","qa":"Dave Jones, creative director on APB at Realtime Worlds","st":"What APB got right, and why it initially failed","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/what-apb-got-right-and-why-it-initially-failed/#:~:text=It%20was%20definitely%20too%20focused,which%20is%20really%20hard","sd":"Aug 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Kept building after two major shifts of focus without prototyping the new vision","s":"The vision stayed unclear and design flaws piled up. When outside testers gave feedback, the team was too busy fixing bugs and optimizing to change much.","w":"A gameplay engineer worked on APB at Realtime Worlds for five years. In that time the project had at least two major shifts of focus but only one prototyping period, which he calls bad. The team never prototyped the new vision after each shift. Internal criticism was often dismissed, and the bug database filled with design flaws. Beta feedback arrived while the team was busy fixing defects and optimizing. He puts the point of no return 6 to 8 months before launch, at the end of June 2010. The game shut down months later as the company dissolved.","r":"Design flaws surfaced only when there was no capacity left to fix them.","re":"After launch, a cross-functional group reworked the core game.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Each time the vision changes, go back and prototype the new version before the team builds more content.","i":"A prototype tells a team what it is making and what it must build. After a refocus, the old prototype answers the wrong question, so flaws surface only in testing. A small change of direction may not need a full new prototype.","a":"Have we prototyped the vision we changed to, or only the old one?","q":"“Each time we refocused we should have sat down and reprototyped the new “vision” extensively both to get a firm idea of what we wanted to make and also to know what we needed to build.”","qa":"meheleventyone (pseudonym), gameplay engineer at Realtime Worlds","st":"APB Postmortem in a Picture!","su":"https://meheleventyone.com/2014/04/15/apb-postmortem-in-a-picture/#:~:text=Each%20time%20we%20refocused%20we,what%20we%20needed%20to%20build","sd":"Apr 2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":3}]},{"k":"apexlegends","n":"Apex Legends","dv":"Respawn Entertainment","y":"2019","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1172470/","ls":"Steam","cv":"apexlegends.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built Apex on Titanfall 2's code and cut titans after daily playtests","s":"Daily one-to-two-hour playtests from spring 2017 drove the cuts: titans, wall-running, double jump, most of two dozen hero prototypes. The game shipped in February 2019 with about 115 developers and no announcement.","w":"Respawn started in spring 2017 from the Titanfall 2 base, an evolution of the Source engine, so the first prototypes had pilots and titans. The team played every day for an hour or two. Titans were an unbalancing force and went; pilot mobility, wall-running and double-jumping were incomprehensible and went too. Two dozen heroes were prototyped and narrowed to 8. The team grew from 85 on Titanfall 2 to about 115. McCoy asked EA executives to trust the team under a giant question mark over revenue. Apex Legends launched in February 2019 with no prior announcement.","r":"About 22 months from first prototype to launch, with about 115 developers.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Team grew from 85 to about 115; about 22 months.","nf":false,"l":"Playtest every day from the first month and cut the features the tests reject, even your signature ones.","i":"Daily play with a working tech base gave the team evidence fast, so even fan-favourite features like titans could be cut with confidence. Reused tech is the enabler: on a new engine the same cadence is much harder to keep.","a":"Are we playing our build every day, and what did yesterday's test cut?","q":"“You're driving around in a papier-mâché titan.”","qa":"Drew McCoy, executive producer, Respawn Entertainment","st":"Respawn Discusses Entering The World Of Battle Royale","su":"https://gameinformer.com/interview/2019/02/04/respawn-discusses-entering-the-world-of-battle-royale#:~:text=driving%20around%20in%20a,papier-m%C3%A2ch%C3%A9%20titan","sd":"Feb 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Cancelled Titanfall 3 after 10 months to build a battle royale prototype instead","s":"The prototype became Apex Legends. Respawn revealed and released it on the same day in February 2019, and it passed 25 million players in its first week.","w":"After Titanfall 2, Respawn spent about 10 months on Titanfall 3, with new tech, several missions and a first playable. Narrative lead Mohammad Alavi said it was only incrementally better, and the multiplayer team was struggling. After PUBG came out, developers built a battle royale map with Titanfall 3 classes. For about two months, 10 people played the regular modes in Friday playtests and 100 played the battle royale. After a break the team decided to pivot; Alavi said EA did not know for six months. Respawn revealed and released Apex Legends on the same day in February 2019.","r":"Titanfall 3 stopped, and the team moved to the battle royale prototype.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"About 10 months of Titanfall 3 work dropped.","nf":false,"l":"Put your sequel's first playable next to a cheap alternative prototype, and count which one your own team keeps playing.","i":"Internal playtest turnout gave a hard signal: 10 people for the sequel modes, 100 for the battle royale. That signal fits multiplayer games the team plays itself; for single-player or story games, staff enthusiasm says less about the market.","a":"Is our first playable a step change, or only a bit better?","q":"“But I’ll make this clear—incrementally better, it wasn’t revolutionary.”","qa":"Mohammad Alavi, narrative lead designer on Titanfall 3 at Respawn Entertainment","st":"Titanfall 3 Was in Development For 10 Months Before Being Canceled, ex-Developer Claims","su":"https://insider-gaming.com/titanfall-3-cancelled/#:~:text=But%20I%E2%80%99ll%20make%20this%20clear,it%20wasn%E2%80%99t%20revolutionary","sd":"Jun 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"apexlegendsmobile","n":"Apex Legends Mobile","dv":"Respawn Entertainment","y":"2022","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Co-developed Apex Legends Mobile with Tencent's Lightspeed & Quantum Studios","s":"Respawn said the content pipeline fell short on quality, quantity and cadence. After months of working with its partner, it shut the game down on 1 May 2023, under a year after worldwide launch.","w":"In January 2020, EA's CFO Blake Jorgensen said a partner was needed to launch Apex on mobile in China and was also helping build a global version. A dedicated Respawn team co-developed the game with Tencent's Lightspeed & Quantum Studios, the PUBG Mobile developers. It launched worldwide on 17 May 2022. In February 2023, with EA's quarterly results, Respawn said the content pipeline had fallen short after a strong start. After months of working with the partner, both sides agreed to end the game. Purchases were switched off at once, and it shut down on 1 May 2023.","r":"Content updates fell short on quality, quantity and cadence within a year.","re":"Respawn and the partner agreed to shut the game down; no refunds.","co":"Game closed 11.5 months after worldwide launch; purchases not refunded.","nf":false,"l":"Before signing a co-developer for a live game, check that it can deliver seasons of content at your quality bar.","i":"A live game needs content every season, so the partner's pipeline is the product after launch. A strong launch build says little about season cadence. The statement names the pipeline, not the partner choice itself, as the cause.","a":"Has our partner shipped content at our quality bar, season after season?","q":"“Following a strong start, the content pipeline for Apex Legends Mobile has begun to fall short of that bar for quality, quantity, and cadence.”","qa":"Respawn Entertainment, company statement","st":"EA Is Shutting Down Apex Legends Mobile And Battlefield Mobile","su":"https://gameinformer.com/2023/02/01/ea-is-shutting-down-apex-legends-mobile-and-battlefield-mobile#:~:text=Following%20a%20strong%20start%2C%20the,quality%2C%20quantity%2C%20and%20cadence","sd":"Feb 2023","sy":"Company statement","w8":3}]},{"k":"arcanum","n":"Arcanum","dv":"Troika","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/500810/","ls":"Steam","cv":"arcanum.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Allowed every race to mix magic and technology, including builds that could not work","s":"With about 12 people, Troika could not test every permutation. Co-founder Tim Cain later called the game buggy and said serious feature cuts early on would have helped.","w":"Troika was founded in 1998, and Leonard Boyarsky, Tim Cain and Jason Anderson wrote the Arcanum design while waiting for a contract. Much of it shipped. The character system set magic against technology inside every character, and players could do virtually anything: a Dwarf was penalised at once for using magic, an Elf for using technology. That allowed builds that could not work. Troika held the team to about 12 people. In 2012 Cain said the games were buggy and needed serious feature cuts early. In 2017 Boyarsky said no reasonable budget could test all the permutations.","r":"The build permutations outgrew testing, and the game shipped buggy.","re":"None documented; in hindsight Boyarsky would cut the builds that cannot work.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you lock a free-form character system, count the build combinations and cut the ones that cannot work before QA must test them.","i":"Every open combination is a case QA has to test, so test cost grows with the permutations while a 12-person team stays the same size. Boyarsky means builds that cannot work: cutting those keeps the same game, cutting real choices would not.","a":"Which build combinations in our design cannot work at all?","q":"“I think [that] was a game where I'm not sure any reasonable person would have put in the amount of money and time needed to successfully test that game with all the permutations we put in there.”","qa":"Leonard Boyarsky, co-founder of Troika Games","st":"The path of Leonard Boyarsky - Vampire, Diablo, and what's next for Obsidian","su":"https://www.pcgamesn.com/leonard-boyarsky-new-obsidian-game-vampire-making-of-diablo-3-story#:~:text=I%20think%20%5Bthat%5D%20was%20a%20game,permutations%20we%20put%20in%20there","sd":"Dec 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"ashesofthesingularity","n":"Ashes of the Singularity","dv":"Oxide Games","y":"2016","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/507490/","ls":"Steam","cv":"ashesofthesingularity.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Set a 4-core, 2GB GDDR5 minimum spec for a new 64-bit engine","s":"The spec excluded about half the user base. Buyers on marginal hardware left poor Steam reviews, and game sites struggled to find reviewers who could run it. The engine itself worked and carried DirectX 12.","w":"Around 2012 Stardock funded a start-up, Oxide, to build a 64-bit, core-neutral engine on a very modest budget; Supreme Commander 1.0's reported budget was 9 times bigger. The engine set its minimum at a 2GB GDDR5 card and a 4-core CPU, a price Wardell says the team accepted to make a future-proof game. Wardell could not focus on design until summer 2015, 8 months before ship. On DirectX 12 the engine gave an 80 percent boost. Ashes released in April 2016. Wardell's postmortem listed the spec's hit on sales reach, Steam reviews and reviewer hardware among what went wrong.","r":"About half of PC gamers could not buy it, and reviewers lacked hardware.","re":"Wardell kept the spec as the price of a future-proof engine; no rescue needed.","co":"About half the user base excluded; $49.99 price a barrier.","nf":false,"l":"Before locking a future-proof minimum spec, count the players and reviewers it removes today.","i":"A 64-bit, multi-core engine needed hardware half of 2016 PCs lacked, so the audience shrank and reviews came from buyers at the edge of the spec. The bet paid in engine terms; it only makes sense if the engine will outlive the first game.","a":"What share of our target players meets our minimum spec today?","q":"“We knew this going in and it was a price we were willing to pay to make sure we could create a future-proof game.”","qa":"Brad Wardell, founder of Stardock Entertainment and cofounder of Oxide Games","st":"Postmortem: Stardock and Oxide Games' Ashes of the Singularity","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-stardock-and-oxide-games-i-ashes-of-the-singularity-i-#:~:text=We%20knew%20this%20going%20in,a%20future-proof%20game.","sd":"Apr 2016","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"assassinscreedunity","n":"Assassin's Creed Unity","dv":"Ubisoft","y":"2014","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/289650/","ls":"Steam","cv":"assassinscreedunity.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Combined next-gen hardware, an open world and Montreal's co-op in one Assassin's Creed","s":"Unity launched without enough polish. Two years later Ubisoft's CEO said the team had tried to do too much at once, and promised more polish time.","w":"In 2014, Ubisoft was moving from old consoles to new ones and did not want to design one game for both, so two teams made two Assassin's Creed games. Unity was the new-generation one. It was next-gen and open world, and Ubisoft Montreal was adding co-op and multiplayer, all at the same time. Unity launched on 11 November 2014 without enough polish. In December 2016, CEO Yves Guillemot told TIME the team had tried to do too many things at once. He said Ubisoft would give its games a lot more time to polish.","r":"Unity shipped without enough polish.","re":"Ubisoft said it would give its games much more time to polish.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you lock the date, list every first-time element in the release and cut any you cannot polish together.","i":"New hardware, an open world and a new mode each need their own learning time, and all of them compete for the same polish window at the end. One new element on proven ground carries far less risk.","a":"How many first-time elements does this release carry at once?","q":"“Then, when Unity arrived, it wasn’t polished enough.”","qa":"Yves Guillemot, CEO of Ubisoft","st":"Ubisoft's CEO Talks Assassin's Creed, Takeover Bids And More","su":"https://time.com/4607762/ubisoft-yves-guillemot-interview/#:~:text=Then%2C%20when%20Unity%20arrived,polished%20enough","sd":"Dec 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"astrobot","n":"Astro Bot","dv":"Team Asobi","y":"2024","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.playstation.com/en-us/concept/10002684","ls":"PlayStation","cv":"astrobot.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Spent a third of Astro Bot's schedule on two-week prototypes owned by programmers","s":"The final game landed close to the pitch, which had gone through 23 internal iterations. When the 3-year plan stretched by 6 months, the studio stayed at 65 people instead of 80 and the budget stayed flat.","w":"Team Asobi picked only 10 per cent of its brainstormed ideas to prototype. Programmers owned the prototyping, in two-week cycles, for around a third of the production schedule. The original pitch was written in May 2021, a few months after prototyping began, and went through 23 iterations before top management saw it. The team asked whether the game should be open world and chose levels for control over variety. The 3-year plan stretched by 6 months; Doucet kept the team at 65 instead of ramping to 80, and the budget remained the same. Astro Bot released in September 2024.","r":"A 6-month slip absorbed with fewer people and no budget increase.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"3 years planned, 6 months more; 65 staff instead of 80; budget unchanged.","nf":false,"l":"Reserve about a third of the schedule for two-week prototypes before you commit to content.","i":"The game landed close to its pitch because its mechanics were proved in prototypes, so the slip could be paid with time instead of people. That holds for a level-based game with a known hero; a new IP with unproven systems may need more.","a":"How much of our schedule is set aside for prototypes before content starts?","q":"“The overall budget remained the same.”","qa":"Nicolas Doucet, game director of Astro Bot, Team Asobi","st":"How did Team Asobi make the best game of 2024?","su":"https://knowledge.edge-online.com/p/how-did-team-asobi-make-the-best-game-of-2024#:~:text=The%20overall%20budget,remained%20the%20same","sd":"Mar 2025","sy":"Participant talk via press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"atomicheart","n":"Atomic Heart","dv":"Mundfish","y":"2023","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/668580/","ls":"Steam","cv":"atomicheart.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned a debut new IP with unique story, structure, mechanics and multiplayer","s":"The release target of late 2018 slipped by over 4 years. Multiplayer was cut along the way, and the founder put the delay down to youth, ambition and new technology.","w":"Mundfish began planning Atomic Heart in 2017 and wanted something unique from the start: story, structure and many mechanics. When Wccftech first covered the game in May 2018, the goal was a release by the end of that year. During development the team dropped big ideas, including multiplayer, and kept the single-player core of story, combat and gunplay. By January 2023 Mundfish had 130 developers in 10 countries. Founder and game director Robert Bagratuni said the ambitions had not always matched reality. The game came out in February 2023.","r":"Release slipped from late 2018 to February 2023, without multiplayer.","re":"Mundfish cut multiplayer and focused on story, combat and gunplay.","co":"Over 4 years of delay against the 2018 target.","nf":false,"l":"On a debut project, cut the big features the team has never shipped, such as multiplayer, before you announce a release date.","i":"Bagratuni described development as a product of dozens of factors, where one zero sinks the whole. Each extra ambition adds a factor. He names several causes, so the slip cannot be pinned on multiplayer alone.","a":"Which of our big features has this team never shipped before?","q":"“Our unwanted delay can be attributed to youth, ambition, innovation, and new technology!”","qa":"Robert Bagratuni, founder and game director of Mundfish","st":"Atomic Heart Final Q&A Part I – Mundfish Head Talks About DLCs, Delays, and Team Geographical Distribution","su":"https://wccftech.com/atomic-heart-final-qa-part-i-mundfish-head-talks-about-delays-team-geographical-distribution-and-more/#:~:text=Our%20unwanted%20delay%20can%20be,and%20new%20technology","sd":"Jan 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"audiosurf","n":"Audiosurf","dv":"Dylan Fitterer","y":"2008","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/12900/","ls":"Steam","cv":"audiosurf.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Kept a self-funded solo project on PC instead of a console download service","s":"No dev kits, publishing deal or certification stood between the game and its players. It became the most successful Steam title of its launch month, by units and by revenue.","w":"Dylan Fitterer built Audiosurf alone and without financial backing, and he stayed on PC rather than move to a console download service. Dev kits were a hurdle he did not want, and a closed platform would have added development time and the hunt for a publishing deal. He kept working until the game was done and needed no one's approval to release it. Audiosurf launched on Steam in February 2008 at $10 and led Steam that month by units and revenue. In June 2008 he said he could still ship features, such as Last.fm stats, with no certification.","r":"Shipped with no approval step and led Steam in its launch month.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you pick a platform, list the dev kits, approvals and publishing deals it needs, and check your budget can pay for them.","i":"A closed platform puts gates before release: kits to buy, a deal to sign, certification to pass. Each gate costs an unfunded solo developer time he cannot spare. The argument weakens for a funded team whose publisher handles certification.","a":"Can we pay for every kit and approval our platforms need?","q":"“I didn't have to ask anyone to release it, except for Elizabeth, my wife.”","qa":"Dylan Fitterer, creator of Audiosurf at Invisible Handlebar","st":"In-Depth: Audiosurf - A PC Gaming Postmortem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/in-depth-i-audiosurf-i---a-pc-gaming-postmortem#:~:text=I%20didn%27t%20have%20to%20ask%20anyone,Elizabeth%2C%20my%20wife","sd":"Jun 2008","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4}]},{"k":"avowed","n":"Avowed","dv":"Obsidian Entertainment","y":"2025","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2457220/","ls":"Steam","cv":"avowed.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Staffing","t":"Rebooted Avowed in 2021 with a large team instead of a small pre-production crew","s":"Director Carrie Patel told Bloomberg that lessons the team learned while building the game should ideally have come from a small, true pre-production. Avowed shipped in February 2025.","w":"Avowed had been in development for a couple of years as a larger game known inside Obsidian as 'multiplayer Skyrim'. KitGuru and Insider Gaming, relaying a Bloomberg interview, report that a 2021 pivot after Microsoft's acquisition rebooted it as it was preparing to enter production. Carrie Patel took over as director and built a new vertical slice and production plan with a team KitGuru put at 80 developers. Multiplayer and the open world gave way to zones like The Outer Worlds. The game shipped in February 2025. A year later, Bloomberg reported it had missed Microsoft's sales forecasts.","r":"Lessons were learned at full team scale instead of in pre-production.","re":"Obsidian said it wants smaller budgets and 3 to 4 year dev cycles.","co":"Team of about 80 during the reboot; 6 to 7 years in development.","nf":false,"l":"When you reset a game's direction, shrink to a small team to build the new vertical slice and production plan.","i":"A large team needs work every day, so open questions get answered in production instead of on a small scale. Bloomberg did not tie the long cycle or the sales miss to this choice, so treat it as a process lesson.","a":"If we reset direction, how small can the team get?","q":"“A lot of lessons we learned as we were building this game, ideally we would’ve learned on a small scale with a true preproduction.”","qa":"Carrie Patel, game director of Avowed at Obsidian Entertainment","st":"Avowed Could Have Been 'Multiplayer Skyrim' Before a Reboot Happened","su":"https://insider-gaming.com/avowed-multiplayer-skyrim-before-reboot/#:~:text=A%20lot%20of%20lessons%20we,with%20a%20true%20preproduction","sd":"Feb 2025","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"babaisyou","n":"Baba Is You","dv":"Hempuli","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/736260/","ls":"Steam","cv":"babaisyou.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built about 80 levels in the first months after the jam prototype","s":"The first testable build had plenty of puzzles, but several were really weak because the designer had not yet got the feel of the game. He later named them among the work that did not make the final game.","w":"Arvi Teikari made Baba Is You at the Nordic Game Jam in Copenhagen in 2017, with about 10 levels in 48 hours. After it was voted best game of the event, he decided to turn it into a full game. In the first couple of months he made about 80 new levels for the first testable build. Several were really weak: he went too fast and did not yet get the feel of the game. The full game took just under 2 years, and in December 2019 he counted that batch among the work cut before release.","r":"Part of the first level batch was too weak for the final game.","re":"Teikari kept iterating and cut words, such as Hold, that made dull puzzles.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you build levels in bulk, make a handful, play them, and write down what makes a good one in this game.","i":"In a rules-driven puzzle game, good levels appear only after the designer learns what the rules can do. Levels built before that are guesses. Early volume still teaches you something, so plan for part of it to go.","a":"Do we know what makes a good level before we build dozens?","q":"“For example, I made ~80 new levels during the first couple months of development for the first testable build of the game, and several levels in this first batch were really weak because I went too fast and didn’t really “get” the feel of the game yet.”","qa":"Arvi Teikari, creator of Baba Is You at Hempuli Oy","st":"2019 Best Original Concept Winner Interview: Arvi Teikari (Baba is You)","su":"https://intoindiegames.com/features/2019-best-original-concept-winner-interview-arvi-teikari-baba-is-you/#:~:text=For%20example%2C%20I%20made%20~80%20new%20levels,the%20feel%20of%20the%20game%20yet","sd":"Dec 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"badpiggies","n":"Bad Piggies","dv":"Rovio","y":"2012","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bad-piggies/id533451786","ls":"App Store","cv":"badpiggies.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Tried Unity on the prototype, then took that prototype straight into production","s":"On a tight timetable, the team could tweak physics and build gadgets in very short cycles. One gadget went from an afternoon test into the game on the spot.","w":"Rovio built the Bad Piggies prototype in Unity to try the engine out. The prototype was high quality, so the team went straight into production in the same tools. The timetable was tight, and emergent physics made balancing complex. Short cycles for new gadgets and physics tweaks gave the team many iterations: programmers built a spring in one afternoon, and it went into the game on the spot. Rovio rewrote a little of its in-house atlas tooling on top of Unity and streamlined sprite management. The game shipped in 2012, and producer Jaakko Haapasalo described the process in January 2013.","r":"The team iterated fast on physics despite a tight timetable.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Build your prototype in the engine you might ship on, and judge the engine by how fast the prototype iterates.","i":"A prototype in the candidate engine tests the tools and the game at once, so a good result can become production code. That works for a small physics game like this one; a bigger game also needs a pipeline and performance check before you commit.","a":"Is our prototype built in the engine we plan to ship on?","q":"“The resulting prototype was high-quality, so we decided to go straight into production.”","qa":"Jaakko Haapasalo, producer of Bad Piggies at Rovio","st":"Unity Focus: Bad Piggies","su":"https://mcvuk.com/development-news/unity-focus-bad-piggies/#:~:text=The%20resulting%20prototype%20was%20high-quality,go%20straight%20into%20production","sd":"Jan 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"balatro","n":"Balatro","dv":"LocalThunk","y":"2024","sc":"Solo","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2379780/","ls":"Steam","cv":"balatro.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Kept Balatro an unpaid side project until Steam wishlists and a streamer showed demand","s":"Spend before signing was a $100 Steam fee and a Fiverr soundtrack. Wishlists went from 48 in May 2023 to 208,401 at launch; day one sold 119,000 Steam units.","w":"LocalThunk started the prototype in December 2021 and stopped in March 2022 when the drive faded. In August 2022 a friend had played the beta for dozens of hours, and scope grew. In March 2023 he paid for a Fiverr soundtrack and the $100 Steam fee, the only money spent at that point. A public beta in late May 2023 had 48 wishlists; a streamer took it to 2,440 by end of June. A Playstack deal followed in July 2023. Balatro launched on February 20, 2024 with 208,401 wishlists and sold 119,000 Steam units that day.","r":"Launch day sold 119,000 Steam units on near-zero spend.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"$100 Steam fee and a Fiverr soundtrack; about 26 months, mostly part-time.","nf":false,"l":"Spend nothing but time until wishlists from strangers, not friends' praise, tell you to commit.","i":"Every step up in scope or money followed a signal from outside: a friend's hours, then wishlists and a streamer. This works for a game one person can build part-time; a team with payroll cannot wait this way.","a":"What outside signal, not a friend's praise, tells us to commit money?","q":"“I was very nervous about this because it was (at the time) the only money I had spent or planned to spend on the game.”","qa":"LocalThunk, solo developer of Balatro","st":"The Balatro Timeline","su":"https://localthunk.com/blog/balatro-timeline-3aarh#:~:text=I%20was%20very%20nervous%20about,spend%20on%20the%20game.","sd":"Mar 2024","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"baldursgate","n":"Baldur's Gate","dv":"BioWare","y":"1998","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/228280/","ls":"Steam","cv":"baldursgate.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the Infinity Engine in parallel with the game's content","s":"With the engine still being written, the team ran short of time for its design goals. The pressure pushed it toward simple areas and a simpler game design.","w":"BioWare had shipped only one title, Shattered Steel, before Baldur's Gate. Its team of inexperienced but talented people built the Infinity Engine and the game's content at the same time. BioWare later said the team lacked time to reach their design goals, and the parallel work created extreme pressure for simple areas and simple design. Baldur's Gate shipped in 1998 after nearly 90 man-years of work, won nearly all PC RPG of the Year awards and sold about 1.5 million copies. In March 2001, in the sequel's postmortem, BioWare named this as the problem it set out to fix.","r":"Areas and game design were kept simple to fit the time.","re":"For the sequel, BioWare kept the engine stable and gave designers time.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before content production starts, list the engine features that are still unbuilt and cut the design scope that waits on them.","i":"Content made on a moving engine waits for the tech or gets redone, so design time shrinks from both ends. The squeeze is sharpest on a first game with a new team; a mature engine removes most of it, as the sequel showed.","a":"Which of our content waits on engine features that are not built yet?","q":"“In the case of the original BALDUR’S GATE, we felt we didn’t have adequate time to reach our design goals; we were simultaneously developing the BioWare Infinity Engine while creating the content in BALDUR’S GATE.”","qa":"James Ohlen, Greg Zeschuk and Ray Muzyka, lead designer and co-founders of BioWare","st":"BioWare’s Baldur’s Gate II","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_March_2001.pdf#page=33","sd":"Mar 2001","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"baldursgate3","n":"Baldur's Gate 3","dv":"Larian Studios","y":"2023","sc":"AAA","se":"Early Access 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1086940/","ls":"Steam","cv":"baldursgate3.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Brought AAA cinematics to a party RPG before the genre had proven it could pay","s":"Larian's pre-production estimate of how big it had to become proved wrong. It chose to grow to 400 people rather than scale the game down, and full release slipped to 2023.","w":"Larian made Divinity: Original Sin 2 with about 150 people, then spent a year in pre-production estimating Baldur's Gate 3 and hiring for cinematics Divinity never had. Early Access began in 2020. In March 2022 founder Swen Vincke said the estimates had been wrong: once the team understood what animated dialogue demanded, it chose to scale up rather than cut, reaching 400 people across 7 studios. Writing became one of the last steps instead of the first. He pushed the finish to 2023. Version 1.0 shipped on 3 August 2023; in September Vincke said the genre had now proven itself.","r":"Team grew to 400 people and full release moved to 2023.","re":"Reworked its writing and iteration process around the cinematic pipeline.","co":"Headcount grew from about 150 to 400; full release pushed to 2023.","nf":false,"l":"Before you commit to animated cinematics for every scene, prototype one and cost the whole pipeline it drags behind it.","i":"Cinematic dialogue turns every line into work for animators, lighting, VFX and casting, so a text-era estimate of team size misses by a wide margin. Larian could absorb it because earlier games paid for it; most studios would have had to cut.","a":"Have we costed the full cinematic pipeline for one dialogue scene?","q":"“And so we had two choices: we could scale it down, or we could scale ourselves up.”","qa":"Swen Vincke, founder and CEO of Larian Studios","st":"Larian's Baldur's Gate 3 team is 10 times bigger than when it made Divinity: Original Sin","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/larians-baldurs-gate-3-team-is-10-times-bigger-than-when-it-made-divinity-original-sin/#:~:text=And%20so%20we%20had%20two%20choices,scale%20ourselves%20up","sd":"Mar 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"baldursgateii","n":"Baldur's Gate II","dv":"BioWare","y":"2000","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/257350/","ls":"Steam","cv":"baldursgateii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Reused the Infinity Engine from the start and scheduled upgrades around designers and artists","s":"Most of the 20-month schedule went to content instead of engine or tool work, and adding most new features was relatively easy.","w":"After Baldur's Gate, where engine and content were built at once, BioWare decided from the very beginning to make Baldur's Gate II on the Infinity Engine. The first step was a complete engine feature list, drawn from an internal list and fan requests; it included higher resolution and 3D card support. Most engine changes were scheduled around designers and artists, so they did not disrupt content work. The 25 to 30 full-time developers had 20 months and about $4 million. The game shipped on 21 September 2000, 6 days after its target, after deathmatch was cut.","r":"Designers and artists spent most of 20 months on content, not engine work.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before production, freeze an engine feature list and schedule every engine change so it never stops designers and artists.","i":"A shipped engine lets content start on day one, and a fixed feature list keeps upgrades from moving under the content team. The benefit covers engine risk only: BioWare still cut deathmatch late because the multiplayer code was fragile.","a":"Does every planned engine change have a slot that does not block content?","q":"“In the end, the stable BioWare Infinity engine allowed for the majority of the 20-month development cycle for BALDUR’S GATE II to focus on content, rather than on engine or tool development.”","qa":"James Ohlen, co-lead designer, and Greg Zeschuk and Ray Muzyka, co-founders and joint CEOs of BioWare","st":"BioWare’s Baldur’s Gate II","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_March_2001.pdf#page=35","sd":"Mar 2001","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"baldursgateenhancededition","n":"Baldur's Gate: Enhanced Edition","dv":"Overhaul Games","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/228280/","ls":"Steam","cv":"baldursgateenhancededition.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Signed a remaster deal planning a quick renderer swap and an all-platform launch","s":"The source art had been lost and the old engine had severe performance and stability problems. Almost a year of renegotiation, hundreds of thousands of lines removed, a renderer rewrite, a ship date moved from September to November 2012.","w":"Beamdog signed a deal for Baldur's Gate: HD and planned to ninja in, swap the graphics system and ninja out, all platforms close together, 13 people. The source art had been lost on an unbacked-up departmental drive. After almost a year of negotiation the project was re-pitched as an Enhanced Edition. Engine threading ran 70 percent no-ops; a couple hundred thousand lines came out. Older Intel chips lacked OpenGL 2.0, so the renderer had to be rewritten. The contract moved ship from September to November; PC launched November 28, iPad December 8, 2012.","r":"Plans discarded after a year; budget overspent; ship date slipped 2 months.","re":"Re-pitched as an Enhanced Edition with new royalty terms and a 2-month contract extension.","co":"Development budget spent 'and then some'; almost a year of renegotiation; 2-month slip.","nf":false,"l":"Audit the legacy code and confirm the source assets exist before you sign a remaster schedule.","i":"The deal was priced on the assumption that old code and art were ready to reuse; neither was, and every surprise arrived after the contract fixed the date. A small team can still remaster a classic, if the schedule starts after the audit, not before.","a":"Have we opened the old code and found the source art before signing?","q":"“Our initial plans were to launch all platforms as close to each other as possible; in retrospect, this was very naive, given the size of our team and the volume of work required.”","qa":"Trent Oster, president of Beamdog / Overhaul Games","st":"Postmortem: Overhaul Games' Baldur's Gate: Enhanced Edition","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/postmortem-overhaul-games-i-baldur-s-gate-enhanced-edition-i-#:~:text=Our%20initial%20plans%20were%20to,volume%20of%20work%20required.","sd":"Apr 2013","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"bastion","n":"Bastion","dv":"Supergiant Games","y":"2011","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/107100/","ls":"Steam","cv":"bastion.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built a 4-week feel-first Bastion prototype with placeholder art before the artist joined","s":"The prototype's core, the world forming under the Kid, enemy behaviour and early music, survived to ship. Systems that were no fun were cut early. A 7-person team released Bastion 20 months later.","w":"In September 2009 designer Amir Rao and engineer Gavin Simon set out to build a playable prototype that would nail the gameplay feel, using Zelda rupees and monsters scanned from D&D books as art. After 4 weeks the world forming around the Kid, enemy behaviour and the first of Darren Korb's music were in place. The prototype told players they were done exploring at 90 percent: hunting the last tiles was no fun, so the full-exploration requirement went. Artist Jen Zee joined in June 2010. A 7-person team released Bastion on XBLA in July 2011, 20 months after the prototype.","r":"A 7-person team shipped Bastion 20 months after the first build.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"4 weeks of 2 people for the prototype; 7 people, 20 months for the game.","nf":false,"l":"Nail the feel in a 4-week prototype with borrowed art before you hire an artist.","i":"Feel was tested with the cheapest possible assets, so the no-fun systems died before any art was spent on them. This is a small-team advantage with a simple core loop; a systems-heavy game will not show its feel in 4 weeks.","a":"Can we feel our core loop in 4 weeks with placeholder art?","q":"“In spite of its simplicity and placeholder art, the prototype does showcase some of the ideas and mechanics we ended up refining and using in the final game.”","qa":"Greg Kasavin, creative director and writer at Supergiant Games","st":"A 4-week-old Bastion looked ugly but played beautifully","su":"https://destructoid.com/?p=127240#:~:text=nail%20the%20gameplay%20feel","sd":"Apr 2012","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4,"qu":"https://www.supergiantgames.com/blog/bastion-bulletin-april-2012/"}]},{"k":"batmanarkhamasylum","n":"Batman: Arkham Asylum","dv":"Rocksteady","y":"2009","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/35140/","ls":"Steam","cv":"batmanarkhamasylum.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Spent a year learning Unreal Engine 3 through prototypes for a next-gen game","s":"Eidos liked the prototype and asked Rocksteady to pitch Batman. The pitch won the project, and the team started Batman already knowing its tools.","w":"Rocksteady was founded in December 2004 and first made Urban Chaos for PS2 and Xbox, which took just over a year. The team then spent about a year building several prototypes in Unreal Engine 3 for a next-gen game on 360 and PS3, its first time with Unreal. Eidos, which had acquired the Batman license, liked how the prototype looked and played and asked what Rocksteady would do with Batman. The presentation won the project. Arkham Asylum took about two years, and by October 2009 it had shipped 2.5 million units.","r":"The prototype won Rocksteady the Batman project.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About a year of prototype work before Batman started.","nf":false,"l":"Between projects, build prototypes in the engine you expect to use next, and make them good enough to show a publisher.","i":"A polished prototype does two jobs: it proves the team to a publisher and teaches the tools before the clock starts. It paid off because Eidos held a license and came looking; without that buyer, the studio carries the year itself.","a":"Could our current prototype win us a project if a publisher saw it?","q":"“There was about a year when we were working on the different prototypes and learning the tools and learning to get the best out of them.”","qa":"Sefton Hill, director of Batman: Arkham Asylum at Rocksteady","st":"Rocksteady's Sefton Hill Unmasks Batman: Arkham Asylum","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/rocksteady-s-sefton-hill-unmasks-i-batman-arkham-asylum-i-#:~:text=There%20was%20about%20a%20year%20when,get%20the%20best%20out%20of%20them","sd":"Oct 2009","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"batmanarkhamknight","n":"Batman: Arkham Knight","dv":"Rocksteady","y":"2015","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/208650/","ls":"Steam","cv":"batmanarkhamknight.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Made the Batmobile a core part of a full-sized open Gotham","s":"The team had to design the street layout and the car's handling at the same time. Driving destruction through the city needed its own technology.","w":"Arkham Knight was the first game in the series with the Batmobile, set in a full-sized open Gotham built for PS4, Xbox One and PC. Gotham is not a grid: its streets curve and change height. So Rocksteady designed the city layout and the Batmobile's functionality at the same time, aiming for driving that felt fast, destructive and powerful. Producer Dax Ginn said a lot of thought and energy went into the street layout, and a lot of technology into the destruction. He described this at a press demo in March 2014. The game shipped in June 2015.","r":"City layout and vehicle handling became one coupled design job.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If a vehicle is central to your open world, prototype its handling and the street layout together before the city is built.","i":"A vehicle's speed, turning and destruction set the width, curves and spacing of every street. Lock the city first and the car has to squeeze into it. The coupling applies to any traversal tool the world is shaped around.","a":"Are we designing our streets and our vehicle handling together?","q":"“Balancing that, the design of the urban environment, with the handling of the Batmobile, like I said, those two things had to go hand in hand.”","qa":"Dax Ginn, producer at Rocksteady","st":"Batman: Arkham Knight — designing a proper Gotham City (and what’s with the Batmobile?) (interview)","su":"https://gamesbeat.com/batman-arkham-knight-designing-a-proper-gotham-city-and-whats-with-the-batmobile-interview/#:~:text=Balancing%20that%2C%20the%20design%20of,had%20to%20go%20hand%20in%20hand","sd":"Mar 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"battlebrothers","n":"Battle Brothers","dv":"Overhype Studios","y":"2017","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2015","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/365360/","ls":"Steam","cv":"battlebrothers.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Left translations and mod support out of the plan for a hobby project","s":"In 2019, after many updates and DLCs, the studio still could not add either feature properly.","w":"Overhype Studios started Battle Brothers some time before 2013 as an evening and weekend hobby: a medieval fantasy take on the original X-com. At first the founders never planned to sell it. Translations and mod support were not part of the plan. The 2015 Early Access launch let the core team of 3 go full time. In May 2019, with the Warriors of the North DLC due, managing director Jan Taaks said the team could not implement translations or mod support properly. He added that the next game would plan for both.","r":"Translations and proper mod support stayed out of reach after release.","re":"Overhype said it would plan for both features on its next game.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Decide in pre-production whether the game will ever need translations or mods, and plan the code for them from day 1.","i":"Translations need text kept apart from code, and mods need open data. Both are cheap to plan at the start and hard to retrofit into years of code. A game that will ship in one language with no mods can skip this.","a":"If this game sells, can we add translations and mods later?","q":"“Another thing that we did not plan for and are thus not able to implement properly now are translations and mod support.”","qa":"Jan Taaks, managing director of Overhype Studios","st":"10 Turns Interview with Battle Brothers developer","su":"https://turnbasedlovers.com/10-turns-interview/with-battle-brothers-developer/#:~:text=Another%20thing%20that%20we%20did%20not%20plan,translations%20and%20mod%20support","sd":"May 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"battleborn","n":"Battleborn","dv":"Gearbox","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/394230/","ls":"Steam","cv":"battleborn.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Built the game's variety on 25 distinct heroes instead of procedural guns","s":"Each hero took about as much effort as a Borderlands character. Gearbox revealed the roster over months, partly because the last few heroes might not make it in.","w":"Battleborn started at Gearbox at the end of Borderlands 2, and a rough competitive prototype was playable about 3 months later. With no procedural gun system to carry the gameplay, every hero needed its own movement, abilities and identity. The studio went from 4 playable characters in Borderlands to 25 heroes. In October 2015, Randy Varnell said each one cost about as much as a Borderlands character in quality, balance and tuning. In March 2016 he said the last few heroes had looked at risk of missing the game. Battleborn launched on May 3, 2016.","r":"Every hero needed Borderlands-level effort, and the last ones risked missing launch.","re":"Gearbox built play-data dashboards to keep tuning balance after launch.","co":"25 heroes, each about the effort of 1 Borderlands character.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock the roster size, cost one finished hero, including balance and tuning, then multiply by the count.","i":"Procedural systems make variety from rules, while a hand-built roster makes it from labor, and each new hero adds balance combinations. Varnell measured effort and tuning, not money, so this gives no budget per hero.","a":"How many weeks does one finished, balanced hero take us?","q":"“Every one of these characters requires about the same amount of effort as one of the characters in Borderlands.”","qa":"Randy Varnell, Gearbox Software","st":"Interview: Randy Varnell On Battleborn’s Characters, Cooperation & Replayability","su":"https://www.thesixthaxis.com/2016/03/23/interview-randy-varnell-on-battleborns-characters-cooperation-replayability/#:~:text=Every%20one%20of%20these%20characters,the%20characters%20in%20Borderlands","sd":"Mar 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"below","n":"Below","dv":"Capybara Games","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/250680/","ls":"Steam","cv":"below.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Unveiled Below with Microsoft in 2013 before sizing the game","s":"After the reveal the team found the game about 100 times bigger than expected. A 2015 date was announced and missed, then a summer 2016 window; Below shipped on December 14, 2018.","w":"Capybara unveiled Below in 2013 when Microsoft offered a first public showing. Then, in Piotrowski's words, they went home and realised the game was like 100 times bigger and more complex than they anticipated. In 2014 the team announced a 2015 release. 2015 came and went. In March 2016 Capy announced a summer 2016 launch, which also passed. Development went on hiatus for about 2 years while the studio made OK K.O.! Let's Play Heroes. In 2018 Piotrowski called it a classic case of announcing your game too early. Below launched on December 14, 2018.","r":"Release slipped from 2015 to December 2018, with a 2-year hiatus.","re":"The studio paused Below for about 2 years, made another game, then finished it.","co":"About 3 years late (2015 target, December 2018 release); 2-year hiatus.","nf":false,"l":"Size the game before you accept a platform holder's reveal slot.","i":"A reveal creates a public expectation before the team knows the cost of its own design; Piotrowski says the toothpaste cannot go back in the tube. Capy could afford to hold the game and ship late; a studio living on milestone payments cannot.","a":"Do we know how big this game is before we show it?","q":"“Then we went home and kind of realized the game was like 100 times bigger and more complex than we anticipated.”","qa":"Kris Piotrowski, creative director, Capybara Games","st":"'Below' is hiding more mysteries than you think","su":"https://www.engadget.com/2016-03-18-below-gdc-2016-interview.html#:~:text=Then%20we%20went%20home%20and,than%20we%20anticipated.","sd":"Mar 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"bioshock","n":"BioShock","dv":"2K Boston","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/7670/","ls":"Steam","cv":"bioshock.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Positioned BioShock as an RPG and shooter hybrid without a clear product message","s":"Systems were tuned for a slow, cerebral game. The switch to a focused shooter came near alpha and needed more change than the six-month finishing buffer could hold.","w":"BioShock was first scoped at about two years with 30 people, 25 in Boston and 5 in Australia. It was positioned as a hybrid RPG and shooter, so weapons, plasmids and AI were tuned for a slower, cerebral game. Milestones were met, so management saw no reason to step in. After the initial production phase in summer 2006, near alpha, the team repositioned it as a focused shooter and simplified many systems. The changes exceeded the six months of finishing in the schedule, and the team got an eleventh-hour extension. BioShock shipped on August 21, 2007, after 3 years.","r":"The late redesign overran the finishing buffer, and an extension was needed.","re":"The team simplified the deep systems and put polish into weapons, plasmids and UI.","co":"Eleventh-hour schedule extension.","nf":true,"l":"Lock the product message, including the genre, before production, so milestones measure the right game.","i":"Milestones were met, so nobody saw an emergency: the plan was on track for the wrong game. Milestone reviews catch this only if they test the target experience as well as task completion.","a":"Can everyone on the team name the genre we compete in?","q":"“Part of the reason for the late course change came from not having our internal product message clear from the beginning.”","qa":"Alyssa Finley, project lead on BioShock at 2K Boston","st":"Postmortem: 2K Boston/2K Australia's BioShock","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-2k-boston-2k-australia-s-bioshock#:~:text=Part%20of%20the%20reason%20for,clear%20from%20the%20beginning","sd":"Sep 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"bioshock2","n":"BioShock 2","dv":"2K Marin","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/8850/","ls":"Steam","cv":"bioshock2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Rewrote the BioShock 1 audio engine around FMOD Designer for the sequel","s":"Sound designers got fast iteration and dynamic design tools. The price was higher system memory use and a long list of engine integration work.","w":"The audio engine inherited from BioShock 1 was text-based, slow to iterate on and hard to integrate, with long baking times. 2K Marin's audio team decided it was time for a rewrite, with one rule: do what you want, but don't break it. Aiming for fast iteration, flexible sound design and modern features, the team chose the FMOD Designer tool. UnrealEd integration already existed, but reverb, memory management, localization and debugging tools still had to be built. BioShock 2 came out in February 2010. At GDC a month later, the team listed the gains and the memory cost.","r":"Audio iteration got faster, and memory use went up.","re":"The team built its own reverb, occlusion, virtualization and debug tools on top.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you adopt audio middleware, list what it will not do for you, such as reverb, memory and localization, and schedule that work.","i":"Middleware gave sound designers fast iteration out of the box, but every game-specific need still had to be built around it. The gain holds when the old pipeline is the bottleneck; on a memory-tight platform the extra memory can cost more than it saves.","a":"Which audio features will we still have to build after adopting middleware?","q":"“Bioshock 1 engine was showing its age”","qa":"Michael Kamper, Michael Csurics and Guy Somberg, audio lead, dialogue supervisor and audio programmer at 2K Marin","st":"A Bioshock 2 Post-Mortem","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/gdc10/slides/Kamper_Bioshock2AudioPostmortem_NEWfinal.pdf#:~:text=Bioshock%201%20engine%20was,showing%20its%20age","sd":"Mar 2010","sy":"Participant talk","w8":3}]},{"k":"bioshockinfinite","n":"BioShock Infinite","dv":"Irrational","y":"2013","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/8870/","ls":"Steam","cv":"bioshockinfinite.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Committed early to a vertical sky city with skyline rails, a system with little precedent","s":"Getting the rides to feel right took a very long time and many late nights. The feel came together only weeks before the debut demo, after a new team member joined.","w":"Irrational moved BioShock from under the sea into the sky and decided early to embrace the height, with vertigo and fear of falling. Rejecting flying and paragliding as too familiar, it built skylines: rails inspired by roller coasters, with some player control. Nobody on the team had built such a system, and Levine saw little precedent in the industry. After a very long time, a new hire finally got the visceral feel down weeks before the pre-E3 event. In October 2011 Levine called it one of the hardest things in the game. It shipped in March 2013.","r":"The skyline feel was ready for the demo only after a long struggle.","re":"Irrational added a person who got the visceral feel right.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Prototype the feel of any core movement system without precedent first, before you plan a public demo around it.","i":"A mechanic with no precedent has no reference for what good feels like, so the team finds it by trial and error. The risk is specific to feel: rails, cameras, controls. A feature with well-known models carries far less of it.","a":"Do we already know what good feels like for our core movement?","q":"“There were many long nights of steady soul searching about that system, and I think we really got it together in a way that we were very happy with not long before E3.”","qa":"Ken Levine, creative director at Irrational Games","st":"BioShock: Infinite – The Making of the E3 Demo","su":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/bioshock-infinite-the-making-of-the-e3-demo/1100-6339640/#:~:text=There%20were%20many%20long%20nights,not%20long%20before%20E3","sd":"Oct 2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"blackandwhite","n":"Black & White","dv":"Lionhead","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Story & world","t":"Left planning the story of a free-form god game until October 1999, budgeting two months","s":"The team found it lacked the skills, hired ex-Bullfrog scriptwriter James Leach freelance, and the story grew to more than 60,000 words with hundreds of quests and all the dialogue.","w":"Lionhead started Black & White in February 1998 with 9 people, planning an open god game. The free-form design needed an unfolding tale for structure, and in October 1999 the team began on the story, expecting no more than two months. Molyneux says they soon saw they lacked the skill set, so he brought in James Leach, Bullfrog's former in-house scriptwriter, freelance; Leach plotted the story and wrote hundreds of quests and all the dialogue, over 60,000 words. Alpha came on 26 December 2000 with over 3,000 bugs; the game shipped in 2001, after the announced end-2000 date.","r":"A two-month story task became a novel-length script needing an outside writer.","re":"Hired ex-Bullfrog scriptwriter James Leach freelance to plot the story and write all dialogue.","co":"Story planned at 2 months grew to over 60,000 words by a freelance writer.","nf":false,"l":"Plan story and writing as a work package at concept stage, with a writer named, not as a two-month job near the end.","i":"An open-ended game has no level order to carry the player, so story is structure, not decoration, and needs a writer's craft the core team lacked. Molyneux credits the hire with a story that drew players through; how long it really took is not stated.","a":"Who writes our story, and when does that work start?","q":"“We thought it would take no more than two months, but after a while we realized that we didn't have the skill set needed to take care of this vital aspect of the game.”","qa":"Peter Molyneux, founder of Lionhead Studios","st":"Postmortem: Lionhead Studios' Black & White","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-lionhead-studios-i-black-white-i-#:~:text=We%20thought%20it%20would%20take,aspect%20of%20the%20game","sd":"Jun 2001","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Designed for PCs stronger than most players owned when development began","s":"The team wrote much of its software from scratch and set a 64MB RAM minimum, double the common 32MB. By release, players' PCs had caught up.","w":"Lionhead's core team of about 6 began at Peter Molyneux's house by building a library of tools, and moved into offices with 9 people in February 1998. The game they planned could not have run on most PCs of the day, which had 32MB of RAM. The team needed a great deal of custom-written software and counted on the minimum PC spec rising. At E3 1998 Molyneux showed a wireframe test bed and mocked-up screenshots that the press doubted. The game shipped in 2001 needing 64MB, and that June Molyneux wrote that the hardware had caught up.","r":"Shipped needing 64MB of RAM, by then a common amount.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Much custom-written software; a 64MB RAM minimum when most PCs had 32MB.","nf":false,"l":"When you design for next year's hardware, write down the spec you are betting on and check it against hardware surveys at each milestone.","i":"Lionhead's bet worked because PC memory grew fast during a 3-year build. The same bet on a short schedule, or on hardware that stops improving, leaves a game most players cannot run.","a":"Which hardware spec are we betting players will own at launch?","q":"“So, if you like, we aimed beyond the horizon, and the world rotated and caught up with us so we still hit our target.”","qa":"Peter Molyneux, founder of Lionhead Studios","st":"Postmortem: Lionhead Studios' Black & White","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-lionhead-studios-i-black-white-i-#:~:text=So%2C%20if%20you%20like%2C%20we%20aimed,so%20we%20still%20hit%20our%20target","sd":"Jun 2001","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Planned to let players raise any living thing, humans included, as their Creature","s":"An artist spent a long time sketching human Creatures before the team saw that raising a human felt wrong. The idea was dropped and the sketches went unused.","w":"Peter Molyneux's first idea let the player pick any creature from the landscape, from an ant to a human from a tribe, and raise it. Artist Christian Bravery spent a long time on concept sketches of Creatures at each stage of growth, humans included. The team then saw that players would expect a human to learn faster than a turtle. Training also meant slapping the Creature, and slapping a child or a woman was out. Humans were dropped, and so were nonmammals. In his June 2001 postmortem Molyneux wrote that the human concept work was all for nothing.","r":"An artist's long run of human Creature sketches went unused.","re":"The team dropped human and nonmammal Creatures from the design.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before artists draw a new character type, walk through the core verbs on it, such as training and punishment, and check they feel right.","i":"The human Creature failed on feel, not on technology: players bring expectations to a human, and the training slap turned ugly. A paper walkthrough catches that kind of problem cheaply. It misses problems that show only in a running build.","a":"Have we played the core verbs on this character before drawing it?","q":"“Christian's work in visualizing humans as player Creatures was all for nothing in the end, and we dropped the idea.”","qa":"Peter Molyneux, founder of Lionhead Studios","st":"Postmortem: Lionhead Studios' Black & White","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-lionhead-studios-i-black-white-i-#:~:text=Christian%27s%20work%20in%20visualizing%20humans,and%20we%20dropped%20the%20idea","sd":"Jun 2001","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"blackmesa","n":"Black Mesa","dv":"Crowbar Collective","y":"2020","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2015","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/362890/","ls":"Steam","cv":"blackmesa.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Shipped the Earthbound chapters as a free mod and redesigned Xen from scratch","s":"Xen became the biggest piece of new work in the remake: 21 maps with up to 3 times the assets of the Earth levels. The 2012 mod grew into a commercial game that shipped in March 2020.","w":"Two fan groups began remaking Half-Life in the Source engine in 2004 and merged in 2005. The goal grew from upgrading textures to re-envisioning the game, with about 25 people working consistently, most with day jobs. Around 2010 or 2011 the team paused Xen and shipped the Earthbound chapters as a free mod in 2012. Valve proposed a commercial licence in 2013. Lead designer Chris Horn took the Xen brief and redesigned it from scratch, with plans for every chapter. Early Access followed in 2015. Black Mesa 1.0, with Xen, shipped on 6 March 2020, 16 years after the start.","r":"Xen alone ran from the 2013 plans to the March 2020 release.","re":"Used Valve's licence and Early Access income to hire people.","co":"16 years of calendar time; about 25 consistent part-time contributors.","nf":false,"l":"Before you redesign a chapter from scratch, write its full design document and plan its asset load separately from the remake.","i":"Redesigned content has none of the remake's shortcuts: no original layouts to copy and up to 3 times the assets per map. Splitting the project let the Earth chapters ship early; it did not make the new part any shorter.","a":"Which parts of our remake are really new content, and have we costed them?","q":"“When I took the original brief for ‘Xen,’ I made the decision to redesign it from scratch and create a unique version of the borderworld.”","qa":"Chris Horn, lead designer of Black Mesa at Crowbar Collective","st":"Q&A: Re-imagining 1998's Half-Life for a 2020 audience in Black Mesa","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/q-a-re-imagining-1998-s-i-half-life-i-for-a-2020-audience-in-i-black-mesa-i-#:~:text=I%20made%20the%20decision%20to%20redesign,version%20of%20the%20borderworld","sd":"May 2020","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"blackmythwukong","n":"Black Myth: Wukong","dv":"Game Science","y":"2024","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2358720/","ls":"Steam","cv":"blackmythwukong.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Staffing","t":"Published a 13-minute gameplay demo years before launch to recruit developers","s":"Several large Chinese outsourcers had turned the work down. The demo brought in more than 10,000 job applications, far more than the small studio could screen.","w":"In February 2018 Feng Ji launched the project with a team of 7 or 8 on Unreal Engine 4, new to them. They built the hardest level, Huaguo Shan, first, then stopped after art director Yang Qi played God of War and saw the gap. Yang planned in-house design with outsourced assets, but several large Chinese outsourcers declined the job. A plan to finish one monster every two weeks also proved too optimistic. The partners chose Black Wind Mountain as the first public showing, and the August 2020 demo drew over 10,000 applications. The game shipped in August 2024.","r":"Open hiring replaced outsourcing as the way to grow the team.","re":"The studio kept polishing Black Wind Mountain as the template level for later ones.","co":"Built from the real level, with no content made only for the demo.","nf":true,"l":"If outsourcers cannot take your content, show a slice built from your real level to recruit the people you need.","i":"A small studio runs out of personal contacts, and a playable level shows candidates the quality bar better than a job ad. It works only when the slice is real and strong; a weak demo tells the same candidates to stay away.","a":"Would our current slice make a senior developer want to join?","q":"“但与真正想要的团队体量相比，我们这种小公司保密项目的招人效率还是太低。”","qa":"Feng Ji, producer at Game Science","st":"IGN 独家 ｜《黑神话：悟空》爆红之后，我走进了游戏科学","su":"https://www.ign.com.cn/blackmythwukong/29838/interview/ign-du-jia-hei-shen-hua-wu-kong-bao-hong-zhi-hou-wo-zou-jin-liao-you-xi-ke-xue#:~:text=%E4%BD%86%E4%B8%8E%E7%9C%9F%E6%AD%A3%E6%83%B3%E8%A6%81%E7%9A%84%E5%9B%A2%E9%98%9F%E4%BD%93%E9%87%8F%E7%9B%B8%E6%AF%94,%E6%8B%9B%E4%BA%BA%E6%95%88%E7%8E%87%E8%BF%98%E6%98%AF%E5%A4%AA%E4%BD%8E","sd":"Sep 2020","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Staffing","t":"Kept the 10 to 15 hire plan after the trailer drew 10,000 applications","s":"Feng Ji refused a fast jump to a 100-person star team to protect shared values and team chemistry. The game shipped in 2024 with about 140 names in its credits, by SCMP's count.","w":"Game Science started Black Myth: Wukong in 2018 and hired through the founders' own networks. In August 2020 it released a 13-minute gameplay trailer, and the recruiting inbox filled with more than 10,000 applications. Publishers, investors and media also called, and Feng Ji turned most of them down. He kept the plan to hire 10 to 15 people and set aside at least 3 months to read the applications. The game launched in August 2024, and the South China Morning Post counted about 140 people in the credits.","r":"The post-trailer hiring round stayed capped at 10 to 15 people.","re":"Feng Ji planned no new public showing for at least a year.","co":"At least 3 months to screen over 10,000 applications.","nf":false,"l":"When hype makes hiring easy, keep the planned hiring number and add people only as fast as the team can absorb them.","i":"People who have worked together for years share taste and need fewer words to agree, which Feng Ji saw as lower communication cost. The cap fits a team whose core roles are filled; this team still lacked camera and AAA process skills.","a":"How many new hires can we absorb this quarter without diluting our standards?","q":"“就像 NBA 全明星队只适合打表演赛，但真正的强队不可能全都是明星，强队不是完全靠钱堆起来的，而是长期磨合锻炼出来的。”","qa":"Feng Ji, producer at Game Science","st":"IGN 独家 ｜《黑神话：悟空》爆红之后，我走进了游戏科学","su":"https://www.ign.com.cn/blackmythwukong/29838/interview/ign-du-jia-hei-shen-hua-wu-kong-bao-hong-zhi-hou-wo-zou-jin-liao-you-xi-ke-xue#:~:text=%E5%B0%B1%E5%83%8F%20NBA%20%E5%85%A8%E6%98%8E%E6%98%9F%E9%98%9F%E5%8F%AA%E9%80%82%E5%90%88%E6%89%93%E8%A1%A8%E6%BC%94%E8%B5%9B,%E8%80%8C%E6%98%AF%E9%95%BF%E6%9C%9F%E7%A3%A8%E5%90%88%E9%94%BB%E7%82%BC%E5%87%BA%E6%9D%A5%E7%9A%84","sd":"Sep 2020","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"bloodstainedritualofthenight","n":"Bloodstained: Ritual of the Night","dv":"ArtPlay","y":"2019","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/692850/","ls":"Steam","cv":"bloodstainedritualofthenight.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Promised Kickstarter backers a March 2017 release built with an outside development partner","s":"Inti Creates left main development in 2016; IGA said the game would miss his quality bar at that rate, added a new team and moved the date to 2018. Beta feedback pushed it to 2019; the Vita version was cancelled.","w":"The May 2015 Kickstarter set a March 2017 release. Inti Creates left main development around E3 2016; that September Igarashi told backers the game might not meet his quality standards at that rate, added a new team and moved the release to the first half of 2018. A 2017 statement named ArtPlay as main developer, with DICO filling in manpower ArtPlay lacked. After the June 2018 backer demo the date moved to 2019, and the Vita version was cancelled as Sony wound the console down. WayForward then joined to finish the game, which shipped on 18 June 2019.","r":"Shipped 18 June 2019, 27 months after the promised date; Vita version cancelled.","re":"Added DICO in 2016 and later WayForward to finish the game.","co":"27 months late; 2 added development partners; Vita version cancelled with refunds.","nf":false,"l":"Before promising backers a date, make sure the design owner can finish the game without its original production partner.","i":"A design-owner studio without production capacity carries its partner's schedule, not its own. When Inti Creates stepped out, Igarashi added a new team and reset the date; why the partner left was never stated. In-house production makes a Kickstarter date a smaller bet.","a":"Can we finish this game if our production partner walks away?","q":"“I've realized that at this rate the game may not end up meeting my quality standards.”","qa":"Koji Igarashi, producer and founder of ArtPlay","st":"Bloodstained: Ritual of the Night Delayed","su":"https://gamespot.com/articles/bloodstained-ritual-of-the-night-delayed/1100-6443438/#:~:text=realized%20that%20at%20this%20rate,my%20quality%20standards","sd":"Sep 2016","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Set a very low field of view and lit the levels mostly automatically","s":"Lights did not reach where they should. After early feedback on the visuals, the team relit the game by hand, and that became most of the graphics overhaul.","w":"Koji Igarashi, a former Castlevania producer, founded Artplay and funded Bloodstained on Kickstarter. To get a 2D look on a 3D plane, the team set the field of view very low from the beginning. There was a lot of content to build, so the lighting was mostly automated. With the low field of view, lights did not reach places they should, and gameplay came first. After early feedback on the visuals, the studio revamped the look, and most of that work was placing lights by hand. The game came out in June 2019, and Igarashi described the fix that October.","r":"The lighting was reworked by hand in a graphics overhaul.","re":"Artplay placed lights by hand and got extra help with animations, models and environments.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Light a finished test room at your real camera settings before you pick automated lighting for the whole game.","i":"A very low field of view gives a 3D scene its 2D look, and it also changes what the lights reach. Automated lighting saved time on a lot of content but missed those spots. The risk grows with any unusual camera.","a":"Have we lit a finished room at our real camera settings?","q":"“Eventually, we strategically placed the lighting by hand after receiving feedback.”","qa":"Koji Igarashi, founder of Artplay","st":"Famed Castlevania producer creates new Igavania hit Bloodstained as an indie developer","su":"https://www.unrealengine.com/developer-interviews/famed-castlevania-producer-creates-new-igavania-hit-bloodstained-as-an-indie-developer#:~:text=Eventually%2C%20we%20strategically%20placed%20the%20lighting,by%20hand%20after%20receiving%20feedback","sd":"Oct 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"boombeach","n":"Boom Beach","dv":"Supercell","y":"2014","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/boom-beach-war-strategy-game/id672150402?uo=4","ls":"App Store","cv":"boombeach.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Trusted the Boom Beach team over 9 of 10 game leads who wanted a kill","s":"The team kept developing and released Boom Beach. Paananen later wrote that the team was right and everyone else was wrong.","w":"About 5 months into development, Supercell held a company playable of Boom Beach. Many founders and game leads did not believe in it, and 9 of 10 people in a key game lead meeting thought it should be killed. Ilkka Paananen judged that overruling the team would end Supercell's culture of independent teams. Leadership let the team decide, and the team kept going and released the game. In May 2020, Paananen wrote that the call would have been right even if the team had been wrong.","r":"Boom Beach survived the review and went on to release.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Decide before the review who owns the kill call, and keep to it when the room disagrees.","i":"If a review can overrule the team whenever it disagrees, team ownership is only a word, so leadership kept the call with the team. The rule protects the culture, not any one game: Paananen backed it even for a wrong call.","a":"Who makes the final kill call if the review says kill?","q":"“We figured that because culture is more important than short term business outcomes, we should trust the team and let them do what they believe.”","qa":"Ilkka Paananen, CEO of Supercell","st":"10 Learning from 10 Years","su":"https://supercell.com/en/news/10-learnings-10-years/7436/#:~:text=We%20figured%20that%20because%20culture,let%20them%20do%20what%20they%20believe","sd":"May 2020","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"borderlands","n":"Borderlands","dv":"Gearbox Software","y":"2009","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/729040/","ls":"Steam","cv":"borderlands.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Art & style","t":"Picked a retro, realism-grounded art style by studio poll in mid-2005","s":"By late 2008 the humor and fantastical skills had outgrown the art. About a year before release, Gearbox switched to a graphic-novel style and reorganized the art team.","w":"In mid-2005 Gearbox ran a studio-wide ideation phase with reference boards and a forum poll, and picked a retro maschinen krieger look. The first prototype had all core features in 2006. Artists kept grounding the style in realism while the design grew funnier and more fantastical. In late 2008 the team saw that art and design had diverged. Creative czar Brian Martel explored a graphic-novel style, the Visual Design Team kit-bashed a Fyrestone test, and CEO Randy Pitchford pitched it to 2K, who backed it. The art director's job was split into six roles. Borderlands shipped in October 2009.","r":"The art style was redone and the art department reorganized late in production.","re":"Gearbox built a kit-bashed Fyrestone test in the new style and pitched it to 2K.","co":"Art restyle and art team reorganization in the final year.","nf":true,"l":"At each pre-production milestone, put the art style next to the latest gameplay and check that both show the same tone.","i":"Art chosen at kickoff fixes a tone before the systems find theirs, and humor and powers then pull away from it. The late switch was affordable partly because the stylized look made assets easier to finish; a late move toward realism would cost more.","a":"Does our art style match the tone of the latest playable build?","q":"“This led to a pivotal realization in late 2008 that the art style and game design had diverged; the fun we were finding as the attitude, humor, and fantastical character skill system evolved had moved too far away from what was represented in the art style to continue to fit well together.”","qa":"Aaron Thibault, vice president of product development at Gearbox Software","st":"Postmortem: Gearbox's Borderlands","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_February_2010.pdf#:~:text=This%20led%20to%20a%20pivotal,continue%20to%20fit%20well%20together","sd":"Feb 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Set milestones around internal and external demos instead of a finished core feature set","s":"Many systems were built in parallel to show intent, but few were polished. The mission system was never refactored for the missions that proved fun, and only one person understood it.","w":"Gearbox began Borderlands in mid-2005, and the core features ran in a first prototype in 2006. From then until the end of 2008, milestones measured progress toward internal and external demos, built by a small core team in about 6-month cycles. Many systems grew in parallel, and few were polished. Missions were sized for short demo play, and the mission system was never refactored for the missions that later proved fun. A final mission framework came only at the start of 2009. The game shipped in October 2009, and Aaron Thibault described the problem in a February 2010 postmortem.","r":"Many systems stayed unpolished, and missions depended on one expert.","re":"The team locked a mission framework in early 2009 and rebuilt the missions.","co":"Late mission rebuild; one designer carried the mission system.","nf":true,"l":"Make each pre-production milestone a finished, polished core loop, not a demo of many half-built systems.","i":"A demo milestone rewards showing many systems at once, so each gets a little attention and none gets finished. Needs like long-form missions appear only when someone plays more than a demo. Partner demos still have a place; they cannot replace finished systems.","a":"Is our next milestone a polished core loop or a demo?","q":"“It was like spinning plates, giving each one a little attention before moving on to the next one.”","qa":"Aaron Thibault, vice president of product development at Gearbox Software","st":"Postmortem: Gearbox's Borderlands","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_February_2010.pdf#:~:text=It%20was%20like%20spinning%20plates%2C,on%20to%20the%20next%20one","sd":"Feb 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"borderlands2","n":"Borderlands 2","dv":"Gearbox","y":"2012","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/49520/","ls":"Steam","cv":"borderlands2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Used the Matinee cinematic tool to mock up new features in-engine","s":"The team could judge proposed features inside the game before building them. Technical director Steve Jones said the mock-ups came at minimal development cost.","w":"Gearbox Software built Borderlands 2 on Unreal Engine 3. Very early in development, the team picked new features it wanted to add to the sequel. Instead of programming each one first, it used Matinee, the engine's cinematic tool, to build quick in-game proofs of concept. Technical director Steve Jones said this let the team judge ideas fast with minimal development cost. He described the method in a June 2012 post on the Unreal Engine blog. The game came out on September 17, 2012.","r":"Ideas were judged in-game before anyone paid to build them.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you assign programmers to a new feature, mock it up with the tools you already have and judge it in-game.","i":"A cinematic tool can fake how a feature looks in the game without the code behind it, so weak ideas die cheaply. It cannot test feel, balance or anything that depends on real player input.","a":"Can we fake this feature in-engine before we build it?","q":"“Very early on in development, we were able to identify new features we wanted to bring to the game, and Matinee was used to rapidly develop in-game proof of concepts that enabled the team to quickly evaluate ideas with minimal development cost.”","qa":"Steve Jones, technical director of Borderlands 2 at Gearbox Software","st":"Borderlands 2 Pushes New Boundaries with Unreal Engine 3 Technology","su":"https://www.unrealengine.com/blog/borderlands-2?lang=en-US#:~:text=Very%20early%20on%20in%20development,with%20minimal%20development%20cost","sd":"Jun 2012","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"braid","n":"Braid","dv":"Number None","y":"2008","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2009","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/26800/","ls":"Steam","cv":"braid.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built Braid on code its creator had written and owned for years","s":"The solo developer started on top of code he already had. He later said that without it, Braid would either not have been finished or would have been worse.","w":"Jonathan Blow started Braid alone, programming a prototype with very low production values so he could focus on gameplay. He built it on code he had written over the years and still had the rights to use anywhere. He planned 1 year of work, and it took more than 3: he would not release the game until the obvious problems were fixed. In February 2009 he said that without that code, he would either not have finished Braid or its quality would have been lower. He warned indies off XNA, which ties code to Microsoft's platforms.","r":"One developer finished Braid on top of code he already owned.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you pick an engine or framework, check that you will own your code and can carry it to other platforms and future games.","i":"Code you own keeps paying off, because each project starts further along. Blow's point is about rights and portability, not reuse alone. He grants that a framework tied to one platform is fine for small projects you plan to throw away.","a":"Can we reuse this game's code on other platforms and later games?","q":"“Without that code, Braid would have been much harder to make and either I would not have finished it, or the quality would have been lower.”","qa":"Jonathan Blow, creator of Braid","st":"Jonathan Blow talks Braid","su":"https://www.gamereactor.eu/jonathan-blow-talks-braid/#:~:text=Without%20that%20code%2C%20Braid%20would%20have,the%20quality%20would%20have%20been%20lower","sd":"Feb 2009","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"brawlstars","n":"Brawl Stars","dv":"Supercell","y":"2018","sc":"AAA","se":"Soft launch 2017","lk":"https://supercell.com/en/games/brawlstars/","ls":"Website","cv":"brawlstars.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Kept Brawl Stars in soft launch to rebuild controls and progression until metrics moved","s":"The beta ran 545 days, the longest in Supercell's history. The core changes barely moved retention. After social features and an Android launch in mid 2018 the team took the chance and went global.","w":"Supercell prototyped the shooter from 2015 and soft launched Brawl Stars on 15 June 2017 in Canada on iOS only. Tap and joystick controls tied on retention; joystick won on switching data, and about 9 months later the game turned to landscape. The progression system was replaced 6 months in and again later, yet metrics did not improve. By May 2018 the team was frustrated and switched to new characters, friends lists and economy fixes, then launched Android and Asia in June 2018 as a last shot. The beta ended after 545 days with the global launch in December 2018.","r":"The longest beta in Supercell's history: 545 days before global launch.","re":"Shifted to social features and Android, then went global in December 2018.","co":"545 days of soft launch; 3 control schemes and 3 progression systems built.","nf":false,"l":"Before soft launch, build the feature you know is most fun (here, playing with friends) so core tests are not masked.","i":"Retention is a broad metric, and the missing social layer swamped every control and progression change, so a year of core iteration read as no signal. Summala thinks social gameplay first would have ended the beta much sooner.","a":"Is our most fun feature in the soft launch build before we test the core?","q":"“It turned out to be the longest beta in Supercell history.”","qa":"Antti Summala, designer at Supercell","st":"Designing Two Tasty Cores Three Times Over: The Case of Brawl Stars","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VSVAmf5LCuo&t=507s","sd":"Jul 2019","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Switched the soft-launched portrait shooter to landscape with dual-stick controls","s":"The game got good at that point, by game lead Frank Keienburg's account. The change unsettled the core community, and long-range brawlers needed a balance patch.","w":"Supercell soft-launched Brawl Stars in June 2017 as a portrait game with tap-to-move controls. One developer, a former Clash of Clans game lead, spent a weekend converting it to landscape with dual sticks for internal playtests. The team adopted it, and the landscape update reached players in March 2018. Supercell said the portrait controls had held back the game's potential. About a week later it patched long-range brawlers such as Brock and Piper. The game launched globally on 12 December 2018, after 544 days in soft launch.","r":"Core players were unsettled, and long-range brawlers needed rebalancing.","re":"Supercell patched long-range brawlers within a week and kept the landscape controls.","co":"A weekend prototype, a community backlash and a rebalance of long-range brawlers.","nf":false,"l":"Before soft launch, prototype the rival screen orientation and control scheme and playtest both on target phones.","i":"Screen orientation sets camera framing and map width, so it changes every character's range and the whole UI. The prototype took one weekend; making the change in front of live players cost a backlash and a balance patch.","a":"Have we playtested the other orientation and control scheme on real phones?","q":"“We changed the game from portrait mode to landscape, which unsettled a big chunk of our core community.”","qa":"Frank Keienburg, game lead of Brawl Stars at Supercell","st":"Supercell on Brawl Stars, a mobile action game tailor-made for tournaments","su":"https://www.redbull.com/us-en/brawl-stars-supercell-interview-meo#:~:text=We%20changed%20the%20game%20from,of%20our%20core%20community","sd":"Jan 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"brokenage","n":"Broken Age","dv":"Double Fine Productions","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/232790/","ls":"Steam","cv":"brokenage.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Designed a full-size adventure after the Kickstarter raised $3.3 million instead of $400,000","s":"The design outgrew even that money. By July 2013 the full game was projected for 2015, so Double Fine split it in two and sold the first half early.","w":"Double Fine asked Kickstarter for $400,000 in 2012 and raised $3.3 million. Tim Schafer made a conscious choice to build a game on par with Psychonauts and Grim Fandango, with about $2 million for the game itself. The finish date moved from April 2013 to September. In July 2013 the final schedule put Act 1 in July 2014 and the full game in 2015. Fitting that into the money would have meant a 75% cut. Double Fine trimmed Act 1 and released it on Steam in January 2014. Act 2 shipped on 28 April 2015.","r":"The full game shipped 2 years after the first April 2013 target.","re":"Double Fine split the game and sold Act 1 to fund Act 2.","co":"About 2 years of slip; overages paid from Double Fine's own game sales.","nf":false,"l":"Every time funding changes, resize the design to the money you actually have before production starts.","i":"A windfall removes the limit that keeps scope honest, and the lead's idea of a proper game fills the gap. Extra money helps only when the design is resized to it.","a":"Has anyone priced the current design against the money we actually have?","q":"“It’s just taking a while because I designed too much game, as I pretty much always do.”","qa":"Tim Schafer, CEO of Double Fine Productions","st":"Somehow, Tim Schafer’s Adventure Game Needs More Money","su":"https://kotaku.com/somehow-tim-schafers-adventure-kickstarter-needs-more-652014092#:~:text=It%E2%80%99s%20just%20taking%20a%20while,I%20pretty%20much%20always%20do","sd":"Jul 2013","sy":"Participant account","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Built the first schedule top-down from headcount and money, then fit the game in","s":"Progress on the first area, Cloud Colony, showed the game would not fit by April. A new bottom-up plan ran to September with 4 to 5 more people and $1.1 million more.","w":"After the 2012 Kickstarter, producer Greg Rice built the schedule by staffing up as far as the money allowed, then fitting a game into that time. His early dates gave about 5 months of production. As work went on, the project was clearly not tracking to anything that fit. With the game posted for April, Rice started a new schedule from Cloud Colony's real pace and the screens still left. The new budget ran to September, added 4 to 5 people and about $1.1 million, and still assumed scene output would almost double. Act 1 came out in January 2014.","r":"The April finish became a September plan needing more people and money.","re":"Rice rebuilt the schedule from scratch using Cloud Colony's actual pace.","co":"4 to 5 more people and about $1.1 million more.","nf":false,"l":"Finish one representative area first, measure its real pace, and build the schedule bottom-up from that rate.","i":"A top-down plan fixes headcount and dates first, so the game has to shrink to fit or the plan breaks. Real pace exists only after one area is done; until then any date is a guess.","a":"Is our schedule built from a measured pace or from the budget?","q":"“And like as we were progressing like it was obvious that we weren't tracking to anything that was going to fit in that time.”","qa":"Greg Rice, producer at Double Fine Productions","st":"Double Fine Adventure! EP07: \"We'll Handle It\"","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NLYV3sPf5J8&t=1455s","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant documentary","w8":4}]},{"k":"brokenswordtheshadowofthetemplars","n":"Broken Sword: The Shadow of the Templars","dv":"Revolution Software","y":"1996","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2024","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/3361990/","ls":"Steam","cv":"brokenswordtheshadowofthetemplars.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Moved from pixel art to drawn animation with artists hired from an animation college","s":"The concept was settled over drinks with the publisher in 1993. Late in development the game ran way over budget and schedule, with whispers of cancellation, before selling over 1 million copies.","w":"In 1993, as Beneath a Steel Sky was finishing, Charles Cecil settled the concept with Virgin's Sean Brennan: Knights Templar, after Brennan said Egyptian games don't sell. Cecil wanted drawn art instead of pixel art and recruited artists from an animation college, then added two leads and a real threat of death. Towards the end, as Game Developer reported from his GDC Europe 2014 talk, the game ran way over budget and schedule amid cancellation whispers, and a near-final build sent to PC Zone was panned for bugs. It shipped in 1996 to 90 percent reviews and 1 million sales.","r":"Ran way over budget and schedule in the final months.","re":"The team hated the game by the end but finished it on pride.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When you raise the art bar from pixel art to drawn animation, re-cost every scene before the publisher signs.","i":"Drawn animation multiplies the cost of every background and character move, and a new art team hired from college has no pipeline yet. The talk, as reported, names the overrun but does not blame the art choice alone; two leads and death also added work.","a":"Have we re-costed the content plan since we raised the art bar?","q":"“It was very important to move from pixel art to drawn art.”","qa":"Charles Cecil, writer and director of Broken Sword, Revolution Software","st":"A classic postmortem for the original Broken Sword","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/a-classic-postmortem-for-the-original-i-broken-sword-i-#:~:text=It%20was%20very%20important,to%20drawn%20art.","sd":"Aug 2014","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"brothersataleoftwosons","n":"Brothers: A Tale of Two Sons","dv":"Starbreeze Studios","y":"2013","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/225080/","ls":"Steam","cv":"brothersataleoftwosons.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Fixed one-player two-stick control as the first design decision and refused co-op","s":"Every puzzle, the camera and the animation load were derived from that one mechanic. Puzzles had to stay soft, one person worked on the camera the whole time, and the publishers' push for co-op was turned down.","w":"Film director Josef Fares got a summer prototype offer from a friend's school about 3 years before release and came up with the two-stick control and the ending first. He took it to Starbreeze's CEO and worked full time for 2 years with a team half of whom were on their first game. Publishers, Microsoft among them, pushed for co-op; he refused and they backed off. Puzzles stayed soft to keep the pace, one person worked on the camera all the time, and many unique mechanics meant more animation and more breakage. The game shipped on 7 Aug 2013.","r":"All content rules came from one mechanic proven in a summer prototype.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"2 years full time; one person on the camera for the whole project.","nf":false,"l":"Lock the one mechanic that defines all content first, prototype it, and derive puzzle difficulty, camera and animation load from it.","i":"When one control scheme is the whole idea, every system downstream inherits its constraints, so settling it first removes rework later. It worked because Fares refused to bend it for co-op; the same discipline costs more when the mechanic turns out wrong.","a":"Which single mechanic will every puzzle and camera decision depend on?","q":"“There were many voices in and outside that were saying, “You should do a co-op game,” but I would rather cut my hands than do that.”","qa":"Josef Fares, director of Brothers: A Tale of Two Sons, Starbreeze Studios","st":"Afterwords – Brothers: A Tale Of Two Sons","su":"https://gameinformer.com/b/features/archive/2013/09/27/afterwords-brothers-a-tale-of-two-sons#:~:text=There%20were%20many%20voices%20in,my%20hands%20than%20do%20that","sd":"Sep 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"brtallegend","n":"Brütal Legend","dv":"Double Fine Productions","y":"2009","sc":"AAA","se":"Port 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/225260/","ls":"Steam","cv":"brtallegend.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Expanded the single-player campaign well beyond the initial design at Vivendi's request","s":"Content changes put the team way behind schedule by June 2007. Two extensions added 17 months to a May 2008 target; the $24 million game shipped in October 2009 after a publisher merger and a lawsuit.","w":"Double Fine started with the multiplayer mode and took 16 months to a playable skirmish in October 2006. At Vivendi's request the team then turned to the single-player campaign and expanded its scope well beyond the initial design. In June 2007 the team delivered the first major part of the campaign and admitted the content changes put it way behind schedule. The schedule was extended by 10 months, then 7 more. Vivendi merged with Activision, payments stopped, EA picked up the game, and Activision sued in June 2009. Brütal Legend shipped on October 13, 2009 with a $24 million budget.","r":"17 months of extensions on a May 2008 target and crunchy milestones.","re":"Double Fine moved art production to waterfall and shipped with EA after the lawsuit settled.","co":"$24 million budget; 17 months late; 74 in-house developers plus 9 contractors.","nf":false,"l":"Scope the campaign to what the team has measured it can build, not to the publisher's request.","i":"Esmurdoc says their Scrum kept a pre-production mindset far into production: systems went to 80 percent to prove ideas, and the last 20 percent cost another 80 percent of the effort. Expanding content on unfinished systems blew the schedule; Scrum itself still worked for them.","a":"Which systems are at 80 percent while we add more content?","q":"“Our implementation of Scrum encouraged a pre-production mindset far too long into production.”","qa":"Caroline Esmurdoc, executive producer and COO, Double Fine Productions","st":"Postmortem: Double Fine's Brütal Legend","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-double-fine-s-i-brutal-legend-i-#:~:text=Our%20implementation%20of%20Scrum%20encouraged,long%20into%20production.","sd":"Dec 2009, reprinted Mar 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Ran Scrum sprints that kept the team in a pre-production mindset into production","s":"Systems stopped at proof-of-idea quality, and finishing them later brought unexpectedly crunchy milestones for the hardest-hit team members.","w":"Double Fine built Brütal Legend over 4.5 years on a $24 million budget, first for Vivendi with a May 2008 target. The team used Scrum, and its version of Scrum kept a pre-production mindset far into production. Many systems went to 80 percent, enough to prove an idea, with the rest left for a future deliverable. The remaining 20 percent took another 80 percent of the effort. The game shipped through EA on 13 October 2009. In her postmortem that December, Caroline Esmurdoc also wrote that Scrum did not push for systems that scaled well.","r":"Unfinished systems turned later milestones into crunch for the hardest-hit staff.","re":"The team absorbed the extra finishing work in crunchy milestones.","co":"The last 20 percent of those systems took another 80 percent of effort.","nf":false,"l":"Set a clear switch from pre-production to production, and from then on plan each system to finished quality.","i":"Sprints reward showing an idea working, so systems stop at proof quality and the finishing work piles up. The postmortem links the 17 months of schedule revisions to content changes and scope growth; it does not blame Scrum for them.","a":"Which of our systems are still at proof-of-idea quality?","q":"“Our implementation of Scrum encouraged a pre-production mindset far too long into production.”","qa":"Caroline Esmurdoc, executive producer of Brütal Legend and COO of Double Fine Productions","st":"Postmortem: Double Fine's Brütal Legend","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-double-fine-s-i-brutal-legend-i-#:~:text=Our%20implementation%20of%20Scrum%20encouraged,far%20too%20long%20into%20production","sd":"Dec 2009, reprinted Mar 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Set up a separate 4-engineer tools group that built back-end services first","s":"Artists waited for the tools they needed. Several critical tools arrived late, the world editor's rollout slipped by a year, and engineers had to drop other work for emergency fixes.","w":"Psychonauts had lacked dedicated tools programmers, so Double Fine gave Brütal Legend a tools group of 4 engineers inside its engineering team. The group sat apart from the runtime teams. With little guidance from content leads, it started on tool deployment and build infrastructure. With no tools to deploy and no code to build, that work delayed the tools artists needed. Some sprints delivered artist features with no tool support, so nobody could use them. The Multi-User-Editor arrived a year late. The game shipped on 13 October 2009, and the postmortem called the team structure a mistake.","r":"Critical tools arrived late, and the world editor slipped by a year.","re":"Engineers made unplanned emergency fixes at the expense of other engineering work.","co":"A 1-year delay to the world editor; reduced artist productivity.","nf":false,"l":"Build the tools your artists and designers touch first, and keep tools programmers on the same teams as the features they support.","i":"Back-end services multiply the value of tools that already exist; with no tools yet, they multiply nothing. A separate tools group also drifted from the features it served. Infrastructure first makes sense once the tools people touch are in use.","a":"Which tool are our artists waiting for right now?","q":"“Unfortunately, without any tools to distribute or code to build, these initial investments significantly delayed the development of content/game-facing tools.”","qa":"Caroline Esmurdoc, executive producer on Brütal Legend and COO of Double Fine Productions","st":"Postmortem: Double Fine's Brutal Legend","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-double-fine-s-i-brutal-legend-i-#:~:text=Unfortunately%2C%20without%20any%20tools%20to%20distribute,development%20of%20content%2Fgame%2Dfacing%20tools","sd":"Dec 2009, reprinted Mar 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"camelotunchained","n":"Camelot Unchained","dv":"City State Entertainment","y":"2026","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2026","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/3997970/","ls":"Steam","cv":"camelotunchained.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built a custom engine for a crowdfunded MMO instead of licensing one","s":"Engine work took most of the programmers, and alpha and beta dates slipped. Six years after the Kickstarter, the game was in its first beta stage and still short of engineers.","w":"City State Entertainment raised $2,232,933 on Kickstarter in April and May 2013 for an RvR-focused MMORPG, and Mark Jacobs chose to build the engine from scratch. In September 2014 the alpha slipped into 2015: an engineer shortage meant most programming went into the engine's foundations. In July 2015 the planned August beta was delayed after a new senior programmer left; Jacobs said engine programmers are hard to come by. In June 2019 the game was in Beta 1, still short of engineers, and Jacobs said the engine came with a price. It entered Steam Early Access in June 2026.","r":"Alpha, beta and launch dates slipped for years.","re":"Jacobs took on investors and stopped announcing dates he could not hit.","co":"Alpha delayed into 2015 and the August 2015 beta delayed.","nf":false,"l":"If the design needs a custom engine, hire the engine programmers before you promise dates; they set the schedule for everything else.","i":"A custom engine puts a few scarce specialists on the critical path, so one lost hire moves every date. Jacobs still says the engine does what no other MMORPG can, so this is a price paid for a goal, not proof custom engines fail.","a":"Do we already have the engine programmers this schedule depends on?","q":"“The advantage of using a pre-built engine is that you can get a game made faster, no doubts about that.”","qa":"Mark Jacobs, City State Entertainment","st":"Interview: Camelot Unchained’s Mark Jacobs on the state of the beta and the NDA","su":"https://massivelyop.com/2019/06/21/interview-camelot-unchaineds-mark-jacobs-on-the-state-of-the-beta-and-the-nda/#:~:text=The%20advantage%20of%20using%20a,no%20doubts%20about%20that","sd":"Jun 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"capcomoverseasoutsourcedlineup","n":"Capcom's overseas-outsourced lineup (DmC Devil May Cry and others)","dv":"Capcom","y":"2013","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/220440/","ls":"Steam","cv":"capcomoverseasoutsourcedlineup.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Handed major sequels to overseas studios as a growth strategy from 2009","s":"Several outsourced titles ran late and missed quality and sales targets. Capcom booked a ¥6,949 million restructuring loss and moved development back in-house.","w":"In 2009 Capcom announced it would use overseas development companies to grow its lineup and overseas sales. By the fiscal year ending March 2013 the results had split: Blue Castle Games, later acquired by Capcom, delivered hits, while other partners had quality problems and frequent schedule delays. Decisions and contract changes between the two companies took time. DmC Devil May Cry sold 1.15 million against a 2 million target. Capcom re-examined its titles in development, booked a ¥6,949 million restructuring loss, and in its 2013 and 2014 annual reports announced a shift to in-house development.","r":"Outsourced titles slipped and missed targets; a ¥6,949 million restructuring loss followed.","re":"Capcom moved development in-house, raising its in-house ratio from 55% to 64%.","co":"¥6,949 million restructuring loss; DmC sold 1.15 million of a 2 million target.","nf":false,"l":"Before you hand a flagship sequel to an external studio, fix how spec changes get decided and who signs them.","i":"Capcom said the delays came from the time both companies needed to make decisions and change contract terms, while technology moved fast. The same policy produced hits at Blue Castle, so the partner's ability and the contract's speed matter more than outsourcing itself.","a":"How long does a spec change take to approve with our external studio?","q":"“As a result, some companies, like Blue Castle Games acquired by Capcom, were able to develop hit titles, while others were plagued by quality issues and frequent schedule delays.”","qa":"Kenzo Tsujimoto, Chairman and CEO of Capcom","st":"Annual Report 2013: CEO's Discussion of Performance Summary and New Medium-Term Business Goals","su":"https://www.capcom.co.jp/ir/english/data/pdf/annual/2013/annual_2013_04.pdf#:~:text=some%20companies%2C%20like%20Blue%20Castle,frequent%20schedule%20delays","sd":"2013","sy":"Company report","w8":4}]},{"k":"celdamage","n":"Cel Damage","dv":"Pseudo Interactive","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2014","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Designed the game around a physics engine that was still being rebuilt","s":"Design documents went stale every week, the engine and playable builds went down for over 8 months, and Microsoft dropped the studio at the end of 2000.","w":"Pseudo Interactive showed its physics engine at GDC in 1997 and began an Xbox racing game with Microsoft. The team of about 8 designed the game to the engine as it changed, and design documents went stale as fast as they were written. Then a full engine overhaul stopped playable builds for over 8 months. Microsoft cut the studio at the end of 2000. Demos won an EA deal in 2001, but all art had to be rebuilt to avoid an IP conflict, and features were locked 4 months before the 2001 Xbox launch. Wu's postmortem came in February 2002.","r":"Lost the first publisher and rebuilt design documents and art several times.","re":"Won an EA deal with demos and locked features 4 months before ship.","co":"Engine and playable builds down for over 8 months; up to 12 revisions per asset.","nf":false,"l":"If you design to an engine that is still changing, set hard cut-off dates for code and features before production starts.","i":"When the engine keeps changing, every design document and asset built on it goes stale, and the team cannot even describe the game to a publisher. Wu does not say stop designing to technology, only cap it with cut-off dates.","a":"On what date does the engine stop changing under our design?","q":"“Essentially, our strategic preplanning was stillborn.”","qa":"David Wu, president and technology director of Pseudo Interactive","st":"Postmortem: Pseudo Interactive's Cel Damage","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-pseudo-interactive-s-i-cel-damage-i-#:~:text=Essentially%2C%20our%20strategic,preplanning%20was%20stillborn","sd":"Feb 2002","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"celeste","n":"Celeste","dv":"Matt Makes Games","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/504230/","ls":"Steam","cv":"celeste.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Expanded a 4-day PICO-8 prototype into a full game planned for months","s":"The core from the prototype held all the way to launch. Scope grew with every new space, and a project expected to take a few months took 2 years.","w":"In July 2015 Maddy Thorson and Noel Berry released Celeste, a mountain-climbing platformer made in 4 days for the PICO-8 fantasy console. A few months later they decided to build a bigger game on it without PICO-8's limits. The first version was almost a port, with a larger play space and a scrolling camera, and climbing came early. They expected a few months of work, but the game grew with every space they explored. For the whole second year they thought it would be done next month. Celeste shipped on 25 January 2018.","r":"A few months of planned work became a 2-year project.","re":"They checked each expansion against the original idea of climbing a mountain.","co":"About 2 years of development instead of the few months first expected.","nf":false,"l":"When you grow a small prototype into a full game, re-estimate the schedule each time you add a new space or mechanic.","i":"A 4-day prototype proves the core verb, not the size of the game around it. Each new space opened more ideas, so the estimate kept falling behind. Berry says a quick prototype does not suit games that need many mechanics before they work.","a":"When did we last re-estimate the schedule after adding content?","q":"“When we later revisited the game, we decided to make something inspired by the original, but without any of the constraints of PICO-8.”","qa":"Noel Berry, co-creator of Celeste at Matt Makes Games","st":"Road to the IGF: Matt Makes Games' Celeste","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/road-to-the-igf-matt-makes-games-i-celeste-i-#:~:text=When%20we%20later%20revisited%20the%20game,the%20constraints%20of%20PICO%2D8","sd":"Mar 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"childrenofmorta","n":"Children of Morta","dv":"Dead Mage","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/330020/","ls":"Steam","cv":"childrenofmorta.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Raised the scope after Kickstarter and again after signing a publisher, with no production gate","s":"Nobody could say when production started. A 6-month game took 5 years, assets were made and thrown away as requirements shifted, and the story was reworked near the end.","w":"Dead Mage started Children of Morta as a small game to make in 6 months, choosing pixel art and a roguelike structure to control scope. After 9 months it ran a Kickstarter, launched in February 2015, and the success raised expectations. After 2 years, 11 bit studios signed on as publisher, and expectations rose again. The team grew from 5 to 18, and new features arrived at every feedback stage. The PC version shipped on 3 September 2019. In November 2020 Amir Fassihi wrote that he could not point to when pre-production ended.","r":"A 6-month plan became a 5-year production with discarded assets.","re":"The team delayed launch several times to act on publisher focus tests.","co":"5 years instead of 6 months; team grew from 5 to 18.","nf":false,"l":"Each time funding or a partner raises expectations, set a dated gate where pre-production ends and the feature list locks.","i":"Each new round of money or feedback reopened the design, so assets went stale before they were used. Fassihi credits the open phase with finding the game's best ideas; it works only while someone can pay for the extra years.","a":"On what date does our pre-production end?","q":"“Our development process was more like a colossal pre-production phase.”","qa":"Amir H. Fassihi, Dead Mage","st":"Postmortem: Children of Morta","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-children-of-morta#:~:text=Our%20development%20process%20was%20more,colossal%20pre%2Dproduction%20phase","sd":"Nov 2020","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Shared UI and UX work among existing staff instead of naming an owner","s":"Usability problems surfaced only in the last quarter of development. The interface was rebuilt from scratch at least 3 times, and new UI staff joined late.","w":"For the first half of the 5-year project, nobody at Dead Mage worked on UI and UX full time, and the tasks were shared across the team. Early players were developers, with the creators beside them. As characters, skill trees, items and upgrade systems piled up, outside players could not follow the game. In the last quarter of development, focus tests kept returning UX issues, made harder by support for 3 consoles besides PC. The team then added people for UI design and implementation. The game shipped on 3 September 2019.","r":"The interface was recreated from scratch at least 3 times.","re":"Dead Mage hired UI staff and put a designer on UX full time.","co":"At least 3 full UI rebuilds; many hours from core team members.","nf":false,"l":"Name a UX owner from the first build, before systems pile up on the interface.","i":"When the creators sit beside every tester, they explain the game for it, so usability debt stays hidden until strangers play. The cost grew with every system added on top of an interface nobody owned.","a":"Who owns UX on our team today?","q":"“Overall the game user interface was recreated at least three times from scratch.”","qa":"Amir H. Fassihi, Dead Mage","st":"Postmortem: Children of Morta","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-children-of-morta#:~:text=Overall%20the%20game%20user%20interface,three%20times%20from%20scratch","sd":"Nov 2020","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"chromasquad","n":"Chroma Squad","dv":"Behold Studios","y":"2015","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2017","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/251130/","ls":"Steam","cv":"chromasquad.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Built about 30 hours of branching story content with a single game designer","s":"Testing all of it fell to one person, and it was the studio's biggest challenge. Two years after the PC launch, the team was still shipping patches and fixes.","w":"Behold Studios raised nearly $100,000 on Kickstarter in 2013 for Chroma Squad. The team grew to about 10 people, with Mark Venturelli as the only game designer, and worked for about 2 years. The content reached about 30 hours, split across several story paths. A full test pass took Venturelli almost 10 hours, even in speedrun mode. The game launched on PC and Mac in April 2015, then on mobile and consoles with Bandai Namco in May 2017. That June, Venturelli called content scope the biggest challenge and said patches and fixes were still coming.","r":"Full test passes took the only designer almost 10 hours each.","re":"Bandai Namco's QA teams helped, and patches continued into 2017.","co":"Almost 10 hours per full test pass, even in speedrun mode.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock content length, time one full test pass and multiply it by the passes your designers can afford.","i":"Test time grows with content length and with every story path, while one designer's week stays the same. Branching makes it worse, because each path needs its own pass. A linear game of the same length needs fewer of them.","a":"How many hours does one full test pass of our game take?","q":"“Picture me, as the only game designer, trying to test this game: even in “speedrun” mode it takes me almost 10 hours to do a full pass!”","qa":"Mark Venturelli, game designer at Behold Studios","st":"Team attack: The making of Chroma Squad","su":"https://www.pocketgamer.biz/team-attack-the-making-of-chroma-squad/#:~:text=Picture%20me%2C%20as%20the%20only,to%20do%20a%20full%20pass","sd":"Jun 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"chroniclesofelyria","n":"Chronicles of Elyria","dv":"Soulbound Studios","y":"2020","sc":"Indie","se":"Cancelled 2020","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Planned to use Kickstarter money for a demo that would attract publishers","s":"Publishers saw too little after a few months of production, and investors balked at the crowdfunding model. In March 2020 Soulbound ran out of money and laid off all employees.","w":"Soulbound Studios launched its Kickstarter in 2016 to hire staff, build a playable demo and quickly attract publishers to fund the rest. A few months into production, there was not enough to show. Publishers asked the team to work faster, show more, or let them take over development and change the business model. Investors asked why they were needed if crowdfunding could carry the game. After Settlers of Elyria failed and five months of thin crowdfunding, Jeromy Walsh wrote on 25 March 2020 that the money had run out and he had laid off all employees.","r":"No publisher or investor signed on, and the money ran out.","re":"Walsh halted development and hoped to revive the game with new funding.","co":"About 4 years of work; all staff laid off; nearly $8 million crowdfunded.","nf":false,"l":"Before you hire on crowdfunding money, line up who will fund full production and what demo they need to see.","i":"Publishers fund what they can play, and a few months of production left little to play. Ongoing crowdfunding also told investors the studio did not need them. The risk drops only when backers alone can fund the whole game.","a":"Who funds full production, and have they told us what they need to see?","q":"“When we launched our Kickstarter back in 2016, it was with the plan to use the money to hire additional staff, put together a playable demo, and quickly attract publishers who would be willing to fund the remainder of development.”","qa":"Jeromy \"Caspian\" Walsh, Soulbound Studios","st":"State of Elyria: Into the Abyss","su":"https://chroniclesofelyria.com/news/34919/State-of-Elyria-Into-the-Abyss#:~:text=When%20we%20launched%20our%20Kickstarter,the%20remainder%20of%20development","sd":"Mar 2020","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"citiesskylines","n":"Cities: Skylines","dv":"Colossal Order","y":"2015","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/255710/","ls":"Steam","cv":"citiesskylines.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Shipped two smaller transit games first, then scoped the city builder to basics","s":"A 5-person studio with limited funding got a full city builder out with its simulation intact, and the game was still being expanded 6 years after release.","w":"Colossal Order was founded in 2009 by 5 people with limited funding and the long-term goal of a city builder. It shipped the Cities in Motion games first, a mass transit slice of a city builder, and learned how its players and modders worked. For Skylines the team aimed at a successor to SimCity 4 but kept the scope to roads, houses, jobs and city services, dropped day/night and seasons, and spent on simulation and modding rather than visuals. The sales target was 300,000 copies. The game came out in 2015; in March 2021 Hallikainen said it was still improving.","r":"A small team shipped a complete city builder with the simulation intact.","re":"Added the day/night cycle in the first expansion after player feedback.","co":"Day/night cycle and seasons left out of the launch game.","nf":true,"l":"Ship a smaller game on the same tech and community first, and cut the visual features your team size cannot carry.","i":"A 5-person studio cannot pay for both a full simulation and a full visual feature set, so Colossal Order spent on the simulation and let modders fill gaps. The stepping-stone route works when the small games share tech and audience with the big one.","a":"Which visual features can we cut so the simulation ships complete?","q":"“This was back in 2009 and we were five people with limited funding, so we decided to start with something smaller to build toward greater challenges.”","qa":"Mariina Hallikainen, CEO of Colossal Order","st":"Cities: Skylines Studio CEO Talks Development, Mod Community, And Six Years Of Success","su":"https://www.gameinformer.com/interview/2021/03/13/cities-skylines-studio-ceo-talks-development-mod-community-and-six-years-of#:~:text=This%20was%20back%20in%202009,toward%20greater%20challenges","sd":"Mar 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"civilizationii","n":"Civilization II","dv":"MicroProse","y":"1996","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Dropped prototype multiplayer from Civ II and left that market test to CivNet","s":"The team avoided a large block of technical polish, and the ship date held. Civ II went out as a single-player game.","w":"After Colonization, MicroProse gave Brian Reynolds a sequel to Civilization, with the working title Civilization 2000. At the same time it was building CivNet, the original Civilization with multiplayer, on a shared graphics engine. The Civ II team got a fair way into integrating multiplayer code, and Reynolds and Doug Kaufman played multiplayer prototypes together. Polishing it would have greatly grown the technical scope, and demand for multiplayer 4X was unproven. The team dropped it and kept the ship date. In a 2019 interview, Reynolds said Civ II beat its sales projection by about 3 million units.","r":"The ship date held, without multiplayer at launch.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When a sister project already tests a feature's market, cut that feature from yours before you polish it.","i":"Multiplayer worked as a prototype, but polish would have greatly grown the technical scope for an unproven market. A parallel project testing the same bet made the cut cheap; without one, the feature has to be weighed on its own.","a":"Is another project already testing this feature's market for us?","q":"“But polishing multiplayer would had substantially increased the technical scope of the game, and with the marketability of multiplayer for a 4X game still being a bit unproven and especially given the existence of another project (CivNet) aimed directly at testing that hypothesis, we decided not to move forward on the multiplayer – thereby keeping our ship-date intact.”","qa":"Brian Reynolds, MicroProse","st":"Brian Reynolds (MicroProse/Firaxis) – Interview","su":"https://www.arcadeattack.co.uk/brian-reynolds/#:~:text=But%20polishing%20multiplayer%20would%20had,keeping%20our%20ship%2Ddate%20intact","sd":"Oct 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"civilizationiv","n":"Civilization IV","dv":"Firaxis","y":"2005","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/3900/","ls":"Steam","cv":"civilizationiv.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built Civilization IV to be moddable, with no game data named in the code","s":"The code became harder to read and easier to break. Soren Johnson said that without modding and multiplayer the game would likely have shipped without significant patching.","w":"Firaxis built Civilization IV for modders: game data sat in XML, scripting in Python, and the code could never refer to anything by name. Fan modders joined during development, but in the first year of the project they could not get anything done, because the XML kept changing. Designer Soren Johnson saw the code grow less readable, with new ways to fail. The game was also the first Civilization with multiplayer out of the box. By December 2005 its first patch was out. Johnson then said that without modding and multiplayer, significant patching would likely not have been needed.","r":"More complex code and significant patching after launch.","re":"Firaxis patched the game and stopped changing the XML to protect mods.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Price moddability as its own feature, with its own programming and test time, before you promise it to players.","i":"Moddable code reaches everything through data instead of names, so every system gains a layer that can fail. Modding was not the only multiplier: Johnson expected multiplayer alone to bring 3 or 4 times as many bugs.","a":"Have we priced moddability as its own feature in the schedule?","q":"“If Civilization IV had no multi-player and no modding, I think that it's pretty likely that we could have released the game without a need for significant patching.”","qa":"Soren Johnson, designer at Firaxis","st":"Soren Johnson Interview","su":"https://planetcivilization.gamespy.com/View9b11.html?id=4&view=Interviews.Detail#:~:text=If%20Civilization%20IV%20had%20no,need%20for%20significant%20patching","sd":"Dec 2005","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"civilizationv","n":"Civilization V","dv":"Firaxis","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/8930/","ls":"Steam","cv":"civilizationv.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Prototyped Civ V gameplay on the Civilization IV engine while building a new one","s":"When the new engine arrived 18 months before release, the gameplay had already been refined for almost 2 years. The swap did not halt either team.","w":"Early in an almost 4-year development, Jon Shafer laid out his vision to a prototype team of 7 artists working on the Civilization IV engine. The new DirectX 11 engine would not be ready until 18 months before release, too late to test ideas like hexes and one unit per tile. So gameplay ran on Civilization IV graphics behind a clear interface, and for a few months during the swap it ran on both engines. Civilization V shipped on time on 21 September 2010. In March 2011, lead producer Dennis Shirk listed the split first under what went right.","r":"Gameplay had almost 2 years of refinement before the new engine arrived.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If your new engine will arrive late, build gameplay on an existing engine behind a clean interface you can swap.","i":"Splitting design risk from engine risk meant neither waited for the other. The swap still needs its own plan: Firaxis lost 2 months of external test builds because Steam DRM was not ready when the new engine arrived.","a":"Can we test our core gameplay before the new engine is ready?","q":"“Our solution was to enable a parallel development track for gameplay using the existing Civilization IV engine as the graphics component.”","qa":"Dennis Shirk, lead producer at Firaxis","st":"Classic Postmortem: Firaxis' Civilization V","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-firaxis-i-civilization-v-i-#:~:text=Our%20solution%20was%20to%20enable,as%20the%20graphics%20component","sd":"Mar 2011, reprinted Sep 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"clang","n":"CLANG","dv":"Subutai Corporation","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"Cancelled 2014","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Put historical accuracy ahead of fun in a Kickstarter-funded sword-fighting prototype","s":"The $526,125 prototype was technically innovative but not fun enough to attract further investment, so the team disbanded and the project was terminated.","w":"Neal Stephenson's Subutai Corporation ran the CLANG Kickstarter from 9 June to 9 July 2012, asking $500,000 to revolutionize sword-fighting games; 9,023 backers pledged $526,125. In 2013 the company delivered the prototype and donor rewards. The prototype was technically innovative but not fun to play, and Stephenson later wrote that he had focused too much on historical accuracy and not enough on fun. Additional fundraising failed, team members put in their own time and money, and the team disbanded. On 18 September 2014 Stephenson announced the termination of CLANG; about two dozen backers asked for refunds, totaling about $700.","r":"No game was built beyond the prototype; the team disbanded.","re":"Sought more funding, failed, then cut the cord in September 2014.","co":"$526,125 raised, plus the team's own time and money; about $700 refunded.","nf":false,"l":"Prove the core loop is fun before you spend the budget on accuracy or realism.","i":"The plan needed a second round of money after the prototype, so the prototype had to sell the game to investors, and a realistic demo that was not fun could not. Accuracy can be a selling point, but only once the fight itself is fun.","a":"Is our prototype fun, or only accurate?","q":"“The prototype was technically innovative, but it wasn't very fun to play.”","qa":"Neal Stephenson, Subutai Corporation","st":"Update 43: Final Update","su":"https://www.kickstarter.com/projects/260688528/clang/posts/989911#:~:text=The%20prototype%20was%20technically%20innovative,very%20fun%20to%20play","sd":"Sep 2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Crowdfunded only a prototype and toolkit, planning to raise the next round later","s":"The prototype reached backers in 2013 but, by Neal Stephenson's account, was not fun enough to attract investment. The money ran out, and the team disbanded in 2014.","w":"In June and July 2012, Subutai raised $526,125 from 9,023 Kickstarter backers to build a sword-fighting game prototype and toolkit. The team delivered the prototype in 2013, and one backer called the demo essentially unplayable. In September 2013, with the Kickstarter money spent, Subutai paused development to find the next round. Further fundraising failed, and the team disbanded. On 18 September 2014, Neal Stephenson ended the project and took responsibility for focusing on historical accuracy over fun.","r":"The prototype did not attract the next round of funding.","re":"Stephenson ended the project and refunded about two dozen backers who asked.","co":"$526,125 raised and spent; team members absorbed significant personal losses.","nf":false,"l":"If your prototype must raise the next round, build it to show the fun investors will pay for.","i":"A prototype built to raise money is a sales tool: investors judge whether it is fun, not whether it is accurate. A realism-first prototype can still work when the funding comes from backers who want realism.","a":"Does our funding prototype show the fun, or only the tech?","q":"“Others are my responsibility in that I probably focused too much on historical accuracy and not enough on making it sufficiently fun to attract additional investment.”","qa":"Neal Stephenson, creator of CLANG at Subutai Corporation","st":"Creator of Swordfighting Game “CLANG” Abandons Its Plans Two Years After Kickstarter Success","su":"https://www.crowdfundinsider.com/2014/09/50198-creator-swordfighting-game-clang-abandons-plans-two-years-kickstarter-success/#:~:text=Others%20are%20my%20responsibility%20in,to%20attract%20additional%20investment","sd":"Sep 2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"clashofclans","n":"Clash of Clans","dv":"Supercell","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/clash-of-clans/id529479190","ls":"App Store","cv":"clashofclans.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Made all game art as 3D models pre-rendered into 2D images","s":"When the new iPad arrived with a retina screen, the team could render its existing models at the higher resolution with little effort.","w":"Supercell built Clash of Clans in 6 months from start to first release, with a team that grew from 5 to 8 people. Its art was pre-rendered 3D, produced with 3ds Max, Photoshop and Flash. When Apple launched the new iPad with a retina screen, the team rendered the 3D models at retina resolution. Product lead Lasse Louhento said the models made this really easy. The game reached the App Store on August 2, 2012. In October 2012 Louhento said the project had no major problems.","r":"Retina art for the new iPad was easy to produce from existing models.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Build art from sources you can re-render at a higher resolution, and test one asset at double size before production.","i":"Pre-rendered 3D keeps the source model apart from the shipped pixels, so a new resolution costs render time instead of artist time. It helps only when the look survives rendering; hand-painted styles do not scale this way.","a":"Can we export our art at double resolution without redrawing it?","q":"“One big advantage of 3D models was that they made it really easy for us to render them in retina resolution for the launch of the new iPad.”","qa":"Lasse Louhento, product lead at Supercell","st":"Chart rush: The making of Clash of Clans","su":"https://www.pocketgamer.biz/chart-rush-making-of-clash-of-clans/#:~:text=One%20big%20advantage%20of%203D,launch%20of%20the%20new%20iPad","sd":"Oct 2012","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"clashquest","n":"Clash Quest","dv":"Supercell","y":"2022","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2022","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Generated every Clash Quest level from data instead of building each one by hand","s":"Designers could rework progression, content and the economy quickly, and the team was only 13 people when the beta opened in April 2021.","w":"Touko Tahkokallio drafted Clash Quest in August 2018 as a side project and iterated a prototype with developer Juho Glad for about nine months. After a company playtest, Supercell kicked off the project in fall 2019 and restarted it on its internal engine. The team built systems that generate every level from data. About 18 months later, with 13 people, it opened the beta in April 2021. In February 2022 Tahkokallio said the data approach made progression and economy changes easy and kept the team small. Supercell ended development in August 2022, saying the game still missed its bar.","r":"Progression and economy changes stayed easy for a team of 13.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before content production starts, check that a designer can change a level's difficulty by editing data alone.","i":"When levels are data, a balance change is an edit, not a rebuild, so headcount does not grow with content. A cheap content pipeline does not prove the game works: Supercell ended Clash Quest in 2022 for missing its bar.","a":"Can a designer retune a level without anyone rebuilding it?","q":"“This allows us to manage and iterate progression, content, and the economy of the game quite easily and also allows us to keep the team very small.”","qa":"Touko Tahkokallio, game lead at Supercell","st":"Supercell talks breaking the mould of puzzle games with Clash Quest","su":"https://www.pocketgamer.biz/making-of-clash-quest/#:~:text=This%20allows%20us%20to%20manage,keep%20the%20team%20very%20small","sd":"Feb 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"clashroyale","n":"Clash Royale","dv":"Supercell","y":"2016","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/clash-royale/id1053012308","ls":"App Store","cv":"clashroyale.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Shelved a real-time PvP prototype on the belief that it would never work","s":"The idea sat untouched while Supercell shipped Clash of Clans and Hay Day. Years later two founders revived it, and the bigger prototype became Clash Royale.","w":"Before Clash of Clans came out, Jon Franzas built a Supercell prototype called The Summoners. Someone, probably CEO Ilkka Paananen by his own account, said real-time PvP would never work. With Clash of Clans and Hay Day to make, nobody followed it up. Many years later founders Niko and Visa used it to inspire a project codenamed Scrawl. Most staff wanted to kill the first version; a few joined and built a bigger one. Given the Clash of Clans theme, it launched as Clash Royale on March 2, 2016. Paananen told the story in a BAFTA lecture posted that September.","r":"The concept sat unused for years before it became Clash Royale.","re":"Two founders revived it as Scrawl, and a bigger prototype followed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When you shelve a prototype, write down the assumption that killed it and the condition that would make you test it again.","i":"The Summoners was dropped on an untested belief about real-time PvP, then forgotten while 2 other games took the studio's time. This applies to ideas dropped on belief or for lack of capacity; a prototype that failed a real player test is another case.","a":"Which shelved prototypes did we drop on an untested belief?","q":"“But then a few of us believed in the game, they joined the Scrawl team, and then they did this bigger prototype.”","qa":"Ilkka Paananen, CEO of Supercell","st":"Ilkka Paananen: BAFTA Games Lecture","su":"https://www.bafta.org/media-centre/press-releases/ilkka-paananen-bafta-games-lecture/#:~:text=But%20then%20a%20few%20of%20us,did%20this%20bigger%20prototype","sd":"Sep 2016","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4}]},{"k":"codenamevanguard","n":"Codename Vanguard","dv":"Remedy Entertainment","y":"2024","sc":"AAA","se":"Rebooted as Codename Kestrel 2024","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Developed Vanguard as a free-to-play co-op multiplayer game with Tencent","s":"At the end of proof of concept, Remedy and Tencent judged the potential was not there. Remedy wrote off EUR 7.2 million, and the premium reboot was later cancelled.","w":"On December 28, 2021, Remedy signed Tencent as global publishing partner for Vanguard, a cooperative free-to-play multiplayer game. Progress looked promising in the first half of 2023. On November 13, 2023, at the end of proof of concept, the two companies rebooted it as Kestrel, a premium co-op game, citing risk in the fast-changing free-to-play market. Part of the team moved to other Remedy projects. Remedy wrote off all EUR 7.2 million of capitalized Vanguard costs in Q4 2023. Kestrel restarted under an amended contract on February 6, 2024, and was cancelled in early concept on May 7, 2024.","r":"All capitalized Vanguard costs were written off and the project returned to concept.","re":"Remedy rebooted it as premium Kestrel, then cancelled it in May 2024.","co":"EUR 7.2 million write-off in Q4 2023.","nf":false,"l":"Before you commit to a free-to-play game, set a proof-of-concept gate where the business model must pass or the project stops.","i":"The gate stopped spending before full production, so the loss stayed at the capitalized EUR 7.2 million. Remedy said the bar for new free-to-play games had risen, so the risk was market timing as much as design.","a":"What result at proof of concept would make us stop this project?","q":"“Despite the promising progress during the first half of the year, we decided with Tencent that the potential was not there.”","qa":"Tero Virtala, CEO of Remedy Entertainment","st":"Remedy Entertainment Plc: Financial Statements Release January-December 2023: Challenging year results in two established franchises after the successful Alan Wake 2 launch","su":"https://news.cision.com/remedy-entertainment-oyj/r/remedy-entertainment-plc--financial-statements-release-january-december-2023--challenging-year-resul,c3948561#:~:text=Despite%20the%20promising%20progress%20during,the%20potential%20was%20not%20there","sd":"Mar 2024","sy":"Company report","w8":5}]},{"k":"commandandconquer","n":"Command & Conquer","dv":"Westwood","y":"1995","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2229830/","ls":"Steam","cv":"commandandconquer.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"External commitments","t":"Built the follow-up to Dune 2 in its own setting, not under the Dune license","s":"Westwood escaped a license with limited access, a time limit and the usual fees. The new setting became the Command & Conquer series.","w":"Joe Bostic was lead programmer and gameplay designer on Dune 2, alongside Brett Sperry. Westwood wanted to build on those real-time strategy roots, but its access to the Dune license was limited. The license came from the film license, had a time limit for exploitation and carried the usual financial burdens. So the team chose a setting of its own. Bostic first pitched fantasy factions of knights, wizards and beasts. With the first Gulf War underway, Sperry argued a modern military theme would be more relatable, and that became Command & Conquer. Bostic told the story in 2018.","r":"The next RTS was free of the license's time limit and fees.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you build a sequel on a license, check how long you may use it and what each game pays.","i":"A license with a time limit puts a clock on the whole series, not just one game, and every sequel pays fees again. Owning the setting pays off only if the game can sell without the famous name.","a":"How many more games can we make before our license runs out?","q":"“We only had limited access to the license – it was derived from the movie license and had time limit for exploitation in addition to the usual financial burdens with using a license.”","qa":"Joe Bostic, lead programmer and designer at Westwood Studios","st":"Joe Bostic (Westwood Studios) – Interview","su":"https://www.arcadeattack.co.uk/joe-bostic/#:~:text=We%20only%20had%20limited%20access,with%20using%20a%20license","sd":"Aug 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"commandandconquertiberiansun","n":"Command & Conquer: Tiberian Sun","dv":"Westwood","y":"1999","sc":"AAA","se":"Re-release 2024","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2229880/","ls":"Steam","cv":"commandandconquertiberiansun.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Switched the core graphics engine to an isometric view for a 3D look","s":"The switch broke systems nobody had anticipated. Destructible bridges alone took over 10 times their programming estimate, and the new view was one of 3 new systems behind a 9-month delay.","w":"Westwood began Tiberian Sun after Command & Conquer and Red Alert, aiming to keep the original's feel. Early on, the team rotated the camera to an isometric perspective for more depth and ruled out a polygonal engine to keep system requirements low. The new view set off a cascade of broken systems. Bridges that could be destroyed and rebuilt touched pathfinding, Z-buffering, rendering, unit behavior and AI. With new scripting triggers and a rebuilt AI on top, the schedule became unpredictable, and the game shipped 9 months late. By April 2000 it had sold more than 1.5 million units.","r":"Bridges took over 10 times their programming estimate.","re":"Westwood kept almost all planned engine features in the final game.","co":"Bridges over 10 times their estimate; part of a 9-month delay.","nf":false,"l":"Before switching a core view or renderer, list every system it touches and prototype the hardest feature end to end.","i":"A new core view reaches every system that reads position and depth, so terrain features multiply the work. Executive producer Brett Sperry's rule: 1 new core technology can slip up to 90 days, 2 can add a year, 3 make dates unpredictable.","a":"Which systems break if we change the camera or renderer now?","q":"“Bridges that could be destroyed and rebuilt, for example, wound up taking over ten times as long to program as we originally estimated.”","qa":"Rade Stojsavljevic, producer at Westwood Studios","st":"Postmortem: Westwood Studios' Command and Conquer: Tiberian Sun","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-westwood-studios-command-and-conquer-tiberian-sun#:~:text=Bridges%20that%20could%20be%20destroyed,as%20we%20originally%20estimated","sd":"Apr 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"External commitments","t":"Kept the video shoot date fixed to secure James Earl Jones and Michael Biehn","s":"Video pre-production ran much shorter than it should have, so complex shots were edited on the fly. Post-production then outgrew the studio network.","w":"Based on its video work for Red Alert and Dune 2000, Westwood decided to push the limits of its in-house video for Tiberian Sun. The team storyboarded every scene, and 6 artists spent 3 months building 3D sets before the shoot. Video pre-production still ran much shorter than it should have, and the shoot date could not move without losing both lead actors. Complex shots got too little time and were edited on the fly. Afterwards, 60 minutes of digitized video swamped a network never built for it. Producer Rade Stojsavljevic described the fallout in April 2000.","r":"Compositing slowed on storage shortages and daily file transfer bottlenecks.","re":"Westwood's IT staff and called-in favors found extra network storage.","co":"Well over 500GB across 4 file servers; daily transfer delays.","nf":false,"l":"Before booking talent on a fixed date, run a complex shot through capture, compositing and storage.","i":"A fixed talent date turns unfinished planning into fixes in post, which Stojsavljevic calls 10 times harder and costlier. The lesson holds for immovable external dates; when the shoot can move, moving it is the cheaper fix.","a":"Have we run a complex shot through the whole video pipeline?","q":"“For various reasons, the pre-production for Tiberian Sun was much shorter than it should have been.”","qa":"Rade Stojsavljevic, producer at Westwood Studios","st":"Postmortem: Westwood Studios' Command and Conquer: Tiberian Sun","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-westwood-studios-command-and-conquer-tiberian-sun#:~:text=For%20various%20reasons%2C%20the%20pre%2Dproduction,than%20it%20should%20have%20been","sd":"Apr 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"concord","n":"Concord","dv":"Firewalk Studios","y":"2024","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Held Concord's user testing and internal evaluation gates late in development","s":"Concord launched in August 2024 at $39.99, drew few players and went offline within about two weeks. Sony refunded all buyers and closed Firewalk Studios.","w":"Firewalk Studios built Concord, a live-service first-person shooter for PlayStation 5 and PC, reportedly over about eight years. Its open beta peaked at 2,388 concurrent players on PC. The game launched on August 23, 2024, at $39.99. Within about two weeks Sony took it offline, refunded every buyer and later closed the studio. In November 2024 Sony president Hiroki Totoki said the user testing and internal evaluation gates should have come much earlier. In June 2025 Hermen Hulst said the game was not differentiated enough for a hyper-competitive segment.","r":"Shut down and refunded within about two weeks of launch.","re":"Sony closed Firewalk and added earlier, more frequent testing of new projects.","co":"Full refunds to all buyers; studio closed.","nf":true,"l":"Put a player test of what makes the game stand out into pre-production, before the team scales up.","i":"In a crowded genre a well-made game still fails if players see nothing new, and only player tests show that early. A beta weeks before launch leaves only two options: ship or cancel.","a":"Have players told us what makes this game different from its rivals?","q":"“And then we need to bring them forward, and we should have done those gates much earlier than we did.”","qa":"Hiroki Totoki, president, COO and CFO of Sony (via interpreter)","st":"Sony president reflects on why Concord failed","su":"https://www.videogameschronicle.com/news/sony-president-reflects-on-why-concord-failed/#:~:text=And%20then%20we%20need%20to,much%20earlier%20than%20we%20did","sd":"Nov 2024","sy":"Company earnings call","w8":4}]},{"k":"conduit2","n":"Conduit 2","dv":"High Voltage Software","y":"2011","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Set the sequel's team goals around show demos instead of a production plan","s":"Features were built only far enough to demo. After a 2010 layoff, the half-built systems added months, and the team crunched to final submission.","w":"High Voltage's team rolled straight from The Conduit into Conduit 2, with Sega already on board for the sequel. Milestones were never defined in enough detail or given to the team early enough. Many goals came from demos for shows and conventions instead, and features were built only until they could be shown. In 2010 the studio had its first significant layoff in nearly 18 years. Remaining staff struggled with unfinished systems, and each fix exposed new bugs, adding months. After E3, management added production staff, leads and a large group of developers. The game shipped in April 2011.","r":"Months added to development and a crunch to final submission.","re":"Management added new producers, leads and a large group of staff.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Define milestones from the production plan, and count any feature built only to demo standard as unfinished work.","i":"A demo needs features to look done, not be done, so each show left more half-built systems behind. That debt hit hardest when the layoff removed people who understood those systems.","a":"How many features in our last demo are actually finished?","q":"“This approach resulted in awesome demos at the expense of the completed game.”","qa":"Kevin Sheller, senior producer of Conduit 2 at High Voltage Software","st":"Postmortem: High Voltage Software's Conduit 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-high-voltage-software-s-i-conduit-2-i-#:~:text=This%20approach%20resulted%20in%20awesome,of%20the%20completed%20game","sd":"Jun 2011","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Story & world","t":"Hired an outside writer for an epic story without setting the tone first","s":"His script ran longer than several feature films and was too serious for show audiences. The team cut and rewrote it at all hours before the final recording session.","w":"The biggest criticism of The Conduit was a dry, short campaign with no cohesive story. For Conduit 2, High Voltage hired an external writer to craft an epic storyline. His cinematic scenes would have run longer than several feature films, and the tone was very serious. At early showings, audiences celebrated the occasional joke instead. Near the deadline the scenes were still too long, so the team cut and rewrote the script at all hours before final recording. The game shipped in April 2011. In June 2011, senior producer Kevin Sheller wrote that early light-hearted direction would have saved time.","r":"A late, all-hours rewrite of the script before final recording.","re":"The team edited and rewrote the script to fit length and tone.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Give an outside writer a written brief on tone and story length before they start the script.","i":"A writer hired to fix a thin story aims big, and with no brief nothing caps length or tone. The team found the right tone by watching audiences at showings, so check the brief against early playtests too.","a":"Has our writer got a written brief on tone and length?","q":"“If we had only given the light-hearted direction to the writer initially, we could have saved a lot of time and effort.”","qa":"Kevin Sheller, senior producer of Conduit 2 at High Voltage Software","st":"Postmortem: High Voltage Software's Conduit 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-high-voltage-software-s-i-conduit-2-i-#:~:text=If%20we%20had%20only%20given,lot%20of%20time%20and%20effort","sd":"Jun 2011","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"control","n":"Control","dv":"Remedy","y":"2019","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/870780/","ls":"Steam","cv":"control.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Set Control's budget at €20m to €30m, a more moderate sum than earlier Remedy games","s":"Remedy's CEO put break-even at 1 to 2 million units. By the end of 2020, Control had sold over 2 million copies and its royalties helped Remedy to a record year.","w":"Remedy made Control with triple-A experience but a smaller budget than its previous titles: €20m to €30m, a sensible budget and scope in CEO Tero Virtala's words. Production ran about 3 years, with about 100 developers on average at the peak. At Devcom in August 2019, Virtala called that budget very safe and put break-even within 1 to 2 million units. Control came out that month. In February 2021, Remedy reported over 2 million copies sold by the end of 2020, and Control royalties helped lift revenue 29.8% in a record year.","r":"Sales passed the top of the 1 to 2 million unit break-even range.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"€20m to €30m; about 100 developers at peak; about 3 years.","nf":false,"l":"Work out the break-even units for your budget before you lock scope, and cut scope until that number is safe.","i":"A smaller budget lowers the number of copies needed to break even, so a solid hit turns a profit instead of just paying back. The lesson assumes the team can still reach triple-A quality on that sum; Remedy had its own Northlight engine.","a":"How many copies must we sell to break even on this budget?","q":"“We would say it's about a sensible budget and scope.”","qa":"Tero Virtala, CEO of Remedy Entertainment","st":"Devcom 2019 - Budget for Remedy's Control between €20m and €30m","su":"https://www.pcgamesinsider.biz/news/69553/devcom-2019-budget-for-remedys-control-between-20m-and-30m/#:~:text=We%20would%20say%20it%27s%20about,a%20sensible%20budget%20and%20scope","sd":"Aug 2019","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"cookservedelicious3","n":"Cook, Serve, Delicious! 3?!","dv":"Vertigo Gaming","y":"2020","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1000030/","ls":"Steam","cv":"cookservedelicious3.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned the third game as a cheap build on the second game's base","s":"Galindo budgeted for half of CSD2's sales and reused most of its base. CSD3 took less than half the development time, earned almost the same revenue and became the best-rated game in the series.","w":"Cook, Serve, Delicious! 2!! had triple the first game's budget and missed Galindo's blockbuster expectations. The third game began as a business plan: many players had seen only half of CSD2's content, so he could build CSD3 mostly from the second game's base and do it cheap. He estimated it would sell half as much, which freed him to cut many CSD2 features and build a new food-truck flow. CSD3 hit a 98% Overwhelmingly Positive rating in Early Access and released 14 October 2020. His GDC 2021 slides showed under half of CSD2's development time for almost the same revenue.","r":"Development time under half of CSD2's, for almost the same revenue.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Less than half of CSD2's development time; absolute figures only on the GDC slides.","nf":false,"l":"For a third game, plan for half the previous sales, cut the budget to match and reuse the previous game's base.","i":"A reused base cuts the cost of every feature, and a low sales target frees the design to change the core flow instead of adding content. Galindo's own rule: this works for a franchise whose audience already exists, not for a first game.","a":"How much of the last game's base can this sequel reuse as is?","q":"“From a business perspective CSD 3 was a must-do, as I could do it for cheap.”","qa":"David Galindo, creator and developer of Cook, Serve, Delicious! at Vertigo Gaming","st":"CSD: Sequels II: Return of the Franchise","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/csd-sequels-ii-return-of-the-franchise#:~:text=From%20a%20business%20perspective%20CSD,do%20it%20for%20cheap","sd":"May 2020","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"costumequest2","n":"Costume Quest 2","dv":"Double Fine","y":"2014","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/275180/","ls":"Steam","cv":"costumequest2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Signed for 8 launch platforms on less money than the 2-platform original","s":"Outsourced last-gen ports pulled the lead programmer off combat, and many months of gameplay polish were lost. Console versions came out 3 weeks after PC.","w":"In fall 2013 Double Fine got a Costume Quest sequel signed with Midnight City, a new Majesco label. The contract asked for 8 simultaneous platforms, PS3, Xbox 360 and Wii U included, in under a year and on a budget below the 2-platform original. The team pushed back, then signed rather than lose the deal. A contractor took the last-gen ports, and the lead programmer and a graphics programmer had to step in. PC shipped on October 7, 2014, consoles from October 28. In 2015 project director Gabe Cinquepalmi called the 8 platforms the team's top source of woes.","r":"Many months of gameplay polish went to port support instead.","re":"Pulled 2 programmers onto the ports and cut some combat features.","co":"Many months of gameplay polish, 2 programmers diverted, some combat features cut.","nf":false,"l":"Before you sign, cost every contracted platform in programmer weeks, and drop or stagger the ones the budget cannot carry.","i":"Each SKU adds rendering code, UI rules and certification work, and most of it lands at the end, when senior programmers are busiest. The risk is smaller when a port house that knows your engine owns those SKUs end to end.","a":"Have we costed each contracted platform in programmer weeks?","q":"“I wouldn't recommend simultaneous launch on this many platforms to anyone shipping a game without a triple-A budget.”","qa":"Gabe Cinquepalmi, project director at Double Fine","st":"The chilling tale of the development of Costume Quest 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/the-chilling-tale-of-the-development-of-i-costume-quest-2-i-#:~:text=I%20wouldn%27t%20recommend%20simultaneous%20launch,without%20a%20triple%2DA%20budget.","sd":"Oct 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Kept the original combat code and patched around it instead of rewriting","s":"The old system was too idiosyncratic to extend, and its inefficiency kept slowing the team through production.","w":"Costume Quest 2 had 10 months: 1 of pre-production and 9 of production, from January 2014. The lead programmer spent most of pre-production moving the 4-year-old Costume Quest code into the current version of the Buddha engine. When the team dug into the original combat system, it proved too idiosyncratic to extend, having been written under deadline pressure. A full rewrite did not fit the schedule, so the team rewrote a few key parts and hacked around the rest. The game shipped in October 2014, and the 2015 postmortem listed this technical debt under what went wrong.","r":"The old combat code kept slowing the team through production.","re":"Rewrote a few key portions and hacked around the rest.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"In pre-production, have a programmer add one new feature to the inherited system before you plan around reusing it.","i":"Code written to ship under a deadline fits only the game it shipped in, so every new feature has to work around it. The cost stays hidden until someone tries to extend the code, which is why the test belongs in pre-production.","a":"Has anyone added a new feature to the code we plan to reuse?","q":"“We were able to rewrite some crucial portions, but continued to get bitten by this inefficiency throughout production.”","qa":"Gabe Cinquepalmi, project director at Double Fine","st":"The chilling tale of the development of Costume Quest 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/the-chilling-tale-of-the-development-of-i-costume-quest-2-i-#:~:text=We%20were%20able%20to%20rewrite,inefficiency%20throughout%20production.","sd":"Oct 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"crackdown","n":"Crackdown","dv":"Realtime Worlds","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Pushed on from a fragile first playable without matching staff to scope","s":"A few milestones later the project was out of control and tens of coders short; a full audit, deep cuts and a slip of about 2 years followed.","w":"Crackdown's first playable went down well but was held together with paperclips and sticking plasters. A couple of milestones later the production team stopped short-term work and ran a full project audit; the coders still to hire numbered in the tens. In early 2004 the move to the next Xbox left the team 15 to 20 people short, and Microsoft would not fund more until progress showed. Stakeholders added ideas in the scope talks; deep cuts were approved, some crept back. Spring 2005 became spring 2006, then May, August and October 2006. Release came on 20 February 2007.","r":"Shipped about 2 years late after a 4-year development and a long crunch.","re":"Ran a full project audit, triaged scope against time and staff, and cut deep.","co":"About 2 years of slip, 15 to 20 people short, crunch far too long.","nf":false,"l":"When the first playable is a show build, audit staffing and scope against the plan before accepting the next milestone.","i":"A demo that wows hides the gap between what was shown and what the team can build, and pressure for visible progress delays the reckoning. Wilson says the stakeholders then added ideas instead of cutting, so an audit works only if someone can say no.","a":"Do we have the people to build what the demo promised?","q":"“We got a couple of milestones past our first playable demo (which went down really well but was held together with paperclips and sticking plasters) before feeling the sinking sensation that the project was out of control.”","qa":"Phil Wilson, producer of Crackdown at Realtime Worlds","st":"Postmortem: Realtime Worlds' Crackdown","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-realtime-worlds-i-crackdown-i-#:~:text=We%20got%20a%20couple%20of%20milestones,was%20out%20of%20control","sd":"Oct 2007, reprinted Sep 2009","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Retargeted the game from Xbox to the next Xbox in early 2004","s":"The move added about a year to the plan. Criterion dropped the RenderWare version the game relied on, and the team ended up rebuilding the middleware itself.","w":"Crackdown was prototyped on PC and aimed at Xbox for spring 2005. In early 2004 it was proposed that the game aim for the next Xbox; the team reckoned it was 15 to 20 people short, and the deadline moved to spring 2006 because another platform would take roughly another year. Criterion did not carry RenderWare Graphics 3.7 across, so the team moved to an early beta of 4.0 that lacked features it relied on. New versions broke compatibility, so the team stopped taking them and rebuilt it in-house. The game shipped on 20 February 2007 after 4 years.","r":"About a year added, and the core middleware broke mid-project.","re":"Stopped taking RenderWare updates and rebuilt the middleware in-house.","co":"About 1 extra year planned; team 15 to 20 people short.","nf":false,"l":"Before you switch platforms, check that every middleware package supports the new target, and plan to replace any that does not.","i":"A platform change moves every dependency at once, and middleware vendors decide which versions survive. Wilson says that with proper checking, replacing RenderWare with in-house tech would have been the right call.","a":"Does every middleware we use support the new platform today?","q":"“In hindsight, we realize that we simply did not adequately investigate the suitability and potential pitfalls of this new version of Renderware.”","qa":"Phil Wilson, producer of Crackdown at Realtime Worlds","st":"Postmortem: Realtime Worlds' Crackdown","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-realtime-worlds-i-crackdown-i-#:~:text=In%20hindsight%2C%20we%20realize%20that,this%20new%20version%20of%20Renderware","sd":"Oct 2007, reprinted Sep 2009","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Chose RenderWare middleware to make up for a small team on a console project","s":"Criterion dropped the version the team used, EA bought Criterion and support dried up, and the asset pipeline stayed far slower than Unreal Engine 3 to the end.","w":"Realtime Worlds began Crackdown with a small team and trouble hiring, so it took middleware wherever possible, above all Criterion's RenderWare Graphics and Studio. The target moved from PC to Xbox, back to PC, then through Xenon alpha and beta to Xbox 360. At the Xenon shift Criterion dropped RenderWare 3.7, so the team took an unfinished 4.0 beta. EA then bought Criterion and onsite support dried up. Testing one asset could take over an hour. The game shipped on 20 February 2007 after 4 years; in October 2007 Wilson wrote the right call would have been in-house tech.","r":"Pipeline stayed far slower than Unreal Engine 3 and could not be fixed mid-project.","re":"Stopped taking new RenderWare versions, rebuilt it in-house and limped to the finish.","co":"Asset tests of an hour or more; the platform switch added about a year.","nf":false,"l":"Before you bet a multi-year project on middleware, check the vendor's roadmap, ownership risk and whether its tools scale to your data volume.","i":"Middleware saves a small team early, but the team then depends on the vendor's roadmap and owner. Wilson says they never properly investigated the new version; the lesson is about checking, not about avoiding middleware.","a":"What happens to our pipeline if the middleware vendor drops our version?","q":"“Criterion opted not to support Renderware Graphics 3.7 in the transition, forcing us to move to an early beta version of 4.0.”","qa":"Phil Wilson, producer of Crackdown at Realtime Worlds","st":"Postmortem: Realtime Worlds' Crackdown","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-realtime-worlds-i-crackdown-i-#:~:text=Criterion%20opted%20not%20to%20support,beta%20version%20of%204.0","sd":"Oct 2007, reprinted Sep 2009","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"crackdown3","n":"Crackdown 3","dv":"Sumo Digital","y":"2019","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built Crackdown 3 around Azure cloud-powered, fully destructible multiplayer","s":"Creative director Joseph Staten said the cloud technology largely drove the schedule and took longer than planned. Announced at E3 2014, the game shipped in February 2019.","w":"Microsoft announced Crackdown 3 at E3 2014, with Sumo Digital, original developer Reagent Games and cloud specialist Cloudgine sharing the work. Its core feature was multiplayer destruction powered by Azure servers, shown at Gamescom 2015. The game slipped several times. By June 2018, Xbox head Matt Booty said only Sumo was still on the project, after Epic bought Cloudgine. In November 2018, Staten said the team knew the tech was a heavy lift and it still took longer than planned. Crackdown 3 launched on 15 February 2019, with destruction in the Wrecking Zone multiplayer mode.","r":"Cloud destruction work drove the schedule; the game shipped nearly 5 years after announcement.","re":"Microsoft used the extra time for a deeper campaign story and more polish.","co":"Nearly 5 years from E3 2014 announcement to February 2019 release.","nf":false,"l":"Before you announce a game built on new server technology, prove it at full scale in a playable build.","i":"A feature that depends on new infrastructure sets the schedule for the whole game, because content cannot be finished until the tech is. Staten said the wait also bought campaign polish, so the delay was not pure loss.","a":"Have we run our new tech at full scale before announcing a date?","q":"“To a large extent, the project schedule has largely been driven by that technology investment.”","qa":"Joseph Staten, creative director at Microsoft Studios","st":"Discussing Crackdown 3 delays, design vision, and a dream of Crackdown 4","su":"https://www.windowscentral.com/design-vision-crackdown-3-multiplayer-campaign-and-more#:~:text=To%20a%20large%20extent%2C%20the,by%20that%20technology%20investment","sd":"Nov 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"crashbandicoot","n":"Crash Bandicoot","dv":"Naughty Dog","y":"1996","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Chose a branching rail camera so visibility and sorting could be precomputed","s":"The levels could draw many more polygons than a free camera allowed. It also meant $100,000 SGI workstations for programmers and overnight level builds, so each change took at least 12 hours to see.","w":"In summer 1994, Andy Gavin and Jason Rubin picked a 3D character action game for the new Sony PlayStation. Experiments with a free-roaming camera led them to a branching rail camera with visibility and polygon sorting computed ahead of time. That choice moved the whole tool pipeline onto SGI workstations, at $100,000 each instead of $3,000 PCs. Levels took hours to process on a 5 to 8 machine farm, so each change took at least 12 hours to see. Crash Bandicoot shipped in 1996, and the four PS1 games sold about 35 to 40 million copies.","r":"Every programmer needed a $100,000 SGI, and level changes took 12+ hours to see.","re":"Gavin wrote tools that spread level processing across office machines, 24 hours a day.","co":"$100,000 per SGI workstation instead of $3,000 per PC; 12+ hours per level change.","nf":false,"l":"When hardware limits drive your camera choice, price the tools and build times it needs before you lock it.","i":"A fixed camera lets you compute ahead of time what a free camera must compute at runtime, so the hardware draws more. The cost moves into tools and build time. It paid here because the look was the selling point.","a":"Do we know what our camera choice costs in tools and build time?","q":"“This meant buying programmers $100,000 SGIs instead of $3,000 PCs.”","qa":"Andy Gavin, co-founder of Naughty Dog","st":"Making Crash Bandicoot – part 3","su":"https://all-things-andy-gavin.com/2011/02/04/making-crash-bandicoot-part-3/#:~:text=This%20meant%20buying%20programmers%20%24100%2C000,instead%20of%20%243%2C000%20PCs","sd":"Feb 2011","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"crimsondesert","n":"Crimson Desert","dv":"Pearl Abyss","y":"2026","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/3321460/","ls":"Steam","cv":"crimsondesert.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Built the open world quest by quest, with each quest defining its area","s":"Every change to a quest goal meant rebuilding environments and content. Production cycles grew longer until the team switched to building a playable world first.","w":"Crimson Desert was Pearl Abyss's first single-player game, made over about 7 years by a team of about 200. In early development the team worked quest-first: design a quest, build the environment it needed, then add content around it. At open-world scale this broke down. Production cycles grew longer, dependencies between teams piled up, and changed quest goals often meant rebuilding environments and content. The team switched to world-first, with quests placed on a playable world built in parallel. The game launched on 19 March 2026 and passed 5 million sales in under a month.","r":"Environments and content were rebuilt whenever a quest goal changed.","re":"Switched to world-first: built a playable world layer first, quests on top.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Decide in pre-production whether the world or the quest line sets each region's layout, before environment artists start building.","i":"When quests define regions, every story change travels down to environments and content, so teams wait on each other. World-first cut that chain at a price: the speakers said it left the main story less cohesive.","a":"If a main quest changes, how many areas must be rebuilt?","q":"","qa":"","st":"「紅の砂漠」は約200人でどう作られたのか。クエストではなく世界から先に作る「ワールドファースト」と，全社共通のXMLが支えた大規模オープンワールド開発［CEDEC 2026］","su":"https://www.4gamer.net/games/484/G048495/20260729039/#:~:text=%E9%96%8B%E7%99%BA%E5%88%9D%E6%9C%9F%EF%BC%8C%E3%83%81%E3%83%BC%E3%83%A0%E3%81%AF%E3%80%8C%E3%82%AF%E3%82%A8%E3%82%B9%E3%83%88%E3%83%95%E3%82%A1%E3%83%BC%E3%82%B9%E3%83%88%E3%80%8D%E3%81%A7%E9%80%B2%E3%82%81%E3%81%A6%E3%81%84%E3%81%9F","sd":"Jul 2026","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"crossyroad","n":"Crossy Road","dv":"Hipster Whale","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/crossy-road/id924373886?uo=4","ls":"App Store","cv":"crossyroad.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Designed Crossy Road as a 12-week experiment monetised only by opt-in video ads","s":"The game launched in November 2014, had 10 million downloads in its first month and took $10 million in its first 90 days, $7 million of it from rewarded video.","w":"Matt Hall says Crossy Road would not have existed without rewarded advertising. After seeing Flappy Bird earn from banner ads and Disco Zoo make opt-in videos fun, he and Andy Sum set out in 2014 to make a game monetised only through opt-in video ads plus a few purchasable characters. The plan was 6 weeks; the experiment took 12. It launched in November 2014, hit 10 million downloads in a month and entered the top-grossing charts. At GDC 2015 Hall opened the books: $10 million in 90 days. In 2017 he added that $7 million came from rewarded video.","r":"$10 million in 90 days from a 12-week build.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"12 weeks of development; 2 developers and an artist; budget not disclosed.","nf":false,"l":"Pick the monetisation model before you design the game, then build a time-boxed experiment around it instead of a long production.","i":"Building for opt-in ads only meant every player mattered, which pushed the design toward retention and short sessions rather than paying conversion. Hall credits the model for the result; it rests on a game that is fun to replay, not on the ads.","a":"Which monetisation model is our design built around, and does every player matter?","q":"“That was literally the reason we wrote Crossy Road was to make the game with rewarded advertising in it.”","qa":"Matthew Hall, co-founder of Hipster Whale","st":"Please, Sir, I Want Some More... Ads | Matthew Hall","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jOpprYT9WZI&t=189s","sd":"Jun 2017","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4}]},{"k":"cultistsimulator","n":"Cultist Simulator","dv":"Weather Factory","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/718670/","ls":"Steam","cv":"cultistsimulator.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Kept scope and budget small and locked a 31 May 2018 release date early","s":"The 2-person studio shipped on the date, spent £142k (the original budget plus one deliberate £10k increase), and sold over 40k copies in 2 weeks.","w":"Alexis Kennedy mocked up a JavaScript prototype in early 2017 to make sure the core loop was fun. Weather Factory, 2 people full-time, asked for £30,007 on Kickstarter with no complicated rewards; it was funded in 12 hours and ended at 273% with nearly 4,800 backers. The team committed early to a 31 May 2018 release and sat under a ticking clock for the 11-month project, meeting every deadline. The release build left out some of the fun stuff. 2 weeks after launch they had sold over 40k copies, against a pessimistic year-one hope of 10k and an optimistic 30k.","r":"Shipped on the date, on budget plus one planned £10k increase.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"£142k total, 11 months, 2 people full-time plus 10 freelancers.","nf":false,"l":"Prototype the core loop before raising money, then set scope so break-even is a small number of copies.","i":"A fixed date and a small budget forced choices early, so no postponed work piled up and launch needed no big cuts. The price was content left for post-launch. It works when break-even is low, as for 2 people; not when the game needs volume.","a":"How many copies do we need to break even?","q":"“We probably could have squeezed more cash out of a Kickstarter if we’d set more complicated stretch goals, but I’m very glad we didn’t.”","qa":"Lottie Bevan, Weather Factory","st":"Cultist Simulator: the retrospective","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/cultist-simulator-the-retrospective#:~:text=We%20probably%20could%20have%20squeezed,very%20glad%20we","sd":"Jul 2018","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"cuphead","n":"Cuphead","dv":"Studio MDHR","y":"2017","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/268910/","ls":"Steam","cv":"cuphead.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Brought back the cut platforming levels after the E3 2015 demo","s":"The founders quit their jobs, remortgaged their houses and grew the team from 3 to 20. The game shipped in September 2017 and sold 1 million copies in under 2 weeks.","w":"Chad and Jared Moldenhauer started Cuphead in 2010 as a 3-person team working weekends, with a few bosses and a cut scope. It was revealed at E3 2014 and playable at E3 2015, where feedback asked for more than boss fights. After that show, the brothers chose to build their original dream scope with full levels. They quit their jobs, remortgaged their houses and grew the studio to 20 people. Every frame stayed hand-drawn, so one new enemy attack could take over 30 frames. Cuphead shipped on 29 September 2017.","r":"The team grew from 3 to 20 people, and the game shipped in 2017.","re":"Every new idea went through team vetting, because each one meant hundreds of frames.","co":"Founders' jobs and remortgaged houses; team grew from 3 to 20 people.","nf":false,"l":"Before you grow scope, price one new enemy attack in frames and days, then multiply by what the new plan needs.","i":"Hand-drawn animation makes every added idea cost hundreds of frames, so the scope jump became a staffing jump. It worked here because the 2015 demo showed real demand and the controls and engine were already built.","a":"How many frames and days does one new enemy attack cost us?","q":"“My brother and I quit our jobs, remortgaged our houses and began expanding the team.”","qa":"Chad Moldenhauer, co-creator and lead designer at Studio MDHR","st":"\"We quit our jobs, remortgaged our houses\" - how relentless passion made Cuphead a reality","su":"https://www.gamesradar.com/we-quit-our-jobs-remortgaged-our-houses-how-relentless-passion-made-cuphead-a-reality/#:~:text=My%20brother%20and%20I%20quit,began%20expanding%20the%20team","sd":"Jun 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"cyberpunk2077","n":"Cyberpunk 2077","dv":"CD Projekt Red","y":"2020","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1091500/","ls":"Steam","cv":"cyberpunk2077.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned a technically harder game than The Witcher 3 in the same timeframe","s":"Staff expected to finish in 2022, not April 2020. The game slipped three times and launched in December 2020 in a state that got it pulled from the PlayStation Store.","w":"CD Projekt announced Cyberpunk 2077 in 2012, but full development started only in late 2016, when the project was essentially reset. The engine, mostly new, was built alongside the game. At one meeting a developer asked how a technically harder game would fit The Witcher 3's timeframe, and the answer was to figure it out along the way. In June 2019 the studio announced an April 2020 release; staff expected 2022. After three delays the game launched on 10 December 2020, and Sony pulled it from its store. Bloomberg reported this in January 2021.","r":"Three delays, then a launch Sony pulled from the PlayStation Store.","re":"CD Projekt apologised and spent the next months on fixes and updates.","co":"Release slipped 8 months to December 2020; shares fell 30% after launch.","nf":false,"l":"Estimate the new game's schedule from its own tasks; do not reuse the last game's timeframe when tech and scope change.","i":"A timeframe copied from the last hit assumes the old team's speed. A new engine, a new camera and twice the staff each slow work down. On a sequel with the same tech and team, a reused timeframe can hold.","a":"Did we estimate this schedule from our own tasks, or copy the last one?","q":"“Someone answered: ‘We’ll figure it out along the way.’”","qa":"Adrian Jakubiak, former audio programmer at CD Projekt","st":"Inside CD Projekt’s Cyberpunk 2077 launch fiasco","su":"https://techcentral.co.za/inside-cd-projekts-cyberpunk-2077-launch-fiasco-2/172856/#:~:text=Someone%20answered%3A%20%E2%80%98We%E2%80%99ll%20figure,out%20along%20the%20way","sd":"Jan 2021","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Showed an E3 2018 gameplay demo before the underlying systems were coded","s":"Developers told Bloomberg the demo cost months that should have gone into the game. Features it showed, such as car ambushes, were missing at launch.","w":"Full development started in late 2016. In June 2018 CD Projekt showed a gameplay slice at E3: a mission and a tour of Night City. Fans and press were impressed, but the gameplay systems behind it were not yet finalised or coded. The game launched in December 2020 without some of what the demo showed, such as car ambushes. In January 2021 Bloomberg reported that developers saw the demo as a waste of months. Studio head Adam Badowski answered that a demo 2 years before launch is a test of vision, not a fake.","r":"Months of work went into a demo whose features did not all ship.","re":"In 2021 CD Projekt said it would show only polished footage, closer to launch.","co":"Months of developer time, by staff accounts.","nf":true,"l":"Show only features whose systems already run in the build, and track every shown feature through to launch.","i":"A demo built ahead of the systems is a promise with no code behind it. Each shown feature that is later cut turns into a broken promise at launch. An internal slice faked to set direction carries no such public cost.","a":"Does every feature in this demo run on real game systems today?","q":"“If you look at that demo now, it's different yes, but that's what the 'work in progress' watermark is for.”","qa":"Adam Badowski, head of CD Projekt Red","st":"CD Projekt studio boss refutes reporting about Cyberpunk 2077 development","su":"https://www.pcgamesinsider.biz/news/71861/cd-projekt-studio-boss-refutes-reporting-about-cyberpunk-2077-development/#:~:text=If%20you%20look%20at%20that,watermark%20is%20for","sd":"Jan 2021","sy":"Participant account","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Built for high-end PCs first and left old-console versions to adapt later","s":"The PS4 and Xbox One versions launched in December 2020 far below expectations. Sony pulled the game from its store with full refunds, and a US investor class action settled for USD 1.85 million.","w":"CD Projekt's core assumption was to make Cyberpunk 2077 look epic on PCs and then adjust it to consoles, especially the old ones. Early on this did not look hard, Marcin Iwiński said later. The streaming system that feeds the dense city kept needing work for old-gen disk bandwidth. After 3 delays the board kept the 10 December 2020 date and ignored signals that base consoles needed more time. Console review keys went out on 8 December. On 18 December Sony removed the game from PlayStation Store and offered full refunds.","r":"Base-console versions shipped way below expectations, and Sony pulled the game from its store.","re":"The team spent the next months patching the last-gen console versions first.","co":"Full PlayStation Store refunds; USD 1.85 million class action settlement.","nf":false,"l":"Make the weakest target console a playable gate early; if the slice fails there, drop the platform or move the date.","i":"Building for the strongest machine first means the hardest platform gets tested last, when no time is left to fix it. A dense, loading-free city hits old-console disk streaming hardest; a smaller or level-based game has more room to adapt.","a":"Have we played a full slice on our weakest target hardware yet?","q":"“We knew the hardware gap, yes, but ultimately, I think that time has proven that we've underestimated the task.”","qa":"Marcin Iwiński, co-founder and joint CEO of CD Projekt","st":"Cyberpunk 2077 — Our Commitment to Quality","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O3V4UBZmC9o&t=108s","sd":"Jan 2021","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"daikatana","n":"Daikatana","dv":"ION Storm","y":"2000","sc":"AAA","se":"Re-release 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/242980/","ls":"Steam","cv":"daikatana.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Staffed Daikatana with first-time developers recruited online, not industry veterans","s":"More than half of ION Storm's roughly 85 staff left within a year, and almost the entire Daikatana team quit in November 1998. Romero later blamed the game's bugs on that hiring.","w":"ION Storm started in Dallas in summer 1996 with $13 million, and on Christmas Eve 1996 signed a six-title, $22 million Eidos deal with a 40% royalty. Romero found most of his Daikatana staff on the internet, and most had never made a game. In spring 1997 the partners spent over $2 million fitting out offices atop Chase Tower, while the studio missed Eidos milestones. By January 1999 ION had burned through $26 million at nearly $900,000 a month, and almost the whole Daikatana team had quit. The game shipped in May 2000, after veterans had replaced the original staff.","r":"Most of the original Daikatana team left, and veterans replaced them.","re":"ION Storm replaced the departed staff with experienced game developers.","co":"$26 million spent by January 1999, at nearly $900,000 a month.","nf":false,"l":"Before you staff up, put people who have shipped games in every key programming and design seat.","i":"Shipping takes skills that level-design passion does not teach, and a $900,000 monthly burn makes every learning month expensive. This does not rule out newcomers; it rules out a team where nobody in the key seats has shipped a game.","a":"Has someone in each key seat shipped a game before?","q":"“If I would have been hiring people who had been in the industry, everything would have been different.”","qa":"John Romero, co-founder of ION Storm","st":"The life and times of John Romero, gaming's original rockstar - part 3: Nightmares","su":"https://www.pcgamesn.com/quake/john-romero-interview-part-3#:~:text=If%20I%20would%20have%20been,would%20have%20been%20different","sd":"Dec 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"darkageofcamelot","n":"Dark Age of Camelot","dv":"Mythic","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.darkageofcamelot.com/","ls":"Website","cv":"darkageofcamelot.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Reused the Spellbinder engine and Darkness Falls server code for a new online RPG","s":"Mythic's producer says the reuse saved at least a year. The game went live in October 2001 with 5 programmers on a team of about 25.","w":"In late 1999 Mythic decided to make a 3D version of Darkness Falls: The Crusade, its text-based online RPG. Early research showed an upgraded Spellbinder engine could be the client and the Darkness Falls server code the back end. That server also came with its database of objects, monsters and weapons. Full production began around January 2000, funded for exactly 18 months. By summer 2000 the team had about 25 people, 5 of them programmers. Dark Age of Camelot went live on October 9, 2001 and sold 51,000 boxes in its first 4 days.","r":"Saved at least a year and cut the programming staff needed.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Saved at least 1 year of development; needed only 5 programmers.","nf":false,"l":"Before you plan new technology, list which client, server and content pieces from your last game can carry the new one.","i":"The new game kept the 3-team design of Darkness Falls, so the old client, server and data fit it. The saving stopped where the design needed new tech: dungeons and cities came late, and the game launched with 3 capital cities and about 15 dungeons.","a":"Which parts of our last game's code can this game ship on?","q":"“Having such a solid client and server right at the start - with associated client/server messaging - alone saved us at least a year of development.”","qa":"Matt Firor, producer at Mythic Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Mythic Entertainment's Dark Age of Camelot","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-mythic-entertainment-s-i-dark-age-of-camelot-i-#:~:text=Having%20such%20a%20solid%20client,at%20least%20a%20year%20of%20development.","sd":"Feb 2002","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Dropped Oracle for free MySQL after a license quote above $900,000","s":"Mythic avoided the license fee and kept account and character data on MySQL under Linux. After a 90-minute outage on launch night, the servers stayed stable.","w":"Mythic had run its servers on Linux since its early games and had deep Linux experience in-house. For Dark Age of Camelot it first planned to keep account and character data in Oracle. Oracle quoted a license fee of more than $900,000, and the team switched to MySQL, a free Linux-based database. The game went live on October 9, 2001. A database configuration problem took it down for about 90 minutes on the first night, and it stayed up after that. In February 2002 producer Matt Firor wrote that open source had saved the studio a pile of money.","r":"The studio avoided a license fee of more than $900,000.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Avoided a quoted license fee of more than $900,000.","nf":false,"l":"Get written license quotes for each server component before you lock the back end, and price a free alternative beside each one.","i":"A free option pays off only when the team can run it. Mythic had years of Linux server work behind it, so MySQL cost little to adopt; a team without that experience may spend the saving on support time.","a":"Do we have real license quotes for every part of our server stack?","q":"“It's saved us a pile of money and has been stable and reliable.”","qa":"Matt Firor, producer at Mythic Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Mythic Entertainment's Dark Age of Camelot","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-mythic-entertainment-s-i-dark-age-of-camelot-i-#:~:text=It%27s%20saved%20us%20a%20pile,been%20stable%20and%20reliable.","sd":"Feb 2002","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"darksector","n":"Dark Sector","dv":"Digital Extremes","y":"2008","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/29900/","ls":"Steam","cv":"darksector.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Reshaped the sci-fi Dark Sector pitch into a grounded modern-day shooter to land a publisher","s":"The signed game looked almost nothing like the pitch, and the team spent production searching for its gameplay while also building a new engine. It shipped in March 2008 to above-average reviews.","w":"Digital Extremes pitched Dark Sector, a hardcore sci-fi game, from 2004; creative director Steve Sinclair spent almost a year pitching while publishers asked for a present-day setting and said sci-fi would not sell. With its runway running out, the studio dropped the sci-fi, went more like Resident Evil and signed. The released game turned the masked space soldier into a CIA agent and, in Sinclair's words, suffered from building new IP and a new engine at once while the team searched for its gameplay. It shipped in March 2008 to above-average reviews. The concept returned as Warframe in 2012.","r":"Production spent searching for the gameplay; the shipped game barely resembled the pitch.","re":"Revived the original sci-fi concept as the free-to-play Warframe from 2012.","co":"Almost a year of pitching; no budget or team figures reported.","nf":false,"l":"If you reshape a concept to win a publisher, re-prototype the core loop on the new concept before you sign.","i":"The pitch video sold a world, not a proven loop, so once the world changed there was nothing proven to build on, and a new engine added more unknowns. A pivot can work when the core gameplay survives it; here that part was still open.","a":"Does our prototype still prove the fun after the publisher's changes?","q":"“The game that became Dark Sector suffered from trying to build new IP, at the same time as building an engine from scratch.”","qa":"Steve Sinclair, creative director at Digital Extremes","st":"Closing Digital Extreme's Psychic Wound","su":"https://www.giantbomb.com/articles/closing-digital-extreme-s-psychic-wound/1100-4555/#:~:text=The%20game%20that%20became%20Dark%20Sector,an%20engine%20from%20scratch","sd":"Feb 2013","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"darksouls","n":"Dark Souls","dv":"FromSoftware","y":"2011","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2018","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/570940/","ls":"Steam","cv":"darksouls.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Turned the prototype's showcase map into the Painted World when it would not fit","s":"The map the team had spent a long time on for the prototype still made it into the game. It shipped as the Painted World, the game's only snow area, apart from the main map.","w":"FromSoftware built its Dark Souls prototype around one map, the team's chance to show its vision for the game. Director Hidetaka Miyazaki said they spent a long time on it, and Gravelord Nito was first made as that map's boss. Miyazaki wanted the map in the full game but could not make it fit with the other areas. He put it in the Painted World instead; artist Masanori Waragai noted it was the only snow area. Dark Souls came out in Europe on October 7, 2011. Miyazaki told the story in the 2012 Design Works interview.","r":"A long-built prototype map shipped in the game as a separate world.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When a prototype map will not join the main world, look for a self-contained slot for it before you scrap it.","i":"Dark Souls joins its areas directly to each other, so a map built on its own had no natural place. A separate world kept the work, which only helps when the fiction allows an area cut off from the rest.","a":"Where could our prototype map live if it does not connect?","q":"“Of course, the prototype is your chance to really get across your vision for the game so we spent a long time on the area.”","qa":"Hidetaka Miyazaki, director of Dark Souls at FromSoftware","st":"Dark Souls Design Works","su":"https://www.barnardtranslations.com/post/dark-souls-design-works#:~:text=Of%20course%2C%20the%20prototype%20is,long%20time%20on%20the%20area","sd":"2012, translated Jan 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"darkestofdays","n":"Darkest of Days","dv":"8monkey Labs","y":"2009","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built a custom engine from day one with a first-time team of four","s":"Nearly a year passed before the engine was usable, and engine upkeep kept pulling programmers off game code. The studio got its own tech in exchange for a slower schedule.","w":"8monkey Labs began its Marmoset engine on the studio's first day, with a core team of four who had never made a game. Two engineers built it cheaply, but nearly a full year passed before it was usable, and gameplay prototyping was held back by it. In production, engineers kept getting pulled into engine maintenance, and a late Xbox 360 port forced a second renderer. Darkest of Days shipped on September 8, 2009, on a $1.7M budget. In November 2009 Jeff Russell wrote that a third-party engine would have shaved significant time from the schedule.","r":"The first year went mostly into engine work, not gameplay.","re":"8monkey kept Marmoset as its technology base for future projects.","co":"Nearly 1 year before the engine was usable; built by 2 engineers.","nf":false,"l":"If your team has never shipped a game, license an engine and spend the first year on gameplay.","i":"An engine built alongside its first game competes with that game for the same few programmers, so every engine bug delays content. The trade can still pay off for a studio that plans several games on the same tech.","a":"Would a licensed engine get us to playable gameplay sooner?","q":"“Nearly a full year had passed by before the engine was full-featured and usable.”","qa":"Jeff Russell, 8monkey Labs","st":"Postmortem: 8Monkey's Darkest of Days","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-8monkey-s-i-darkest-of-days-i-#:~:text=Nearly%20a%20full%20year%20had,was%20full%2Dfeatured%20and%20usable","sd":"Nov 2009","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"darksiders","n":"Darksiders","dv":"Vigil Games","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"Remaster 2016","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/50620/","ls":"Steam","cv":"darksiders.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Designed four-player co-op and two worlds each bigger than the final game","s":"Co-op went early, but the Underworld stayed in the design until late production. Work on several areas was thrown away, and artists and designers lost heart.","w":"Vigil Games was a first-time studio making Darksiders for THQ. The design called for four-player co-op with all four Horsemen, plus an Overworld and an Underworld, each larger than the final game. The team did not cut scope hard in pre-production and built several areas at once to show the vision. Co-op went early; the Underworld stayed until late in production and was then cut, wasting much of that work. The game shipped in January 2010. In June 2010 production director Timothy Bell traced the problem to not knowing the amount of work or the hiring rate.","r":"Much of the work on cut areas never reached players.","re":"Management resolved to make scope decisions as early as possible.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Price the full design against your real hiring rate in pre-production, and cut whole sections before anyone builds them.","i":"A first-time studio has no data on its own pace or hiring speed, so the design grows to fit hopes. Building several sections at once to show the vision then turns every later cut into lost work.","a":"Have we cut the sections we cannot staff before building any of them?","q":"“We should have adjusted our scope more aggressively during pre-production.”","qa":"Timothy Bell, production director at Vigil Games","st":"Postmortem Highlights: Behind The Scenes of Darksiders","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/postmortem-highlights-behind-the-scenes-of-i-darksiders-i-#:~:text=We%20should%20have%20adjusted%20our,more%20aggressively%20during%20pre%2Dproduction","sd":"Jun 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"dayofthetentacle","n":"Day of the Tentacle","dv":"LucasArts","y":"1993","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2016","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/388210/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dayofthetentacle.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Cut the playable characters from 6 to 3 before production started","s":"Letting players pick a team from 6 characters, as in Maniac Mansion, meant too much work across all the combinations. The 1993 game shipped with 3 friends working together.","w":"Tim Schafer and Dave Grossman co-led Day of the Tentacle at LucasArts, a sequel to Maniac Mansion. The first plan copied that game: 6 playable characters, and the player picks a team. Before production started, the team decided that all the possible team combinations meant far too much work, and cut the cast to 3. Chester was reused as Ned and Jed Edison, while Razor and Moonglow were dropped. The game came out in 1993 with 3 friends who work together. In May 2025 Grossman gave the cut as his example of what the team removed.","r":"The character-combination work was cut before any of it was built.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before production, count the character combinations each puzzle must support, and cut characters until that count fits your team.","i":"With a pickable team, every puzzle has to work for every possible group, so the work grows with the combinations rather than the cast. A fixed trio removes that branching, at the price of the replay variety team picking gives.","a":"How many character combinations must each of our puzzles support?","q":"“Originally we were going to have six playable characters, and you’d pick a team, just like in Maniac Mansion, but before production even started we decided that was way too much work with all the combinations, and we cut it down to three.”","qa":"Dave Grossman, co-designer of Day of the Tentacle at LucasArts","st":"A conversation with Dave Grossman","su":"https://spillhistorie.no/2025/05/23/a-conversation-with-dave-grossman/#:~:text=Originally%20we%20were%20going%20to,cut%20it%20down%20to%20three","sd":"May 2025","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"daysgone","n":"Days Gone","dv":"Bend Studio","y":"2019","sc":"AAA","se":"Port 2021","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1259420/","ls":"Steam","cv":"daysgone.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Took a handheld studio into a high-fidelity open-world game on PS4","s":"Bend had to grow from a 50-person team to more than 130, hiring artists, designers and engineers. Garvin called growing the studio the right way a challenge.","w":"Bend Studio's last big game was handheld: Uncharted: Golden Abyss, a PS Vita launch title. For Days Gone, writer and director John Garvin wanted a dynamic open world with high visual fidelity, where Freaker hordes roam and hibernate. To build it, Bend grew from a 50-person team to more than 130, hiring artists, designers and engineers. Garvin said growing the studio in the right way was a challenge. Asked about the game's delays, he called the cycle generous for a game this big. Days Gone came out on PS4 on April 26, 2019.","r":"The studio more than doubled in size to build the open world.","re":"Bend focused on hiring the right artists, designers and engineers.","co":"Team grew from 50 to over 130 people.","nf":false,"l":"Before you pitch an open world at a new visual bar, count the hires it needs and plan when they arrive.","i":"Fidelity sets the cost of each asset and the open world sets how many assets there are, so the two multiply into headcount. Garvin's figure is team size only; it gives no budget or schedule.","a":"How many people does our world size at this visual bar need?","q":"“Days Gone is certainly an extension of that, and yeah, we’ve had to grow from a 50 man team to over 130 now, and just hire the right people, like really talented artists and designers, engineers and so on.”","qa":"John Garvin, writer and director of Days Gone at Bend Studio","st":"Interview: John Garvin on surprising players with Days Gone’s story and open world","su":"https://www.thesixthaxis.com/2019/03/06/interview-john-garvin-on-surprising-players-with-days-gones-story-and-open-world/#:~:text=Days%20Gone%20is%20certainly%20an,engineers%20and%20so%20on","sd":"Mar 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"dayz","n":"DayZ","dv":"Bohemia Interactive","y":"2018","sc":"AA","se":"Early Access 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/221100/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dayz.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Greenlit a standalone DayZ as the mod went viral, with no time to plan","s":"A few developers in a room had to add 20 to 30 staff and a new office, while ownership and revenue split were still unsettled. The standalone sold over 3 million copies in its first Early Access year.","w":"In 2012 Dean Hall put DayZ online as an Arma 2 mod, expecting about 100 players. It reached 500,000 users in 5 months and 1 million a month later. At 20,000 users Bohemia's CEO asked for a standalone product; a week later there were 100,000 and the plan had to change. A few developers in a room became a team adding 20 to 30 staff, with ownership and revenue split still unsettled. The standalone entered Early Access in December 2013 and sold over 3 million copies in a year. Hall left Bohemia in late 2014; 1.0 shipped in December 2018.","r":"Strategy changed week to week; the standalone stayed in Early Access until December 2018.","re":"Bohemia added staff, a new office and, by 2013, a 10-year strategy.","co":"Adding 20 to 30 staff and a new office in a short time.","nf":false,"l":"Before you greenlight a standalone from a viral mod, settle ownership, revenue split and the hiring plan in writing.","i":"A viral hit forces decisions faster than a studio can plan them, so the gaps already there (who owns it, who gets paid, who gets hired) become the crisis. Lawyers, Hall said, guard against failure, not success. It does not argue against a fast greenlight.","a":"If this prototype goes viral tomorrow, who owns it and who gets paid?","q":"“The biggest thing I learned about going viral is there was no time to plan.”","qa":"Dean Hall, project lead of DayZ at Bohemia Interactive","st":"Surviving a viral outbreak, DayZ style","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/production/surviving-a-viral-outbreak-i-dayz-i-style#:~:text=The%20biggest%20thing%20I%20learned,no%20time%20to%20plan","sd":"Sep 2013","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Forked the DayZ standalone from an old engine branch to reach Early Access fast","s":"Bohemia hit that engine's limits and built a new one, Enfusion, while players were already in the game. Early Access ran 5 years, and the lead producer blamed the delay mostly on building game and engine together.","w":"Bohemia started the DayZ standalone on a fork of the Take On Helicopters branch of its RV engine, because Arma 3's branch was still changing. The aim was a working prototype, and that version went into Early Access in December 2013. The team soon hit the engine's limits. In June 2014 Dean Hall announced a new engine, later called Enfusion: a complete rip out of everything. In May 2018 lead producer Eugen Harton said the game was taking longer than intended, mostly because game and engine were built together. DayZ 1.0 shipped on 13 December 2018.","r":"Early Access lasted 5 years, and 1.0 shipped without some expected features.","re":"Bohemia formed a separate engine team to build Enfusion for its future games.","co":"5 years in Early Access, December 2013 to December 2018.","nf":false,"l":"Pick the engine the full game needs before you open Early Access, not the one that ships a prototype fastest.","i":"An engine chosen for a quick prototype sets limits the full game will hit. Replacing it after launch means rebuilding systems while paying players wait for features. If the prototype engine can carry the full design, the fast start costs nothing later.","a":"Can this engine carry the full game, or only the prototype?","q":"“A lot of it comes down to us not really communicating our development plans clearly at times, but yes, DayZ is certainly taking longer than we had originally intended – mostly because of our effort to develop the game along with making a new engine for it.”","qa":"Eugen Harton, lead producer at Bohemia Interactive","st":"DayZ Interview: ‘We Certainly Did Not Take The Money And Run Off’","su":"https://gamingbolt.com/dayz-interview-we-certainly-did-not-take-the-money-and-ran-off#:~:text=A%20lot%20of%20it%20comes,a%20new%20engine%20for%20it","sd":"May 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"deadcells","n":"Dead Cells","dv":"Motion Twin","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2017","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/588650/","ls":"Steam","cv":"deadcells.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Dropped the free-to-play tower defense alpha and kept the single-player event prototype","s":"Instead of a cancellation, the team had a game players liked. Dead Cells sold over 700,000 copies in Early Access before its August 2018 full release.","w":"Three or four years before a 2018 interview, Motion Twin started a free-to-play tower defense game as a spiritual sequel to Hordes, also known as Die2Nite. The alpha was shown to people and was not fun, so the team considered cancelling it. A friend said its single-player prototype, made for events like Gamescom, was more fun. The team cut multiplayer, mobile and free-to-play and built a Castlevania-style game. Dead Cells entered Steam Early Access in May 2017 and launched in full in August 2018; the developer said Early Access held about 40 percent of the final content.","r":"The project changed genre and business model instead of being cancelled.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"The tower defense alpha and its F2P, mobile and multiplayer plans were dropped.","nf":true,"l":"Before you cancel an unfun project, playtest its side prototypes and keep the one people enjoy.","i":"An event demo has to be fun within minutes, so it can show the core better than the main build. The pivot worked because the team dropped the F2P, mobile and multiplayer plans with it instead of keeping the old plan.","a":"Is any of our side prototypes more fun than the main build?","q":"“We removed a lot of elements from this old side-scrolling tower defense, including multiplayer, mobile and free-to-play elements and it led to this Castlevania kind of game that we made.”","qa":"Sébastien Bénard, lead developer and game designer, Motion Twin","st":"When We Made... Dead Cells","su":"https://mcvuk.com/development-news/when-we-made-dead-cells/#:~:text=We%20removed%20a%20lot%20of,game%20that%20we%20made","sd":"Nov 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"deadisland2","n":"Dead Island 2","dv":"Dambuster Studios","y":"2023","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/934700/","ls":"Steam","cv":"deadisland2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Handed the Dead Island sequel to outside studio Yager under the publisher's vision","s":"After 3 years the two companies' visions fell out of alignment and Deep Silver dropped Yager. Two more studios followed; the game that shipped in April 2023 was started from scratch in 2018.","w":"Deep Silver announced Dead Island 2 at E3 2014 with Yager as developer. In April 2015 the game slipped to 2016 because it had not reached the goal set for it. On 15 July 2015 Deep Silver pulled Yager off the title; Yager's managing director Timo Ullmann said the two companies' visions had fallen out of alignment. Yager Productions, the division built for the game, closed. Sumo Digital took over in March 2016 and was gone by 2019. Dambuster Studios took the project in 2018, started from scratch, and shipped it on 21 April 2023.","r":"Three studios and 9 years from reveal to release.","re":"Deep Silver moved the game to Sumo, then to its own Dambuster studio, which restarted.","co":"3 years of Yager work discarded; division closed, over half its staff lost their jobs.","nf":false,"l":"Before an outside studio starts a sequel, write down the vision both sides sign, and recheck it at every milestone.","i":"When the publisher owns the vision and an outside team owns the build, drift shows up late, and the late call is what costs the work. The risk is split ownership, not outsourcing itself.","a":"Have the publisher and the studio signed the same vision document for this game?","q":"“Yager and Deep Silver's respective visions of the project fell out of alignment.”","qa":"Timo Ullmann, managing director of Yager","st":"The messy 9-year development of Dead Island 2","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/the-messy-9-year-development-of-dead-island-2/#:~:text=Yager%20and%20Deep%20Silver%27s%20respective,fell%20out%20of%20alignment","sd":"Sep 2022","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Moved the troubled sequel to an internal studio and let it rebuild from scratch","s":"Dead Island 2 shipped nine years after its announcement and became Deep Silver and Plaion's biggest launch by units and revenue, with over 2 million sold by late May 2023.","w":"Deep Silver announced Dead Island 2 in 2014 with Yager as developer. The two parted ways in July 2015 after about three years of Yager's work, and Sumo Digital took over in March 2016. In 2018 the project went to Deep Silver's own Dambuster Studios, and game director David Stenton later said the game was built from scratch. In 2019 Koch Media's CEO said the company wanted to be judged on the final result. Dead Island 2 launched on April 21, 2023, and reached 10 million players by October 2024.","r":"The game was rebuilt from scratch and shipped in April 2023.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"About 3 years of Yager work and Sumo's tenure replaced by a rebuild.","nf":false,"l":"When an outside build no longer matches your vision, restart pre-production with your own team and give it time.","i":"An owned studio can rebuild without a contract clock, and the publisher sets the vision directly. It worked for a strong brand that could afford to wait; it does not show that every late restart earns back the years already spent.","a":"Does the external build still match our vision for this game?","q":"“It is rewarding to see that the decision to give [Dambuster Studios] time to polish the game has paid off.”","qa":"Lars Wingefors, co-founder and CEO of Embracer Group","st":"Dead Island 2 sales top two million","su":"https://www.gematsu.com/2023/05/dead-island-2-sales-top-two-million#:~:text=It%20is%20rewarding%20to%20see,the%20game%20has%20paid%20off","sd":"May 2023","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"deadstate","n":"Dead State","dv":"DoubleBear Productions","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/239840/","ls":"Steam","cv":"deadstate.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Chose Torque 3D for an RPG for its low cost and programmer familiarity","s":"The engine had no RPG tools, so the team built its own and features such as Perks and Traits slipped. It also made Mac, Linux and console ports hard.","w":"Dead State started in late 2009 as a part-time project and went full-time after a June 2012 Kickstarter raised about $300,000. The team used Torque 3D for the lead programmer's familiarity and its low cost, but it lacked native RPG tools. Plans to reuse Iron Tower's tools gave way to tweaking or building new ones, each needing design, programming and QA time. Tool snags often delayed Perks and Traits. The game slipped a year from December 2013, reaching Early Access in February 2014 and full release on 4 December 2014. Mitsoda later called staying on Torque a big mistake.","r":"Tool building delayed major features such as Perks and Traits.","re":"DoubleBear plans to rely more on out-of-box systems in future projects.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you lock the engine, list the genre's core tools, such as dialogue, stats and saves, and check the engine has them.","i":"An engine without genre tools turns every feature into a tools project first, and a team of 8 to 10 pays for that twice. Familiarity still saves time when the engine fits; the trap is choosing on familiarity and price alone.","a":"Which core tools for our genre does our engine lack today?","q":"“Sticking with Torque 3D instead of switching to Unity early on was a big mistake, and the lack of ability to easily port the game impacts our ability to profit off of potential future ports.”","qa":"Brian Mitsoda, creative director of DoubleBear Productions","st":"Postmortem: DoubleBear's Dead State - Controlling scope in an RPG","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-doublebear-s-i-dead-state-i---controlling-scope-in-an-rpg#:~:text=Sticking%20with%20Torque%203D%20instead,off%20of%20potential%20future%20ports","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"deadlypremonition","n":"Deadly Premonition","dv":"Access Games","y":"2010","sc":"AA","se":"Expanded edition 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/247660/","ls":"Steam","cv":"deadlypremonition.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Entered production on next-gen consoles with loose control of memory use","s":"RAM ran short for the rest of the project. Motion data alone took a huge share of memory and forced the team to restructure systems late on.","w":"Access Games began Deadly Premonition in early fall 2004, its first next-gen project. The team was amazed by how much RAM the new consoles had, grew overconfident and let data management slip. It went into production unaware of the risk. RAM then ran short throughout: motion data took a huge chunk of memory and forced system restructuring later on, and outdoor scenes held more objects than the game could process. Constant optimization across the code cost a huge amount of working time. The game came out in 2010, and that August the postmortem called memory management a failure.","r":"The team spent the project fighting for RAM and restructuring systems.","re":"The team optimized code and collision data right up to the end.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before production starts, give each type of content a memory budget on the target hardware and track it every build.","i":"Plenty of RAM hides early waste, so the bill arrives when content fills the memory. The point is budgets per content type; it does not mean starting with tight limits that block early experiments.","a":"Does each type of content have a memory budget yet?","q":"“Attempting to work on memory allocation in such a state was very dangerous, but we went into production unaware of the risk.”","qa":"Wataru Nishide and Hideki Kataoka, lead level artist and lead programmer at Access Games","st":"Classic Postmortem: Deadly Premonition","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-i-deadly-premonition-i-#:~:text=Attempting%20to%20work%20on%20memory,unaware%20of%20the%20risk","sd":"Aug 2010, reprinted Aug 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Built the asset data formats around real-time lighting, with no light baked into textures","s":"Dozens of real-time lights choked the renderer. Because the data assumed them, the fix meant changing almost all the game's resources and settling for softer shadows than the team wanted.","w":"With pixel shaders on the new consoles, the Deadly Premonition team tried flat lighting, point lights and spot lights, all in real time. It based its data formats on that plan and did no lighting with textures. Interiors such as the diner were lit with dozens of point and spot lights, and sharp outdoor shadows ate VRAM. The team spent long stretches testing shadow-map techniques. In the end it compromised on shadows and had to modify almost all its resources. The August 2010 postmortem said the team did not yet have the technical know-how.","r":"Almost all resources had to be modified, and the sharp shadows were compromised.","re":"The team tested several shadow-map techniques and reworked its resources.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you lock asset formats, light one full interior and one outdoor scene the way you plan to, and check the frame rate.","i":"A data format that assumes one lighting method turns a rendering fix into an asset rework. The risk is that coupling; real-time lighting itself is fine once a heavy scene has proved it early.","a":"Have we lit our heaviest scene before locking the asset formats?","q":"“Even worse, we based our data formats on the assumption that we would be implementing these different lighting techniques as is, so there were absolutely no lighting effects done with textures; this had a huge impact on later code revisions.”","qa":"Wataru Nishide and Hideki Kataoka, lead level artist and lead programmer at Access Games","st":"Classic Postmortem: Deadly Premonition","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-i-deadly-premonition-i-#:~:text=Even%20worse%2C%20we%20based%20our,impact%20on%20later%20code%20revisions","sd":"Aug 2010, reprinted Aug 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Ran physics through PhysX on everything that could move, down to fishing rods","s":"Physics fought the scripted cheats the game needed. Approved scenes broke whenever animation changed, and the load forced the team to cut PhysX actors and tune each scene by hand.","w":"At the start of development, programmer Yutaka Ohkawa was convinced PhysX would build a never-before-seen world. It drove vehicles, object interaction and hair, and the team ran clothing and fishing rods through it too. Physics then clashed with the cheats that demo scenes and vehicle navigation needed. Character physics changed whenever motion data changed, so approved scenes broke after animation tweaks. The processing load was much higher than expected. To hold the frame rate, the team cut PhysX actors and tuned the load scene by scene, which took a lot of time. The game came out in 2010.","r":"Approved scenes kept breaking, and each scene needed its own physics tuning.","re":"The team cut PhysX actors and adjusted the physics load per scene.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before production, list the objects that really need simulated physics, and script or animate everything else.","i":"Games need cheats and physics will not allow them: a simulated prop does not stay where a cutscene puts it. Each simulated object also ties its behavior to animation and frame budget, so every change means retesting.","a":"Which of our simulated objects would players miss if we scripted them?","q":"“Physics behavior can certainly bring dynamic expression to game worlds, but we learned that it isn’t necessary for everything.”","qa":"Yutaka Ohkawa, programmer at Access Games","st":"Classic Postmortem: Deadly Premonition","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-i-deadly-premonition-i-#:~:text=Physics%20behavior%20can%20certainly%20bring,necessary%20for%20everything","sd":"Aug 2010, reprinted Aug 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"deathloop","n":"Deathloop","dv":"Arkane Lyon","y":"2021","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1252330/","ls":"Steam","cv":"deathloop.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Locked the time loop to 4 periods, 8 targets and 1 way to finish","s":"The team never broke the rules. Game director Dinga Bakaba said they were the only reason Deathloop survived pre-production, because time-loop games explode in complexity.","w":"Arkane had watched its earlier games, the Dishonored series and Prey, explode in complexity. Early in Deathloop's development the team found that a time-loop game needs strict scope control, or it explodes. It set fixed rules: 4 time periods, 8 Visionaries as targets and 1 golden loop to reach the true ending. The single golden loop also kept the design from growing too complex. The team never deviated from those rules. In May 2021 Bakaba said they were the only reason the project survived pre-production. Deathloop launched on PS5 and PC on September 14, 2021.","r":"The project survived pre-production, by its director's account.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Where content multiplies, write hard numeric limits in pre-production and refuse every change that breaks them.","i":"In a time loop each period, target and route can meet every other, so work grows with the combinations. Fixed counts cap that growth. They only work if nobody reopens them; a limit that bends at every milestone caps nothing.","a":"Which numbers in our design are fixed, and who can change them?","q":"“I think that the only reason this project was able to survive the preproduction phase is because we did set those rules and never deviated.”","qa":"Dinga Bakaba, game director at Arkane Lyon","st":"Deathloop is a puzzle, says Game Director Dinga Bakaba","su":"https://stevivor.com/features/interviews/deathloop-is-a-puzzle-says-game-director-dinga-bakaba/#:~:text=I%20think%20that%20the%20only,rules%20and%20never%20deviated","sd":"May 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"defcon","n":"DEFCON","dv":"Introversion Software","y":"2006","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1520/","ls":"Steam","cv":"defcon.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built a rough whole-game prototype with LAN multiplayer in a week from reused code","s":"Multiplayer worked from the start, and production stayed a two-programmer job of under a year on a £50,000 budget.","w":"During Darwinia's long development, lead developer Chris Delay set out to build an entire multiplayer game in 24 hours, inspired by the 1983 film WarGames. He took code from Introversion's other games, including Darwinia's network architecture, and finished a rough prototype within a week, with multiplayer working over LAN. He went back to Darwinia, and Introversion later hired Gary Chambers to help finish DEFCON. Programming took 2 people a little less than a year, and early multiplayer games helped refine the experience. DEFCON shipped on 29 September 2006.","r":"The team played multiplayer early and moved faster than writing networking from scratch.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"£50,000 (about $95,000), 2 full-time developers, 12 months.","nf":false,"l":"Before you staff up, build a throwaway prototype of the whole loop, multiplayer included, from code you already have.","i":"Reused network code got multiplayer running in days, so the team tuned the real experience from the start instead of building plumbing. The lesson depends on having code to reuse; a studio without its own tech cannot build this prototype in a week.","a":"Can we play the whole loop, multiplayer included, in a rough build?","q":"“I stole code from all of our other games in development and built a very rough prototype as quickly as I could.”","qa":"Chris Delay, lead developer at Introversion Software","st":"Detonating Introversion's DEFCON","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_December_2006.pdf#:~:text=I%20stole%20code%20from%20all,as%20quickly%20as%20I%20could","sd":"Dec 2006","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4,"sh":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_December_2006.pdf#page=28"}]},{"k":"deltarune","n":"Deltarune","dv":"Toby Fox","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1671210/","ls":"Steam","cv":"deltarune.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Searched for an engine to replace GameMaker before building the next chapters","s":"About 99% of the development work from March 2019 went into the search. The team went back to GameMaker and started Chapter 2 on Chapter 1's base in May 2020.","w":"Toby Fox released Chapter 1 of Deltarune, made in GameMaker, on Halloween 2018. Development of the rest started around March 2019, and about 99% of that work went into investigating engines other than GameMaker. Fox kept designing in parallel and finished outlines for every chapter. In 2020 he decided to go back to GameMaker, which still felt like the best fit. Using Chapter 1 as a base, Fox and about 3 active team members started Chapter 2 in May 2020. In September 2020 he wrote that he did not regret the lost time.","r":"More than a year of development work ended on the original engine.","re":"The team restarted on GameMaker, using Chapter 1 as the base.","co":"About 99% of development work from March 2019 until the 2020 switch back.","nf":false,"l":"Set a deadline and a pass test for any engine search before it starts, and stop when the deadline arrives.","i":"An engine search has no natural end: each candidate raises new questions, and the known engine keeps looking safe. Fox lost little because design was his bottleneck and kept moving. A team whose programmers set the pace pays the full time.","a":"When will we stop testing engines and lock one?","q":"“Development started around March 2019 and a 99% work was spent on investigating engines alternate to GameMaker, which I used for Chapter 1.”","qa":"Toby Fox, creator of Deltarune","st":"DELTARUNE Status Update - Sept 2020","su":"https://deltarune.com/update-092020/#:~:text=Development%20started%20around%20March%202019,which%20I%20used%20for%20Chapter%201","sd":"Sep 2020","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"destiny","n":"Destiny","dv":"Bungie","y":"2014","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.playstation.com/en-us/concept/200959","ls":"PlayStation","cv":"destiny.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Prototyped gameplay on Halo Reach through stand-in APIs to port to the new engine","s":"The engine layers under the scripts arrived out of order, so each replaced stand-in forced another rewrite. Bungie wrote 4 script compilers between 2008 and 2014.","w":"Bungie began the Tiger engine for Destiny in mid 2008 as a branch of Halo Reach code. In August 2008 engineers built a script environment for gameplay experiments on Reach, with temporary APIs to swap for Tiger code later. The engine pieces under the scripts arrived out of order, so replacing one shim often meant rewriting another. In 2011 the old prototypes stopped running on the new renderer and were dropped. Bungie wrote script compilers in 2008, 2010, 2012 and 2014. At GDC 2015 engineering director Chris Butcher said such shim debt costs 10 to 15 times what you expect.","r":"Bungie wrote 4 script compilers, and scripting shipped with slow iteration.","re":"Deprecated the old prototypes in 2011 and moved gameplay to final APIs in 2012.","co":"4 script compilers in 6 years; debt put at 10 to 15 times expected.","nf":false,"l":"Build on a stand-in API only if you could ship that stand-in as is; otherwise help finish the real system or wait for it.","i":"Each shim hides assumptions about data, lifetime and performance, and every layer built on it inherits them, so the bill arrives late. The lesson covers code meant to survive: Bungie's time-boxed engine prototypes, planned for the bin, did work.","a":"Could we ship each placeholder API we are building on today?","q":"“We always felt we were a lot closer to a shippable system than we really were.”","qa":"Chris Butcher, engineering director at Bungie","st":"Learning from the Core Engine Architecture of Destiny","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/gdc2015/presentations/Butcher_DestinyEngine_GDC2015_final.pdf#page=62","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant talk","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Signed a fixed four-game release schedule with Activision before the story was locked","s":"The first game slipped a year from its contract date, and the July 2013 story rewrite had to fit Activision's calendar. Sources told Kotaku the unwavering schedule caused many of Destiny's problems.","w":"Bungie's contract with Activision took effect on 16 April 2010, years before much of the game was conceived. It set four games: the first on Xbox 360 in fall 2013, sequels in 2015, 2017 and 2019, and 'Comet' expansions every other year to 2020. The first game slipped from September 2013 to March 2014. After the July 2013 story reboot, Bungie had to negotiate with Activision for September 2014. After launch, one source said, a team crunched out the first DLC in 9 weeks.","r":"Destiny shipped in September 2014, a year after its contract date.","re":"Bungie asked Activision to replace later DLC packs with microtransactions.","co":"One-year slip; first DLC crunched out in 9 weeks, one source said.","nf":false,"l":"Lock and test the first game's story structure before you sign fixed release dates.","i":"A fixed cadence leaves no room for a reboot, so a late story rewrite becomes a stitching job instead of a redesign. The risk sits in whatever is unsettled at signing; a studio with a proven structure and tools can live with fixed dates.","a":"Is our story structure locked before we sign these release dates?","q":"“A lot of the problems that came up in Destiny 1, and that happened in development of The Taken King, are results of having an unwavering schedule and unwieldy tools,”","qa":"Unnamed Bungie source, quoted by Kotaku","st":"The Messy, True Story Behind The Making Of Destiny","su":"https://kotaku.com/the-messy-true-story-behind-the-making-of-destiny-1737556731#:~:text=A%20lot%20of%20the%20problems,schedule%20and%20unwieldy%20tools","sd":"Oct 2015","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built a new engine and toolset alongside the game itself","s":"Kotaku's sources said the engine handled matchmaking and graphics well, but its tools slowed designers: a tiny map change took an overnight import plus about 40 minutes.","w":"After Halo: Reach in 2010, Bungie made Destiny on a new engine, Tiger, built alongside the game from a 2008 branch of the Halo: Reach code. Its GDC 2015 talk described a 6-year engine development arc from inception to ship. Destiny launched on 9 September 2014. In 2015, four sources told Kotaku the tools held development back. Moving one resource node meant an 8-hour overnight map import, 20 minutes to open the map and a 15 to 20 minute compile. Sources called the engine one of Bungie's fundamental problems as expansions kept being rescoped.","r":"A half-second map edit took a night plus about 40 minutes.","re":"Bungie kept evolving the engine's asset pipeline for Destiny 2.","co":"Overnight map imports and 15 to 20 minute compiles for tiny edits.","nf":false,"l":"Before content production starts, time a designer's edit-to-test loop on the new tools and fix it if it takes hours.","i":"Every hour in the edit loop is paid again on every map, mission and patch, so slow tools tax all later content. A new engine can still pay off in rendering and networking; the check is the designers' loop, not the feature list.","a":"How long does a small map change take to test today?","q":"“It takes eight hours to input their map overnight.”","qa":"Unnamed source familiar with the engine, quoted by Kotaku","st":"The Messy, True Story Behind The Making Of Destiny","su":"https://kotaku.com/the-messy-true-story-behind-the-making-of-destiny-1737556731#:~:text=It%20takes%20eight%20hours,their%20map%20overnight","sd":"Oct 2015","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Story & world","t":"Scrapped the writers' story in July 2013 and rebuilt it from existing missions","s":"Bungie needed a 6-month extension from Activision and moved the Dreadnaught to an expansion. Writer Joe Staten left during the reboot, and reviewers singled out the story at launch.","w":"By mid-2013 Joe Staten's writing team had built a 2-hour 'supercut' of the story. In July, six sources told Kotaku, project lead Jason Jones and senior leadership found it too campy and linear and scrapped it against Staten's wishes. A small 'Iron Bar' group spent 2 weeks on a new, less linear plot, then stitched existing missions and cinematics into a new campaign. Staten left that summer, and the planned March 2014 launch moved to September 2014. Destiny shipped on 9 September 2014 and scored 76 on Metacritic, against internal surveys of about 90.","r":"Shipped missions were cut and stitched together from the old story.","re":"Bungie hired a new slate of writers after launch.","co":"6-month delay; Metacritic 76 against an internal forecast of about 90.","nf":false,"l":"Review the full story cut with leadership in pre-production, before missions are built around it.","i":"Leadership judged the whole story only after missions and cinematics existed, so a rejection meant cutting up content that had cost millions of dollars. An early rough cut is cheap, but it only helps if the people who can veto it watch it.","a":"Has our project lead watched the full story cut yet?","q":"“It was literally like making Franken-story.”","qa":"Unnamed Bungie source, quoted by Kotaku","st":"The Messy, True Story Behind The Making Of Destiny","su":"https://kotaku.com/the-messy-true-story-behind-the-making-of-destiny-1737556731#:~:text=It%20was%20literally%20like%20making,Franken%2Dstory","sd":"Oct 2015","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"destiny2","n":"Destiny 2","dv":"Bungie","y":"2017","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1085660/","ls":"Steam","cv":"destiny2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Story & world","t":"Reorganised leadership and rebooted Destiny 2's story 16 months before launch","s":"Kotaku's sources said the April 2016 reset under Luke Smith aimed for a true sequel that left old planets behind. Veterans left, Activision studios were added, and the game launched in September 2017 to record sales.","w":"In April 2016 Bungie reorganised: Luke Smith became director of Destiny 2 and Mark Noseworthy its executive producer, and the story written so far was rebooted. Several veteran staff left around then. In September 2016 Kotaku reported that leadership wanted a proper sequel, even if old planets, characters and activities were left behind, with most of Bungie's 750-plus staff and Activision's High Moon and Vicarious Visions on it. In July 2017 Activision's Eric Hirshberg said he had not been happy with Destiny 1's content cadence. Destiny 2 launched on 6 September 2017 with the year's biggest console launch week.","r":"A new story and sequel built in about 16 months with outside studios.","re":"Activision added High Moon and Vicarious Visions to keep content flowing.","co":"Story written before April 2016 discarded; several veteran staff left.","nf":true,"l":"Before you reset a sequel's story and leadership, write down what ships in the months left and what gets cut.","i":"A reset 16 months out leaves no slack for a 750-person team on tools that already made content slow, so the publisher bought capacity with extra studios. Kotaku's sources describe the reset; Bungie did not comment, so the reasons behind it are reported, not confirmed.","a":"If we reset the story now, what ships in the months we have left?","q":"“We got a lot right with Destiny 1, but one of the things we didn't do was keep up with the demand for new content.”","qa":"Eric Hirshberg, CEO of Activision Publishing","st":"\"Destiny 1 Didn't Release New Content Fast Enough,\" Says Activision CEO","su":"https://twinfinite.net/news/destiny-1-didnt-release-new-content-fast-enough-says-activision-ceo/#:~:text=We%20got%20a%20lot%20right,demand%20for%20new%20content","sd":"Jul 2017","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Built Destiny 2 as a boxed sequel, assuming players would leave between expansions","s":"Players who ran out of content stayed and turned the community against the game. In February 2018, at the rate players were leaving, Bungie's math said the population would be gone in 5 weeks.","w":"Destiny 1 was still a live service when Bungie, committed to its publisher, set out to ship a full sequel. The team's razor was to put a two on the box: a better campaign, faster rewards, wider appeal. Destiny 2 launched in 2017 with reviews more than 10 points above Destiny 1. About 6 weeks later, hardcore players ran out of content, and casual players followed. They stayed online and warned others off. By February 2018 weekly players were falling fast. The Forsaken expansion, about a year after launch, rebuilt the endgame.","r":"By February 2018, Bungie feared it was a month from shutting Destiny 2.","re":"Bungie worked on trust, then retention, then revenue, and rebuilt the endgame with Forsaken.","co":"Almost a year rebuilding trust; multi-million dollar losses from pulling microtransactions.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock a sequel's direction, plan what your most engaged players will do once the content runs out.","i":"Bungie still judged success like a box product, by reviews and polish, while a live game lives on engagement. Players who finish a live game do not quietly leave; they stay and talk. The lesson is for live games, not one-off releases.","a":"What will our most engaged players do the week they run out of content?","q":"“Our primary razor during development was that we wanted to put a two on the box.”","qa":"Justin Truman, general manager of Destiny 2 at Bungie","st":"From Box Products to Live Service: How 'Destiny 2' Transformed Bungie","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZLbvMWEAoyY&t=147s","sd":"2022","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Added Adventures and Lost Sectors as smaller activities next to Strikes","s":"Bungie could not build content as fast as players finished it. The two smaller formats cost less to develop than a Strike and gave the team more activities to offer.","w":"On Destiny, Bungie learned that players finished content faster than the studio could build it, even at its best pace. For Destiny 2 the team kept Strikes and added two smaller activity types. Adventures take players deeper into a destination's story. Lost Sectors send them into a cave to kill a strong enemy and take his loot. In a June 2017 E3 interview, PC lead David Shaw said both cost less to develop than a Strike. The PC version was due in October 2017, about 6 weeks after the consoles.","r":"Bungie could offer more activities than Strikes alone allowed.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Price each activity type before you plan the content list, and add cheaper formats where players burn through content fastest.","i":"Players finish a flagship activity far faster than a team can build one, so cost per activity sets how much there is to play. Cheaper formats add volume next to Strikes; they do not replace the flagship activities.","a":"Which of our activity types gives the most play for its build cost?","q":"“These are things that are not as full-bodied as a Strike in the cost it takes to develop them, but they’re still really rewarding and they give us opportunities to give you more.”","qa":"David Shaw, PC lead at Bungie","st":"Destiny 2’s developers on balancing for mouse and keyboard, content droughts, and poledancing","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/destiny-2s-developers-on-balancing-for-mouse-and-keyboard-content-droughts-and-poledancing/#:~:text=These%20are%20things%20that%20are,opportunities%20to%20give%20you%20more","sd":"Jun 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"deusex","n":"Deus Ex","dv":"Ion Storm Austin","y":"2000","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/6910/","ls":"Steam","cv":"deusex.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned more than 25 missions and a Christmas 1998 release during preproduction","s":"The date was never possible. After 2 missions became playable in May 1999, the team cut the design document from over 500 pages to 270 and shipped in June 2000.","w":"A team of about 6 spent the first 6 months in preproduction. They planned more than 25 missions from New York to the Moon, over 200 characters and a raid freeing 2,000 prisoners, with Christmas 1998 as the target. By March 1998 the design ran to 300 pages, and by April 1999 to more than 500. In May 1999 the first two missions were playable start to finish. That showed what really worked, and the document was cut to 270 pages. The White House, large outdoor areas and multiplayer were dropped. Deus Ex shipped on 23 June 2000.","r":"Large parts of the design were cut and the 1998 date was missed.","re":"The team cut the design down to what the playable missions proved worked.","co":"Shipped June 2000 against a Christmas 1998 target; design cut from 500+ to 270 pages.","nf":false,"l":"Cost the mission list against your team size and renderer before you set a release date.","i":"A big budget and no outside deadline removed the pressure that forces cuts during preproduction. The paper design kept growing until playable missions showed what the engine and the team could carry.","a":"Can our team and engine build every mission on the list by that date?","q":"“A really big budget, no external time constraints, and a marketing budget bigger than any of us had ever had before made us soft.”","qa":"Warren Spector, director of Deus Ex at Ion Storm Austin","st":"Postmortem: Ion Storm's Deus Ex","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-ion-storm-s-i-deus-ex-i-#:~:text=A%20really%20big%20budget%2C%20no%20external,before%20made%20us%20soft","sd":"Dec 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Scheduled the project around playable proto-missions and built the riskiest systems first","s":"Early playable missions exposed slow large maps and dull skill and augmentation systems. Lead designer Harvey Smith then reworked both systems.","w":"Spector built the schedule around proto-missions: missions playable start to finish that capture the whole game. The May 1998 milestone had rough conversation, inventory, skills and augmentations, plus test maps of the White House and Hong Kong, the hardest map first. Both maps ran too slowly, so maps were split up and the White House was later cut. In May 1999 the first two missions were playable. Gabe Newell of Valve, then friends from Looking Glass and Irrational, found no tension in skills and augmentations. Harvey Smith redesigned both around consumable resources and energy costs before the June 2000 release.","r":"Core system flaws surfaced in May 1999, over a year before release.","re":"Harvey Smith reworked skills and augmentations, and large maps were split up.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Make an early milestone one mission playable start to finish, and have developers from outside the team play it.","i":"Systems that read well on paper show their weak spots only in play, and outsiders spot missing tension faster than the team. A proto-mission checks only what is in it: Spector left NPC AI out and found its problems late.","a":"Has anyone outside the team played a full mission yet?","q":"“None of this would have happened without prototype missions and some harsh (but fair) criticism they allowed.”","qa":"Warren Spector, director of Deus Ex at Ion Storm Austin","st":"Postmortem: Ion Storm's Deus Ex","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-ion-storm-s-i-deus-ex-i-#:~:text=None%20of%20this%20would%20have,criticism%20they%20allowed","sd":"Dec 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Left NPC AI out of the early prototypes and designed it in a vacuum","s":"Early AI features proved unnecessary once the design settled, and NPC behavior kept changing until the last day of development. Warren Spector said the game delivered human-like AI inconsistently.","w":"Ion Storm Austin spent the first 6 months of Deus Ex in preproduction with a team of about 6. The team started thinking about NPC AI then, before anyone knew what the game needed from it. In May 1998 the first proto-missions had conversation, inventory, skills and augmentations hacked in, but no AI prototype. The AI grew on Unreal Tournament's shooter code, and the team tweaked it to the end. Deus Ex shipped on June 23, 2000. That December, Spector wrote that the team was weighing a patch with radical AI changes.","r":"AI tweaking ran right up to the final day of development.","re":"The team discussed a post-release patch with radical AI changes.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Put every risky system, including AI built on licensed code, into the first playable test map.","i":"AI designed away from real levels guesses at what the game needs, so early features go to waste and the real work lands late. Here the damage stayed limited: Spector still called the final AI compelling.","a":"Is our riskiest system running in a real level yet?","q":"“I should have insisted on early prototyping of our AI but I didn't.”","qa":"Warren Spector, director of Deus Ex at Ion Storm Austin","st":"Postmortem: Ion Storm's Deus Ex","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-ion-storm-s-i-deus-ex-i-#:~:text=I%20should%20have%20insisted%20on,early%20prototyping%20of%20our%20AI","sd":"Dec 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Licensed the Unreal engine and tools instead of building an in-house engine","s":"Three programmers carried the whole project. Learning the engine took much of the time it saved, and shooter-built AI and pathfinding stayed in the game.","w":"After about 6 months of preproduction, Ion Storm Austin licensed Unreal and later built on the Unreal Tournament code. The deal let Spector run the project with 3 programmers and larger design and art teams. Roughly 6 to 9 months of experimenting passed before the team really started working with the engine. Nobody knew the code well enough to change it freely, so the team built on Unreal's shooter AI, pathfinding and sound propagation. Deus Ex shipped in June 2000 with Direct3D performance issues the team could not safely fix. Spector still planned to license engines for his next projects.","r":"A 3-programmer team, but months went into learning someone else's engine.","re":"Spector planned to license engines again for Deus Ex 2 and Thief 3.","co":"About 6 to 9 months before the team worked productively in Unreal.","nf":false,"l":"Before signing an engine licence, list the systems built for another genre that you will have to rewrite.","i":"A licensed engine removes the engine team but not the learning curve, and code nobody on the team wrote feels too risky to change. The saving holds for content work and shrinks when your genre needs different AI.","a":"Which engine systems were built for a different genre than ours?","q":"“However, to my surprise, licensing technology didn't save us all the time I'd hoped it would.”","qa":"Warren Spector, director of Deus Ex at Ion Storm Austin","st":"Postmortem: Ion Storm's Deus Ex","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-ion-storm-s-i-deus-ex-i-#:~:text=However%2C%20to%20my%20surprise%2C%20licensing,hoped%20it%20would","sd":"Dec 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Created two design teams with their own leads instead of naming one lead designer","s":"The design group was split from the start. Spector merged the two teams under a single lead designer, later than he says he should have.","w":"Spector started with a core of 6 developers from Looking Glass's Austin office. Two experienced designers then arrived expecting to lead. Instead of choosing one, he gave each a team: a traditional RPG group and an immersive sim group. He hoped the friction would produce a synthesis for a genre-busting game. The design team was fragmented from the start, and the groups ended up named Design Team 1 and Design Team A because neither would accept 2 or B. Spector later merged them under a single lead designer. His December 2000 postmortem put this first under What Went Wrong.","r":"Design work split between two camps until a late merge.","re":"Spector merged both groups under a single lead designer.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Name one lead designer before production starts, even if the other candidate then walks away.","i":"Two leads with different philosophies each defend their own version of the game, so every design call turns into a negotiation. Competing ideas still help when one person has the final say.","a":"Who has the final say on design?","q":"“Instead of doing the sensible thing and picking one of them, even if that meant the other chose not to sign on, I got cute.”","qa":"Warren Spector, director of Deus Ex at Ion Storm Austin","st":"Postmortem: Ion Storm's Deus Ex","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-ion-storm-s-i-deus-ex-i-#:~:text=Instead%20of%20doing%20the%20sensible,sign%20on%2C%20I%20got%20cute","sd":"Dec 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"deusexhumanrevolution","n":"Deus Ex: Human Revolution","dv":"Eidos Montreal","y":"2011","sc":"AAA","se":"Expanded edition 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/238010/","ls":"Steam","cv":"deusexhumanrevolution.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built the whole game on paper in three months of daily cross-discipline meetings","s":"The blueprint let the team plan about 80% of content precisely and showed what to cut. When part of Hengsha had to go late, the story was reworked in about 3 weeks.","w":"Eidos Montreal started Human Revolution in early 2007 with a core team of four. After a concept phase of about five months, it ran the blueprint: three months of meetings from 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. every day with writers, level designers, level artists, game designers, the art director and the game director. They built each mission around the story points and broke down enemies, environments, dialogues and cutscenes. During development the team used the blueprint daily and relied on it to cut the Bangalore hub and part of Hengsha. The game shipped on August 23, 2011.","r":"The team planned about 80% of content up front and cut cleanly.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"3 months of daily all-day meetings across writing, level, art and design.","nf":false,"l":"Before production, map every mission's enemies, environments, dialogues and cutscenes with writers and designers in the same room.","i":"Counting content per mission turns the story into a build list, so the team sees early what it cannot afford and where cuts hurt least. It did not solve everything: boss fights and cutscenes still ran into trouble the paper plan missed.","a":"Can we list every mission's content counts before production starts?","q":"“In a nutshell, the blueprint process was our way of building the game on paper before starting any real production.”","qa":"Jean-François Dugas, game director at Eidos Montreal","st":"Postmortem: Deus Ex: Human Revolution","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_January_2012.pdf#:~:text=In%20a%20nutshell%2C%20the%20blueprint,starting%20any%20real%20production","sd":"Jan 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4,"sh":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_January_2012.pdf#page=24"},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Left the planned boss fights unstaffed while the small team built core systems","s":"A year in, the bosses had not moved past design points. Late outsourcing and a new AI system cut them down to typical fights that players called frustrating.","w":"Early in the project the team planned boss fights to celebrate choice, combat versus stealth, and lethal versus non-lethal play. It flagged the need for a boss team, but all resources went to core mechanics. About a year in, nothing existed beyond design points, and an inexperienced level designer became owner without programming or animation support. After two years in production only basics for one of four bosses existed, so a Montreal AI company built two on an aggressive schedule. A new internal AI system then made those mostly incompatible, and after the 2011 release players called the bosses frustrating.","r":"Bosses were scaled back twice and ended up inconsistent with the game.","re":"Two bosses went to an outside AI company; two stayed in-house.","co":"About 1 year with no progress, then two rounds of re-scoping.","nf":false,"l":"Give every pillar feature an owner, programming and animation support, and a prototype during pre-production.","i":"A feature that touches every pillar depends on systems that keep changing, so starting it late means building on moving ground. Late outsourcing made it worse: the contractor's bosses broke when the internal AI changed.","a":"Does every pillar feature have an owner and a prototype today?","q":"“Nevertheless, about a year into the project, no progress beyond these design points had been made.”","qa":"Jean-François Dugas, game director at Eidos Montreal","st":"Postmortem: Deus Ex: Human Revolution","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_January_2012.pdf#:~:text=Nevertheless%2C%20about%20a%20year%20into,design%20points%20had%20been%20made","sd":"Jan 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4,"sh":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_January_2012.pdf#page=27"}]},{"k":"device6","n":"Device 6","dv":"Simogo","y":"2013","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/device-6/id680366065","ls":"App Store","cv":"device6.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Chose Unity for a puzzle game told through text laid out as maps","s":"Unity's font rendering looked poor in a game made of words. Simogo wrote its own column alignment, edited the font, and switched to a text plugin mid-project.","w":"After Year Walk, Simogo came up with text maps: the words are the path the player travels. A team of 6 built DEVICE 6 in Unity over 6 months. Unity's font rendering looked poor, so the team wrote its own column alignment and, early on, edited the font so it would not look awful. In the middle of the project it dropped Unity's font rendering for a plugin. The game launched on iOS on 17 October 2013. On 30 October Simon Flesser said he would keep Unity but would have explored other font options.","r":"Mid-project, the team replaced Unity's font rendering with a plugin.","re":"Flesser would keep Unity but explore other font rendering options.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before production, build one screen of your densest text in the engine and check it on the target device.","i":"In DEVICE 6 the text is the level, so weak font rendering touched every screen. The lesson is about whatever content dominates your game; it is not a case against Unity, which Flesser would pick again.","a":"Have we rendered our main content type in the engine yet?","q":"“We had to make our own column alignment, and at start we even had to edit the font in odd ways to make it render in way that just didn't look awful.”","qa":"Simon Flesser, co-founder of Simogo","st":"Textual orientation: The making of DEVICE 6","su":"https://www.pocketgamer.biz/textual-orientation-the-making-of-device-6/#:~:text=We%20had%20to%20make%20our%20own,just%20didn%27t%20look%20awful","sd":"Oct 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"diablo","n":"Diablo","dv":"Condor","y":"1996","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.gog.com/en/game/diablo","ls":"GOG","cv":"diablo.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Signed a $300,000 contract to make Diablo with a 15-person studio","s":"The money came to about $20,000 per person per year and arrived only at milestones. Condor nearly went broke, skipped taxes for a while and sold itself to Blizzard in 1996.","w":"Blizzard agreed to pay Condor $300,000, released at milestones, to make Diablo in just over a year. The studio had 15 people, which left about $20,000 per person per year and nothing for office rent. Development took almost two years instead of one. Brevik later said Condor did not pay taxes for a while, and Erich Schaefer recalled back-tax demands. In 1996 Blizzard bought the studio, and Brevik told GameStar the founders had signed the contract for far too little money.","r":"The budget could not pay the team, and the studio nearly went broke.","re":"Condor gave up its independence when Blizzard bought it in 1996.","co":"$300,000 for 15 people; almost 2 years instead of 1.","nf":false,"l":"Before you sign, multiply headcount by months, add overhead, and refuse any deal below that number.","i":"Milestone money arrives after the work is done, so an underpriced deal makes the studio fund its own delays with cash it does not have. Condor's way out was a sale to its publisher, which is not an option most studios can plan on.","a":"Does this contract cover our headcount, overhead and a schedule slip?","q":"“We were terrible businesspeople who signed the Diablo contract for far too little money.”","qa":"David Brevik, co-founder of Condor (later Blizzard North)","st":"Diablo-Entwickler wären fast bankrott gegangen - »Wir waren furchtbare Geschäftsleute«","su":"https://www.gamestar.de/artikel/diablo-entwickler-pleite-bankrott-interview-furchtbare-geschaeftsleute,3341477.html#:~:text=Wir%20waren%20furchtbare%20Gesch%C3%A4ftsleute%2C%20die,zu%20wenig%20Geld%20unterschrieben%20hatten","sd":"Mar 2019","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Put off writing multiplayer code until the last months before launch","s":"A Blizzard North team moved south to build Battle.net support in the final months. The peer-to-peer design let cheats spread right after launch, and Diablo II went client-server to stop them.","w":"Condor pitched Diablo to Blizzard as a turn-based game, and after development began Blizzard asked for real time and multiplayer. The team switched to real time, but Diablo had no multiplayer modes or code for most of development. The Battle.net idea came about six months before launch, and a team from Blizzard North moved south to build it in with Blizzard South. The game shipped on 31 December 1996 with peer-to-peer play, and cheats spread at once. At GDC 2016 David Brevik said fixing cheating drove Diablo II to client-server.","r":"Cheats spread across peer-to-peer games as soon as the game launched.","re":"Blizzard North built Diablo II on a client-server model to stop cheating.","co":"A Blizzard North team relocated south for the last months of development.","nf":true,"l":"If multiplayer is in scope, build and test the network model, including how it resists cheating, during pre-production.","i":"Networking touches every system that changes game state, so adding it late pushes the team to the simplest model that works. Peer-to-peer trusts every client, so one shared cheat reaches every game. Purely local co-op carries much less of this risk.","a":"Have we played a networked build and tried to cheat in it?","q":"“Going client-server for Diablo II was definitely necessary.”","qa":"David Brevik, co-founder of Blizzard North and lead programmer on Diablo","st":"Diablo's Battle.net originally ran on a single PC","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/diablos-battlenet-originally-ran-on-a-single-pc/#:~:text=Going%20client%2Dserver%20for%20Diablo%20II,was%20definitely%20necessary","sd":"Mar 2016","sy":"Participant talk","w8":3},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Switched the turn-based design to real time after a team vote","s":"The change Brevik expected to cause a huge delay took him a few hours on one afternoon. He later called that moment the birth of the action-RPG.","w":"Condor designed Diablo as a turn-based game, and lead programmer David Brevik wanted to keep it that way. Blizzard South kept asking for real time. About six months into development the team voted in the kitchen: Brevik voted no and everyone else voted yes. He told Blizzard it meant a huge delay and asked for another milestone payment. Then one Friday afternoon he made the turns happen about 20 times a second, and in a few hours it ran. At GDC 2016 he called it the moment the action-RPG was born.","r":"The real-time conversion took a few hours, not the delay Brevik feared.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"A few hours of programming, plus one extra milestone payment from Blizzard.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock the core loop, prototype the alternative mode, such as real time versus turns, and play both.","i":"Brevik only had to make the turns run about 20 times a second, so the switch was cheap. That speed came from how his turn system was built; on most projects a core loop change six months in costs far more than an afternoon.","a":"Have we played our core loop in its alternative mode yet?","q":"“So we all met in the kitchen and we voted, and I voted no and everybody else voted yes.”","qa":"David Brevik, co-founder and lead programmer of Condor (later Blizzard North)","st":"The moment the action-RPG was born","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/the-moment-the-arpg-was-born/#:~:text=So%20we%20all%20met%20in,everybody%20else%20voted%20yes","sd":"Mar 2016","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"diabloii","n":"Diablo II","dv":"Blizzard North","y":"2000","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned four times Diablo's content for twice the work, without a full design document","s":"The two-year estimate became more than three years, the team grew from 24 to over 40, and the last year was crunch. The game sold to over a million customers in three weeks.","w":"Blizzard North started Diablo II after the original went gold in December 1996, working from a rough plan and no complete design document. The team expected to make four times the original game with twice the work, and estimated two years. Staff grew from 24 at the start to more than 40. Tools fell short: some were abandoned, and artists had to re-animate monsters months after the first pass. Diablo II went gold on June 15, 2000, after a 12-month crunch, and shipped on June 28. That October, Erich Schaefer called the workload inhuman.","r":"The schedule ran over three years instead of two, ending in yearlong crunch.","re":"Schaefer planned better scheduling and better tools for the next projects.","co":"Over 1 extra year, 40+ people and a 12-month crunch.","nf":false,"l":"Estimate a sequel's schedule from the measured cost of each new piece of content, not from how polished the core feels.","i":"A polished core made new content look cheap, but four towns, five classes and new systems each needed tools the team did not have. The bet still produced a hit; the overrun was paid in crunch, not in sales.","a":"Have we costed the new content piece by piece, or guessed a multiplier?","q":"“Because we had the gameplay basics already polished, we figured we would hire some new employees, create some good tools, and essentially make four times the original game doing only two times the work.”","qa":"Erich Schaefer, vice president and co-founder of Blizzard North","st":"Postmortem: Blizzard's Diablo II (2000)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-blizzard-s-i-diablo-ii-i-#:~:text=Because%20we%20had%20the%20gameplay,only%20two%20times%20the%20work","sd":"Oct 2000, reprinted 2024","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"diabloiv","n":"Diablo IV","dv":"Blizzard Entertainment","y":"2023","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2344520/","ls":"Steam","cv":"diabloiv.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Designed the next Diablo as an over-the-shoulder, Dark Souls-style dungeon crawler","s":"Codenamed Hades, it was Team 3's main project from 2014 to 2016 and was then shelved, Kotaku reported. The team restarted as Fenris, and Diablo IV shipped in 2023.","w":"Blizzard cancelled Diablo III's second expansion in late 2013 or early 2014, and Team 3 split up in March 2014. Those who stayed began a new Diablo, codenamed Hades, led by Reaper of Souls director Josh Mosqueira. Kotaku's sources described a gothic, challenging Diablo take on Dark Souls with an over-the-shoulder camera. It was Team 3's main project from 2014 to 2016. One person involved told Kotaku it was not shaping up. In mid-2016 Mosqueira left and Hades was shelved. The team restarted as Fenris, which used an isometric camera by 2018. Diablo IV shipped in 2023.","r":"Hades was shelved after 2 years as Team 3's main project.","re":"Team 3 made Rise of the Necromancer and restarted Diablo IV as Fenris.","co":"About 2 years, 2014 to 2016, of Team 3's main project shelved.","nf":false,"l":"When a sequel changes the series' camera or core feel, prototype that change first and set a date to keep or drop it.","i":"A new camera changes combat, level layouts and art at once, so the bet reaches all content. Kotaku names no single cause: Mosqueira's exit and the shelving came together, and Blizzard says it ships about half of the projects it starts.","a":"Have we proven the new camera with our core loop yet?","q":"“It was not shaping up at all,”","qa":"Anonymous developer involved with Hades at Blizzard, quoted by Kotaku","st":"The Past, Present, And Future Of Diablo","su":"https://kotaku.com/the-past-present-and-future-of-diablo-1830477839#:~:text=It%20was%20not%20shaping,up%20at%20all","sd":"Nov 2018","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"dishonored","n":"Dishonored","dv":"Arkane","y":"2012","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/205100/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dishonored.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Art & style","t":"Kept a core group on research and concept for 2 years before pitching","s":"Art director Sebastien Mitton credited that pre-production with the game getting made. The research gave Dunwall a look built from London and Edinburgh side streets, with no specific game references.","w":"Before Dishonored was shown to publishers, a core group at Arkane spent 2 years researching and conceptualizing it, by art director Sebastien Mitton's account. Set themes such as architecture, mystical lighting, morphology and illustration let the group experiment without drifting off tone. Mitton and visual design director Viktor Antonov photographed people and side streets in London and Edinburgh. At the Develop Conference in July 2012, before release, Mitton said pre-production was why the game existed. Dishonored later won the BAFTA for Best Game.","r":"Mitton credited the long concept phase with the game getting made.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"2 years of core-group research before the pitch.","nf":false,"l":"Give a small core group time to set the world's visual rules from real references before you pitch or staff up.","i":"Fixed themes and real references let a small group experiment without drifting off tone, so the world was coherent before full production. The lesson fits a world-driven game; a mechanics-first game needs its early time for prototypes more than reference trips.","a":"Are our world's visual rules written down before we pitch?","q":"“Pre-production was the crucial element.”","qa":"Sebastien Mitton, art director at Arkane Studios","st":"Dishonoured wouldn’t exist without pre-production","su":"https://mcvuk.com/business-news/media-pr/dishonoured-wouldnt-exist-without-pre-production/#:~:text=Pre%2Dproduction%20was%20the,crucial%20element","sd":"Jul 2012","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"dishonored2","n":"Dishonored 2","dv":"Arkane Lyon","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/403640/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dishonored2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the new Void Engine from id Tech 5 while producing the sequel","s":"The engine took 1 or 2 years to become stable. Until then the team sometimes had to redo a whole piece of content because it no longer fit the technology.","w":"After Dishonored, Arkane Lyon doubled to 120 people and took the sequel to the new console generation. It built a new engine, Void, on id Tech 5 and rewrote 70% of its code. Getting the engine stable enough to do justice to the art direction took 1 or 2 years. In those years the team sometimes redid a whole part of the content because it no longer worked with the technology. Dishonored 2 shipped on 11 November 2016. That month, Romuald Capron called the engine long work and an investment for later games.","r":"Some content was rebuilt from scratch while the engine settled.","re":"Arkane treated the engine as an investment for its later games.","co":"1 to 2 years of engine instability; some content redone completely.","nf":false,"l":"While the engine is still being rewritten, build only content you can afford to redo once it stabilizes.","i":"Content built on a moving engine breaks each time the engine changes, so the team pays for it twice. Capron counted the engine as an investment, which only pays back if later games reuse it.","a":"How much content are we building before the engine is stable?","q":"“Pendant ces deux ans, ça n’a pas été facile, il fallait parfois refaire intégralement une partie d’un contenu parce que ça n’allait plus avec la technologie.” (For those two years it was not easy: sometimes we had to redo part of a piece of content entirely because it no longer worked with the technology.)","qa":"Romuald Capron, COO and production director of Arkane Studios","st":"Dishonored 2 : entrez dans les coulisses du studio Arkane","su":"https://www.01net.com/actualites/dishonored-2-entrez-dans-les-coulisses-du-studio-arkane-1054256.html#:~:text=Pendant%20ces%20deux%20ans,plus%20avec%20la%20technologie","sd":"Nov 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"divinityoriginalsin","n":"Divinity: Original Sin","dv":"Larian Studios","y":"2014","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/230230/","ls":"Steam","cv":"divinityoriginalsin.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Self-funded through investors, Kickstarter and Early Access with a 2013 release promised","s":"When Larian chose to fold community feedback into the game, the delay needed extra budget that Early Access could not cover, creditors were waiting for the earlier date, and the release became one big crunch.","w":"Larian funded Original Sin with private investors, a Kickstarter that ran from 27 Mar to 26 Apr 2013 and raised $944,282 from 19,541 backers against a $400,000 goal, and Steam Early Access. The campaign said the game would release in 2013. Early Access money was not enough, and creditors expected the earlier date. The team decided to fold the best Kickstarter and Early Access feedback into the game, which meant extra delays and extra budget, and the release became one big crunch. Two months after the 2014 launch, Vincke reported well over 500,000 units sold, break-even reached and debts paid.","r":"Delays, extra budget and a crunch release while creditors waited for an earlier date.","re":"Repaid debts from sales and chose to self-invest before crowdfunding again.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If you plan to act on Early Access feedback, budget the delay and tell your creditors before you promise a date.","i":"Self-funding with investors and backers puts the release date in the hands of people who lent money against it, so every feedback-driven improvement becomes a debt problem. It paid off here because the game sold; a weaker launch would have left the debt.","a":"Do our creditors know we may delay to act on feedback?","q":"“A lot of the crunch was caused by our decision to listen to the feedback we received through our Kickstarter and Steam Early Access communities.”","qa":"Swen Vincke, CEO of Larian Studios","st":"Thoughts after releasing Divinity:Original Sin and what comes next","su":"http://www.lar.net/2014/09/12/thoughts-after-releasing-divinityoriginal-sin-and-what-comes-next/#:~:text=A%20lot%20of%20the%20crunch,Steam%20Early%20Access%20communities","sd":"Sep 2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"doom2016","n":"DOOM","dv":"id Software","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/379720/","ls":"Steam","cv":"doom2016.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Conceived Doom 4 as a cinematic, scripted shooter on Earth with cover combat","s":"After Rage shipped in 2011, id judged the game did not feel like Doom and ran a rolling reboot into 2013. Years of work were set aside, staff left or were let go, and the studio refocused on one project.","w":"John Carmack hinted at Doom 4 at QuakeCon 2007 and id announced it in May 2008 as an Earth invasion. Creative lead Kevin Cloud drove it toward a realistic, cinematic, heavily scripted game with cover shooting and far more story and characters. After Rage shipped in 2011 came a long evaluation: the game did not feel like Doom. From late 2011 to early 2013 the studio went through a rolling reboot, refocused on one project, and some staff left or were let go. DOOM shipped in 2016, and its glory kills grew out of Doom 4's synced melee.","r":"Years of Doom 4 work set aside; the studio was restructured around one project.","re":"A rolling reboot from late 2011 to early 2013 rebuilt the game with Bethesda's backing.","co":"About 3 years of Doom 4 work; a studio of almost three full teams.","nf":false,"l":"Test a franchise reimagining against the core fantasy in a playable slice before you fund years of production value.","i":"A bigger story and more characters sacrificed the Doom Slayer, the one guy at the centre of big things, and the mismatch was judged on feel only after Rage shipped. Reimagining can work; the feel gate has to come before years of production.","a":"Does our playable slice feel like the franchise, and who judges that?","q":"“It didn't feel as much like Doom as I think a lot of us expected it would feel, or hoped it would feel.”","qa":"Marty Stratton, project lead on DOOM at id Software","st":"DOOM Documentary: Part 1 - To Hell & Back","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PS6SBnccxMA&t=973s","sd":"Dec 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4,"qu":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/production/devs-at-id-reflect-on-why-i-doom-4-i-was-a-bust#:~:text=It%20didn%27t%20feel%20as%20much,hoped%20it%20would%20feel"},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Cancelled the scripted, Call of Duty-like Doom 4 and restarted from the series' core","s":"About three years of work were thrown out. The new game was built around glory kills with story cut back, and DOOM launched in 2016 to wide critical acclaim.","w":"id announced Doom 4 in 2008 and built it alongside Rage on id Tech 5; insiders joked it was 'Call of Doom' for its scripted set pieces. In late 2011, after three years of work, id decided to reboot the game completely, and resets kept rolling until 2013. In April 2013 Bethesda said the earlier version lacked the expected quality. Game director Marty Stratton and the team listed Doom's DNA on a whiteboard and built the new game around glory kills, cutting back the story. DOOM launched in 2016, and critics acclaimed it.","r":"About three years of Doom 4 work were discarded and the game restarted.","re":"The team kept cutting story and chatty characters as production went on.","co":"About 3 years of Doom 4 work discarded; announced 2008, shipped 2016.","nf":false,"l":"Write the core fantasy on one page, and restart when the playable build stops matching it.","i":"A famous brand promises a way of playing, and chasing a genre leader's scripted set pieces broke that promise. Restarting is affordable for a publisher with a valuable franchise; a small studio with one project rarely has that option.","a":"Does our current build still feel like the game on the one-pager?","q":"“We cancelled a thing that people had spent a long time working on and we'd spent a lot of money to get to that point and then we cancelled it and basically started over.”","qa":"Pete Hines, vice president of marketing and PR at Bethesda","st":"Bethesda's Pete Hines talks cancelling Doom 4: 'It has to be the right thing executed the right way'","su":"https://www.shacknews.com/article/98438/bethesdas-pete-hines-talks-cancelling-doom-4-it-has-to-be-the-right-thing-executed-the-right-way#:~:text=We%20cancelled%20a%20thing%20that,and%20basically%20started%20over","sd":"Jan 2017","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"doom1993","n":"Doom","dv":"id Software","y":"1993","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2280/","ls":"Steam","cv":"doom1993.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Spent a month designing one big world while its memory use was still unchecked","s":"The memory numbers ruled the big world out, so id went back to separate levels and Tom Hall's month of design work was lost.","w":"Tom Hall finished the Doom Bible, id's design document, at the end of November 1992. John Carmack wanted one big continuous world instead of separate levels. Hall spent about a month designing a large base with a computer room and tunnels. In parallel, the team calculated sprite and map sizes and found the world would not fit in memory. id went back to separate levels, and Hall's month of work was lost. More of his ideas were cut in 1993, and he left in August after 10 months as creative director. Doom went online on December 10, 1993.","r":"About a month of design on the big world was thrown away.","re":"id switched back to separate levels and kept building.","co":"About 1 month of the creative director's design work.","nf":false,"l":"Run the memory numbers on your world structure before anyone spends weeks designing content for it.","i":"Carmack wanted the big world, and its memory cost was worked out only while Hall was already designing it. The loss here was 1 month; Hall's later exit had more causes than this one cut.","a":"Have we checked our world structure against the memory budget yet?","q":"“There's no way that this is going to work. I mean we don't have enough memory.”","qa":"Tom Hall, creative director at id Software","st":"'Doom:' A Classic Game Postmortem","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NnkCujnYNSo&t=538s","sd":"2011","sy":"Participant talk","w8":3,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NnkCujnYNSo&t=525s"}]},{"k":"doomii","n":"Doom II","dv":"id Software","y":"1994","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2300/","ls":"Steam","cv":"doomii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Kept the original Doom formula unchanged and only added to it","s":"Doom II's designers spent their time on new levels, building on a full game of examples. John Romero later named the unchanged formula as the main reason the sequel stood above the original.","w":"id Software released Doom in 1993. Its early development went largely into building the level editor and experimenting; real level production began only around the middle of that year. For Doom II, the team kept everything that worked and only added to it, with the double-barrel shotgun as the main new weapon. With a whole game of level examples behind them, the designers could concentrate on better levels. Doom II came out in 1994. In a July 2023 interview, John Romero named the unchanged formula as the number one reason the sequel improved on Doom.","r":"Designers spent Doom II on levels instead of editor work and experiments.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"For a content sequel, freeze the core rules that already work and spend the schedule on new content.","i":"Doom had already paid for the editor and the design experiments, so Doom II's time could go into levels. That saving lasts only while players want more of the same; Romero said Quake would have been easier split into separate engine and design releases.","a":"Which systems in our sequel actually need to change?","q":"“It was critical that we retained everything that we already put in the original DOOM, because that worked really well, so we didn’t want to break it or change it.”","qa":"John Romero, co-founder and designer at id Software","st":"DOOM Guy: John Romero Talks DOOM, Quake, Virtual Reality & More","su":"https://culturedvultures.com/doom-guy-john-romero/#:~:text=It%20was%20critical%20that%20we%20retained,break%20it%20or%20change%20it","sd":"Jul 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"doublefinesamnesiafortnight","n":"Double Fine's Amnesia Fortnight games (Costume Quest, Stacking, Iron Brigade, Once Upon a Monster)","dv":"Double Fine Productions","y":"2010","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/115100/","ls":"Steam","cv":"doublefinesamnesiafortnight.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Split 60 people into four teams to build four complete small games in two weeks","s":"With Brütal Legend 2 cancelled and no new ideas, the prototypes became Costume Quest, Stacking, Iron Brigade and Once Upon a Monster. Schafer says creativity saved the company; nobody bailed it out.","w":"In 2007, during Brütal Legend, Schafer first split Double Fine's 60 staff into four groups for two weeks, each building a complete small game. Brütal Legend shipped in October 2009 to strong reviews, but the Brütal Legend 2 pitch was cancelled; the studio had no new ideas and thought it would go out of business. Schafer ran the format again in 2009, this time aiming at commercial products. The four prototypes became Costume Quest, Stacking, Iron Brigade and Sesame Street: Once Upon a Monster, all well received and close to their two-week versions. He told the story at GDC 2012.","r":"Four sellable games from two weeks of prototyping instead of one unfunded AAA sequel.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Two weeks of a 60-person studio.","nf":false,"l":"When the big pitch dies, give small teams two weeks to build complete, sellable prototypes before staffing one new concept.","i":"Two weeks forces a scope small enough to finish and cheap enough to sell: Lee Petty built Stacking around one mechanic and dropped hat combat to fit time and budget. It worked because the studio already had people able to lead four projects.","a":"Could four of our people each lead a two-week complete prototype next month?","q":"“Creativity saved our company.”","qa":"Tim Schafer, studio head of Double Fine Productions","st":"How Amnesia Fortnight Saved Double Fine","su":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/how-amnesia-fortnight-saved-double-fine/1100-6365180/#:~:text=Creativity%20saved%20our%20company","sd":"Mar 2012","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"draconuscultofthewyrm","n":"Draconus: Cult of the Wyrm","dv":"Treyarch","y":"2000","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built a new engine from scratch on a 10-month Dreamcast launch schedule","s":"The team missed three ship dates, fought a bug list of over 3,000 and shipped 9 months late, with bug fixing about $400,000 over budget.","w":"Treyarch planned 2 years for Die by the Sword II and spent 3 months on research, design, a new renderer and scripting. Interplay then wanted a Dreamcast launch title, which left about 10 months and a 7-person team on a new platform. The team cut network play but kept the new engine, built to the Dreamcast's published limits. It missed September, then Christmas, then the day before Christmas. The bug list passed 3,000 and took 5 months to clear. Draconus shipped in 2000, 9 months late. Fristrom later wrote that the old engine offered a fighting chance.","r":"Shipped 9 months late after a 5-month bug crunch.","re":"Artists simplified levels and the team hid distant characters to hold frame rate.","co":"9 months late; about $400,000 over budget on a 3,000-bug list.","nf":false,"l":"When the schedule gets cut, keep the engine you already have and cut the content plan to match.","i":"A new engine and a new platform are two large unknowns, and a halved schedule left no slack for either. Fristrom's own fix was to replace old engine parts one at a time instead of starting over.","a":"Could we ship this on the engine we already have?","q":"“We should have replaced components from Die By the Sword one at a time instead of throwing the whole thing out.”","qa":"Jamie Fristrom, programmer at Treyarch","st":"Postmortem: Treyarch's Draconus","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/postmortem-treyarch-s-i-draconus-i-#:~:text=We%20should%20have%20replaced%20components,throwing%20the%20whole%20thing%20out","sd":"Aug 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"dragonageii","n":"Dragon Age II","dv":"BioWare","y":"2011","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1238040/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dragonageii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Greenlit a Dragon Age sequel in late 2009 to cover for SWTOR slipping","s":"The game shipped in March 2011, about 16 months later, with reused levels the lead designer admits were a known risk. Reviews ranged widely and the planned expansion was dropped as the series moved to Frostbite.","w":"In late 2009 BioWare went into a board room to find something to cover for SWTOR slipping and came out with a Dragon Age sequel. Mark Darrah wanted to call it Dragon Age: Exodus; EA wanted a title linked to Origins, so it became Dragon Age II. Lead designer Mike Laidlaw says just the senior, core team designed it, and that reusing levels was a known risk taken to have more content. It shipped in March 2011 to reviews from perfect scores to 6/10. The expansion was cancelled as Dragon Age moved to Frostbite. Darrah recalled this in March 2021.","r":"Shipped about 16 months after the decision with reused levels and a split reception.","re":"The planned expansion was dropped as BioWare moved Dragon Age to Frostbite.","co":"About 16 months from board room to ship; the expansion was cancelled.","nf":false,"l":"Do not greenlight a numbered sequel as schedule filler; if another project sets your date, name and scope it as the smaller game.","i":"The date came from SWTOR's slip, not from the game, so levels were reused to fill a full sequel in about 16 months, and the numbered title set expectations the schedule could not meet. Darrah wishes it had been Dragon Age: Exodus.","a":"Is our ship date set by this game's needs or by another project's slip?","q":"“We went into a board room in late 2009 to find something to cover for SWTOR slipping and then we shipped a game in March 2011…”","qa":"Mark Darrah, BioWare","st":"Dragon Age 2 developers look back on the game during its 10th anniversary","su":"https://gaymingmag.com/?p=21303#:~:text=We%20went%20into%20a%20board,game%20in%20March%202011","sd":"Mar 2021","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"dragonageinquisition","n":"Dragon Age: Inquisition","dv":"BioWare","y":"2014","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1222690/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dragonageinquisition.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Switched Inquisition to Frostbite in 2011 to bring back exploration and scale","s":"Artists could build levels at once, but Frostbite had no stats, classes or other RPG systems. Tools took 30% of the tech budget, and some arrived after alpha.","w":"In mid-2011, after Dragon Age II disappointed and with Skyrim coming, Mark Darrah and Mike Laidlaw wanted exploration and scale back in the series. The old engine needed heavy updating, and EA's Patrick Söderlund offered his support for Frostbite, so BioWare chose it. Frostbite was good at level art but at first knew nothing of stats or classes. Artists started content right away while designers waited for systems, some until after alpha. The game shipped in November 2014 on 5 platforms. Darrah reviewed the choice in November 2022.","r":"Designers waited for RPG systems while artists were already building levels.","re":"BioWare spent 30% of its tech budget on tools to cover Frostbite's gaps.","co":"30% of the tech budget on tools; some systems arrived after alpha.","nf":false,"l":"Before you switch engines, list the genre systems the engine lacks and schedule the tools for them before content production.","i":"An engine built for another genre gives artists a head start and leaves designers waiting for systems, so the game takes shape around the art. Tools money closes the gap; Darrah said later BioWare Frostbite games that skipped that share suffered for it.","a":"Which systems our genre needs does the new engine lack today?","q":"“DAI spent 30 percent of its Tech Budget on tooling on building tools to allow it to build the game that ultimately came out.”","qa":"Mark Darrah, executive producer of Dragon Age: Inquisition at BioWare","st":"Dragon Age: Inquisition - Memories and Lessons #DAI","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4Q5_RsII_Ho&t=342s","sd":"Nov 2022","sy":"Participant talk","w8":5}]},{"k":"dragonageorigins","n":"Dragon Age: Origins","dv":"BioWare","y":"2009","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/47810/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dragonageorigins.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Story & world","t":"Started a new fantasy IP with a small team building the world first","s":"Writing the history, goals and world from scratch took years before the full team arrived. Lead designer Mike Laidlaw put development at about 6 years, with roughly 3 on a full team.","w":"BioWare decided to return to fantasy with an IP it fully owned. With no source books to draw on, a small team first set the vision, goals, history and world, so that later content stayed consistent. Full production followed: lead designer Mike Laidlaw counted about 3 years with a full team, peaking above 180 people for a short all-hands period. In a pre-release interview at BioWare's Edmonton studio, he put the total at about 6 years. Origins went on to win more than 30 Best of 2009 awards.","r":"Development ran about 6 years, only about 3 of them with a full team.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About 6 years in total; about 3 with a full team, peaking above 180.","nf":false,"l":"For a new IP, plan a small-team phase to write the world's history and rules before you staff up for content.","i":"Writers, designers and artists need a fixed reference for history and tone, or each invents their own and consistency breaks. Licensed source books supply that reference, so the long lead time belongs to original worlds; a licensed game can staff up sooner.","a":"Is our world's history written down before the full team starts?","q":"“You basically need something to build on or else the internal consistency breaks down.”","qa":"Mike Laidlaw, lead designer of Dragon Age: Origins at BioWare","st":"Interview with Mike Laidlaw, lead designer for Dragon Age: Origins","su":"https://classic.rpgfan.com/features/Dragon_Age_Interview/#:~:text=You%20basically%20need%20something%20to,internal%20consistency%20breaks%20down","sd":"","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"dragonagetheveilguard","n":"Dragon Age: The Veilguard","dv":"BioWare","y":"2024","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1845910/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dragonagetheveilguard.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Cancelled the Joplin pre-production and rebuilt Dragon Age 4 around live service","s":"The restart rebuilt the game's core for live service, so dropping it later meant undoing the core. Darrah calls that pivot a burden the game carried to its October 2024 launch.","w":"After the 2015 Trespasser expansion, a few dozen developers under Mark Darrah and Mike Laidlaw began Joplin, and spent about 2 years in pre-production. In October 2017, with Anthem in trouble, EA and BioWare cancelled it and moved most of the staff, Darrah included, to Anthem. A tiny team restarted the game as Morrison, a live service on Anthem's tools and codebase; Laidlaw left soon after. The team came back in 2019, and the plan slid from FY21 to FY23. The live-service design was later dropped, and the game shipped on 31 October 2024.","r":"The plan slid about 2 years while the team worked on Anthem.","re":"The team later dropped the live-service design and returned to single-player.","co":"About 2 years of Joplin work restarted from scratch; the plan slid 2 years.","nf":false,"l":"If a restart adds a new business model, build it as a layer you can remove, not into the game's core.","i":"The 2017 restart had enough time to rebuild the whole game around live service, so returning to single-player meant undoing its core, not peeling off a layer. If the new model is the point of the game, a removable layer is the wrong choice.","a":"If this business model fails, can we remove it without rebuilding the core?","q":"“So, we move into this very confusing period for Dragon Age 4 where it's rebooted to be a live service.”","qa":"Mark Darrah, executive producer of Dragon Age at BioWare","st":"Dragon Age: The Veilguard - Lessons From A Perpetual Deadline (Veilguard Part 0)","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9FzPhmC6Mv0&t=613s","sd":"Oct 2026","sy":"Participant account","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9FzPhmC6Mv0&t=498s"}]},{"k":"drakanorderoftheflame","n":"Drakan: Order of the Flame","dv":"Surreal","y":"1999","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Combined dragon flight, ground combat and RPG elements in the studio's first game","s":"The design bred new ideas so easily that feature creep overtook the project halfway through. Drakan slipped from February to August 1999, cut 14 levels to 10 and shipped with known bugs.","w":"Four recent graduates founded Surreal and sold a dragon concept to Virgin Interactive in 1996. When Virgin cut projects in early 1997, Psygnosis took it and gave the team freedom to add a human rider, RPG elements and more content, called ambitious at E3 1998. A 175-page design document outlined the game, but feature creep overtook the project halfway and the document fell behind. In March 1998 the designers cut 14 planned levels to the 10 best and dropped coded weapons. Slated for February 1999, Drakan shipped six months late and then won PC Game of the Year awards.","r":"Released six months late with cut content and a patch needed at launch.","re":"Cut to the 10 best levels and finished the multiplayer fix as a patch.","co":"Six-month slip; 14 levels cut to 10; nearly 2 weeks lost per demo build.","nf":false,"l":"When a concept mixes several genres, cap the feature list in the design document and update it every time you change scope.","i":"A world with immense possibilities makes new ideas cheap to have and expensive to ship; without a maintained design document nothing stopped them. Denman still counts the design as a strength, so the fix is a scope gate, not a narrower concept.","a":"Who says no to new features, and is the design document still current?","q":"“The majority of the scheduling problems were due to feature creep and other improvements that were considered necessary during development.”","qa":"Stuart Denman, lead programmer and co-founder of Surreal Software","st":"Postmortem: Surreal Software's Drakan: Order of the Flame","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-surreal-software-s-i-drakan-order-of-the-flame-i-#:~:text=The%20majority%20of%20the%20scheduling,necessary%20during%20development","sd":"Apr 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Art & style","t":"Made the art with character concepts but no art bible for models or environments","s":"Without a shared standard, artists rebuilt geometry and textures up to 3 times, and the art lead often redid other artists' work while spread thin.","w":"Psygnosis took on Drakan in early 1997 with a bigger budget, and Surreal hired more people and grew the art content. The team had plenty of character concept art but no art bible for the game's models and environments. When the art lead disliked another artist's work, he often rebuilt it himself, though his time was spread thin. Geometry and model textures were sometimes rebuilt 3 times before they passed. The art team also had communication problems and power struggles. Drakan shipped in August 1999, and the April 2000 postmortem blamed part of the rework on the missing artistic vision.","r":"Artists rebuilt geometry and textures up to 3 times.","re":"Kept rebuilding assets until the team was satisfied.","co":"Some geometry and textures rebuilt up to 3 times.","nf":false,"l":"Before art production starts, write an art bible with target examples for every model and environment type.","i":"Without a written target, approval lives in one lead's head, so work bounces back and the lead turns into the bottleneck. Denman also blamed the studio's own high standards: an art bible fixes direction, not a team that keeps raising the bar.","a":"Does every artist have a written target for finished models and environments?","q":"“This may have been partially due to Surreal’s high aesthetic standards, but a lack of consistent artistic vision is also to blame.”","qa":"Stuart Denman, lead programmer and co-founder of Surreal Software","st":"Postmortem: Surreal Software's Drakan: Order of the Flame","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-surreal-software-s-i-drakan-order-of-the-flame-i-#:~:text=This%20may%20have%20been%20partially,is%20also%20to%20blame.","sd":"Apr 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Designed a generic editor where game code defines everything designers can tweak","s":"Designers combined parameter-driven modules in ways programmers never planned, and one ice sword effect became ghost auras, guide paths and ice arrows. Surreal reused the engine for later games with little change.","w":"Before it had a publisher, Surreal set out to build technology it could pitch and keep. Lead programmer Stuart Denman designed a toolset where game code supplied every game-specific property and the editor stayed a generic interface. Drakan had no scripting language: programmers wrote modules such as triggers, doors and weapons with parameters, and designers wired them together visually. Psygnosis signed the game in early 1997, and it shipped in August 1999. In his April 2000 postmortem Denman described designers turning one ice sword particle effect into ghost auras, snow on invisible platforms and ice-trailing arrows.","r":"Designers built new effects and events with little extra programming.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Expose gameplay behavior as parameter-driven modules that designers can combine without asking a programmer for each variation.","i":"When behavior lives in configurable modules, each new variation costs designer time, not programmer time. It does not replace early collaboration: Drakan's AI, built to the design document, worked only once designers and AI programmers sat down together.","a":"Can a designer build a new scripted event without a programmer?","q":"“The system meant designers could tweak parameters and combine modules in ways that the programmers never intended.”","qa":"Stuart Denman, lead programmer and co-founder of Surreal Software","st":"Postmortem: Surreal Software's Drakan: Order of the Flame","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-surreal-software-s-i-drakan-order-of-the-flame-i-#:~:text=The%20system%20meant%20designers%20could,the%20programmers%20never%20intended.","sd":"Apr 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Hired a movie scriptwriter new to fantasy to write the first script","s":"Several more people rewrote the script under deadline pressure. It shipped with weak dialogue that was too costly to re-record except for the worst lines.","w":"Surreal hired a movie scriptwriter for the first draft of Drakan's script. He did not know the fantasy genre and did not have a firm grasp of lead designer Alan Patmore's vision. Members of Surreal, members of Psygnosis and even one voice actor then edited and rewrote it. Under pressure to finish, the script was completed with cheesy one-liners. Once recorded, bad lines were hard to redo, so only the completely failed ones were re-recorded. Drakan shipped in August 1999, and in April 2000 lead programmer Stuart Denman listed the story among what went wrong.","r":"The game shipped with dialogue the team itself called badly written.","re":"Re-recorded only the completely failed lines.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you sign a writer, have them write one scene in your genre and check it against the lead designer's vision.","i":"A writer who does not know the genre or the design hands over a draft others must rewrite, and each new hand moves the voice further off. Once lines are recorded, every fix costs studio time, so the check has to come first.","a":"Has our writer delivered a sample scene in this genre we approved?","q":"“We hired a movie scriptwriter to do the initial work on the script, but he was not familiar with the fantasy genre and did not have a firm grasp on Alan’s vision for the design.”","qa":"Stuart Denman, lead programmer and co-founder of Surreal Software","st":"Postmortem: Surreal Software's Drakan: Order of the Flame","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-surreal-software-s-i-drakan-order-of-the-flame-i-#:~:text=We%20hired%20a%20movie%20scriptwriter,vision%20for%20the%20design.","sd":"Apr 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"dredge","n":"Dredge","dv":"Black Salt Games","y":"2023","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1562430/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dredge.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Kept Dredge to its pitched size and timed every feature before building it","s":"The team never really had to crunch. A feature went in only if something else could come out, and the shipped game stayed close to the earliest pitch.","w":"Black Salt Games set out with a clear idea of how large Dredge should be, and held to it. Features still came and went through iteration: a harpoon gun was dropped, and time replaced fuel as the resource. Every feature the team kept was scoped with a time estimate, and adding one could mean pulling something from another area. Dredge came out in March 2023, close to the earliest pitch. In April 2023 the team said there was never really a time when it had to crunch.","r":"Shipped close to the earliest pitch without a crunch period.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Estimate every feature before you build it, and for each one you add, name the work you will pull to pay for it.","i":"A fixed game size turns each new idea into a trade, so scope cannot grow unnoticed. The method depends on good estimates; a team new to its genre or tech may find the estimates are the weak point.","a":"What will we cut to pay for the next feature?","q":"“We knew that for one thing to happen, we might have to pull something away in another areas.”","qa":"Mikey Bastiaens, 3D artist and animator at Black Salt Games","st":"Trawling in the deep: How Black Salt Games made spooky fishing RPG, Dredge","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/trawling-in-the-deep-how-black-salt-games-made-spooky-fishing-rpg-i-dredge-i-#:~:text=We%20knew%20that%20for%20one%20thing,pull%20something%20away%20in%20another%20areas","sd":"Apr 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"driveclub","n":"Driveclub","dv":"Evolution Studios","y":"2014","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Fed 3 server systems and a mobile app into one curated dynamic menu","s":"The menu, not the racing, held the game back. Driveclub missed the PS4 launch and got an extra year, with up to half the team working on the menu.","w":"Evolution Studios had wanted a socially connected racing game for 10 years and built Driveclub for PS4 on its new engine. Three server infrastructures, a phone and tablet app and curated push content all met in one dynamic menu. The racing was ready for the PS4 launch, but the menu was not, so the studio delayed the game. The menu took about a third of the team, up to half at times. Driveclub launched on 7 October 2014. Just before that, Simon Barlow said artists had used the extra 12 months to build weather.","r":"Driveclub missed the PS4 launch and took an extra year.","re":"Artists and the render team used the year to build a weather update.","co":"An extra year; a third to half of the team on the menu.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock a launch date, build the screen where all your online services meet and test it end to end.","i":"Every online service fed one menu, so the menu could only work once all of them did, and it held back a finished racing core. The risk sat in the integration point, not in the racing or the art.","a":"Have we tested the screen where all our online services meet?","q":"“We tend to curate content for the player and suggest things for the player to do, and that was the thing that took the time.”","qa":"Simon Barlow, design director of Evolution Studios","st":"Interview – Simon Barlow, Lead Developer of DRIVECLUB","su":"https://folionic.wordpress.com/writing/game-journalism/save-game-online/interview-simon-barlow-lead-developer-of-driveclub/#:~:text=We%20tend%20to%20curate%20content,thing%20that%20took%20the%20time","sd":"Oct 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"dukenukemforever","n":"Duke Nukem Forever","dv":"3D Realms","y":"2011","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/57900/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dukenukemforever.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Restarted the game on each new 3D engine without a development roadmap","s":"Each switch threw away work already built. The game shipped 14 years after its announcement and drained 3D Realms' money along the way.","w":"3D Realms announced Duke Nukem Forever in April 1997, with release planned for no later than the end of 1998. The switch from Quake II tech to the Unreal engine wiped out over a year of work, USgamer reports. Co-owner Scott Miller says the team never had a good roadmap, so each new 3D technology set off another reboot. In 2004 he tried to move the game to Digital Extremes and was overruled internally. Development halted in May 2009, and Gearbox helped finish the game for June 2011. In May 2022 Miller called it a constant money pit.","r":"Release took 14 years and the project drained 3D Realms' cash.","re":"3D Realms handed the game to Gearbox, which finished it.","co":"About 14 years from announcement to release; team understaffed by at least 50%.","nf":false,"l":"Freeze the engine at greenlight, and treat any later engine switch as a re-plan with a new date and budget.","i":"Each engine switch threw away built work, and slow progress made the next new engine look worth a restart, so the loop fed itself. A planned and costed engine upgrade is a different decision.","a":"Who has to sign off before we change engines?","q":"“Because of the game's slow development, when new 3D technology became available, the project--in effect--rebooted to make use of the newest tech causing massive delays over and over.”","qa":"Scott Miller, founder of Apogee and former co-owner of 3D Realms","st":"The Truth About DNF","su":"https://www.apogeeent.com/devblog/dnf-truth#:~:text=Because%20of%20the%20game%27s%20slow,massive%20delays%20over%20and%20over","sd":"May 2022","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"dungeonkeeper","n":"Dungeon Keeper","dv":"Bullfrog","y":"1997","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1996630/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dungeonkeeper.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Cut back map-building because navigation took 1 second to update","s":"By Dene Carter's account the change ruined the game. Peter Molyneux had the navigation optimized instead, which let the team make the game its premise promised.","w":"On the original Dungeon Keeper at Bullfrog, the navigation system took 1 second to update after any change to the map. Dene Carter and his brother Simon altered the game so players built less of the map. By Dene's account, that ruined the game. Peter Molyneux stepped in and demanded that navigation be optimized immediately. With map-building back, the team could make the game the premise promised. Carter told the story at GDC in 2017, under the rule not to let technology dictate a game's core promise.","r":"The workaround was dropped and the navigation code was optimized.","re":"Peter Molyneux demanded that navigation be optimized immediately.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you trim the core mechanic to fit a slow system, cost the work to make the system faster.","i":"A design workaround is cheaper this week than an engineering fix, so it is the first thing a team reaches for. That trade is fine for side features; when the limit hits the premise itself, the workaround removes the reason to play.","a":"Are we cutting any core feature because a system is slow?","q":"“The navigation system took 1 second to update after any map change… so we altered the game to minimize the amount of map-building which utterly ruined the game.”","qa":"Dene Carter, core team member on Dungeon Keeper at Bullfrog","st":"From Indie to Fable and Back. 30 Years of ‘Wisdom’","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/gdc2017/Presentations/Carter_Dene_FromIndieTo.pdf#page=30","sd":"2017","sy":"Participant talk","w8":3}]},{"k":"dungeonsiege","n":"Dungeon Siege","dv":"Gas Powered Games","y":"2002","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/39190/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dungeonsiege.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Aimed a new studio's first RPG at a bigger 3D world with no loading screens","s":"Scope outgrew the team. A game planned for about 2 years shipped in almost 4, after years of crunch, and still became a hit.","w":"Chris Taylor founded Gas Powered Games with Total Annihilation veterans, and they picked an action RPG, a genre nobody on the team had really worked on. The goal was a bigger game than the genre leader, in 3D, with no loading screens. Most wish-list features were cut before development, but others kept the team busy for months. A custom animation editor took most of a year and was dropped for 3DS Max plug-ins. About a year in, 3 of the 6 engineers left. Tech lead Bartosz Kijanka's December 2002 postmortem says the scope ended up larger than the team's ability.","r":"The schedule nearly doubled, from about 2 years to almost 4.","re":"The team crunched for years and set up its own test lab.","co":"Almost 4 years against a plan of about 2, with years of crunch.","nf":false,"l":"Before production, cut every tech feature the core loop does not need, especially in a genre the team has never shipped.","i":"A team new to a genre cannot price the work, so ambition fills the gap with features nobody can schedule. Kijanka notes that original features need research, which is hard to plan. A team that has shipped the genre estimates better.","a":"Which tech features can we cut without hurting the core loop?","q":"“Our ambition translated into a number of painful symptoms such as feature creep, over-optimism, and a project scope that was ultimately larger than our ability.”","qa":"Bartosz Kijanka, tech lead and VP of technology at Gas Powered Games","st":"Postmortem: Gas Powered Games' Dungeon Siege","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-gas-powered-games-i-dungeon-siege-i-#:~:text=Our%20ambition%20translated%20into%20a,ultimately%20larger%20than%20our%20ability","sd":"Dec 2002","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Committed to a data-driven engine before writing the first line of code","s":"Game behavior lived in configuration, text files and scripts rather than code. The script system's back end later became the base for multiplayer networking and for save and load.","w":"Gas Powered Games formed after Total Annihilation, and its engineers started Dungeon Siege from an empty header file. Total Annihilation had taught them, before they wrote any code, that the engine had to be data-driven. The architecture was reworked many times, but the goal held: data and scripts set how features interact. Scott Bilas wrote the Skrit scripting system, and its back end became a remote procedure call layer that made multiplayer much easier and later save and load. By December 2002 the game had shipped after 4 years, and the tech lead listed the architecture among what went right.","r":"Multiplayer and save and load were built on the script system.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you write engine code, decide which behavior designers will set in data and scripts, and build the export path for it first.","i":"Planned from the start, one script back end carried networking and saves as well as gameplay; many engines then drifted into data by accident. The team knew the need from Total Annihilation. A first-time team may not know where to draw the line.","a":"Can designers change how features interact without a programmer?","q":"“Based on our experiences during the development of Total Annihilation, we knew even before we wrote the first line of code that we had to focus on a data-driven design.”","qa":"Bartosz Kijanka, tech lead on Dungeon Siege and VP of technology at Gas Powered Games","st":"Postmortem: Gas Powered Games' Dungeon Siege","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-gas-powered-games-i-dungeon-siege-i-#:~:text=Based%20on%20our%20experiences%20during%20the,focus%20on%20a%20data%2Ddriven%20design","sd":"Dec 2002","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"dustanelysiantail","n":"Dust: An Elysian Tail","dv":"Humble Hearts","y":"2012","sc":"Solo","se":"Port 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/236090/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dustanelysiantail.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Let a 3-month solo 8-bit project grow into an XBLA game built alone from scratch","s":"One developer wrote the engine, tools, art, animation and code for an open-world action RPG. The job took 3.5 years, cost a town-building system and a whole chapter, and ended in 20-hour workdays.","w":"In early 2009 Dean Dodrill asked his wife for 3 months to learn programming and make a small 8-bit game. The idea grew into an open-world game, and after it won Dream.Build.Play, Microsoft moved it to Xbox Live Arcade. Dodrill built the level editor, animation tool and particle system himself and did all art, animation, code and level design. He cut town-building, farming, fishing and a sixth chapter to finish. In 2012 a Summer of Arcade slot squeezed 8 to 10 months of work into 3, at nearly 20-hour days. The game shipped on 15 Aug 2012 after 3.5 years.","r":"One developer worked 3.5 years without weekends instead of 3 months.","re":"Cut the town-building, farming, fishing and sixth-chapter plans to ship.","co":"3.5 years solo; 8 to 10 months of work done in 3 at 20-hour days.","nf":false,"l":"When a hobby project turns into a publisher deal, re-plan the team and tools before you accept the scope.","i":"One person building tools, art, animation and code from scratch pays for every feature twice, and nobody else can absorb a late deadline. It shipped because Dodrill accepted the personal toll and cut a chapter; that is not a plan to copy.","a":"Can one person still carry this scope now that the platform changed?","q":"“I would spend the next three years, without breaks or weekends off, finishing the game.”","qa":"Dean Dodrill, developer, Humble Hearts","st":"Postmortem: Humble Hearts' Dust: An Elysian Tail","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-humble-hearts-i-dust-an-elysian-tail-i-#:~:text=I%20would%20spend%20the%20next,off%2C%20finishing%20the%20game","sd":"Oct 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Art & style","t":"Hand-animated the hero frame by frame and hand-painted the background pieces","s":"The hero alone took over 500 frames and most of the Xbox 360's memory. Painting backgrounds and assembling maps became the biggest time sink of the production.","w":"Dean Dodrill, an animator teaching himself to code, started Dust alone in XNA at the start of 2009. He animated the hero the traditional Disney way: pencil roughs, frames inked on a light table, then cleanup and color in Photoshop. The hero ended up with over 500 frames on four 4k sprite sheets and used most of the Xbox 360's RAM. Backgrounds were painted in small pieces and assembled into over 230 maps. The game shipped on Xbox Live Arcade on 15 August 2012, after 3.5 years. Dodrill then named the maps his most time-consuming task.","r":"Background art and maps took more time than any other task.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you lock the art style, time one finished character and one finished map, then multiply by the full count.","i":"Hand-drawn frames and painted map pieces cost the same effort every time, so the bill grows with every area added. Dodrill listed the art under what went right, so the lesson is to price the style, not to avoid it.","a":"How long does one finished map take, times the map count?","q":"“Creating the background assets and maps ended up being the most time-consuming task through the entire production.”","qa":"Dean Dodrill, developer at Humble Hearts","st":"Postmortem: Humble Hearts' Dust: An Elysian Tail","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-humble-hearts-i-dust-an-elysian-tail-i-#:~:text=Creating%20the%20background%20assets%20and,through%20the%20entire%20production","sd":"Oct 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"dyinglight","n":"Dying Light","dv":"Techland","y":"2015","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/239140/","ls":"Steam","cv":"dyinglight.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Made free first-person parkour a core mechanic and prototyped it on Dead Island","s":"Hand-placed climb points could not give the freedom the team wanted, so it switched to reading level geometry. Fixing the camera and motion sickness took 2 years or more.","w":"Techland's publisher wanted another game in the vein of Dead Island, but the team's list of improvements grew into a different game. It prototyped its 2 vital mechanics, natural parkour movement and a day and night cycle, using Dead Island as a basis. The first approach placed thousands of hooks by hand and could not reach the freedom the team wanted. Programmer Bartosz Kulon prototyped a system that read level geometry as the player moved, and the team went with it. Tuning it and solving motion sickness took 2 years or more. The game shipped in 2015.","r":"The movement system needed 2 years or more of tuning.","re":"Techland built almost 4 animation sets to show parkour skill growth.","co":"2 years or more of tuning; almost 4 animation sets.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock a first-person movement feature, test it for motion sickness and budget the camera and animation work it will need.","i":"In first person the camera is the player's body, so every movement change has to be tuned for comfort, and every skill tier needs its own animations. Techland paid that because parkour was the game's identity; a side feature rarely justifies the same bill.","a":"Have we tested our movement for motion sickness yet?","q":"“It was two years, maybe even more, of tweaking to make sure the system worked right and solving all of the problems with motion sickness.”","qa":"Tymon Smektala, producer at Techland","st":"The Develop Post-Mortem: Dying Light","su":"https://mcvuk.com/business-news/publishing/the-develop-post-mortem-dying-light/#:~:text=It%20was%20two%20years%2C%20maybe%20even%20more,problems%20with%20motion%20sickness","sd":"Feb 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"earthblade","n":"Earthblade","dv":"Extremely OK Games","y":"2024","sc":"Indie","se":"Cancelled 2024","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2239640/","ls":"Steam","cv":"earthblade.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Scaled up the core team after Celeste to make a bigger follow-up","s":"After years of work the game was behind where it should have been. The founders cancelled it in December 2024 and went back to small prototypes.","w":"Maddy Thorson and Noel Berry started Earthblade a few years after Celeste and grew the core team. Unlike their earlier games, it did not grow out of a game jam prototype. In a 2024 podcast, about 2.5 years in, Berry said its larger mechanical scope made it slower. Then a dispute over Celeste's IP rights split the founders from art director Pedro. Taking stock, they found the game not as far along as it should be, and cancelled it in December 2024. Thorson's January 2025 post blamed the pressure to go bigger and called the team scale-up a failure.","r":"The game was cancelled after years of work, with no release.","re":"The founders returned to small-scale prototyping at their own pace.","co":"More than 2.5 years of development, then cancellation.","nf":false,"l":"Before growing the team for a follow-up to a hit, prove the new concept with a small prototype at your old size.","i":"A hit raises the founders' own bar, and a bigger team locks in a bigger game before the design is found. Earthblade was also their first game not grown from a jam prototype. The IP split only triggered the review.","a":"Have we proven this concept at our old team size yet?","q":"“Scaling the core team up post-Celeste has ultimately been a failure, and that's okay.”","qa":"Maddy Thorson, co-founder of Extremely OK Games","st":"Final Earthblade Update","su":"https://exok.com/posts/2025-01-22-earthblade-final-update/#:~:text=Scaling%20the%20core%20team%20up,and%20that%27s%20okay","sd":"Jan 2025","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"earthbound","n":"EarthBound","dv":"HAL","y":"1994","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Rebuilt Mother 2's program from scratch while keeping its finished design and art","s":"Satoru Iwata estimated 2 years to fix the old code and 6 months to rebuild it. The rebuild came together in 6 months, followed by 6 months of polish.","w":"Mother 2 had been in development for about four years and was stuck when HAL Laboratory, fresh from Kirby's Adventure, was asked to help. President Yamauchi arranged for Shigesato Itoi to meet Satoru Iwata. Iwata said fixing the existing code would take 2 years, while starting over would take about 6 months. HAL planned only to advise but rewrote the entire program, using graphics, events and design work already done. The game came together in 6 months, then had 6 months of polish before release. Iwata described the overhaul in 1999; Itoi gave the numbers in 2019.","r":"The stalled game came together in 6 months, not an estimated 2 years.","re":"The team spent 6 more months polishing before release.","co":"About 6 months to rewrite the program, then 6 months of polish.","nf":false,"l":"When code stalls a project, estimate fixing it against rewriting it, counting the design and art you can keep.","i":"The art, events and design already existed, so the rewrite only rebuilt the code joining them, and Iwata split it so more people could work at once. Without finished design, a rewrite restarts design work too.","a":"Would rewriting this code be faster than fixing it?","q":"“Everything came together in six months, and we spent six more months polishing all the details before it went on sale.”","qa":"Shigesato Itoi, creator of Mother 2","st":"Shigesato Itoi Visits Arino Kacho’s Challenge Room","su":"https://www.1101.com/gccxmother/en/2019-09-17.html#:~:text=Everything%20came%20together%20in%20six,before%20it%20went%20on%20sale","sd":"Sep 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"eldenring","n":"Elden Ring","dv":"FromSoftware","y":"2022","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1245620/","ls":"Steam","cv":"eldenring.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Pushed player freedom past what Elden Ring's original plan allowed for","s":"Debugging and QA took a lot more effort than expected, and release slipped one month, from 21 January to 25 February 2022.","w":"Elden Ring was FromSoftware's first world at this size, and Hidetaka Miyazaki said the team kept its usual way of laying out and breaking down maps. The freedom the team wanted grew past the initial plan, and complexity built up through development. In October 2021 Bandai Namco moved release from 21 January to 25 February 2022, citing depth and freedom beyond initial expectations. Miyazaki told Edge, in an interview GamesRadar posted in February 2022, that debugging and QA took a lot more effort. The team and outsourcing grew with the world.","r":"Release moved one month, to 25 February 2022.","re":"The team took one more month for final adjustments.","co":"One month of delay and extra debug and QA effort.","nf":false,"l":"When designers add player freedom, add QA and debug time to the schedule in the same change.","i":"Every new route and free order adds characters and events that must not collide, so testing grows with each combination. Here the freedom grew during production, so the case does not show the original plan was wrong.","a":"Did we add QA time when we added player freedom?","q":"“The level of freedom that we wanted to ultimately achieve in Elden Ring exceeded what we were initially planning for.”","qa":"Hidetaka Miyazaki, president of FromSoftware and creative director of Elden Ring","st":"Hidetaka Miyazaki interview: FromSoftware's president explores the unknowns of Elden Ring","su":"https://www.gamesradar.com/elden-ring-fromsoftware-hidetaka-miyazaki-interview/#:~:text=The%20level%20of%20freedom%20that,we%20were%20initially%20planning%20for","sd":"Feb 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"elitedangerous","n":"Elite Dangerous","dv":"Frontier Developments","y":"2014","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/359320/","ls":"Steam","cv":"elitedangerous.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Promised Kickstarter backers an offline single-player mode on a server-run galaxy","s":"Frontier dropped offline play about a month before the December 2014 launch, calling it a largely separate game. Pre-order buyers who had not played the alpha or beta were offered refunds.","w":"Elite: Dangerous raised £1,578,316 from 25,681 backers on Kickstarter between November 2012 and January 2013. The FAQ described a shared galaxy kept on a central server, and an update then promised a single-player game without connecting to it. In development, the galaxy mechanics all sat on the online servers. In November 2014, a month before the 16 December release, Frontier announced there would be no offline mode. Executive producer Michael Brooke said the data and processes would not translate offline. After an outcry, Braben offered refunds to pre-order buyers who had not played the alpha or beta.","r":"A backer-promised mode was cut a month before launch.","re":"Braben offered refunds and said the decision would be reviewed after release.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you publish a crowdfunding promise, check it against the server and simulation architecture you plan to build.","i":"The FAQ already put the galaxy on a central server, so offline play meant a second simulation nobody had scoped. A promise to backers stays on record, and cutting it late turns a design call into a refund problem.","a":"Does every public promise we make fit our planned architecture?","q":"“It is technically possible, but it would be a largely separate game development.”","qa":"David Braben, CEO of Frontier Developments","st":"The Elite: Dangerous online-only saga ends in refunds","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/the-i-elite-dangerous-i-online-only-saga-ends-in-refunds#:~:text=It%20is%20technically%20possible%2C,a%20largely%20separate%20game%20development","sd":"Nov 2014","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"enslavedodysseytothewest","n":"Enslaved: Odyssey to the West","dv":"Ninja Theory","y":"2010","sc":"AA","se":"Port 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/245280/","ls":"Steam","cv":"enslavedodysseytothewest.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Licensed Unreal Engine 3 instead of updating the Heavenly Sword engine","s":"Artists and designers could start experimenting as soon as the engine arrived. Designers used its visual scripting to build much bigger play areas than in Heavenly Sword.","w":"Ninja Theory had built the Heavenly Sword engine and toolset entirely in-house for PS3. Keeping it current would have taken another large development effort, and its tools for designers and artists were its weak point. Once the studio decided to take Enslaved multi-platform, it moved to Unreal Engine 3 to start iterating gameplay sooner. Artists and designers could experiment right away. Designers used Kismet scripting to build much bigger environments, after critics called Heavenly Sword too short. About 80 core staff worked through production. Co-founder Mike Ball described the switch in October 2010.","r":"Gameplay iteration started early, with no engine update project first.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you extend your own engine for new platforms, cost the update against a licensed engine your artists and designers can use on day one.","i":"A custom engine is paid for again at every new platform and every tools upgrade. The switch fits when designer and artist tools are the weak point; it gives less when the engine's own tech is what makes the game.","a":"What would updating our engine cost compared with licensing one?","q":"“Once we took the decision to go multi-platform we decided to move to Unreal Engine 3 to allow us to start iterating gameplay much quicker.”","qa":"Mike Ball, co-founder and Chief Technical Ninja of Ninja Theory","st":"Ninja Theory Takes an Odyssey with Unreal Engine 3 for Namco Bandai Action Game","su":"https://www.unrealengine.com/blog/enslaved#:~:text=Once%20we%20took%20the%20decision,start%20iterating%20gameplay%20much%20quicker","sd":"Oct 2010","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"everwild","n":"Everwild","dv":"Rare","y":"2025","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2025","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Revealed Everwild in November 2019 while in pre-production and still exploring gameplay ideas","s":"8 months later Rare's studio head said the team was still playing around with gameplay ideas. VGC reported a from-scratch design restart in 2021, and Microsoft stopped development in July 2025 after more than a decade.","w":"Rare announced Everwild at X019 in November 2019 and confirmed it was in pre-production, after what VGC called many years of prototyping. A second trailer followed in July 2020. That month studio head Craig Duncan told Polygon the team was still playing around with gameplay ideas. Creative director Simon Woodroffe left in 2020; Gregg Mayles joined late that year. In June 2021 VGC reported that the small team had struggled to define a direction beyond its art style and soundtrack and that the design had been restarted from scratch. On 2 July 2025 Matt Booty announced that development had stopped.","r":"Design restarted from scratch in 2021; project cancelled in July 2025.","re":"Rare brought in Gregg Mayles as creative director and rebooted the design.","co":"Over a decade of development including prototyping; no release.","nf":false,"l":"Do not reveal a game until the team can state its core gameplay in one sentence; a trailer is not a prototype.","i":"A public reveal fixes the art and the promise while the design is still open, so every later restart happens under a deadline. VGC's sources, not Rare, link the missing direction to the reboot and the cancellation. A reveal after a proven loop costs little.","a":"Can we state this game's core gameplay in one sentence today?","q":"“We're still playing around with gameplay ideas.”","qa":"Craig Duncan, studio head of Rare","st":"Rare hasn't discussed Everwild much 'because it's still playing around with gameplay ideas'","su":"https://videogameschronicle.com/news/rare-hasnt-discussed-everwild-much-because-its-still-playing-around-with-gameplay-ideas#:~:text=still%20playing%20around,with%20gameplay%20ideas","sd":"Jul 2020","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"fable","n":"Fable","dv":"Big Blue Box","y":"2004","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/204030/","ls":"Steam","cv":"fable.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Dropped the Wishworld pitch for a fairy-tale RPG built on its terrain tech","s":"The new pitch filled an RPG gap in Microsoft's first-party lineup. After about 12 months of failed publisher meetings, Big Blue Box passed due diligence and signed.","w":"In 1998 Simon and Dene Carter founded Big Blue Box and built Wishworld, a multiplayer wizard battle prototype with its own terrain system. After nearly a year of pitching they were ready to sign, until Interplay announced Sacrifice, a Shiny game with the same premise. Peter Molyneux urged them to reuse the tech for the next idea on their list. In late 1999 they switched to an RPG in a fairy-tale world, running on the Wishworld terrain engine. Microsoft needed an RPG for its Xbox lineup and signed them after due diligence. Simon Carter told the story in March 2026.","r":"Big Blue Box signed with Microsoft to make the RPG.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Nearly a year of Wishworld pitching dropped; the terrain tech was kept.","nf":false,"l":"When a pitch stalls, rebuild it on the prototype tech you already have instead of starting from zero.","i":"The terrain system survived the change of genre, so the new pitch did not start from zero. The deal also needed timing: Microsoft happened to lack an RPG, and reused tech will not save a pitch no publisher needs.","a":"Which parts of our prototype would survive a change of game?","q":"“Peter pointed out it would be next to impossible to sell Wishworld to journalists as a new concept, and strongly encouraged us to see if we could use the tech we had built to pivot into the next game on our list.”","qa":"Simon Carter, co-founder of Big Blue Box Studios","st":"The Origins of Fable","su":"https://blog.simoncarter.me/blog/the-origins-of-fable/#:~:text=Peter%20pointed%20out%20it%20would,next%20game%20on%20our%20list","sd":"Mar 2026","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"fablelegends","n":"Fable Legends","dv":"Lionhead Studios","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2016","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built Fable Legends on Unreal Engine 4 rather than inventing new tools","s":"Designers built working prototypes instead of writing design documents. Programmers could step back from routine work and focus on custom features.","w":"Lionhead had long used Unreal Engine and stayed with it for Fable Legends, a co-op action game for the then unreleased Xbox One. In August 2013, executive producer Geoff Smith said the UE4 toolset let the team dive in without waiting for its own tech. Blueprint scripting let designers set up characters and AI routines and build prototypes themselves. Game director David Eckelberry said programmers, always a limited resource, could step aside. In March 2016 Microsoft stopped development and proposed closing Lionhead.","r":"Designers prototyped ideas themselves while programmers handled custom features.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When the hardware is not out yet, pick an engine whose tools let designers prototype before your own tech is ready.","i":"A mature toolset moves the first playable work from programmers to designers, which matters most while the hardware is still changing. Fast tools do not decide the outcome: Fable Legends was still cancelled in 2016.","a":"Can our designers build a prototype without waiting on programmers?","q":"“Where previously they’d have written a document, they can now build a working prototype to show off their ideas.”","qa":"Ben Brooks, lead content designer at Lionhead Studios","st":"Lionhead’s Fabled Leap to Xbox One with Unreal Engine 4","su":"https://www.unrealengine.com/blog/fable-legends?lang=en-US#:~:text=Where%20previously%20they%27d%20have%20written,show%20off%20their%20ideas","sd":"Aug 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3,"sh":"https://www.unrealengine.com/blog/fable-legends?lang=en-US#:~:text=Where%20previously,show%20off%20their%20ideas"}]},{"k":"factoriospaceage","n":"Factorio: Space Age","dv":"Wube Software","y":"2024","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/645390/","ls":"Steam","cv":"factoriospaceage.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Planned a bare-bones playthrough of the entire expansion as the first goal","s":"The first full run in 2022 took 370 hours and showed the expansion was too long and repetitive. After cuts, the next run took 240 hours and felt more fun.","w":"Wube announced the expansion in February 2021 and doubled the team to almost 30 people. Its first plan was a vertical slice: the most bare-bones way to play through the whole expansion. Earendel often drafted planet mechanics in Lua so they could be tested before C++ work, and the team usually cut each draft to half, then half again. In 2022 kovarex pushed through a first full playthrough over 7 months and 370 hours. It was too long, too repetitive and too grindy, so the team set new guidelines. Space Age shipped on 21 October 2024.","r":"Pacing problems showed up in 2022, two years before release.","re":"The team set guidelines for faster progression and cut Gleba's plants from 12 to 2.","co":"A first full playthrough took 7 months and 370 hours of play.","nf":false,"l":"Get the whole game playable from start to finish in its crudest form before you polish any single part.","i":"Length, repetition and grind only show up when someone plays everything in order. Cheap Lua drafts made cuts easy, since shaving a prototype costs less than rebuilding. The method suits a long systemic game, where pacing problems hide between the parts.","a":"Can someone play our game start to finish today, however rough?","q":"“The vertical slice was meant to be the most bare-bones way of playing through the expansion so we could start improving based on the feedback.”","qa":"Michal Kovařík (kovarex), founder and company director of Wube Software","st":"Friday Facts #417 - Space Age development","su":"https://www.factorio.com/blog/post/fff-417#:~:text=The%20vertical%20slice%20was%20meant,improving%20based%20on%20the%20feedback","sd":"Jun 2024","sy":"Participant account","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built support for independent surfaces years ahead of the planets expansion","s":"Mods had used multiple surfaces for years, so the code was already tested when planet work began. It saved trouble, though surfaces and planets still needed a lot of tweaking.","w":"Wube always knew it wanted an expansion about travelling to different planets. So support for independent surfaces sat in the Factorio code for years before the expansion started. Mods often used multiple surfaces, which tested the feature in real play. The Planet class was created on 26 January 2021, days before the expansion was announced. In June 2024 kovarex wrote that the existing support saved a lot of trouble, but surfaces and planets still needed many tweaks to meet the team's standards. Space Age shipped on 21 October 2024.","r":"Planet work began on code that mods had already tested.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If you know where your expansion is heading, build its core engine support early and get it used in the shipped game.","i":"Code that players and modders use for years gets tested for free, so the riskiest part of the expansion was already proven. This works when the direction is known early; hooks for features nobody plans become dead weight.","a":"Does our shipped game already use the tech our next project needs?","q":"“This saved us a lot of trouble, but there was still a lot of things to tweak to make the independent surfaces and planets work well enough to our standards.”","qa":"Michal Kovařík (kovarex), founder and company director of Wube Software","st":"Friday Facts #417 - Space Age development","su":"https://www.factorio.com/blog/post/fff-417#:~:text=This%20saved%20us%20a%20lot,well%20enough%20to%20our%20standards","sd":"Jun 2024","sy":"Participant account","w8":3}]},{"k":"fallout","n":"Fallout","dv":"Interplay","y":"1997","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/38400/","ls":"Steam","cv":"fallout.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Started Fallout as one programmer writing a custom engine with no spec or budget","s":"For six months there was no team, genre or design, and the first artist's work was thrown away. Interplay nearly cancelled the project twice before it shipped in October 1997 for about $3 million.","w":"In early 1994 Tim Cain began an engine in his spare time at Interplay instead of using BioWare's Infinity engine, with no licence, spec, budget or plan; nobody said no. For six months it was just him. The first artist had nothing to make because no genre was picked, and months of rocks and trees were thrown out. In late 1994 marketing pushed CEO Brian Fargo to cancel the project as too competitive with Interplay's D&D games; Cain begged and showed the prototype. Management later called it too nebulous until Chris Taylor's vision statement. Fallout shipped in October 1997.","r":"Months of unusable art, two near-cancellations, and a ship at about $3 million.","re":"Chris Taylor's vision statement gave the team and management a shared picture.","co":"About $3 million; 3.5 years; 1 person for 6 months, about 30 at peak.","nf":false,"l":"Write the vision statement before you staff up; an engine with no genre leaves artists nothing to build.","i":"An engine first is cheap when it is one person, but every hire after that needs a concept to work against, or their output is waste and management sees nothing to back. The lesson is about sequencing, not about avoiding custom engines.","a":"Can every new hire read what this game is before they start?","q":"“I just kind of wanted to make my own engine, and nobody said no.”","qa":"Timothy Cain, producer, programmer and designer of Fallout at Interplay","st":"20 years of Fallout: Lessons learned shipping games in the wasteland","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/20-years-of-i-fallout-i-lessons-learned-shipping-games-in-the-wasteland#:~:text=I%20just%20kind%20of%20wanted,nobody%20said%20no","sd":"Nov 2015","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Built the game's rules on the licensed GURPS tabletop system","s":"Steve Jackson Games later objected to the game's violence and art, and the licence was lost. The team rebuilt every system except perks in two weeks while the game nearly got cancelled.","w":"Interplay boss Brian Fargo asked staff which tabletop licence to build a game on, and the Fallout team picked GURPS, the rules they played at home. Cain taught the team GURPS by running small dungeon sessions. Steve Jackson Games, which owned GURPS, later objected to the game's design, art and violence, and for a while Fallout looked set to be cancelled. GURPS was torn out, and every system except perks was redesigned and recoded in two weeks. Lead designer Chris Taylor's homemade rules already held most of SPECIAL. Fallout shipped on 30 September 1997.","r":"The licence was lost, and the core rules had to be rebuilt fast.","re":"The team swapped in Chris Taylor's homemade rules, which became SPECIAL.","co":"Two weeks to redesign and recode every system except perks; near cancellation.","nf":false,"l":"Before building core systems on a licence, show the licensor your tone, violence and art, and get written sign-off.","i":"A licensor with approval rights can veto content late, and core rules touch every system. The two-week rebuild worked only because the lead designer already had a system holding most of SPECIAL; most teams would face months of rework.","a":"Has the licensor seen our most violent content and signed off on it?","q":"“I think all I added was ‘Luck’.”","qa":"Tim Cain, producer and lead programmer of Fallout at Interplay","st":"Tim Cain on Fallout reactivity and professional limits: “I miss making a game without constant oversight”","su":"https://gameworldobserver.com/2022/11/18/tim-cain-fallout-inverview-reactivity-rpg-system-professional-limits#:~:text=I%20think%20all%20I,added%20was%20%E2%80%98Luck%E2%80%99","sd":"Nov 2022","sy":"Participant podcast","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Placed every Fallout tile by hand rather than ask programmers for copy and paste","s":"Every map in Fallout 1 was built one tile at a time, which made levels hard to build. Feargus Urquhart only found out at the start of Fallout 2.","w":"Scott Everts created the maps for every level in Fallout 1. The Fallout Editor had no copy and paste, so each tile in the game was placed on its own. Everts found it made level building hard, but he did not want to bother the programmers. Interplay started Fallout 2 in mid-1997, and Fallout shipped that fall. Early on Fallout 2, Feargus Urquhart asked area designer John Deiley why he did not copy a finished building, and learned the editor had no copy and paste. In November 2015 Urquhart said he now asks every designer about missing tools.","r":"Every Fallout 1 map was built one tile at a time.","re":"Urquhart now asks level designers and artists if they have every tool.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Ask each level designer and artist which missing tool slows them most, and ask again at every milestone.","i":"Designers often treat a missing tool as their own problem and work around it quietly, so the cost never reaches the schedule. Asking catches the workarounds people know about; it will not surface tools nobody on the team expects to have.","a":"Is anyone on the team working around a missing tool right now?","q":"“He said it made making the levels hard, but he didn’t want to bother the programmers.”","qa":"Feargus Urquhart, Fallout 2 project lead and head of Black Isle Studios at Interplay","st":"20 years of Fallout: Lessons learned shipping games in the wasteland","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/20-years-of-i-fallout-i-lessons-learned-shipping-games-in-the-wasteland#:~:text=He%20said%20it%20made%20making,bother%20the%20programmers","sd":"Nov 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"fallout2","n":"Fallout 2","dv":"Black Isle","y":"1998","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/38410/","ls":"Steam","cv":"fallout2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Locked a Christmas 1998 ship date that assumed work began in mid-1997","s":"Most of the game was made in about 8 months instead of 18. Fallout 2 hit its date, but shipped buggy.","w":"While Fallout was still being finished, Interplay started a sequel on the same tech and assets and set a strict holiday 1998 date. The team set that date assuming work began in mid-1997, which left about 18 months. In practice it made most of the game in about 8 months, and project lead Feargus Urquhart pushed everyone hard. Fallout 2 made Christmas 1998, with bugs such as a car trunk that followed the player around maps. In November 2015 Urquhart said he had pushed the team too hard.","r":"Fallout 2 hit Christmas 1998 and shipped buggy.","re":"Urquhart later advised reasonable dates, or a delay instead of crunch.","co":"Most of an 18-month plan squeezed into about 8 months.","nf":false,"l":"Re-plan the ship date from the day full production really starts, not from the day the date was set.","i":"The date was fixed from a planned mid-1997 start, so when most of the work landed in the last 8 months, crunch and bugs absorbed the gap. Reusing Fallout's tech and assets did not make up for it.","a":"If full production started today, would our ship date still hold?","q":"“The biggest challenge of Fallout 2 was that we had set its launch date based upon having started it in the middle of 1997.”","qa":"Feargus Urquhart, project lead and head of Black Isle Studios at Interplay","st":"20 years of Fallout: Lessons learned shipping games in the wasteland","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/20-years-of-i-fallout-i-lessons-learned-shipping-games-in-the-wasteland#:~:text=The%20biggest%20challenge%20of%20Fallout,the%20middle%20of%201997","sd":"Nov 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"fallout76","n":"Fallout 76","dv":"Bethesda Game Studios","y":"2018","sc":"AAA","se":"Steam release 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1151340/","ls":"Steam","cv":"fallout76.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built its live-service game on the single-player Creation Engine to avoid retraining senior staff","s":"The engine fought a persistent world and needed ever more QA. NPCs were cut to protect the schedule, and the game shipped on its E3 date with bugs QA had already logged.","w":"According to former staff, Bethesda's higher-ups built Fallout 76 on the single-player Creation Engine because Rockville developers knew it, and retraining senior staff would cost time and money. The engine treats an area as existing only when the player is there; an online world must exist all the time. One source said adapting it squeezed the schedule, so leadership cut NPCs. QA kept needing more people. The game came out in November 2018 on the date set at E3. In June 2022 Kotaku published accounts from 10 former employees; one said QA had known every major launch bug.","r":"NPCs were cut, and design and QA staff crunched to meet fixed deadlines.","re":"Bethesda addressed problems raised by its multiplayer staff only after the launch reviews.","co":"Workdays of 10 to 16 hours; one source clocked over 60 hours a week.","nf":false,"l":"If you pick an engine because the team knows it, list what it cannot do for the new genre and who will build that.","i":"A single-player engine only simulates what is near one player; an online world must exist everywhere at once, so every system needs rework and more testing. Familiarity saves retraining cost only when the engine's gaps are small; here the studio also sidelined its online experts.","a":"What does our engine not do for this genre, and who builds it?","q":"","qa":"","st":"The Human Toll Of Fallout 76’s Disastrous Launch","su":"https://kotaku.com/bethesda-zenimax-fallout-76-crunch-development-1849033233#:~:text=While%20we%20had%20experienced%20multiplayer%20designers,routinely%20sidelined%20and%20ignored","sd":"Jun 2022","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"falloutonline","n":"Fallout Online","dv":"Interplay Entertainment","y":"2012","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2012","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Licensed back a Fallout MMO on condition of $30 million funding and a 2009 start","s":"Interplay had 4 employees and about $1.1 million in cash. Bethesda sued in 2009, saying neither condition was met, and a 2012 settlement voided the license.","w":"In April 2007 Interplay sold the Fallout IP to Bethesda and licensed back the right to make a Fallout MMO. The license required $30 million in financing and full-scale development by April 2009. At the end of 2007 Interplay had 4 employees and about $1.1 million in cash. Design began in November 2007. In September 2009 Bethesda sued to end the license, saying neither condition was met. In January 2010 Interplay signed Masthead Studios to develop it, aiming for a 2012 public beta. The January 2012 settlement voided the license, and ZeniMax paid Interplay $2 million.","r":"The license was voided in 2012, ending Interplay's Fallout MMO.","re":"Interplay signed Masthead Studios to develop the game and kept seeking funding.","co":"Needed $30 million with about $1.1 million in cash; the MMO was lost.","nf":false,"l":"Before you sign a deal with funding or start deadlines, show where the money and the team will come from.","i":"The license made the MMO's survival depend on money Interplay did not have, by a fixed date. With 4 staff and $1.1 million in cash, any funding delay put the license at risk. No court ruled on whether the conditions were met.","a":"Do we already have the money and team this contract's deadlines need?","q":"“We are planning to exploit our license back of \"Fallout\" MMOG and are reviewing the avenues for securing financing of at least $30 million to fund its production but no assurance can be made that we will be able to do so, and our license back may as a result be terminated.”","qa":"Interplay Entertainment Corp., annual report (Form 10-K) for 2007","st":"Interplay Entertainment Corp. Form 10-K, Annual Report for the fiscal year ended December 31, 2007","su":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1057232/000117091808000174/fm10k-2007.htm#:~:text=We%20are%20planning%20to%20exploit%20our,may%20as%20a%20result%20be%20terminated","sd":"2008","sy":"Company report","w8":4}]},{"k":"fallouttacticsbrotherhoodofsteel","n":"Fallout Tactics: Brotherhood of Steel","dv":"Micro Forte","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/38420/","ls":"Steam","cv":"fallouttacticsbrotherhoodofsteel.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Signed up for 12 months from first design to gold on a thin design document","s":"The genre drifted from turn-based RPG to real-time tactics, the first single-player demo failed in August 2000, and most missions were rewritten in the last 4 months.","w":"In May 1999 Micro Forte pitched Chimera to Interplay, whose 14 Degrees East studio proposed a Fallout tactical game instead. The ship date was fixed for Christmas 2000, 12 months from design to gold, and the spec left NPCs, progression and equipment undefined. Fan flak then pushed the team from linear missions to a world map with random encounters and bunkers. The genre drifted toward real-time tactics until the first single-player demo in August 2000 failed. The team refocused on tactics, rewrote most missions, hired 6 more artists, and went gold on 5 March 2001, about 4 months late.","r":"Gold on 5 March 2001, about 4 months late, with most missions rewritten.","re":"Micro Forte's CEO stepped in to refocus the game on tactics after the failed demo.","co":"4 months late, higher burn for longer, half the art and QA teams let go.","nf":false,"l":"Do not lock a 12-month gold date on a design document that leaves genre, progression and NPCs undefined.","i":"With 10 real months of development, every open design question got answered ad hoc by whoever was implementing, and levels built on assumptions were wasted when the demo failed. Oakden still credits the short cycle with focus; the problem was what stayed undefined.","a":"Which design questions are still open that the schedule assumes are closed?","q":"“The game absolutely stunk!”","qa":"Tony Oakden, producer at Micro Forte","st":"Postmortem: Micro Forte's Fallout Tactics","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-micro-forte-s-i-fallout-tactics-i-#:~:text=The%20game,absolutely%20stunk","sd":"Apr 2001","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Based the art budget on reusing Interplay's Fallout 1 and 2 models","s":"The models could not be recovered from tape backups. The art schedule collapsed, 6 more artists were hired, and every creature was rebuilt from scratch.","w":"In May 1999 Micro Forte's engine demo led Interplay's 14 Degrees East to propose a tactical Fallout game. The plan gave 12 months from design to gold master, for Christmas 2000. Art estimates assumed Interplay would supply the characters and creatures from Fallout 1 and 2. After work began, Interplay said it could not retrieve that art from its tape backups. Micro Forte asked for time and money and got only money. It hired 6 more artists and rebuilt the creatures. The game went gold on March 5, 2001, about 4 months late.","r":"The art schedule was blown and the art team grew to 13.","re":"Micro Forte hired 6 artists through a local academy and rebuilt the creatures.","co":"6 extra artists and a burn rate higher than planned.","nf":false,"l":"Before you base the art budget on reused assets, get the files in hand and load them in your engine.","i":"Reuse looks free on paper, so the art budget shrinks to fit it. When the files turn out to be lost, the work comes back as new art on a schedule with no slack left for it.","a":"Have we loaded every asset our budget assumes we can reuse?","q":"“This meant that our original art schedule was blown away and we needed many more artists.”","qa":"Tony Oakden, producer at Micro Forte","st":"Postmortem: Micro Forte's Fallout Tactics","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-micro-forte-s-i-fallout-tactics-i-#:~:text=This%20meant%20that%20our%20original,needed%20many%20more%20artists","sd":"Apr 2001","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"falloutnewvegas","n":"Fallout: New Vegas","dv":"Obsidian","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/22380/","ls":"Steam","cv":"falloutnewvegas.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Took on an 18-month Fallout game on an engine the team had never used","s":"Streaming, memory and scripting limits only became clear near the end. They caused many of the bugs and left a lot of content scrapped.","w":"Obsidian had 18 months to make Fallout: New Vegas, and nobody on the team had used Bethesda's engine. The most experienced person was Jorge Salgado, an Oblivion modder hired as a junior designer. How streaming and memory management worked only became clear near the end, and it caused many bugs. The scripting system was powerful but easy to misuse, so a lot of content was scrapped. The in-engine opening scene kept breaking and was pre-rendered. The game shipped on 19 October 2010. In May 2024 game director Josh Sawyer looked back on it.","r":"Late bugs, scrapped scripted content and a pre-rendered opening.","re":"The team avoided risky changes and pre-rendered the broken opening.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you sign a short schedule on someone else's engine, build one full area and stress its streaming, memory and scripts.","i":"Streaming and memory limits in this engine only show near the end, when the world is full, so a team new to it learns them too late on a short schedule. A team that knows the engine, as Bethesda did, faces far less of this.","a":"Have we tested streaming and memory on a full-size area yet?","q":"“This was the source of many of our bugs because much of that information isn't clear until you approach the end of development.”","qa":"Josh Sawyer, game director of Fallout: New Vegas at Obsidian Entertainment","st":"Josh Sawyer Reflects on Design Evolution: From Icewind Dale to Pentiment","su":"https://feed4gamers.com/game-news/297795/josh-sawyer-reflects-on-design-evolution-from-icewind-dale-to-pentiment.htm#:~:text=This%20was%20the%20source%20of,the%20end%20of%20development","sd":"May 2024","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"farcry3blooddragon","n":"Far Cry 3: Blood Dragon","dv":"Ubisoft Montreal","y":"2013","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/233270/","ls":"Steam","cv":"farcry3blooddragon.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Chose new assets and a whole new world for a 6-month standalone DLC","s":"Reusing Far Cry 3's engine and systems made the date possible, but the extra art scope ran out the clock. Two planned boss fights were dropped, one replaced by a pixel-art cutscene.","w":"Before Far Cry 3 shipped in late 2012, executive producer Dan Hay asked the team for unexpected DLC ideas. Dean Evans pitched a 1980s sci-fi parody on Far Cry 3's engine and systems. Ubisoft's editorial team in Paris greenlit it with 6 months to finish. The team kept enemy design simple but built new art, a new setting and giant dragons. Time ran short, so a boss fight with The Plague and a final fight with Colonel Sloan were dropped. Blood Dragon launched on 1 May 2013 and passed half a million downloads.","r":"Planned boss fights were cut to fit the 6-month schedule.","re":"The team swapped complex lighting work for a simple glow behind enemies.","co":"Cut content: 2 planned boss fights and a scripted giant-dragon finale.","nf":false,"l":"On a short project that reuses an engine, list every asset built from scratch and cut that list before greenlight.","i":"Engine and systems reuse saves code time, but a new setting resets art and world-building to zero. The schedule was sized for a DLC and the art scope for a new game. Reuse saves the most when the content is reused too.","a":"How many assets on this short project are built from scratch?","q":"“I think our biggest hurdle was our own fault, which was our own ambition.”","qa":"Dean Evans, creative director of Far Cry 3: Blood Dragon at Ubisoft Montreal","st":"The ‘80s Strike Back: The Complete Story Behind Blood Dragon","su":"https://www.gameinformer.com/b/features/archive/2013/07/26/the-80s-strike-back-the-complete-story-behind-blood-dragon.aspx#:~:text=I%20think%20our%20biggest%20hurdle,which%20was%20our%20own%20ambition","sd":"Jul 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"fez","n":"Fez","dv":"Polytron","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/224760/","ls":"Steam","cv":"fez.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Rendered the Fez prototype with hardware geometry instancing, a Shader Model 3 technique","s":"About 75% of graphics cards still only supported Shader Model 2.0. The last couple of weeks of the 101-day IGF build went into rewriting the engine.","w":"Phil Fish and programmer Renaud Bédard of KOKOROMI worked on Fez in their spare time, without funding, for 101 days before the IGF entry deadline. Bédard chose hardware geometry instancing from the start, not knowing it was a Shader Model 3.0 technique. About 75% of machines still had Shader Model 2.0 cards. So the last couple of weeks went into an engine rewrite, on top of collision and polygon reduction work. Bédard described it in a February 2008 interview, when Fish expected about a year of work left. The PC version reached Steam in May 2013.","r":"The last couple of weeks of the build went into an engine rewrite.","re":"Bédard rewrote the engine so it would run on Shader Model 2.0 cards.","co":"About 2 weeks of the programmer's time in a 101-day build.","nf":false,"l":"Before you pick a rendering technique, check which hardware features it needs against the cards your target players own.","i":"Bédard picked the technique for what it did, not for the hardware it needed, and the gap showed only near the deadline. On a 101-day build, 2 weeks is a large share; on a long project the same slip is small.","a":"Does every rendering feature we use run on our minimum spec?","q":"“So the last couple of weeks were engine-rewrite hell.”","qa":"Renaud Bédard, programmer on Fez at KOKOROMI","st":"IGF 08 Interviews Part 4","su":"https://gamedev.net/tutorials/industry/interviews/igf-08-interviews-part-4-r2464#:~:text=So%20the%20last%20couple%20of,weeks%20were%20engine%2Drewrite%20hell","sd":"Feb 2008","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"finalfantasyvii","n":"Final Fantasy VII","dv":"Square","y":"1997","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/39140/","ls":"Steam","cv":"finalfantasyvii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Art & style","t":"Made Final Fantasy VII a real-time 3D game in one cinematic style","s":"Square hired far more CG staff, and its programmers had to learn 3D and the PlayStation while building the game. The team had about a year for it.","w":"After Final Fantasy VI, Square's staff made an experimental CG movie and decided the next game should render in real time; Kitase recalled about 3 months to work that out. Because FF7 would be a real 3D game, the team drew very detailed designs, locked the script and storyboarded scenes before production. Square hired far more CG staff. Programmers learned the PlayStation and 3D while building the game in about a year. The battle system got about half a year and the last dungeon was rushed. The team described this in 1997 interviews.","r":"Square grew its CG staff, and the last dungeon was rushed.","re":"Existing staff learned CG from the hires and built 80 to 90% of the field.","co":"Many more CG hires; the battle system squeezed into about half a year.","nf":false,"l":"Before production on new hardware, list the skills the team lacks and budget both the hires and the learning time.","i":"Real-time 3D on a new console needed CG skills Square's game staff lacked, so it hired them and trained its own people. Detailed plans told the team what to build; they did not shorten the learning, which still squeezed battle and the last dungeon.","a":"Which skills does this game need that nobody on our team has?","q":"“But we had to learn the hardware and create the game all in the same year.”","qa":"Ken Narita, main programmer at Square","st":"Final Fantasy VII – 1997 Developer Interviews","su":"https://shmuplations.com/ff7/#:~:text=But%20we%20had%20to%20learn,in%20the%20same%20year","sd":"1997, translated Dec 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"finalfantasyviiremake","n":"Final Fantasy VII Remake","dv":"Square Enix","y":"2020","sc":"AAA","se":"Expanded edition 2022","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1462040/","ls":"Steam","cv":"finalfantasyviiremake.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Started the remake with an external partner, then pulled development in-house in 2017","s":"Square Enix recruited core staff to rebuild the team two years after the E3 2015 reveal. The game shipped on 10 April 2020, almost 5 years after it was announced.","w":"Square Enix teased the remake at E3 2015 and in December 2015 named CyberConnect2 as an external partner. Producer Yoshinori Kitase told Famitsu that the partner's production tastes differed from Square Enix's, so the company kept close to the work, and director Tetsuya Nomura had the first gameplay trailer pulled closer to the company's style. On 27 May 2017 project lead Naoki Hamaguchi said on a livestream that the company had decided to shift to an internal setup, while job listings sought planners, designers and a programmer. The remake shipped on 10 April 2020: 3.5 million units in 3 days.","r":"Core team rebuilt in-house in 2017; release came almost 5 years after the reveal.","re":"Recruited battle and level planners, designers and a programmer for the internal team.","co":"About 2 years of external co-development before the in-house reset; money not disclosed.","nf":false,"l":"Decide at greenlight whether an outside studio owns production or only supports it, and keep that split fixed.","i":"A partner whose production taste differs from yours needs constant supervision, and once the publisher wants full control of quality and schedule the fix is to rebuild the team in-house. A publisher that can hire affords that reset; a small studio cannot.","a":"Who owns production quality on this project: us or the partner?","q":"“As for the whole story, (it’s a sensitive topic, but) until now, development was moving forward with external cooperation, but the company has decided to shift to an internal setup, including mass production and quality, because we want to control everything, including quality, on a stable schedule.”","qa":"Naoki Hamaguchi, project lead on Final Fantasy VII Remake at Square Enix","st":"Final Fantasy VII Remake development shifts from external cooperation to internal focus","su":"https://gematsu.com/2017/05/final-fantasy-vii-remake-development-shifts-external-cooperation-internal-focus#:~:text=until%20now%2C%20development%20was%20moving,on%20a%20stable%20schedule","sd":"May 2017","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the remake on Unreal Engine instead of a Square Enix in-house engine","s":"Many new staff already knew Unreal, and Kingdom Hearts III veterans shared what they had learned. Co-director Naoki Hamaguchi said learning periods got shorter.","w":"Square Enix usually made its AAA Final Fantasy games on in-house engines, so every new team member needed time to learn the tools. The remake was the first game in the series on Unreal. Development first ran through external partners; around 2017 Square Enix moved it in-house while keeping many partners. Hamaguchi said a public engine suited a team drawing on expertise inside and outside the company. Staff from Kingdom Hearts III joined and spread their Unreal know-how. In June 2020, after launch, Hamaguchi credited the engine with shorter learning periods.","r":"New staff needed less time to learn the tools.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When you expect to add staff and partners mid-project, pick tools that the people you will hire already know.","i":"An in-house engine makes every new hire pay a learning cost, and that cost grows as the team ramps up or adds partners. A public engine cuts it only as far as the people you hire already know it.","a":"Do the people we plan to hire already know our engine?","q":"“This led to shorter learning periods, which was a huge plus for development efficiency.”","qa":"Naoki Hamaguchi, co-director of Final Fantasy VII Remake at Square Enix","st":"How Square Enix leveraged Unreal Engine to modernize FINAL FANTASY VII REMAKE","su":"https://www.unrealengine.com/developer-interviews/how-square-enix-leveraged-unreal-engine-to-modernize-final-fantasy-vii-remake#:~:text=This%20led%20to%20shorter%20learning,plus%20for%20development%20efficiency","sd":"Jun 2020","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"finalfantasyxii","n":"Final Fantasy XII","dv":"Square Enix","y":"2006","sc":"AAA","se":"Remaster 2017","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/595520/","ls":"Steam","cv":"finalfantasyxii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Replaced separate battle screens with real-time fights in the field, run by Gambits","s":"Nobody could tell whether the system worked until the very end. As content grew, getting it all playable took far longer than planned; development ran about 6 years.","w":"Final Fantasy XII set out to drop the series' switch to a separate battle screen and fight in real time inside the world. Fearing real-time commands would be too hard to control, the team built Gambits to automate party actions. Lead programmer Takashi Katano said everyone knew from the start Gambits would be hard to build. Battle designer Hiroyuki Ito found it hard to judge whether it worked until the very end. Meanwhile the content volume exploded. The PS2 game took about 6 years. In July 2017 project manager Hiroaki Kato said integration took far longer than expected.","r":"Getting the game playable and integrated took far longer than planned.","re":"The team held back a planned job system until the International version.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before content production ramps up, build a playable slice of the new battle system with a realistic amount of content.","i":"A system whose fun only shows when it is finished gives no early warning, and each new piece of content makes it harder to fit together. Kato names content growth as the bigger delay, so a proven system alone would not have saved the schedule.","a":"Can we play our new core system at full content density yet?","q":"“From the very early stages of the project, we all knew the gambit system was going to be difficult to create, but we really wanted to go for that idea so we pushed on.”","qa":"Takashi Katano, lead programmer on Final Fantasy XII at Square Enix","st":"Extended Play: How Final Fantasy XII’s gambit created one of the most distinct RPGs ever","su":"https://blog.playstation.com/archive/2017/07/07/extended-play-how-final-fantasy-xiis-gambit-created-one-of-the-most-distinct-rpgs-ever#:~:text=From%20the%20very%20early%20stages,so%20we%20pushed%20on","sd":"Jul 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"finalfantasyxiii","n":"Final Fantasy XIII","dv":"Square Enix","y":"2009","sc":"AAA","se":"Port 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/292120/","ls":"Steam","cv":"finalfantasyxiii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Ramped up development with no playable build to share the vision","s":"Specs stalled, key elements were still disputed late in development, and the team only lined up behind one vision once an unplanned demo gave it something to play.","w":"Square Enix announced Final Fantasy XIII with a concept trailer at E3 2006, before anything was playable. Few team members saw that trailer as the target for the game. A universal engine built to serve every project could not settle its specs, so neither could the game, and data was built without coordination. Even late in development, the team still disagreed on key elements. An unplanned demo for the Japan-only Advent Children Complete Blu-ray became the vertical slice, and after it the team did not miss a milestone. The game launched in North America in March 2010.","r":"Specs stayed open, and the timetable suffered while debates went unresolved.","re":"An unplanned demo became the vertical slice; no milestone was missed after.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before ramping staff for full production, build one playable slice at final quality that the whole team can play.","i":"Without a playable build, each department works from its own picture of the game, and debates stay abstract. A slice turns them into concrete ones. It does not fix an engine trying to serve too many projects.","a":"Can every team member play a slice of the game today?","q":"“Although a vertical slice is commonplace in Western development, this was never actually practiced with our teams unless there was a company requirement.”","qa":"Motomu Toriyama and Akihiko Maeda, Square Enix","st":"Exclusive: Behind The Scenes Of Square Enix's Final Fantasy XIII","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/exclusive-behind-the-scenes-of-square-enix-s-i-final-fantasy-xiii-i-#:~:text=Although%20a%20vertical%20slice%20is,was%20a%20company%20requirement","sd":"Oct 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"finalfantasyxiv","n":"Final Fantasy XIV Online","dv":"Square Enix","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"Relaunch 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/39210/","ls":"Steam","cv":"finalfantasyxiv.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Built FFXIV 1.0 with graphics quality as the top priority and without studying other MMOs","s":"A single flowerpot used 1,000 polygons and 150 lines of shader code, on-screen player characters were capped at 20, the launch failed, and the game was rebuilt from scratch in 2 years 8 months.","w":"The original team fixated on graphics quality and, per Yoshida, was unstudied about MMORPG development, assuming fans would follow through mishaps. One flowerpot carried 1,000 polygons and 150 lines of shader code, so on-screen player characters were limited to 20, and the one continuous map looked repetitive. The 2010 launch failed; in December 2010 Square Enix renewed the development structure under Yoshida. He judged it faster to rebuild the server with a new design than to patch. A Realm Reborn shipped on 27 August 2013, 2 years 8 months later, and sold 1.45 million units by 30 September.","r":"The 1.0 launch failed and the whole game had to be rebuilt from scratch.","re":"Rebuilt server and client under a new producer after months of design-only work.","co":"2 years 8 months of rebuild while the original stayed live.","nf":false,"l":"Put the play experience above visual quality in the concept, and study the genre's current leaders before you design.","i":"Graphics-first spending put 1,000 polygons into a flowerpot, so the engine could not show a crowd, which an MMO needs. The rebuild was possible because Square Enix chose fan trust over a shutdown; a smaller publisher may not get that choice.","a":"Is play experience ranked above visuals in our concept?","q":"“They were concentrating too much on just trying to upgrade the quality of the graphics.”","qa":"Naoki Yoshida, producer and director of Final Fantasy XIV, Square Enix","st":"Understanding the successful relaunch of Final Fantasy XIV","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/understanding-the-successful-relaunch-of-i-final-fantasy-xiv-i-#:~:text=They%20were%20concentrating%20too%20much,quality%20of%20the%20graphics","sd":"Apr 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":5},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Reboot & relaunch","t":"Rebuilt the whole MMO in under 2 years while patching the live 1.0 alongside","s":"Square Enix chose the rebuild over patching alone, even though it meant splitting the team and paying for two MMOs. A Realm Reborn launched in August 2013, sold 1.45 million units in 5 weeks and passed 600,000 paying users.","w":"Final Fantasy XIV launched on 30 September 2010 and failed; in November 2010 Square Enix put Naoki Yoshida in charge. After 7 weeks of research he told management patches could not fix it and offered two plans: patch only, or patch 1.0 while building a new FFXIV to ship within the PS3's life. Management chose the rebuild. Yoshida split the team in half to finish in under 2 years. The new version was announced on 14 October 2011. A Realm Reborn launched on 27 August 2013 and sold 1.45 million units by 30 September, with paying users past 600,000.","r":"2 years of parallel work on two MMOs; 1.45 million units sold in 5 weeks.","re":"Square Enix capped server access and paused digital sales to cope with demand.","co":"Team split in half for 2 years to run 1.0 and build 2.0.","nf":false,"l":"When patches cannot fix the shipped core, fund a full rebuild as a new project and keep the old one running for its players.","i":"Patching a broken MMO keeps damaging the brand; a rebuild under the same name costs two teams for two years but can win players back. A flagship brand, a PS3 promise and corporate backing justified the spend here; few studios can afford it.","a":"If patches cannot save this, have we costed a full rebuild?","q":"“Plan B was apply patches to the current version of Final Fantasy 14, but alongside that, in the background we would build a completely new Final Fantasy 14.”","qa":"Naoki Yoshida, producer and director of Final Fantasy XIV at Square Enix","st":"FINAL FANTASY XIV Documentary Part #1 - \"One Point O\"","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xs0yQKI7Yw4&t=2766s","sd":"Jun 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Froze code until a tiny team finished the design, while everyone else patched 1.0","s":"The rebuilt game relaunched in 2013 after 2 years and 9 months of development, against the 4 to 5 years Yoshida says an MMO usually takes.","w":"Naoki Yoshida took over Final Fantasy XIV in late November 2010. After 7 weeks of research, corporate chose his Plan B: keep patching 1.0 while building a new game in the background, inside the PS3's life cycle. In the design phase, about 0.5% of staff spent roughly 2 months on some 400 core designs, and programmers had a rule not to write code. Everyone else worked on 1.0 updates, sharing workflows with the new game. Yoshida tracked tasks down to 2-hour spec documents. The new version relaunched in 2013.","r":"A full MMO rebuild shipped in under 3 years.","re":"After 1.0 closed in November 2012, the patch team joined the main team.","co":"2 years of running 1.0 and the new game side by side.","nf":false,"l":"Have a tiny group lock the core design before programmers start coding, and keep the rest of the team on existing work.","i":"Code written before the design settles gets thrown away, and an MMO has too many linked systems to redo. The 1.0 patches kept staff busy and tested workflows. It needs a lead who answers design questions fast, and Yoshida warns against copying his micromanagement.","a":"Is the core design locked before our programmers start writing code?","q":"“Plan B was apply patches to the current version of Final Fantasy 14, but alongside that, in the background we would build a completely new Final Fantasy 14.”","qa":"Naoki Yoshida, producer and director of Final Fantasy XIV at Square Enix","st":"FINAL FANTASY XIV Documentary Part #1 - \"One Point O\"","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xs0yQKI7Yw4&t=2766s","sd":"Jun 2017","sy":"Participant documentary","w8":4}]},{"k":"finalfantasyxv","n":"Final Fantasy XV","dv":"Square Enix","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"Port 2018","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/637650/","ls":"Steam","cv":"finalfantasyxv.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Rebooted Versus XIII as Final Fantasy XV on new consoles at 25 percent complete","s":"Announced in 2006, the project had reached only 20 to 25 percent, with no designs fixed and technology from the PS2 era. Four more years of work followed; the game shipped in November 2016 and broke even on day one.","w":"Final Fantasy Versus XIII was announced in 2006. It entered full production around 2011, on technology XV director Hajime Tabata later called an extension of the PS2 era. Around 2012, with the game at 20 to 25 percent and no character designs or visuals fixed, Square Enix restarted it as Final Fantasy XV for the next console generation; the team took the Versus XIII setting apart and kept only what made sense. Final Fantasy XV shipped on 29 November 2016, 10 years after the announcement, shipped 5 million on day one and, by Square Enix's account, broke even that day.","r":"Four more years of development on new hardware before release.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"4 more years (2012 to 2016); 10 years from announcement to release.","nf":false,"l":"Before moving a long-running project to new hardware, measure what is really fixed; if little is, restart it as pre-production.","i":"Six years produced concept and world changes but no fixed designs, so the move to new hardware meant taking the setting apart and redoing much of it. A reboot still needs a full cycle: XV took 4 more years.","a":"What percentage of this project is really fixed before we change hardware?","q":"“It’s a little bit rough, but in terms of how much it had progressed in terms of development, Versus XIII was around 20 to 25 percent,”","qa":"Hajime Tabata, director of Final Fantasy XV, Square Enix","st":"Final Fantasy Versus XIII was around 25% complete when it became Final Fantasy XV","su":"https://novacrystallis.com/2015/08/final-fantasy-versus-xiii-was-around-25-complete-when-it-became-final-fantasy-xv/#:~:text=It%E2%80%99s%20a%20little%20bit%20rough,20%20to%2025%20percent","sd":"Aug 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Made believable travel companions one of 3 core keywords for the game","s":"Each companion needed small habits and its own sense of distance in its relationship with Noctis. Director Hajime Tabata said that work moved slowly and took a lot of AI and animation effort.","w":"After Final Fantasy Versus XIII was restarted as FFXV, the team set 3 keywords: Journey, Companions and Car. The companions had to feel real so players would care about the party. The team worked on small habits and behaviors, down to the distance each one keeps in their relationship with Noctis. Combat also leaned on fighting alongside them, and the team spent a lot of time tuning enemy AI. In April 2016, ahead of a planned 30 September release, Tabata said this work moved slowly and needed a lot of time in AI and animation.","r":"Companion behavior became a long-running AI and animation workload.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If a character feature is a core pillar, budget AI and animation time for its everyday behavior, not just its cutscenes.","i":"Companions feel real through many small behaviors, and each one needs AI and animation work, so the cost builds slowly and is easy to under-plan. It weighs most when the party is on screen all the time, as on FFXV's road trip.","a":"Have we budgeted AI and animation time for companions' everyday behavior?","q":"“This kind of development doesn’t happen in leaps and bounds, needing a lot of time and effort in AI and animation, but I think it’s shaping up into something we can be proud of.”","qa":"Hajime Tabata, director of Final Fantasy XV at Square Enix","st":"Head-to-head with Hajime Tabata and Takeshi Nozue about FFXV - Making FF Great Again","su":"https://www.famitsu.com/matome/ff15/interview01_EN.html#:~:text=This%20kind%20of%20development,we%20can%20be%20proud%20of","sd":"Apr 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"finalfantasyxvi","n":"Final Fantasy XVI","dv":"Square Enix","y":"2023","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2515020/","ls":"Steam","cv":"finalfantasyxvi.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Had designers dress Valisthea's towns without input from the lore team","s":"The areas filled with generic barrels and did not feel lived in. Work got much easier once a member of the scenario and lore team joined the designers.","w":"Early in Final Fantasy XVI's development, designers placed objects across Valisthea on their own. The result did not feel real: art director Hiroshi Minagawa recalled deserts with nothing but barrels everywhere. The studio brought a member of the scenario and lore team to the designers to explain each town's lore and share pictures of what each area should be. With less clutter and better set dressing, towns began to reflect their regions. In a May 2023 interview before launch, Minagawa said things got a lot easier once that person joined.","r":"Early areas felt generic until the lore team got involved.","re":"The studio brought a scenario and lore team member to work with designers.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before designers start set dressing, put someone from the lore team with them to say what each place is and why.","i":"Designers placing props without a backstory fall back on generic filler, because nothing tells them what a place needs. A lore writer next to them turns history into concrete set dressing. That only works if the lore is written before levels are dressed.","a":"Does each area have a lore brief before set dressing starts?","q":"“And once that person from the lore team entered, you know, joined with the designers then things got a lot easier.”","qa":"Hiroshi Minagawa, art director of Final Fantasy XVI at Square Enix","st":"How Square Enix built Final Fantasy XVI’s fantastical, believable, lived-in world","su":"https://blog.playstation.com/2023/05/22/how-square-enix-built-final-fantasy-xvis-fantastical-believable-lived-in-world/#:~:text=And%20once%20that%20person%20from,things%20got%20a%20lot%20easier","sd":"May 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"fireemblemawakening","n":"Fire Emblem Awakening","dv":"Intelligent Systems","y":"2012","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/3ds/afej/index.html","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"fireemblemawakening.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Reframed the next Fire Emblem as a series compilation after a year of stalled planning","s":"The plan came together in about a month under the rule 'put in anything that achieves the theme'. Workload jumped as features piled in, and Awakening shipped in April 2012.","w":"While New Mystery of the Emblem (July 2010) was being finished, planning for the next game struggled for about a year with no exit. Nintendo producer Hitoshi Yamagami proposed to Masahiro Higuchi and Kouhei Maeda a compilation of everything Fire Emblem instead of a complete break from the past. The team then put in anything that achieved the theme; map units got faces that change by character, which Higuchi says sharply increased the work. Iwata observed that each added system multiplies the tasks. Fire Emblem Awakening released in Japan on 19 April 2012.","r":"A year-long planning stall ended; the plan was set in about a month.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"About a year of stalled planning before the reframing; workload rose as features were added.","nf":false,"l":"When concept work stalls for months, change the framing question, not the amount of exploration time.","i":"A complete break gave the team nothing to build on, while a compilation let them pull from 13 games, so the plan came fast. The same rule invites features, and the team accepted the multiplied work; it worked because the series history was deep.","a":"Is our concept stuck because the question we set is unanswerable?","q":"““集大成”というテーマは考えやすいフィールドでしたので、ほぼ１カ月で企画がまとまりました。” (The compilation theme was an easy field to think in, so the plan came together in about a month.)","qa":"Masahiro Higuchi, project manager at Intelligent Systems","st":"社長が訊く『ファイアーエムブレム 覚醒』","su":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/3ds/interview/afej/vol1/index2.html#:~:text=%E2%80%9C%E9%9B%86%E5%A4%A7%E6%88%90%E2%80%9D%E3%81%A8%E3%81%84%E3%81%86%E3%83%86%E3%83%BC%E3%83%9E%E3%81%AF,%E4%BC%81%E7%94%BB%E3%81%8C%E3%81%BE%E3%81%A8%E3%81%BE%E3%82%8A%E3%81%BE%E3%81%97%E3%81%9F","sd":"2012","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"fireemblemfates","n":"Fire Emblem Fates","dv":"Intelligent Systems","y":"2015","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Story & world","t":"Asked manga writer Shin Kibayashi for 3 short plots of about 10 pages each","s":"Kibayashi wrote about 500 pages for the first route alone, and the other 2 routes then needed the same. The writing ended up at about 2 books' worth.","w":"Fire Emblem Fates was planned as 3 versions with fully different stories, due in Japan on 25 June 2015. Director Kouhei Maeda, a big fan of Kibayashi, assumed someone that busy could only write plots. The brief was about 10 manuscript pages per route. Kibayashi began with the Hoshido route, used the 10 pages on its opening alone and wrote about 500, because his characters only come alive in dialogue. The other 2 routes then needed the same amount. Before launch, Kibayashi told Iwata Asks the job had been fun and painful at once.","r":"The script grew from about 10 pages per route to about 500.","re":"Maeda took the script, which already followed the series' one-battle-per-chapter rule.","co":"About 500 pages per route against a brief of about 10.","nf":false,"l":"Before you commission a story, ask how the writer works, and size the brief to that method, not to a page count.","i":"Kibayashi builds stories by writing dialogue until the characters move on their own, so a plot-only brief could not hold. With 3 routes, growth in the first route set the volume for the other 2.","a":"Does our writing brief match how this writer actually works?","q":"“I had to write the same amount for the other two stories.”","qa":"Shin Kibayashi, story writer for Fire Emblem Fates","st":"Iwata Asks: Fire Emblem Fates","su":"https://iwataasks.nintendo.com/interviews/3ds/fire-emblem-fates/0/2/#:~:text=I%20had%20to%20write%20the,the%20other%20two%20stories","sd":"2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"fireemblemthreehouses","n":"Fire Emblem: Three Houses","dv":"Intelligent Systems","y":"2019","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/fire-emblem-three-houses-switch/","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"fireemblemthreehouses.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Brought in Koei Tecmo's strategy team to shorten development for a 2019 Switch release","s":"The game hit its 2019 window. The three-nation setting was designed around the partner's history-strategy team, and the game grew to about twice its planned volume.","w":"Nintendo and Intelligent Systems started planning a new Fire Emblem around the release of Fire Emblem Fates, first for 3DS. They paused it to make Fire Emblem Echoes, then moved the project to Switch. Nintendo wanted it out in 2019 at the latest, and a normal schedule looked longer. Koei Tecmo was already making Fire Emblem Warriors, so Nintendo asked it for help and was introduced to its Shibusawa Kou team. The three-nation setting came after that choice. The game shipped on 26 July 2019, at about twice the volume first planned.","r":"Shipped in July 2019 with about twice the planned content.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Choose your co-development partner before you lock the concept, then design toward that team's strengths.","i":"Koei Tecmo already knew the series from Warriors, so the choice was quick, and the concept grew around its history-strategy team. Extra capacity also invites extra content: volume doubled here, and the date still held.","a":"Have we picked our partner before locking the concept?","q":"“ですが、通常の開発ですともうちょっと時間がかかりそうだったため、開発期間を短縮するために、開発をしていただけるメーカーさんを探していたんです。” (But a normal development looked like it would take a bit longer, so to shorten the development period we were looking for a company that could develop it for us.)","qa":"Genki Yokota, director at Nintendo","st":"『ファイアーエムブレム 風花雪月』開発陣インタビュー。最新作で描かれる壮大な大河ドラマ","su":"https://www.famitsu.com/news/201908/06180699.html#:~:text=%E9%80%9A%E5%B8%B8%E3%81%AE%E9%96%8B%E7%99%BA%E3%81%A7%E3%81%99%E3%81%A8,%E6%8E%A2%E3%81%97%E3%81%A6%E3%81%84%E3%81%9F%E3%82%93%E3%81%A7%E3%81%99","sd":"Jul 2019, reprinted Aug 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"florence","n":"Florence","dv":"Mountains","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1102130/","ls":"Steam","cv":"florence.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Defined Florence as a relationship story told through 3D jigsaw puzzle chapters","s":"The 3D puzzles were awkward on a touchscreen and added nothing to the story. Nine hard months of chapter prototypes followed, and the game took 22 months against a 12-month plan.","w":"Ken Wong started Mountains with an Annapurna deal and a handbook rule to ship a game about every 12 months. After 6 months of prototypes, the concept was a relationship told through 3D jigsaw puzzles, one variation per chapter. The next 9 months were an uphill battle: turning 3D objects on a touchscreen was awkward, and depth added nothing to the story, so the team moved to 2D. Each chapter prototype took 2 or 3 weeks, and many failed to carry the emotion. The first full playthrough came in July 2017. Florence shipped in February 2018, after 22 months.","r":"First playthrough came at month 15; the game took 22 months, not 12.","re":"Wong dropped the jigsaw rule and let each chapter mix several mechanics.","co":"9 uphill months; 2 to 3 weeks per chapter prototype, some thrown away.","nf":false,"l":"Before you plan every chapter around one mechanic, check on the target device that the mechanic adds to the story.","i":"Every chapter hung on one mechanic, so when 3D manipulation failed on a phone, each chapter had to be rethought. The lost months were affordable because Wong had planned room for failure and Annapurna stayed patient.","a":"Does our core mechanic carry the story's emotion on the target device?","q":"“I slowly realized that my initial exploration direction of 3D manipulation was just not going to work out.”","qa":"Ken Wong, creative director and founder of Mountains","st":"Letting Go: A Florence Postmortem","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MhI03UIT70c&t=455","sd":"Mar 2019","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MhI03UIT70c&t=465s"}]},{"k":"flower","n":"Flower","dv":"thatgamecompany","y":"2009","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2019","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/966330/","ls":"Steam","cv":"flower.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Cut Flower's planned dozens of levels to 6 and narrowed its theme","s":"The first idea, an abstract feeling of sharing love, was beyond the team's experience. Harmony with your surroundings was within reach, and the designer said the game came out much stronger.","w":"thatgamecompany started Flower with a more ambitious concept: giving players an abstract feeling of sharing love. The team soon realized it was not experienced enough to pull that off. It re-envisioned the game as reaching harmony between yourself and your surroundings. The story arc had been planned as dozens of levels, and the team cut it to 6. Later it also cut free-roaming elements to finish the core game. Flower came out on PS3, and in May 2009 designer Nick Clark said narrowing the scope made the game much stronger.","r":"Flower shipped as a short, linear game of 6 levels.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you plan levels, ask whether your team has the experience to deliver the core feeling, and narrow it if not.","i":"A vague emotional goal is hard to hit, and dozens of levels mean dozens of attempts at it. A clearer theme let 6 levels carry the whole arc. The cut fits a short, linear game built around one emotional journey.","a":"Has our team ever delivered the feeling our concept promises?","q":"“In the end, the game was much stronger because we were able to successfully narrow the scope into something achievable and focus only on the most important aspects of the game.”","qa":"Nick Clark, designer at thatgamecompany","st":"Interview: Nick Clark","su":"https://www.gameandplayer.net/articles/2009/05/interview_thatgamecompanys_nic.html#:~:text=In%20the%20end%2C%20the%20game,important%20aspects%20of%20the%20game","sd":"May 2009","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"footballmanager25","n":"Football Manager 25","dv":"Sports Interactive","y":"2025","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2025","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Combined a switch to Unity with the largest-ever UI and graphics renewal in FM25","s":"Reaching the target quality took more time than expected, so Sega cancelled FM25 in February 2025 and wrote off about ¥4.0 billion of development assets.","w":"Sega changed the Football Manager engine to Unity starting with FM25, aiming for the largest-ever renewal of the series' UI and graphics. By the scheduled March 2025 release it was found that more time was needed than expected to reach the quality targeted. On 7 February 2025 Sega announced the cancellation of FM25's development and sales and recorded a one-time expense of about ¥4.0 billion, mainly a write-down of development assets. Football Manager 2024 stayed on sale and a subscription was explored. Some assets carried over to the next title, aimed at fall 2025 with a limited profit contribution expected.","r":"FM25 cancelled; about ¥4.0 billion of development assets written off.","re":"Kept FM24 on sale, explored a subscription, carried assets into the next title.","co":"About ¥4.0 billion one-time expense, a lowered sales plan and one skipped annual instalment.","nf":false,"l":"Do not put an engine switch and a visual reset into the same fixed annual window; migrate the tech first.","i":"An annual series has a fixed release window, so any slip in a tech migration eats the whole year's product. Sega says it sees no structural problems; the lesson is about scheduling two resets at once, not about Unity or the studio.","a":"Can the engine change ship a year before the visual reset?","q":"“We aimed for the largest-ever renewal of the UI and graphics of the game in order to improve the quality of the series in the future, but it turned out that it would take more time than expected to reach the quality we targeted, so we decided to discontinue its development.”","qa":"Sega Sammy Holdings management, Q3 FY2025/3 results briefing","st":"Financial Results for Q3 of the Fiscal Year Ending March, 2025: Major Questions in Results Briefing for Analysts and Institutional Investors (Summary)","su":"https://www.segasammy.co.jp/cms/wp-content/uploads/pdf/en/ir/20250226_q3_major_QA_e.pdf#:~:text=We%20aimed%20for%20the%20largest-ever,discontinue%20its%20development","sd":"Feb 2025","sy":"Company statement","w8":5}]},{"k":"forced","n":"FORCED","dv":"BetaDwarf","y":"2013","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/249990/","ls":"Steam","cv":"forced.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Bootstrapped a co-op action game with 8 founders living together and no publisher","s":"Development took 3 years on personal debt. FORCED launched in October 2013 at number 2 on Steam and sold 70,000 units in a few weeks, with peer-to-peer network problems and no reviews from the top 15 sites.","w":"In 2010, 8 BetaDwarf founders moved into a university classroom, lived there illegally for 7 months, then kept living together for 3 years while building FORCED with no investor or publisher, on money borrowed at private risk. The game shipped on Steam on 24 October 2013, the same day as a Batman release, and none of the top 15 gaming sites reviewed it. It still reached rank 2 on Steam's top sellers that day and sold 70,000 units in a few weeks, with network problems because it used peer-to-peer connections, not dedicated servers. Grønning wrote this 3 weeks after launch.","r":"3 years of unpaid work and personal debt before any revenue.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"3 years, 8 founders, personal debt; 70,000 units sold in a few weeks.","nf":false,"l":"Decide dedicated servers versus peer-to-peer in pre-production with launch-day player counts in mind; bootstrap only with a known runway.","i":"With no publisher, the founders' living costs become the budget and the launch has to pay the debt back. It worked for 8 people on one game; the same bet with a larger team or a slower launch leaves no way out.","a":"Who is paying our living costs until launch, and for how long?","q":"“3 years later we've launched our game with success!”","qa":"Steffen Kabbelgaard Grønning, co-founder, BetaDwarf","st":"How we lived together for 3 years while making FORCED","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/how-we-lived-together-for-3-years-while-making-forced#:~:text=3%20years%20later%20we've,game%20with%20success","sd":"Nov 2013","sy":"Participant account","w8":3}]},{"k":"forspoken","n":"Forspoken","dv":"Luminous Productions","y":"2023","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1680880/","ls":"Steam","cv":"forspoken.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Designed Forspoken around the PS5 SSD to show assets without load times","s":"Hardware decompression moved the bottleneck to the CPU. Luminous Productions made over 100 adjustments to its engine to reach the read speeds behind the game's fast travel.","w":"Takeshi Aramaki, head of Luminous Productions and director of Forspoken, expected the PS5's SSD to let games show assets with no load times. The team designed Forspoken for the PS5, with very fast travel across its open world. The SSD is about 100 times faster than the PS4's, and the console handles memory allocation and decompression in hardware. That left CPU processing of the data as the bottleneck. The team made over 100 adjustments to the Luminous Engine to reach the read speeds. Aramaki described the work in February 2023, after launch.","r":"The engine needed over 100 changes to reach the target read speeds.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Over 100 adjustments to the Luminous Engine.","nf":false,"l":"When a new platform removes one bottleneck, profile the next one in your engine before you promise a loading target.","i":"Fast storage and hardware decompression hand data to the CPU faster, so the engine code that processes it becomes the limit. A platform feature sets the ceiling; reaching it is still engine work for your own team.","a":"What limits our loading once the drive is no longer the bottleneck?","q":"“The CPU processing of that data then became the bottleneck, so we made over 100 adjustments to our Luminous Engine in order to realize these read speeds.”","qa":"Takeshi Aramaki, head of Luminous Productions and director of Forspoken","st":"PS5 Creators: How Forspoken harnesses PS5’s power","su":"https://blog.playstation.com/2023/02/01/ps5-creators-how-forspoken-harnesses-ps5s-power/#:~:text=The%20CPU%20processing%20of%20that,realize%20these%20read%20speeds","sd":"Feb 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"fortnite","n":"Fortnite","dv":"Epic Games","y":"2017","sc":"AAA","se":"Early Access 2017","lk":"https://store.epicgames.com/en-US/p/fortnite","ls":"Epic","cv":"fortnite.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Built a battle royale mode inside Save the World with the Unreal Tournament team","s":"Reusing an existing game and a borrowed team, Epic shipped the mode in about 2 months. A late switch made it free and separate from the paid game.","w":"Epic had worked on Fortnite since 2011, and its paid PvE mode, Save the World, launched on July 21, 2017. Around then Epic started a PvP battle royale mode inside the same game, and the Unreal Tournament team moved over to do most of the work. The plan was an optional mode in a $40 early access game. In the final 2 weeks Epic made it free-to-play and separate from Save the World. Battle Royale launched on September 26, 2017. At GDC in March 2018 Ed Zobrist said few publishers could have pivoted that fast.","r":"A new mode shipped about 2 months after work started.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"About 2 months of development, mostly by the Unreal Tournament team.","nf":false,"l":"Before scoping a new product, list what your existing systems and assets could ship in 2 months.","i":"Most of the work already existed in Save the World, so the borrowed team only built the mode on top. The speed rests on years of prior work; without a finished base, the 2-month figure does not transfer.","a":"What could we ship in 2 months from systems we already have?","q":"“And it was the Unreal Tournament team that popped over to pick up the charge for us to basically put originally what we thought would be a PvP version inside our PvE game.”","qa":"Ed Zobrist, Epic Games","st":"Fortnite Battle Royale was developed in just two months, wasn't originally free-to-play","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/fortnite-battle-royale-was-developed-in-just-two-months-and-wasnt-originally-going-to-be-free-to-play/#:~:text=And%20it%20was%20the%20Unreal,inside%20our%20PvE%20game","sd":"Mar 2018","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Made Fortnite the test bed for Unreal Engine 4 and games as a service","s":"Early builds would not start reliably, and a working prototype came only in 2014. Paid early access opened in July 2017, five years after the announcement.","w":"Epic announced Fortnite on Unreal Engine 3. Once prototyping began, its ambition grew, and Epic switched to Unreal Engine 4, built partly to make the game. About six months after the announcement, Tencent bought around 40% of Epic for $330 million. When lead designer Darren Sugg joined in 2012, the game would not start reliably. A functional prototype came in 2014, and Donald Mustard estimated 3 more years to deepen and polish it, while Epic reorganized to run live games. Early access opened on July 25, 2017.","r":"Fortnite reached paid early access five years after its announcement.","re":"Epic reorganized itself to build and run a live-service game.","co":"About 3 more years after the 2014 prototype.","nf":false,"l":"Do not stack a new engine and a new business model on one prototype; prove one before adding the other.","i":"A game that tests the engine and the business model inherits every engine bug and every company change, so its schedule stops being its own. Epic could afford that because the engine paid its way; a studio living off one game cannot.","a":"Is our game carrying a new engine or business model it doesn't need?","q":"“We basically made Unreal 4 to help make Fortnite.”","qa":"Donald Mustard, worldwide creative director at Epic Games","st":"From vapourware to 40 million players: how Epic made a massive success of Fortnite","su":"https://www.pcgamesn.com/fortnite/why-has-fortnite-taken-so-long#:~:text=We%20basically%20made%20Unreal%204,help%20make%20Fortnite","sd":"2017, republished Jan 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"freerealms","n":"Free Realms","dv":"Sony Online Entertainment","y":"2009","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Took on the planned staff influx before the new engine and tools were ready","s":"Artists and designers waited months for tech. Content then got little time on untried tools, and key features such as the microtransaction back-end arrived near beta.","w":"SOE started Free Realms in 2005, and new senior leaders joined in 2006. In late 2006 the team rebooted it onto a completely new engine and streaming tech. About 40 people were on the team, but only about 10 were programmers. Engine and tools took longer than planned, and the planned staffing influx arrived while tools were still shaky. Art tools got 75 percent of the attention, so minigames and content had little time on untried tools. The microtransaction back-end came online within a month of internal beta. The game launched on April 28, 2009.","r":"Content was built late on untried tools, and morale dropped.","re":"With about a year left, the team of 80 switched to scrum.","co":"4 years including 1 of pre-production; about 40 staff waiting on tech.","nf":false,"l":"Schedule each staffing wave against tool readiness, and check the tools can carry final content before new hires arrive.","i":"Artists and designers can only plan until the tools exist, so an early team spends months and morale on work that never feels final. McWilliams calls the reboot itself essential; the timing of the hires was the mistake.","a":"Can our tools keep the next hiring wave busy on final content?","q":"“All of that is what you would expect to happen when you staff a project too early, but there’s a significant secondary effect that shouldn’t get lost in the shuffle: the impact on morale.”","qa":"Laralyn McWilliams, creative director of Free Realms at Sony Online Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Sony Online Entertainment's Free Realms","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_April_2010.pdf#:~:text=All%20of%20that%20is%20what,the%20impact%20on%20morale","sd":"Apr 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4,"sh":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_April_2010.pdf#page=25"}]},{"k":"frozensynapse","n":"Frozen Synapse","dv":"Mode 7 Games","y":"2011","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/98200/","ls":"Steam","cv":"frozensynapse.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Started single-player design before the multiplayer core was finished","s":"The design circled through a random loss track, base building and other meta-game ideas while the rest of the 3-person team had little to do. Mode 7 postponed single-player until multiplayer was done.","w":"Mode 7 spent almost 4 years on Frozen Synapse with 3 in-house people and £140k. Ian Hardingham built the multiplayer core first with programmer art and locked it before any assets went into production. Single-player design began too early and went down dead ends: a random loss track, base building and other meta-game ideas, when a fun AI and linear levels were all that was needed. The rest of the team sat unproductive at times, so single-player was postponed until multiplayer was done. A paid beta in April 2010 raised about $135,000, and the game shipped on 26 May 2011.","r":"Design dead ends left the rest of the team idle and burned money.","re":"Postponed single-player until multiplayer was done, then built a fun AI and linear levels.","co":"Idle periods inside a 4-year, £140k project; not costed separately.","nf":false,"l":"Finish the mode you have proven before you design the second one; a team waiting on design costs money.","i":"A mode designed before its foundation exists has nothing to test against, so it drifts into meta-game ideas while programmers and artists wait. Taylor still values the room to go wrong; the lesson applies when a small team cannot afford idle weeks.","a":"Is anyone idle while we design the second mode?","q":"“Single player design was attempted too early, and went down some very significant dead-ends before the decision was taken to completely postpone it until after the multiplayer was done.”","qa":"Paul Taylor, Joint Managing Director of Mode 7 Games","st":"Postmortem: Mode 7 Games' Frozen Synapse","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-mode-7-games-i-frozen-synapse-i-#:~:text=Single%20player%20design%20was%20attempted,the%20multiplayer%20was%20done","sd":"Jan 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"ftlfasterthanlight","n":"FTL: Faster Than Light","dv":"Subset Games","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/212680/","ls":"Steam","cv":"ftlfasterthanlight.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Used indie competition deadlines every 3 to 4 months as playable milestones","s":"The core game came together in a 2-week push before the IGF China deadline, and the judges' praise told the pair to keep going. Contest exposure later helped launch their Kickstarter.","w":"Justin Ma and Matthew Davis built FTL as a 2-person team with no publisher and no marketers. They entered it in IGF China in 2011, and the core game came together in a 2-week crunch before that deadline. Judges told them it was fun, which convinced them to continue. They went on to IndieCade, Fantastic Arcade and the main IGF, with a deadline every 3 to 4 months. An IGF honorable mention put a demo online through OnLive, which helped their 2012 Kickstarter. FTL launched on Steam on 14 September 2012.","r":"The core game came together under the first contest deadline.","re":"They kept entering contests, each time with a balanced, playable build.","co":"A 2-week crunch before the IGF China deadline.","nf":false,"l":"Without a publisher, pick outside deadlines every 3 to 4 months and bring a balanced, playable build to each.","i":"A deadline with outside judges forces a build that works end to end, and their reaction is a cheap greenlight test. It replaces publisher milestones only if the judges' feedback actually decides whether you continue.","a":"What is our next outside deadline that needs a playable build?","q":"“These deadlines would come up every 3-4 months, reminding you that it has to be a balanced, functioning game that people can play.”","qa":"Matthew Davis, programmer and co-founder of Subset Games","st":"How competition deadlines whipped FTL into shape","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/how-competition-deadlines-whipped-i-ftl-i-into-shape#:~:text=These%20deadlines%20would%20come%20up,game%20that%20people%20can%20play","sd":"Nov 2012","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Started with one target feeling and no design document, cutting whatever did not fit","s":"Multiplayer and other hoped-for features were dropped, a $10,000 Kickstarter brought in $200,000, and the single focus told the pair where to spend it while sticking to the release date.","w":"Matthew Davis and Justin Ma began FTL with only a target atmosphere, no genre, pacing or scope, expecting a 3-month side project. They used that one vague concept as the guiding light and dropped anything that did not fit it, including the multiplayer features they had wanted. The Kickstarter asked for $10,000 and raised $200,000 from 10,000 backers, which made it hard to use the new money while sticking to the release date; the focus told them where to spend. FTL shipped in 2012 to wide critical and commercial success, and the pair explained the approach at GDC 2013.","r":"Shipped in 2012 to wide critical and commercial success.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Multiplayer and other hoped-for features dropped.","nf":true,"l":"If you skip the pitch, name one non-negotiable focus and cut every feature that does not serve it, even after the money arrives.","i":"One clear focus does the job of a design document for a 2-person team: every build gets judged against it, so cuts are quick and extra money does not become extra scope. With a bigger team or a publisher, that focus still needs writing down.","a":"What is the one focus we judge every feature against?","q":"“We kept ditching things to keep moving towards the goal.”","qa":"Justin Ma, co-founder of Subset Games","st":"Designing without a pitch - An FTL postmortem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/designing-without-a-pitch---an-em-ftl-em-postmortem#:~:text=We%20kept%20ditching%20things,towards%20the%20goal","sd":"Mar 2013","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Kept the release date and scope after Kickstarter raised 20 times the goal","s":"The 2-person team spent the surplus on polish and a 4,000-person beta instead of new features. FTL shipped in September 2012, a few months after the campaign.","w":"In 2012 Subset Games put FTL, already shown at indie contests, on Kickstarter with a $10,000 goal. It raised $200,542 from about 10,000 backers. Justin Ma and Matthew Davis had promised a release only a few months away, so they offered no stretch goals and did not greatly increase scope. They spent the extra money on quality and on supporting 4,000 beta testers instead of the few hundred they expected. FTL launched on Steam on 14 September 2012. In January 2013 Ma judged the balance reasonably successful.","r":"Scope held, and the surplus went into polish and a bigger beta.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When funding beats the goal, keep the promised date and spend the surplus on polishing the scope you already have.","i":"Extra money buys polish quickly, but new features restart design, art and testing, and a public date makes every slip visible. The rule fits a game close to done; at the concept stage, extra funding can rightly change the scope.","a":"If our budget doubled tomorrow, would we keep the same scope and date?","q":"“We tried to walk the thin line between using the extra funds to increase the quality of the final product while trying not to delay the release too long.”","qa":"Justin Ma, co-founder of Subset Games","st":"FTL designer wary of another Kickstarter pitch: \"It just adds a whole new layer of stress\"","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/ftl-kickstarter-justin-ma-kickstarter/#:~:text=We%20tried%20to%20walk%20the%20thin%20line,delay%20the%20release%20too%20long","sd":"Jan 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Planned FTL's first release for Windows, Mac and Linux on the same day","s":"Finishing three builds at once piled onto the release crunch. A Linux failure four days before launch meant all-nighters, and every early patch needed 4 tested builds.","w":"Justin Ma and Matthew Davis planned FTL from day one for Windows, Mac and Linux, and a Linux development build ran 5 months into 18 months of work. They expected to finish the Mac and Linux builds once Windows was clean. Release crunch meant 12 to 15 hour days just to finish Windows. Four days before launch, the Linux build would not run on many Linux flavors, and fixing it took all-nighters and calls to porting experts. The launch went smoothly, but the two patches in the first month each needed 4 tested builds.","r":"Late Linux fixes added all-nighters to a 12 to 15 hour crunch.","re":"They called Linux porting experts and fixed the build before launch.","co":"All-nighters in the last 4 days; 4 builds to test per patch.","nf":false,"l":"On a first game, ship one platform first and schedule the other builds 2 to 4 weeks after it.","i":"Shared code made the ports look almost done, but finishing a build means installers, distributions and testing, and that work lands in the same crunch. The founders expected it to be easier once they had a clean build pipeline and a shipped game.","a":"Can we finish and test every platform build in launch week?","q":"“But attempting a simultaneous, three-platform release for your very first game project is not a great plan.”","qa":"Justin Ma and Matthew Davis, Subset Games","st":"A Mini-Postmortem Roundup","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/a-mini-postmortem-roundup#:~:text=But%20attempting%20a%20simultaneous,is%20not%20a%20great%20plan","sd":"Apr 2013","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"gabrielknight3bloodofthesacredbloodofthedamned","n":"Gabriel Knight 3: Blood of the Sacred, Blood of the Damned","dv":"Sierra Studios","y":"1999","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/497360/","ls":"Steam","cv":"gabrielknight3bloodofthesacredbloodofthedamned.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Dropped Sierra's SCI engine to build a new 3D engine and tools from scratch","s":"The budget grew from under $2 million to $4.2 million, and the game shipped over a year late after a mid-project engine rebuild.","w":"Sierra Northwest stopped using SCI, the engine behind Gabriel Knight 1 and 2, and gave the new 3D engine to Shivers 2 developers with almost no 3D experience. Artists started building content long before a stable engine existed. When Scott Bilas joined in early 1998, the game had been in development for over 1.5 years, was due that summer and still ran on hacked-up sample code. A month later the team decided to rebuild the engine, keeping much of the old code. Several kept systems caused problems to the end. GK3 shipped in November 1999, over a year late.","r":"The engine was rebuilt mid-project, and several kept systems never worked correctly.","re":"Bilas rearchitected the engine around data files and the Sheep scripting language.","co":"Budget from under $2 million to $4.2 million; shipped over a year late.","nf":false,"l":"Before dropping a working engine, plan the new engine and tools as their own project, with engineers who have built one.","i":"A new engine also means new tools and new processes, and a team of scripters cannot build them while making content. The lesson is about an inexperienced team; an experienced engine team changes the math.","a":"Has anyone on the team shipped an engine like this before?","q":"“The team had to build a new game engine and most of the related content development tools from scratch, but team members severely underestimated the time, cost, effort, and experience required to construct these tools and establish effective development processes around them.”","qa":"Scott Bilas, engine architect at Sierra Studios","st":"Postmortem: Sierra Studios' Gabriel Knight 3","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/postmortem-sierra-studios-i-gabriel-knight-3-i-#:~:text=The%20team%20had%20to%20build,effective%20development%20processes%20around%20them","sd":"Oct 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"gabrielknight","n":"Gabriel Knight: Sins of the Fathers","dv":"Sierra","y":"1993","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/495700/","ls":"Steam","cv":"gabrielknight.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Committed Gabriel Knight to Sierra's new SCI32 engine version","s":"The team fought engine bugs for 6 months. The game still made its Christmas date, but a smooth project turned into hard going.","w":"Gabriel Knight: Sins of the Fathers was a Sierra adventure released in 1993. During its development, Sierra's SCI engine moved to a new version, SCI32, and the team committed to switching over. Until then the project had run smoothly. For six months the team fought bugs and problems in the new system. The game still shipped on its Christmas date. Jane Jensen recalled the episode in a June 1998 GameSpot diary for Gabriel Knight 3, the first project she worked on with an engine built from scratch.","r":"Six months went to fighting engine bugs.","re":"The team pushed through and kept the Christmas date.","co":"About 6 months of engine bug fixing.","nf":false,"l":"Before you commit to a new engine version, put its bug fixing on the schedule as its own task.","i":"A new engine version brings its own bugs, and the game team finds them while building content. Jensen's team kept the date, so the cost showed up as strain on the team rather than a slip; a fixed holiday date can hide it.","a":"Have we scheduled time for this engine upgrade's bugs?","q":"“We made the commitment to move to the new system and fought bugs and snafus for six months.”","qa":"Jane Jensen, Gabriel Knight series designer at Sierra","st":"GK3 Designer Diaries","su":"https://gkpages.altervista.org/Interviews/DesignerDiaries_04.html#:~:text=We%20made%20the%20commitment%20to,snafus%20for%20six%20months","sd":"Jun 1998","sy":"Participant account","w8":3}]},{"k":"galacticcivilizations","n":"Galactic Civilizations (Windows)","dv":"Stardock","y":"2003","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/214150/","ls":"Steam","cv":"galacticcivilizations.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Held back part of the self-funded budget for updates after release","s":"Free updates built from player feedback added about 15,000 lines of code after launch. The plan only worked because the game shipped on schedule and complete.","w":"Stardock started the Windows remake of Galactic Civilizations in fall 2001 with 3 developers and 2 graphic designers. Publishers passed on the concept, so Stardock funded it alone and set part of the budget aside for after release. Its plan was word of mouth: a small studio with little pre-release coverage would keep the game on shelves with frequent free updates. That meant staying on schedule, since a slip would eat the post-release money. The game came out in spring 2003 to favorable reviews. By May 2003, updates had added about 15,000 lines of code.","r":"The game launched complete and kept getting large free updates.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About 15,000 lines of new code and megabytes of graphics after release.","nf":false,"l":"If your sales depend on word of mouth, set aside budget for post-launch updates and protect it by holding the schedule.","i":"The reserve works only if the boxed game is complete, because reviewers judge launch content. A schedule slip spends the reserve before launch. With weak reviews, the same updates would have looked like the team finishing the game late.","a":"How much update budget is left if we slip a month?","q":"“On the other hand, if your development schedule falls behind, you eat into your after-release budget.”","qa":"Brad Wardell, CEO and president of Stardock","st":"Postmortem: Stardock's Galactic Civilizations","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-stardock-s-galactic-civilizations#:~:text=On%20the%20other%20hand%2C%20if,eat%20into%20your%20after%2Drelease%20budget","sd":"May 2003","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Dropped multiplayer from Galactic Civilizations after beta tester feedback","s":"New features no longer had to account for synchronization, latency or multiplayer game flow. Brad Wardell said the single-player game came out much better overall.","w":"Stardock began the Windows remake of Galactic Civilizations in fall 2001, knowing the strategy market would be hard to compete in. The team leaned heavily on beta testers to shape a game that would meet strategy players' expectations. Based on their feedback, it decided to drop multiplayer. From then on, new features did not have to be checked for synchronization, latency or multiplayer game flow. The game shipped in spring 2003, and in his May 2003 postmortem Brad Wardell listed the cut among what went right.","r":"Features could be added without network synchronization work.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If a mode taxes every feature you add, ask your testers early whether they need it, and cut it if they do not.","i":"Multiplayer taxes every later feature, since each one has to stay in sync and survive latency. Here testers did not need it. In a game sold on playing together, the same cut removes the reason to buy.","a":"Do our testers actually need the mode that slows every feature?","q":"“Not having a multiplayer component allowed us to make Galactic Civilizations a much better game overall.”","qa":"Brad Wardell, CEO and president of Stardock","st":"Postmortem: Stardock's Galactic Civilizations","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-stardock-s-galactic-civilizations#:~:text=Not%20having%20a%20multiplayer%20component,much%20better%20game%20overall","sd":"May 2003","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"galacticcivilizationsiidreadlords","n":"Galactic Civilizations II: Dread Lords","dv":"Stardock","y":"2006","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/202200/","ls":"Steam","cv":"galacticcivilizationsiidreadlords.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Published the sequel in-house, with separate budgets for development, marketing and support","s":"Stardock expected $15 to $21 a unit instead of about $7. With Take 2 as distributor and a hired publishing lead, first-week retail sell-in was 3 times the original's.","w":"The first Galactic Civilizations shipped in March 2003 and sold well, but publisher Strategy First went bankrupt without paying much of the royalties. Stardock's business software was earning enough to fund both development and publishing of the sequel. The studio set a $900,000 development budget, a $400,000 marketing budget and a $300,000 reserve for post-release support, over 3 times the original's budget. Take 2 handled distribution, Brian Clair was hired to run publishing, and AEG took media relations. The game shipped on 21 February 2006 with a first-week sell-in 3 times the original's.","r":"First-week retail sell-in reached 3 times the first game's.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Planned: $900,000 development, $400,000 marketing, $300,000 post-release reserve.","nf":false,"l":"If you self-publish, set marketing and post-launch support as their own budget lines next to development before production starts.","i":"Self-publishing raised the expected money per unit from about $7 to $15 or more, and Stardock's software income carried the bigger risk. The money did not buy know-how: Wardell admits the team still did not know how retail promotion was bought.","a":"Do we have separate budget lines for marketing and post-launch support?","q":"“For the sequel, we put together a $900,000 development budget and a $400,000 marketing budget.”","qa":"Brad Wardell, CEO of Stardock","st":"Postmortem: Stardock's Galactic Civilizations 2: Dread Lords","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-stardock-s-i-galactic-civilizations-2-dread-lords-i-#:~:text=For%20the%20sequel%2C%20we%20put%20together,a%20%24400%2C000%20marketing%20budget","sd":"Apr 2006","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the sequel on the 3D engine Stardock had made for The Political Machine","s":"Work sped up, and 3D allowed smooth zoom and a ship designer that became almost as popular as the game. At launch, 2.5% of buyers had problems, 10 times the first game's rate.","w":"Stardock's first design for the sequel was modest, and the team came to see it as a cop out. The Political Machine had shipped in 2004 on a 3D engine with no 3D content, so an engine was ready. Going 3D allowed smooth zoom, planets on the main map, and ships whose every shape and color players could design. After that last decision the team had 18 months, and work moved quickly. After the February 2006 release, 2.5% of buyers had problems, against under 0.25% on the first game, much of it from hot new graphics cards.","r":"Development moved fast, but launch problems ran 10 times the first game's rate.","re":"Stardock added a GPU throttling option within days of release.","co":"Launch problem rate of 2.5%, against under 0.25% on the first game.","nf":false,"l":"When you move a series to 3D, put the newest graphics cards in the test plan from the start, next to the old machines.","i":"An engine already built for another game removed the start-up work, so the 18 months went into features like the ship designer. It did not cover hardware risk: a team used to 2D had no feel for how finicky PCs are with 3D.","a":"Is the newest graphics hardware in our test plan yet?","q":"“Once we settled on a 3D engine things started to progress quickly.”","qa":"Brad Wardell, CEO of Stardock","st":"Postmortem: Stardock's Galactic Civilizations 2: Dread Lords","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-stardock-s-i-galactic-civilizations-2-dread-lords-i-#:~:text=Once%20we%20settled%20on%20a,started%20to%20progress%20quickly","sd":"Apr 2006","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"goatsimulator","n":"Goat Simulator","dv":"Coffee Stain Studios","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"Mobile port 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/265930/","ls":"Steam","cv":"goatsimulator.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Scoped Goat Simulator to match its trailer, bought the assets online and shipped fast","s":"The goat model cost $20 on sale. Coffee Stain recouped its money within about 10 minutes of release, and in 5 months the game outsold both Sanctum games combined.","w":"At Coffee Stain, while the animators were busy on a new secret IP, a few people kept noodling on a goat idea. Ibrisagic posted the first trailer and woke up to 80,000 views; by the time the team asked Steam about a release, it had about 5 million. The team decided the game was only funny if it stayed small, bought all the assets online (the goat cost $20, 75 percent off) and released fast. Goat Simulator launched on 1 April 2014, recouped its cost within about 10 minutes and in 5 months outsold both Sanctum games combined.","r":"A game built on bought assets that paid back within 10 minutes.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Assets bought online; the goat model cost $20.","nf":false,"l":"When a trailer has already told you what players want, scope the game to the trailer and ship fast.","i":"The trailer set the promise, so anything beyond it cost money without adding to what people were buying. Bought assets and little planning worked because the joke was the product; a game sold on depth or polish cannot skip the planning the same way.","a":"What did our trailer promise, and are we building more than that?","q":"“We figured the game is only funny if we make it small, and stupid.”","qa":"Armin Ibrisagic, Coffee Stain Studios","st":"The hilarious success story of Goat Simulator","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/the-hilarious-success-story-of-i-goat-simulator-i-#:~:text=We%20figured%20the%20game%20is,small%2C%20and%20stupid","sd":"Aug 2014","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Turned an internal game jam joke into a Steam release with no project plan","s":"It shipped in 10 weeks with about 10 developers and became the studio's most successful game. The jam code needed heavy refactoring, and Mac and Linux came months late.","w":"In January 2014 Coffee Stain had spare staff while pre-production on a bigger game got going, so it ran an internal game jam. A couple of weeks later a goat prototype trailer got about a million YouTube views in a week. The studio chose to make the game the trailer showed, not a bigger one, and kept working with no plan or fixed roles. Jam code had to be refactored for Steam, and extra maps meant copy-pasting classes. The game launched on April 1, 2014. Ibrisagic's February 2015 postmortem calls it the studio's most successful game.","r":"Shipped in 10 weeks, with rework on code, maps and ports.","re":"The team refactored the jam code and shipped Mac and Linux months later.","co":"About 10 developers for 10 weeks, plus refactoring and late Mac and Linux ports.","nf":false,"l":"Once a jam prototype is headed for release, list the maps and platforms it must support and refactor for them first.","i":"With no plan, nobody builds for maps, platforms or performance that nobody expects to need. At 10 weeks the rework stays small. Over a production measured in years, the same shortcuts pile up.","a":"Which jam shortcuts will break once we add maps or platforms?","q":"“Since we never intended for the game to be released initially, we didn’t start out with a proper project plan and we didn’t have predetermined roles of who was going to do what.”","qa":"Armin Ibrisagic, Coffee Stain Studios","st":"Goat Simulator Post Mortem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/goat-simulator-post-mortem#:~:text=Since%20we%20never%20intended%20for,was%20going%20to%20do%20what","sd":"Feb 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"godofwar","n":"God of War","dv":"Sony Santa Monica","y":"2018","sc":"AAA","se":"Port 2022","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1593500/","ls":"Steam","cv":"godofwar.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Planned one continuous camera shot with no cuts for the entire game","s":"Every cinematic now needed the actors performing together on stage. The old method, a stand-in on stage and the voice recorded later, projected a 2036 finish, so Kratos was recast.","w":"Cory Barlog came back to Santa Monica Studio in June 2013 to pitch a new God of War. Four weeks in, he began seeding a camera that never cuts, without yet admitting it would run the whole game. The earlier games animated scenes first and recorded the voice afterwards, so an early test put a large stand-in on stage as Kratos and recorded TC Carson's voice later. Run through the schedule, that method put the finish at about 2036. Barlog recast Kratos; casting Kratos and Baldur took 3 to 3.5 years, and the game shipped in April 2018.","r":"The old capture method would have pushed completion to about 2036.","re":"Barlog recast Kratos with Christopher Judge so scenes could be shot on stage.","co":"Casting Kratos and Baldur took 3 to 3.5 years.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock a camera or presentation rule, run one scene through capture, casting and animation, and time it.","i":"A camera rule applies to every scene, so its cost multiplies through capture, casting and animation. Running the numbers early turned a pipeline problem into a casting decision while there was still time to make it.","a":"Have we timed one full scene through our capture pipeline under this rule?","q":"“But to get the scenes that I wanted I knew I had to have all the actors on stage because we were doing that one single camera shot.”","qa":"Cory Barlog, creative director at Santa Monica Studio","st":"Reinventing God of War","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aIb-Lt7WX_s&t=3252s","sd":"Mar 2019","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aIb-Lt7WX_s&t=3234s"},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Story & world","t":"Wrote a first-year script that assumed players already knew the father-son relationship","s":"The writers had to throw the story out and start again. Barlog then built a skeleton of the whole game and pitched it for 3 years.","w":"Barlog started the new God of War in June 2013 around a father and son. For the first year, he and the writers worked on a full script. It assumed players already knew the bond between Kratos and his son, and it drifted away from the two main characters. Barlog told the writers to throw the story out, which did not go well, and they started over. By the 2014 pitch to Shuhei Yoshida, the narrative pillar read father-son. Barlog then spent 3 years walking people through the story skeleton in three-hour pitches.","r":"A year of script work was thrown out and rewritten.","re":"Barlog built a skeleton of the whole game before writing scenes again.","co":"About 1 year of script work.","nf":false,"l":"Write a short skeleton of the whole story around its main relationship and get it agreed before anyone writes full scenes.","i":"A full script fixes scenes before anyone checks the premise, so a wrong premise costs every page. In pre-production that loss stays with the writers; once levels and cinematics depend on the script, it spreads to the whole team.","a":"Does our story skeleton put the main relationship at the center?","q":"“Here's another point in which I completely lost the plot for the first year of the game and ended up writing a story that assumed you already knew the relationship of the father-son and we sort of had to go and reboot that.”","qa":"Cory Barlog, creative director at Santa Monica Studio","st":"Reinventing God of War","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aIb-Lt7WX_s&t=628s","sd":"Mar 2019","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aIb-Lt7WX_s&t=745s"},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"External commitments","t":"Revealed the reboot with a live 10-minute demo played on the E3 2016 stage","s":"The demo took about a year and a half to build and ate time from the full game. After the show, the team had about 18 months left to make around 30 hours of play.","w":"Barlog's team fixed E3 2016 as the reveal about 9 or 10 months ahead and, as he told GDC in March 2019, picked the hardest option: a demo played live on stage instead of a logo or a trailer. The demo ran 10 minutes 45 seconds and took about a year and a half to reach. A month before its alpha the art director left. Right after the June 2016 show, with a year and a half left, the team still had around 30 hours of game to build. God of War shipped in April 2018 after nearly 5 years.","r":"After E3, 18 months were left to build about 30 hours of game.","re":"Later, out of time and money, Barlog cut about an hour of gameplay.","co":"About 18 months of a 5-year project went into a 10-minute-45-second demo.","nf":false,"l":"Set a time cap for a show demo before you start it; a slice that took 18 months is not a production template.","i":"A live stage demo must be flawless in every second, so the team polishes one slice far beyond what the full game can afford. The reveal buys attention, but that polish per minute does not carry over to the remaining 30 hours.","a":"What is the time cap on our show demo, and who enforces it?","q":"“We were trying to take an inaugural flight in a plane while we were building the plane and also drawing the blueprints of the plane as it was taking off.”","qa":"Cory Barlog, creative director at Sony Santa Monica Studio","st":"God of War - Raising Kratos: Full Length Feature | PS4","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lJZWKBDXXFY&t=3566s","sd":"May 2019","sy":"Participant documentary","w8":4,"qu":"https://www.gamerevolution.com/originals/514861-god-of-war-director-doubt-gdc-2019#:~:text=We%20were%20trying%20to%20take%20an%20inaugural%20flight"},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Absorbed 110 to 120 staff from a cancelled sister project during the concept phase","s":"The staff arrived 8 to 9 months early. The small concept team had no pipeline or engine plan to keep them busy, and mistrust started on day one.","w":"Santa Monica Studio leadership had turned the studio into a 1.5 team: two large projects under one roof, sharing staff. Internal 7, a new futuristic IP, spent close to 36 months in pre-production. Meanwhile a core group of about 11 people worked out the new God of War. When Internal 7 halted, layoffs followed, and God of War inherited 110 to 120 people about 8 to 9 months early. Its leads still had to settle the pipeline and the engine. The newcomers asked whether the team even knew what game it was making.","r":"The concept team had to keep 110 people busy without a pipeline.","re":"The leads pushed on and worked to regroup the combined team.","co":"110 to 120 staff arrived 8 to 9 months ahead of plan.","nf":false,"l":"If a sister project could be cancelled, plan holding work for its staff before they land on an unlocked concept.","i":"A team without a pipeline or engine plan has nothing for 110 people to build, so they wait and start to doubt the leads. Sharing staff between projects works only when the receiving project is ready to take them.","a":"If 100 people joined us next month, would we have work for them?","q":"“And then when things were not working out and kind of the big sort of halt happened on the project, we had to suddenly say we're about to get 110 people.”","qa":"Cory Barlog, game director of God of War at Santa Monica Studio","st":"God of War | Raising Kratos | \"Making Of\" Documentary","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ra_R-K_IoUc&t=921s","sd":"May 2019","sy":"Participant documentary","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ra_R-K_IoUc&t=893s"}]},{"k":"godofwarascension","n":"God of War: Ascension","dv":"SCE Santa Monica Studio","y":"2013","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Ran R&D on an entirely new project while God of War III was finishing","s":"The team settled on a new God of War story later than it wanted. Ascension's pre-production started behind, which leaders said hurt the whole project.","w":"Finishing God of War III took all hands at Sony Santa Monica. At the same time, the studio ran R&D experiments on an entirely new project. The team came back to a new God of War story later than it wanted, and lost pre-production time. When Ascension began, pre-production was not where game director Todd Papy and senior leadership wanted it. In production, multiplayer, the studio's first, needed substantial rewrites, and key mechanics arrived after deadlines. The game shipped in March 2013. In June 2013 the producers wrote that the lost time was very difficult to recover from.","r":"Ascension started with pre-production behind where its leaders wanted it.","re":"Leaders said they now plan and set priorities up front on new projects.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Pick the next game's direction and staff its pre-production before the current game enters its final push.","i":"A finishing push takes the leads who should shape the next game, and R&D on a direction you later drop uses the same weeks. Both cut pre-production. The risk is highest when one team with the same leads makes big games back to back.","a":"Who is doing pre-production on our next game while we finish this one?","q":"“Our excitement ultimately came back around to telling a new God of War story, but that conclusion came later than we would have liked, and we lost out on invaluable pre-production time.”","qa":"Whitney Wade, senior producer, and Chacko Sonny, director of internal development, Sony Santa Monica","st":"Postmortem - Sony Santa Monica's God of War: Ascension","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem---sony-santa-monica-s-i-god-of-war-ascension-i-#:~:text=Our%20excitement%20ultimately%20came%20back,invaluable%20pre%2Dproduction%20time","sd":"Jun 2013","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"godus","n":"Godus","dv":"22cans","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/232810/","ls":"Steam","cv":"godus.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Crowdfunded the PC game at concept stage, then moved the team to mobile first","s":"The mobile phase, planned for 9 months, took far longer. By February 2015 the PC game still lacked the multiplayer, Linux version and art book backers paid for, and 3 core developers had left.","w":"22cans raised £526,563 from 17,184 backers on Kickstarter in November and December 2012, at the very start of Godus. In May 2013 the studio signed with mobile publisher DeNA and released an unfinished PC alpha. The company plan then put the team on the mobile version, which Molyneux expected to take 9 months. Mobile launched in August 2014, and work on it ran into early 2015. In February 2015 Molyneux said some pledges should have been met and that he would never run a Kickstarter that way again.","r":"PC backer features waited behind the mobile version and stayed undelivered.","re":"Molyneux apologised in February 2015 and set story, combat, then multiplayer as next.","co":"Mobile phase planned at 9 months ran far longer; 3 core developers left.","nf":false,"l":"Before you crowdfund, decide which platform ships first and promise backers only what that build will contain.","i":"Backers paid for the PC game, but the team built mobile first, so every mobile delay pushed the PC promises back. The risk is specific to campaigns where the funded version is not the one the team builds first.","a":"Which platform ships first, and do our pledges match that build?","q":"“Doing Kickstarter at the very start of a game can be very challenging.”","qa":"Peter Molyneux, co-founder and head of 22cans","st":"Rocky Road Ahead for Godus as Key Devs Depart","su":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/rocky-road-ahead-for-godus-as-key-devs-depart/1100-6425211/#:~:text=Doing%20Kickstarter%20at%20the%20very,can%20be%20very%20challenging","sd":"Feb 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"goldeneye007","n":"GoldenEye 007","dv":"Rare","y":"1997","sc":"AA","se":"Re-release 2023","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Wrote an ambitious design for a film tie-in planned as a short project","s":"Smarter enemies, a long weapon list and a full page of visual effects turned it into a project of 2 years 8 months, and it was already late when multiplayer went in.","w":"Martin Hollis volunteered for the Bond game at Rare and made his first hire, Mark Edmonds, in January 1995. His April 1995 document asked for smarter enemy AI, a weapon list with a sniper rifle, gadgets and a page of visual effects. The N64 did not exist yet, and most of the team had never made a game. Multiplayer was only started around March or April 1997, when the game was already late. GoldenEye 007 shipped in 1997, and at GDC Europe in 2012 Hollis called his list naive.","r":"A short tie-in became a project of about 1,000 days.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"2 years 8 months, about 1,000 days, for a planned short project.","nf":false,"l":"Before you staff the team, check the concept document's feature list against the planned duration and the team's experience.","i":"On new hardware with a first-time team, every feature on the list cost more than it looked. The overrun was affordable only because Rare's management gave the team time and left it alone; under a fixed date, cut the list up front.","a":"Does our feature list fit the planned time with this team?","q":"“The game wasn't supposed to take nearly three years to make but you can already see the degree of ambition within me and also within the team in this document.”","qa":"Martin Hollis, director of GoldenEye 007 at Rare","st":"Classic Postmortem: GoldenEye 007","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z1Fx18cppZk&t=1141s","sd":"2012, reprinted Dec 2019","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z1Fx18cppZk&t=1144s"},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Pitched GoldenEye as an on-rails shooter and built assets before the N64 existed","s":"Early asset work on SGI machines did not go well at first, and the concept became a free-roaming shooter mid-project; Hollis worked on it for nearly 3 years and it sold over 8 million copies.","w":"Rare programmer Martin Hollis pitched GoldenEye to Tim Stamper with a short document. The team liked Virtua Cop and planned an on-rails shooter because it felt makeable and not too big in scope. The Nintendo 64 was not designed yet, so assets were built on a $150,000 SGI Onyx on the guess they would work on the console; the start did not go well. Once the team believed the technology allowed it, the game became a free-roaming shooter. Multiplayer took one month at the end. GoldenEye shipped in 1997 after nearly 3 years and sold over 8 million copies.","r":"Rough start on stand-in hardware; concept changed mid-project; nearly 3 years' work.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Nearly 3 years of development; a $150,000 SGI Onyx as stand-in hardware.","nf":false,"l":"Do not build production assets for hardware that does not exist, and decide on-rails or free-roaming before level production.","i":"Assets made for a guessed target carry the risk that the real hardware rejects them; Rare's guess happened to hold and even pushed the technology. Hollis calls it fairly lucky, so the lesson is to budget the risk, not to count on it.","a":"Are we building final assets for a platform whose specs we do not have?","q":"“I don't recommend making assets for a platform that doesn't exist, in general.”","qa":"Martin Hollis, director of GoldenEye 007 at Rare","st":"From Me to Wii: Martin Hollis' Journey","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/from-me-to-wii-martin-hollis-journey#:~:text=I%20don't%20recommend%20making%20assets,doesn't%20exist%2C%20in%20general","sd":"Aug 2009","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"gonehome","n":"Gone Home","dv":"The Fullbright Company","y":"2013","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/232430/","ls":"Steam","cv":"gonehome.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Set immovable constraints for 4 people: one house, exploring and reading, no combat","s":"The 4-person team shipped a full story game about a year after going indie. Fullbright reported more than 50,000 copies sold in the first month.","w":"Steve Gaynor left the BioShock series in 2012 and founded Fullbright alongside Johnnemann Nordhagen and Karla Zimonja; environment artist Kate Craig joined soon after. With 4 people the team could not build a city, so it chose one house that a family had just moved into, which kept object density low and clues easy to spot. It removed combat, loot and open world, kept exploring and reading as the only verbs, and set the story in 1994-95 so clues could live on paper. Gone Home shipped on 15 August 2013. On 11 September Gaynor reported more than 50,000 copies sold.","r":"A 4-person team shipped a complete story game in about a year.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"4 people and about a year of development; budget not disclosed.","nf":false,"l":"Before you write the story, fix constraints your team can produce: one space, two verbs, no systems you cannot afford.","i":"A small team's content capacity sets the feature set, and dropping combat and loot removed the systems that eat the most assets while opening a story those systems would block. That fits a story game; an action game that cuts combat loses its core.","a":"Which feature could we cut so our team can finish the content?","q":"“Our production abilities and the amount of content we could make required us to constrain our feature set a lot.”","qa":"Steve Gaynor, co-founder of The Fullbright Company","st":"How Gone Home's design constraints lead to a powerful story","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/how-i-gone-home-i-s-design-constraints-lead-to-a-powerful-story#:~:text=Our%20production%20abilities%20and%20the,our%20feature%20set%20a%20lot","sd":"Aug 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"gorogoa","n":"Gorogoa","dv":"Buried Signal","y":"2017","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/557600/","ls":"Steam","cv":"gorogoa.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Art & style","t":"Made detailed pencil drawings a fixed requirement for Gorogoa's art","s":"Every design step became a struggle. Jason Roberts later said simpler, more regular art would have allowed more puzzles and might have let him finish sooner.","w":"Jason Roberts, a first-time developer, set out to make a visual art project as much as a game. Drawing in pencil was a requirement that had nothing to do with game design. He worked mostly alone and finished too much art too soon. After demo feedback in 2013 he hit his lowest point, and he later estimated that at least 60 percent of the material was thrown out and rebuilt. Gorogoa shipped in December 2017 after 5 years. In July 2018 he said simpler art might have meant more puzzles and an earlier finish.","r":"Design stayed a constant struggle and the game came out relatively short.","re":"Roberts kept the style and rebuilt much of the game until it worked.","co":"5 years; at least 60 percent of material rebuilt; Roberts spent all his money.","nf":false,"l":"Test each idea with rough drawings first, and finish a scene's final art only once its puzzle works.","i":"Hand-drawn scenes are expensive to change, so every puzzle redesign meant redrawing. Roberts chose the style on purpose and would choose it again; the lesson is about when art gets finished, not about avoiding a demanding style.","a":"Are we finishing art for ideas we have not tested yet?","q":"“It would have allowed more puzzles, and might have allowed me to finish the game sooner while putting more gameplay in it.”","qa":"Jason Roberts, creator of Gorogoa at Buried Signal","st":"Jason Roberts interview: Creating Gorogoa required awkwardness and chaos","su":"https://gamesbeat.com/jason-roberts-interview-creating-gorogoa-required-awkwardness-and-chaos/#:~:text=It%20would%20have%20allowed%20more,more%20gameplay%20in%20it","sd":"Jul 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"gothamknights","n":"Gotham Knights","dv":"WB Games Montréal","y":"2022","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1496790/","ls":"Steam","cv":"gothamknights.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Let players take any of four heroes into every mission and cutscene","s":"Every mission cutscene had to work for all four heroes. 74 of the game's 139 cinematics were shot in a four-in-one style, after a Harley Quinn benchmark scene proved the approach.","w":"Gotham Knights let players take any of four heroes into any mission, so the story had to work for each of them. During pre-production, WB Montreal saw it had to manage content efficiently without cutting quality or scope. When cinematics director Wilson Mui joined, it was unclear how the four-in-one scenes would work, so he built a benchmark scene around Harley Quinn to test them. The game shipped in 2022 with 6 hours of cinematics: 139 scenes, 74 of them shot four ways. Mui described the work at GDC in March 2023.","r":"74 of 139 cinematics had to be built in four versions.","re":"Mui built a Harley Quinn benchmark scene to prove the four-in-one approach.","co":"74 of 139 cinematics built as four-version scenes.","nf":false,"l":"If players can pick any hero for a mission, build one full cutscene for every hero before you plan the rest.","i":"Each playable hero multiplies every scene: different bodies, rigs, costumes and lines must fit one timing and camera plan. A master scene that the other three versions follow keeps the multiplier in check. Mui called four-player co-op in cutscenes impossible.","a":"Have we built one complete scene in every hero variant yet?","q":"“We made a benchmark scene out of [a Harley] scene to validate that we could the 4-in-1 technically, mentally, and as a team.”","qa":"Wilson Mui, cinematics director at WB Montreal","st":"How WB Montreal made Gotham Knights' cutscenes work for four playable heroes","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/marketing/how-wb-montreal-made-i-gotham-knights-i-cutscenes-work-for-four-heroes#:~:text=We%20made%20a%20benchmark%20scene,and%20as%20a%20team","sd":"Mar 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"gothic1remake","n":"Gothic 1 Remake","dv":"Alkimia Interactive","y":"2026","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1297900/","ls":"Steam","cv":"gothic1remake.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Released a free playable teaser and survey before greenlighting the remake","s":"Over 43,000 players answered and 94.8% asked for a full remake. Production started, but the team kept only some teaser graphics and rebuilt the rest.","w":"On 13 December 2019, THQ Nordic gave owners of Piranha Bytes games a free 2-hour Gothic teaser, built in Unreal Engine 4 by its Barcelona team. A survey asked whether to remake the game, and the company said it would start production only if players asked for it. In February 2020 it reported 180,000 players, 43,111 answers and 94.8% for a remake, and moved into full production. In 2023 producer Reinhard Pollice said only some teaser graphics survived. The rest was a complete restart. The game shipped on 5 June 2026.","r":"Full production started, but most of the teaser build was thrown away.","re":"The team restarted and kept the original game's skill and progression rules.","co":"Teaser build discarded except some graphics.","nf":true,"l":"Before you staff a remake, test demand with a cheap playable teaser and a survey, and plan to throw the build away.","i":"Only owners of the old games got the teaser, so the answers came from fans with sharp expectations, down to the hero biting into an apple. That sample tells you what fans want, not whether new players will buy.","a":"Do we have player evidence for this remake before we staff it?","q":"“Abschließend zum Playable Teaser: Wir haben zwar einige Grafiken daraus übernommen, aber der Rest ist ein kompletter Neustart.”","qa":"Reinhard Pollice, producer of the Gothic Remake","st":"Gothic Remake: Wie ist der aktuelle Entwicklungsstand? Ein Interview mit Reinhard Pollice","su":"https://global.techradar.com/de-de/news/gothic-remake-wie-ist-der-aktuelle-entwicklungsstand-ein-interview-mit-reinhard-pollice#:~:text=Abschlie%C3%9Fend%20zum%20Playable%20Teaser,ist%20ein%20kompletter%20Neustart","sd":"Feb 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"grimdawn","n":"Grim Dawn","dv":"Crate Entertainment","y":"2016","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/219990/","ls":"Steam","cv":"grimdawn.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Licensed Titan Quest's full engine and codebase instead of building or buying an engine","s":"A team that started with 2 people had a working action RPG base from day one, and avoided hiring programmers it could not afford.","w":"Crate Entertainment, led by Titan Quest's lead gameplay designer, started Grim Dawn with 2 people on the Titan Quest engine and its full codebase. Its Kickstarter in April and May 2012 raised $537,515 against a $280,000 goal. In 2015 Arthur Bruno said that without the codebase they would have needed at least a few more programmers they could not pay for. Grim Dawn launched on Steam on February 25, 2016, after Early Access. In January 2018 a Crate designer said the codebase saved a massive amount of time and money, though it tied the engine to old art and DirectX.","r":"A 2-person start had a full action RPG codebase from day one.","re":"Crate later rewrote the renderer and replaced physics, pathing and sound.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before choosing an engine, price the cheapest proven codebase in your genre that you can license, gameplay code and tools included.","i":"A general engine leaves the genre's gameplay, features and tools to build; a codebase from a similar game ships with them. The gain shrinks the further the new game drifts from the old one, and old constraints come along.","a":"Is there a proven codebase in our genre we could license?","q":"“Without it, we'd have needed to hire at least a few more programmers, which we wouldn't have had the money for early on.”","qa":"Arthur Bruno, Crate Entertainment","st":"Grim Dawn Interview, Expansion Planned","su":"https://www.gamebanshee.com/news/115361-grim-dawn-interview-expansion-planned.html#:~:text=Without%20it%2C%20we%27d%20have%20needed,money%20for%20early%20on","sd":"Apr 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"grimfandango","n":"Grim Fandango","dv":"LucasArts","y":"1998","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2015","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/316790/","ls":"Steam","cv":"grimfandango.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Cut about a quarter of the designed game very early, before it was built","s":"The budget was tight, and the cut parts turned out to be extraneous. The lead programmer said the game was huge enough without them.","w":"Grim Fandango began with a 5-page design document that sold people on the story and setting before there were puzzles. In the full design, the team planned about 25% more game than it would make. That extra quarter was cut very early on, and it hurt to drop bits that had seemed brilliant. The game shipped after more than 3 years of work, with almost 30 people at its peak. In 2000, lead programmer Bret Mogilefsky said the cut parts had been extraneous, and the game was huge enough without them.","r":"About 25% of the designed game was never built.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About 25% of the design work discarded.","nf":false,"l":"Design more than you can build, then cut the extra before production starts, while a cut still costs only design time.","i":"A cut at the design stage throws away only documents, while the same cut after art and animation wastes their work too. The early cut did not prevent crunch: Mogilefsky still describes months of it before ship.","a":"Which quarter of our design would we cut before production starts?","q":"“In terms of game content, we designed about 25% more game than we actually made, but that much was cut very early on.”","qa":"Bret Mogilefsky, lead programmer of Grim Fandango at LucasArts","st":"Interview with Bret Mogilefsky","su":"https://grimfandango.network/features/interviews/interview-with-bret-mogilefsky#:~:text=In%20terms%20of%20game%20content,cut%20very%20early%20on","sd":"2000","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"guacamelee","n":"Guacamelee!","dv":"DrinkBox","y":"2013","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/214770/","ls":"Steam","cv":"guacamelee.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Held off committing to Guacamelee! until a concept video and demos proved its mechanics","s":"Every key mechanic was proven in a vertical slice before full production. The game took 8 months of pre-production and 11 of production, with minimal overtime.","w":"In fall 2010, as About a Blob neared completion, DrinkBox ran pitch sessions, and a pure brawler pitch grew into a Metroidvania with overlapping worlds and attacks that doubled as movement. On About a Blob the team had spent most of development making mechanics work together, which cut polish time. So they held back from committing and made a Flash concept video that set the basic attacks. A combat demo and a vertical slice with dimension swapping followed. After 8 months of pre-production and 11 of production, Guacamelee! shipped on 9 April 2013 with minimal overtime.","r":"Full production started with every key mechanic already proven.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"8 months of pre-production with a small group.","nf":false,"l":"Before full production, prove all your core mechanics together in a concept video, a combat demo and a vertical slice.","i":"The team was mixing several mechanics and feared one would not work, the problem that had eaten its previous game. A small group answered that with cheap prototypes before the team grew from 5 to 13 people.","a":"Have we proven every key mechanic in a vertical slice yet?","q":"“However, in light of our concerns we decided to be cautious and avoid committing to making the game until we knew it would really work.”","qa":"Chris Harvey, co-founder of DrinkBox Studios","st":"Postmortem: DrinkBox Studios' Guacamelee!","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-drinkbox-studios-i-guacamelee-i-#:~:text=However%2C%20in%20light%20of%20our,knew%20it%20would%20really%20work","sd":"Sep 2013","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Set Guacamelee!'s milestones by public shows, from a GDC pitch to PAX demos","s":"Each show came several months after the last, so the team had to choose which features people would notice. Every show also brought outside playtests and press coverage.","w":"DrinkBox had not planned it, but its milestones became external showings. The run started with a 3 to 5 page publisher pitch at GDC in March 2011, a concept video and combat demo for E3 in June 2011, and an IGF build in November 2011. Public and press demos followed at PAX East, E3 and PAX Prime through September 2012. Showing the game so often forced hard choices about features. After each show the team had outside playtest results and adjusted its production approach. Guacamelee! shipped in April 2013.","r":"Work stayed on features players would notice at the next show.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Tie milestones to public showings a few months apart, and cut each build to what an outside player will notice.","i":"A public date cannot slip quietly, so it forces the cuts that internal milestones leave unmade; Harvey found internal milestones gave unrefined features. He also names the risk: external milestones can produce throwaway work, which is why the spacing matters.","a":"Is our next milestone a public showing a few months away?","q":"“Having to show the game constantly while still making progress towards the final release forced us to make hard decisions about what features to include.”","qa":"Chris Harvey, co-founder of DrinkBox Studios","st":"Postmortem: DrinkBox Studios' Guacamelee!","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-drinkbox-studios-i-guacamelee-i-#:~:text=Having%20to%20show%20the%20game,about%20what%20features%20to%20include","sd":"Sep 2013","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"guitarhero","n":"Guitar Hero","dv":"Harmonix","y":"2005","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built RedOctane's guitar game on the beat-matching design proven in Frequency and Amplitude","s":"The core loop did not need proving again. The team built the game in under a year on about $1 million, close to the original spec.","w":"RedOctane, which made dance mats, asked Harmonix for a game for its new guitar hardware. Harmonix agreed if it could focus only on rock, and a team had just come free from AntiGrav. Lessons from Frequency and Amplitude already set gem speed, gem size and the UI. The first prototype ran beat matching on imported Guitar Freaks guitars, while the two companies iterated on the controller. The budget was about $1 million. The game was built in under a year and shipped in November 2005. In 2020 project director Greg LoPiccolo said it went very smoothly.","r":"The game shipped in under a year, close to its first spec.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"About $1 million and under a year of development.","nf":false,"l":"Pick projects where your proven core loop carries over, and spend pre-production on the new parts, such as hardware and content.","i":"Gem speed, gem size and UI were solved in earlier games, so prototyping time went to the controller and the setlist. The shortcut holds only while the core loop is the same; a new genre or loop resets it.","a":"Which parts of our core loop have we already proven elsewhere?","q":"“Relatively speaking, it was a pretty low-budget game -- about a million dollars, which is pretty tiny as a game budget.”","qa":"Rob Kay, lead designer of Guitar Hero at Harmonix","st":"Book Excerpt: Inside Game Design: Harmonix Music Systems","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/book-excerpt-inside-game-design-harmonix-music-systems#:~:text=Relatively%20speaking%2C%20it%20was%20a,tiny%20as%20a%20game%20budget","sd":"Dec 2007","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"gunsoficarusonline","n":"Guns of Icarus Online","dv":"Muse Games","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/209080/","ls":"Steam","cv":"gunsoficarusonline.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Signed a back-loaded $300K publisher deal with the spec list as a contract exhibit","s":"The publisher failed the prototype on alpha-level demands and withheld payment. The team left the deal nearly broke and threw out 5 months of art.","w":"In 2010 Howard Tsao went to Taiwan looking for distribution, and a publisher offered a co-development deal. It paid $300K to release, with $100K for concept and prototype and only $25K upfront. The scope, Tsao says, was 3 big games' worth in 18 months, and the spec list became a contract exhibit gated by an undefined inspection. At the last prototype milestone a one-day review took a month, and the CTO failed it for missing alpha features. The team terminated the deal and threw out 5 months of art. The game launched in October 2012.","r":"Prototype payment was withheld, the deal ended and the studio was nearly broke.","re":"The team rebuilt its art pipeline and moved networking from Smartfox to Photon.","co":"5 months of art thrown out; Tsao borrowed from his parents.","nf":false,"l":"Before you sign, put acceptance criteria and a review deadline for each milestone into the contract.","i":"A spec list fixed in the contract cannot change as the game changes, and an undefined inspection lets the payer decide when work is done. Back-loaded pay means the developer carries the risk until then.","a":"Does our contract define how and when each milestone is accepted?","q":"“If there was a lesson for us, it would be to never again relinquish development control and agree to an opaque process.”","qa":"Howard Tsao, Muse Games","st":"Guns of Icarus Online Post-Mortem - Part I: Publisher Nightmare","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/guns-of-icarus-online-post-mortem-part-i-publisher-nightmare#:~:text=If%20there%20was%20a%20lesson,agree%20to%20an%20opaque%20process","sd":"May 2014","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"hacknslash","n":"Hack ’n’ Slash","dv":"Double Fine","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/246070/","ls":"Steam","cv":"hacknslash.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Spent 3 months of pre-production on technical risks and locked all puzzles on paper","s":"Design risks went untested into production. Mechanics came online already tied to puzzle chains and story beats, so none could be reworked, and the game shipped thinner than hoped.","w":"Hack 'n' Slash began as Brandon Dillon's 2012 Amnesia Fortnight pitch, and Indie Fund led the investment in the full game. With 6 full-time developers and a short schedule, Dillon wrote a waterfall plan: a design document that set every mechanic and puzzle. About 3 months of pre-production went on technical risks such as self-modifying code and procedural rooms. Mechanics then came online in production, already tied to puzzle chains and narrative beats. The schedule still slipped, and puzzles were whittled down. The game shipped in September 2014, and Dillon later called it thinner than hoped.","r":"Mechanics could not be reworked, and the open world felt thin.","re":"The team released an Early Access version for expert feedback before 1.0.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Split pre-production between technical and design risks, and playtest each new mechanic before you chain puzzles to it.","i":"A puzzle chain freezes a mechanic the moment a puzzle uses it, so a mechanic tested late cannot change without breaking the structure. The plan could work for known mechanics; Dillon says it was ill-suited to an experimental game.","a":"Has every new mechanic been playtested before puzzles depend on it?","q":"“This means that we didn’t have time in our preproduction phase to address our game design risks.”","qa":"Brandon Dillon, project lead at Double Fine","st":"Double Fine's 'heartfelt and personal' Hack 'n' Slash: A postmortem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/double-fine-s-heartfelt-and-personal-i-hack-n-slash-i-a-postmortem#:~:text=This%20means%20that%20we%20didn%E2%80%99t,address%20our%20game%20design%20risks","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Replaced a narrative-heavy first act with a door the player hacks open","s":"One email from backer Adam Saltsman removed a large content production risk. The game's very first moment now taught its core mechanic.","w":"Early in development, Brandon Dillon was designing a complicated adventure-style first act, with social engineering riddles and expensive, time-consuming narrative sequences. He sent progress details to the investor group led by Indie Fund. Adam Saltsman replied with a long email of design advice, urging him to make the very first moment show the game's unique mechanics. His example was a door the player opens by switching its locked variable from true to false. Dillon made it the game's first puzzle, and in 2015 he said the email removed a huge content production risk.","r":"The costly first act was dropped before production.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you build an opening, check that its first minute shows the core mechanic, and cut costly setup that delays it.","i":"The narrative act was expensive content that delayed the one thing only this game offered, so one cheap puzzle fixed both cost and messaging. An outside reader with no stake in the plan saw it first.","a":"Does our first minute show the core mechanic?","q":"“In one email, he showed me how to eliminate a huge content production risk and dramatically improve the way the game communicated to the player.”","qa":"Brandon Dillon, project lead at Double Fine","st":"Double Fine's 'heartfelt and personal' Hack 'n' Slash: A postmortem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/double-fine-s-heartfelt-and-personal-i-hack-n-slash-i-a-postmortem#:~:text=In%20one%20email%2C%20he%20showed,game%20communicated%20to%20the%20player","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"hades","n":"Hades","dv":"Supergiant Games","y":"2020","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2018","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1145360/","ls":"Steam","cv":"hades.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Decided Hades would be an Early Access game before choosing anything else","s":"A small version shipped in December 2018 and grew with player feedback for nearly two years. Kasavin says the game grew as big as it did because of that.","w":"Supergiant had built Bastion, Transistor and Pyre without Early Access. For Hades, it decided on Early Access before it settled anything else. A small version went out on the Epic Games Store in December 2018. Each large update had to add new content, such as a new god, and answer player feedback openly. Feedback reshaped the game: Skelly and Dusa grew beyond comic relief, and Weapon Aspects became almost new weapons. Hades left Early Access in September 2020. In October 2020 Kasavin told PC Gamer the game grew as big as it did due to Early Access.","r":"The game's scope grew update by update with player feedback.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Decide the release format first, then plan every milestone as a playable update around it.","i":"Early Access suits a loop that is replayable while content is still being built, as a roguelike is. Supergiant also reused systems from three shipped games. A linear story game cannot ship a small version the same way.","a":"Have we chosen our release format before planning milestones?","q":"“Before we decided anything else, we decided that we wanted to make an Early Access game,”","qa":"Greg Kasavin, writer and designer at Supergiant Games","st":"A journey through Early Access helped make Hades a masterpiece","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/a-journey-through-early-access-helped-make-hades-a-masterpiece/#:~:text=Before%20we%20decided%20anything%20else%2C,make%20an%20Early%20Access%20game","sd":"Oct 2020","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"halflife","n":"Half-Life","dv":"Valve","y":"1998","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/70/","ls":"Steam","cv":"halflife.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Scrapped the November 1997 release and started over from one prototype level","s":"The prototype became the game's vision, and a cross-discipline Cabal wrote a 200-page design around it. Half-Life shipped a year late and won over 50 Game of the Year awards.","w":"Valve planned a November 1997 release, a year after starting work. By late September 1997 the levels did not fit together, monsters broke when fought off-plan and the game was not fun. Valve chose to start over and rework every stage. A small group spent a month folding the best working pieces into one prototype level. A Cabal of 3 engineers, a level designer, a writer and an animator then met 6 hours a day, 4 days a week, for 5 months. More than 200 playtests followed. Half-Life shipped in November 1998.","r":"Release slipped a full year, to November 1998.","re":"Valve built the design in Cabals and checked it in 200+ silent playtests.","co":"1 year of delay; the Cabal spent 5 months doing no other work.","nf":false,"l":"If the pieces do not add up to a playable whole, stop and build one level that shows the whole game.","i":"Good parts do not add up to a good game until something forces them together. One fun level gave everyone a shared target to measure the rest against. Valve could pay for the year because it was fairly independent of its publisher.","a":"Is there one level that already plays like the finished game?","q":"“There were some really wonderful individual pieces, but as a whole the game just wasn’t working.”","qa":"Ken Birdwell, senior designer at Valve","st":"The Cabal: Valve’s Design Process For Creating Half-Life","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/the-cabal-valve-s-design-process-for-creating-i-half-life-i-#:~:text=There%20were%20some%20really%20wonderful,game%20just%20wasn%E2%80%99t%20working","sd":"Dec 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"halflife2","n":"Half-Life 2","dv":"Valve","y":"2004","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/220/","ls":"Steam","cv":"halflife2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Wrote a complete design document while the new engine was still being built","s":"Design moved slowly and leaned on features nobody could test yet. When the Source engine matured, Valve had story material and loose content but no game you could play through.","w":"Half-Life shipped in November 1998, and the sequel set far bigger goals. Valve nearly tripled the team and built acting, physics, AI, sound, rendering and networking from scratch. During that push, an expanded cabal met for months to write a complete design document. Design moved slowly because nobody could predict what the unfinished tech would allow. When Source matured, the team had a story timeline and raw content, but the levels were not tied together. The initial phase ran 48 months. Valve split design into three cabals and shipped on November 16, 2004.","r":"Early design crawled, and the levels did not form a playable game.","re":"Valve split design into three cabals plus art, sound and acting teams.","co":"48-month initial phase before a 23-month final phase.","nf":false,"l":"Hold off on a full design document until the team can play the core technology it depends on.","i":"Designers cannot judge mechanics they cannot play, so a document written ahead of the tech fills with guesses. The story timeline, characters and locations written in the same period still held up later.","a":"Which parts of our design depend on tech we cannot run yet?","q":"“Design work during the early phase of development progressed very slowly because we found it difficult to predict what kinds of designs our technology would enable once it was finished.”","qa":"Brian Jacobson, software developer, and David Speyer, software engineer, at Valve","st":"Scaling the Cabal: Valve's Design Process for Creating Half-Life 2","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_November_2005.pdf#:~:text=To%20make%20matters%20worse,that%20were%20purely%20theoretical","sd":"Nov 2005","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4,"sh":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_November_2005.pdf#page=22"},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Prototyped every Half-Life 2 chapter on orange grid maps before any art","s":"Level designers proved mechanics in playtests before investing in art, which cut iteration cost and left artists free to explore visual themes. Gameplay still broke sometimes after the art pass.","w":"Valve carried its cabal design process from Half-Life (1998) into the sequel. Once the Source technology matured, design split into three gameplay cabals of four or five level designers and programmers. Each chapter's first prototypes ran on orange maps: orange grid walls, gray floors, no final art. Levels that playtested well went to the art team for final models and lighting. Art could still break gameplay: playtesters refused to cross one thin suspension bridge model, so cabals always playtested after the art pass. Half-Life 2 shipped in November 2004; Brian Jacobson and David Speyer described the method a year later.","r":"Designers could rework mechanics cheaply before artists committed to a look.","re":"Cabals playtested again after each art pass to catch broken gameplay.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Playtest each level in untextured blockout until its mechanics work, then start the art pass.","i":"Art locks a space: once it is built, every layout change means rework for artists, so blockout keeps changes cheap while mechanics are unproven. The bridge shows the limit: final art can change how players read a space, so playtest again after art.","a":"Do our levels pass a playtest before artists touch them?","q":"“Effecting this practice dramatically reduced the iteration cost and avoided any early commitment to the look of an area.”","qa":"Brian Jacobson and David Speyer, Valve","st":"Postmortem: The making of Valve's Half-Life 2 (2004)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-the-making-of-i-half-life-2-i-#:~:text=Effecting%20this%20practice%20dramatically%20reduced,the%20look%20of%20an%20area","sd":"Nov 2005, reprinted Nov 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built its own engine from scratch after judging no licensed engine fit Valve's plans","s":"The estimate became at least 3 years; development ran more than 5 years, was reported at $40 million, and Newell said the project chewed people up.","w":"Newell launched Half-Life 2 in June 1999. Valve looked at licensing an engine, but by late summer the team concluded no outside technology matched its plans and it would build its own engine from scratch, which meant at least 3 years. By mid-2001, almost 2 years in, Valve had a rough script and concept art. After a proof-of-concept reel in March 2002 Newell told the team they were not there yet, and the game skipped E3 2002. Half-Life 2 shipped on 16 November 2004 after what Valve called more than 5 years of development; Keighley puts it at $40 million.","r":"Development took more than 5 years instead of the 3 estimated.","re":"Newell planned smaller and shorter projects next to avoid burnout.","co":"More than 5 years, reported $40 million, over $1 million a month by late 2003.","nf":false,"l":"If you build your own engine, plan for the years before anything is showable and set a milestone that proves the engine.","i":"A new engine pushed the first showable build out by years, and the 3-year estimate was a floor, not a plan. Valve could keep going; a studio that must show progress at every publisher milestone rarely survives 2 years with nothing to show.","a":"What will we have to show after 2 years on this engine?","q":"“We absolutely have chewed people up on this project.”","qa":"Gabe Newell, Valve","st":"The Final Hours of Half-Life 2","su":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/the-final-hours-of-half-life-2/1100-6112889/#:~:text=We%20absolutely%20have%20chewed,on%20this%20project","sd":"Nov 2004, reprinted Apr 2016","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Announced a 30 September 2003 ship date on the day Half-Life 2 was revealed","s":"By July the team knew it would miss; Valve kept confirming the date until 23 September. Levels were cut, the game slipped about 14 months, and Newell later called it a fiasco.","w":"Gabe Newell set the 30 September 2003 ship date at a late February 2003 meeting and stated it publicly on 21 March 2003, the day Valve revealed the game. By July Newell found it obvious the date would be missed, yet on 24 August Valve said the release date was unchanged. On 23 September Valve told Shacknews the game was pushed to the holidays with no firm date. On 4 October the source code leaked. Half-Life 2 shipped on 16 November 2004. In Keighley's November 2004 account Newell said he should have answered 'When it's done'.","r":"Levels cut, crunch through 2003, and a public date missed by about 14 months.","re":"Valve pulled the date on 23 September 2003 and Newell later apologised publicly.","co":"Crunch, Jet Ski levels cut, fan trust lost; burn above $1 million a month.","nf":false,"l":"Announce a ship date only after a content-complete build proves the plan; otherwise say 'when it's done'.","i":"Newell believed the date, so the team stared at the ceiling rather than argue, and leadership froze once the miss was obvious with no new date to offer. A public date works as a target only when the team can say no to it.","a":"Would a content-complete build back the date we are about to announce?","q":"“In retrospect I probably should have just said, 'When it's done.'”","qa":"Gabe Newell, founder and managing director of Valve","st":"The Final Hours of Half-Life 2","su":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/the-final-hours-of-half-life-2/1100-6112889/#:~:text=In%20retrospect%20I%20probably%20should,When%20it%27s%20done","sd":"Nov 2004, reprinted Apr 2016","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"halflife2episodethree","n":"Half-Life 2: Episode Three","dv":"Valve","y":"2008","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2008","lk":"https://www.half-life.com/en/halflife2/20th","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Paused Episode Three 6 months in to ship Left 4 Dead and never restarted it","s":"By the time the team considered going back, the argument was that it was too late and a new engine was needed. The episode never shipped; staff later called that reasoning wrong in hindsight.","w":"After Episode Two (October 2007), Valve worked on Episode Three: an ice gun, blob enemies, the Borealis. Six months in, the estimate was 1.5 to 2.5 more years to ship. Left 4 Dead needed an all-hands effort, so Valve put Episode Three down to ship it in November 2008. When the team considered going back, the argument won that it was too late and the series needed a new engine. Those people made Left 4 Dead 2 and Portal 2 instead. In Valve's 2024 documentary, staff said they could have spent 2 years and shipped it.","r":"Six months of pre-production shelved; the episode was never made.","re":"Staff made Left 4 Dead 2 and Portal 2 instead.","co":"6 months of pre-production work; the 1.5 to 2.5 years to finish were never spent.","nf":false,"l":"If you pull a team off a pre-production, set a date to restart or kill it; do not let 'too late' decide by default.","i":"A paused pre-production loses its window: the longer the pause, the stronger the case that the tech has moved on. The restart was argued away rather than decided, and one speaker felt the series had run out of fresh elements, so restarting was not free.","a":"If we pause this project, what date do we restart or kill it?","q":"“We could have definitely gone back and spent two years to make episode three.”","qa":"Valve developer (speaker not labelled in the subtitles), Valve","st":"Half-Life 2: 20th Anniversary Documentary","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YCjNT9qGjh4&t=7056s","sd":"Nov 2024","sy":"Participant documentary","w8":4}]},{"k":"halo","n":"Halo","dv":"Bungie","y":"2001","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1064221/","ls":"Steam","cv":"halo.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Accepted Microsoft's offer to buy Bungie and make Halo an Xbox launch title","s":"Once the Xbox specs arrived, basically all earlier work was thrown out. Code and content started over in fall 2000, and audio work began only in February 2001.","w":"Bungie had shown Halo since 1999 as a PC and Mac tech demo, including a Macworld keynote and a hands-on E3 2000 demo with an almost 10-minute movie. After that E3 showing, Microsoft offered to buy Bungie, move the team to Redmond and make Halo an Xbox launch title. Very little of the game had been finalized, so the offer looked like a great opportunity. Once the Xbox specs arrived, earlier work was basically thrown out, and new code and content began in fall 2000. Audio waited until February 2001, after the team finished Oni's audio and built new studios.","r":"Bungie restarted code and content from scratch in fall 2000.","re":"Audio was redesigned for the Xbox, and real-time 5.1 surround shipped.","co":"Work from 1998 to mid-2000 basically discarded; restart in fall 2000.","nf":false,"l":"Make a platform switch while little of the game is final, and plan for the earlier work to be thrown out.","i":"The switch was affordable because little of the game was final: most earlier work was demos built for shows on PC and Mac. A project with locked content would lose far more from the same move.","a":"How much of our work would survive a platform change today?","q":"“All previous work on Halo was basically broomed once we got the specs on the Xbox and we started over with new code and content development in the fall of 2000.”","qa":"Marty O'Donnell, Bungie","st":"Producing Audio for Halo","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/producing-audio-for-halo#:~:text=All%20previous%20work%20on%20Halo,the%20fall%20of%202000","sd":"May 2002","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"halo2","n":"Halo 2","dv":"Bungie","y":"2004","sc":"AAA","se":"Remaster 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1064270/","ls":"Steam","cv":"halo2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Built the E3 2003 demo on a graphics engine that could never ship on Xbox","s":"After E3 Bungie threw the engine away and started from scratch. For over a year there was no working version of Halo; features were still being cut 4 to 5 months before gold.","w":"At E3 2003 Bungie showed Halo 2, and the audience gasped and cheered. The engine behind the demo could never ship a game on the Xbox. Back from E3, the team admitted it and scrapped the entire graphics engine. For over a year there was no working version of Halo: designers and artists built assets they could not test. Features were still being cut 4 to 5 months before gold, and the team worked past 11 at night, 7 days a week, for months. Halo 2 shipped on 9 November 2004 and sold 2.4 million copies in 24 hours.","r":"A full engine rewrite and over a year with no playable build.","re":"Bungie rewrote the engine, cut features late and crunched 7 days a week.","co":"Graphics engine rewritten; over a year without a working build; months of 7-day crunch.","nf":false,"l":"Build the show demo on the engine that will ship, at target hardware performance.","i":"A demo engine tuned for one show proves nothing about shipping on the hardware, so the real engine work started after the show instead of before it. The game still sold, but the price was the campaign's scope and the team's health.","a":"Does our show demo run on the engine we will actually ship?","q":"“So we literally scrapped the entire graphics engine and started from scratch.”","qa":"Chris Butcher, engineering lead on Halo 2, Bungie","st":"Better Than Halo: The Making of Halo 2","su":"https://primagames.com/?p=2885#:~:text=So%20we%20literally%20scrapped%20the,started%20from%20scratch","sd":"Apr 2010","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"halo3","n":"Halo 3","dv":"Bungie","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1064271/","ls":"Steam","cv":"halo3.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Put the lightmapper on a server farm for the cost of 1 or 2 artists","s":"Every artist got lighting results faster, and Bungie spent the gain on more iteration and polish passes instead of more content. Engineering lead Chris Butcher called it a cost-saver.","w":"Bungie's habit was to invest early in technology so it would not have to grow to 200 people; Chris Butcher said its processes would not scale that far. For Halo 3, the team took the lightmapper off a single artist's machine and made it run on a back-end server farm. Butcher put the total effort at about the man-hours of 1 or 2 extra artists. In return, every artist worked faster. Halo 3 shipped in 2007, and in a June 2008 interview Butcher said the gain went into iteration and polish passes, not extra content.","r":"Artists got more iteration and polish passes without a bigger team.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About the man-hours of 1 or 2 extra artists.","nf":false,"l":"Before you add headcount, price the tool that would speed up every artist, and compare it with 1 or 2 hires.","i":"A slow bake on one machine taxes every artist's iteration, so speeding it up multiplies the whole team. Butcher spends the gain on polish; a team that turns the saved time into more levels gives the saving back.","a":"Which slow tool step costs our artists more than one hire would?","q":"“I would say instead that this investment in technology and in process is ultimately a cost-saver for us.”","qa":"Chris Butcher, engineering lead at Bungie","st":"Bungie In 2008: Reflecting On Halo 3, Moving Beyond","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/bungie-in-2008-reflecting-on-halo-3-moving-beyond#:~:text=I%20would%20say%20instead%20that,ultimately%20a%20cost%2Dsaver%20for%20us","sd":"Jun 2008","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"haloinfinite","n":"Halo Infinite","dv":"343 Industries","y":"2021","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1240440/","ls":"Steam","cv":"haloinfinite.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the Slipspace engine in parallel with an open-world Halo Infinite","s":"Unfinished engine parts slowed content, cuts went too deep, and after a poorly received summer 2020 demo the launch slipped a year to December 2021.","w":"After Halo 5 in 2015, 343 Industries committed to a new engine, Slipspace, for Halo Infinite, plus a more open world and free-to-play multiplayer. Parts of the engine were still unfinished as the team moved into preproduction and even production. Content took far longer to get into the game and polish, and the team made a tremendous amount of cuts. Fans reacted badly to the summer 2020 Ascension demo, and Microsoft delayed the game a year. The campaign was due on 8 December 2021. Looking back, Ross said she should have picked free-to-play or the bigger world, not both.","r":"Content took far longer to get in, and the launch slipped a year.","re":"After the demo, 343 set up weekly in-office build reviews and took the extra year.","co":"A 1-year delay; the release came 6 years after the previous Halo.","nf":false,"l":"Do not build a new engine and the biggest game on it at the same time; lock the tools before you scale the content team.","i":"When the engine is unfinished, every content team builds on moving ground, so content lands late and polish slips. Ross also named free-to-play and a bigger world taken on at once, so the engine was not the only cause.","a":"Will our engine be stable before the content team scales up?","q":"“It’s kind of like we’re trying to fly the plane while we’re building the plane.”","qa":"Bonnie Ross, head of 343 Industries at Microsoft","st":"How Microsoft fixed Halo Infinite after facing the franchise’s biggest test yet","su":"https://www.cnet.com/tech/gaming/how-microsoft-fixed-halo-infinite-after-facing-the-franchises-biggest-test-yet/#:~:text=It%E2%80%99s%20kind%20of%20like%20we%E2%80%99re,while%20we%E2%80%99re%20building%20the%20plane","sd":"Dec 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"hardspaceshipbreaker","n":"Hardspace: Shipbreaker","dv":"Blackbird Interactive","y":"2022","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1161580/","ls":"Steam","cv":"hardspaceshipbreaker.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Greenlit the jam prototype in 2 days with three goals and no sales targets","s":"Two years of concept pivots followed with a team of 10 to 15. The 2018 vertical slice showed hand-built ships meant a 5 to 10 hour game, so the design was rebuilt around systems as content before Early Access.","w":"After Deserts of Kharak shipped in 2016, Blackbird held an internal jam. Hello, Collector got a green light in about 2 days, with three goals (ship, be unusual, own the IP) and no sales targets. An end-2016 check found the tone slow and dark; the retooled Falling Skies was judged generic and the team refused to continue it. A salvage concept won in 2017. The 2018 vertical slice was approved for production but showed hand-built ships capped the game at 5 to 10 hours, so the design was rebuilt around modular ships and hazards. Early Access launched in June 2020.","r":"Four years from jam to Early Access, with two concept pivots and a redesign.","re":"The design was rebuilt around systems as content before Early Access.","co":"4 years from jam to Early Access; 2 pivots; team of 10 to 15.","nf":false,"l":"If you greenlight a prototype in days, keep the team at 10 to 15 and time-box each pivot until the pillars hold.","i":"A green light without a locked concept buys time to find the game; Blackbird could afford it because the team stayed small and had no sales target to defend. With a bigger team or a publisher deadline, the same pivots become a crisis.","a":"How many people are on this before the pillars are locked?","q":"“Changing the game, pivoting, and refining it is super healthy.”","qa":"Elliot Hudson, Blackbird Interactive","st":"Inside the twisting, turning development of Hardspace: Shipbreaker","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/production/inside-the-twisting-turning-development-of-i-hardspace-shipbreaker-i-#:~:text=Changing%20the%20game%2C%20pivoting%2C,is%20super%20healthy","sd":"Aug 2020","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"haunt","n":"Haunt","dv":"NanaOn-Sha","y":"2012","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Kept an unlimited replayability pillar after cutting the user-content concept to XBLA size","s":"Halfway through production the pillar was cut. Haunt shipped as a game of about 3 hours with almost no replay value, and reviews marked it down for that.","w":"In September 2009 Microsoft asked NanaOn-Sha for a Kinect game. Haunt was first pitched around user-made content: players would build haunted houses and record their own monster animations. Microsoft suggested pitching it as an Xbox Live Arcade title. By the 2010 signing the concept was far smaller, but unlimited replayability stayed as a core pillar. Halfway through the 12-month production the team had to cut it, along with many items from the burn list. Haunt shipped in 2012 with almost no replay value. In the June/July 2012 postmortem Dewi Tanner said the short length cost Metacritic points.","r":"The replayability pillar was cut, leaving about 3 hours of play.","re":"Cut features repeatedly and used Scrum to keep quality up.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When the budget tier drops, rewrite the pillar list and drop every promise the new budget cannot pay for before you sign.","i":"A pillar inherited from a bigger pitch keeps its claim on the plan after its money is gone, so the team builds toward it and cuts it later. The team hoped Limbo had made short games acceptable, but reviews still marked Haunt down.","a":"Does every pillar still fit the budget we are signing?","q":"“Half way through production we were forced to cut that pillar, and the end product features almost no replay value whatsoever.”","qa":"Dewi Tanner, NanaOn-Sha","st":"Classic Postmortem: NanaOnSha's Haunt (2012)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-nanaonhas-haunt-2012#:~:text=Half%20way%20through%20production%20we,no%20replay%20value%20whatsoever.","sd":"Jun 2012, reprinted Oct 2024","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Budgeted the Kinect game without room to tune its walk-in-place navigation","s":"Walking in place and pointing took about 15% of total resources, none of it in the initial budget, so the team had to cut content again and again.","w":"When NanaOn-Sha pitched Haunt to Microsoft from 2009, it chose a first-person view where players walk in place and point an arm to aim a flashlight. The initial budget did not plan for refining that system. Microsoft worried that walking corridors would be a chore, so the team tuned the step threshold, built an autoturn option and added more small scares. It ended up dominating development and took about 15% of total resources. Reviews praised it when Haunt shipped in 2012. In the postmortem Dewi Tanner told teams on their first Kinect game to at least double their usual contingency.","r":"Navigation took about 15% of resources the budget never planned for.","re":"Cut content repeatedly to make room for the input work.","co":"About 15% of total resources, not in the initial budget.","nf":false,"l":"On a new input device, budget at least double your usual contingency for control tuning before you lock content scope.","i":"Motion input needs hacks and tuning no design document predicts: lag against smoothing, false positives, players who walk differently. That money comes out of content. The advice fits immature hardware; on a mature controller the usual contingency may be enough.","a":"Have we doubled contingency for tuning the new controls?","q":"“I would definitely suggest that any dev team embarking on its first Kinect project plan to at least double the usual contingency into its budget.”","qa":"Dewi Tanner, NanaOn-Sha","st":"Classic Postmortem: NanaOnSha's Haunt (2012)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-nanaonhas-haunt-2012#:~:text=I%20would%20definitely%20suggest%20that,contingency%20into%20its%20budget.","sd":"Jun 2012, reprinted Oct 2024","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Routed feedback between Microsoft, Zoe Mode and NanaOn-Sha through one contact per company","s":"Notes reached coders and artists through up to 5 middlemen, the navigation dispute never got settled, and the publisher's lost confidence cost Haunt its launch marketing.","w":"Haunt had 3 creative centers from its 2010 start: NanaOn-Sha in Tokyo, Microsoft's publishing team in Washington and Zoe Mode in the U.K. Each company had one point of contact. Feedback from Microsoft's top management passed through up to 5 middlemen before it reached the coder or artist, and it arrived reading like an order. After management changes at the publisher, the team had to re-justify its navigation system several times. Nobody flew to the publisher's headquarters to settle it. Haunt shipped in 2012 without marketing support, and the 2012 postmortem traced that to the dispute.","r":"Haunt got no marketing support at launch.","re":"Re-justified the navigation choice several times but never met in person.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Set up a direct line between your creative lead and the publisher's decision makers before production starts.","i":"Each hand-off strips the reasons from a note, so a suggestion arrives as an order and a defense arrives without its argument. One point of contact still helps focus; the failure was having no route around it for contested calls.","a":"Can our creative lead reach the publisher's decision maker directly?","q":"“Our failure to resolve these differing opinions on the navigation system really shook the publisher’s confidence in the project, which ruined any chance we had of getting marketing support at launch.”","qa":"Dewi Tanner, NanaOn-Sha","st":"Classic Postmortem: NanaOnSha's Haunt (2012)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-nanaonhas-haunt-2012#:~:text=Our%20failure%20to%20resolve%20these,marketing%20support%20at%20launch.","sd":"Jun 2012, reprinted Oct 2024","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"haydaypop","n":"Hay Day Pop","dv":"Supercell","y":"2020","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2020","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":"haydaypop.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Built a match-3 live-ops puzzle game with a team that did not play the genre","s":"The ex-Clash Royale team shipped fast and followed the data but never improved the core puzzle. Supercell ended development in soft launch in November 2020 and closed the servers on 1 February 2021.","w":"Supercell quietly soft-launched Hay Day Pop, a casual match-3 puzzle game with base building, in Australia, Finland and New Zealand; PocketGamer.biz spotted it on 16 March 2020. Many on the team had worked on Clash Royale, a game they played every day, but programmer Tuomas said nobody on the team played this type of game or knew its user-acquisition business model. Supercell says the team executed well and responded to data but never innovated in the core puzzle. On 30 November 2020 Supercell ended development, saying the game did not meet its standards; servers closed on 1 February 2021.","r":"Killed in soft launch; servers closed on 1 February 2021.","re":"The team regrouped to make a game it understood.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you greenlight a genre, check that the team plays it and knows its business model.","i":"A team that does not play the genre cannot tell a good change from a safe one, so it guesses from metrics. At Supercell the cell picks its own game, which makes team-genre fit the main gate; a studio that assigns teams needs other checks.","a":"Does anyone on this team play our genre every day?","q":"“All this user acquisition, this kind of business model, it’s not in our DNA.”","qa":"Tuomas, game programmer on Hay Day Pop at Supercell","st":"What We’ve Learned from Failures","su":"https://supercell.com/en/news/learning-from-failures/#:~:text=All%20this%20user%20acquisition%2C%20this,not%20in%20our%20DNA","sd":"Jan 2026","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"hearthstoneheroesofwarcraft","n":"Hearthstone","dv":"Blizzard Entertainment","y":"2014","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://hearthstone.blizzard.com/en-us","ls":"Website","cv":"hearthstoneheroesofwarcraft.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Designed Hearthstone on paper and in Flash while the engineers were on another project","s":"Two designers tried and discarded tons of bad ideas cheaply before code existed. Dodds says engineers available early would have been given unfinished guidance, and late changes would have cost far more.","w":"A small team inside Blizzard set out to make a digital card game. A lot of its engineers were moved to another project, so lead designer Eric Dodds and designer Ben Brode spent an unexpectedly long time planning the design on paper before any code was written. They tried tons of bad ideas and dropped them. They then built prototypes in Flash so they could swap elements in and out without junking up the final game's code. Hearthstone left beta and launched in March 2014, and Dodds told GDC that early engineer access would have produced half-baked guidance.","r":"Bad ideas died on paper instead of in code.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Two designers' time on paper and Flash prototypes.","nf":false,"l":"Keep engineers off the project until paper and throwaway prototypes have killed the bad ideas.","i":"Code makes an idea expensive to change, so guidance given to engineers early is half-baked and gets built anyway. Paper and Flash worked because card rules can be tested without an engine; a game whose feel depends on the engine needs in-engine prototypes sooner.","a":"Which of our open design questions can we answer on paper this week?","q":"“I wish I could say it was planned, but a lot of our engineers were moved to another project, so we got a lot of unintended design time,”","qa":"Eric Dodds, lead designer of Hearthstone, Blizzard Entertainment","st":"'Iterate fast' and other design lessons learned from Hearthstone","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/-iterate-fast-and-other-design-lessons-learned-from-i-hearthstone-i-#:~:text=I%20wish%20I%20could%20say,unintended%20design%20time","sd":"Mar 2014","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"heatsignature","n":"Heat Signature","dv":"Suspicious Developments","y":"2017","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/268130/","ls":"Steam","cv":"heatsignature.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built a galaxy meta-game before proving the ship-boarding core of the game","s":"The galaxy layer made missions feel more tedious. Francis found the real core about 2.5 years in, and a planned quick project took 3.5 years of full-time work.","w":"After Gunpoint shipped in 2013, Tom Francis called Heat Signature a quick GameMaker break from learning Unity. To test boarding ships, he had to build nearly the whole game: ship generator, AI, combat and destructible rooms. Players at EGX and Rezzed asked why they were doing the missions. Francis answered with a generated galaxy of warring factions, which could only be judged once fully built. It made the game more tedious. At a developer retreat in Sweden, about 2.5 years in, he switched to items for ship interiors, and the game improved fast. It shipped in 2017.","r":"Two years in, the game still was not good.","re":"Francis refocused on items and tools for the ship interiors.","co":"3.5 years of full-time work on a planned quick project.","nf":false,"l":"Before building any meta layer, name the core of the game and prototype only enough of it to see if it works.","i":"A concept you can only judge once most of it exists hides its problems for years. Francis applies this to games with one core; a game where many parts must all be good needs a different plan.","a":"How much of this game must we build before we know it works?","q":"“So this is clearly what I should have been working on instead of the galaxy thing.”","qa":"Tom Francis, writer, designer and programmer at Suspicious Developments","st":"Dealing with Scope Change in Heat Signature and Gunpoint","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r4-O_7wSyAQ&t=1262s","sd":"2018","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r4-O_7wSyAQ&t=916s"}]},{"k":"heavensvault","n":"Heaven's Vault","dv":"inkle","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/774201/","ls":"Steam","cv":"heavensvault.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Extended the ink scripting engine to handle free movement between scenes","s":"The rework took a lot of effort up front. Near the end of the project, the team could redraft whole scenes and add dialogue quickly.","w":"inkle had used ink, its own narrative scripting language, as a plain branching structure in Sorcery! and 80 Days. Heaven's Vault was a 3D world where the player can go from anywhere to anywhere at any time. At the start of the project, narrative director Jon Ingold worried ink could not cope with that. The team put a lot of work into extending it, so each choice now produces a new piece of film script. It came out in April 2019. In a launch-week article, Ingold said the payoff came at the end, when whole scenes could be rebuilt fast.","r":"Late scene rewrites and new dialogue went in quickly.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before writing at volume, check that your narrative tool fits this game's structure, not the structure of your last game.","i":"A tool that fits the structure badly makes every late rewrite slow, and late is when rewrites pile up. inkle paid that cost at the start instead. It helped that inkle owned ink; with a third-party tool the usual fix is choosing another one.","a":"Does our narrative tool fit this game's structure, or our last game's?","q":"“And it's quite a lot of work to get it to do that, but I'm super glad that we did, because the payoff is at the end of the project when actually we can redraft whole scenes and restructure things and add in new lines of dialogue, and completely alter the way scenes work extremely quickly and efficiently.”","qa":"Jon Ingold, narrative director at inkle","st":"How Inkle developed its own ancient language for Heaven's Vault","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/how-inkle-developed-its-own-ancient-language-for-i-heaven-s-vault-i-#:~:text=And%20it%27s%20quite%20a%20lot,work%20extremely%20quickly%20and%20efficiently","sd":"Apr 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"heavenlysword","n":"Heavenly Sword","dv":"Ninja Theory","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Licensed Havok physics for Heavenly Sword instead of writing an in-house system","s":"Ninja Theory could put its people on the parts of Heavenly Sword that were unique to the game. Havok already did what the team needed.","w":"Heavenly Sword was in development at Ninja Theory for more than 4 years. The studio's rule was to buy high-end technology where it could. For physics it licensed Havok, which had many man-years of development behind it and did what the team wanted. That let Ninja Theory deploy its team to areas unique to the game. Elsewhere the studio went its own way: it worked with Weta Digital on real-time facial animation and hired film and TV staff when the art pipeline proved harder than expected. Co-founder Nina Kristensen described the choice after release.","r":"Physics came off the shelf, and staff went to game-specific work.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"List the systems middleware already does well, license those, and spend your own people only on what makes your game different.","i":"A licensed system carries years of work you do not pay to repeat, and its value is the people it frees. It saves less when the middleware forces workarounds, or when physics itself is the core feature.","a":"Which systems are we building that we could license instead?","q":"“Sure, we could write our own physics system but that wouldn’t make sense.”","qa":"Nina Kristensen, co-founder and Chief Development Ninja of Ninja Theory","st":"Developer Afterthoughts: Heavenly Sword Interview","su":"https://www.psu.com/news/developer-afterthoughts-heavenly-sword-interview/#:~:text=Sure%2C%20we%20could%20write%20our,that%20wouldn%E2%80%99t%20make%20sense","sd":"","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"heavygearii","n":"Heavy Gear II","dv":"Activision","y":"1999","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Scheduled a new-engine game with 40 missions and multiplayer in 13 months","s":"Every system had to be built from scratch, and a demo planned as 2 weeks for 3 people took almost 3 months. The game missed its October 1998 date by 9 months and shipped in July 1999.","w":"In the fall of 1997 Activision Studios started Heavy Gear II with a team of 20 and a 13-month schedule to a fall 1998 release. The design called for a brand new engine, more than 40 single-player missions in three modes, and multiplayer. The schedule gave a playable demo 2 weeks for a designer, a programmer and an artist; with most systems not yet working, it ate almost 3 months. After E3 1998, the game slipped past Christmas, then past a March 1999 target. It shipped in July 1999, 9 months late after 19 months, to great reviews.","r":"Shipped 9 months late after months of crunch.","re":"Activision retargeted March 1999, then let the team decide when it was done.","co":"13 months planned, 19 actual; demo took almost 3 months instead of 2 weeks.","nf":false,"l":"When the engine is new, schedule every system as new work, including the show demo.","i":"A from-scratch engine means every feature needs significant development time, so a 13-month plan assumed systems that did not exist yet. The demo showed the pattern: scheduled as a 2-week side job, it needed 3 months because the systems it depended on were not working.","a":"Which items in our schedule assume systems that do not exist yet?","q":"“Alas, our aggressive schedule and risk taking proved to be our Achilles’ heel.”","qa":"Clancy Imislund, AI, scripting and tools programmer on Heavy Gear II, Activision","st":"Postmortem: Activision's Heavy Gear 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-activision-s-i-heavy-gear-2-i-#:~:text=Alas%2C%20our%20aggressive%20schedule,risk%20taking%20proved%20to%20be","sd":"Dec 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Scheduled a polished public demo as a two-week side task for three people","s":"The demo took most of the team while core systems were still unfinished. It pushed the plan almost 3 months off course, and the game missed its date by 9 months.","w":"Activision started Heavy Gear II in fall 1997 on a 13-month plan for a fall 1998 release. The schedule included a polished demo for the top gaming websites, given to a designer, a programmer and an artist for part of their day over about two weeks. Most critical systems did not work yet and many assets did not exist, so the demo absorbed most of the team. The game was revealed at E3 1998, and the demo followed. By then the project was almost 3 months off plan. It missed October 1998 and shipped in July 1999.","r":"The team fell almost 3 months behind and wore itself out.","re":"The team aimed for March 1999, then shipped in July 1999.","co":"Almost 3 months off plan; release slipped 9 months overall.","nf":false,"l":"Schedule a public demo inside the main build plan, with its real staff and weeks, not as a side task.","i":"A demo needs working systems and finished assets. When they do not exist yet, the team builds them early for the demo and the whole team gets pulled in. The postmortem also blames staff turnover and an aggressive plan for the 9-month slip.","a":"Can the build we will have that month carry this demo?","q":"“Unfortunately, this feeling quickly evaporated when we realized that we had taken almost a three-month departure from our original development schedule and totally exhausted ourselves in the process.”","qa":"Clancy Imislund, AI, scripting and tools programmer, Activision","st":"Postmortem: Activision's Heavy Gear 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-activision-s-i-heavy-gear-2-i-#:~:text=Unfortunately%2C%20this%20feeling%20quickly%20evaporated,totally%20exhausted%20ourselves%20in%20the%20process","sd":"Dec 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built a script-free autopilot AI with little input from mission designers","s":"Designers could not tune difficulty, so some missions were trivial and others impossible. The team had to leave the design document behind and fix missions one by one.","w":"Activision started Heavy Gear II in fall 1997 on a new engine. Early on, the programmers chose a pure autopilot AI: friendly and enemy units would act sensibly with no script, to take load off the designers. They designed most of the game logic with little designer input. During production, designers and outside testers found the play flat. Enemies were too vicious, squadmates disobeyed, and difficulty had no ramp; in some fights the player lived about 4 seconds. Ad hoc brainstorms and mission-by-mission playtests fixed it before the July 1999 release. The December 1999 postmortem blamed the missing designer input.","r":"Missions needed late emergency tuning, one at a time.","re":"The team left the design document and playtested mission by mission.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before the AI architecture is locked, check that designers can tune a mission's difficulty without a programmer.","i":"An AI that runs without scripts takes control away from the people who tune missions. Designers can fix only what the system lets them touch, so the fixes land late. The risk is in shutting designers out, not in autonomous AI as such.","a":"Can our designers tune a mission's difficulty without asking a programmer?","q":"“Most of this was due to the fact that we arrogant programmers designed and implemented most of the game logic without enough consultation and input from our able game design staff.”","qa":"Clancy Imislund, AI, scripting and tools programmer at Activision","st":"Postmortem: Activision's Heavy Gear 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-activision-s-i-heavy-gear-2-i-#:~:text=Most%20of%20this%20was%20due,from%20our%20able%20game%20design%20staff","sd":"Dec 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"heavymetalfakk2","n":"Heavy Metal: F.A.K.K. 2","dv":"Ritual","y":"2000","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Reused and extended Sin's text-driven scripting tools on the Quake 3 engine","s":"Designers and artists could change the game without recompiling, and the visuals won praise. But they had to work like programmers, and learning the tools took longer than planned.","w":"Ritual got a snapshot of the unreleased Quake 3 engine in February 1999 and dropped in its game code from Sin, scripting language included. Production began in earnest in early March 1999. The programmers built most tools around text files and plain English commands, and grew the Morpheus scripting language from about 500 commands to about 700. Level designers had to script puzzles, events and cinematics; artists drove effects through text files. The game went gold on July 31, 2000, with nearly 10,000 lines of script. Scott Alden's December 2000 postmortem said the complexity added more time than anticipated.","r":"Learning the extended tools added unplanned development time.","re":"The team absorbed the extra learning during production.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you extend a scripting tool, time a level designer building one scripted scene with it, and put that learning in the schedule.","i":"Text-file tools are fast for programmers and slow for everyone else until they learn them. Ritual counted on engine familiarity, but the new commands were the part nobody knew. Familiar tools still carry a learning cost for every feature added on top.","a":"Have we timed a level designer using the new scripting commands?","q":"“This complexity added more to the development time than we had anticipated.”","qa":"Scott Alden, senior programmer at Ritual Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Ritual Entertainment's Heavy Metal: F.A.K.K. 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-ritual-entertainment-s-i-heavy-metal-f-a-k-k-2-i-#:~:text=This%20complexity%20added%20more%20to,than%20we%20had%20anticipated","sd":"Dec 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Committed the small team to single-player only, with multiplayer if time allowed","s":"The campaign stayed tight, with new monsters and weapons in almost every level. The deadline left no time for multiplayer, and the short campaign became the top complaint.","w":"Early in development, Ritual decided the small F.A.K.K. 2 team would build single-player only. It was a hard call in a studio where Quake 3 deathmatch was an office pastime. Multiplayer would go in only if time allowed. Every level was sketched on paper first, and almost every level brought new monsters and weapons. The July 2000 deadline came too fast for multiplayer, and hardcore players could finish the game in under 10 hours. In December 2000, Scott Alden named length as the biggest complaint from reviewers and players.","r":"Shipped with no multiplayer and a campaign under 10 hours.","re":"Ritual had an arena-style multiplayer patch in the works by December 2000.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Set a fixed date to decide whether optional multiplayer ships, and plan single-player length as if it will not.","i":"Cutting multiplayer bought the small team a tight, varied campaign. It also removed the mode that keeps a short game alive after the first run. A feature parked for spare time is close to cut, so plan the product without it.","a":"If optional multiplayer never ships, is the campaign long enough?","q":"“We were going to try to put multiplayer into the final release if we had the time, but our July deadline came so quickly that we just didn't have the time to finish it.”","qa":"Scott Alden, senior programmer at Ritual Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Ritual Entertainment's Heavy Metal: F.A.K.K. 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-ritual-entertainment-s-i-heavy-metal-f-a-k-k-2-i-#:~:text=We%20were%20going%20to%20try,have%20the%20time%20to%20finish%20it","sd":"Dec 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"hellbladesenuassacrifice","n":"Hellblade: Senua's Sacrifice","dv":"Ninja Theory","y":"2017","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/414340/","ls":"Steam","cv":"hellbladesenuassacrifice.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Self-funded Hellblade as a small-team game at half the length and cost of AAA","s":"About 20 people shipped it in 3 years. Hellblade sold 500,000 copies in 3 months and broke even in 3 months instead of the 9 the studio expected.","w":"Ninja Theory chose to fund Hellblade itself instead of working with a big publisher. Its own profits from DmC and Disney Infinity 3.0 paid more than half; loans, a Wellcome grant and UK tax relief covered the rest. In 2015 Dom Matthews put the budget way below $10 million, with 15 people and most disciplines filled by one person. He built a motion-capture space in the studio boardroom. Break-even needed 300,000 copies. Hellblade launched in August 2017, sold 250,000 in its first week and reached 500,000 within 3 months.","r":"Profit came within 3 months of launch instead of the expected 9.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Budget way below $10 million; about 20 people for 3 years.","nf":false,"l":"Set the break-even sales number first, then size the team, budget and game length to hit it.","i":"A break-even target turns scope arguments into arithmetic, so every hire and feature gets checked against copies sold. It worked with experienced AAA developers; a junior team that small has less margin.","a":"How many copies do we need to sell to break even?","q":"“We've put in our money; we've got loans and some support from the Wellcome Trust.”","qa":"Dominic Matthews, Ninja Theory","st":"Former 'DmC' devs are risking millions to make a 'AAA' indie","su":"https://www.engadget.com/2015-09-16-ninja-theory-hellblade-indie-game.html#:~:text=We%27ve%20put%20in%20our%20money,support%20from%20the%20Wellcome%20Trust","sd":"Sep 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":5}]},{"k":"helldivers2","n":"Helldivers 2","dv":"Arrowhead Game Studios","y":"2024","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/553850/","ls":"Steam","cv":"helldivers2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Stayed on Autodesk Stingray after the vendor discontinued the engine in 2018","s":"Arrowhead's own engineers had to bring the engine up to parity with no vendor support. Technical problems later held up content, and in 2026 the CEO said the studio had underinvested in its tech.","w":"Arrowhead built Helldivers 2 on Autodesk Stingray, the engine that grew out of Bitsquid and ran the first game. Autodesk discontinued Stingray in 2018, after the project had started, and the studio stayed on it. In February 2024, just after launch, CEO Johan Pilestedt said on X that his engineers had to build everything to parity with other engines with no support. FRVR reported that the 2025 Into the Unjust update made the technical problems critical, and Arrowhead delayed content to ship months of fixes. In March 2026 CEO Shams Jorjani said the studio had underinvested in its tech.","r":"Arrowhead's engineers built parity features alone, with no vendor support.","re":"Arrowhead delayed content for fixes and now puts more into the engine.","co":"Months of fixes and delayed content after the 2025 update.","nf":true,"l":"If you build on an engine its vendor has dropped, budget a standing engine team for every feature a supported engine would give you.","i":"A dropped engine gets no fixes or features from the vendor, so every gap lands on your own programmers. The debt showed most in live updates. Jorjani says any engine, Unreal included, yields back what you put into it.","a":"Who maintains our engine if the vendor stops, and is that team budgeted?","q":"“Our crazy engineers had to do everything, with no support to build the game to parity with other engines.”","qa":"Johan Pilestedt, CEO of Arrowhead Game Studios","st":"Helldivers 2 was built on an obscure Swedish engine discontinued in 2018","su":"https://pcgamer.com/helldivers-2-engine-bitsquid-autodesk-stingray#:~:text=Our%20crazy%20engineers%20had%20to,parity%20with%20other%20engines","sd":"Feb 2024","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"hellgatelondon","n":"Hellgate: London","dv":"Flagship Studios","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"Re-release 2018","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/939520/","ls":"Steam","cv":"hellgatelondon.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Made Hellgate a single-player, free online and subscription game, RPG and shooter at once","s":"Flagship grew to 100 people when its founders wanted 25, shipped a game everyone knew needed more months, and by August 2008 had lost the Hellgate IP to its lender.","w":"Blizzard veterans founded Flagship Studios to make Hellgate: London. It was to be a single-player game, a free online game and an optional subscription service, shipped in 17 languages on a high-end in-house engine. Carrying all of it pushed the company to about 100 people, including Ping0, a second company for servers and billing, and a second game, Mythos. Hellgate launched in 2007, and everyone knew it needed 4 to 6 more months. By August 2008 Flagship was barely open and Comerica Bank held the Hellgate IP. In 2011 Roper said they tried to do too much.","r":"Flagship grew to 100 staff and shipped a game it knew was unfinished.","re":"Investor talks failed; most of the Mythos team re-formed as Runic Games.","co":"100 employees against a planned 25; Hellgate IP passed to the lender.","nf":false,"l":"Pick one primary game type and one business model at concept, and write down what the game will not be.","i":"Each extra mode, model and platform added its own content, tech and support work, and the company grew to carry it. Roper also blamed a weak 2007 PC market and limited funding, so scope was one cause among several.","a":"Can we say in one sentence what this game is not?","q":"“The biggest failure with Hellgate is we just tried to do too much.”","qa":"Bill Roper, former CEO of Flagship Studios","st":"Bill Roper: Reflections on Hellgate","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/bill-roper-reflections-on-i-hellgate-i-#:~:text=The%20biggest%20failure%20with%20Hellgate,tried%20to%20do%20too%20much","sd":"Feb 2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"helloneighbor2","n":"Hello Neighbor 2","dv":"Eerie Guest","y":"2022","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1321680/","ls":"Steam","cv":"helloneighbor2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"External commitments","t":"Announced the sequel with a public alpha built around a stalking crow creature","s":"Playtests showed players could not read the creature's AI, and it was later cut. Fans who had played it in the alpha felt let down at launch.","w":"tinyBuild planned Hello Neighbor 2 as a bigger game that would ship on every platform at once, after a long pre-production. It announced that pre-production vision with a playable alpha, in which a crow-like creature stalked the player through an open town. The alpha went viral, but playtests showed players could not read the stalking AI. In production, with over 100 people in 6 teams, the pandemic, the war in Ukraine and a retail build due 6 months early, the team cut the crow to keep the tech stable. The game shipped on 6 December 2022 to mixed-to-negative Steam reviews.","r":"The creature shown at announcement was missing from the final game.","re":"tinyBuild patched the game quickly after launch to win back player trust.","co":"Headline feature cut; launch Steam reviews mixed to negative.","nf":true,"l":"Put into an announcement or public alpha only features that passed internal playtests and fit your platform performance budget.","i":"A public alpha turns a pre-production idea into a promise fans hold you to. The alpha still did its job as a test: it exposed the unreadable AI early. The backlash came from cutting something fans had already played.","a":"Has every feature in our announcement build passed internal playtests?","q":"“The more we saw how players engaged with it the more we realized it wasn't the best idea mostly because it's really difficult to read AI behavior when it changes.”","qa":"Alex Nichiporchik, CEO of tinyBuild","st":"Hello Neighbor 2: Post-Mortem / Alex Nichiporchik (tinyBuild)","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=azTogDFpV-U&t=1950s","sd":"Jul 2023","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=azTogDFpV-U&t=1042s"}]},{"k":"herouroguetoredemption","n":"Hero-U: Rogue to Redemption","dv":"Transolar Games","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/375440/","ls":"Steam","cv":"herouroguetoredemption.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Finished the script and character models before the programming team was assembled","s":"The scripts could not be tested, and the models did not work with Unity's Mecanim animation system. Much of the art was redone late, and the game shipped 2 years past its Kickstarter date.","w":"Hero-U's first Kickstarter ran in about 2013, and the Coles expected a 2016 release. When programmer Cidney Hamilton joined in January 2015, Corey estimated art and writing at 90% done and programming at 10%. Only the first scene was programmed, and Lori's scripts for most of the game were untested and untestable. The character models did not fit Mecanim, released mid-production, so cutscenes stayed flat. The team redid the UI, 3D environments and vignette paintings. Alpha started in 2017, and the game shipped in July 2018. The April 2019 postmortem says this belonged in pre-production.","r":"Content locked early could not be cheaply fixed once the code arrived.","re":"The team hired an animator and redid the UI, environments and paintings.","co":"Budget spent on assets before code; release 2 years past the Kickstarter date.","nf":false,"l":"Have programmers build the core systems before you write the full script or finalize character models.","i":"Scripts and models made before the systems exist rest on guesses about how those systems will work. When the guesses are wrong, the budget is already spent. The delay also came from scope growth and a choice not to cut features.","a":"Is any final content being made for systems that do not run yet?","q":"“This led to a lot of issues that couldn’t cheaply be solved, because so much of the game’s budget had already gone into creating the assets we had.”","qa":"Cidney Hamilton, programmer, Transolar Games","st":"Hero-U: Rogue to Redemption Postmortem","su":"https://gamedeveloper.com/production/hero-u-rogue-to-redemption-postmortem#:~:text=This%20led%20to%20a%20lot,creating%20the%20assets%20we%20had","sd":"Apr 2019","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Expanded a 2D puzzle-RPG pitch toward a full Quest for Glory game","s":"The budget was still based on the early, low-budget Quest for Glory games. Kickstarter money covered 25% of what Quest for Glory IV cost, and the game shipped 2 years late.","w":"The original Hero-U pitch was a 2D puzzle-RPG, fairly easy to program. Backers wanted something closer to Lori and Corey Cole's Quest for Glory games, and the Coles wanted more ambition too. Corey still based the budget on the early Quest for Glory games, with 16-color art and Sierra's tools. When programmer Cidney Hamilton joined in January 2015, only the first scene was programmed and the Coles expected a 2016 release. They chose not to cut features. The game shipped in July 2018, 2 years after the promised date. Hamilton's April 2019 postmortem set out the gap.","r":"Shipped in July 2018, 2 years after the promised date.","re":"The Coles moved to a much smaller follow-up with 2D art and a smaller team.","co":"2 years late; Kickstarter money was 25% of a Quest for Glory IV budget.","nf":false,"l":"When backers ask for a bigger game, re-price it against the game they expect, then cut scope or raise money before production.","i":"Fans measured Hero-U against Quest for Glory IV, while the budget came from 16-color games built on Sierra's tools. Backers set the bar at the best game they remember. The slip also had other causes, such as art restarts and a remote, part-time team.","a":"Have we priced the game our backers expect, or the game we pitched?","q":"“We could have shipped sooner by cutting features; the Coles chose not to do this.”","qa":"Cidney Hamilton, programmer at Transolar Games","st":"Hero-U: Rogue to Redemption Postmortem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/production/hero-u-rogue-to-redemption-postmortem#:~:text=We%20could%20have%20shipped%20sooner,Coles%20chose%20not%20to%20do%20this","sd":"Apr 2019","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"hifirush","n":"Hi-Fi RUSH","dv":"Tango Gameworks","y":"2023","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1817230/","ls":"Steam","cv":"hifirush.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Kept the pitch to 2 people for a year to build a playable first level","s":"The 15-minute prototype, not the 10-page document, won Bethesda's greenlight, and the project stayed small for its first 2 years before the team grew.","w":"John Johanas pitched Hi-Fi Rush to studio head Mikami with about a 10-page document, and Mikami said to make it. Johanas and programmer Yuji Nakamura then prototyped alone for a year, building a 15-minute playable demo close to the first level. A literal take on the design made a slow action game; after about the fourth month the feel clicked. Bethesda's Todd Vaughn passed the prototype around without naming Tango, so the second pitch had hype behind it. The project stayed small for 2 years, then grew, and launched in 2023 as a shadow drop after nearly 5 secret years.","r":"Won the publisher greenlight on the prototype and shipped after nearly 5 years.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"2 people for 1 year of prototyping; nearly 5 years in total.","nf":false,"l":"Win the greenlight with a 15-minute playable slice, and keep the team at 2 until the core loop feels right.","i":"A playable slice let the publisher feel a genre the studio had never shipped, which no document could do, and the 2-person year kept the bet cheap while the feel was still wrong. Johanas calls the prototype the reason the game got made.","a":"Can we show a playable 15 minutes before asking for the budget?","q":"“In a way, I think that prototype was, realistically, the way that this game got greenlit and got made.”","qa":"John Johanas, game director of Hi-Fi Rush at Tango Gameworks","st":"Hi-Fi Rush: From a Little Idea to a Very Big Surprise – The Exclusive Oral History","su":"https://news.xbox.com/en-us/2023/10/04/hi-fi-rush-exclusive-oral-history/#:~:text=In%20a%20way%2C%20I%20think%20that%20prototype,greenlit%20and%20got%20made","sd":"Oct 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"hitman","n":"HITMAN","dv":"IO Interactive","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/236870/","ls":"Steam","cv":"hitman.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Turned the Hitman reboot into a digital-only episodic live game before launch","s":"Players were confused by the model and the launch had tech issues; IO's CEO later said the studio was too early with the concept and made many mistakes.","w":"After Hitman: Absolution in 2012, IO Interactive chose a purely digital, episodic release: digital margins were more than twice those of discs, so fewer sales covered the same revenue. Studio head Hannes Seifert spent a lot of effort convincing internal and external partners. The game premiered in March 2016 as the first episodic live digital AAA game, and confusion about the business model followed. Square Enix announced in May 2017 that it was selling IO; the studio completed a management buyout in June and kept Hitman. During 2017 players grew from 2 million to 7 million.","r":"Confusion at launch; Square Enix put IO up for sale in May 2017.","re":"Management bought the studio out in June 2017 and kept the Hitman IP.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Treat a new business model as a hypothesis: test how players read it before you change it at launch.","i":"Switching the model before launch meant players met the episodic plan without context and read it as risk, which no amount of praise for the design undid. Abrak credits the live model with what did work; the format itself was not the whole problem.","a":"Have we tested how players will read our business model?","q":"“We were, for sure, too early with the concept, and there were many, many mistakes made.”","qa":"Hakan Abrak, CEO of IO Interactive","st":"007: First Light Studio CEO Says \"There Are Learnings\" About Episodic Releases From Hitman","su":"https://gamingbolt.com/?p=632274#:~:text=We%20were%2C%20for%20sure%2C%20too%20early,many%20mistakes%20made","sd":"Nov 2025","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"hitmanabsolution","n":"Hitman: Absolution","dv":"IO Interactive","y":"2012","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/203140/","ls":"Steam","cv":"hitmanabsolution.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built Absolution as a linear cinematic action game while its new tech was unfinished","s":"Content teams waited on technology, and after E3 demos drew criticism the team reworked linear levels into open ones. Absolution took 7 years and did not recoup its costs in time.","w":"IO Interactive formed Absolution's concept in the era of Max Payne and Gears of War: linear, cinematic and action heavy. Content creators started while the technology was not ready and waited, with frustration and finger pointing. Early E3 demos drew the reply that it did not feel like Hitman, so mid-project the team opened up locations and put targets into the levels. Until then, targets died only in cutscenes. The game shipped in November 2012 after 7 years, reviewed favourably and did not sell enough. Layoffs and a cancelled project followed. IOI described all this to Noclip in 2019.","r":"Absolution took 7 years and needed a redesign halfway through.","re":"IOI later built Hitman 2016 on Absolution's fundamentals, back in open sandboxes.","co":"7 years of development, 2 years of crunch, costs not recouped in time.","nf":false,"l":"Do not staff full content production until the engine can run the levels you plan to build.","i":"Content made ahead of the tech waits or gets redone, and the long wait let the market move back to open games. A concept that drifts from the series' core fantasy can force a redesign even when the tech is ready.","a":"Can our engine run these levels today, or are artists waiting?","q":"“We didn't feel right at that point either so it was a complete crunch, it was a bit of a monster to deal with to try to get Hitman back into Hitman, all consuming crunch for two years.”","qa":"Hakan Abrak, executive producer of Hitman: Absolution at IO Interactive (CEO when interviewed)","st":"The Fall & Rise of Hitman | Noclip Documentary","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_stW6JvB4SI&t=430s","sd":"Jul 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_stW6JvB4SI&t=563s"}]},{"k":"hollowknight","n":"Hollow Knight","dv":"Team Cherry","y":"2017","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/367520/","ls":"Steam","cv":"hollowknight.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Set the Kickstarter goal for a 2-hour Metroidvania and let funding grow the world","s":"The campaign raised AU$57,138 against a AU$35,000 goal and paid for about 2 years of work on a bigger game. The money lasted only because of the team's personal situation.","w":"Team Cherry set its Kickstarter goal at AU$35,000, up from AU$30,000 a few days before launch, for a very small Metroidvania of about 2 hours. The campaign ran from 18 November to 18 December 2014 and raised AU$57,138 from 2,158 backers. The extra money made the game a level bigger and let the team of 3 work for about 2 years. Near the end, Indie Fund paid for extras that were not essential. Hollow Knight shipped on Steam on 24 February 2017. At BitSummit 2018, Ari Gibson said the budget held only because of the team's situation.","r":"The game grew past its 2-hour plan on a small crowdfunded budget.","re":"Near release, the team took Indie Fund money for nonessential extras.","co":"AU$57,138 over about 2 years, plus savings, partners' incomes and Indie Fund money.","nf":false,"l":"Before you set a crowdfunding goal, define the smallest game you could ship and what each extra funding level adds.","i":"A Metroidvania can be tied off at any size, so extra money went straight into more world. The budget lasted because the founders had no children, two partners worked full time and Adelaide was cheap; it will not cover 2 years of salaries.","a":"What would we ship if we raise only the goal?","q":"“And then because of funding that we got entirely from that campaign, it allowed us to work for a period of two years, essentially, and that allowed the game to get bigger again.”","qa":"Ari Gibson, co-director of Team Cherry","st":"Straight from the Source: Ari Gibson & William Pellen (Team Cherry)","su":"https://sourcegaming.info/2025/04/09/straight-from-the-source-team-cherry/#:~:text=And%20then%20because%20of%20funding,the%20game%20to%20get%20bigger%20again","sd":"2018, published Apr 2025","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"hollowknightsilksong","n":"Hollow Knight: Silksong","dv":"Team Cherry","y":"2025","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1030300/","ls":"Steam","cv":"hollowknightsilksong.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Turned the promised Hornet DLC into a full-scale sequel","s":"The 3-person team missed its own plan for a release in the first half of 2023 and shipped in September 2025. Kickstarter backers promised the DLC got the whole sequel free.","w":"Team Cherry's 2014 Kickstarter included a stretch goal for a second playable character with her own quests and abilities, Kotaku reported. Work on Hornet's adventure began right after Hollow Knight's PC release on 24 February 2017. It was planned as DLC, but in February 2019 the team announced it had become too large and too unique to stay one, and named it Silksong. The team planned to ship in the first half of 2023 but kept building. Silksong came out on 4 September 2025, and co-founder Ari Gibson said its world, towns and quests had grown to rival Hollow Knight.","r":"A DLC begun in 2017 shipped as a full sequel in 2025.","re":"Gibson settled on a fixed scope in the last few years before release.","co":"About 8.5 years from first work to release; backers got the sequel free.","nf":false,"l":"When a promised add-on outgrows its DLC size, re-plan it as a new game with its own date and budget.","i":"Team Cherry's method turns ideas into playable content fast, so every tangent became more game. Hollow Knight's sales took away the pressure to ship; a funded team with a monthly burn cannot let scope float like this.","a":"Is our add-on still DLC-sized, or should we re-plan it?","q":"“We do know that makes the wait a little longer, but we think the final, fresh world you’ll get to explore is worth it.”","qa":"Team Cherry, developer of Hollow Knight","st":"HOLLOW KNIGHT: SILKSONG REVEALED!","su":"https://www.teamcherry.com.au/blog/hollow-knight-silksong-revealed#:~:text=We%20do%20know%20that%20makes,is%20worth%20it","sd":"Feb 2019","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"horizonzerodawn","n":"Horizon Zero Dawn","dv":"Guerrilla Games","y":"2017","sc":"AAA","se":"Port 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1151640/","ls":"Steam","cv":"horizonzerodawn.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Prototyped the riskiest of 40 pitches with 10 people while the studio shipped Killzone","s":"The machine combat was running early on a small team. When Killzone Shadow Fall shipped, the whole studio joined a concept that already worked, and the game sold 2.6 million copies in two weeks.","w":"In 2011, after Killzone 3 shipped, Guerrilla asked its staff to pitch new IPs and got about 40 concepts. Game director Mathijs de Jonge called Horizon the most risky of them, and prototyped it with 10 people at first and 15 to 20 at most. The basics ran early. The rest of the company joined after Killzone Shadow Fall shipped; by June 2015 the full studio had been on it for 18 months. Guerrilla brought in writer John Gonzalez and outside open-world designers. It launched on February 28, 2017 and sold 2.6 million copies in 2 weeks.","r":"The whole studio joined a concept whose core combat already worked.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"10 to 20 people on the prototype; full studio only after Shadow Fall shipped.","nf":false,"l":"When changing genre, keep the new concept on a small prototype team until its core loop works, and ship the familiar game meanwhile.","i":"A 10-person prototype costs little while the rest of the studio earns on a known franchise, so the risky pitch can fail cheaply. It worked because Guerrilla's combat skills carried over; a studio with no transferable strengths gets less from the same plan.","a":"Does our core loop work on the prototype team before the studio joins?","q":"“One of the concepts that was most appealing in terms of different elements in it, funnily enough, was the most risky project there, Horizon.”","qa":"Mathijs de Jonge, game director at Guerrilla Games","st":"The Origins & World Of Horizon Zero Dawn","su":"https://gameinformer.com/b/features/archive/2015/06/26/the-origins-and-world-of-horizon-zero-dawn#:~:text=One%20of%20the%20concepts%20that,most%20risky%20project%20there%2C%20Horizon","sd":"Jun 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Moved a first-person shooter studio into its first open-world action RPG","s":"Guerrilla had to build quest design and open-world teams from scratch and deeply overhaul its shooter technology. The project ran about 7 years from the first pitches.","w":"Guerrilla had only made first-person shooters, mostly Killzone. Toward the end of Killzone 3, the studio started internal pitching for something new and chose an open-world action RPG. It had no quest design or open-world design expertise, so those teams were built from scratch and narrative work grew. The technology, built for shooters, needed a complete and deep overhaul, which became the Decima engine. Horizon Zero Dawn came out in 2017, about 7 years after pitching began. That year managing director Hermen Hulst said shooter skills such as tactical combat and precise controls carried over.","r":"New quest and open-world teams, plus a deep technology overhaul.","re":"Guerrilla rebranded the reworked engine as Decima and kept investing in it.","co":"Quest and open-world teams built from scratch; about 7 years from pitch to release.","nf":false,"l":"Before you switch genre, list every discipline the new genre needs that your studio lacks, and plan the hires and tool work for each.","i":"A new genre needs skills the studio has never hired for, and tools built for another kind of game. Hulst found that shooter skills in combat and controls carried over. Count what transfers as well as what is missing before you price the switch.","a":"Which disciplines does this genre need that we have never hired?","q":"“Those were all teams that we had to essentially build from scratch.”","qa":"Hermen Hulst, managing director and co-founder of Guerrilla","st":"A Conversation with Hermen Hulst","su":"https://www.interactive.org/Interviews/conversation_with_hermen_hulst.asp#:~:text=Those%20were%20all%20teams%20that,build%20from%20scratch","sd":"2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"hyenas","n":"HYENAS","dv":"Creative Assembly","y":"2023","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2023","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Greenlit an online FPS at an offline strategy studio during the early COVID boom","s":"Sega cancelled HYENAS in September 2023, wrote down the work in progress, cut headcount at Creative Assembly and expected about 14.3 billion yen of losses from the reform.","w":"In the early COVID period, strong results led Sega to push its studios into areas they had not tried. Creative Assembly, good at offline strategy games, took on HYENAS, an online FPS. Sega revealed it on 22 June 2022 for a 2023 release and ran a public alpha. On 28 September 2023 Sega Sammy announced: HYENAS and some unannounced titles cancelled, work in progress written down, fixed costs cut at Creative Assembly, about 14.3 billion yen of losses expected. On 8 November 2023 CEO Haruki Satomi called the game good but not good enough for long-term online play.","r":"Cancelled after a public alpha; the studio cut staff and offices.","re":"Sega refocused each studio on its specialty genres.","co":"About 14.3 billion yen of expected losses, including other cancelled titles and restructuring.","nf":false,"l":"Greenlight a genre the studio has shipped before, or gate the genre change with its own prototype milestone before full funding.","i":"A competitive online shooter needs a quality bar the studio had never had to hit, and the boom-time budget that funded the attempt vanished when European sales slowed. Sega also cites inflation and wages, so the genre mismatch is one cause among several.","a":"Has this studio shipped a game in this genre before?","q":"“However, although the game itself was good, we decided to cancel the development of HYENAS because we did not think it would reach a quality that would satisfy our users when we considered whether we could really operate this as a competitive online game for a long period of time.”","qa":"Haruki Satomi, President and Group CEO of Sega Sammy Holdings","st":"Q2 for the Fiscal Year Ending March 2024 Results Presentation","su":"https://www.segasammy.co.jp/cms/wp-content/uploads/pdf/en/ir/20231108_q2_presentation_with_scripts_e.pdf#:~:text=However%2C%20although%20the%20game%20itself,for%20a%20long%20period%20of%20time","sd":"Nov 2023","sy":"Company report","w8":4}]},{"k":"hyperlightdrifter","n":"Hyper Light Drifter","dv":"Heart Machine","y":"2016","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/257850/","ls":"Steam","cv":"hyperlightdrifter.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Added PS4, Vita and Wii U versions as Kickstarter stretch goals","s":"The Wii U port stalled in a contract dispute between the platform holder and the engine developer. In 2016 Heart Machine cancelled the Wii U and Vita versions and offered refunds.","w":"Heart Machine's Kickstarter closed in October 2013 with $645,158 against a $27,000 goal. It hit 19 stretch goals, which added PS4, PS Vita and Wii U to the planned Windows, Mac and Linux versions. In February 2016 the team told backers the Wii U version was in limbo, over contract issues between the platform holder and the engine developer. The PC game shipped on 31 March 2016. In September 2016 Alx Preston cancelled the Wii U and Vita versions, citing his health and the GameMaker engine, and offered refunds or other platforms.","r":"Wii U and Vita versions were cancelled 3 years after being promised.","re":"Heart Machine offered full refunds or PC, PS4 and Xbox One copies.","co":"Two promised console versions cancelled, with refunds offered.","nf":true,"l":"Before you promise a platform, run a test build on it with your engine and confirm the engine is licensed there.","i":"A stretch goal promises a port before anyone has tried one, so it rests on the engine vendor's roadmap and deals. When that deal broke down, the team could not fix it alone. Preston's health problems also played a part.","a":"Have we run a test build on every platform we plan to promise?","q":"“We pushed the engine to do things it was never meant to do.”","qa":"Alx Preston, creator of Hyper Light Drifter at Heart Machine","st":"Hyper Light Drifter Wii U, Vita versions cancelled","su":"https://hardcoregamer.com/news/hyper-light-drifter-wii-u-vita-versions-cancelled/225003/#:~:text=We%20pushed%20the%20engine%20to,never%20meant%20to%20do","sd":"Sep 2016","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"hytale","n":"Hytale","dv":"Hypixel Studios","y":"2026","sc":"AA","se":"Early Access 2026","lk":"https://store.hytale.com","ls":"Website","cv":"hytale.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Rebuilt the client and server engine in C++ to reach more platforms","s":"The 2023 launch window was lost at once. Three years later the new engine was still far behind on gameplay, and development ended in June 2025.","w":"In July 2022, after months of research, the Hytale team announced it would rebuild the client and server in C++. The goals were more platforms, better performance and easier patching. The same post said the game would miss 2023, its earliest launch window. On 23 June 2025 Hypixel Studios ended development and began winding down. CEO Aaron Donaghey said that even after a major engine reboot, Hytale still wasn't far enough along. In November 2025 founder Simon Collins-Laflamme wrote that the C++ engine, about 3 years in, was significantly behind in gameplay and needed about 2 more years.","r":"Three years of engine work left gameplay significantly behind the old engine.","re":"Hypixel Studios ended development and began winding down in June 2025.","co":"About 3 years on the new engine; 2 more years estimated to early access.","nf":false,"l":"Before rewriting the engine for future platforms, price the rewrite in years without new gameplay and compare it with shipping on the current engine.","i":"An engine rewrite sends the team back to rebuilding what already worked, and every new feature waits for the foundation. Here the old engine was playable; a rewrite makes more sense when the current tech cannot ship at all.","a":"How many months without new gameplay does this rewrite cost us?","q":"“These were difficult facts to come to terms with, and the decision to rebuild the engine followed months of painstaking research and soul-searching on the part of the team.”","qa":"Hytale Team, Hypixel Studios","st":"Summer 2022 Development Update","su":"https://hytale.com/news/2022/7/summer-2022-development-update#:~:text=These%20were%20difficult%20facts%20to,on%20the%20part%20of%20the%20team","sd":"Jul 2022","sy":"Company statement","w8":5},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Dropped the 3-year-old C++ engine and returned to the playable legacy engine","s":"Hytale reached Early Access on Windows 8 weeks after the founders bought it back, instead of the 2 years the new engine still needed.","w":"Hytale's development under Riot ended in June 2025. On 17 November 2025, founders Simon Collins-Laflamme and Philippe Touchette announced they had bought the game back and rehired more than 30 developers. They found two engines: the C#/Java legacy engine from the trailer, and a C++ engine about 3 years in that needed an estimated 2 more years before early access. They abandoned the C++ engine, made Windows the launch platform and promised years of early access. Hytale entered Early Access on 13 January 2026 with a $19.99 Starter Edition.","r":"Early Access arrived about 8 weeks after the buyback.","re":"The team planned to make the legacy engine cross-platform later, with no rush.","co":"About 3 years of C++ engine work dropped; other platforms pushed much later.","nf":false,"l":"When new tech is years from playable, ship on the old playable tech and move to new platforms later.","i":"Dropping sunk engine work is hard, but a playable build lets content, modding and player feedback start now. The trade holds when players accept one platform first; a console-first plan would not have this exit.","a":"Could we ship sooner on the tech that already plays?","q":"“It lets us build a game rather than an engine.”","qa":"Simon Collins-Laflamme, founder of Hypixel","st":"HYTALE IS SAVED!","su":"https://hytale.com/news/2025/11/hytale-is-saved#:~:text=It%20lets%20us%20build%20a,rather%20than%20an%20engine","sd":"Nov 2025","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"ico","n":"Ico","dv":"Team Ico","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Began Ico on the PS1 while only the director knew how the game fit together","s":"Bugs were hard to find and the hardware could not do key things. The team moved to PS2, threw away about 2 years of work and redid the art it hoped to reuse.","w":"Fumito Ueda pitched Ico with a 3-minute CG movie, made before he knew the limits of game hardware. About 5 people began building it on the original PlayStation. Ueda had the big picture in his head; the programmers did not know how it fit together, so bugs were hard to find. At a presentation deadline only the visuals were ready, and some problems could not be solved on PS1. Development moved to PS2, throwing away about 2 years of work. Ueda hoped to reuse the high-detail original art, but it all had to be redone. Ico came out in 2001.","r":"Development restarted on PS2 and the art was redone.","re":"A programmer who knew PS2 graphics hardware joined, and programming became more systematic.","co":"About 2 years of PS1 work thrown away; art redone for PS2.","nf":false,"l":"Before full development, get the core behavior running on the target hardware, not just the visuals.","i":"Ico started from a CG movie made without hardware limits in mind, so the look was proven first and the behavior last. Programmers who cannot see how the design fits together cannot tell early what the hardware will refuse.","a":"Does our core behavior run on the target hardware yet, or only the visuals?","q":"“I had the big picture already in my head, so I would tell them it would all work out, but when the project began they really had no idea how it was all supposed to fit together.”","qa":"Fumito Ueda, director of Ico at Sony Computer Entertainment","st":"Fumito Ueda – 2005 Developer Interview","su":"https://shmuplations.com/ueda/#:~:text=I%20had%20the%20big%20picture,supposed%20to%20fit%20together","sd":"2005, reprinted Dec 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"immortalsofaveum","n":"Immortals of Aveum","dv":"Ascendant Studios","y":"2023","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2009100/","ls":"Steam","cv":"immortalsofaveum.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Founded a studio to build one AAA-scope single-player shooter on a new IP","s":"The game missed its sales targets in a crowded August 2023 window, and about 45% of the 80 to 100 staff were laid off 3 weeks after launch.","w":"Bret Robbins founded Ascendant Studios in 2018 to make one game, a first-person magic shooter from his own 60-page design document. The team spent its first year unsure what it was making, then focused on shooting fundamentals; the combat demo brought EA in as publisher. Robbins said Unreal Engine 5 let a modest team build it below a normal AAA budget. It shipped on 22 August 2023 into a crowded window. On 14 September Robbins announced layoffs of about 45% of staff. In November he said a new IP from an unknown studio could not cut through the noise.","r":"Sales missed targets; about 45% of staff laid off 3 weeks after launch.","re":"Ran a half-price sale with a new update and helped laid-off staff find jobs.","co":"45% of 80 to 100 staff laid off; former employee put total cost near $125M.","nf":false,"l":"Before you found a studio around one new IP, check that the sales your cost base needs are realistic for an unknown team's debut.","i":"An unknown studio's first new IP has no audience waiting, so a cost base that needs AAA-level sales turns a crowded window into layoffs. Robbins blamed timing; a former employee told IGN the game was overscoped. A known IP or smaller budget changes the maths.","a":"How many copies must our debut sell, and why would buyers find it?","q":"“And it’s hard when you’re a new IP, especially from a new studio that no one had heard of, to cut through that noise.”","qa":"Bret Robbins, founder and CEO of Ascendant Studios","st":"Immortals of Aveum – Brett Robbins on Layoffs, Echollector Update & UE5 Development | XEP Interview","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o0mCqv4qX1g&t=778s","sd":"Nov 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4,"qu":"https://www.videogameschronicle.com/news/immortals-of-aveum-studio-layoffs-were-down-to-poor-sales-in-a-crowded-market-ceo-says/"}]},{"k":"indycargameandmotorsportgameslicensedracingslate","n":"INDYCAR game (Motorsport Games, unreleased)","dv":"Motorsport Games","y":"2023","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2023","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Signed an INDYCAR licence with minimum guarantees and announced a 2023 debut title","s":"No INDYCAR game shipped. In October 2023 the company suspended the title until it could find funding and capable resources, and on 8 November INDYCAR terminated the licence and claimed $2.9M in liquidating damages.","w":"On 13 July 2021 Motorsport Games licensed the INDYCAR SERIES: an annual development fee for 2021 and 2022, then royalties with annual minimum guarantees. The press release promised a debut title in 2023 on Xbox, PlayStation and PC. In early 2022 the company moved several releases later and listed INDYCAR for 2023 or 2024. Its 2022 annual report carried a going-concern warning. On 29 October 2023 it closed its Australian studio and suspended the INDYCAR title until it could secure funding and capable resources. On 8 November INDYCAR terminated both agreements, citing the missing US game and esports events.","r":"The INDYCAR game was cancelled by licence loss, with a $2.9M damages claim.","re":"Kept $2.9M of licence liability on the books and explored further asset sales.","co":"$2.9M damages claim; $3.6M licence impairment in 2023; development fees for 2021 and 2022.","nf":false,"l":"Do not sign minimum guarantees or announce a launch year until the production budget for that title is financed.","i":"Minimum guarantees and an announced launch year run on the licensor's clock, not the studio's. When cash tightens, the unbuilt title is the first cut, and the licensor can then terminate and claim the guarantees. It bites hardest when one franchise carries the company.","a":"Is the budget for every licensed title we have announced already financed?","q":"“As a consequence of the closure of the Australia development studio, the Company determined it was necessary to suspend the development of its previously planned INDYCAR title until such time that it is able to secure additional funding and capable resources to be able to deliver a high-quality INDYCAR video game title.”","qa":"Motorsport Games Inc., company filing (Form 10-Q)","st":"Form 10-Q for the quarterly period ended September 30, 2023 (Motorsport Games Inc.)","su":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1821175/000149315223039752/form10-q.htm#:~:text=As%20a%20consequence%20of%20the%20closure,INDYCAR%20video%20game%20title","sd":"Nov 2023","sy":"Company report","w8":4}]},{"k":"infamous","n":"inFamous","dv":"Sucker Punch Productions","y":"2009","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Started an open-world game with no clear core and prototyped scattered scenarios","s":"Nearly a year in, the combat game still had no weapon fire and no enemies that shot back. Sucker Punch lost months of work to ideas it later discarded.","w":"After Sly 3, Sucker Punch began inFamous, its first realistic-looking game, sure only of a superhero, a city and a T rating. The team described it as Animal Crossing, the Sims and Grand Theft Auto combined. It prototyped many small gameplay scenarios, expecting a vision to emerge, and got a set of unrelated prototypes. Nearly a year in, the player still could not fire a weapon. The team then settled on an action game, and basic gameplay moved fast. The game shipped in 2009, and Chris Zimmerman's October 2009 postmortem put the cost at months and months of work.","r":"Months of work went into ideas the team later threw away.","re":"The team chose action, built basic combat, and shelved most scenarios.","co":"About a year without a clear concept; months and months of work lost.","nf":false,"l":"Write the core of the game in one sentence and make it playable before prototyping side scenarios.","i":"Small prototypes test ideas, but they do not add up to a game without a core to judge them against. Once the team named the genre, the same prototype, test and iterate cycle moved fast.","a":"Can each of us state the game's core in one sentence?","q":"“For the first year of the project, we really didn’t have much of an idea of what kind of game we were building.”","qa":"Chris Zimmerman, co-founder of Sucker Punch Productions","st":"Postmortem: Behind The Making Of Sucker Punch's Infamous","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/postmortem-behind-the-making-of-sucker-punch-s-i-infamous-i-#:~:text=For%20the%20first%20year%20of,game%20we%20were%20building","sd":"Oct 2009","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"infinityblade","n":"Infinity Blade","dv":"Chair Entertainment","y":"2010","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Picked a one-finger sword-duel game from concepts pre-scoped for six people","s":"Chair started building within days of Epic's call and shipped in about five months. Within 24 hours of release the game was number one on every chart.","w":"Before Epic bought it, Chair had its six people pitch concepts every day for two weeks, keeping only games they could build in under a year. That left 20 to 25 one-sheets. In late June 2010 Epic asked for an iOS game to show off its mobile engine by November or December. Chair applied its Pocket Pillars: one finger, device-specific design, truly skill-based. By the next Monday it had chosen sword fighting, and the animator started. The game came out on December 9 and was number one on every chart within 24 hours. Mustard told it at GDC 2011.","r":"A five-month build that landed inside Epic's deadline.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"About 5 months; 12 core developers plus 10 Epic Games China staff briefly.","nf":false,"l":"Keep a backlog of concepts scoped to your real team and a fixed time, and pick from it when a window opens.","i":"The scoping was done before the deadline existed, so the five months went into building, not choosing. The filter only works if it matches the team that will build the game; Chair also had Epic's engine team and extra staff on call.","a":"Which of our concepts could this team ship in under a year?","q":"“This proved to be one of the most valuable game exercises we've ever done in the history of Chair.”","qa":"Donald Mustard, creative director of Chair Entertainment","st":"Infinity Blade in finite time: How ChAIR made its iOS hit in five months","su":"https://www.engadget.com/2011-03-02-infinity-blade-in-finite-time-how-chair-made-its-ios-hit-in-fiv.html#:~:text=This%20proved%20to%20be%20one,the%20history%20of%20Chair","sd":"Mar 2011","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Staffing","t":"Scheduled borrowed staff from Epic Games China into the plan from the start","s":"Chair's small core team could pull in extra people for a month or two at planned points. Donald Mustard said it gave the studio the advantages of a small team and a big team.","w":"In late June 2010 Epic asked Chair, its small Salt Lake City studio, for an iOS game by the end of the year. On Shadow Complex, Chair had borrowed Epic staff for a few days when time ran short. On Infinity Blade it planned that help from the start and scheduled it, for example 10 people from Epic Games China for a month or two. The game came out on 9 December 2010, about 5 months after the call. In April 2011 the team said borrowed staff were now a regular part of its production process.","r":"The small core team could add people for set tasks at planned times.","re":"Chair made borrowed staff a regular part of its production process.","co":"Example given: 10 Epic Games China staff for a month or two.","nf":false,"l":"When you can borrow staff from a sister team, book their weeks into the schedule at the start, not when you fall behind.","i":"Help booked in advance lets the lending team plan around it; help asked for in a panic does not. The lesson needs a parent or partner with people to lend: Epic staff went back to Gears 3 afterward.","a":"Have we booked the borrowed people we will need, by week?","q":"“So we were able to go, \"We have these 10 people in Epic Games China that we could swing in for a month or two and do this,\" and that's now definitely part of our regular production process.”","qa":"Donald Mustard, creative director of Chair Entertainment","st":"Infinity Blade: A New Era Of Games","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/-i-infinity-blade-i-a-new-era-of-games#:~:text=So%20we%20were%20able%20to%20go,part%20of%20our%20regular%20production%20process","sd":"Apr 2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"inscryption","n":"Inscryption","dv":"Daniel Mullins Games","y":"2021","sc":"Solo","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1092790/","ls":"Steam","cv":"inscryption.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Let a 48-hour jam entry prove the card loop before planning a multi-year game","s":"The sacrifice mechanic, tug-of-war win condition and lane combat were set in the jam with little planning. The 10-minute entry grew into a 14-hour game released in October 2021.","w":"Daniel Mullins built Sacrifices Must Be Made in a 48-hour Ludum Dare jam, with the theme as its title and, in his words, not much planning behind the core rules: a card game about sacrificing, a tug-of-war victory condition and lane combat. The entry did well and the response convinced him it could be his next multi-year project. He added persistent progression through cabin puzzles and a roguelike structure, kept the low resolution and dropped card text except names. Inscryption shipped on 19 October 2021. At GDC in March 2022 he described a 10-minute entry grown into a 14-hour game.","r":"Core rules were fixed in 48 hours and never needed a redesign.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"48 hours for the prototype; multi-year solo development, no figures given.","nf":false,"l":"Let a 48-hour jam prove the loop before you plan the years, and keep the jam rules as the core.","i":"A jam forces a playable loop and an outside verdict before any money is spent, so the years that follow build on something tested. Mullins does not claim the jam saved time or money; the gain is a loop that held for 14 hours.","a":"Has an outside audience played our core loop before we planned the years?","q":"“To be honest, not much planning went into the core rules of the game.”","qa":"Daniel Mullins, developer, Daniel Mullins Games","st":"How a game jam on 'sacrifices' became Inscryption","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/how-game-jam-sacrifices-became-inscryption#:~:text=To%20be%20honest%2C%20not%20much,rules%20of%20the%20game.","sd":"Mar 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"intothebreach","n":"Into the Breach","dv":"Subset Games","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/590380/","ls":"Steam","cv":"intothebreach.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Designed the strategy layer in parallel with combat, before combat's rules were settled","s":"A large world map with squads, repairs and research took six months to a year and was cut. Davis says faster cutting would have shortened a four-year schedule.","w":"After FTL shipped in 18 months, Matthew Davis and Justin Ma started Into the Breach with the security FTL provided. They designed the combat and the strategy layer at the same time. The strategy side began as a large world of nodes with several mech squads, pilots, repairs and research, heading toward a 20-hour game. They cut it after six months at least, maybe a year, and tried smaller versions. Years in, they considered quitting. They then cut the layer down to choosing between missions, and playtesters found it fun. The game shipped in February 2018 after about four years.","r":"Six months to a year of strategy-layer work was thrown away.","re":"They cut the layer down to one choice: which mission to play next.","co":"6 to 12 months of strategy work cut; about 4 years total.","nf":false,"l":"Settle the core combat rules before building the meta layer, and cut any system that fights them.","i":"A meta layer designed beside an unsettled core gets built for rules that later change, so it fails once the core lands. Davis also blames the time FTL's success bought them: with no pressure, the two of them cut too slowly.","a":"Which meta systems are we building before the core rules are settled?","q":"“In hindsight I think that all of the strategy layer stuff was failing because we were ignoring the constraints that were imposed by the combat.”","qa":"Matthew Davis, programmer and designer at Subset Games","st":"Into the Breach Design Postmortem","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s_I07Iq_2XM&t=2249s","sd":"2019","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4}]},{"k":"jakanddaxtertheprecursorlegacy","n":"Jak and Daxter: The Precursor Legacy","dv":"Naughty Dog","y":"2001","sc":"AAA","se":"Port 2024","lk":"https://store.playstation.com/en-us/concept/10010137","ls":"PlayStation","cv":"jakanddaxtertheprecursorlegacy.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Dropped the Crash formula for one connected no-load world and a custom Lisp language","s":"The compiler took over a year from a single programmer, gameplay code came late, and level estimates borrowed from Crash ran far too short. The game still shipped after 3 years of work.","w":"By the end of 1998, Naughty Dog decided to leave the Crash formula and build one connected world with no load screens for the unreleased PlayStation 2. A single programmer wrote GOAL, a custom Lisp language and compiler. The compiler took over a year, and every fix had to come from him. Programmers were so busy on world technology that gameplay code came late, so designers built levels without being able to test them. Level estimates taken from Crash proved far too short. After 3 years, 2 of them full production, the game shipped in 2001.","r":"Gameplay code and artist tools came late; programming was the bottleneck.","re":"Programmers were moved off technology tasks onto gameplay; tools got GUIs for the next project.","co":"3 years of work, 2 in full production; over a year on the compiler.","nf":false,"l":"When you bet on custom tech, schedule gameplay code and artist tools as early milestones, not as leftovers after the engine works.","i":"The world streaming and the compiler ate the programmers' time, so the fun could not be tested until the technology worked. A veteran team with a flexible macro-level schedule absorbed it; a team without Crash-level experience would have had less margin.","a":"When can designers first play a level with real gameplay code?","q":"“Because we were so busy creating the technology for our seamless world, we didn't have time to work on gameplay code until fairly late in the project.”","qa":"Stephen White, programming director at Naughty Dog","st":"Postmortem: Naughty Dog's Jak and Daxter: the Precursor Legacy","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-naughty-dog-s-i-jak-and-daxter-the-precursor-legacy-i-#:~:text=Because%20we%20were%20so%20busy,until%20fairly%20late%20in%20the%20project","sd":"Jul 2002","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"johnwoopresentsstranglehold","n":"John Woo Presents Stranglehold","dv":"Midway Games","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Adopted the still-unfinished Unreal Engine 3 as Midway's company-wide shared engine","s":"The engine gave art a big head start, but merging Epic's code drops every three months took the whole team down for weeks at a time.","w":"Midway started Stranglehold for PS3, Xbox 360 and PC while that hardware was at alpha or did not exist yet. Midway licensed Unreal Engine 3, still in development, as a shared base for all its studios, and Stranglehold led that push. UE3 was mostly a renderer and toolset, so the team prototyped core gameplay on its finished Psi-Ops engine. About every three months the team merged Epic's new code, which at times stopped everyone for weeks. The game shipped in September 2007 after 34 months.","r":"Engine merges cost the project about 2 months of team time.","re":"The team added Havok and Ai Implant where the stock engine fell short.","co":"About 2 months lost to quarterly engine merges.","nf":false,"l":"If you build on an engine still in development, merge its updates less often and keep content teams working during merges.","i":"Each merge of upstream code broke the team's own changes, and content creators waited while programmers fixed them. Eddy still called UE3 a win for its head start, so the lesson is about merge policy, not about avoiding the engine.","a":"How often do we merge engine updates, and who stops working then?","q":"“We lost probably two months of time on the project due to completing these merges.”","qa":"Brian Eddy, creative director on Stranglehold at Midway","st":"Postmortem: Midway's John Woo Presents Stranglehold","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_January_2008.pdf#:~:text=We%20lost%20probably%20two%20months,due%20to%20completing%20these%20merges","sd":"Jan 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4,"sh":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_January_2008.pdf#page=39"},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Cut the open-world plan to a linear game but kept the full feature list","s":"A year in, the team knew the features could not all reach AAA quality. Vehicles were cut only at the two-year mark, and the last six months were crunch.","w":"Stranglehold started as a free-roaming GTA-style game, Midway's first for next-gen consoles, on an Unreal Engine 3 still in development. After about six months the team convinced management to cut it back to a more linear sandbox. The feature list stayed: single player, world interactions, four special moves, massive destruction, drivable vehicles with combat and extensive multiplayer. A year in, they clearly could not all reach AAA quality. At the two-year mark management set a hard end date, and vehicles were cut. The last six months were crunch. In January 2008, Brian Eddy said the cut came a year late.","r":"Vehicles were cut a year late, at the cost of mission variety and polish.","re":"Management set a hard end date, and the team cut drivable vehicles.","co":"Vehicles cut a year late; last 6 months of the project in crunch.","nf":false,"l":"At the end of pre-production, set a hard end date and cut the features that will not fit it.","i":"Without a hard end date, no feature ever has to lose, so all of them stay half-finished together. The new engine and unreleased hardware made any early date uncertain, which is why the date was left open.","a":"Which feature would we cut first if the end date were fixed today?","q":"“Ultimately cutting vehicles helped get the game shipped, but if we had made that decision a year earlier we could have put more time into mission variety and overall polish.”","qa":"Brian Eddy, creative director of John Woo Presents Stranglehold at Midway","st":"Postmortem: Midway's John Woo Presents Stranglehold","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_January_2008.pdf#:~:text=Ultimately%20cutting%20vehicles%20helped%20get,mission%20variety%20and%20overall%20polish","sd":"Jan 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4,"sh":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_January_2008.pdf#page=40"}]},{"k":"journey","n":"Journey","dv":"thatgamecompany","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/638230/","ls":"Steam","cv":"journey.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built the whole game around one emotion and stripped out lobby, names, chat and power","s":"The emotion could only be tested with the full game. The first-year version failed, Sony allowed an extra year, staff went unpaid for half a year and the studio was bankrupt at launch.","w":"thatgamecompany starts with an emotion and works everything around it, so Journey removed the HUD, lobby, names, chat and any power over other players. Shared resources and push-pull physics were tried and dropped. After the first year the game still did not deliver: testers said the end was terrible. Jenova Chen could ship it or spend another year on the ending; Sony allowed the extra year. The summit went from rails to free movement. The two-hour game took three years. Staff were unpaid for the last half year, and the company was bankrupt at launch in March 2012.","r":"An extra year of development for a two-hour game that took three years.","re":"Sony allowed the extra year; the team rebuilt the ending, mountain and summit.","co":"About 1 extra year; staff unpaid for the last half year; all savings spent.","nf":false,"l":"If your core value is an emotion the player feels only in the full arc, budget a whole extra year to fix the ending.","i":"An emotional arc is tested end to end, so it fails late and whole, unlike a combat loop you can tune early. It paid off for a 2-hour game with a patient publisher; a longer game or a fixed contract would not survive it.","a":"If the ending fails in year one, can we afford another year?","q":"“A lot of people in our studio were not paid in the last half year, and we paid out all our savings.”","qa":"Jenova Chen, co-founder of thatgamecompany","st":"The journey to create Journey -- the quest for emotion","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/the-journey-to-create-i-journey-i----the-quest-for-emotion#:~:text=A%20lot%20of%20people%20in,paid%20out%20all%20our%20savings","sd":"Feb 2013","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4}]},{"k":"judas","n":"Judas","dv":"Ghost Story Games","y":"","sc":"AA","se":"Unreleased 2026","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/388860/","ls":"Steam","cv":"judas.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Set 11 Irrational veterans to build a triple-A narrative Lego game by fall 2017","s":"Bloomberg reported that the scope grew and grew and half the founders left; the fall 2017 target passed with no release. Levine later said the years went into working out the modular narrative system and how to write for it.","w":"Ken Levine founded Ghost Story Games in 2014 with 11 former Irrational developers to build what he called narrative Lego. Bloomberg reported in January 2022 that the team worked to a fall 2017 deadline, that Levine wanted a triple-A game with a budget team size, and that the scope just grew and grew. By then half of the 11 had left and the game was about 2 years away. In March 2024 Levine said he had fallen in love with the world and it kind of scaled; in May 2026 he said the years went into the narrative system.","r":"Fall 2017 target missed; still unreleased in 2026 after 12 years.","re":"Levine says a system is now in place to build the next game faster.","co":"12 years so far; half of the 11 founders left; no release.","nf":false,"l":"Before promising a date, prove the untested core system with a prototype and size the team to the ambition, not the budget.","i":"A system nobody has built before has no schedule you can trust, and the reported blank cheque and free rein meant nothing forced the scope to stop. Levine says the years went into that system. With a proven mechanic, patience is a strength.","a":"Which core system is still unproven, and what date depends on it?","q":"“I guess it kind of scaled.”","qa":"Ken Levine, creative director and founder of Ghost Story Games","st":"None Of This Is New","su":"https://aftermath.site/ken-levine-judas-interview/#:~:text=I%20guess%20it,kind%20of%20scaled","sd":"Mar 2024","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"katamaridamacy","n":"Katamari Damacy","dv":"Namco","y":"2004","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2018","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/848350/","ls":"Steam","cv":"katamaridamacy.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Deleted absorbed objects from memory to keep the katamari's CPU load down","s":"Objects could not come back when a katamari crumbled, so the planned shrinking feature shipped only as minor crumbling that Takahashi called vague and not very effective.","w":"Keita Takahashi built a Katamari Damacy prototype at Namco before full development, then recruited the team. To keep CPU load down, objects the katamari rolled up were absorbed into it, and once out of sight their memory was deleted. Takahashi also wanted a crumbling feature: bumping into something large would knock items off, so a 300m katamari could shrink to 2m. Deleted objects had no clean way to reappear and vanish again. The team implemented only minor crumbling. In the December 2004 Game Developer postmortem, Takahashi listed it under what went wrong.","r":"Full shrinking was cut; only minor crumbling shipped.","re":"The team kept minor crumbling and dropped the full feature.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you lock a memory shortcut, check whether any planned feature needs the deleted data back.","i":"Deleting objects saved CPU because growing only ever needed one direction. Shrinking needed the reverse, and the data was gone. The postmortem does not say when the choice was made or whether crumbling was already planned then.","a":"Does any planned feature need data we plan to delete?","q":"“As a result, the whole crumbling feature became vague and wasn't very effective in the end at all.”","qa":"Keita Takahashi, director of Katamari Damacy at Namco","st":"Postmortem: The singular design of Namco's Katamari Damacy (2004)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-the-singular-design-of-namco-s-katamari-damacy-2004-#:~:text=As%20a%20result%2C%20the%20whole,in%20the%20end%20at%20all","sd":"Dec 2004, reprinted Dec 2024","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"kentuckyroutezero","n":"Kentucky Route Zero","dv":"Cardboard Computer","y":"2013","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/231200/","ls":"Steam","cv":"kentuckyroutezero.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Set the game's tone and level of detail with a 2011 Kickstarter pitch video","s":"Co-founder Tamas Kemenczy said the bar he worked from proved too detailed and ambitious for the small team. Five acts and the interludes took until 2020, and Jake Elliott called the commitment at times really difficult financially.","w":"In January 2011 Jake Elliott and Tamas Kemenczy, a two-person studio, pitched Kentucky Route Zero on Kickstarter and raised $8,583 on a $6,500 goal. Kemenczy later said the video set the tone and detail he worked from, and was too ambitious for the team. Ben Babbitt joined in 2013 as the scope grew. Engadget reported in February 2016 that the studio had pledged one act every three months, finale due October 2014; Act 4 alone took 21 months. Act 5 arrived in 2020. Elliott said that once interested players owned the game, the studio still needed support to finish.","r":"Five acts took nine years, far past the pledged cadence, with money tight.","re":"The trio said their next project will not be episodic.","co":"$8,583 Kickstarter; 9 years from pitch to Act 5; at times really difficult financially.","nf":false,"l":"Size the detail bar in your pitch demo to the team you will have, because every later scene inherits it.","i":"The pitch video is the first unit of content, and everything after it has to match. A two-person team that sets the bar at its own best work pays for that in every scene. A studio that can hire as it goes has more room.","a":"Could our current team build every scene to the pitch video's standard?","q":"“That's where I started working from, which ended up being a little bit too detailed and ambitious for our small team.”","qa":"Tamas Kemenczy, co-founder and art director, Cardboard Computer","st":"\"It Feels Like We Made 10 Games:\" Kentucky Route Zero at the End of the Road","su":"https://www.usgamer.net/articles/kentucky-route-zeros-creators-at-the-end-of-the-road-it-feels-like-we-made-10-games#:~:text=That%27s%20where%20I%20started%20working,for%20our%20small%20team","sd":"2020","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"killzone2","n":"Killzone 2","dv":"Guerrilla Games","y":"2009","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"External commitments","t":"Showed a pre-rendered internal benchmark as the PlayStation 3 trailer at E3 2005","s":"The trailer became a public promise before the rookie team had an engine. Everything was built from scratch and the game took nearly four years, shipping in February 2009.","w":"Guerrilla had started Killzone 2 on PlayStation 2 and cancelled it. With Sony it made a trailer of what the franchise could do on PlayStation 3, meant as an internal benchmark for the team. Because it was so well liked, Sony chose to show the pre-rendered video at the PS3 presentation at E3 2005, and Hermen Hulst agreed. The team that started in 2005 was still very rookie; a brand new engine had to be built from scratch, and there was no way back. Development ran about 3 years and 10 months until release in February 2009.","r":"No way back: a new engine and nearly four years to match the trailer.","re":"Sony kept funding and believing; Killzone 3 was then put on a two-year cycle.","co":"About 3 years 10 months of development; a brand new engine built from scratch.","nf":false,"l":"Before a target render goes public, confirm the team has the engine and experience to hit it, or keep it internal.","i":"A published benchmark fixes the quality bar before anyone knows what it costs, so the schedule follows the trailer instead of the team. Guerrilla had a publisher that funded the overrun; without that patience the same promise ends in a visible downgrade.","a":"Can the current team and engine hit what the trailer shows?","q":"“We had to deliver what we had communicated externally.”","qa":"Hermen Hulst, Guerrilla Games","st":"The Playstation and Me: Hermen Hulst, Part 2","su":"https://techland.time.com/2010/09/24/47286/2/#:~:text=We%20had%20to%20deliver%20what,communicated%20externally","sd":"Sep 2010","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"kingdomcomedeliverance","n":"Kingdom Come: Deliverance","dv":"Warhorse Studios","y":"2018","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/379430/","ls":"Steam","cv":"kingdomcomedeliverance.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Ran a Kickstarter to prove demand to the investor after publishers said no","s":"The campaign showed players wanted the game, and Warhorse raised investor money to start full production on its own. The game shipped in February 2018.","w":"Publishers turned down Warhorse's prototype, and the studio's investor stopped putting money in. In February 2014 creative director Daniel Vávra said the Kickstarter had to prove the game was worth investing in, and without it there would be no game. In July 2015 he said the campaign showed everybody wanted to play it, and new investor money funded full production with about 70 people, aiming for 2016. In July 2016 CzechCrunch reported over 36 million CZK from Kickstarter and pre-orders and an expected budget of 100 to 110 million CZK. The game shipped on 13 February 2018.","r":"Investor money for full in-house production followed the campaign.","re":"Warhorse grew to about 70 people and built the game itself.","co":"Expected budget of 100 to 110 million CZK, per CzechCrunch in 2016.","nf":false,"l":"If publishers reject your prototype, run a public demand test such as crowdfunding before asking investors for the full budget.","i":"A funded campaign is evidence an investor can check: players paid for a game that did not exist yet. It proves demand for the pitch, not that the team can ship on time. Vávra's 2016 target became February 2018.","a":"What public proof of demand can we show our investor before asking for more?","q":"“His money is not our money and we had to prove to him that the game is worth investing in.”","qa":"Daniel Vávra, creative director at Warhorse Studios","st":"Dan Vávra Discusses Kingdom Come: Deliverance","su":"https://cliqist.com/2014/02/07/dan-vavra-discusses-kingdom-come-deliverance/#:~:text=His%20money%20is%20not%20our,game%20is%20worth%20investing%20in","sd":"Feb 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"kingdomheartsiii","n":"Kingdom Hearts III","dv":"Square Enix","y":"2019","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2552450/","ls":"Steam","cv":"kingdomheartsiii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Switched from the in-house Luminous engine to Unreal Engine 4 a year in","s":"Part of the work had to be redone. Staff transfers also came later than the plan approved at the start, and the game shipped in January 2019.","w":"In July 2013 director Tetsuya Nomura told Famitsu the team was testing mainly on Square Enix's in-house Luminous engine, building above spec and porting to PS4 and Xbox One. In October 2014 he announced the move to Epic's Unreal Engine 4, which GameSpot placed about a year into development. In July 2017 Nomura said the switch was a company decision, and some time had to be rewound and started over. Planned staff transfers also came later than the plan headquarters had approved. The game shipped in January 2019.","r":"Some work was rewound and started again after the switch.","re":"The team rebuilt on Unreal Engine 4, with Epic's help.","co":"Work rewound and restarted after the engine switch.","nf":true,"l":"Settle the engine with company leadership before you build content, so a later switch does not rewind finished work.","i":"Content built for one engine's renderer and tools does not move cleanly to another, so a switch rewinds part of the work. Nomura said the delay was not a team problem: late staff transfers slowed the game too.","a":"Is our engine choice final, and does leadership agree?","q":"“Unfortunately there was a bit of time that needed to be rewinded and started over.”","qa":"Tetsuya Nomura, director of Kingdom Hearts III at Square Enix","st":"Kingdom Hearts 3 Director Tetsuya Nomura Explains What Took So Long","su":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/kingdom-hearts-3-director-tetsuya-nomura-explains-/1100-6451732/#:~:text=Unfortunately%20there%20was%20a%20bit,rewinded%20and%20started%20over","sd":"Jul 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"kingdomsandcastles","n":"Kingdoms and Castles","dv":"Lion Shield","y":"2017","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/569480/","ls":"Steam","cv":"kingdomsandcastles.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Set numeric audience-test bars before committing to build Kingdoms and Castles","s":"The concept page beat its bar three months in and exposed a Stonehearth look-alike problem early. Crowdfunding then sized the game, which peaked at number 3 on Steam.","w":"Lion Shield's Cannon Brawl took about twice as long with a similar team and found only a niche audience. For Kingdoms and Castles, Peter Angstadt and his partner set a 25-retweet bar for GIF tests, then a Steam Greenlight concept page goal: top 100 ideas and 50 comments. About three months in, the page passed. Comments compared it to Stonehearth, so they redid the few assets in low poly. Crowdfunding beat expectations and paid for simulation changes and a military system. Ten months later it launched and peaked at number 3 on Steam. Angstadt presented it at GDC 2019.","r":"The art style changed while few assets existed, so the redo was quick.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"About 1.5 years in 35-hour-week terms; Cannon Brawl took about twice as long.","nf":false,"l":"Before committing, write a numeric pass bar for each audience test, and go back to the drawing board if you miss it.","i":"A written bar stops the team from reading weak interest as encouragement. Lion Shield set its bars from comparable Greenlight pages and how those games later sold, so a bar is only as good as its comparables.","a":"What number would tell us to stop or rethink this concept?","q":"“We're using these success criteria as a way to be honest with ourselves and force us to course-correct early if the idea isn't resonating.”","qa":"Peter Angstadt, co-founder of Lion Shield","st":"Kingdoms and Castles Postmortem","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NpkLUoIgcXQ&t=345s","sd":"2019","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4}]},{"k":"kingdomsofamalurreckoning","n":"Kingdoms of Amalur: Reckoning","dv":"Big Huge Games","y":"2012","sc":"AAA","se":"Remaster 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/102500/","ls":"Steam","cv":"kingdomsofamalurreckoning.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Spent pre-production pitching for a publisher, then went straight into full production","s":"Budget and schedule were set before the team picked combat as its hook, so it could not plan the extra animators and designers. It also made too much game.","w":"Big Huge Games came to Reckoning after two false starts and a sale to 38 Studios. At the start, the team was in heavy pitch mode, chasing a publishing deal instead of preproduction outputs. Once EA Partners signed, it jumped into full production with a basic content scope but no clear feature set, hook, content density or dialogue system. Combat became the focus soon after, with budget and schedule already set. Tools trailed content, and late main-quest beats cost cinematics. The game shipped in February 2012, and both studios closed in May 2012, three months later.","r":"Production started with the feature set, hook and content density undefined.","re":"The team went all-in on combat and cut several planned cinematics.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before signing a publishing deal, define the feature set, the hook and the content density the budget must pay for.","i":"A budget set before the hook is known cannot fund what the hook needs, so too few people carry it. The studios closed after rocky sales, mismanagement and bad luck, so the closure has more causes than this one.","a":"Do we know our hook and content density before the budget locks?","q":"“Even though we had a lot of production time during the false starts before Reckoning, our preproduction time for Reckoning itself was entirely too short.”","qa":"Mike Fridley, executive producer, Big Huge Games","st":"Postmortem: Kingdoms of Amalur: Reckoning","su":"https://gamedeveloper.com/production/postmortem-i-kingdoms-of-amalur-reckoning-i-#:~:text=Even%20though%20we%20had%20a,itself%20was%20entirely%20too%20short","sd":"Apr 2012, reprinted Jul 2013","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"kirbyandtheforgottenland","n":"Kirby and the Forgotten Land","dv":"HAL Laboratory","y":"2022","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/kirby-and-the-forgotten-land-switch/","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"kirbyandtheforgottenland.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Approved Kirby's first full 3D platformer once the proposal solved its control problems","s":"Work began soon after. 3D stages needed more objects, lighting and shadows, so HAL built an automatic terrain-modeling pass that freed time for gameplay experiments.","w":"For a little over a decade HAL Laboratory could not release a mainline Kirby on a home console, and 3D concepts kept hitting walls. Smaller games such as Kirby's Blowout Blast in 2017 tested 3D ideas. Inhaling and jumping did not work when moved into 3D as they were. Tatsuya Kamiyama's proposal offered a fix for each 3D platforming problem, with specific control plans, and general director Shinya Kumazaki started the project shortly after. 3D areas meant more objects, lighting and shadows, so the team built an automatic terrain-modeling pass. The game launched on 25 March 2022.","r":"The project started with its main 3D control risks already answered.","re":"HAL built automatic terrain modeling to absorb the extra 3D stage work.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you greenlight a move to 3D, require a concrete fix for each known control problem, not a new setting.","i":"Earlier 3D attempts stalled on basic actions, so a proposal that answered each one removed the reason past projects failed. Approval did not cover content cost: 3D still meant more objects and lighting per area, and that needed its own tool.","a":"Does our proposal answer every known control problem with a concrete fix?","q":"“With a proposal that solid, I knew we'd be OK, so work on the project officially began shortly after that.”","qa":"Shinya Kumazaki, general director of the Kirby series at HAL Laboratory","st":"Ask the Developer Vol. 4: Kirby and the Forgotten Land — Part 1","su":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/whatsnew/ask-the-developer-vol-4-kirby-and-the-forgotten-land-part-1/#:~:text=With%20a%20proposal%20that%20solid,began%20shortly%20after%20that","sd":"Mar 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"kirbysreturntodreamland","n":"Kirby's Return to Dream Land","dv":"HAL Laboratory","y":"2011","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2011","lk":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/wii/sukj/index.html","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"kirbysreturntodreamland.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Built three new-concept Kirby games in a row without a release date and scrapped each","s":"Eleven years passed without a console Kirby. The fourth attempt got a fixed date, a 1.5-year limit and a single-player-first brief, and shipped in October 2011 on parts from the scrapped builds.","w":"Right after Kirby 64 in 2000, HAL started a GameCube Kirby built around 4-player simultaneous play; it was shown at E3, but single and multiplayer would not coexist. A second attempt put Kirby in a free-roaming 3D garden and missed the quality HAL wanted. A third, a playable storybook animation with new copy abilities, also ended unfinished. The release date stayed undecided and the platform moved to Wii. In March 2010 Nintendo told HAL the game would sell in August or September 2011 and asked for single-player first; director Shinya Kumazaki got 1.5 years. It released on 27 October 2011.","r":"Three unfinished builds and 11 years without a console Kirby.","re":"Nintendo fixed the date, asked for single-player first, and HAL reused the third build's parts.","co":"11 years from the first concept to release; 3 scrapped builds.","nf":false,"l":"Give every concept restart a fixed ship date and a single-player-first brief, or it becomes the next phantom build.","i":"Without a date, each build chased surprise and perfect feel until it stalled, and nobody had to ship or kill it. The fourth worked because 3 builds of movement and ability work already existed; without that backlog the same brief gives no 1.5-year game.","a":"What is the ship date for this restart, and who can kill it?","q":"“この３作をつくっては壊しながら、１１年間・・・やってきました。”","qa":"Shigefumi Kawase, producer at HAL Laboratory","st":"社長が訊く『星のカービィ Wii』","su":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/wii/interview/sukj/vol1/index.html#:~:text=%E3%81%93%E3%81%AE%EF%BC%93%E4%BD%9C%E3%82%92%E3%81%A4%E3%81%8F%E3%81%A3%E3%81%A6%E3%81%AF%E5%A3%8A%E3%81%97%E3%81%AA%E3%81%8C%E3%82%89,%E3%82%84%E3%81%A3%E3%81%A6%E3%81%8D%E3%81%BE%E3%81%97%E3%81%9F","sd":"2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Designed the first GameCube Kirby around four people playing at once","s":"Making one game work for both solo and four-player play proved hard, and the game never came out. The later Wii Kirby got four players only after a single-player base worked.","w":"Right after Kirby 64 came out in March 2000, HAL Laboratory began a new home console Kirby for GameCube. Its concept was four people playing at once, and screenshots appeared at E3. The game never came out and became the first of three lost Kirby games over 11 years. Producer Shigefumi Kawase later said this was when he learned how hard it is to make a game both multiplayer and single player. For Kirby's Adventure Wii, the Nintendo staff overseeing HAL asked for a single-player game first and brought in four players step by step.","r":"The four-player GameCube Kirby was never released.","re":"Kirby's Adventure Wii built single-player first, then added four players.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If one game must serve solo and four-player play, prove the solo game first, then add players step by step.","i":"Levels, camera and difficulty have to work for one player and for four at once, so the two modes pull the design apart. Nintendo's Yamagami could not pin down why the lost games died, so treat this as one cause, not the full story.","a":"Does our solo game play well before we add the other players?","q":"“That was when I learned how difficult it is to make a game that is both multiplayer and single player.”","qa":"Shigefumi Kawase, producer at HAL Laboratory","st":"Iwata Asks: Kirby's Adventure Wii, 1. The Three Lost Kirby Games","su":"https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Iwata-Asks/Iwata-Asks-Kirby-s-Adventure-Wii/Iwata-Asks-Kirby-s-Adventure-Wii/1-The-Three-Lost-Kirby-Games/1-The-Three-Lost-Kirby-Games-230159.html#:~:text=That%20was%20when%20I%20learned,both%20multiplayer%20and%20single%20player","sd":"2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Restarted console Kirby with a fixed 1.5-year deadline, reusing parts of three cancelled versions","s":"After 11 years and 3 scrapped versions, HAL shipped its first new mainline console Kirby in October 2011, about 19 months after the restart.","w":"After Kirby 64, HAL built three console Kirby versions: a 4-player GameCube game, a free-roaming 3D Kirby and a storybook-style Kirby with reworked copy abilities. None was finished. In March 2010 Shinya Kumazaki became director, with the half-built output of all three in front of him. He was told at the start that the deadline was 1.5 years. Yamagami, newly put on Kirby, stopped hunting for why earlier versions failed, chose to reuse the existing parts, fixed a sale in August or September 2011 and asked for single-player first. The game shipped in Japan on 27 October 2011.","r":"Shipped in October 2011, about 19 months after the restart.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"19 months from restart to release, after 11 years of cancelled attempts.","nf":false,"l":"After repeated failed concepts, restart with a fixed deadline, a narrower first goal and the best parts of earlier builds.","i":"Open-ended quality goals let each version restart the search for something new. A fixed date and single-player first gave the team a finish line. It worked because earlier builds left movement and parts the team already knew how to make.","a":"Do we have a fixed ship date the whole team believes in?","q":"“制作期限もはじめに「１年半で」と言われましたし。” (We were also told at the very start that the production deadline was one and a half years.)","qa":"Shinya Kumazaki, director at HAL Laboratory","st":"社長が訊く『星のカービィ Wii』","su":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/wii/interview/sukj/vol1/index.html#:~:text=%E5%88%B6%E4%BD%9C%E6%9C%9F%E9%99%90%E3%82%82%E3%81%AF%E3%81%98%E3%82%81%E3%81%AB,%E3%81%A8%E8%A8%80%E3%82%8F%E3%82%8C%E3%81%BE%E3%81%97%E3%81%9F%E3%81%97","sd":"Oct 2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":5}]},{"k":"knack","n":"Knack","dv":"Japan Studio","y":"2013","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned Knack as a small Crash-style game with Mark Cerny as part-time producer","s":"To work today the game had to be far bigger than Crash Bandicoot. Cerny ended up directing it, which sharply raised his hours while he also led PS4 hardware design.","w":"Mark Cerny had consulted part-time on Sony games whose teams grew past 100 people. For Knack at Japan Studio, he pitched a smaller title, Crash Bandicoot for the 21st century, and planned to stay a producer. The team found it needed a more complex game: Crash had 2 minutes 15 seconds of story and used 2 buttons. As the game grew, Cerny became director, while also serving as lead architect of the PS4. Knack shipped on time with the hardware, to mixed reviews. In December 2013 Cerny said a nostalgic game now needs 5 or 10 times as much.","r":"The game outgrew its small plan and Cerny became director.","re":"Cerny used Sony Santa Monica's structured production methods, and the game shipped on time.","co":"Cerny's estimate: a nostalgic game today needs 5 or 10 times as much.","nf":false,"l":"Size a nostalgia project against what players expect today in story and controls, not against the original game's scope.","i":"The original was small because its era was small: 2 buttons and a little over 2 minutes of story. Matching today's expectations grows the game, and every part-time role on it grows too. This holds for nostalgia projects, not for small games in general.","a":"Have we sized this game against today's players or the original's?","q":"“Now what happened is [laughs] I ended up being director rather than producer, and the game stopped being a very small title, both of which dramatically increased the amount of time I need to spend on the game.”","qa":"Mark Cerny, director of Knack and lead system architect of the PS4","st":"Making Knack: An interview with Mark Cerny","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/making-i-knack-i-an-interview-with-mark-cerny#:~:text=Now%20what%20happened%20is,to%20spend%20on%20the%20game","sd":"Dec 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"lawbreakers","n":"LawBreakers","dv":"Boss Key Productions","y":"2017","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Kept the hero-shooter direction after Overwatch's reveal, calling it counter-programming","s":"LawBreakers launched in August 2017 and never found enough players to fund live operations. Bleszinski later said he wished he had made big changes when Overwatch was announced.","w":"Cliff Bleszinski co-founded Boss Key in 2014 to make a wall-running, gravity-flipping arena shooter in about 18 months. Months in, the core play felt thin, and programmer Matt Fischman suggested character abilities. Abilities added depth and turned the game into a hero shooter. In November 2014 Blizzard revealed Overwatch, and Bleszinski told the team to aim for a grittier, Call of Duty style instead of changing course. The team grew from 15 to 20 people to about 75. LawBreakers launched in August 2017, launch-day player counts climbed slowly and levelled off, and Boss Key closed in May 2018.","r":"The team built a hero shooter in direct competition with Overwatch.","re":"After launch the team added modes and maps, then moved to Radical Heights.","co":"Nexon wrote down about $32.6m; the studio of about 75 closed.","nf":false,"l":"When a bigger rival reveals the game yours has turned into, redo the genre and competitor check before you grow the team.","i":"The genre arrived through one programmer's fix, so nobody chose it on purpose. The Overwatch reveal was the moment to decide again. Bleszinski calls the failure death by 1,000 paper cuts, so this is one cause among several.","a":"Has our game drifted into a genre we never chose on purpose?","q":"“I hadn't realized we were making a hero shooter at that point.”","qa":"Cliff Bleszinski, co-founder and CEO of Boss Key Productions","st":"Cliff Bleszinski Knows What Went Wrong","su":"https://80.lv/articles/cliff-bleszinski-knows-what-went-wrong#:~:text=I%20hadn%27t%20realized%20we%20were,hero%20shooter%20at%20that%20point","sd":"Jul 2026","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"left4dead","n":"Left 4 Dead","dv":"Turtle Rock","y":"2008","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/500/","ls":"Steam","cv":"left4dead.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Prototyped the zombie co-op idea as a mod on a Counter-Strike map","s":"Two Valve staffers could not stop playing it. By that afternoon Gabe Newell had offered Turtle Rock their help, and the prototype grew into Left 4 Dead.","w":"Turtle Rock Studios started what became Left 4 Dead in 2004, just after shipping Counter-Strike: Condition Zero. Its prototype, Terror Strike, put counter terrorists on a night version of the CS Italy map, planting zombie bait and fending off a horde. Mike Booth showed it to Valve staffers Chet Faliszek and Erik Wolpaw, who could not stop playing. That afternoon Gabe Newell offered Booth their help, and the two studios worked out where to take it. Valve bought Turtle Rock in January 2008, and the game shipped that November.","r":"Valve offered Turtle Rock two of its staff the same day.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Put a playable build of the core loop in front of the people you need before you ask for their help.","i":"A prototype people cannot put down makes the case faster than any pitch, because the people deciding feel the loop themselves. Turtle Rock already worked closely with Valve on Counter-Strike; a prototype from strangers would have needed a way in first.","a":"Could we hand a partner a playable build of our core loop today?","q":"“It captured the thing we always loved about the zombies,”","qa":"Chet Faliszek, writer at Valve","st":"The Making Of... Left 4 Dead","su":"https://www.gamesradar.com/making-left-4-dead/#:~:text=It%20captured%20the%20thing%20we,loved%20about%20the%20zombies","sd":"Apr 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"left4dead2","n":"Left 4 Dead 2","dv":"Valve","y":"2009","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/550/","ls":"Steam","cv":"left4dead2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Picked only the sequel ideas the team could finish before a known ship date","s":"Valve shipped Left 4 Dead 2 a year after the original, without the long gap its games were known for. Melee weapons cut from the first game went in first.","w":"Left 4 Dead shipped in 2008 without content the team had wanted in it. Between going gold and launch, small groups worked on their own ideas and presented them to each other. The pile grew big enough for a sequel. The team knew its deadline going in and tried Scrum for the first time, which Kim Swift said helped sort out which ideas were doable in time. Melee weapons, cut from the first game, came first. Left 4 Dead 2 reached Steam on 16 November 2009, and Swift said the team had not moved at Valve time.","r":"The sequel shipped about a year after the first game.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before production starts, fix the ship date and cut the idea list to what the team knows it can finish by then.","i":"A fixed date turns a pile of ideas into a ranking, and the team builds what it already knows works, such as content cut from the last game. That suits a sequel on a proven base; a new concept still needs room to experiment.","a":"Which ideas on our list can we finish by the ship date?","q":"“Since we knew that our shipping date was a lot sooner, we were able to sort out which ideas were actually doable in that amount of time, and that we knew were actually going to be successful, rather than trying a whole bunch of different stuff that wasn't necessarily going to be what we shipped.”","qa":"Kim Swift, designer at Valve","st":"Q&A: Valve's Swift On Left 4 Dead 2's Production, AI Boost","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/q-a-valve-s-swift-on-i-left-4-dead-2-i-s-production-ai-boost#:~:text=Since%20we%20knew%20that%20our,be%20what%20we%20shipped","sd":"Nov 2009","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"lifebyyou","n":"Life by You","dv":"Paradox Tectonic","y":"2024","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2024","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Founded a new studio under a Sims veteran to take Paradox into a new genre","s":"After three announced early access dates slipped, Paradox cancelled Life by You in June 2024, wrote off MSEK 208 and closed the 24-person studio.","w":"Paradox Tectonic, led by The Sims 2 and 3 veteran Rod Humble, developed Life by You from 2019. Early access was set for 12 September 2023, then moved to 5 March 2024 so the team could work in player feedback. In February 2024 it moved again to 4 June, for character polish and bug fixing, and that date slipped too. On 17 June 2024 Paradox cancelled the game, saying a version it would be satisfied with was too far away. It wrote down MSEK 208 and closed the 24-person studio.","r":"Paradox wrote down MSEK 208 and closed the studio.","re":"Paradox said it would review how it manages and organizes projects.","co":"MSEK 208 write-down, 24 jobs and about 5 years of development.","nf":false,"l":"When you enter a genre you have never shipped, set a playable benchmark against the genre leader as a kill gate before announcing dates.","i":"A new team in a new genre has no shipped game to measure its build against, so the distance to good enough shows only when players try it. Each announced early access date then became a public promise to move.","a":"Which playable test proves we can match the genre leader, and when?","q":"“A version that we’d be satisfied with is too far away, and therefore we are taking the difficult decision to cancel the release.”","qa":"Fredrik Wester, CEO of Paradox Interactive","st":"Paradox Interactive cancels the release of Life by You which impacts the second quarter with MSEK 208 in a write-down","su":"https://www.paradoxinteractive.com/media/press-releases/press-release/paradox-interactive-cancels-the-release-of-life-by-you-which-impacts-the-second-quarter-with-msek-208-in-a-write-down#:~:text=be%20satisfied%20with%20is,too%20far%20away","sd":"Jun 2024","sy":"Company statement","w8":3,"sh":"https://www.paradoxinteractive.com/media/press-releases/press-release/paradox-interactive-cancels-the-release-of-life-by-you-which-impacts-the-second-quarter-with-msek-208-in-a-write-down#:~:text=A%20version%20that%20we,cancel%20the%20release"},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Delayed Early Access twice to polish details instead of judging the whole game","s":"Each delay brought only incremental improvements. In June 2024 Paradox cancelled the game before any release, closed the studio and wrote down SEK 208 million.","w":"Paradox announced Life by You in March 2023, with Early Access set for 12 September 2023. In July 2023 Paradox Tectonic's general manager Rod Humble pushed it to 5 March 2024 to work in player feedback. A second slip moved it to 4 June 2024, and on 21 May 2024 Paradox held the release indefinitely. On 17 June 2024 deputy CEO Mattias Lilja cancelled the game: each delay had brought incremental improvements, and more time would not get it close enough. The July 2024 interim report wrote down SEK 208.0 million, and Tectonic was closed.","r":"No release; all capitalised development cost written off and the studio closed.","re":"Paradox said it would examine what led to the decision and change its practices.","co":"SEK 208.0 million write-down; Q2 2024 operating profit down 90% to SEK 28.9 million.","nf":false,"l":"At each slip, judge the whole game against your release bar, not the list of fixes made since the last date.","i":"Every delay showed incremental gains, so attention went to details while the whole game stayed short of the bar. A hold-and-reassess gate only works if that bar is written down before the first slip.","a":"If we slip again, what proves the whole game is good enough?","q":"“For each delay we made, we've seen incremental improvements, which in hindsight may well have led us to focus on details rather than the whole picture.”","qa":"Mattias Lilja, Deputy CEO of Paradox Interactive","st":"Life By You is Cancelled","su":"https://forum.paradoxplaza.com/forum/threads/life-by-you-is-cancelled.1688889/#:~:text=For%20each%20delay%20we%20made,the%20whole%20picture","sd":"Jun 2024","sy":"Company statement","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Invested heavily early in Life by You and built a large team","s":"Changes became expensive and slow, and the game could not be turned around. Paradox cancelled it in June 2024 and wrote down MSEK 208.","w":"Paradox Tectonic, led by former Sims 2 lead Rod Humble, worked on Life by You for over 4 years. Paradox spent heavily early, and the team grew large. After several delays, Paradox cancelled the release on June 17, 2024. It wrote down MSEK 208, the game's total capitalized development costs, and CEO Fredrik Wester said a version Paradox would be satisfied with was too far away. In October 2024 deputy CEO Mattias Lilja said the early spending had made every change expensive and slow. Each further step, he said, would have cost more and probably solved nothing.","r":"Paradox cancelled the game and wrote down MSEK 208.","re":"Cancelled the release and changed how Paradox starts new projects.","co":"MSEK 208 write-down of total capitalized development costs.","nf":false,"l":"Keep the team small until a playable build proves the core idea, so changing direction stays cheap.","i":"A large team turns every change of direction into a payroll problem: each fix costs more, so leaders stop making them. Lilja still defended the strategy itself, so the lesson is about how fast to scale, not whether to enter a new genre.","a":"Would changing direction today still be cheap at our team size?","q":"“We thought we had a good idea and we invested too heavily early on, so the team was too big and making changes became expensive and slow,”","qa":"Mattias Lilja, deputy CEO of Paradox Interactive","st":"Life by You was too big to be saved, but Paradox has not given up on making a Sims-like","su":"https://www.dbltap.com/features/life-by-you-was-too-big-to-be-saved-but-paradox-has-not-given-up-on-sims-likes#:~:text=We%20thought%20we%20had%20a,became%20expensive%20and%20slow","sd":"Oct 2024, reprinted May 2025","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"limittheory","n":"Limit Theory","dv":"Procedural Reality","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"Cancelled 2018","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Pitched an infinite procedural space game mixing RPG, RTS and sandbox play on Kickstarter","s":"Six years later the game was still far from feature complete. Josh Parnell ran out of money and energy and stopped development in 2018.","w":"Josh Parnell launched Limit Theory on Kickstarter on November 20, 2012, as an RPG, RTS and sandbox space game in a procedural universe. The campaign asked for $50,000 and closed on December 22, 2012 with $187,865 from 5,449 backers. Parnell worked on it for six years. In his final backer update in 2018 he wrote that, despite a lot of progress in the last year, the game was still far from feature completion. He had spent past the initial investment and most of his personal savings. He blamed his own underestimation of the work and stopped.","r":"Development stopped in 2018 with the game far from feature complete.","re":"Parnell later opened the source code, reported in July 2022.","co":"Six years, $187,865 in pledges and most of his personal savings.","nf":false,"l":"Before you pitch, cost every promised system in developer-months and compare the total with your team and your money.","i":"Procedural generation replaces hand-made content, not the systems that produce it, and this pitch combined three genres of systems. Raising nearly 4 times the goal did not shrink the work. Parnell also ran out of energy, not only money.","a":"Have we costed every promised system against our real team size?","q":"“It is my own fault, for having underestimated at every turn the amount of work that goes into such a creation.”","qa":"Josh Parnell, developer of Limit Theory","st":"Update 53: The End","su":"https://www.kickstarter.com/projects/joshparnell/limit-theory-an-infinite-procedural-space-game/posts/2270873#:~:text=It%20is%20my%20own%20fault,goes%20into%20such%20a%20creation","sd":"2018","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"littleinferno","n":"Little Inferno","dv":"Tomorrow Corporation","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/221260/","ls":"Steam","cv":"littleinferno.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Grew a 6-month fireplace game into an adventure with a town and time travel","s":"The project ran about 2.5 years, 5 times the plan. The Wii U launch window forced the team to cut back to the core fireplace game.","w":"Tomorrow Corporation started the game in 2009 or 2010 as My Fireplace, planned as a 6-month project. The concept grew into an adventure with a snowy town, characters, puzzles, a time-travelling subway, home decorating and a Katamari-style burning mode. When the Wii U launch became an option, the 3 founders went back to the original idea and tightened it up. Little Inferno launched with the Wii U in November 2012, about 2.5 years after work began. At GDC 2014 Kyle Gray called the overthinking a mistake and said he wished they could have those years back.","r":"The schedule ran 5 times over the 6-month plan.","re":"The team cut back to the fireplace core to hit the Wii U launch.","co":"About 2.5 years against a 6-month plan, for a team of 3.","nf":false,"l":"Write the target duration next to the concept's feature list, and cut features until the two match.","i":"Each added mode looked cheap on paper, but a 3-person team pays for every one in years. The core burning tech alone took about 2 years, so even the cut-down game ran long. A fixed outside date was what finally forced the cuts.","a":"Does our feature list fit the time we have planned for it?","q":"“We went crazy and overscoped.”","qa":"Kyle Gray, co-founder of Tomorrow Corporation","st":"Trial by Fire: The Making of Little Inferno","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-0gKWZRK17Y&t=761s","sd":"Mar 2014, reprinted Dec 2017","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"qu":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-the-many-mistakes-we-made-while-making-i-little-inferno-i-"}]},{"k":"lordsofthefallen2023","n":"Lords of the Fallen","dv":"Hexworks","y":"2023","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1501750/","ls":"Steam","cv":"lordsofthefallen2023.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Outsourced the whole sequel to a 2-year-old studio under a milestone contract","s":"The contracted vertical slice (Milestone 11) came in below the agreed quality three times. CI Games ended the deal in May 2019 and restarted the game with its internal studio; it shipped in October 2023.","w":"On 4 June 2018 CI Games signed Defiant Studios, a New York studio founded in 2016, to develop Lords of the Fallen 2. Milestone 11 of the contract was the vertical slice. On 16 May 2019 CI Games terminated the deal: the slice had not reached the quality defined in the agreement despite three calls to fix it. CI Games said it had paid in full for all accepted work. Defiant founder David Grijns said the studio categorically disagreed with that portrayal. CI Games carried on with its internal studio and subcontractors, and the game shipped on 13 October 2023.","r":"Eleven months of outsourced work dropped; the sequel restarted with the internal studio.","re":"CI Games continued development in its internal studio with external subcontractors.","co":"11 months of contracted work, paid in full for accepted milestones; amount undisclosed.","nf":false,"l":"Write the vertical slice's acceptance criteria into the contract and decide after one failed redelivery, not three.","i":"The vertical slice was the first point where the publisher could see whether the partner could deliver the game, and the contract made it the key gate. Three rounds of fixes bought time, not confidence. Only CI Games' side of the quality dispute is public.","a":"What exactly must the vertical slice show before we accept it?","q":"“Oświadczenie o wypowiedzeniu Umowy zostało złożone z uwagi na nienależyte wykonanie przez Defiant kluczowego w świetle Umowy etapu pracy (Milestone nr 11), tzw. Vertical Slice, w jakości oczekiwanej przez Spółkę, określonej precyzyjnie w Umowie - mimo trzykrotnego wezwania Defiant do poprawy jakości tego etapu prac”","qa":"CI Games S.A., management board statement","st":"CI Games kończy współpracę z Defiant Studios. Lords of the Fallen 2 skończy wewnętrzne studio","su":"https://www.biznesradar.pl/a/80078,ci-games-konczy-wspolprace-z-defiant-studios-lords-of-the-fallen-2-skonczy-wewnetrzne-studio#:~:text=O%C5%9Bwiadczenie%20o%20wypowiedzeniu%20Umowy%20zosta%C5%82o,jako%C5%9Bci%20tego%20etapu%20prac","sd":"May 2019","sy":"Company statement","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Ended the outsourced sequel when Defiant's vertical slice missed the contract's quality bar","s":"CI Games restarted Lords of the Fallen 2 in-house for next-gen consoles and PC. It shipped in October 2023 and earned back its PLN 298 million outlay by March 2026.","w":"In June 2018 CI Games signed New York's Defiant Studios to make the sequel to Defiant's own creative vision. It was CI's biggest project, budgeted well above the first game. One main purpose of a revolving bank loan was to fund the deal. Milestone 11, the vertical slice, had a quality level set precisely in the contract. On 15 May 2019 CI Games ended the contract after the slice fell short, despite three calls to improve it. CI stopped drawing the loan, hired in-house in three locations and retargeted next-gen consoles. Lords of the Fallen shipped on 13 October 2023.","r":"Production restarted with an internal team and a new vision for next-gen.","re":"CI Games built internal teams in three locations and reused Defiant's work where possible.","co":"Production about PLN 178 million; release nearly 4.5 years after the termination.","nf":false,"l":"Write the vertical slice into the external developer's contract as a precisely defined quality gate, and act when it fails.","i":"A precise, contractual slice gives the publisher a clean exit before most of the budget is spent. It does not save the schedule: the restart still had to hire teams, set a new vision and rebuild.","a":"Does our partner contract define the vertical slice quality precisely?","q":"“The statement on termination of the Agreement was submitted due to Defiant’s improper performance of the stage of work (Milestone 11), the so-called Vertical Slice, which is crucial in the light of the Agreement, in the quality expected by the Company, specified precisely in the Agreement – despite three times calling on Defiant to improve the quality of this stage of work.”","qa":"Marek Tymiński, President of the Management Board (CEO) of CI Games","st":"Sprawozdanie Zarządu z działalności Grupy Kapitałowej CI Games za rok 2019","su":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1U6P-2muEI4tiZNuuZwJJ8AuvC4xegJaN/preview#:~:text=W%20drugim%20kwartale%202019%20zdecydowali%C5%9Bmy,z%20siedzib%C4%85%20w%20Nowym%20Jorku","sd":"Mar 2020","sy":"Company report","w8":4,"qu":"https://cigames.com/en/62019-termination-of-a-significant-agreement/"}]},{"k":"lordsofthefallen2014","n":"Lords of the Fallen","dv":"Deck13 Interactive","y":"2014","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/265300/","ls":"Steam","cv":"lordsofthefallen2014.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Started the studio's largest project with no managing producer empowered to cut scope","s":"Creative leads on both sides piled on features, asset limits were ignored in pre-production, every sprint looked like a failure, and a producer arrived only with 3/4 of the time gone.","w":"Deck13 began Lords of the Fallen for CI Games over 3 years before its February 2015 postmortem, with about 40 people and key areas understaffed. No managing producer was in place, so creative people on both sides chased the most content-packed game they could imagine, and the tech team's asset limits were ignored in pre-production to find the right look. Two-week sprints felt like failures and people came close to resigning. Deck13 hired a development director; the publisher added a managing producer with 3/4 of the time gone. The team grew to about 65, crunched, and shipped in October 2014.","r":"Over-budget assets, unfulfilled sprints and months of crunch before an October 2014 release.","re":"Hired a development director, then the publisher added a managing producer who cut features.","co":"Team grew from about 40 to about 65; crunch through the final months.","nf":false,"l":"Before production starts, appoint the person who owns schedule and cuts, and make the tech team's asset limits binding in pre-production.","i":"Without someone whose job is to say no, the loudest departments set the scope, and scenes built over the asset budget cannot be scaled back once people have seen them. Klose and Lange still call the game the step that made Deck13 a serious studio.","a":"Who on this project is empowered to cut features, and are they here now?","q":"“Missing throughout most of the project was a managing producer, someone who loves reading and writing schedules and charts, asking the tough questions and making tough decisions regarding scale, features, and deadlines.”","qa":"Jan Klose, director, and Thorsten Lange, technical director, Deck13 Interactive","st":"Postmortem: Deck13 Interactive's Lords of the Fallen","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-deck13-interactive-s-i-lords-of-the-fallen-i-#:~:text=Missing%20throughout%20most%20of%20the%20project,features%2C%20and%20deadlines","sd":"Feb 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"lostsoulaside","n":"Lost Soul Aside","dv":"UltiZero Games","y":"2025","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/3378960/","ls":"Steam","cv":"lostsoulaside.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Took a viral one-man demo into full production before a team or shipping experience existed","s":"Producer Yang Bing said his experience and ability could not then carry a full commercial release, so years went into building the team and trial and error. The game shipped on 28 August 2025, about nine years after the reveal.","w":"Lost Soul Aside began as one person's demo. After the 2016 reveal it drew wide attention, partners came to help, and Sony's China Hero Project took it on. In December 2024 Yang Bing said his experience and ability at that point were not enough to make a complete commercial game, so a long stretch went into assembling a team, his own growth and trial and error. The project started on PS4 and later moved to PS5 Pro, forcing repeated art iteration. A 10-plus-minute trailer in 2021 fixed the tone; the rest followed that slice. It released on 28 August 2025.","r":"About nine years from reveal to release, with the tone only fixed in 2021.","re":"Cut long quests, kept UE4 rather than moving to UE5, and shipped in 2025.","co":"9 years from reveal to release; team grew from under 5 to about 40.","nf":false,"l":"Before you announce a game off a demo, check that the team to ship it exists, or plan the first years as team-building.","i":"A viral demo proves that a slice looks good, not that anyone can finish it. When team and producer learn during production, the learning years sit on the schedule and early assets age against new hardware. A seasoned team makes the demo a head start.","a":"Do we have the people to ship what the demo promises?","q":"“第一个原因就是很现实的问题，当年《失落之魂》虽然有很多人关注，但那时候我个人的经验和能力不足以支撑把它做成一个可以正式完整发售的游戏。” (The first reason is a very practical one: back then Lost Soul Aside had a lot of people watching it, but at that time my own experience and ability were not enough to carry it to a complete, officially released game.)","qa":"Yang Bing, producer of Lost Soul Aside, UltiZero Games","st":"八年时间，索尼「中国之星」成了吗？| 游戏专访","su":"https://eu.36kr.com/zh/p/3115327914823429#:~:text=%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E4%B8%AA%E5%8E%9F%E5%9B%A0%E5%B0%B1%E6%98%AF%E5%BE%88%E7%8E%B0%E5%AE%9E%E7%9A%84%E9%97%AE%E9%A2%98,%E6%AD%A3%E5%BC%8F%E5%AE%8C%E6%95%B4%E5%8F%91%E5%94%AE%E7%9A%84%E6%B8%B8%E6%88%8F","sd":"Jan 2025","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"lostwithin","n":"Lost Within","dv":"Human Head Studios","y":"2015","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Kept quick prototype systems as production code under an 18-month schedule","s":"The team meant to rewrite the prototypes after approval, but once systems like doors were entrenched, a rewrite threatened other departments and programmer time. The game shipped in April 2015 with doors that work 95 percent of the time.","w":"Human Head built Lost Within for Amazon Game Studios in about 18 months from concept to release, with a core team of about 20 that peaked at 32. Early on, the team quickly prototyped core systems: movement, enemy behaviour, searching objects and doors. Each approved prototype was to be replaced by a system rewritten from scratch. Because of the pace, the prototypes went into use as the game systems. Once doors were entrenched, ripping them out threatened level and game design, and a rewrite would have eaten programmer time needed for remaining bugs. It launched on 16 April 2015.","r":"Doors shipped on prototype code, clipping the view about 5 percent of the time.","re":"Dropped the rewrite as too costly before release and shipped the prototype doors.","co":"A 5 percent door failure rate shipped; rewrite time was never budgeted.","nf":false,"l":"When you approve a prototype, schedule its rewrite on the spot, or decide now that the prototype ships.","i":"Prototype code spreads: level and game design build on its quirks, so each month it stays raises the replacement cost. On an 18-month mobile project the window to rewrite closed early. With a longer schedule, or a system nothing depends on, deferring costs less.","a":"Which approved prototypes are still running as production systems today?","q":"“Due to the rapid pace of development, we began to make use of the prototype game systems, fully intending to go back and rewrite them later.”","qa":"Chris Rhinehart, lead game designer, and Ryan Jackson, executive producer, Human Head Studios","st":"Into the asylum: A postmortem of Human Head Studios' Lost Within","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/into-the-asylum-a-postmortem-of-human-head-studios-i-lost-within-i-#:~:text=Due%20to%20the%20rapid%20pace,rewrite%20them%20later","sd":"Aug 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"luftrausers","n":"Luftrausers","dv":"Vlambeer","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/233150/","ls":"Steam","cv":"luftrausers.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Took a small arcade game to PlayStation consoles with no console experience","s":"The spring 2012 target slipped twice. The team spent almost 18 months polishing a near-finished game, and Luftrausers reached PS3 and PS Vita in March 2014.","w":"Vlambeer started Luftrausers in mid-2011 to expand a two-day prototype from 2010. Devolver Digital signed it, and PlayStation's Shahid negotiated terms on the spot. The studio had made games for PC and mobile, added programmers and aimed for spring 2012. Console work was new and took time to learn, and one exact hardware spec created an urge to make everything flawless. The team polished a near-finished game between events for almost 18 months, while the plane count grew to 125 and two launch dates slipped. It launched on PS3 and PS Vita on 18 March 2014.","r":"Launch moved from a spring 2012 target to March 2014.","re":"The team wrapped up in 2013 once event players stopped suggesting changes.","co":"About 2 years past the spring 2012 target; almost 18 months of polish.","nf":false,"l":"On a first console project, add time to learn the platform and agree up front when polishing stops.","i":"One exact hardware spec pulls a team toward making everything flawless, and every event adds new feedback. With no agreed stop point, polish runs on. Learning time shrinks on later console projects; the stop rule is still needed.","a":"Have we set aside platform learning time and a date when polish stops?","q":"“We got stuck in that little loop of trying to make Luftrausers better for almost a year and a half, and we started showing what was essentially a finished game at events around the world.”","qa":"Rami Ismail, Vlambeer","st":"Luftrausers Launches Today on PS3, PS Vita","su":"https://blog.playstation.com/2014/03/18/luftrausers-launches-today-on-ps3-ps-vita/#:~:text=We%20got%20stuck%20in%20that,at%20events%20around%20the%20world","sd":"Mar 2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"mafiaii","n":"Mafia II","dv":"2K Czech","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/50130/","ls":"Steam","cv":"mafiaii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Moved the sequel to next-gen consoles and built its engine almost from scratch","s":"Nearly finished assets had to be redone and gameplay came online late. Even 40 programmers were not enough, and the game shipped in August 2010.","w":"In 2004 a small team at Illusion Softworks was prototyping Mafia II and building assets, planning to license a proven engine for the current consoles. Then the engine company was sold, and the game looked too big for that generation. In 2005 the studio moved it to next-gen consoles on in-house technology started in 2004 as a backup. Nearly finished assets were redone and prototyping started again. Car traffic and player controls landed near the end. The game shipped in August 2010, and in 2012 a designer told GDC Europe that the team had kept underestimating its own tech.","r":"Assets were redone and core gameplay features arrived near the end.","re":"2K bought the studio in 2008, and teams were regrouped by feature.","co":"40 programmers, still not enough; nearly finished assets redone; release 5 years later.","nf":false,"l":"Before you rebuild assets for a new in-house engine, get the core gameplay playable on it, even as a rough prototype.","i":"A new platform and a new engine at once reset both the art and the code. Visuals showed progress early, so gameplay systems kept slipping. The risk is specific to switching mid-project; tech built before pre-production starts does not reset finished work.","a":"Can we play the core loop on the new engine yet?","q":"“So the consequence was that we decided to build our own technology almost from scratch.”","qa":"Jarek Kolář, designer at 2K Czech","st":"Mafia II Postmortem","su":"https://www.gdcvault.com/play/1016705/Mafia-II","sd":"Aug 2012","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4}]},{"k":"mafiaiii","n":"Mafia III","dv":"Hangar13","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"Expanded edition 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/360430/","ls":"Steam","cv":"mafiaiii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Staffing","t":"Moved combat AI developers onto shopkeeper and police reactions to Lincoln","s":"Shopkeepers and police reacted to Lincoln's race, and the setting landed with players. Combat stayed a competent cover shooter, and the gameplay loops drew criticism.","w":"Hangar 13 was a new studio building Mafia III and its engine at the same time. At a certain point in development, studio head Haden Blackman had to decide where to put people. He moved developers who would have improved combat AI onto shopkeepers who reacted to Lincoln's race and police who kept an eye on him. The game launched in October 2016. In November Blackman said players were disappointed by the gameplay loops and bugs, while the story and setting had resonated. He could not say whether more cover AI work would have changed the review scores.","r":"Combat AI got less work, and the cover shooting stayed merely competent.","re":"Blackman said the studio wanted to do better on core gameplay next time.","co":"Combat AI improvements that were never made.","nf":true,"l":"When you move people off a core system, write down the quality bar that system will now ship at.","i":"Every person moved to a signature feature comes out of another system, here the cover AI. Blackman himself could not say whether the weaker combat cost review points, so this is a judgement call with a known price, not a proven loss.","a":"Which system ships weaker if we staff this feature?","q":"“Those things came at the expense of some of our AI in combat, for example.”","qa":"Haden Blackman, studio head and creative director at Hangar 13","st":"Mafia III postmortem: How Hangar 13 made the critical decisions in the epic game’s design","su":"https://gamesbeat.com/mafia-iii-postmortem-how-hangar-13-made-the-critical-decisions-in-the-epic-games-design/#:~:text=Those%20things%20came%20at%20the,in%20combat%2C%20for%20example","sd":"Nov 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"magicka","n":"Magicka","dv":"Arrowhead","y":"2011","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/42910/","ls":"Steam","cv":"magicka.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Story & world","t":"Designed Magicka's adventure around hidden paths unlocked by how well players did","s":"The structure never felt right. Level cuts reduced it to two parallel campaigns, and a couple of months before the final milestone the team rewrote the whole script.","w":"Magicka was first thought of in college and won a Swedish Game Awards trophy in 2008. The original adventure design let players unlock different paths depending on how well they did in certain levels. About three quarters into development the team was still trying to make it feel right. As levels were cut, the unlock goals thinned out until the branches became two parallel campaigns. A couple of months before the final milestone, the team rewrote the entire script in one evening, reusing the second campaign's assets and levels. Magicka launched on Steam in January 2011.","r":"The branching plan shrank into two parallel campaigns, merged late.","re":"The team rewrote the script in an evening, reusing second-campaign levels.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Build one complete campaign path before you add branches that need their own levels.","i":"Each branch needs its own levels and unlock goals, so branching multiplies content, and scope cuts hollow it out first. The late rewrite was cheap here because levels and assets could be reused; a voiced story would cost far more to merge.","a":"Can we finish one campaign before we build a second branch?","q":"“We learned the hard way that when you're struggling to make one campaign, don't ever make two.”","qa":"Johan Pilestedt, CEO of Arrowhead Game Studios","st":"Postmortem: Arrowhead Game Studios' Magicka","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-arrowhead-game-studios-i-magicka-i-#:~:text=We%20learned%20the%20hard%20way,don%27t%20ever%20make%20two","sd":"Aug 2011","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Budgeted an inexperienced team's first game at 5 developers for 6 months","s":"The game took 8 full-time and 2 to 4 part-time developers 24 months, an estimate off by more than 700 percent, with 16-hour crunches before milestones that were missed anyway.","w":"Arrowhead's team had no real game development experience. They ironed Magicka out to 5 full-time developers for 6 months and judged ideas without regard to viability or budget. After 3 months they doubled the timeline and added 2 developers, still about 200 percent short. Then came 16-hour crunches before missed milestones, and half the boss fights were non-functional. The game shipped on January 25, 2011 after 24 months at about $400,000, and the team patched it daily for 2 weeks. By August 2011 it had sold nearly 800,000 copies, and Pilestedt put the estimate at more than 700 percent off.","r":"Development took 24 months instead of 6, with 8 full-time developers instead of 5.","re":"Doubled the schedule, added staff, crunched, then patched daily for 2 weeks after launch.","co":"24 months and 8 full-time plus 2 to 4 part-time developers; about $400,000.","nf":false,"l":"When your first milestone slips by 200 percent, re-estimate the whole project, not the next milestone.","i":"Nobody on the team had real game development experience, so the estimate had nothing behind it, and the milestone mentality made them crunch instead of cutting features. The game sold anyway, which proves the product, not the plan.","a":"If the first milestone slips badly, will we re-plan everything?","q":"“Our initial estimate was off by more than 700 percent.”","qa":"Johan Pilestedt, CEO of Arrowhead Game Studios and Game Director of Magicka","st":"Postmortem: Arrowhead Game Studios' Magicka","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-arrowhead-game-studios-i-magicka-i-#:~:text=Our%20initial%20estimate%20was,than%20700%20percent","sd":"Aug 2011","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"majescopremiumslatepsychonautsadventrisingbloodrayne2andothers","n":"Majesco premium console slate (Psychonauts, Advent Rising, BloodRayne 2)","dv":"Majesco Entertainment","y":"2005","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Moved a value-priced publisher into premium console games needing larger budgets","s":"Weak sales of the premium titles in fiscal 2005 brought $36.8 million of impairments, a $70.9 million net loss, a CEO resignation and a retreat to value and handheld games.","w":"Majesco had focused on value-priced games for most of its history, then over a few years grew into premium console titles, which it says need larger budgets and higher unit sales to profit. BloodRayne 2 shipped in October 2004, Advent Rising in May 2005 and Psychonauts in 2005. In late fiscal 2005 sales were weak across all lines, above all premium console games, and the CEO resigned. For the year to 31 October 2005 Majesco booked $36.8 million of impairments and a $70.9 million net loss, sold or cancelled premium titles in development, and returned to value and handheld products.","r":"A $70.9 million net loss and the premium console strategy abandoned within a year.","re":"Sold or cancelled premium titles in development and refocused on value and handheld games.","co":"$36.8 million of impairments, $26.3 million of them for stopped games; $70.9 million net loss.","nf":false,"l":"Greenlight a slate of bigger-budget titles only if the company can survive one miss without reversing its strategy.","i":"Premium titles need higher unit sales to break even, so a publisher built on value titles has no cushion when several miss at once. The 10-K blames weak sales, not the quality of the games, so the point is portfolio sizing, not any single title.","a":"Can we absorb this title's miss without changing the company's strategy?","q":"“These premium products require larger budgets for development and marketing and require higher unit sales to generate a profit.”","qa":"Majesco Entertainment Company, Form 10-K for fiscal 2005","st":"Form 10-K: Majesco Entertainment Company, Annual Report for the fiscal year ended October 31, 2005","su":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1076682/000095013606000658/file001.htm#:~:text=These%20premium%20products%20require%20larger%20budgets,to%20generate%20a%20profit","sd":"Feb 2006","sy":"Company report","w8":4}]},{"k":"markoftheninja","n":"Mark of the Ninja","dv":"Klei","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"Remaster 2018","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/214560/","ls":"Steam","cv":"markoftheninja.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built elaborate fire and enemy reaction systems before finding the stealth core","s":"After 4 months nothing was fun. A 2-month rethink threw away large amounts of art and animation and pushed pre-production into production.","w":"In the first months of 2011 development, the team built multistep cause-and-effect chains: fire that spread and enemies that reacted to every situation differently. The systems made real progress, but none of them was fun. About 4 months in, the core experience was still unclear and production had to start. In summer 2011 the team regrouped and spent 2 months building level after level to find the essential mechanics. Large amounts of art and animation were wasted. Story, late abilities and level design slipped into production, and the last 6 months before the 2012 release were especially hard for designers.","r":"Pre-production spilled into production, and designers took the brunt late on.","re":"The team spent 2 months testing level after level to find the core.","co":"About 4 months without a core, a 2-month rethink, much art and animation scrapped.","nf":false,"l":"Find the fun of the core loop in simple test levels before you build elaborate systems or order art.","i":"Systems and art were made before anyone knew which player decisions they had to support, so a change of direction wasted them. The core appeared once the team tested level after level, which makes cheap playable levels the faster check.","a":"Is the core loop fun in plain test levels yet?","q":"“As a result, we ended up wasting large amounts of art and animation as we drastically changed the direction of the game over a few short months.”","qa":"Nels Anderson, lead designer, and Jamie Cheng, founder of Klei Entertainment","st":"Classic Postmortem: Klei Entertainment's Mark of the Ninja","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-klei-entertainment-s-i-mark-of-the-ninja-i-#:~:text=As%20a%20result%2C%20we%20ended,over%20a%20few%20short%20months","sd":"Feb 2013, reprinted Sep 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the 2D stealth prototypes on the proven Shank 2 pipeline","s":"The team could start testing stealth ideas right away, and artists were never held up by engine work or memory limits.","w":"Klei began Mark of the Ninja in 2011 from a 2-minute concept video, with one big design risk: nobody knew how stealth would work in 2D. Instead of writing new technology, the team built on the pipeline it already had from Shank 2. Prototyping started almost at once, and many early ideas failed. Artists made animation and background art without waiting on engine work or worrying about memory. The game shipped on Xbox Live Arcade in September 2012. In the 2013 postmortem, the leads wrote that new technology could easily have meant over a year of pre-production.","r":"Prototyping started at once, and art never waited on technology.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Prototype a risky new design on technology your team has already shipped a game with.","i":"With proven technology, the design was the only open question, so each failed prototype was cheap. The reuse helps only where the old pipeline fits the new game: Klei still spent months building new level tools for Ninja.","a":"Is the design our only big unknown, or is the tech new too?","q":"“Had we also started our technology from scratch, we could easily have spent over a year in pre-production.”","qa":"Nels Anderson, lead designer, and Jamie Cheng, founder of Klei Entertainment","st":"Classic Postmortem: Klei Entertainment's Mark of the Ninja","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-klei-entertainment-s-i-mark-of-the-ninja-i-#:~:text=Had%20we%20also%20started%20our,a%20year%20in%20pre%2Dproduction","sd":"Feb 2013, reprinted Sep 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Spent months up front on tile-based level art and preview tools","s":"Designers could reshape levels without costly repainting, but the tools later slowed down badly on the game's large levels.","w":"On Shank and Shank 2, Klei hand-painted every level, so any layout change meant repainting large parts of the art. For Mark of the Ninja, the team knew the game depended on reworking levels many times. In 2011 it spent months on a texture-tiling system for environment art and better preview tools. Designers could then change a level without knock-on work for artists. Later the levels grew large, and the tools were not built for that. An incremental change took minutes and a full export up to an hour, which slowed design in the final months before the 2012 release.","r":"Levels stayed editable, but large levels took up to an hour to export.","re":"The design team pushed through it in a late design crunch.","co":"Months of upfront tool work, then up to an hour per full level export.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock your level tools, test them on a level as big as your largest planned one.","i":"Tiling separated level layout from final art, so a layout change no longer meant repainting. The tools were sized for the levels the team pictured at the start; when levels grew, iteration slowed again.","a":"Have we tested our level tools on our biggest planned level?","q":"“We invested months into building a robust texture-tiling system for the environment art and better preview tools, because we knew that building good tools would have a multiplicative effect on our productivity.”","qa":"Nels Anderson, lead designer, and Jamie Cheng, founder of Klei Entertainment","st":"Classic Postmortem: Klei Entertainment's Mark of the Ninja","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-klei-entertainment-s-i-mark-of-the-ninja-i-#:~:text=We%20invested%20months%20into%20building,effect%20on%20our%20productivity","sd":"Feb 2013, reprinted Sep 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"marvelsnap","n":"MARVEL SNAP","dv":"Second Dinner","y":"2022","sc":"AA","se":"Early Access 2022","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1997040/","ls":"Steam","cv":"marvelsnap.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Prototyped the core gameplay with two people on paper before hiring a team","s":"Ben Brode said the core took about two days on cards and later served to prototype 2v2, while the core loop and progression took four years of struggle and a late, team-wide pivot. MARVEL SNAP launched on 18 October 2022.","w":"Ben Brode and Hamilton Chu started Second Dinner in 2018 and tested the doubling-cube idea by asking each other, turn by turn in Hearthstone, whether they would double or concede. The first physical prototype went on the back of business cards; the core design took about two days, a hot-seat digital prototype about two weeks. The studio was still two people, which Brode said made pivots cheap. Then came four years of struggle over the core loop and progression, including a late pivot that changed the theming and took weeks of change management. It launched globally on 18 October 2022.","r":"The core held for four years; progression took four years and a late team-wide pivot.","re":"Simplified repeatedly, cutting leagues, a hero collector and a rotating shop before launch.","co":"2 days for the core, about 2 weeks for a digital prototype, 4 years overall.","nf":false,"l":"Prove the core on paper with the fewest people possible, then budget the meta and progression as the bulk of the work.","i":"Two people can bin a prototype in an afternoon; a large team needs weeks of buy-in for each pivot, so settle the core before you hire. A fast core proves little about the rest: Snap's progression still took four years. It covers design, not content.","a":"Is our core gameplay proven on paper before we add more people?","q":"“But I think having the smallest team possible really allows for a dramatic pivot because you spend less time explaining the vision and getting buy-in from a large team.”","qa":"Ben Brode, co-founder and chief development officer of Second Dinner","st":"Designing 'MARVEL SNAP'","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HjhsY2Zuo-c&t=2588s","sd":"Mar 2023","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HjhsY2Zuo-c&t=2311s"}]},{"k":"marvelsavengers","n":"Marvel's Avengers","dv":"Crystal Dynamics","y":"2020","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/997070/","ls":"Steam","cv":"marvelsavengers.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Took on the Games as a Service model for Marvel's Avengers","s":"Launch sales did not cover the development costs. Square Enix's president later said the company must pick designs that fit its studios and teams.","w":"Crystal Dynamics and Eidos-Montréal built Marvel's Avengers for Square Enix as a Games as a Service title. The final phase of development hit unexpected problems, including the move to working from home in the pandemic. The game launched on September 4, 2020. In November 2020 president Yosuke Matsuda said initial sales were below expectations and could not offset amortisation of the development costs, which put the HD Games sub-segment into an operating loss. In the 2021 annual report he said the GaaS model showed the need to choose game designs that fit each studio and team.","r":"Initial sales did not cover the amortised development costs.","re":"Square Enix planned ample additional content to grow sales after launch.","co":"HD Games operating loss of about ¥7 billion that quarter, per VGC.","nf":false,"l":"At concept, check that the business model and design match what this studio has shipped before, and plan for every gap.","i":"A service game needs live-ops skills, content cadence and tools from the start. Matsuda's lesson is about fit: choose a design the studio is built to make. He did not reject service games; he expected GaaS to grow in importance.","a":"Has this studio shipped this kind of game before?","q":"“Nonetheless, taking on the GaaS model highlighted issues that we are likely to face in future game development efforts such as the need to select game designs that mesh with the unique attributes and tastes of our studios and development teams.”","qa":"Yosuke Matsuda, President and Representative Director of Square Enix Holdings","st":"Annual Report 2021","su":"http://www.hd.square-enix.com/eng/ir/library/pdf/ar_2021en.pdf#:~:text=Nonetheless%2C%20taking%20on%20the%20GaaS,our%20studios%20and%20development%20teams","sd":"2021","sy":"Company report","w8":4,"sh":"http://www.hd.square-enix.com/eng/ir/library/pdf/ar_2021en.pdf#page=7"}]},{"k":"masseffect","n":"Mass Effect","dv":"BioWare","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/17460/","ls":"Steam","cv":"masseffect.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Licensed an unfinished Unreal Engine 3 and built the missing RPG pieces in house","s":"Every new engine drop from Epic broke BioWare's changes. Some updates took 4 to 6 months to merge, and the audio lead said the churn drove months of crunch.","w":"In 2003, as KOTOR neared completion, BioWare had no current-generation engine for its new IP, so it evaluated Valve's and Epic's technology. It licensed Unreal 3 before it was finished and built the pieces an RPG needed. The engine leaned toward a Gears of War style game. Each new drop from Epic forced BioWare to refactor its hook-ups, and some updates took 4 to 6 months. Partway through production the engine would not start for 4 or 5 months, and designers could not work in their levels. Mass Effect shipped at the end of 2007.","r":"Engine merges ate months and pushed the team into crunch.","re":"The team took its lessons into Mass Effect 2, which the art director found easier.","co":"Some engine updates took 4 to 6 months to merge; months of crunch.","nf":false,"l":"Before you modify a vendor engine that is still changing, estimate the merge time for each new drop and put it in the plan.","i":"Every change to engine code is something the next vendor drop can break, so merge cost grows with each change. The cost falls once the vendor engine stops moving or the team freezes on one version.","a":"How long will each vendor engine update take to merge into our code?","q":"“And it would take us in some cases four to six months to update to that new drop of the engine because we had changed so many things.”","qa":"Trent Oster, Director of Technology and co-founder of BioWare","st":"Mass Effect: The oral history of a game-changing RPG","su":"https://www.techradar.com/features/mass-effect-the-oral-history-of-a-game-changing-rpg#:~:text=And%20it%20would%20take%20us,had%20changed%20so%20many%20things","sd":"Nov 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"masseffect2","n":"Mass Effect 2","dv":"BioWare","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2362420/","ls":"Steam","cv":"masseffect2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Started Mass Effect 2 on the first game's world and technology before it shipped","s":"The team ramped up faster and had a fairly complete sequel after about 18 months, while still overhauling the game's systems.","w":"BioWare began Mass Effect 2 at the end of work on Mass Effect, shortly before the first game reached shelves in late 2007. The sequel started from the first game's world building and shared technology. In July 2009 lead producer Casey Hudson said the team was about 18 months in and had a pretty complete game. He expected roughly 2 years in total and said ramp-up had been faster. He also said the team had overhauled every part of the game he could think of. The release was planned for early 2010.","r":"Ramp-up was faster, and the sequel was fairly complete after 18 months.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When you plan a sequel, list what carries over from the first game and put the saved months into fixing its weakest systems.","i":"A shipped world and engine remove the slowest part of a new project: building the foundation. The saved time went into new content and overhauls. The account is pre-release, so the 2 years is a plan, not a delivered schedule.","a":"Which parts of the first game can the sequel reuse as is?","q":"“We had a terrific starting point to build from, by which I mean all the work that went into Mass Effect’s world building, and now after a year and a half of designing tons of new content and perfecting new features we have a pretty complete next game in the series.”","qa":"Casey Hudson, lead producer of Mass Effect 2 at BioWare","st":"Building Bridges: Casey Hudson Talks Mass Effect 2","su":"https://www.pcworld.com/article/524447/mass_effect_2_interview_p1.html#:~:text=We%20had%20a%20terrific%20starting,next%20game%20in%20the%20series","sd":"Jul 2009","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"masseffectandromeda","n":"Mass Effect: Andromeda","dv":"BioWare Montreal","y":"2017","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1238000/","ls":"Steam","cv":"masseffectandromeda.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built Andromeda on Frostbite, which lacked RPG systems, and swapped animation tools in pre-production","s":"Kotaku reported that coders built party, inventory and animation systems from scratch, maps were capped at 100 by 100 km, and technical animators lost months to the tool switch. The launch's most remembered images were animation glitches.","w":"BioWare chose Frostbite in late 2011; Aaryn Flynn later said it was BioWare's decision, not EA's. Kotaku reported in June 2017 that the engine lacked party management, inventory and an animation system, so coders built almost everything from scratch. Andromeda entered pre-production by 2013; early on, leadership switched animation software from 3DS Max to Maya, and 3 developers said technical animators lost months of progress. Maps were capped at 100 by 100 km while the team planned large planets. The game shipped in March 2017; its animation glitches became its most remembered images, and BioWare Montreal was scaled down.","r":"Coders rebuilt basic RPG systems; animators lost months and shipped glitchy animation.","re":"Some animators went back to 3DS Max for much of their work.","co":"Months of technical-animation work lost; the bulk of the game built in under 18 months.","nf":false,"l":"Switch engines or tools before pre-production work starts, and test them first against the game's hardest needs: map size, animation, party systems.","i":"An engine built for shooters gives an RPG team nothing to stand on, so every basic system costs programmer time before content exists. A tool switch during pre-production throws away finished pipeline work. An engine that already runs your genre has no such gap.","a":"Which of our basic systems does the engine not have yet?","q":"“All that technology was invalid, simply because we’d used a red pen instead of a blue pen.”","qa":"Unnamed developer who worked on Mass Effect: Andromeda, anonymous source in Kotaku","st":"The Story Behind Mass Effect: Andromeda's Troubled Five-Year Development","su":"https://kotaku.com/the-story-behind-mass-effect-andromedas-troubled-five-1795886428#:~:text=All%20that%20technology%20was%20invalid,instead%20of%20a%20blue%20pen","sd":"Jun 2017","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Left pre-production still undecided between procedural and hand-made planets","s":"The procedural plan was dropped at the end of 2015 and the planet count fell from hundreds to 7. Most of the game was then built in under 18 months.","w":"BioWare Montreal was in pre-production on Andromeda by 2013, and director Gérard Lehiany wanted hundreds of procedurally generated planets. The backup was a galaxy map of hand-made planets, and the team left pre-production without choosing. By summer 2015 the planet prototypes worked as technology but were not fun. At the end of 2015 the leads rescoped from hundreds of planets to 30, then to 7. The game shipped in March 2017 and settled at 70 on Metacritic for PS4. Most developers told Kotaku the bulk of it was built in under 18 months.","r":"The planet plan was rescoped at the end of 2015, late in development.","re":"Leads dropped procedural planets and cut the count to 30, then 7.","co":"Bulk of a 5-year project built in under 18 months.","nf":false,"l":"Set a dated gate for your riskiest feature, and leave pre-production only after you commit to one plan.","i":"With both plans open, content for the final plan could not start until the procedural bet failed at the end of 2015. A fallback helps only if someone sets the date when it takes over.","a":"Have we picked one plan for our riskiest feature, with a date?","q":"“We spent a lot of time developing down that road, and tailoring the story towards it and so forth, and we quickly found it wasn't yielding the results we wanted.”","qa":"Ian Frazier, lead designer on Mass Effect: Andromeda at BioWare","st":"Mass Effect Andromeda was once a 'giant procedural game' reveals BioWare","su":"http://www.ibtimes.co.uk/mass-effect-andromeda-was-once-giant-procedural-game-reveals-bioware-1616714#:~:text=We%20spent%20a%20lot%20of%20time,the%20results%20we%20wanted","sd":"Apr 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"maxpayne","n":"Max Payne","dv":"Remedy","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/12140/","ls":"Steam","cv":"maxpayne.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Told the story in graphic novel panels instead of cinematics","s":"Plot changes became cheap. The team could pin the panels on walls, move a scene in 30 seconds and redo the images in a day or two.","w":"Max Payne took several years to build, with about two dozen people in Espoo. Writer Sam Lake pitched graphic novel panels for the cutscenes, using heavily Photoshopped pictures of himself as examples. Development director Markus Mäki saw two gains: readers fill in the nuance, and production gets easier. Once the panels existed, the team cut them up, put them on walls and followed the whole game. A plot change took 30 seconds, and redoing images took a day or two instead of a week of cinematics. The game came out in 2001, and Mäki told Edge the story in 2008.","r":"Each story change cost a day or two instead of a week.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"A month or more of the writer's time in photo shoots.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock the cutscene format, time how long one plot change takes in it.","i":"Still panels are cheap to reorder and redraw, so the plot could keep moving late in production. Mäki tied the quality argument to the tech of the time; the cost argument still holds for any story told in stills.","a":"How long does one plot change take in our cutscene format?","q":"“Even if it meant redoing some of the imagery, you were talking a day or two instead of a week of doing cinematics.”","qa":"Markus Mäki, co-founder and development director of Remedy Entertainment","st":"Max Payne turns 20: Remedy Entertainment looks back on the making of its iconic action game","su":"https://www.gamesradar.com/making-of-max-payne-20th-anniversary/#:~:text=Even%20if%20it%20meant%20redoing,week%20of%20doing%20cinematics","sd":"2008, reprinted Jul 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"maxpayne2","n":"Max Payne 2","dv":"Remedy","y":"2003","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/12150/","ls":"Steam","cv":"maxpayne2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Staffing","t":"Hired a professional actor for Max so the writer could stay on the script","s":"Sam Lake kept a month or more of writing time for a script that grew from 150 to 160 pages to 600. Timothy Gibbs played Max.","w":"On the first Max Payne, writer Sam Lake was the face of Max and cast friends and relatives for the photo panels. The shoots took a lot of work. For the sequel the schedule was much tighter and the story much longer: the first screenplay ran 150 to 160 pages, the second ended at 600. Lake did not want to lose a month or more of writing time to photo shoots again. Professional actor Timothy Gibbs took the lead. Max Payne 2 followed in 2003, reviewed well and was ported to PS2 and Xbox.","r":"Lake kept his writing time for a script about 4 times longer.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"List the jobs your writer holds besides writing, and hand them off before the script grows.","i":"The writer's side job grew with the cast while his main job grew 4 times. Paying an actor freed the scarcest person on the team. If the writer is not the bottleneck, the old habit can stay.","a":"Which side jobs is our writer doing that someone else could take?","q":"“It didn't make any sense to waste a month or more of precious writing time for those photo shoots again.”","qa":"Sam Lake, writer at Remedy Entertainment","st":"Max Payne turns 20: Remedy Entertainment looks back on the making of its iconic action game","su":"https://www.gamesradar.com/making-of-max-payne-20th-anniversary/#:~:text=It%20didn%27t%20make%20any%20sense,those%20photo%20shoots%20again","sd":"2008, reprinted Jul 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"mdk","n":"MDK","dv":"Shiny","y":"1997","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/38450/","ls":"Steam","cv":"mdk.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the team's first 3D engine during a one-year game production","s":"Waiting for the tech ate design time. Planned stealth went, and the game shipped with 6 worlds instead of 8.","w":"Nick Bruty drew the first MDK character while crunching on Earthworm Jim. A team of 6 then made the game in just over a year while building its first 3D engine, all from scratch. Building a game while waiting for the tech to come online cost a lot of design time. Planned stealth was cut, the fourth world lost its proper boss, and 8 planned worlds became 6. MDK came out on 1 May 1997. In a 2014 interview Bruty called the schedule kind of crazy in retrospect, but said he was happy with the result.","r":"Stealth and 2 of 8 planned worlds were cut.","re":"The team cut scope to hold the date, and BioWare made the sequel.","co":"2 of 8 planned worlds and the planned stealth play.","nf":false,"l":"Before production, count the months designers will wait for the engine and cut scope to match.","i":"Designers cannot tune levels or stealth on tech that does not run yet, so every month of engine work comes out of design time. On a one-year schedule that loss lands on content. A longer schedule or a ready engine changes the sum.","a":"How many months will designers wait before the engine runs?","q":"“The whole game was made in just over 1 year with 6 people while building our first 3D engine.”","qa":"Nick Bruty, creator of MDK, Shiny Entertainment","st":"Hectic HQ Interview : Nick Bruty","su":"https://hectichq.com/viewpage.php?page_id=54#:~:text=The%20whole%20game%20was%20made,building%20our%20first%203D%20engine","sd":"2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"rockmandash3project","n":"Mega Man Legends 3 (Rockman DASH 3 Project)","dv":"Capcom","y":"2011","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2011","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Opened Mega Man Legends 3 to fan co-development before its prototype had passed Capcom's gate","s":"The gate held: in July 2011 Capcom judged the project did not meet its criteria for full production and cancelled it in public, prototype included, 10 months after the announcement.","w":"Capcom announced the Rockman DASH 3 Project on 29 September 2010 and opened a Devroom where fans voted on designs, while the game was at prototype stage. Producer Keiji Inafune left Capcom in October 2010. On 25 February 2011 the prototype was evaluated and the prototype period extended. By April, sales of a paid Prologue version were to decide whether main development would start; on 20 May that release was postponed. On 18 July 2011 Capcom said the project did not meet the required criteria for full production and cancelled both prototype and game; the Devroom closed on 19 August.","r":"Cancelled at prototype stage, 10 months after a public announcement; nothing released.","re":"Closed the Devroom, disabled comments and said there was no plan to restart.","co":"About 10 months of prototype work; money not disclosed.","nf":false,"l":"Write down the conditions for entering main development before you announce the project to players.","i":"Capcom's gate did its job: it stopped the project before full-production spend. Because the prototype had been announced and co-developed in public, a routine kill became a public cancellation with a 28,000-member fan campaign. The lesson is about announcing early, not about having a gate.","a":"Are our main-development conditions written down before we go public?","q":"“Unfortunately it was not felt that the Mega Man Legends 3 Project met the required criteria, and it is with regret that we must announce that the Mega Man Legends 3 Project has been cancelled, meaning that Capcom will not be releasing the Prototype or the full game.”","qa":"GregaMan, Capcom spokesperson on the Capcom-Unity site","st":"Capcom Cancels Mega Man Legends 3","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/capcom-cancels-i-mega-man-legends-3-i-#:~:text=Unfortunately%20it%20was%20not%20felt,or%20the%20full%20game.","sd":"Jul 2011","sy":"Company statement","w8":3}]},{"k":"messiah","n":"Messiah","dv":"Shiny","y":"2000","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/577980/","ls":"Steam","cv":"messiah.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Removed polygon limits so artists built one high-resolution model per character","s":"Characters grew from 400 slices to 1,500 and raw files reached 150MB. Tools built for 400 slices slowed down, and the team had to build a render network.","w":"When Michael Persson's team joined Shiny around 1997, he set a goal for the Messiah character system: as few limits on artists as possible. Artists built one high-resolution model, and the engine scaled it for the game, cutscenes and cinematics. Characters came to average 300,000 to 500,000 polygons. The tools were written for 400 slices per character, and exports slowed once the limit rose. A distributed renderer cut 1,000-slice renders from overnight to 1.5 hours. By September 1999 models had 1,500 slices and 150MB raw files, and the programmers were giving up weekends to keep the tools up.","r":"Tools slowed down and needed a render network and constant rework.","re":"The team built a distributed renderer and planned caching and regional updates.","co":"Programmer weekends; 150MB raw models; overnight renders before the render network.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lift asset limits for artists, test your tools on the largest asset you expect to ship, not today's.","i":"Assets grew with the artists' ambition, but the tools were sized for the first limit. Every jump in detail hit export, memory and editing at once. The source shows the cost to tools programmers only, not to the game's schedule.","a":"Can our tools open the biggest asset we plan to ship?","q":"“The tools were written with 400 slices in mind, and work beautifully even at around 800 slices, but with insane (Darran) resolutions, we need to come up with better ways to influence and generate the finished model.”","qa":"Torgeir Hagland, tools programmer for Messiah at Shiny Entertainment","st":"MESSIAH’s Character Animation Tools (Or, Why I’m Losing My Hair)","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_September_1999.pdf#page=33","sd":"Sep 1999","sy":"Participant-written article","w8":3}]},{"k":"metro2033","n":"Metro 2033","dv":"4A Games","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/43110/","ls":"Steam","cv":"metro2033.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Cut Metro 2033 from an open, RPG-style design down to a tight linear game","s":"The game shipped in March 2010 as a linear, atmospheric shooter made by a team of about 50 to 60. Sandbox-style levels waited until Metro Exodus.","w":"4A Games sold a Metro 2033 prototype to THQ at the Leipzig Games Convention in 2006. The original design was much more open, with more RPG-style play, drawing on the team's S.T.A.L.K.E.R. experience. The game was later redesigned as a tightly built linear experience. It shipped in March 2010, made by a team that topped out at about 50 or 60 people. In 2018 Deep Silver's Huw Beynon said an open game at that team size had been out of the question. In 2020 executive producer Jon Bloch said the small team still fought the clock, and burnout followed.","r":"The shipped game was linear, and open levels waited for Metro Exodus.","re":"4A brought sandbox-style larger levels back in Metro Exodus.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before production, check whether your team can fill the world structure you picked, and redesign early if it cannot.","i":"An open world multiplies content and systems, and a team of 50 to 60 could not fill one at 4A's level of detail. A linear game put that detail where players look. The limit is headcount and tech: the same studio later built larger levels.","a":"Can our team fill this world structure at our quality bar?","q":"“The original design for Metro 2033 was a lot more ambitious than the released product.”","qa":"Jon Bloch, executive producer at 4A Games","st":"“It’s a story set in Eastern Europe, told by Eastern Europeans, that doesn’t speak to international politics, but to who we are as a species” – Celebrating 10 years of Metro 2033","su":"https://www.mcvuk.com/business-news/its-a-story-set-in-eastern-europe-told-by-eastern-europeans-that-doesnt-speak-to-international-politics-but-to-who-we-are-as-a-species-celebrating-10-years-of/#:~:text=The%20original%20design%20for%20Metro,than%20the%20released%20product","sd":"Aug 2020","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"metroexodus","n":"Metro Exodus","dv":"4A Games","y":"2019","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/412020/","ls":"Steam","cv":"metroexodus.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Opened up the linear Metro formula with sandbox areas for the third game","s":"Finding a mix that kept Metro's atmosphere took 2.5 years before production could start in earnest. Production then grew several times larger than on the first two games.","w":"Metro 2033 was pitched as an open world, since much of the core team came from S.T.A.L.K.E.R., but it was scaled back to a linear game. For Exodus, 4A had the technology and budget to open things up. The team did not want a generic open world that lost the series' atmosphere. Finding the formula took 2.5 years, and only then did production start in earnest. Production grew several times past anything the studio had done, with more people and new pipelines. Exodus came out in February 2019, and executive producer Jon Bloch looked back in October 2020.","r":"Full production started only after 2.5 years of searching.","re":"4A scaled production up with more people and new pipelines.","co":"2.5 years of search; production several times larger than before.","nf":false,"l":"When you add open areas to a linear series, plan the search for the formula as its own phase before full production.","i":"Open areas pull against a tightly authored atmosphere, and content can only be built at scale once the balance is found. A sequel that changes less of its formula needs a shorter search.","a":"Have we set aside time to find the formula before we staff up?","q":"“It took us 2.5 years to find it, and that's really when Exodus production could start in earnest.”","qa":"Jon Bloch, Executive Producer at 4A Games","st":"Metro: A look into what made this apocalypse so special","su":"https://www.gamereactor.eu/metro-a-look-into-what-made-this-apocalypse-so-special/#:~:text=It%20took%20us%202.5%20years,production%20could%20start%20in%20earnest","sd":"Oct 2020","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"metroidprime2","n":"Metroid Prime 2: Echoes","dv":"Retro Studios","y":"2004","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned to reuse light world environments as dark world variants to fit the schedule","s":"No technical trick made the dark variants different enough. Artists did far more unplanned work than anyone had budgeted, and only Sanctuary Fortress was built with the plan in mind.","w":"In Metroid Prime 2, Samus moves between a light world and a dark world. Retro Studios chose to reuse environments between the two, because that way the schedule worked. No technical solution made the dark variants different enough to be interesting, and early fully dark concepts, with a spotlight on Samus, were impossible to play. Artists then did far more work than anyone had budgeted. Only Sanctuary Fortress was built with both versions in mind, and there was no time to rebuild the rest. The game shipped on GameCube in 2004; engineer Jack Mathews looked back in 2018.","r":"Artists took on unbudgeted work, and most dark areas still looked samey.","re":"The team built Sanctuary Fortress with distinct light and dark versions.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you count reuse as a saving, build one area in both versions and time the art needed to make them feel different.","i":"Reuse saves time only if a cheap change makes the copy feel new. Here the tech for that never arrived, so artists made the variants work by hand against a fixed date. Reuse that players expect to look identical carries no such risk.","a":"Have we built one reused area and timed the art to make it different?","q":"“So, the artists had to do a ton of work to make the dark world variants work and, well, it was a ton more work than anyone had budgeted for and was completely unplanned.”","qa":"Jack Mathews, technical lead engineer at Retro Studios","st":"Interview: Jack Mathews","su":"https://shinesparkers.net/interviews/jack-mathews/#:~:text=So%2C%20the%20artists%20had%20to,and%20was%20completely%20unplanned","sd":"Jan 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"metroidprime3","n":"Metroid Prime 3: Corruption","dv":"Retro Studios","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Kept the SkyTown area in Metroid Prime 3 at the series' 60 FPS target","s":"Technical lead Jack Mathews had tried to get the area killed. Keeping it meant writing a set of new tools and extra constraints for the artists.","w":"Retro Studios held the Metroid Prime games to 60 FPS. Jack Mathews, a technical lead engineer on all three, enforced it: every artist ran the game on the console, and QA had tools to flag frame-rate drops. For Prime 3 on Wii, engineering pushed a more modern lighting model and larger environments. When Mathews first saw the SkyTown concept, he tried to get the area killed, but it stayed. Engineers wrote tools to support it, and artists worked under extra constraints. In January 2018 Mathews called SkyTown the hardest area in all three games to hold at 60 FPS.","r":"SkyTown shipped but needed extra engineering and art work.","re":"Engineers wrote new tools and set extra limits for the artists.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When a concept area threatens the frame-rate target, cost the tools and art limits it needs before you approve it.","i":"A fixed frame rate turns an ambitious area into engineering work: someone builds tools or the art gets cut back. Mathews still judged SkyTown worth it, so the lesson is to price the area, not to kill it.","a":"Have we priced the tools this area needs to hold frame rate?","q":"“We had to write a bunch of tools to support it and make the artists jump through a bunch of hoops.”","qa":"Jack Mathews, technical lead engineer at Retro Studios","st":"Interview: Jack Mathews","su":"https://shinesparkers.net/interviews/jack-mathews/#:~:text=We%20had%20to%20write%20a,through%20a%20bunch%20of%20hoops","sd":"Jan 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"metroidprime4","n":"Metroid Prime 4: Beyond","dv":"Retro Studios","y":"2025","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/metroid-prime-4-beyond-switch/","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"metroidprime4.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Restarted Metroid Prime 4 from the beginning with Retro Studios","s":"Nintendo said progress since the 2017 announcement had not reached its bar for the series. Development began again, and the game shipped in December 2025.","w":"Producer Kensuke Tanabe began planning the game around 2016, and Nintendo announced Metroid Prime 4 at E3 2017. Automaton noted that Nintendo of America had then said a talented new team, not Retro Studios, was making it. In January 2019 Shinya Takahashi said in a video that progress had not reached the standard for a Metroid Prime sequel. Nintendo changed the development structure and restarted with Retro Studios, keeping Tanabe as producer. Retro put over 100 of its own staff on it, over 300 people in total. The game shipped on December 4, 2025.","r":"Completion slipped well past Nintendo's initial internal plan.","re":"Retro rebuilt the game under Tanabe, with over 300 staff in total.","co":"Development restarted; release came about 8.5 years after the E3 2017 reveal.","nf":false,"l":"Set the franchise quality bar and check a new team's build against it before you announce the game.","i":"Nintendo measured the build against earlier Metroid Prime games, and the gate caught the gap. It came about 19 months after the public reveal, so the delay and the apology were public too. A project not yet announced can restart more quietly.","a":"Has a build passed our franchise quality bar before we announce?","q":"“This change will essentially mean restarting development from the beginning, so the completion of the game will be delayed from our initial internal plan.”","qa":"Shinya Takahashi, senior managing executive officer at Nintendo","st":"Development Update on Metroid Prime 4 for Nintendo Switch","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zLIv_UXI_So&t=127s","sd":"Jan 2019","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"middleearthshadowofmordor","n":"Middle-earth: Shadow of Mordor","dv":"Monolith Productions","y":"2014","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/241930/","ls":"Steam","cv":"middleearthshadowofmordor.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Wrote the game's spec in pre-production without data on the team's production capacity","s":"The spec proved over-ambitious. The team made big and painful cuts, then built large amounts of new content to fill the gaps.","w":"Shadow of Mordor took about 3 years. It was most of the team's first third-person open-world action game and first new-gen title, and many had not worked together before. During pre-production the team had no good metrics on its production capacity, so the spec was over-ambitious. Features such as a climbable Great Beast were later cut. The team then built significant new work, including large wilderness areas, to replace cut content. The game shipped on September 30, 2014. In January 2015 design director Michael de Plater called it a double dip of pain.","r":"Late, painful cuts, plus replacement work bigger than expected.","re":"Demos at most 3 months apart gave the team content-cost data and better schedules.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Build a few pieces of content at shippable quality in pre-production, time them, and size the spec to that rate.","i":"Without a measured content rate, nobody can tell whether the spec fits the team, so the gap shows up as cuts after the work is done. Each cut also needs replacement content. The risk is highest for a new team in a new genre.","a":"Do we know how long one shippable piece of content takes us?","q":"“During pre-production, we didn’t have good metrics on our production capacity.”","qa":"Michael de Plater, design director at Monolith Productions","st":"Postmortem: Monolith Productions' Middle-earth: Shadow of Mordor","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-monolith-productions-i-middle-earth-shadow-of-mordor-i-#:~:text=During%20pre%2Dproduction%2C%20we%20didn%E2%80%99t,metrics%20on%20our%20production%20capacity","sd":"Jan 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"mightyno9","n":"Mighty No. 9","dv":"Comcept","y":"2016","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/314710/","ls":"Steam","cv":"mightyno9.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Tied Kickstarter stretch goals to new platforms, ending with 10 versions to build at once","s":"The base game and the ports were made at the same time. The work ran past estimates, and the April 2015 delivery window slipped to June 2016.","w":"Comcept's Kickstarter ran from August 31 to October 1, 2013 and raised $3,845,170 against a $900,000 goal. An early update moved the PS3, Xbox 360 and Wii U versions closer as stretch goals, and backers asked for next-gen and handhelds too. On April 28, 2015, the month of the projected delivery window, Comcept set a September 15 release, with Vita and 3DS later. In August 2015 an online multiplayer bug pushed it to early 2016. The game launched on June 21, 2016. On the launch stream, Keiji Inafune said Capcom had never built 10 versions of one title at once.","r":"The game shipped about 14 months after its projected April 2015 window.","re":"Inafune took responsibility for the game's problems on the launch stream.","co":"Delivery moved from April 2015 to June 2016.","nf":false,"l":"Before you sell a platform as a stretch goal, cost the port and decide whether it ships with the base game or later.","i":"Every extra version adds QA, certification and fixes on top of the base game. At Capcom, outside studios ported games while one team built the base game; Comcept did both at once, and the work grew beyond estimates.","a":"Is every platform we promised costed and scheduled?","q":"“But never did they ever do 10 SKUs all at the same time, 10 different versions all for one title.”","qa":"Keiji Inafune, creator of Mighty No. 9 at Comcept","st":"Mighty No. 9 Creator Owns 'All the Problems' With the Game","su":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/mighty-no-9-creator-owns-all-the-problems-with-the/1100-6441146/#:~:text=But%20never%20did%20they%20ever,all%20for%20one%20title","sd":"Jun 2016","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"minimetro","n":"Mini Metro","dv":"Dinosaur Polo Club","y":"2015","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/287980/","ls":"Steam","cv":"minimetro.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Wrote down the founders' constraints first and only considered concepts that fit them","s":"The list ruled out 90% of ideas at once and pointed to procedural levels and abstract visuals. A weekend jam prototype became the first game the brothers released.","w":"In 2013 Peter Curry was a stay-at-home dad with a few hours a day, and Robert had a full-time programming job. Both had only ever abandoned game projects. They listed their strengths and weaknesses and set hard limits: no hand-built levels, nothing art-heavy, no reliance on audio. That discarded 90% of their ideas and pointed to procedural levels and abstract visuals. Their Ludum Dare entry in April 2013 took a weekend. The game still missed its Christmas 2013 target by a mile, reached Early Access in August 2014 and shipped in 2015.","r":"The brothers finished and released a game for the first time.","re":"When release slipped, they went to Early Access in August 2014.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you pick a concept, list the hours and skills you really have and drop every idea that needs more.","i":"Hard limits remove ideas before anyone gets attached to them, and the survivors fit the team. The limits shaped the concept, not the schedule: the first date still slipped by a mile once scope grew after Greenlight.","a":"Does our concept fit the hours and skills we actually have?","q":"“Not only could we immediately discard 90% of our potential ideas without agonizing for days over the viability of each one, but the constraints themselves inspired ideas.”","qa":"Peter and Robert Curry, Dinosaur Polo Club","st":"Postmortem: Dinosaur Polo Club's Mini Metro","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-dinosaur-polo-club-s-i-mini-metro-i-#:~:text=Not%20only%20could%20we%20immediately,constraints%20themselves%20inspired%20ideas","sd":"Jun 2016","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Kept a free browser build of the game online from the jam to release","s":"The web build spread fast, the game sailed through Steam Greenlight, and many free players moved to the paid Early Access version. The founders doubt the studio would exist without it.","w":"The brothers built their April 2013 Ludum Dare entry in Unity because so many people had the Unity Web Player. They reasoned a game in the browser would get more players than a download. Weeks later, with Lucas Pope's Papers, Please betas in mind, they chose to keep it free to play up to release. The alpha went up in September 2013, front and centre on their website. It spread, sailed through Greenlight, and many players moved to the paid Early Access build in August 2014. In 2016 they wrote that the studio likely owed its existence to that build.","r":"The game sailed through Greenlight and helped the Steam launch succeed.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Put your prototype where people can play it in one click, and keep it there while you build.","i":"A free build that runs in a browser costs nothing to try, so it spreads and grows a community before launch. It fits a game built for replay; a game that is spent after one play gives its sales away.","a":"Can a stranger play our prototype in one click today?","q":"“Again, it’s impossible to know for sure, but we don’t think Dinosaur Polo Club would be operating now if it wasn’t for that web-playable build.”","qa":"Peter and Robert Curry, Dinosaur Polo Club","st":"Postmortem: Dinosaur Polo Club's Mini Metro","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-dinosaur-polo-club-s-i-mini-metro-i-#:~:text=Again%2C%20it%E2%80%99s%20impossible%20to%20know,for%20that%20web%2Dplayable%20build","sd":"Jun 2016","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"monsterhunterworld","n":"Monster Hunter: World","dv":"Capcom","y":"2018","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/582010/","ls":"Steam","cv":"monsterhunterworld.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Funded an 18-month prototype with 50 to 70 people before full production","s":"The prototype proved the series' biggest structural change, a dense map without zone barriers where the environment is a weapon, before the full team was committed. Capcom's Peter Fabiano says it raised quality and sped up production.","w":"The project began in 2014, and prototypes were built from storyboards to test whether the concept worked. Over about 18 months, 50 to 70 people tested monster density, darkness and exploration, hiding in vegetation, monster pathfinding into narrow spaces, water traps and monster-versus-monster fights. A spore plant went straight into the product; a light-moss idea and an early monster did not. Western playtests in North America and the UK added an option for damage numbers; Japan got no playtests. The game shipped early in 2018. At GDC in March 2018 Tokuda and Fabiano showed the prototype footage publicly.","r":"The riskiest change was proven before the full team was committed.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"50 to 70 people for about 18 months of prototyping.","nf":false,"l":"When the core structure changes, fund a real prototype at scale before production; Capcom used 50 to 70 people for 18 months.","i":"A map without zone barriers changes pathfinding, pacing and recovery at once, so only a playable prototype shows what breaks. This works when a publisher can carry 50 to 70 people without revenue for 18 months; a small studio needs a smaller slice.","a":"Has our biggest structural change been played at scale yet?","q":"“This is how we were able to realize Monster Hunter: World’s most ambitious change: to make it possible to use the environment.”","qa":"Yuya Tokuda, game director of Monster Hunter: World, Capcom","st":"How Capcom designed Monster Hunter: World to feel approachable and alive","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/how-capcom-designed-i-monster-hunter-world-i-to-feel-approachable-and-alive#:~:text=This%20is%20how%20we%20were,to%20use%20the%20environment","sd":"Mar 2018","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Connected every area of the map and added the passage of time","s":"Each area could no longer get its own designed light source. Artists reshaped landscapes and placed glowing objects, and engineers added physics-based lighting to keep dark areas readable.","w":"Work on Monster Hunter: World began about 3.5 years before this November 2017 interview, aiming for a living ecosystem on current consoles. Art director Kaname Fujioka chose dense, connected 3D landscapes. In earlier games the screen changed between areas, so each area had its own light setup. Now every area was connected, and time passed as well. Artists reshaped landscapes so light reached dark spots and added objects that glow. Engineers supplied physics-based lighting. The first Ancient Forest model was too dense, and the team got lost trying to find the monster. The game launched in 2018.","r":"Lighting moved from per-area setups to landscape and engine solutions.","re":"The team added Scout Flies, territory maps and voice guidance to guide players.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you join separate areas into one map, list every per-area trick, such as lighting, that the join will break.","i":"Separate areas let artists tune each space on its own. Connect them and add moving time, and that tuning has to come from landscape shapes and engine features. The shift lands on engineers as much as on artists.","a":"Which per-area tricks break when our areas join into one map?","q":"“However, this time around all areas are connected, so that wasn’t possible.”","qa":"Kaname Fujioka, art director at Capcom","st":"Creative Minds: Voices from the Frontline of Development","su":"https://www.capcom.co.jp/ir/english/feature/2017_mh_crvoice.html#:~:text=However%2C%20this%20time%20around%20all,so%20that%20wasn%E2%80%99t%20possible","sd":"Nov 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"monumentvalley2","n":"Monument Valley 2","dv":"ustwo","y":"2017","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2022","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1927740/","ls":"Steam","cv":"monumentvalley2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Staffing","t":"Gave one freelancer all of Monument Valley 2's music, sound and audio implementation","s":"Todd Baker shaped every part of the audio, but the final months became an intense workload that he said was on the limit of what he could handle.","w":"Todd Baker, a freelance composer and sound designer, had worked with ustwo on Lands End VR. On Monument Valley 2 he was the only audio person, covering music, sound design and implementation in Wwise with Unity. Early on he recorded a concept album in a couple of weeks to feed art and design. Over the 18-month project, characters and story were not locked until the last 10 weeks, and the final level appeared about 3 weeks before launch. The late months became an intense workload. In November 2017 Baker said extra help then may have been wise.","r":"The audio shipped, but the last months pushed one person to his limit.","re":"Baker finished all the audio himself.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If one person owns all the audio, book a second pair of hands for the final months.","i":"Audio peaks at the end, when levels and story lock and every sound has to be hooked up. MV2's story locked only 10 weeks before launch. One audio owner suits a small game; plan help for the last months.","a":"Who helps our only audio person in the final months?","q":"“It was definitely on the limit in terms of what I could have handled.”","qa":"Todd Baker, freelance sound artist on Monument Valley 2 for ustwo games","st":"Behind the beautiful sound of Monument Valley 2 – with Todd Baker","su":"https://www.asoundeffect.com/behind-beautiful-sound-monument-valley-2-todd-baker/#:~:text=It%20was%20definitely%20on%20the,what%20I%20could%20have%20handled","sd":"Nov 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"mother3","n":"MOTHER 3 (Nintendo 64 version)","dv":"HAL Laboratory","y":"2000","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2000","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Started MOTHER 3 on a mandate to make something unprecedented in full 3D","s":"The early design asked more of the hardware than it had. After about 6 years and two platform changes, the Nintendo 64 game was cancelled and the team disbanded in 2000.","w":"MOTHER 3 began on the Super Famicom, moved to the 64DD and then to the Nintendo 64. The team set out to make something unprecedented, and Shigesato Itoi wrote 12 chapters, each with different play. For a time the design demanded more performance and memory than the machine had. Producer Satoru Iwata, often away, spent his first year on site reworking it, with little visible progress for about 9 months. In August 2000 Itoi, Iwata and Shigeru Miyamoto explained the cancellation: finishing would have sacrificed several future projects. Iwata said a rebuild 2 years earlier would already be done.","r":"A year went into reworking the design, and the N64 version was cancelled.","re":"Nintendo disbanded the team rather than sacrifice future projects to finish.","co":"About 6 years of development; roughly 1 year spent reworking the early design.","nf":false,"l":"Check the design against the target machine's memory and performance before production, while rebuilding is still cheap.","i":"Freedom from old limits made everyone dream big, and nobody costed what each chapter's unique play needed. Work already built made a restart feel too expensive, so the team kept patching. Iwata named the missed moment: two years earlier.","a":"Does the design fit the target hardware's memory and performance budget?","q":"“そもそも、「今までにないものを作ろう」とものすごくふりかぶって作り始めましたから。” (To begin with, we started out swinging hard, saying ‘let’s make something that has never existed’.)","qa":"Satoru Iwata, producer of MOTHER 3 (HAL Laboratory, Nintendo)","st":"『ＭＯＴＨＥＲ ３』の開発が中止になったことについての 糸井重里・岩田聡・宮本茂の座談会","su":"https://www.1101.com/nintendo/nin13/nin13_5.htm#:~:text=%E3%81%9D%E3%82%82%E3%81%9D%E3%82%82%E3%80%81%E3%80%8C%E4%BB%8A%E3%81%BE%E3%81%A7%E3%81%AB%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84%E3%82%82%E3%81%AE%E3%82%92%E4%BD%9C%E3%82%8D%E3%81%86%E3%80%8D%E3%81%A8,%E3%82%82%E3%81%AE%E3%81%99%E3%81%94%E3%81%8F%E3%81%B5%E3%82%8A%E3%81%8B%E3%81%B6%E3%81%A3%E3%81%A6%E4%BD%9C%E3%82%8A%E5%A7%8B%E3%82%81%E3%81%BE%E3%81%97%E3%81%9F%E3%81%8B%E3%82%89","sd":"Aug 2000","sy":"Participant interview","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Skipped the prototype phase on the strength of MOTHER 2's track record","s":"Nobody checked whether the changed team could repeat MOTHER 2. The project ran about 6 years without a go or stop decision and was cancelled in 2000.","w":"MOTHER 3 began on the Super Famicom, then moved to the 64DD and Nintendo 64. A normal project, Iwata said, starts with an experimental period: build a prototype, then commit if it feels right. MOTHER 3 skipped that step because the team had made MOTHER 2. Yet not all MOTHER 2 people formed its core, the staff grew and some members left. Nobody made a go or stop call, and the team kept believing it could finish. In August 2000 the producers announced the cancellation after about 6 years. Miyamoto said continuing would have hurt other projects too much.","r":"The project ran about 6 years without a go or stop decision.","re":"Nintendo cancelled the Nintendo 64 project and disbanded the team in 2000.","co":"About 6 years of work, cancelled when 30% to over 60% done.","nf":false,"l":"Even with a hit behind you, have the current team build a prototype before you commit to production.","i":"A past hit proves the team that made it. When people leave and the team grows, that proof expires, and without a prototype gate there is no planned moment to stop. The cancellation had other causes too, including pressure on other Nintendo projects.","a":"Has this team, as it is today, built a prototype that works?","q":"“作ることが前提で、実験期間なしだった。” (It was a given that we would make it; there was no experimental period.)","qa":"Satoru Iwata, producer of MOTHER 3 and director at Nintendo","st":"『ＭＯＴＨＥＲ ３』の開発が中止になったことについての糸井重里・岩田聡・宮本茂の座談会　その2","su":"https://www.1101.com/nintendo/nin13/nin13_3.htm#:~:text=%E4%BD%9C%E3%82%8B%E3%81%93%E3%81%A8%E3%81%8C%E5%89%8D%E6%8F%90%E3%81%A7%E3%80%81%E5%AE%9F%E9%A8%93%E6%9C%9F%E9%96%93%E3%81%AA%E3%81%97%E3%81%A0%E3%81%A3%E3%81%9F","sd":"Aug 2000","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"myththefallenlords","n":"Myth: The Fallen Lords","dv":"Bungie","y":"1997","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Staffing","t":"Started a 3D engine, 4 tools, 2 platforms and an online service with 7 people","s":"Programmer Jason Regier wrote that the team was understaffed for such an ambitious undertaking. Losing one programmer midway forced Bungie to drop its announced Java scripting language, yet the game shipped on time and sold 350,000 copies by late 1997.","w":"Bungie began Myth in January 1996 with four programmers, two artists and a product manager, aiming at a true 3D landscape, a simultaneous Windows 95 and Macintosh release, Internet play and a free online service, bungie.net. The team also built four tools and started a Java-based player scripting language. Midway through, one programmer left and an artist was added. The remaining programmers, who doubled as level designers, absorbed his work, and the network administrator was asked to help with AI scripting and level design. The scripting language was dropped as incomplete. The game shipped on time after about two years.","r":"The announced scripting language was cut and a third artist arrived almost too late.","re":"Pulled the network administrator into AI scripting and level design to cover the gap.","co":"1 of 4 programmers lost; the announced scripting feature dropped; sound redone late.","nf":false,"l":"Staff up as early as possible and plan to weather the unexpected: one departure on a six-person team cuts a feature.","i":"When seven people own an engine, tools, two platforms and a service, each is a single point of failure: one departure removes a feature. The bet paid because the team shared one vision and shipped on time; it does not prove big teams are unnecessary.","a":"Which feature dies if one programmer leaves next month?","q":"“On the flip side, it became clear very early in the project that we were understaffed for such an ambitious undertaking.”","qa":"Jason Regier, Bungie","st":"Postmortem: Bungie's Myth: The Fallen Lords","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-bungie-s-i-myth-the-fallen-lords-i-#:~:text=On%20the%20flip%20side%2C%20it,such%20an%20ambitious%20undertaking","sd":"Jul 1998","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Designed Myth from the ground up to run on Mac and Windows","s":"About 90% of the code was shared, so 3 programmers shipped both versions at the same time and spent their days on features, not the operating system.","w":"Bungie's programmers came from the Macintosh, and porting Marathon to Windows 95 had been a learning experience. When Myth started in January 1996, they vowed to do it right this time. They designed the game to be cross-platform from the start: 90% of the code was platform independent, and all data sat in tag files that byte-swapped automatically. Their own dialog and font managers made both versions look the same. With only 3 programmers, Myth shipped on Mac and Windows together in 1997. The German version followed a couple of weeks after the English one.","r":"Both versions shipped together with only 3 programmers.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If you plan to ship on 2 platforms, keep platform-specific code in one thin layer from the start.","i":"With 90% of the code shared, each feature was written once and ran on both machines. The approach rested on lessons from the Marathon port, so it fits teams that already know where their platforms differ.","a":"How much of our code is platform-specific today?","q":"“Ninety percent of the code in the game is platform independent; the other ten percent is split evenly between routines that handle PC- and Macintosh-specific functionality.”","qa":"Jason Regier, Bungie Software","st":"Postmortem: Bungie's Myth: The Fallen Lords","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-bungie-s-i-myth-the-fallen-lords-i-#:~:text=Ninety%20percent%20of%20the%20code,PC%2D%20and%20Macintosh%2Dspecific%20functionality","sd":"Jul 1998","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Planned the renderer for 3D accelerator cards before they were common","s":"When the chance came, support for 3Dfx and Rendition chipsets went in within about a week, with on-site help from both vendors.","w":"When Myth started in January 1996, 3D acceleration hardware was only beginning to catch on. The programmers still kept it in mind while designing the rendering pipeline. Later in the 2-year project, the chance came to add hardware acceleration. Working closely with people from 3Dfx and Rendition, the team added support for their chipsets in about a week. The postmortem credits both the design and the vendors' on-site developer help. Myth shipped in 1997, and the accelerators gave it smoother terrain, more fluid camera movement and more realistic units and effects.","r":"Support for 2 accelerator chipsets took about a week.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Leave a clear place in your renderer for hardware your players will likely buy during production.","i":"A pipeline designed with acceleration in mind gave the vendors a clean place to plug in. The week also depended on on-site engineers from 3Dfx and Rendition, so do not expect the same speed without that help.","a":"Which hardware might players buy before launch, and can our renderer use it?","q":"“We worked closely with people from 3Dfx and Rendition and added support for their chipsets in about a week.”","qa":"Jason Regier, Bungie Software","st":"Postmortem: Bungie's Myth: The Fallen Lords","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-bungie-s-i-myth-the-fallen-lords-i-#:~:text=We%20worked%20closely%20with%20people,in%20about%20a%20week","sd":"Jul 1998","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"n","n":"N+","dv":"Metanet","y":"2008","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Kept building level sharing after Microsoft raised concerns about offensive content","s":"After the Forza incident, Microsoft told Metanet to switch sharing off near launch. The late change failed certification and caused a lengthy launch delay.","w":"Metanet wanted N+ players to create and share levels on Xbox Live Arcade. Microsoft backed it but needed to block offensive content, and tight limits such as 8x8 tile levels would cripple the editor. The founders settled on several passable solutions and started building. Then Forza showed Microsoft could not delete one user's offensive upload without deleting the account. Near the end, Metanet was told to disable sharing for launch. The change failed certification, and bug fixing ran until a December resubmission. N+ debuted during GDC 2008, and the founders' September 2008 postmortem called it possible poor risk management.","r":"A late cut failed certification and delayed the launch.","re":"Metanet disabled sharing for launch, hoping to switch it back on later.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When the platform holder raises doubts about a feature, get its sign-off in writing or cut the feature before you spend more on it.","i":"A feature that rests on platform policy can be pulled by events outside the team, here another game's moderation failure. Agreed solutions did not protect it, so treat platform doubts as a cut signal, not a design problem.","a":"Has the platform holder signed off on every feature it questioned?","q":"“It was a lot of work for everyone, and since there was a chance that it would be cut, it's possible this was a case of poor risk management on our part.”","qa":"Mare Sheppard and Raigan Burns, co-founders of Metanet","st":"Exclusive: Behind The Scenes of Metanet's N+","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/exclusive-behind-the-scenes-of-metanet-s-i-n-i-#:~:text=It%20was%20a%20lot%20of,poor%20risk%20management%20on%20our%20part","sd":"Sep 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Ported the Flash game to a playable C# version before writing final C++","s":"Slick Entertainment could tune the feel at 60 frames per second instead of 40 and match the Flash original before locking in console code. Waanders said it worked pretty well.","w":"Metanet's N was a freeware Flash platformer, and Slick Entertainment converted it for Xbox Live Arcade. Metanet sent Nick Waanders the Flash source code and style drawings. He first built a complete C# version where you could run around. It let him learn quickly how the game worked and move it from 40 to 60 frames per second. Some variables did not carry over, and the team matched the Flash feel before pouring the C++ concrete. Multiplayer and Microsoft's rules came next and took quite a lot of time. N+ debuted on XBLA during GDC 2008.","r":"The console version matched the Flash feel before final code began.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When you port a game to a new frame rate, rebuild it in a quick language first and match the feel before final code.","i":"Movement tuned at 40 frames per second can feel different at 60, and a cheap rebuild shows which variables drift. It pays when feel is the product, as in a precision platformer; a menu-driven port gains little.","a":"Have we matched the original's feel before writing final code?","q":"“There were some variables that were not really getting across, and we wanted to get as close to the Flash version as possible before we started pouring the C++ concrete basically.”","qa":"Nick Waanders, president of Slick Entertainment","st":"N+: Beyond The Postmortem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/n-beyond-the-postmortem#:~:text=There%20were%20some%20variables%20that,pouring%20the%20C%2B%2B%20concrete%20basically","sd":"Mar 2008","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"naturalselection2","n":"Natural Selection 2","dv":"Unknown Worlds Entertainment","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/4920/","ls":"Steam","cv":"naturalselection2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Replaced a quick Source-engine sequel with its own engine, Lua game code and IDE","s":"The sequel took 6 years and $2.9 million instead of bringing in money quickly, the studio nearly went out of business several times, and nothing was left to promote the launch.","w":"Unknown Worlds started NS2 at the end of 2006. The plan was a quick commercial sequel on the Source engine to start making money. Instead the team built its own engine, Spark, wrote all game code in Lua for moddability, and its own Lua IDE, Decoda. Angel funding came in 2008; pre-orders then brought in over $1 million across two years and saved the company. Fundamental engine optimizations were found close to release. The game shipped on October 31, 2012 after 6 years. In February 2013 Cleveland wrote he would not build his own technology for a first product again.","r":"Six years and $2.9 million for a sequel meant to earn money quickly.","re":"Pre-orders over two years and angel funding kept the studio alive.","co":"6 years, $2.9 million ($1.1M pre-orders, $1.8M investment), 7 in-house plus 11 contractors.","nf":false,"l":"Do not build your own engine inside a first commercial product; ship on licensed tech and earn first.","i":"Each new layer (engine, scripting, IDE) added years before the game could earn, and a studio without income cannot absorb that. Cleveland's own verdict: the jury is still out on whether engine flexibility and modding will repay the lost time.","a":"Could we ship this first game on an existing engine instead?","q":"“Instead, we spent six tough years developing our own engine and almost going out of business multiple times.”","qa":"Charlie Cleveland, CEO of Unknown Worlds Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Unknown Worlds Entertainment's Natural Selection 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-unknown-worlds-entertainment-s-i-natural-selection-2-i-#:~:text=Instead%2C%20we%20spent%20six%20tough,of%20business%20multiple%20times","sd":"Feb 2013","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Hired early staff for skill and likability, not for working without managers","s":"Some talented hires needed structure the studio did not have and became demoralized. The founders noticed only low morale, and the unhappiness spread through the team.","w":"Charlie Cleveland and Max McGuire started Natural Selection 2 in earnest at the end of 2006 and began growing the team after angel funding in 2008. Unknown Worlds ran with no producer, schedule, milestones, meetings or job descriptions, and the core team was seven people in a room. Early on, it hired some talented people who did not fit. They were demoralized without structure, and the founders, focused on the game, saw only that morale was low. NS2 shipped on Steam on Halloween 2012. In February 2013 Cleveland wrote that interviews now test whether candidates expect structure.","r":"Low morale among mismatched hires spread through a small team.","re":"Interviews now tell candidates there are no managers and watch their reaction.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"In interviews, describe exactly how much direction the job gives, and watch how the candidate reacts.","i":"Without managers, each person has to choose their own work, and people who need direction stall and lose heart. With seven people in one room, one unhappy hire affects everyone. A studio with producers and task lists can absorb the same hire.","a":"Have we told candidates how little direction they will get?","q":"“They had the skills and we liked them as people, but we now realize that they were demoralized without structure.”","qa":"Charlie Cleveland, founder of Unknown Worlds Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Unknown Worlds Entertainment's Natural Selection 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-unknown-worlds-entertainment-s-i-natural-selection-2-i-#:~:text=They%20had%20the%20skills%20and,were%20demoralized%20without%20structure","sd":"Feb 2013","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"neveralone","n":"Never Alone (Kisima Ingitchuna)","dv":"Upper One Games","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/295790/","ls":"Steam","cv":"neveralone.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Switched launch platforms to Xbox One and PS4 with less than a year left","s":"Dev kits arrived 3 months late, a few weeks before a public E3 demo, and the console builds chased the PC build until launch. The team then pushed the release 2 weeks for extra polish.","w":"Work on Never Alone started in February 2012, aimed at Xbox 360, PlayStation 3 and Steam: platforms the team knew, with tens of millions of consoles in homes. As the audience widened, the team switched to Xbox One and PlayStation 4 with less than a year to launch. The dev kits took 3 more months to arrive, weeks before the E3 playable in Microsoft's booth. The console versions trailed the PC build until launch. The game shipped on November 18, 2014, after 31 months. In February 2015 lead designer Grant Roberts listed the late dev kits under what went wrong.","r":"Console builds lagged the PC build until launch.","re":"Kept scrambling on console builds until launch and took 2 extra weeks for polish.","co":"3 months waiting for dev kits; console catch-up work through launch; 2-week slip.","nf":false,"l":"Lock your launch platforms before production starts and order the dev kits the same day.","i":"Switching to new hardware late means waiting for scarce dev kits while content work runs at full speed, so console builds trail the PC build. The pain came from newly launched consoles the team had never shipped on; moving to familiar hardware risks less.","a":"Do we have dev kits for every launch platform today?","q":"“We never stopped having to scramble to get the console versions caught up to the current state of the PC build.”","qa":"Grant Roberts, lead game designer on Never Alone at E-Line Media","st":"Postmortem: E-Line Media and Upper One Games' Never Alone","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-e-line-media-and-upper-one-games-i-never-alone-i-#:~:text=We%20never%20stopped%20having%20to,of%20the%20PC%20build","sd":"Feb 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"neverwinternights","n":"Neverwinter Nights","dv":"BioWare","y":"2002","sc":"AAA","se":"Remaster 2018","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/704450/","ls":"Steam","cv":"neverwinternights.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"External commitments","t":"Announced the game early with a promised feature set and built many press demos","s":"Finishing the game with the promised features took a long time, and more demos than usual for a major release pulled focus and schedule from the team. BioWare judged the press coverage worth it.","w":"BioWare conceived Neverwinter Nights in 1997 and announced it early in development. In the years before release the team built more demos for trade shows and press visits than is usual for a major game. Each demo hit focus and schedule, and demos always took longer than planned. Completing the game with the promised feature set took a long time: just over 5 years from idea to completion, with a peak team of over 75 people. The game shipped in June 2002. In the November 2002 postmortem BioWare's founders and leads wrote that future projects would schedule demo time.","r":"Each demo took focus and schedule time from a 5-year project.","re":"Future BioWare projects plan demo time into the schedule from the start.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If you announce early, put every planned demo in the schedule with time to spare.","i":"An early announcement creates press demand, and each demo is a small ship that stops feature work. BioWare still found the coverage paid back in sales, so the fix is to budget demo time, not to refuse demos.","a":"How many demos are in our schedule, and how long is each?","q":"“Each time we built a demo there was an impact on the team in terms of both focus as well as schedule.”","qa":"Greg Zeschuk and Ray Muzyka, co-founders of BioWare, with Scott Greig, James Ohlen and Trent Oster","st":"Postmortem: Bioware's Neverwinter Nights","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-bioware-s-i-neverwinter-nights-i-#:~:text=Each%20time%20we%20built%20a,as%20well%20as%20schedule","sd":"Dec 2002","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Staffing","t":"Started art and design assets on day one while the new engine was unfinished","s":"Artists and designers worked from specs taken from early prototypes. Many specs changed during 3.5 years of engine work, so some assets were rebuilt or kept alive with code workarounds.","w":"BioWare conceived Neverwinter Nights in 1997 and built a new engine for it. Because of the schedule, art and design started on assets at the beginning, using technical specs taken from early prototypes. The programmers needed three and a half years to finish the game systems, and many specs changed on the way. Some assets were rebuilt, and code workarounds let old and new assets run together. The team peaked at over 75 people, 22 of them programmers. In the December 2002 postmortem the team called the ideal plan engine first, with a skeleton crew of artists and designers.","r":"Some assets were rebuilt, and others needed code workarounds.","re":"BioWare said it would schedule future new-engine projects with this in mind.","co":"Rebuilt assets and code workarounds for mixed asset versions.","nf":true,"l":"On a new engine, keep art and design to a skeleton crew making prototypes until the engine systems are done.","i":"Assets built to a prototype's specs carry that prototype's assumptions, and every spec change turns them into rework. BioWare saw this on new engines only: on an existing engine, art and design ran with little rework risk.","a":"Is our engine stable enough for full asset production to start?","q":"“Due to our schedule, we needed to start working on art and design assets right from the beginning of the project.”","qa":"Scott Greig and co-authors, BioWare","st":"Postmortem: Bioware's Neverwinter Nights","su":"https://gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-bioware-s-i-neverwinter-nights-i-#:~:text=Due%20to%20our%20schedule%2C%20we,beginning%20of%20the%20project","sd":"Dec 2002","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Designed every system for multiplayer, with single-player as a one-client game","s":"Each system took longer to build. In return, multiplayer integration took less time overall, and BioWare avoided the late multiplayer compromises it had made on Baldur's Gate.","w":"BioWare conceived Neverwinter Nights in 1997, partly inspired by early online games like Ultima Online. On Baldur's Gate the team had planned multiplayer from the start but programmed it late, and some parts worked poorly: every player had to watch every dialogue. This time multiplayer went into the original design. Even single-player ran as a multiplayer game with one client attached, so each system took longer to develop. The game shipped in 2002 after slightly more than 5 years. The December 2002 postmortem listed the approach under what went right.","r":"Systems took longer each, but total multiplayer integration time fell.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Extra development time on every game system.","nf":true,"l":"If the game has multiplayer, build every system on the networked model from the first prototype, single-player included.","i":"With single-player running as a one-client multiplayer game, there is one code path and nothing to retrofit late. It does not shrink testing: BioWare still needed 3 to 5 times more QA for multiplayer than for single-player.","a":"Does our single-player run on the same networked code as multiplayer?","q":"“Although this deep integration increased the time to develop each system (compared to a single-player-only system), it did result in an overall reduction in the time required to integrate multiplayer and ensured that all the systems were optimized for multiplayer play.”","qa":"Scott Greig and co-authors, BioWare","st":"Postmortem: Bioware's Neverwinter Nights","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-bioware-s-i-neverwinter-nights-i-#:~:text=Although%20this%20deep%20integration%20increased,optimized%20for%20multiplayer%20play","sd":"Dec 2002","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"nierautomata","n":"Nier: Automata","dv":"PlatinumGames","y":"2017","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/524220/","ls":"Steam","cv":"nierautomata.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Combined a smartphone pitch and a Vita remake offer into a new PlatinumGames sequel","s":"Square Enix expected about 300,000 sales after the first Nier fell short. Producer Yosuke Saito says the game exists only because PlatinumGames joined, and it passed 9 million copies by December 2024.","w":"By producer Yosuke Saito's account, the first Nier was not a commercial success, and fans rated its combat below its world and music. Director Yoko Taro pitched a smaller Nier at smartphone scale. At the same time, young PlatinumGames staff who loved the original offered to remake it on PS Vita. Saito merged the two into a proper new game, with Platinum on the action. Platinum then had to build genre mixes it was not used to. The game launched on 23 February 2017. In 2022 Saito said he had threatened to quit to get it approved.","r":"A full console sequel, with Platinum taking on genre mixes new to it.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Name the weakest part of your last game and pick the partner whose main strength is exactly that part.","i":"Saito kept the director, composer and character designer fans loved, and brought Platinum in for the combat they rated lowest. Two parts of the fit are rare: Platinum's own staff were fans, and approval still took Saito staking his job.","a":"Is our partner strongest where our last game was weakest?","q":"“The truth is that Nier Automata only really exists because PlatinumGames got involved.”","qa":"Yosuke Saito, producer at Square Enix","st":"Nier Automata Interview: Square's niche kings on their trip into a robotic future","su":"https://www.rpgsite.net/interview/5299-nier-automata-interview-squares-niche-kings-on-their-trip-into-a-robotic-future#:~:text=The%20truth%20is%20that%20Nier,because%20PlatinumGames%20got%20involved","sd":"Feb 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"nomanssky","n":"No Man's Sky","dv":"Hello Games","y":"2016","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/275850/","ls":"Steam","cv":"nomanssky.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Revealed No Man's Sky at VGX 2013 when the team was 4 people building tools","s":"The reaction set years of hype in motion. Hello Games kept discussing features still in development, and after the 2016 launch it stopped talking to the press.","w":"Sean Murray built the first technology alone in his spare time, then three more people joined. About 3 or 4 weeks before the VGX awards in late 2013, host Geoff Keighley offered to show whatever they had. The team was still building tools and technology, tried at the last moment to pull the trailer, and showed it anyway. Over the next years Murray talked to press about features still in development. The team averaged 6 people over five years and was 15 at the August 2016 launch. In 2018 Murray said they had shared too much, too early.","r":"A team averaging 6 people launched under deadlines it could not move.","re":"Hello Games stopped talking to the press and kept updating the game.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before a public reveal, decide which features you will discuss and describe only what already works in the build.","i":"A reveal turns a private plan into a public promise, and every interview adds to it. Players hear features in development as features that will ship. The reveal also kept the studio going: Murray says they would otherwise have given up at times.","a":"Can we ship everything we plan to show and describe?","q":"“Looking back, that was naive, and it was naive to talk so excitedly about the game, and it was naive to think that we could talk about features that were in development.”","qa":"Sean Murray, Managing Director of Hello Games","st":"We Spent an Hour Talking to Hello Games About Everything ‘No Man’s Sky’","su":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/we-spent-an-hour-talking-to-hello-games-about-everything-no-mans-sky/#:~:text=Looking%20back%2C%20that%20was%20naive,features%20that%20were%20in%20development","sd":"Jul 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"norco","n":"Norco","dv":"Geography of Robots","y":"2022","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1221250/","ls":"Steam","cv":"norco.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built Norco in Phaser.js for a short, 3-episode browser release","s":"When Raw Fury signed the game and wanted consoles, Phaser.js would have needed a custom console solution. Porting to Unity took about 3 months before work went on.","w":"Yuts, who is from Norco, Louisiana, started experimenting with the Phaser.js library in late 2015. NORCO began in Phaser as a small, quick game in 3 episodes that would run in a web browser. After the game signed with Raw Fury, the publisher wanted console releases, and staying in Phaser meant building a bespoke console solution. Yuts brought in Aaron Gray, and the 2 ported the game to Unity in about 3 months. The last year of development ran in Unity, and NORCO shipped on 24 March 2022. Yuts described the switch in a February 2023 interview.","r":"Console plans forced an engine port partway through development.","re":"Yuts brought in Aaron Gray to port the game to Unity.","co":"About 3 months for the Unity port.","nf":false,"l":"Before you pick an engine, list every platform a publisher might want and check that the engine can ship there.","i":"Phaser.js targets browsers, so the first console request turned the engine into a port. The port took only 3 months because NORCO is a 2D point-and-click game; the same switch costs more as the codebase grows.","a":"Can our engine ship on every platform a publisher might ask for?","q":"“Sticking with Phaser.js would have required a bespoke solution for consoles, so I enlisted the help of Aaron Gray to port the game to Unity.”","qa":"Yuts, developer of NORCO at Geography of Robots","st":"NORCO and capturing the spirit of Louisiana through sci-fi storytelling","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/-i-norco-i-and-capturing-the-spirit-of-louisiana-through-sci-fi-storytelling#:~:text=Sticking%20with%20Phaser.js%20would%20have,port%20the%20game%20to%20Unity","sd":"Feb 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"oceanhorn2","n":"Oceanhorn 2","dv":"Cornfox & Bros.","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2023","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1622710/","ls":"Steam","cv":"oceanhorn2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Picked Unreal Engine 4 over the studio's internal tech for the bigger sequel","s":"The 6-person team skipped building another engine and had prototype content running within a few days. Level designers could build small puzzles themselves in Blueprints.","w":"Cornfox & Bros made the first Oceanhorn, a top-down App Store adventure, with 3 people. The sequel moved the camera behind the player and needed a new combat system and full 3D freedom. When work on it began, the Helsinki studio chose Unreal Engine 4 over its internal tech so it would not spend time developing an engine. Prototype content ran within days. In February 2019 the team, then 6 people, described the switch on Epic's site. The game had been in development about 5 years when Apple came in for Apple Arcade.","r":"The team says it could focus on the game, not technical implementation.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When a sequel needs more than your in-house tech does, price a licensed engine against the months of engine work it replaces.","i":"A licensed engine moves a small team's hours from engine code to content and lets designers script their own puzzles. It does not shorten a schedule by itself: Oceanhorn 2 still spent about 5 years in development.","a":"How many months of engine work would a licensed engine save us?","q":"“We wanted to jump into developing the game itself.”","qa":"Heikki Repo, creative director and co-founder of Cornfox & Bros.","st":"Switching to Unreal Engine, Cornfox & Bros. is moving mobile gaming forward with Oceanhorn 2","su":"https://www.unrealengine.com/spotlights/switching-to-unreal-engine-cornfox-bros-is-moving-mobile-gaming-forward-with-oceanhorn-2#:~:text=We%20wanted%20to%20jump%20into,developing%20the%20game%20itself","sd":"Feb 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"octodaddadliestcatch","n":"Octodad: Dadliest Catch","dv":"Young Horses","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/224480/","ls":"Steam","cv":"octodaddadliestcatch.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Picked open-source Irrlicht and PhysX over Unity and UDK for the student prototype","s":"Licence savings were eaten by time spent adding basic features, but the game ran on PS4 in 4 weeks, months before Unity games could.","w":"In 2010 the DePaul student team built the Octodad prototype on Irrlicht and Havok, then swapped Havok for PhysX. Unity looked too expensive in pro licences for up to 18 people, and UDK's 25% royalty looked bad for future projects. The commercial sequel kept the same base. The team lost time adding basic engine features, which probably cancelled out the licence savings. In 2013 Sony offered an E3 stage slot if the game ran on PS4, and the first level was playable in 4 weeks. Sony went on to show it at over 20 events worldwide.","r":"The game ran on PS4 hardware in 4 weeks and made E3.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you pick an engine to save licence fees, weigh the savings against the time to build missing features and reach each platform.","i":"An open engine is code the team controls, so it could port to new hardware without waiting on a vendor. The same control meant building features Unity gives for free. The PS4 edge existed only because Unity's PS4 version was months away.","a":"Have we weighed licence savings against feature and porting time?","q":"“The upfront costs, such as licensing, were much smaller than had we gone with Unity, though the loss of time in adding basic features probably made any savings a wash.”","qa":"Kevin Geisler and Devon Scott-Tunkin, Young Horses","st":"Octodad: Dadliest Catch Post-Mortem Pt. 4: Tech","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/octodad-dadliest-catch-post-mortem-pt-4-tech#:~:text=The%20upfront%20costs%2C%20such%20as,any%20savings%20a%20wash","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Cut the student team from 18 to 8 and deferred all wages until launch","s":"Most of the team kept day jobs and averaged about 20 hours a week. The game took 2.5 years, and the team kept a larger share of sales.","w":"At GDC 2011, after the student Octodad made the IGF Student Showcase, the team chose to build a commercial sequel. It cut the team from 18 to 8, since so many people was too risky, and agreed an equal hourly wage paid only after launch. A Kickstarter in summer 2011 raised $24,320, enough for company costs but not living expenses. Most members kept full or part-time jobs. Octodad: Dadliest Catch shipped on January 30, 2014. Hours came close to the $400,000 estimate at $15 an hour; after good sales, backpay rose to $20, about $550,000 in total.","r":"8 part-timers equalled about 4 to 5 full-time people over 2.5 years.","re":"After launch, the whole studio quit their day jobs and went full-time.","co":"About $550,000 in deferred wages, and a longer schedule on part-time hours.","nf":false,"l":"Before you defer wages, measure how many hours a week people can give while holding day jobs, and schedule on that.","i":"Deferred pay keeps ownership, but it turns a full-time team into evening workers, so calendar time stretches. It held here because the team knew each other well and sales covered the backpay; the authors call that success uncommon.","a":"How many real hours a week can each person give us?","q":"“Because of that, the game took longer to make, but the financial risk we took on led to us keeping a higher percentage of sales.”","qa":"Kevin Geisler, programmer with production duties at Young Horses","st":"Octodad: Dadliest Catch Post-Mortem Pt. 2: Production","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/octodad-dadliest-catch-post-mortem-pt-2-production#:~:text=Because%20of%20that%2C%20the%20game,higher%20percentage%20of%20sales","sd":"Feb 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"okabu","n":"Okabu","dv":"HandCircus","y":"2011","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Took on PS3, a large 3D world, self-funding and self-publishing in one game","s":"Every area took more work than planned, and with no publisher nobody could check the estimates. The art and design schedule proved optimistic, and the end of development was very pressured.","w":"HandCircus had shipped two Rolando games on iPhone with publisher Ngmoco and wanted to think bigger. In autumn 2009 it began planning Okabu: its first console game, a narrative 3D world, self-funded and self-published. The plan had 4 worlds, 6 playable characters, 4 minigames, vehicles and 12 to 16 hours of play. With no console experience and no publisher, nobody could check the estimates. After 9 months of pre-production and 12 of production, Okabu launched on PSN in October 2011 after a very pressured finish. In April 2012 Simon Oliver wrote that they should have cut scope earlier.","r":"Art and design ran past the schedule, leaving a very pressured finish.","re":"The team pushed through and shipped on PSN in October 2011.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"List everything your team will do for the first time on this project, and delay all but one of those changes.","i":"Each new task carried its own estimating error, and with no console experience and no publisher, nobody could check the sum. One change at a time is manageable: Oliver says a single major change would only have been a challenge.","a":"How many things are we doing for the first time on this game?","q":"“Riding high off the successes of Rolando, we felt like any challenge could be overcome, and that each major change to the process could be dealt with once we got started.”","qa":"Simon Oliver, founder of HandCircus","st":"Postmortem: HandCircus' Okabu","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-handcircus-i-okabu-i-#:~:text=Riding%20high%20off%20the%20successes,once%20we%20got%20started","sd":"Apr 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Planned to run the PS3 game on the engine from its PC prototype","s":"At the move into full production, the engine could not deliver the large 3D worlds at 60 frames per second. The team switched to Sony's PhyreEngine, a major job nobody had planned for.","w":"Okabu started as a PC and Mac prototype on the open-source libraries Ogre and Bullet Physics, which gave HandCircus a playable build quickly. The plan assumed this PC-centric engine would be enough for PS3. After 9 months of pre-production, as full production began, the large 3D levels and the 60 fps target needed the PS3's SPUs. The team moved to Sony's PhyreEngine, and getting every feature stable on PS3 took a lot of work. Okabu shipped in October 2011. In April 2012 Simon Oliver wrote that an off-the-shelf engine would have covered most of those features.","r":"An unplanned engine switch to PhyreEngine during full production.","re":"The team moved to PhyreEngine to use the PS3's SPUs.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before production starts, run the prototype on the target console at the target frame rate with full-size levels.","i":"A PC prototype proves the game plays well, not that the console can run it. The gap showed only with large 3D levels at 60 fps on very different hardware; by Oliver's account a simple 2D port would not have been much of a problem.","a":"Has our prototype run on the target console at full frame rate?","q":"“This shift to PhyreEngine was obviously a major undertaking, and was not something that we'd factored in during the planning stages (we'd assumed that our PC-centric engine would have been sufficient).”","qa":"Simon Oliver, founder of HandCircus","st":"Postmortem: HandCircus' Okabu","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-handcircus-i-okabu-i-#:~:text=This%20shift%20to%20PhyreEngine%20was,would%20have%20been%20sufficient","sd":"Apr 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"okami","n":"Okami","dv":"Clover Studio","y":"2006","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2017","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/587620/","ls":"Steam","cv":"okami.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Dropped the realistic drawing concept after 2 months for an ink-painting art style","s":"The art style was fixed early, and drawing inside a 3D ink painting became the game's central idea. The game system itself still took much longer to come together.","w":"Director Hideki Kamiya started Okami at Clover Studio wanting to draw nature: players would create realistic drawings through gameplay. Producer Atsushi Inaba later said that concept was impossible on the consoles of the time. About 2 months into the project the team saw this and changed the art style. Out of that work came the idea of drawing a 3-dimensional ink painting. The art style stayed in place from then on, while the game system took much longer to settle. Okami launched on PlayStation 2 in Japan on 20 April 2006.","r":"The art style was settled in the first months of the project.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About 2 months on a concept the hardware could not run.","nf":false,"l":"In the first weeks, test your core visual idea on the target hardware, and change the art style if it fails.","i":"Okami's drawing mechanic ties the art style to the gameplay, so the hardware limit hit the core idea. Catching it in month 2 kept the loss small. An early art lock does not settle the game system, which here took much longer.","a":"Have we run our core visual idea on the target hardware yet?","q":"“About 2 months into the project we realized this and changed the art style accordingly.”","qa":"Atsushi Inaba, producer at PlatinumGames","st":"PG inside: Atsushi Inaba & Hideki Kamiya (PT. 2)","su":"https://www.platinumgames.com/official-blog/article/7064#:~:text=About%202%20months%20into%20the,changed%20the%20art%20style%20accordingly","sd":"Feb 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"operationflashpoint","n":"Operation Flashpoint","dv":"Bohemia Interactive","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/65790/","ls":"Steam","cv":"operationflashpoint.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Picked DirectPlay for multiplayer to avoid writing low-level network code","s":"Bugs in the API forced the team to write its own code without low-level control. Server slowdowns were hard to debug, and Windows-only servers ruled out Linux hosts.","w":"Bohemia built Operation Flashpoint as a single-player game first, with mission-based multiplayer sharing as much code as possible. The team already used Direct3D and DirectSound, so it chose DirectPlay to avoid low-level network code. The API had bugs that made high-level features almost unusable. Programmers wrote custom code for what DirectPlay should have provided, without the control they needed, and chased server slowdowns even with no traffic. The game shipped on 22 June 2001 and sold over 500,000 copies in 3 months. That December the lead programmer called DirectPlay one of the project's most unfortunate decisions.","r":"Multiplayer needed custom network code and long, hard debugging.","re":"The team wrote its own code for features DirectPlay failed to deliver.","co":"Custom network code and server slowdowns that were hard to debug.","nf":true,"l":"Before you build multiplayer on a middleware API, prototype dedicated servers and failure cases on it, on every server OS you plan to support.","i":"A high-level API saves work only while it does what it promises. When it fails, you rebuild its features without access to its internals. The lesson covers closed, poorly documented middleware, not high-level APIs in general.","a":"Have we run a dedicated server on this networking API yet?","q":"“Overall, selecting DirectPlay as our network API was one of the most unfortunate decisions in the whole game's development.”","qa":"Ondrej Spanel, lead programmer at Bohemia Interactive Studios","st":"Postmortem: Bohemia Interactive Studios' Operation Flashpoint","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-bohemia-interactive-studios-operation-flashpoint#:~:text=Overall%2C%20selecting%20DirectPlay%20as%20our,the%20whole%20game's%20development","sd":"Dec 2001","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"oriandtheblindforest","n":"Ori and the Blind Forest","dv":"Moon Studios","y":"2015","sc":"Indie","se":"Expanded edition 2016","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/261570/","ls":"Steam","cv":"oriandtheblindforest.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Chose Unity over an in-house engine so prototyping could start immediately","s":"Moon Studios skipped about a year of engine work. A playable 'Sein' prototype let Microsoft sit down and play what the team had made, and Ori shipped in March 2015 at 1080p and 60fps on Xbox One.","w":"Moon Studios formed in 2010 as a 'virtual studio'. The founders decided at once to use Unity rather than spend a year on their own tech, so they could prototype and pitch publishers quickly. A Metroidvania prototype called 'Sein' stood out, and Microsoft could sit down and play it. Mid-development, Microsoft let Moon change its contract and retarget from Xbox 360 to Xbox One and PC, and a Microsoft-Unity deal gave Moon access to Unity engineers for the port. Ori launched in March 2015 after about 4 years, became a critical darling and sold hundreds of thousands of copies.","r":"Prototype convinced both the team and Microsoft that the game was real.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"About a year of engine work avoided; development still took about 4 years.","nf":false,"l":"Pick an engine that lets you prototype right away, and pitch publishers with a playable build.","i":"Middleware moved the first playable forward by about a year, and a playable is what let Microsoft trust a scattered new studio. It did not shorten the 4-year build, which Mahler blames on a team kept too small for production.","a":"Can a publisher play our pitch rather than read it?","q":"“We did not want to invest a year of work into our tech before we could start creating.”","qa":"Thomas Mahler, CEO and Game Director at Moon Studios","st":"Postmortem: Moon Studios' heartfelt Ori and the Blind Forest","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-moon-studios-heartfelt-i-ori-and-the-blind-forest-i-#:~:text=We%20did%20not%20want%20to,we%20could%20start%20creating","sd":"May 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Had the animator make throwaway videos of Ori's moves before building in-game animations","s":"The whole team could watch the clips and pick the moves and abilities with the most potential. No full animation sets were built for ideas that would be dropped.","w":"Moon Studios started in 2010 as a small virtual studio making only prototypes; one of them, Sein, became Ori. Early on, animator James Benson made short videos of how Ori could move through the world, deal with enemies and use abilities planned for later in the game. Because they were only videos, the team did not have to commit to full in-game animation sets. Everyone at Moon watched them and chose the moves and abilities with the most potential. Ori shipped in March 2015 after about 4 years. In May 2015 Thomas Mahler compared the method to storyboarding gameplay.","r":"Moves and abilities were chosen before full in-game animation sets existed.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you animate a new move in-engine, have an animator make a quick video of it and let the team pick.","i":"A video needs no game code, state logic or tuning, so the team can drop an idea for the price of a clip. A video cannot show how a move feels on a controller: Moon still tested controls in gray-box builds first.","a":"Have we seen a video of this move before animating it in-game?","q":"“Since these were videos and we didn’t have to commit to a full set of in-game animations yet, we were able to have the entire team at Moon look at these videos and decide on which moves and abilities had the most potential.”","qa":"Thomas Mahler, CEO and game director of Moon Studios","st":"Postmortem: Moon Studios' heartfelt Ori and the Blind Forest","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-moon-studios-heartfelt-i-ori-and-the-blind-forest-i-#:~:text=Since%20these%20were%20videos%20and,abilities%20had%20the%20most%20potential","sd":"May 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"outcast","n":"Outcast","dv":"Appeal","y":"1999","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/336610/","ls":"Steam","cv":"outcast.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Built a working voxel-engine prototype before looking for a publisher to fund the game","s":"Ubisoft passed, but Infogrames signed within days. It bought the prototype assets, took worldwide publishing rights and handed over a 150,000 euro cheque to set up the studio.","w":"In 1994 the future Appeal founders were at Art & Magic, whose arcade games had disappointed. By early 1995 they had an alien-world concept and chose to prove it with a working prototype. They set up Appeal, and the hardware holding Themes funded the prototype for a large minority stake. Yves Grolet and programmer Philippe Zondack built the voxel engine while Franck Sauer made the first art. Ubisoft saw it and passed; Infogrames signed a few days after its meeting. Outcast shipped in 1999 after 4 years, on a total budget of about 1.5 million euros.","r":"Infogrames funded full production and published the game worldwide.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Themes took a large minority stake in Appeal to fund the prototype.","nf":true,"l":"Before pitching for full funding, build a prototype that runs your core technology, and show it to several publishers.","i":"A running engine showed publishers what a pitch document could not, and Infogrames wanted a technology-driven project at that moment. The same prototype did not move Ubisoft, so fit with a publisher's plans mattered as much as the build.","a":"Could a publisher play our core technology today?","q":"“Before we went back home, Bruno Bonnell gave us a 150k euros check, he signed on the corner of a table, to setup our new office and start working on the game, something he told us with a smile he had never done before.”","qa":"Franck Sauer, co-founder of Appeal","st":"Outcast","su":"https://www.francksauer.com/index.php/games?catid=15:published-games&id=47:outcast-pc&view=article#:~:text=Before%20we%20went%20back%20home,had%20never%20done%20before","sd":"","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"outerwilds","n":"Outer Wilds","dv":"Team Outer Wilds","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/753640/","ls":"Steam","cv":"outerwilds.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Rebuilt the whole Outer Wilds story as a 2D text adventure to test it","s":"Players could test the full mystery without learning to fly a spaceship. The demake showed the telescope worked better there, so the team changed it in the real game.","w":"Outer Wilds began in fall 2012 as Alex Beachum's master's thesis at USC. Its story is spread across planets that players reach only after learning to fly, so each test of whether players understood the clues took a long time. The team first made a paper prototype with note cards for locations. Beachum then rebuilt it in Processing as a 2D text adventure with probe, telescope and ship computer mechanics. In July 2015 he wrote that players used the telescope more there, so the real one would change. The 3D planets were still to be built.","r":"The whole narrative was testable before the 3D content was built.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you build the content, test the story structure in the cheapest form that keeps the clues and cuts the travel.","i":"When travel is a skill players must learn, every narrative test costs a long playthrough. Stripping travel out made each test fast. It suits clue-based structures; the demake also packed the story too tightly, so it could not test pacing.","a":"Can we test our whole story without playing the full game?","q":"“We needed a way for players to test the game's entire narrative structure in a fraction of the time it would take during a normal playthrough.”","qa":"Alex Beachum, creative director at Mobius Digital","st":"Demaking Outer Wilds","su":"https://www.mobiusdigitalgames.com/news/demaking-outer-wilds#:~:text=We%20needed%20a%20way%20for,during%20a%20normal%20playthrough","sd":"Jul 2015","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"outriders","n":"Outriders","dv":"People Can Fly","y":"2021","sc":"AAA","se":"Expanded edition 2022","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/680420/","ls":"Steam","cv":"outriders.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"External commitments","t":"Signed a publisher deal paying royalties only after the publisher recouped its costs","s":"Outriders launched on 1 April 2021, day one on Xbox Game Pass, with 3.5 million players in its first month. PCF got no royalties for the first quarter and no sales, revenue or cost data.","w":"On 16 February 2016 PCF Group signed a production and publishing agreement with Square Enix for Outriders. Royalties were due only once net sales revenue had returned the publisher's production, distribution and promotion costs to an agreed level. The game released on 1 April 2021, free to Xbox Game Pass subscribers on launch day, and over 3.5 million people played it in the first month. The 45-day payment window after the quarter ended on 14 August 2021 with no payment. On 16 August 2021 PCF reported that it had received no royalties and no sales, revenue or cost information.","r":"No royalties for the launch quarter and no sales data to plan with.","re":"PCF decided to also build projects whose IP it owns and publishes itself.","co":"0 royalties for Q2 2021 on an estimated 2 to 3 million copies sold.","nf":false,"l":"Before signing, model the recoupment waterfall against a day-one subscription launch and write sales-data access into the contract.","i":"Under a recoup-first deal the studio's income depends on the publisher's spending and sales choices, which PCF could neither see nor influence. The model still funded a 5-year AAA game; the price is that the studio can only plan on milestone income.","a":"Do we get sales data and a modelled recoupment point before signing?","q":"“Praca z Wydawcą ma bardzo wiele korzyści, ale też swoje wady – jedną z nich jest niewielki wpływ PCF na działania sprzedażowe oraz niekompletność czy też – jak w tym przypadku – brak danych uzyskiwanych od Wydawcy w tym zakresie.”","qa":"Sebastian Wojciechowski, CEO of PCF Group","st":"Po pierwszym kwartale sprzedaży gry Outriders PCF Group nie otrzymała tantiem od wydawcy","su":"https://www.biznesradar.pl/a/102652,po-pierwszym-kwartale-sprzedazy-gry-outriders-pcf-group-nie-otrzymala-tantiem-od-wydawcy#:~:text=Praca%20z%20Wydawc%C4%85%20ma%20bardzo,w%20tym%20zakresie","sd":"Aug 2021","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"overboard","n":"Overboard!","dv":"inkle","y":"2021","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1546920/","ls":"Steam","cv":"overboard.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Started the game as a speed test with reused code and little prototyping","s":"Overboard! went from first idea to finished game in about 100 days. Almost every planned player action made it in; the one feature that would have doubled development time was cut.","w":"After the 3D Heaven's Vault, which Jon Ingold calls often very frustrating to build, inkle began Overboard! as an experiment: how fast could it put a game together? The team reused a lot of old code, took only design decisions it was confident in and prototyped as little as possible. One untested idea, characters moving mid-scene, worked well enough to go in. A metal cross for bludgeoning people was cut because it would easily have doubled development time. The game took about 100 days and launched on Steam on 2 June 2021.","r":"About 100 days from first idea to finished game.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"One planned feature cut: the metal cross weapon.","nf":true,"l":"For a short project, use only code and design patterns your team has shipped before, and cut any feature that needs new systems.","i":"Speed came from removing unknowns: reused code and familiar design left little to discover. The method needs a mature toolset, such as the ink language inkle knows well, and a 2D game; Ingold says 3D is a million times harder to build for.","a":"Which parts of this game have we never built before?","q":"“To make that work we re-used a lot of old code, took design decisions we could be really confident with, and prototyped as little as possible.”","qa":"Jon Ingold, narrative director and co-founder of inkle","st":"Indie Developer Interview: inkle","su":"https://the-avocado.org/2021/07/02/indie-developer-interview-inkle/#:~:text=To%20make%20that%20work%20we,prototyped%20as%20little%20as%20possible","sd":"Jul 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"overkillsthewalkingdead","n":"Overkill's The Walking Dead","dv":"Overkill Software","y":"2018","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the game on the newly bought Valhalla engine, then switched to Unreal","s":"Developers told Eurogamer the switch scrapped two years of assets while the release date held. They described 100-hour weeks, and the game missed Starbreeze's sales expectations.","w":"Starbreeze paid SEK 73 million for the Valhalla engine and started the game on it. In August 2017, CEO Bo Andersson Klint announced a move to Unreal Engine 4, with Valhalla kept as a plugin. Eurogamer's sources said the switch threw away two years of assets, and the release date was set one year out. Developers worked 100-hour weeks for much of 2018, and the PC version launched that November. In February 2019, Skybound ended its contract with Starbreeze and the game was cancelled on all formats.","r":"Two years of assets were scrapped, and the release date was not moved.","re":"Starbreeze entered reconstruction and named Mikael Nermark acting CEO.","co":"SEK 73 million engine purchase; two years of assets scrapped; 100-hour weeks.","nf":false,"l":"After an engine switch, rebuild the schedule from the new engine's real velocity before you confirm the date.","i":"An engine switch resets tools, assets and the team's skills, so the old date no longer matches the work left. The switch itself can be the right call: here the developers had pushed for Unreal.","a":"Did we replan the date after the engine change, or just keep it?","q":"“The decision will not impact the release schedule for OTWD.”","qa":"Bo Andersson Klint, CEO of Starbreeze","st":"Starbreeze AB (publ) Interim Report January 2017 - June 30 2017","su":"https://prnewswire.co.uk/news-releases/starbreeze-ab-publ-interim-report-january-2017---june-30-2017-641595523.html#:~:text=The%20decision%20will%20not%20impact,release%20schedule%20for%20OTWD","sd":"Aug 2017","sy":"Company report","w8":3}]},{"k":"overwatch","n":"Overwatch","dv":"Blizzard","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2357570/","ls":"Steam","cv":"overwatch.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Hired about 70 environment artists in one year before Titan's art style was defined","s":"Leads had to invent busy work for idle specialists while the engine kept failing. One technical artist could use the editor for 20 of 40 hours, and Blizzard shut Titan down in 2013.","w":"Real development on Titan began around 2007, with a new engine, a new team and a new IP. Leadership hired ahead of need: in one year about 70 environment artists joined, before the story, world plan, art style or art tech were defined. Leads then made up busy work, such as which set a prop artist should build next. The engine did not work, and a technical artist logged only 20 working hours in the editor in a 40-hour week. Kaplan asked CEO Mike Morhaime to shut the team down in 2010, and Titan was cancelled in 2013.","r":"Specialists sat idle or on busy work while the team burned cash.","re":"After cancellation, Kaplan kept a core team and pitched a new game in 6 weeks.","co":"About 70 artists hired in a year; editor usable 20 of 40 hours.","nf":false,"l":"Keep the team to the smallest group until a prototype proves the idea, then hire the people it needs.","i":"Artists hired before the art style and tools exist have nothing real to build, so leads spend time inventing tasks instead of finding the game. The rule covers incubation: once the idea and pipeline are proven, late hiring becomes its own risk.","a":"Have we proven the idea before we add the next hire?","q":"“We don’t know technically how we’re gonna make the art or what the constraints of it are, but we know we’re gonna build a really big world, so let’s just start hiring environmental artists.”","qa":"Jeff Kaplan, part of Titan's team leadership at Blizzard","st":"Transcript for Jeff Kaplan: World of Warcraft, Overwatch, Blizzard, and Future of Gaming | Lex Fridman Podcast #493","su":"https://lexfridman.com/jeff-kaplan-transcript#:~:text=We%20don%E2%80%99t%20know%20technically%20how,start%20hiring%20environmental%20artists","sd":"Mar 2026","sy":"Participant interview","w8":5},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Gave the remaining Titan team 6 weeks to conceive a new game or disband","s":"Kaplan split the time into 3 concepts of 2 weeks each. One idea, built on Titan's concept art and shooter know-how, became Overwatch, due in May 2016.","w":"After six years of work, Blizzard cancelled Titan, a first-person MMO shooter, internally in spring 2013. Of its 140 developers, 80 went to other projects. The rest got 6 weeks to conceive a new game or be disbanded. Jeff Kaplan planned 3 concepts at 2 weeks each. In a meeting on the second, designer Geoff Goodman proposed dozens of classes with fewer abilities. Kaplan matched the idea to Arnold Tsang's Titan concept art, and Chris Metzen backed it at once. Overwatch was due on 24 May 2016 and won Game of the Year at the 2017 DICE Awards.","r":"The hero concept came out of the second of 3 planned concepts.","re":"Kaplan kept it first-person, building on the team's Titan shooter experience.","co":"80 of 140 developers moved to other projects; 6 weeks of concept work.","nf":false,"l":"When a big project fails its fun check, shrink the team and time-box a few concepts with a hard deadline.","i":"A hard deadline with disbandment at stake forced quick choices and stopped the team from rebuilding Titan. It worked because the smaller team kept Titan's art and shooter experience; a fresh team with nothing to reuse would need longer.","a":"Does each new concept have a fixed end date?","q":"“There was an intense amount of pressure on us,”","qa":"Jeff Kaplan, director of Overwatch at Blizzard Entertainment","st":"Extended Play: How Blizzard’s larger than life hero shooter Overwatch was born","su":"https://blog.playstation.com/archive/2017/05/24/extended-play-how-blizzards-larger-than-life-hero-shooter-overwatch-was-born/#:~:text=There%20was%20an%20intense%20amount,of%20pressure%20on%20us","sd":"May 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Scoped Titan as a next-generation WoW sequel with multiple modes in a new universe","s":"Blizzard ended up building two games in parallel; the engine was not where it needed to be and the team had grown too large to fix it. Titan was cancelled in 2014 and Overwatch came out of the wreckage.","w":"Titan was meant to be Blizzard's next-generation sequel to World of Warcraft: multiple modes in a brand-new universe. By Mike Morhaime's account Blizzard failed to control scope and was building two games in parallel. The team reported that the engine was not where it needed to be and asked for time to rebuild the tooling, but the team had grown too large to fix it while keeping everyone busy. The team got a couple of months on other ideas; one was Overwatch. Titan was cancelled in 2014 after seven years. Morhaime told the story at Gamelab Barcelona in June 2019.","r":"Titan was cancelled in 2014 after seven years in development.","re":"Gave the team a couple of months on other ideas; one became Overwatch.","co":"7 years of development on a cancelled game.","nf":false,"l":"If the engine is not ready, do not grow the team; a large team cannot fix the foundation while being kept busy.","i":"Scope that needs two games' worth of systems hides engine problems until the team is too big to pause. The rescue worked because Blizzard could afford to park the staff for a couple of months on new ideas; smaller studios have no such cushion.","a":"Is our engine ready for the content team we are about to hire?","q":"“We failed to control scope.”","qa":"Mike Morhaime, founder of Blizzard Entertainment","st":"Blizzard Founder Explains The Cancellation Of World Of Warcraft Follow-Up Titan","su":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/blizzard-explains-cancellation-of-world-of-warcraf/1100-6468080/#:~:text=We%20failed%20to%20control%20scope","sd":"Jun 2019","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"overwatch2","n":"Overwatch 2","dv":"Blizzard Entertainment","y":"2022","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2357570/","ls":"Steam","cv":"overwatch2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Followed the years-old plan to build PvE next and announced it at BlizzCon 2019","s":"More and more of the team moved off the live game to PvE. The scope kept growing, and in May 2023 Blizzard cut Hero Missions.","w":"The Overwatch team came out of the cancelled Project Titan with a plan: Overwatch first, then dedicated PvE, then an MMO. After Overwatch launched in 2016, the team stayed on that plan and moved more and more people onto PvE. At BlizzCon 2019, Blizzard announced Overwatch 2 with Story Missions and Hero Missions, a replayable mode with talent trees. Hero Missions struggled early, scope grew, and people kept coming off the live game. In May 2023 game director Aaron Keller cut Hero Missions, kept Story Missions for Season 6 and apologised for not pivoting sooner.","r":"Years of Hero Mission work were cancelled before release.","re":"Blizzard kept Story Missions for Season 6 and stopped pulling people from the live game.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"After a hit ships, re-plan: decide how many people the live game keeps before you staff and announce the next big mode.","i":"A roadmap written before launch cannot know the game will be a hit that needs people. Each move to the new mode drained the live game. The lesson fits live products; a finished single-player game has no live team to drain.","a":"How many people does the live game keep while we build this?","q":"“Instead, we stayed focused on a plan that was years old.”","qa":"Aaron Keller, Game Director of Overwatch 2 at Blizzard Entertainment","st":"Director’s Take – Reflecting on the future of Overwatch 2","su":"https://overwatch.blizzard.com/en-gb/news/23952480/#:~:text=Instead%2C%20we%20stayed%20focused%20on,plan%20that%20was%20years%20old","sd":"May 2023","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"owlboy","n":"Owlboy","dv":"D-Pad Studio","y":"2016","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/115800/","ls":"Steam","cv":"owlboy.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Kept redesigning and redrawing Owlboy to match a rising pixel-art bar instead of shipping","s":"Development ran nearly 10 years across a whole console generation. A finished dungeon was scrapped at a cost the team put at 1.5 years, and updates to side game Savant - Ascent took about another year.","w":"D-Pad Studio started Owlboy around 2007 on XNA for Xbox, at 800 by 600, and expected to ship in 2011. The 2011 demo drew feedback that pushed the team toward giant rooms and more polish, and the whole game was reprogrammed for widescreen. As pixel art moved on, the team kept raising its own bar, redesigning and redrawing several times. In 2013 it built Savant - Ascent in about 5 weeks; Madsen later estimated its updates cost Owlboy at least a year. A working third-guardian dungeon was scrapped, at least 1.5 years of work. Owlboy shipped on November 1, 2016.","r":"Nearly 10 years of development for a 5-person team.","re":"Set a deadline, cut the Advent city and the third dungeon, and shipped.","co":"About 10 years; a scrapped dungeon at least 1.5 years; Savant updates about a year.","nf":false,"l":"Freeze the visual bar and the ship date before production; chasing a moving market bar means redrawing finished content.","i":"A game that outlives a console generation competes with the art of games that started later, so each public showing resets what good enough looks like. The redraws were a choice to compete on art; a game selling on mechanics can freeze its look earlier.","a":"What is our ship date, and which assets are final today?","q":"“We ended up maybe overimproving on the game.”","qa":"Jo-Remi Madsen, programmer and co-founder of D-Pad Studio","st":"'Owlboy': The Evolving Art of a 10 Year Project","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rOkq7fUdgYQ&t=1601","sd":"Mar 2017","sy":"Participant talk","w8":3,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rOkq7fUdgYQ&t=745s"},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Story & world","t":"Built levels gameplay-first, with the story only a loose outline","s":"When the team found the story's red thread years later, the structure changed: areas were cut and dungeons restructured. Every area was remade several times, and little of the old demos survived.","w":"Simon Stafsnes Andersen started Owlboy as an XNA prototype for Xbox Live Arcade, nearly 10 years before launch, with a team that had no industry experience. The game was gameplay-first, with the story only a general outline. Andersen expected to ship in 2011, and the game earned a 2010 IGF nomination. Later the team found the red thread connecting the whole story, and story became a much larger focus. Areas were cut, dungeons were restructured and the gunners' role changed. Owlboy launched on 1 November 2016.","r":"Every area of the game was remade several times.","re":"Andersen says the next game will avoid a design where no part can be cut.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Lock the story's spine before you build final levels, so a late story idea does not reshape every area.","i":"In a Metroid-style game the story spine sets the map: which areas exist, in what order, with which abilities. Finding it late means rebuilding the map as well as the text. Story-light games with modular levels carry less of this risk.","a":"Do we know the story's spine well enough to lock the level structure?","q":"“Probably the biggest change came when we discovered the red thread that connects the entire story.”","qa":"Simon Stafsnes Andersen, creator and art director at D-Pad Studio","st":"[Interview] Owlboy dev on the game’s 10-year creation, Switch, and what’s next for D-Pad Studio","su":"https://nintendoeverything.com/interview-owlboy-dev-on-the-games-10-year-creation-switch-and-whats-next-for-d-pad-studio/#:~:text=Probably%20the%20biggest%20change%20came,connects%20the%20entire%20story","sd":"Mar 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Stayed on the homemade XNA engine because release always seemed close","s":"The engine's workflow stayed slow, and code from scrapped designs piled up as technical debt. Programming only stopped being the hard part once the team paid that debt upfront.","w":"D-Pad Studio built Owlboy on a homemade engine running on XNA, with homemade tools. As the scope grew and prototypes were scrapped at the last minute, the team always thought release was close. So it never rewrote the engine or moved to GameMaker or Unity, and only refined it step by step. The workflow the engine allowed was very slow, and repeated rebuilds left code that often stitched 3 designs together. Once the team started paying its technical debt upfront, the hard problems became design and morale. Owlboy shipped on PC on 1 November 2016.","r":"Workflow stayed slow and technical debt piled up.","re":"The team started paying its technical debt upfront.","co":"Slow engine workflow and code built from up to 3 designs sewn together.","nf":true,"l":"Each time the release date slips, recheck whether a rewrite or engine switch pays back over the time really left.","i":"Each almost-done estimate made a rewrite look too expensive, so the debt grew with every redesign. The trap belongs to projects whose end date keeps moving; on a fixed scope, step-by-step refinement of a working engine is often the cheaper call.","a":"Would a rewrite pay off over the time we really have left?","q":"“We always thought that we were fairly close to release because the scope of the game kept growing and prototypes kept being scrapped at the last minute, so we never thought we had time to do a full rewrite of our engine, or that we had time to switch to some third-party engine like GameMaker or Unity.”","qa":"Henrik Stafsnes Andersen, gameplay and technical programmer at D-Pad Studio","st":"INTERVIEW – In Conversation With D-Pad Studio (Creators of Owlboy)","su":"https://alternativemagazineonline.co.uk/2017/12/05/interview-in-conversation-with-d-pad-studio-creators-of-owlboy/#:~:text=We%20always%20thought%20that%20we,GameMaker%20or%20Unity","sd":"Dec 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"palworld","n":"Palworld","dv":"Pocketpair","y":"2024","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2024","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1623730/","ls":"Steam","cv":"palworld.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Made a 3-month trailer first and let the audience reaction decide the budget","s":"The June 2021 INDIE Live Expo reaction was far stronger than expected, so a 4-person side project became the studio's main bet. Palworld reached Early Access in January 2024 with 1.8 million Steam wishlists.","w":"Pocketpair, then about 10 people, started Palworld with a new team of about 4 while the rest kept working on Craftopia. CEO Takuro Mizobe began with a trailer for an event: if the reaction was poor, the game was not worth a budget. The team built it in about 3 months and showed it at INDIE Live Expo in June 2021. The response from Japan and abroad far exceeded expectations, so development started in earnest. The 1-year plan became about 3 years and more than 40 hires. Palworld entered Early Access on 19 January 2024 with 1.8 million Steam wishlists.","r":"The trailer reaction turned a 4-person side project into the studio's main bet.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"About 3 months of a 4-person team before any budget was committed.","nf":false,"l":"Before committing a budget, test demand with a cheap trailer or demo and decide in advance what reaction means go.","i":"A trailer measures demand before money is spent, and a strong signal justified a bigger bet than planned. The gate only tells you people want the game; by Mizobe's own account it did nothing to size the plan, which ran 3 times longer.","a":"What audience reaction would make us cancel this before budgeting it?","q":"“トレイラーの反響が悪かったならば、そもそも予算を掛けて作るほどのゲームではないのだ。”","qa":"Takuro Mizobe, CEO of Pocketpair","st":"3日後に命運が決まる、パルワールドという偶然の物語","su":"https://note.com/pocketpair/n/n54f674cccc40#:~:text=%E3%83%88%E3%83%AC%E3%82%A4%E3%83%A9%E3%83%BC%E3%81%AE%E5%8F%8D%E9%9F%BF%E3%81%8C%E6%82%AA%E3%81%8B%E3%81%A3%E3%81%9F%E3%81%AA%E3%82%89%E3%81%B0,%E3%82%B2%E3%83%BC%E3%83%A0%E3%81%A7%E3%81%AF%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84%E3%81%AE%E3%81%A0","sd":"Jan 2024","sy":"Participant account","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Switched from Unity to Unreal Engine 4 on one freelance engineer's word, discarding all code","s":"Nobody in the studio knew Unreal, so everything restarted from zero, including version control. The planned 1 year became about 3 years, 40-plus hires and roughly 1 billion yen, with the bank balance as the only budget cap.","w":"Just before the first trailer went public in June 2021, a freelance engineer with 10 years of experience but none in Unity asked to join. Palworld then had 2 engineers. Mizobe weighed 3 options and chose to trust him and rebuild Palworld from zero in Unreal Engine 4. All existing code and the Unity-dependent purchased animations were dropped; version control moved from git to svn. Later hires had no Unreal experience either. By August 2023 dedicated servers still did not work, and a November 2023 network test became the real deadline. Early Access came on 19 January 2024.","r":"A full rebuild; the 1-year plan became about 3 years with 40-plus hires.","re":"A November 2023 network test with real players became the forcing deadline.","co":"About 1 billion yen and roughly 3 years; nearly all the company's cash.","nf":false,"l":"If you change engines after code exists, re-plan the project as a restart and hire or train engine experience before you rebuild.","i":"An engine change is almost a complete rebuild, as Mizobe writes, and with no Unreal experience on staff every hire learned on the project. The trailer's demand signal is what made the gamble survivable; without it the same choices read as a classic overrun.","a":"If we switch engines now, what is our real restart date?","q":"“要するに、エンジン移行というのは、ほぼ丸ごと作り直しなのだ。”","qa":"Takuro Mizobe, CEO of Pocketpair","st":"3日後に命運が決まる、パルワールドという偶然の物語","su":"https://note.com/pocketpair/n/n54f674cccc40#:~:text=%E8%A6%81%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B%E3%81%AB%E3%80%81%E3%82%A8%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B8%E3%83%B3%E7%A7%BB%E8%A1%8C%E3%81%A8%E3%81%84%E3%81%86%E3%81%AE%E3%81%AF,%E4%B8%B8%E3%81%94%E3%81%A8%E4%BD%9C%E3%82%8A%E7%9B%B4%E3%81%97%E3%81%AA%E3%81%AE%E3%81%A0","sd":"Jan 2024","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"papersplease","n":"Papers, Please","dv":"Lucas Pope","y":"2013","sc":"Solo","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/239030/","ls":"Steam","cv":"papersplease.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Let one developer's resources set the look, music and content model before design","s":"Lucas Pope said the low resolution let him make visuals and animations very quickly, ambient sound replaced a full score, and the world grew only as mechanics needed it. First mockups to release took about 9 months.","w":"Lucas Pope started Papers, Please from mockups in late 2012 as a solo developer, after noticing the document checks immigration inspectors do. He said a large part of production was dictated by his lack of resources: he could not fill the game with music, so he mixed ambient and triggered sounds, and the low resolution let him create visuals and animations very quickly. He knew only that two bordering countries had a strained relationship and wrote the rest as mechanics required. Each day has 2 or 3 scripted immigrants; the rest are procedural. It released on 8 August 2013.","r":"A solo developer shipped in about 9 months and kept working on it afterwards.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About 9 months solo to release; 14 months and counting by February 2014.","nf":false,"l":"Set the visual bar, audio plan and content model from the resources you actually have before you design any content.","i":"Every content unit inherits the starting bar, so a low-resolution look and a procedural generator made each new day cheap for one person. Pope says the constraints also gave the bleak mood he wanted. It suits a game whose feel survives low fidelity.","a":"Does our visual bar match the team we actually have?","q":"“A large part of the production was dictated by my lack of resources.”","qa":"Lucas Pope, developer of Papers, Please","st":"Road to the IGF: Lucas Pope's Papers, Please","su":"https://gamedeveloper.com/design/road-to-the-igf-lucas-pope-s-i-papers-please-i-#:~:text=A%20large%20part%20of%20the,my%20lack%20of%20resources","sd":"Feb 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Pushed localization of Papers, Please until after the English launch","s":"Text baked into the document images later needed a custom localization tool. The version in 7 languages came out on 12 February 2014, nearly 6 months after launch.","w":"Lucas Pope made Papers, Please alone and decided to leave localization until after release. He was unsure it would pay, had no time to set up a system, and could iterate faster on English-only documents in Photoshop. He still moved some text into data, cut planned text and kept layouts simple. The game shipped in English on 8 August 2013. After a month of bug fixing, he restructured code and data, and about 4 months of work produced a WYSIWYG tool to re-letter the documents. The version in 7 languages came out on 12 February 2014.","r":"Localized versions arrived nearly 6 months after the English launch.","re":"Pope built string extractors and a web-based tool to re-letter documents.","co":"About 4 months of localization work; 7 languages nearly 6 months after launch.","nf":false,"l":"If you defer localization, count the text baked into images and fonts now and budget months to redo it later.","i":"Deferring kept a solo developer iterating fast on English documents, and the work came due as a separate 4-month project. It works only if the game can launch in one language: a Russian producer wanted Russian at launch, and Pope could not deliver it.","a":"How much of our text is baked into images or fonts?","q":"“In the end, that process took 4 months so in retrospect I’m glad I didn’t try to fit that into the initial release.”","qa":"Lucas Pope, solo developer of Papers, Please","st":"Localizing 'Papers, Please' - Part 1","su":"https://dukope.com/devlogs/papers-please/localizing-1/#:~:text=In%20the%20end%2C%20that%20process,into%20the%20initial%20release","sd":"Apr 2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"pathologic2","n":"Pathologic 2","dv":"Ice-Pick Lodge","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/505230/","ls":"Steam","cv":"pathologic2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Skipped prototyping the remake because the 2005 original was treated as the prototype","s":"The first two years went on a foundation and untested art, development had to start again, and only 1 of 3 promised campaigns shipped in May 2019, about 3 years late.","w":"In 2014 Ice-Pick Lodge raised $333,127 on a $250,000 Kickstarter goal, with release set for 2016. Dybovsky thought prototyping gameplay and script unnecessary because the 2005 game was the prototype, so the first two years went into an over-complex foundation and graphic content never tested. In April 2015 an investor withdrew a $2 million deal. After Classic HD, the team concluded it could not remake the game and started again; tinyBuild stepped in with money. Only the Haruspex campaign shipped in May 2019; that August Dybovsky called sales unsatisfactory and expected the team to shrink to 4 or 5 people.","r":"Development started again, and 2 of 3 campaigns were pushed to later.","re":"tinyBuild came in with money and outside producing; the game was split into campaigns.","co":"Two years lost; release planned for 2016, shipped May 2019 with 1 of 3 campaigns.","nf":false,"l":"Prototype the new loop and script of a remake before building foundations; the old game is not your prototype.","i":"Dybovsky thought the game would barely change, so the team built an over-complex gameplay and narrative base and art nobody had tested. Once the remake became a new game, that base did not fit. Only a remake that keeps the old design can skip prototyping.","a":"Have we prototyped the new loop, or are we trusting the original?","q":"“Я вначале считал, что прототипирование геймплея и сценария вообще не нужно, потому что игра 2005 года, по сути, и является готовым прототипом: мне тогда казалось, что игра почти не изменится.”","qa":"Nikolai Dybovsky, head of studio, director and lead writer, Ice-Pick Lodge","st":"«Иногда выгоднее проиграть войну, чем выиграть её»: большое интервью с Ice-Pick Lodge о «Море»","su":"https://dtf.ru/gamedev/65410-inogda-vygodnee-proigrat-voynu-chem-vyigrat-ee-bolshoe-intervyu-s-ice-pick-lodge-o-more#:~:text=%D0%AF%20%D0%B2%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5%20%D1%81%D1%87%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BB%2C%20%D1%87%D1%82%D0%BE%20%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5,%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%87%D1%82%D0%B8%20%D0%BD%D0%B5%20%D0%B8%D0%B7%D0%BC%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%81%D1%8F","sd":"Aug 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":5}]},{"k":"pentiment","n":"Pentiment","dv":"Obsidian Entertainment","y":"2022","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1205520/","ls":"Steam","cv":"pentiment.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Kept most scenes as uncolored rough sketches in-game while the story took shape","s":"The team of about 13 could rework scenes cheaply and cover more content. It took much longer to learn what a finished scene cost and to budget the final schedule.","w":"Obsidian built Pentiment, a 2D game in the style of European block prints, with about 13 developers at peak. At that size, narrative and art moved forward together, with no dedicated story preproduction. The hand-drawn final art was too slow to redo after every story and design change. So the team put simple, uncolored roughs of each scene in the game while it worked out quests and story. By the prototype, only a handful of scenes were finished. The game shipped in November 2022, and in January 2023 art director Hannah Kennedy judged the risk worth it.","r":"Scenes stayed cheap to change, but finished-scene cost was learned late.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When most scenes stay rough, time every scene you finish and rebuild the art schedule from those numbers.","i":"Rough scenes are cheap to throw away, so art could follow a story that was still moving. The price is a schedule that rests on a guess until enough scenes are final; Kennedy ties the benefit to a team of about 13.","a":"Do we know how long one finished scene takes?","q":"“It made it take a lot longer to understand what the time cost of a finished scene was and to budget in the final schedule accordingly, but the benefits outweighed those risks.”","qa":"Hannah Kennedy, art director of Pentiment at Obsidian Entertainment","st":"Deep Dive: Behind the evocative medieval manuscript art of Pentiment","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/art/deep-dive-the-art-of-pentiment#:~:text=It%20made%20it%20take%20a,benefits%20outweighed%20those%20risks","sd":"Jan 2023","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"penumbraoverture","n":"Penumbra: Overture","dv":"Frictional Games","y":"2007","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/22180/","ls":"Steam","cv":"penumbraoverture.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Extended a drawer physics fix to every interaction in the student prototype","s":"Emergent physics puzzles made each level far slower to design. The team cut its multi-level school game down to a small demo.","w":"Penumbra began as a school project: a survival horror game with no direct violence. The team wanted players to search drawers for clues, and used its Newton physics engine so items would slide with the drawer. After testing it, they chose physics for all interaction and redesigned large parts of the game. Emergent puzzles were much harder than expected, and levels took far longer to design. The plan shrank to a small demo for a Swedish game competition. Released online, the demo passed 250,000 downloads, and in August 2006 Frictional announced the commercial Penumbra: Overture for 2007.","r":"The planned multi-level game shrank to a small demo.","re":"The team released the demo online, then built the commercial Penumbra: Overture.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before one system drives every puzzle, build a full level with it and time how long the design takes.","i":"Open-ended physics puzzles have to hold up for any approach a player tries, so each level needs more design. The cost sits in the emergent puzzles, not in physics handling itself, which Frictional kept for Overture.","a":"How long did one full level take to design with this system?","q":"“This was a lot harder than we first thought and the levels took a lot longer to design and led us to limiting the scope of the project.”","qa":"Thomas Grip, programmer and project lead at Frictional Games","st":"Thomas Grip, Programmer/Project Lead of Penumbra: Overture Interview","su":"https://www.justadventure.com/2006/12/11/thomas-grip-programmer-project-lead-of-penumbra-overture-int/#:~:text=This%20was%20a%20lot%20harder,the%20scope%20of%20the%20project","sd":"Dec 2006","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"pcfselfpublishingportfolio","n":"People Can Fly self-publishing portfolio (Dagger, Bifrost, Victoria, Red)","dv":"People Can Fly","y":"2025","sc":"AAA","se":"Early Access 2025","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Committed to self-funding several own-IP games at once while most were in pre-production","s":"The roughly PLN 350 million needed to keep that scale was never raised. By June 2025 three of the four projects were abandoned or suspended.","w":"On January 31, 2023, PCF Group set out to self-publish its own games next to its Gemini work-for-hire deal with Square Enix. Targets were Dagger 50 to 140 people, Bifrost 70 to 150 and Victoria 30 to 120; Red was a concept. PCF said many games at once would limit self-publishing risk. Dagger was abandoned in April 2024 and Red in September 2024. In December 2024 a strategic review ended without about PLN 350 million of new financing. Victoria was suspended and Bifrost cut. When Gemini's next contract did not arrive, Bifrost was suspended in June 2025.","r":"Three of four own-IP projects were abandoned or suspended by June 2025.","re":"PCF restarted Victoria with over 10 people and a revised scope.","co":"Write-offs of PLN 68.3m (Dagger), 7.7m (Red) and 92.0m (Lost Rift unit).","nf":false,"l":"Before ramping several self-funded games, secure the money for each one's next gate without counting on one client contract.","i":"Parallel projects spread market risk but multiply cash burn, and this plan depended on new financing. When the money did not come and Gemini stalled, every own project lost funding at once. Diversification lowers risk only when each project is already funded.","a":"Is each project funded to its next gate without new money?","q":"“We are aware that this model carries greater risk, but we can mitigate the risk by diversifying the portfolio of games and developing multiple games simultaneously.”","qa":"Vladimir Makarychev, head of publishing at People Can Fly (PCF Group)","st":"PCF GROUP S.A. & PEOPLE CAN FLY GROUP | AKTUALIZACJA STRATEGII | NOWE OTWARCIE","su":"https://peoplecanfly.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/20230131-pcf-group-sa-aktualizacja-strategii-nowe-otwarcie.pdf#:~:text=PCF%20planuje%20ogranicza%C4%87%20ryzyka,nad%20wieloma%20grami%20jednocze%C5%9Bnie","sd":"Jan 2023","sy":"Company report","w8":4,"qu":"https://peoplecanfly.com/new-opening/#:~:text=We%20are%20aware%20that%20this%20model%20carries%20greater%20risk"}]},{"k":"perfectdark","n":"Perfect Dark","dv":"The Initiative","y":"2025","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2025","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Paired the new studio with co-developer Certain Affinity before fixing the game's vision","s":"The partners split after a 2021 proof of concept, about half the studio left, and the game was rebuilt in Unreal Engine 5. Microsoft cancelled it in 2025.","w":"Microsoft set up The Initiative in 2018 with industry veterans under Darrell Gallagher, who said the studio set out to use co-development partnerships. It teamed up with Certain Affinity, and Perfect Dark was revealed at The Game Awards in 2020. A 2023 report said leadership would not commit to a shape for the game, and the partners split after a 2021 proof of concept. It counted 47 exits in 2021 and early 2022. Crystal Dynamics joined in September 2021, and the game was rebuilt in Unreal Engine 5 in 2022. In July 2025 Microsoft cancelled it and closed the studio.","r":"No game shipped; Microsoft closed the studio after 7 years.","re":"Crystal Dynamics took growing control, and the game restarted in Unreal Engine 5.","co":"About half the studio left; 7 years with no release.","nf":false,"l":"Fix the game's vision in a document and a playable slice before you bring in a co-development partner.","i":"A partner used to building from a clear spec needs a fixed vision, and a new studio still searching for one gives it nothing to build. Matt Booty later drew the line between partners with creative DNA and partners used to outsourcing work.","a":"Could a partner build from our vision document today without asking us?","q":"“One of the things we learned on Perfect Dark is there is a difference between studios that have that creative DNA versus studios that have done more traditionally outsourcing, no matter how complex, and we just had to find the right rhythm there.”","qa":"Matt Booty, head of Xbox Game Studios","st":"Report: Xbox exclusive Perfect Dark's release is 'years away' due to development struggles","su":"https://www.windowscentral.com/gaming/xbox/report-xbox-exclusive-perfect-darks-release-is-years-away-due-to-development-struggles#:~:text=One%20of%20the%20things%20we,find%20the%20right%20rhythm%20there","sd":"Jun 2023","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"periachronicles","n":"Peria Chronicles","dv":"Thingsoft","y":"2019","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2019","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Bet an MMO on extreme player freedom and first tested it externally in year 9","s":"ThisIsGame reported that closed tests promised for 2013, 2014 and 2017 never happened. The first and only test, 3 days in May 2019, drew complaints about dated graphics and low completeness, and Nexon cancelled the project on 27 August 2019.","w":"Jeong Sang-won founded Thingsoft in February 2010 and revealed Project NT in June 2011 with extreme freedom as its concept: players reshape terrain, build towns and write laws. Nexon became publisher in October 2012 and bought the studio in September 2013. A closed test was promised for 2013, then 2014, then 2017; none happened. A G-Star 2016 demo drew two-hour queues and complaints that the game was far too different from existing games. The first closed test, 3 days in May 2019, was judged old-fashioned and unfinished. On 27 August 2019 Nexon cancelled it, saying it could not satisfy users.","r":"Cancelled after about nine years without a public release; the studio founder resigned.","re":"Cancelled the project outright; Jeong Sang-won resigned as Nexon's development chief.","co":"About 9 years of development, 2010 to 2019, with no release.","nf":false,"l":"Put an unusual core loop in front of outside players in the first year, not the ninth.","i":"A sandbox built on systems players have never seen cannot be judged from inside the team; only external tests show whether the freedom is fun. Each postponed test let more content stack on an unproven core. In a known genre, late testing costs far less.","a":"When do outside players first touch our core loop?","q":"“그동안 여러 차례 <페리아연대기> 내부 및 외부 테스트를 진행하며 게임성을 점검해왔으나, 테스트 단계인 <페리아연대기>가 유저를 만족시킬 수 없다고 최종 결론을 내렸고 결국 프로젝트를 취소했다” (We had checked the game's quality through several internal and external tests of Peria Chronicles, but we reached the final conclusion that Peria Chronicles, at the test stage, could not satisfy users, and in the end cancelled the project.)","qa":"Nexon, company statement quoted by ThisIsGame","st":"프로젝트 NT부터 개발중단까지… 페리아 연대기 9년의 기록","su":"https://www.thisisgame.com/articles/97382#:~:text=%EA%B7%B8%EB%8F%99%EC%95%88%20%EC%97%AC%EB%9F%AC%20%EC%B0%A8%EB%A1%80,%ED%94%84%EB%A1%9C%EC%A0%9D%ED%8A%B8%EB%A5%BC%20%EC%B7%A8%EC%86%8C%ED%96%88%EB%8B%A4","sd":"Aug 2019","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"persona4","n":"Persona 4","dv":"Atlus","y":"2008","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1113000/","ls":"Steam","cv":"persona4.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Tied every dungeon's time limit to in-game weather the player could not predict","s":"Players rushed every dungeon, and the social side of the game fell out of balance. Each weather fix held up a large amount of dependent data and created work nobody had planned.","w":"Atlus built Persona 4 on the Persona 3 base and added one new rule: each dungeon's time limit depended on the in-game weather. The team expected urgency, since players never knew when the damaging fog would arrive. Once it was in the game, players cleared dungeons as fast as they could, and dungeons and Social Links split apart. The team adjusted the weather, in-game messages and story progression to compensate. Every change moved the weather again, and a large amount of data waited on it. The October 2009 postmortem lists it under What Went Wrong.","r":"Dungeons and Social Links fell out of balance, and dependent data stayed open.","re":"The team kept adjusting weather, in-game messages and story progression.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Test a timer or weather rule with players before writers and designers build data that depends on it.","i":"The weather fed scripts, messages and story beats, so every tuning pass reopened finished data. The trap applies to rules that much content depends on; a self-contained mechanic can be tuned late at low cost.","a":"Which content is waiting on a rule we are still tuning?","q":"“A huge amount of data could not be finalized until the weather was set, but the weather kept changing due to our design adjustments.”","qa":"Persona 4 development team at Atlus","st":"Exclusive: Behind The Scenes Of Atlus' Persona 4","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/exclusive-behind-the-scenes-of-atlus-i-persona-4-i-#:~:text=A%20huge%20amount%20of%20data%20could,due%20to%20our%20design%20adjustments","sd":"Oct 2009","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"phantasystaronline","n":"Phantasy Star Online","dv":"Sonic Team","y":"2000","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Held the project start until network experiments showed online play could work","s":"The experiments, begun right after Sonic Adventure, made the network the base of the game before its genre was even chosen. By February 2001 Sega reported nearly 100,000 registered Japanese users.","w":"The Dreamcast finally put a modem in the box, and Sonic Team wanted a game built on it. Right after Sonic Adventure was done, the team started network experiments, and Yuji Naka spent a lot of time researching networking. The team agreed the game would use the network as its basis before it knew the genre. Naka started the project only once he felt it could work. Takao Miyoshi's team then built play around strangers sharing one goal: adventuring together. In February 2001, about 2 years after the first experiments, Naka said the game was doing really well in Japan.","r":"The network became the base of the design before any genre was set.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before starting a game that depends on one new technology, run experiments until you can say it works, or do not start.","i":"Online play was the whole premise, so a network that failed meant no game at all. A test-first gate fits a game with one make-or-break dependency; a game with many smaller unknowns needs a different check.","a":"Have we proved the one technology this game cannot exist without?","q":"“Only after I felt, \"OK, this could work\" did we actually begin the project.”","qa":"Yuji Naka, producer and head of Sonic Team at Sega","st":"Yuji Naka – 2000 Developer Interview","su":"https://shmuplations.com/yujinaka/#:~:text=Only%20after%20I%20felt,begin%20the%20project","sd":"2000, reprinted Apr 2025","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"pikmin4","n":"Pikmin 4","dv":"Nintendo","y":"2023","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Made player choice of order and approach a goal from the start of development","s":"Supporting many playstyles meant far more content: Pikmin 4 has more collectible items than all earlier Pikmin games combined. The chief director replayed Story mode about 20 times while debugging.","w":"From the beginning of development, the Pikmin 4 team set out to support whatever players came up with. Chief director Yuji Kando compared it to Zelda breaking its conventions: players, not the game, choose the order in which they meet each Pikmin type. Planning director Yutaka Hiramuki said the team had to increase the volume of content to make that work. While debugging, Kando finished Story mode around 20 times. In the July 2023 interview, days before the 21 July launch, Hiramuki gave the scale: more collectibles than all previous titles combined.","r":"The collectible count grew beyond all earlier games combined.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"More collectibles than all previous Pikmin games combined; Story mode debugged about 20 times.","nf":false,"l":"When players can pick the order, count the extra content each possible order needs before you lock the scope.","i":"Each route a player can take needs its own rewards and reasons to keep going, so content grows with the number of routes. A linear game can reuse one sequence of rewards; open order cannot.","a":"How much extra content does open order add to our plan?","q":"“The amount of things you can collect in Pikmin 4 is more than the number of items in all the previous titles combined.”","qa":"Yutaka Hiramuki, planning director of Pikmin 4 at Nintendo","st":"Ask the Developer Vol. 10, Pikmin 4—Part 2","su":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/whatsnew/ask-the-developer-vol-10-pikmin-4-part-2/#:~:text=The%20amount%20of%20things%20you,all%20the%20previous%20titles%20combined","sd":"Jul 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"plantsvszombies3","n":"Plants vs. Zombies 3","dv":"PopCap Games","y":"","sc":"AAA","se":"Early Access 2026","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Changed the series formula to portrait 3D, faster combat and no required Sunflowers","s":"After the 2020 soft launch PopCap pulled the game and started again from the basics. Six years later it was still in limited early access.","w":"PvZ3 entered soft launch in February 2020. Executive producer Bruce Maclean described the aims: social play, faster combat and deeper plant synergies, in portrait mode with 3D art and no need to plant Sunflowers for sun. In October 2020 PopCap pulled it to reimagine a few things. In September 2021 a pre-alpha returned, and game director Matt Townsend said the team had gone back to its roots. A January 2024 soft launch was pulled in October 2024 for an overhaul. In April 2026 PvZ3: Evolved entered early access in 2 countries.","r":"The game went back to pre-alpha and missed a global launch for years.","re":"PopCap restarted from the series' basics and kept testing in small regions.","co":"Over 6 years of soft launches, 2020 to 2026, with no global release.","nf":false,"l":"When a sequel changes how the series feels, test those changes with series fans in a prototype, before soft launch.","i":"Fans judge a sequel against the game they already love, so changes to its feel meet resistance. A soft launch finds that out after the game is built, which forces a restart. A new game without a fan base has more room to change.","a":"Have fans of the original played our changes yet?","q":"“So we took the time to start at the basics and brought back things that made the original game great.”","qa":"Matt Townsend, game director at EA Mobile","st":"PopCap resurrects Plants vs. Zombies 3 - now back in soft launch","su":"https://www.pocketgamer.biz/popcap-games-resurrects-plants-vs-zombies-3-back-soft-launch/#:~:text=So%20we%20took%20the%20time,made%20the%20original%20game%20great","sd":"Sep 2021","sy":"Company statement","w8":3}]},{"k":"playwayportfoliomodel","n":"PlayWay portfolio model (House Flipper, Car Mechanic Simulator)","dv":"PlayWay","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/613100/","ls":"Steam","cv":"playwayportfoliomodel.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Published each game's pre-production on Steam and greenlit full production only at 15,000 wishlists","s":"PlayWay ran about 80 games at once on budgets of a few hundred thousand zloty. House Flipper found 800,000 buyers, and 60 PlayWay titles sat on Steam's global wishlist chart in 2019.","w":"PlayWay CEO Krzysztof Kostowski described the rule in a June 2019 Forbes Polska interview. Earlier the company built games and watched how they sold; now it prepares a pre-production, checks it, and makes the full game only if players accept it. The test is Steam interest: 15,000 sign-ups in a short time means the game should be built. Games cost a few hundred thousand zloty, not 30 million. One in five incoming teams is screened out. By mid-2019 PlayWay had about 80 games in development, over 40 subsidiaries and about 400 people; House Flipper had found 800,000 buyers.","r":"About 80 cheap games in parallel, each with demand checked before full production.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"A few hundred thousand zloty per game.","nf":false,"l":"Put the pre-production on the store first and set a numeric wishlist threshold as your greenlight gate.","i":"The store page turns pre-production into a market test, so money goes only to games with proven interest. It works for cheap simulators sold to a defined niche with cross-promotion; Kostowski himself says 30-million-zloty productions are not the intention.","a":"What wishlist number must the store page hit before full production?","q":"“Jeśli w krótkim czasie zapisze się na nią 15 tysięcy użytkowników na Steamie, to wtedy wiemy, że powinniśmy tę grę robić.”","qa":"Krzysztof Kostowski, CEO of PlayWay","st":"Krzysztof Kostowski i PlayWay. W poszukiwaniu nowego Minecrafta","su":"https://www.forbes.pl/biznes/playway-szuka-w-polsce-nowego-minecrafta-krzysztof-kostowski-chce-by-projekty-gier/2vgn3kh#:~:text=Je%C5%9Bli%20w%20kr%C3%B3tkim%20czasie%20zapisze,t%C4%99%20gr%C4%99%20robi%C4%87","sd":"Jun 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"pocketplanes","n":"Pocket Planes","dv":"NimbleBit","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/pocket-planes-airline-tycoon/id491994942","ls":"App Store","cv":"pocketplanes.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Turned a train sim into a plane game well into development","s":"The switch was a big change and took a lot more work. Pocket Planes took 7 to 8 months, against the 2 months NimbleBit calls its sweet spot.","w":"NimbleBit started Pocket Planes as a train simulation. Pretty far into development, the team decided it just wasn't fun enough and changed it to planes. Ian Marsh called it a big change that needed a lot more work, worth it because the team can't finish a game it doesn't enjoy. Pocket Planes reached the App Store in June 2012 after 7 to 8 months; Tiny Tower had taken 4. At GDC in March 2013 Marsh named 2 months as the studio's sweet spot, and said Nimble Quest, at 5 to 6 real months, went a lot better.","r":"The plane rebuild was a big change that needed a lot more work.","re":"On Nimble Quest, NimbleBit brought real development down to 5 to 6 months.","co":"A 7 to 8 month cycle in total; the studio's sweet spot is 2 months.","nf":false,"l":"Play the core loop for fun before you build content around it, and switch while the switch is still cheap.","i":"The team judged fun only once the train game was far along, so the switch redid work already built. NimbleBit's cycles run months, so the late switch cost months; on a multi-year project the same move costs far more.","a":"Have we played our core loop and agreed it is fun?","q":"“The biggest example I can give is how we stared Pocket Planes as a train sim game and, pretty far into development, thought it just wasn't fun enough and decided to change it to the plane game it became - which was a pretty big change and required a lot more work.”","qa":"Ian Marsh, co-founder of NimbleBit","st":"NimbleBit's Ian Marsh on the NimbleDo's and NimbleDon'ts of development","su":"https://www.pocketgamer.biz/nimblebits-ian-marsh-on-the-nimbledos-and-nimbledonts-of-development/#:~:text=The%20biggest%20example%20I%20can%20give,required%20a%20lot%20more%20work","sd":"Apr 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"pokmongoldandsilver","n":"Pokémon Gold and Silver","dv":"Game Freak","y":"1999","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Set out to make the ultimate Pokémon game with about 4 programmers","s":"Planned for 1998, Gold and Silver shipped in November 1999 after 3.5 years. Game Freak's Shigeki Morimoto ties the difficult birth to packing many features into a very small team.","w":"Game Freak started Gold and Silver right after Red and Green came out in February 1996. Tsunekazu Ishihara saw them as the ultimate Pokémon games and aimed for a 1998 release. The team had only about 4 programmers, yet wanted a long list of features. Meanwhile Blue and Yellow came out, and work on overseas versions of Red and Green set Gold and Silver back further. Satoru Iwata, then president of HAL Laboratory, built graphics compression tools for the team. The games shipped in Japan on 21 November 1999, 3.5 years after the start.","r":"The release slipped from 1998 to November 1999.","re":"HAL president Satoru Iwata built graphics compression tools to help the team.","co":"Release slipped from a 1998 plan to November 1999; 3.5 years total.","nf":false,"l":"Count your programmers before you lock the feature list, and cut features until the list fits that team.","i":"A feature list sized for the ultimate sequel landed on about 4 programmers, so every addition queued behind the same few people. Side releases and localisation pulled at the same team, so the feature list was not the only cause of the slip.","a":"Can our programmers build every feature on this list by the date?","q":"“What’s more, though we were creating the games with a very small team, we were very greedy in terms of all the features we wanted to include in the games.”","qa":"Shigeki Morimoto, director of HeartGold and SoulSilver at Game Freak","st":"Iwata Asks | 3. Just Being President Was A Waste! | Iwata Asks - Pokémon HeartGold Version & SoulSilver Version","su":"https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Iwata-Asks/Iwata-Asks-Pokemon-HeartGold-Version-SoulSilver-Version/Iwata-Asks-Pokemon-HeartGold-Version-SoulSilver-Version/3-Just-Being-President-Was-A-Waste-/3-Just-Being-President-Was-A-Waste--225951.html#:~:text=What%E2%80%99s%20more%2C%20though%20we%20were,to%20include%20in%20the%20games","sd":"2009","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"pokmonrubyandsapphire","n":"Pokémon Ruby and Sapphire","dv":"Game Freak","y":"2002","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Planned a roster of only brand new Pokémon for the first Game Boy Advance games","s":"Past the halfway point, balancing showed that some types had too few Pokémon. The team brought older species back to fill the gaps.","w":"After Gold & Silver, players said not much had changed, and art director Ken Sugimori blamed the many familiar Pokémon. So from the early stages of development, Game Freak planned Ruby & Sapphire with nothing but brand new Pokémon. That meant drawing new versions of basic roles, such as Wurmple for the early caterpillar slot Caterpie used to fill. Past the halfway point, balancing showed too few Pokémon of certain types, and older ones were added. The games launched in Japan in November 2002, and in 2003 Sugimori said type balancing was harder than on Gold & Silver.","r":"The all-new roster plan was dropped past the halfway point.","re":"Game Freak added older Pokémon picked by the types it lacked.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Check the roster's coverage of types and roles in an early balance pass, before you lock a rule against reusing old content.","i":"A no-reuse rule fixes the roster before balancing can say what it needs, and balancing comes late. Here the fix was cheap because old Pokémon already existed; a new series would have had to design every missing creature.","a":"Does our roster cover every role that balancing will need?","q":"“From the early stages of development until past the halfway point, we planned on Ruby & Sapphire featuring nothing but brand new Pokemon.”","qa":"Junichi Masuda, director of Pokémon Ruby & Sapphire at Game Freak","st":"Translation: Sugimori and Masuda on Gen 3’s Development","su":"https://lavacutcontent.com/sugimori-masuda-gen-3-interview/#:~:text=From%20the%20early%20stages%20of,nothing%20but%20brand%20new%20Pokemon","sd":"2003, translated Nov 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"portal2","n":"Portal 2","dv":"Valve","y":"2011","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/620/","ls":"Steam","cv":"portal2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Staffing","t":"Kept the Portal 2 concept team to about 15 people before the full ramp","s":"That small team spent about 3 months on a version without portals, Chell or GLaDOS. Playtesters said it wasn't Portal 2, so the team brought all three back.","w":"After Valve released The Orange Box, the company spent about 4 months on design experiments in groups of 3 to 4 people. About 15 people then took on Portal 2, to poke holes in the concept before a 60 or 70 person shipping team joined. Their first version cut Chell, GLaDOS and portals for a new mechanic called F-Stop. After about 3 months, playtesters kept saying it wasn't Portal 2. The team restored all three and added the paint mechanic. Portal 2 shipped in April 2011, and the writers told the story at GDC in March 2012.","r":"The F-Stop direction was dropped after about 3 months of small-team work.","re":"The team restored Chell, GLaDOS and portals and added the paint mechanic.","co":"About 3 months of work by a team of about 15 people.","nf":false,"l":"Keep the concept team small and playtest the new direction before the full shipping team joins.","i":"A team of 15 can throw away 3 months of work without stranding 60 people. Playtesters judged F-Stop against the first game, so this check is sharpest on sequels, where players already know what the game should feel like.","a":"Have players confirmed this direction before we add the rest of the team?","q":"","qa":"","st":"Portal 2 - Post Mortem (GDC)","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OLqk4aqpXlQ&t=196s","sd":"Mar 2012","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Dropped portals, Chell and GLaDOS from the first version of Portal 2","s":"Months went into a new mechanic and villain before playtesters rejected the result. The team brought all three back for a story set long after Portal 1.","w":"After Valve released The Orange Box, a team of about 15 started Portal 2. It kept Aperture Science but cut Chell, GLaDOS and portals, for a new mechanic called F-Stop and Cave Johnson as the lead character. Once the version was solid enough to playtest, testers kept saying it was a lot of things but not Portal 2. The team set the game far in the future and restored all three. Portal 2 shipped on April 18, 2011. In May 2011 Wolpaw said the detour took 6 months; the 2012 GDC postmortem said about 3 months.","r":"The team reversed months of work on a new direction.","re":"The team restored all three and revived unused Portal 1 mechanics like light bridges.","co":"3 to 6 months on the F-Stop version, by the two accounts.","nf":false,"l":"List what players remember the original for, and keep those elements until a playtest shows they can go.","i":"The writers had lived with GLaDOS for years and were ready to move on, while most players had met her for a few hours and wanted more. A small concept team kept this reversal cheap; with a full team it costs far more.","a":"Which signature elements are we cutting, and have players tried the game without them?","q":"“People didn't want a clean slate.”","qa":"Erik Wolpaw, writer at Valve","st":"Valve's Wolpaw Offers Behind-The-Scenes Peek Into Portal 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/valve-s-wolpaw-offers-behind-the-scenes-peek-into-i-portal-2-i-#:~:text=People%20didn't%20want%20a%20clean%20slate","sd":"May 2011","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"primalrageii","n":"Primal Rage II","dv":"Atari Games (later WMS","y":"1997","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 1997","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Kept stop-motion puppets for a sequel roster grown from 7 to 17 characters","s":"Each character took months to become playable, the summer 1996 date slipped, and the team never had a polished vertical slice to show the people deciding its fate.","w":"Atari Games started Primal Rage II on 1 March 1995 with a $2.3 million budget and 20 people. The sequel kept physical stop-motion puppets, but the roster grew from 7 to 17 characters at 1,500 to 2,000 unique frames each, up from 400. Frames streamed off a hard drive on custom hardware, which caused glitches. Each character took months to become playable, and the summer 1996 date slipped. WMS bought Atari Games in March 1996 and cancelled the game on 5 December 1996.","r":"The summer 1996 date slipped and no vertical slice ever existed.","re":"The team kept polishing until WMS cancelled the project in December 1996.","co":"Whole $2.3 million, 20-person project unreleased; months per character to playable.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock a costly art method for the full roster, get one character playable and polished on the target hardware.","i":"Physical puppets fix a character's look before anyone sees it in play, and each one took months to reach the game. That left nothing polished to show decision makers. Tang blames the cancellation itself on the sale to WMS, not on stop-motion.","a":"How long until one character is playable and polished in the game?","q":"“A physical model for graphics meant absolute commitment.”","qa":"Chris Tang, game designer on Primal Rage II at Atari Games","st":"Primal Rage II from Heartbreak to Resurrection: The Journey After Cancellation","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YAutxATlgLA&t=1350s","sd":"2018","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Built the sequel on CoJag arcade hardware weaker than the first game's board","s":"The board could not match the graphics of Killer Instinct. The team later moved to a PlayStation-based arcade board, which added another round of delay.","w":"Management put Primal Rage II on CoJag, a Jaguar-derived arcade board, for budget and political reasons. CoJag performed worse than the Primal Rage 1 arcade hardware, while Killer Instinct had raised the bar for graphics. Later an arcade variant of Sony's PlayStation hardware became available and gave the team translucency, effects and playfield scaling. Moving again added delay to a schedule aimed at summer 1996. In his 2018 GDC talk, Tang said relying on outside hardware may have fed into the December 1996 cancellation.","r":"Hardware migrations helped push the game past its summer 1996 date.","re":"The team moved to a PlayStation-based arcade board once it became available.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Check the target hardware against the graphics bar of current competitors before production starts.","i":"A board picked on budget set the visual ceiling below the market, so the team had to switch hardware mid-project and redo work. The cancellation followed the sale of Atari Games, so this decision explains delay, not the cancellation.","a":"Can our target hardware reach the visual bar of current rivals?","q":"“Though relying on this non internal hardware it may have led to other factors contributing to our cancelation we could now make this a competitive product despite some additional delays as a result of having to migrate again.”","qa":"Chris Tang, game designer on Primal Rage II at Atari Games","st":"Primal Rage II from Heartbreak to Resurrection: The Journey After Cancellation","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YAutxATlgLA&t=1276s","sd":"2018","sy":"Participant talk","w8":3,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YAutxATlgLA&t=1306s"}]},{"k":"princeofpersiathesandsoftime","n":"Prince of Persia: The Sands of Time","dv":"Ubisoft Montreal","y":"2003","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/13600/","ls":"Steam","cv":"princeofpersiathesandsoftime.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Started level production while combat and tools were still in pre-production","s":"With no clear line between approved and unfinished work, the team built and rejected several complete fight systems. Farah's AI started late, and debugging slipped against a fixed gold master date.","w":"Ubisoft Montreal began the conceptual phase in June 2001 with a team of 7. The plan was a long pre-production ending in a playable proof, with character behavior, macro designs, story and tools complete. Pre-production was planned for 10 months and took more than 14, and level production began with many tools and fight behaviors still unfinished. The team designed, implemented and rejected several complete fight systems in animation and AI. Farah's AI started late, debugging was postponed, and the gold master went out at the end of October 2003.","r":"Production time went into rebuilding combat, so AI and debugging started late.","re":"Cut scope twice, before Christmas 2002 and after E3 2003, to hold the date.","co":"Several complete fight systems built and rejected; pre-production over 14 months, not 10.","nf":false,"l":"Before level production starts, list which mechanics and tools are approved, and treat any later change to them as a scheduled exception.","i":"Levels are built around the hero's moves and combat, so each change to an unapproved system reworks content and delays what depends on it, such as companion AI. With a fixed ship date, that delay turns into a pile of bugs at the end.","a":"Is there a written list of mechanics and tools approved for production?","q":"“Maintaining the right balance between creation and production was hard, and there was no clear distinction between what was approved and what still needed improvement.”","qa":"Yannis Mallat, producer at Ubisoft Montreal","st":"The making of Prince of Persia: The Sands of Time","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/the-making-of-i-prince-of-persia-the-sands-of-time-i-#:~:text=Maintaining%20the%20right%20balance%20between,what%20still%20needed%20improvement","sd":"Apr 2004, reprinted Nov 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"princeofpersiathesandsoftimeremake","n":"Prince of Persia: The Sands of Time Remake","dv":"Ubisoft","y":"2026","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2026","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Moved the announced remake to Ubisoft Montreal and restarted it from conception","s":"Work went on for almost 4 more years. Ubisoft cancelled the game in January 2026, and the team said finishing it needed more time and money than it could commit.","w":"Ubisoft revealed the remake in September 2020, built by Ubisoft Pune and Ubisoft Mumbai, for a January 2021 launch. The reveal drew criticism of its visuals, and the date slipped to March 2021, then indefinitely. In May 2022 Ubisoft moved development to Ubisoft Montreal, maker of the 2003 original, to regroup on the scope. A year later producer Jean-Francois Naud said the game was in conception: building the team, setting priorities and making prototypes. On January 21, 2026, after more than 5 years of development, Ubisoft cancelled it with 5 other games.","r":"The game went back to concept in 2023 and never shipped.","re":"Ubisoft cancelled the remake in January 2026 with 5 other games.","co":"More than 5 years of development; part of Ubisoft's EUR 650M cancellation and delay hit.","nf":false,"l":"When a project moves to a new studio, run a fresh greenlight: new concept, new budget, new go or stop date.","i":"A restart under an announced title keeps the old clock and the old expectations running, so the new concept is judged against years already spent. Ubisoft cited only quality and portfolio criteria, so this does not show that restarts fail.","a":"Does our restart have its own budget and a go or stop date?","q":"“In terms of the development stage, we're in conception right now.”","qa":"Jean-Francois Naud, producer at Ubisoft Montreal","st":"An Update on Prince of Persia: The Sands of Time Remake","su":"https://news.ubisoft.com/en-us/article/6rk3dzrT4ndn9ofFfZg4GV/an-update-on-prince-of-persia-the-sands-of-time-remake#:~:text=In%20terms%20of%20the%20development,in%20conception%20right%20now","sd":"May 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"prisonarchitect","n":"Prison Architect","dv":"Introversion Software","y":"2015","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2012","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/233450/","ls":"Steam","cv":"prisonarchitect.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Put Subversion on hold and gave Prison Architect a six-week first playable","s":"After six weeks the new game had more to play than Subversion ever had. A paid alpha on Introversion's own site later raised $1M in its first month.","w":"Introversion had worked on Subversion so long that the team struggled to remember what it was about. Chris Delay pitched Prison Architect with a PowerPoint, and the team agreed to pause Subversion for a six-week first playable. By the end of it, the new game was already more of a game than Subversion. The studio announced the switch in October 2011. In autumn 2012 it opened a paid alpha with Kickstarter-style tiers on its own site, and Mark Morris says it made $1M in the first month. By October 2015, before full release, the game had earned $19M.","r":"Prison Architect replaced Subversion as the studio's main project.","re":"Funded the rest of development through a paid alpha on their own site.","co":"6 weeks of prototype time; Subversion put on hold.","nf":false,"l":"Give a new concept a fixed first playable of a few weeks, then compare it with the project it would replace.","i":"A short timebox makes the team build the core game first, the piece Subversion never found. The paid alpha worked because the prototype was already fun. Selling early access does not fix a loop nobody enjoys.","a":"Could we show a playable core of this game in six weeks?","q":"“By the end of that period we already had more of a game than Subversion had ever been.”","qa":"Chris Delay, co-founder of Introversion Software","st":"Introversion's Chris Delay on shifting from Subversion to Prison Architect: \"I wanted to build Alcatraz\"","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/au/introversions-chris-delay-on-shifting-from-subversion-to-prison-architect-i-wanted-to-build-alcatraz#:~:text=By%20the%20end%20of%20that,Subversion%20had%20ever%20been","sd":"Oct 2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":5}]},{"k":"project8","n":"Project 8","dv":"11 bit studios","y":"2024","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2024","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Started Project 8 in 2018 as a story-rich game built for console players","s":"Critical parts of the game stayed unresolved through 6 years of milestones. In December 2024 the board cancelled it and wrote off about PLN 48.4 million.","w":"11 bit studios began Project 8 as early as 2018, its first game designed for console players. The concept came from a time when narrative-driven, story-rich games had stronger appeal. COVID-19 made the team hard to stabilise. Some areas improved, but several critical aspects stayed problematic through many iterations, and the budget grew with each milestone. By September 2024 it had cost over PLN 48.4 million, with 37 people and PLN 0.8 to 0.9 million spent per month. The latest milestone review found issues needing more time and money, and in December 2024 the board cancelled it.","r":"Every milestone added delay and budget while critical problems stayed open.","re":"The board cancelled the game and offered over half the team other roles.","co":"About PLN 48.4 million written off; 37 people; about 6 years.","nf":false,"l":"At every milestone, rerun the sales forecast against today's market, not the market the concept was born in.","i":"A 6-year project ships into a market that did not exist at greenlight, and each milestone that misses the core widens that gap. The board named market change as one reason among several, so this does not mean narrative games stopped selling.","a":"When did we last update the sales forecast for this concept?","q":"“Over time, delays accumulated, and with each milestone, the project’s budget grew.”","qa":"Przemysław Marszał, President of the Management Board of 11 bit studios","st":"11 bit studios ends development of Project 8","su":"https://ir.11bitstudios.com/en/news/11-bit-studios-ends-development-of-project-8#:~:text=Over%20time%2C%20delays%20accumulated%2C%20and,the%20project%E2%80%99s%20budget%20grew","sd":"Dec 2024","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"projectcopernicus","n":"Project Copernicus","dv":"38 Studios","y":"2012","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2012","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Took a $75 million state loan to finish the MMO despite a $22 million gap","s":"Only about $50 million reached the company, which was spending $4 to $5 million a month. In May 2012 38 Studios missed a $1.125 million payment, laid off every employee and went bankrupt; Copernicus never shipped.","w":"In 2010 Rhode Island's EDC board approved a $75 million loan guarantee to move 38 Studios from Massachusetts to Providence. The state's lawsuit says the company's own projections showed about $22 million missing from the $75 million needed to finish Copernicus, and only about $50 million of the bond money reached the company. Vice chairman Thomas Zaccagnino emailed that the company should not highlight being under capitalized. The studio spent $4 to $5 million a month. In May 2012 it missed a $1.125 million payment and laid off everyone; bankruptcy followed in June. Rhode Island sued 14 participants that November.","r":"Copernicus cancelled and the studio bankrupt within 2 years of the loan.","re":"Borrowed $9.6 million against film tax credits in 2012; it was not enough.","co":"$133 million spent since 2006; taxpayers' net cost about $38.6 million after $61 million settlements.","nf":false,"l":"Do not enter or continue full production until committed money covers the plan to ship; a known shortfall is a delayed cancellation.","i":"A loan that covers most of the plan buys time, not a finished game, and the gap was known before the money was spent. Who knew what is disputed: the state says it was misled, the defendants say the board knew needs exceeded $125 million.","a":"Does the money we have cover the full plan to ship?","q":"“I really do not think we should highlight the fact that we might be under capitalized…won't go over well with the staff or board.”","qa":"Thomas Zaccagnino, vice chairman of 38 Studios, in an email filed in the Rhode Island lawsuit","st":"38 Studios Knew State Funding Wouldn't be Enough for Cancelled MMO","su":"https://gameranx.com/updates/id/22039/article/38-studios-knew-state-funding-wouldn-t-be-enough-for-cancelled-mmo/#:~:text=I%20really%20do%20not%20think,staff%20or%20board","sd":"May 2014","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"projectdagger","n":"Project Dagger","dv":"People Can Fly","y":"2024","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2024","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Cut Project Dagger to a 10-person team to rebuild it as a pre-production version","s":"The redefined game also failed its scope and commercial review. PCF abandoned it five months later and wrote off 100% of its spend: PLN 68.3m.","w":"PCF Group had produced Project Dagger with its own money since Take-Two ended their publishing deal in September 2022. In November 2023, after a key milestone and an external evaluation of the creative concept, the board cut the project to about 10 people. They were to redefine the game and build a pre-production version that answered the feedback. The AAA release planned for 2025 to 2026 was suspended, and most of the team moved to Maverick, Bifrost and Victoria. In April 2024 the new direction failed its review of scope and commercial potential. PCF abandoned Dagger.","r":"Dagger was abandoned five months later with a 100% write-off.","re":"PCF stopped all work on Dagger in April 2024.","co":"PLN 68.3m consolidated write-off (PLN 79.9m standalone): 100% of capitalised spend.","nf":false,"l":"Get an outside review of the creative concept before you scale production, while the spend is still small.","i":"By the time the concept failed its review, the money was already spent, so the reboot could only limit new spending. A small team with other projects to absorb staff keeps that cheap. It does not rescue a concept that fails twice.","a":"Has anyone outside the team judged this concept before we scale up?","q":"“The discontinuation of work on the Project is the result of re-analysis of the development plans for projects implemented by the capital group of PCF Group S.A. (the “Group”) and introducing changes to these plans in connection with the unsatisfactory results of the evaluation of the scope and commercial potential of the Project after redefining the direction of the game’s development, which the Company informed about in current report No. 59/2023 of 28 November 2023.”","qa":"Management Board of PCF Group S.A.","st":"Podjęcie decyzji o ograniczeniu zakresu Projektu Dagger – korekta raportu bieżącego nr 59/2023","su":"https://peoplecanfly.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/20231128-raport-biezacy-59-2023-zmniejszenie-zakresu-projektu-dagger-korekta.pdf#:~:text=preparing%20a%20preproduction%20version%20of,external%20evaluation%20of%20Project%20Dagger","sd":"Nov 2023","sy":"Company report","w8":5,"qu":"https://www.bankier.pl/wiadomosc/PCF-GROUP-S-A-Podjecie-decyzji-o-zaniechaniu-dalszych-prac-nad-Projektem-Dagger-8723617.html#:~:text=The%20discontinuation%20of%20work%20on%20the%20Project"}]},{"k":"projectgeminiandprojectbifrost","n":"Project Gemini / Project Bifrost","dv":"People Can Fly","y":"2025","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2025","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Signed a work-for-hire deal run in content riders while self-funding a VR game","s":"When the last rider ran out in 2025, Square Enix sent no draft of the next one and no word on continuing. PCF stopped Gemini after nearly 5 years, then Bifrost, and cut more than 60 jobs.","w":"People Can Fly signed the Gemini publishing agreement with Square Enix on 12 August 2020: work-for-hire, run through successive content riders. Alongside it the studio self-funded Project Bifrost, a VR game. In 2025 the rider expired. Square Enix presented no draft of the next one and did not say whether it wanted to continue or end the project. PCF suspended Gemini on 1 June 2025, ran a cash-flow analysis, found no way to fund Bifrost, suspended it too and cut more than 60 jobs. On 19 November 2025 a settlement ended the Gemini agreement with a mutual waiver of claims.","r":"Gemini stopped at a rider boundary; Bifrost lost its funding with it.","re":"Settled with Square Enix in November 2025, ending both agreements with mutual waiver.","co":"More than 60 jobs cut; nearly 5 years of Gemini work stopped.","nf":false,"l":"In a work-for-hire deal, fix what happens between riders: notice period, decision deadline and kill fee.","i":"A rider-by-rider contract lets the publisher stop by doing nothing while the studio carries the team. Exposure grew because a self-funded project leaned on the same cash flow. PCF had Gears and Sony work to fall back on; a one-project studio would not.","a":"What happens to the team if the publisher never signs the next rider?","q":"“As a result, we have to significantly regroup as a studio and scale down our teams, which hurts the most.”","qa":"Sebastian Wojciechowski, CEO of People Can Fly (PCF Group)","st":"People Can Fly cancels Square Enix-published Project Gemini, VR game Project Bifrost","su":"https://www.gematsu.com/2025/06/people-can-fly-cancels-square-enix-published-project-gemini-vr-game-project-bifrost#:~:text=As%20a%20result%2C%20we%20have,hurts%20the%20most","sd":"Jun 2025","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"projectphoenix","n":"Project Phoenix","dv":"Creative Intelligence Arts","y":"","sc":"Indie","se":"Unreleased 2026","lk":"https://www.kickstarter.com/projects/1300298569/project-phoenix-japans-indie-rpg-feat-aaa-talent","ls":"Kickstarter","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Planned a $1 million Kickstarter RPG around one programmer still busy on another game","s":"Production waited for him to finish his other title, and hiring a full team at market rates would have used up the money. Promised for about 2015, the game is now due by the end of 2031.","w":"Hiroaki Yura's Kickstarter ran from 12 August to 11 September 2013 and raised $1,014,600 from 15,802 backers; about $800,000 arrived, 20% less than expected. The plan leaned on one uniquely capable programmer on a below-market arrangement, and production waited for his next title to ship. In June 2015 Yura apologised for the lack of updates: he had taken several jobs and worked on the game evenings and weekends. The studio built Tiny Metal on about $350,000 from investors. By 2019 the game was 4 years past its promised release. In July 2026 Yura promised completion by end of 2031.","r":"No game delivered 13 years after funding; the team worked other jobs to survive.","re":"Yura built Tiny Metal with investor money and now funds Phoenix from his later studio.","co":"$1,014,600 pledged, about $800,000 received; 13 years and counting with no release.","nf":false,"l":"Never plan production around one person who is not yet free; cost the full team at real rates before you set a date.","i":"A below-market deal with one key person looked cheap on paper, so the budget was never checked against a full team at real rates. When he was delayed, there was no money to replace him. This holds wherever one hire is the schedule.","a":"Which single person is our schedule waiting on, and what if they slip?","q":"“Hiring a full team at proper rates would have taken us well past what was left.”","qa":"Hiroaki Yura, producer and founder of CIA, Inc.","st":"Creator of infamous $1m Kickstarter Project Phoenix says he 'deserved the backlash' – and insists it's still coming","su":"https://www.videogameschronicle.com/features/project-phoenix/#:~:text=Hiring%20a%20full%20team%20at,what%20was%20left","sd":"Jul 2026","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"projectsirius","n":"Project Sirius","dv":"The Molasses Flood","y":"","sc":"AA","se":"Unreleased","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Wrote down Project Sirius and sent its original concept back for a new framework","s":"Two months later the new framework reused a material part of the built game. PLN 21.5 million of the write-down was reversed, and PLN 2.7 million of 2023 costs stayed written off.","w":"Project Sirius, a game set in The Witcher universe, was in development at The Molasses Flood, which CD Projekt bought in 2021. On March 20, 2023, the management board evaluated the scope and commercial potential of the original concept. It wrote down PLN 33.4 million of 2022 costs and PLN 9.5 million from January and February 2023, and began work on a new framework. On May 11, 2023, it announced the framework, built on a material part of the existing game. It reversed PLN 21.5 million of the 2022 write-down and wrote off PLN 2.7 million of first-quarter costs.","r":"A material part of the built game was kept under the new framework.","re":"The board approved a new framework within two months, on May 11, 2023.","co":"PLN 33.4M written down, 21.5M reversed; PLN 2.7M of 2023 costs written off.","nf":false,"l":"When a concept fails its commercial review, list which built work a new framework can reuse before you write it all off.","i":"The board judged the concept, not the assets, so a reframe kept much of the work and let CD Projekt reverse PLN 21.5 million of the write-down. Salvage works when built content fits the new concept; a broken core loop leaves little to reuse.","a":"If this concept fails review, which of our built work survives?","q":"“The aforementioned decision is based on results of evaluation of the scope and commercial potential of the original concept of Project Sirius, and ongoing work on formulating a new framework for this project.”","qa":"Management Board of CD PROJEKT S.A.","st":"Current report no. 8/2023","su":"https://www.cdprojekt.com/en/investors/regulatory-announcements/current-report-no-8-2023/#:~:text=The%20aforementioned%20decision%20is%20based,new%20framework%20for%20this%20project","sd":"Mar 2023","sy":"Company statement","w8":5}]},{"k":"prune","n":"Prune","dv":"Joel McDonald","y":"2015","sc":"Solo","se":"Port 2025","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/3903490/","ls":"Steam","cv":"prune.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Prototyped anything to do with trees for 6 months without defining the design space","s":"A planned 2 to 3 month solo project took 15 months. After 6 months the 60-level build playtested as clinical and frustrating, and McDonald rebooted it down to a tree, sunlight and shadow.","w":"Former AAA designer Joel McDonald started Prune as a 2 to 3 month solo project. The first prototype took a couple of evenings. He then misread the advice to explore the design space and spent 6 months prototyping everything tree-related: power-ups, fractal trees, tree planets, co-op and endless modes. At 6 months he had over 60 levels, but playtests showed the game was clinical and frustrating. He rebooted to a bare minimum and rewrote tree growth over a couple of months. Prune reached the App Store in July 2015, after 15 months, and won TIME's 2015 Game of the Year.","r":"A 3-month plan became 15 months, with 6 months of prototypes mostly thrown away.","re":"McDonald rebooted the game and used award deadlines to force decisions.","co":"About 12 months over a 3-month plan; money not stated.","nf":false,"l":"Before you prototype, write down the one question the prototypes must answer; exploring everything is not pre-production.","i":"Prototyping without a constraint is search without a target, so 6 months produced no decision. McDonald also says the spare time let him find the game's soul; the lesson is to narrow the search early, not to cut the time.","a":"What question does our current prototype answer?","q":"“I didn’t know what my design space really was, I had no focus.”","qa":"Joel McDonald, solo developer of Prune","st":"Postmortem: Joel McDonald's Prune","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-joel-mcdonald-s-i-prune-i-#:~:text=I%20didn%E2%80%99t%20know%20what%20my,had%20no%20focus","sd":"Feb 2016","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"psychonauts","n":"Psychonauts","dv":"Double Fine Productions","y":"2005","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/3830/","ls":"Steam","cv":"psychonauts.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Made pre-production documents and demos mainly to get through the publisher's green-light","s":"The team never built a picture of the whole game. Psychonauts slipped twice, by 6 and then 16 months, and core areas needed rework late.","w":"During pre-production of Double Fine's first game, the publisher asked for volumes of documents and a polished unit of gameplay. The team made both mainly to get through Microsoft's green-light, and neither helped it understand its own game. The design kept growing, and earlier levels needed rework to match later decisions. The game slipped by 6 months in mid 2002 and by 16 months in early 2003. Only in the last months, after Microsoft dropped the game, did the team build a rough full-game walkthrough. It showed fundamental areas needed rework. Psychonauts shipped in April 2005 on an $11.8M budget.","r":"Two slips added 22 months to the schedule.","re":"Built a full-game walkthrough and global feature design to set a realistic schedule.","co":"6-month slip in mid 2002, 16-month slip in early 2003, late rework.","nf":false,"l":"Plan pre-production to answer your own design questions, and build a rough walkthrough of the whole game before production.","i":"Deliverables made for a publisher answer the publisher's questions, not the team's. Without a global design, each later decision forced rework on earlier levels. A polished slice for green-light still has value if the team also maps the whole game.","a":"Could we walk through the whole game today, start to finish?","q":"“Neither one facilitated our creative process, nor did either help us understand the game at a deeper level ourselves.”","qa":"Caroline Esmurdoc, executive producer at Double Fine Productions","st":"Classic Postmortem: Double Fine's Psychonauts","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/classic-postmortem-double-fine-s-i-psychonauts-i-#:~:text=Neither%20one%20facilitated%20our%20creative,deeper%20level%20ourselves","sd":"Aug 2005, reprinted Aug 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Shared each level between a level designer and an artist","s":"The two roles fought over who owned the levels, and the levels still were not fun. In 2003 Double Fine let go all but one level designer and started the levels again.","w":"On Psychonauts a level designer laid out and scripted each world, and an artist built the mesh around it. Later only artists could make visual geometry, and the designers resented it. Tim Schafer says the game looked pretty but was not fun. In 2003 all but one level designer was let go, and Erik Robson led a new World Builder department that owned design and visuals. Microsoft gave the team 3 months to make the game fun, then dropped it in February 2004. Majesco offered a deal that July, and the team finished the game in February 2005.","r":"Most of the level design team was let go and levels restarted.","re":"Double Fine created a World Builder department owning both design and visuals.","co":"3 people fired in one day; levels restarted with junior world builders.","nf":false,"l":"Before level production starts, name one owner per level who has the final call on both layout and visuals.","i":"Shared ownership turned each level into a negotiation, and the fights crowded out work on fun. Once one department owned layout and visuals, Schafer says the fighting dropped and things got done. The fix is one final call per level, not less collaboration.","a":"Who has the final call on each of our levels?","q":"“Even though things were starting to look kind of pretty in the game, things were not fun yet.”","qa":"Tim Schafer, head of Double Fine Productions","st":"Double Fine PsychOdyssey · Episode 01: “The Color of the Sky in Your World”","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AKoy_KMVIyU&t=1390s","sd":"Feb 2023","sy":"Participant documentary","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Started Psychonauts without a producer while Tim Schafer also ran the company","s":"Design, art and code approvals all waited on Schafer. Microsoft later made a producer hire a contract condition, and the project recovered once one joined in 2003.","w":"Double Fine was founded to make Tim Schafer's games, and Psychonauts was the first. For several years Schafer designed and wrote the game while also acting as president, producer, CFO, COO and webmaster. He started missing meetings, and design, art and code approvals queued behind him. The leads were senior developers with little management experience and struggled to estimate tasks. When the ship date moved, the amended Microsoft contract required a producer hire within 3 months. Caroline Esmurdoc joined as executive producer in summer 2003. She writes that the project was then back on track.","r":"The design document went stale and leads struggled with estimates.","re":"Hired Caroline Esmurdoc as executive producer in summer 2003.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Hire a producer from day one of pre-production, so the creative director is not also scheduling and running the company.","i":"With one person holding the vision and every business role, design, art and code all queue behind that person. The lesson scales with team size: at a peak of 42 developers the bottleneck hurt, while a very small team may not need a full-time producer.","a":"Does anyone besides the creative director own our schedule?","q":"“He was simply stretched too thin without a producer.”","qa":"Caroline Esmurdoc, executive producer at Double Fine Productions","st":"Classic Postmortem: Double Fine's Psychonauts","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/classic-postmortem-double-fine-s-i-psychonauts-i-#:~:text=He%20was%20simply%20stretched%20too,thin%20without%20a%20producer","sd":"Aug 2005, reprinted Aug 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"psychonauts2","n":"Psychonauts 2","dv":"Double Fine Productions","y":"2021","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/607080/","ls":"Steam","cv":"psychonauts2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Used Fig crowdfunding to start pre-production and prototypes before signing a publisher","s":"Footage from the prototypes helped close a Starbreeze deal after about 30 pitches. Until the deal, the number of brains in the game stayed open, and years later the schedule was still not funded to the end.","w":"In December 2015 Double Fine asked Fig backers for $3.3M, what its first crowdfunding had raised, and got over $3.8M. The money let the team start the game and build prototypes before any publisher signed. Tim Schafer said they could not leave pre-production without the final budget, because the money set how many brains the game had. Greg Rice pitched about 30 publishers while new footage kept coming, and Starbreeze signed. Later Schafer told the team the schedule was not funded to the end. Microsoft took over publishing in June 2019, and the game shipped in August 2021.","r":"The team prototyped without a publisher, but scope waited on the deal.","re":"Schafer and Greg Rice kept raising money until Microsoft bought out Starbreeze.","co":"About 30 publisher pitches before a deal.","nf":false,"l":"Lock the final budget before you leave pre-production, and keep a scope plan for each possible budget until then.","i":"Crowd money bought time to prototype, and footage of a real game did more in pitches than an art test. But content count, here the number of brains, waited on the deal. That works while the crowd money covers pre-production.","a":"Do we know our final budget before we leave pre-production?","q":"“And you don't know what game you are making, until you know your final-final budget.”","qa":"Tim Schafer, head of Double Fine Productions","st":"Double Fine PsychOdyssey · Episode 08: “A Couple Years of a Lot of Work”","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eaA0P2dDHSg&t=1320s","sd":"Feb 2023","sy":"Participant documentary","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Kept the leads who prototyped the game building their own levels as the team grew","s":"The leads had little time to review everyone else's work. No level had reached representative quality, and the team planned to pull one designer off levels so he could manage full-time.","w":"After the December 2015 Fig campaign, a small group of leads designed the game, wrote the documents and built the prototypes themselves for over a year. As new hires arrived, the leads kept their own assets and levels. It was also Double Fine's first game in a long time with a full level designer role, and its split of work with world builders was unclear. At a review, the leadership admitted none of the levels had reached representative quality. They planned to pull Ryan off levels to do management, review and direction full-time. The game shipped in August 2021.","r":"Every level was still below the representative quality bar.","re":"The team planned to move Ryan off levels to manage full-time.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When production starts, take leads off their own content tasks so they have time to review the whole team's work.","i":"A lead who also builds a level reviews less as the team grows, because every new hire adds questions and the work waits on feedback. In a small prototype team hands-on leads are normal; the problem starts when headcount climbs.","a":"Do our leads still own levels or assets of their own?","q":"“You can't be a really good effective Lead who is looking at all the work that's happening, from all the teams, and giving feedback, and be building your own level at the same time.”","qa":"Andy, Double Fine Productions","st":"Double Fine PsychOdyssey · Episode 16: “Written into a Corner”","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uqozV9v_7Y8&t=570s","sd":"Feb 2023","sy":"Participant documentary","w8":3},{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Published a 2018 ship estimate in the Fig campaign before scope and team existed","s":"Two years later Double Fine said it only now knew the game's size and scope, and 2018 was off. Psychonauts 2 shipped in August 2021, about 3 years after the crowdfunded estimate.","w":"Psychonauts 2 was funded on Fig about 2 years before December 2017, with a third-quarter 2018 release estimate. Double Fine's documentary shows the aftermath: Tim Schafer and new lead designer Zak McClendon spent months in a conference room defining the game, then the team staffed up and built a first mental landscape while Schafer pushed for stronger concepts. On 21 December 2017 McClendon told backers the team now knew the game's size and scope, and that it would not be done in 2018. Starbreeze went bankrupt during development; a pandemic pushed the date further. The game shipped in August 2021.","r":"The ship date moved from Q3 2018 to August 2021, about 3 years late.","re":"Double Fine stopped naming a date until it could give a firm one.","co":"About 3 years of schedule slip; budget figures not disclosed.","nf":false,"l":"Do not publish a ship year in a crowdfunding campaign before the concept, scope and team are defined.","i":"The campaign date was a promise made before the work that produces a date, so the first real estimate could only replace it. Crowdfunding needs a date to sell; give a range and name the milestone that will fix it.","a":"What do we know about scope today that justifies the date we published?","q":"“The less good news is because of that, we now know that it is not going to be done in 2018.”","qa":"Zak McClendon, project lead on Psychonauts 2 at Double Fine Productions","st":"Psychonauts 2 delayed past 2018","su":"https://gematsu.com/2017/12/psychonauts-2-delayed-past-2018#:~:text=The%20less%20good%20news%20is,done%20in%202018","sd":"Dec 2017","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Tried Unreal Engine 4 first on a small VR spin-off before Psychonauts 2","s":"The VR team took the rough first year of learning Unreal. Psychonauts 2 then started on the same engine with those early hurdles already cleared.","w":"Double Fine had run its own engine, first built for Brutal Legend, across about 20 games in 10 years. Shipping on every platform made it costly to maintain. The studio made the small VR game Psychonauts in the Rhombus of Ruin its first Unreal Engine 4 project, with Psychonauts 2 also set on Unreal. Rhombus lead Chad Dawson said a new engine's first year or so is usually code that clashes and crashes. In February 2017 he said the Psychonauts 2 team could now hit the ground running. Psychonauts 2 launched in August 2021.","r":"The Psychonauts 2 team kicked off with the Rhombus team's Unreal experience.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Run your first year on a new engine on a small project, then move those people to the big one.","i":"Most of a new engine's first year goes to learning how its makers think and where the pipeline breaks, and a small game pays for that at lower stakes. It helps only if the pilot's people and tools move to the big project.","a":"Which small project could carry our first year on this engine?","q":"“Got through a lot of the initial hurdles to be able to come in and hit the ground running with that project, so to speak.”","qa":"Chad Dawson, Rhombus of Ruin lead at Double Fine","st":"Making Psychonauts humor work in VR -- without making people sick","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/making-i-psychonauts-i-humor-work-in-vr----without-making-people-sick#:~:text=Got%20through%20a%20lot%20of,that%20project%2C%20so%20to%20speak","sd":"Feb 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Spent a year and a half on level design before hiring an art director","s":"Art got 5 months to take levels to the December 2018 alpha that design had worked on for a year and a half. Three levels had no whitebox at alpha, and production said the milestone would not have been paid.","w":"Pre-production ran under project lead Zak McClendon. The level team built blockouts while the player's moveset was still changing, with no art director; Tim Schafer called the blockouts random spaces with no concept. When art director Lisette Titre-Montgomery arrived, art had 5 months to reach the 21 December 2018 alpha on levels design had worked on for a year and a half. Three levels had no whitebox, production said the milestone would not have been paid, and the team had to choose what it could afford. The planned submission was 28 July 2019; the game shipped on 24 August 2021.","r":"Alpha missed: 3 levels without whitebox; milestone judged unpayable.","re":"Reviewed options to cut or fund; the July 2019 submission became August 2021.","co":"Art had 5 months against design's 18; release 2 years after the planned submission.","nf":false,"l":"Hire the art director and lock a level concept before building blockouts at scale.","i":"Blockouts without an art direction or an agreed concept are not finished design; they move the hard decisions into the art phase, where the schedule is shortest. The game still shipped, so this is about where the time went, not whether iterating was wrong.","a":"Is an art director in the room before we blockout levels at scale?","q":"“They've essentially had a year and a half to work on their particular levels.”","qa":"Lisette Titre-Montgomery, art director of Psychonauts 2 at Double Fine","st":"Double Fine PsychOdyssey · Episode 21: “Much More Drastic Things”","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_hWPtumg_Js&t=373s","sd":"Feb 2023","sy":"Participant account","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_hWPtumg_Js&t=368s"}]},{"k":"quake","n":"Quake","dv":"id Software","y":"1996","sc":"Indie","se":"Remaster 2021","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2310/","ls":"Steam","cv":"quake.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built Quake's new 3D engine first and left the game design unwritten for a year","s":"Levels were made and deleted as the engine changed, and the burned-out team dropped the RPG ideas for a plain shooter. Half of id left within six months of release.","w":"After Doom 2 in 1994, id's nine-person team started Quake with a full-3D, internet-ready, scriptable engine and hopes of a design closer to D&D. For the first year there was only a loose medieval idea and no written design. The engine kept improving, so the team built levels and deleted them. When the engine was ready at the end of November, the team was burned out. At a company meeting it chose Doom-style weapons over new design, and Romero later cut the RPG elements entirely. Quake shipped in 1996 and sold millions. Within six months, half the company was gone.","r":"The design shrank to a pure shooter built around the finished engine.","re":"Romero dropped the RPG ideas to speed up levels, then parted ways with id.","co":"Levels deleted through the first year; half the company gone within 6 months.","nf":false,"l":"Write down the core design and prove it in a playable build while the engine is still being built.","i":"While the engine kept changing, every level built on it was disposable, so the team used up its energy before design work could start. Engine-first works when the game design is already known. Quake's was not.","a":"Is our core design written down and playable before the engine is done?","q":"“So by November the team was really burned out trying to make a game that didn't really have an identity.”","qa":"John Romero, co-founder of id Software","st":"Quake turns 25: John Romero looks back on the legendary FPS that almost tore id Software apart","su":"https://www.gamesradar.com/the-making-of-quake/#:~:text=So%20by%20November%20the%20team,really%20have%20an%20identity","sd":"Aug 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"quantumbreak","n":"Quantum Break","dv":"Remedy","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/474960/","ls":"Steam","cv":"quantumbreak.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Story & world","t":"Grew the live-action scenes into a TV show told from the villain's side","s":"Live action spared Remedy's small animation team from 20-minute animated cutscenes. An outside company in Los Angeles made the show, and fitting it to the game took a long time to work out.","w":"Remedy had used live action since Max Payne, and its pitch to Microsoft included live-action episodes. The first plan for Quantum Break was small live-action scenes. Later, with the creative director pushing for it, the team grew them into a show about the villain while the game followed the hero. Thomas Puha says animated 20-minute cutscenes would have needed an army of animators that Finland could not supply. Lifeboat Productions in Los Angeles made the show, and the game had to make sense without it. The logistics took a long time. The game was out by April 2016.","r":"Long logistics work and a second production run by an outside company.","re":"Remedy made the game's story stand on its own without the show.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before choosing a storytelling format, check which specialists you can actually hire, and plan the work of joining that format to the game.","i":"Live action bought story time without an animation workforce Remedy could not hire in Finland. It moved the work rather than removing it: an outside crew, a second production schedule and a game story that had to stand alone.","a":"Can we hire the specialists this format needs, here or outside?","q":"“It was supposed to be on a small scale, and then it just grew.”","qa":"Thomas Puha, head of media and partners at Remedy Entertainment","st":"Interview: Thomas Puha of Remedy Entertainment on Quantum Break","su":"https://www.cgmagonline.com/interviews/interview-thomas-puha-remedy-entertainment-quantum-break-microsoft-xbox-one-pc/#:~:text=It%20was%20supposed%20to%20be,then%20it%20just%20grew","sd":"Apr 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"ragnarokonlineii","n":"Ragnarok Online II","dv":"Gravity","y":"2012","sc":"AA","se":"Open beta 2007","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/231060/","ls":"Steam","cv":"ragnarokonlineii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Ran a 39-month open beta of Ragnarok Online II while correcting it after market feedback","s":"Launch slipped from 2008 to March 2012; the Taiwan, Hong Kong and Macau licence was terminated and deals in five other countries were amended on less favourable terms.","w":"Gravity opened beta testing of Ragnarok Online II in May 2007 and told investors it would launch in late 2008. The launch kept slipping. The company's annual reports blamed technical difficulties, the departure of key developers and corrective actions taken in response to market feedback during testing. The open beta ran until August 2010, when a closed beta of an upgraded version began. The Taiwan, Hong Kong and Macau licence was terminated in December 2010 and other deals were amended on worse terms. The game launched in Korea in March 2012 and made under 1% of that year's revenue.","r":"Launch delayed from late 2008 to March 2012; licence deals terminated or amended.","re":"Ran a closed beta of an upgraded version from August 2010 and launched in 2012.","co":"39-month open beta; 64 staff on the game in 2011; development cost not disclosed.","nf":false,"l":"Prove the sequel's core experience with your current players in closed tests before you open a public beta.","i":"Corrections after market feedback in a public beta happen in the open, against announced dates, while licensees who signed for the game wait. Gravity's filings list market feedback as one cause alongside technical problems and staff departures, so test timing was not the only driver.","a":"Have current players confirmed the core experience before we announce a public beta?","q":"“For example, although we have indicated our plan to release Ragnarok Online II at various times in the past, the launch of this game has been significantly delayed on a number of occasions for a variety of reasons, including technical difficulties, loss of key personnel and corrective actions taken in response to market feedback during the testing and development phase.”","qa":"Gravity Co., Ltd., in its Form 20-F annual report","st":"Gravity Co., Ltd. Form 20-F: Annual Report pursuant to Section 13 or 15(d) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 for the fiscal year ended December 31, 2007","su":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1313310/000114554908001180/h02250e20vf.htm#:~:text=the%20launch%20of%20this%20game%20has%20been%20significantly%20delayed,testing%20and%20development%20phase.","sd":"Jun 2008","sy":"Company report","w8":3}]},{"k":"rainbowsix","n":"Rainbow Six","dv":"Red Storm Entertainment","y":"1998","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.gog.com/en/game/tom_clancys_rainbow_six","ls":"GOG","cv":"rainbowsix.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built a C++ game on an unfinished third-party renderer and a Java networking library","s":"Both packages were still in development. The renderer ran at single-digit frame rates on high-end PCs and the Java bridge code outgrew the library itself, so Red Storm replaced both in late 1997 and shipped 4 months late.","w":"Red Storm started 4 titles in 1996; the Rainbow Six team was Brian Upton and one other programmer. Understaffed, they licensed Virtus's unfinished renderer and IBM's Inverse, a Java networking library, for a C++ game. By late 1997 the renderer ran at single-digit frame rates and the C++-to-Java interface code outgrew the library itself. A November 1997 crisis plan pushed the spring ship date back 4 months, swapped Inverse for an in-house library and overhauled the renderer from source. The team crunched from February 1998 and shipped in July. Upton's verdict in 2000: own tech would have saved money.","r":"Both external packages replaced late; ship date slipped 4 months.","re":"Crisis plan in November 1997: more staff, in-house networking, renderer overhauled from source.","co":"4-month slip; 2 hectic months of rewrites; crunch February to July 1998.","nf":false,"l":"Do not build on middleware that is still in development; test it against your frame-rate and language needs first.","i":"Both packages were in flux, so Red Storm never did a thorough evaluation, and the interfaces kept changing until it was too late to push for fixes. The lesson is about unfinished middleware: Inverse worked fine in Red Storm's Java titles.","a":"Have we run our middleware at target frame rate in our language?","q":"“In retrospect, we would have saved money and had a much smoother development process if we’d bitten the bullet early on and committed ourselves to building our own technology base.”","qa":"Brian Upton, director of production design at Red Storm Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Redstorm's Rainbow Six","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-redstorm-s-i-rainbow-six-i-#:~:text=In%20retrospect%2C%20we%20would%20have,our%20own%20technology%20base","sd":"Jan 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Started production on a shared vision instead of a written design document","s":"The team built code and art it later scrapped, and with nothing to measure progress against, it saw the slip only when it became glaringly obvious.","w":"Rainbow Six began in fall 1996 with Brian Upton and one other programmer; the first artist joined in spring 1997. The team shared a clear idea of the game, so much of the design was never written down. Without a detailed outline, a lot of code and art were built and later scrapped, and progress could not be measured. The crisis came in October 1997, with single-digit frame rates on high-end systems. A crisis plan in early November pushed the spring ship date back 4 months. The team then crunched from February to its July 1998 ship date.","r":"Code and art were scrapped, and the slip surfaced late.","re":"A November 1997 crisis plan added people and troubleshooters and moved shipping 4 months.","co":"A lot of scrapped code and art; 4-month slip shared with tech problems.","nf":false,"l":"Before content production starts, write down what you are building in enough detail to track progress against it.","i":"A shared vision keeps a small team pointed the same way, but it gives no yardstick, so a slip stays invisible until it is large. The 4-month slip had other causes too: an overloaded lead, 16 unique levels and licensed tech that failed.","a":"Can we measure our progress against a written list of what we are building?","q":"“We never had a proper design document, which meant that we generated a lot of code and art that we later had to scrap.”","qa":"Brian Upton, director of production design at Red Storm Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Redstorm's Rainbow Six","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-redstorm-s-i-rainbow-six-i-#:~:text=We%20never%20had%20a%20proper,we%20later%20had%20to%20scrap","sd":"Jan 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Committed to 16 unique mission spaces with almost no shared art","s":"The art load helped push Rainbow Six behind schedule. Artists tried 4 or 5 production pipelines before they could build levels fast enough for a ship date moved back 4 months.","w":"Red Storm started Rainbow Six in fall 1996, and by April 1997 its story ran parallel to Tom Clancy's novel in progress. Because the novel drove level design, the team committed to 16 completely unique spaces, with almost no texture or geometry shared between missions. Artists built every space by hand in 3D Studio Max, with no shortcuts for corridors or stairwells. By October 1997 the project was slipping. The November crisis plan moved the spring ship date back 4 months. Artists tried 4 or 5 pipelines before levels came fast enough for July 1998.","r":"The art load was one of 3 causes of a 4-month slip.","re":"Artists sketched levels on paper and play tested geometry before texturing.","co":"4 or 5 art pipelines tried; a share of a 4-month schedule slip.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock the number of unique levels, build one to final quality and multiply its time by that number.","i":"With no shared geometry and no level tool, every space was modeled by hand, so art time grew with each level. Unique spaces were the point of the design here; the fix was a cheaper pipeline, not fewer levels.","a":"Have we built one level to final quality and timed it?","q":"“The art team tried out four or five different production pipelines before they finally found one that would produce the levels that we wanted in the time that we had available.”","qa":"Brian Upton, director of production design at Red Storm Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Redstorm's Rainbow Six","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-redstorm-s-i-rainbow-six-i-#:~:text=The%20art%20team%20tried%20out,that%20we%20had%20available","sd":"Jan 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"ratchetandclankfuturetoolsofdestruction","n":"Ratchet & Clank Future: Tools of Destruction","dv":"Insomniac Games","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Set pre-production to finish one complete playable level and used it to cut scope","s":"The level showed the 25-planet design would not fit. Insomniac cut to 16 planets and single player before production, so no production work was thrown away.","w":"In late 2005 about 12 Insomniac developers began a new Ratchet & Clank for PS3 with no hardware, engine or assets. At the start of 2006 they had 10 months before the full team joined, and set one goal: a fully realised playable level. By fall 2006 they had one semi-complete level, later Kerchu City. Its data showed the design of 25 planets, 5 space missions, 1 hour of cutscenes, co-op and online would not fit. The team cut to 16 planets, 3 missions, 45 minutes and single player. It shipped on October 23, 2007 after 12 months of production.","r":"Production started with an achievable plan, and no work was discarded.","re":"Insomniac added more post-production time to later projects.","co":"Almost a year for one level; 9 planets, co-op and online dropped.","nf":false,"l":"End pre-production with one finished level, measure what it took, and cut the full design to fit before production starts.","i":"One real level gives the team its own cost per level, so scope cuts happen on paper instead of in finished work. The test level missed finishing costs such as optimisation, crash bugs and localisation, so plan polish time separately.","a":"Have we costed one finished level and checked the full design against it?","q":"“When we scaled back, it was not a popular decision but it represented a turning point in the project.”","qa":"John Fiorito, COO of Insomniac Games","st":"Postmortem: Insomniac's Ratchet & Clank Future: Tools of Destruction","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-insomniac-s-i-ratchet-clank-future-tools-of-destruction-i-#:~:text=When%20we%20scaled%20back%2C%20it,turning%20point%20in%20the%20project","sd":"Feb 2008, reprinted Dec 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Made a non-interactive fly-through of Metropolis as the PS3 vision target","s":"Sony and the 2006 GDC audience compared it to film CGI. Sony then asked that later gameplay deliveries drop jaws the same way, and the team doubted it could match that in-game.","w":"In late 2005 most of Insomniac was getting Resistance: Fall of Man running on early PS3 hardware. About a dozen people began the new Ratchet & Clank with no hardware, engine, code or assets. They rebuilt Metropolis as a diorama in the Resistance engine, rendered a camera fly-through frame by frame and added audio. The team knew it showed hopes, free of the memory, frame rate and design limits of a real level. Sony's producers, then the 2006 GDC audience, compared it to film CGI, and Sony set the same bar for later deliveries. The game shipped in October 2007.","r":"Sony raised its bar for every later gameplay delivery to match the video.","re":"The team used the demo as its starting point to plan the game.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Label a vision video as a target, and tell the publisher which limits it ignores before they see it.","i":"A render without memory, frame rate or design limits looks better than any real level, so it sets the bar the publisher will measure gameplay against. The same video can still give a small team a shared picture of the game.","a":"Does the publisher know which limits our vision video ignores?","q":"“We were pleased with the outcome, but we knew that this was a guess at best and revealed more about our hopes for the game without the memory, frame rate, and game design constraints of a real level.”","qa":"John Fiorito, Insomniac Games","st":"Postmortem: Insomniac's Ratchet & Clank Future: Tools of Destruction","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-insomniac-s-i-ratchet-clank-future-tools-of-destruction-i-#:~:text=We%20were%20pleased%20with%20the,constraints%20of%20a%20real%20level","sd":"2008, reprinted Dec 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"raymanorigins","n":"Rayman Origins","dv":"Ubisoft Montpellier","y":"2011","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/207490/","ls":"Steam","cv":"raymanorigins.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Limited pre-production to the engine, the characters and one jungle world","s":"The other five or so worlds had only concept art. Designers worked blind in production, and gameplay elements that proved hard to reuse sent art, code and level work back to square one.","w":"Rayman Origins began at Ubisoft Montpellier as a 5-person team building the UbiArt framework under Michel Ancel. Pre-production built the engine, the camera, character and controls, the GenAnim tool and the start of a jungle world. The team chose engine and characters as the priority, so the other five or so worlds entered production with concept art only. Without tests of each world's powers, designers were flying blind until enough content existed. The team grew to about 90 and shipped on time in late 2011. In October 2012 designer Chris McEntee wrote that broader tests would have wasted less work.","r":"Gameplay elements for most worlds were tested in production, and some work was discarded.","re":"The team grew to about 90 people and shipped on time.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before production, prototype the defining mechanic of every world, even roughly, instead of finishing the first world.","i":"Concept art fixes how a world looks, not whether its powers carry enough levels, so that question moves into production, where each wrong answer costs art, code and level work. McEntee says testing every element up front was impossible: test the defining ones.","a":"Has each world's main mechanic been played, not just drawn?","q":"“But concept art alone does not satisfy the requirements for pre-production on a world.”","qa":"Chris McEntee, level and boss designer at Ubisoft Montpellier","st":"Rayman Origins","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_October_2012.pdf#:~:text=But%20concept%20art%20alone%20does,pre%2Dproduction%20on%20a%20world","sd":"Oct 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Staffing","t":"Started with a five-person team and grew it only once production began","s":"The small team set the game's identity and built the engine without deadline pressure. The team then grew to about 90 and got the game out on time.","w":"Rayman Origins began at Ubisoft Montpellier with 5 people under Michel Ancel, building an artist-friendly engine before anyone knew it would be a Rayman game. After several months of experiments, more people trickled in. For a year of pre-production the small core team built the UbiArt framework and the three Cs. Then the team grew to about 75 in Montpellier and 15 in Casablanca, with Ubisoft Reflections artists helping at the end. That team of about 90 got the game out on time in late 2011. In 2012 designer Chris McEntee called this one of the major things done right.","r":"The core team set the game's identity before production pressure began.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Keep the team small while the engine and three Cs are still moving, and add people only when production can use them.","i":"A small team can change direction cheaply, and nobody sits waiting on a moving engine. It leaned on UbiArt, which let a few people prototype fast. The short production that followed was still tough and needed help from Casablanca and Reflections.","a":"Are we adding people before the engine and three Cs are settled?","q":"“This is one of the major things that was done right on RAYMAN ORIGINS; the team was small when there was time to breathe, but when it was time to get the game out there, the team was able to expand with talent just in time to go full steam ahead and build a RAYMAN worthy of the franchise.”","qa":"Chris McEntee, level and boss designer at Ubisoft Montpellier","st":"Rayman Origins","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_October_2012.pdf#:~:text=This%20is%20one%20of%20the%20major,worthy%20of%20the%20franchise","sd":"Oct 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3,"sh":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_October_2012.pdf#page=30"}]},{"k":"redalert","n":"Red Alert","dv":"Westwood","y":"1996","sc":"AA","se":"Re-release 2024","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2229840/","ls":"Steam","cv":"redalert.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Allowed new unit ideas into a planned Command & Conquer expansion whenever they felt fun","s":"The new units piled up until Westwood decided to release the work as its own game, Red Alert, instead of an expansion pack.","w":"After Command & Conquer, Westwood planned its next release as an expansion pack. The team was free to invent new units and kept any that were fun to play. The additions grew fast. Soon there was so much new material that Westwood decided to package it as a standalone game. Red Alert came out in November 1996 on a modified version of the C&C 2D engine. Hardcore Gaming 101 reports it sold 1.5 million copies in its first month. Joe Bostic told the story in a 2018 interview.","r":"An expansion pack turned into a full standalone game.","re":"Westwood released the work as a new game, not an expansion.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Compare what the expansion now contains with its original plan at each milestone, and decide on purpose whether it has become a full game.","i":"Fun was the only filter for new units, so nothing capped the content. That worked because the engine and the base game already existed. Bostic gives no schedule or cost, so the growth may not have come free.","a":"Is our expansion still an expansion, or a new game?","q":"“Pretty soon, there were so many new things added to the game that it was decided to package it as a new stand-alone game – Red Alert.”","qa":"Joe Bostic, co-creator of Command & Conquer at Westwood Studios","st":"Joe Bostic (Westwood Studios) – Interview","su":"https://www.arcadeattack.co.uk/joe-bostic/#:~:text=Pretty%20soon%2C%20there%20were%20so,as%20a%20new%20stand%2Dalone%20game","sd":"Aug 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"redfall","n":"Redfall","dv":"Arkane Austin","y":"2023","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1294810/","ls":"Steam","cv":"redfall.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Committed a sub-100-person immersive-sim studio to a co-op games-as-a-service shooter in 2018","s":"Smith later said the team got swept up in games-as-a-service and should never have made one. Bloomberg reported 70% of the Prey veterans left; the game launched in 2023 to poor reception and Arkane Austin closed a year later.","w":"Development began in 2018, when ZeniMax was preparing for sale and, Bloomberg reported, pushing studios toward live-service games with microtransactions. Co-directors Harvey Smith and Ricardo Bare set out to mix what Arkane loved with games-as-a-service; references drifted from Far Cry to Borderlands, and the microtransaction plan was dropped in 2021. Hiring for a live-service title with under 100 people was hard, and 70% of the Prey staff left before the end. Redfall launched in 2023 to poor reception; after a year of patching thousands of bugs, Microsoft closed Arkane Austin, then let laid-off staff return to ship patch 1.4.","r":"A poorly received launch, a year of bug patching, then studio closure.","re":"Patched for a year; Smith's new studio stays small and independent.","co":"Under 100 staff; 70% of Prey veterans left; a year of post-launch patching.","nf":false,"l":"Match the business model to the team you have; do not greenlight live service for a single-player studio without buy-in and a staffing plan.","i":"A studio's craft is its people. Bloomberg's sources say the Prey veterans did not want a multiplayer game and left, while new hires wanted immersive sims. Smith said he assumed it would always work out. A team that wants the genre has no such drain.","a":"Does our current team actually want to build a live-service game?","q":"“We got swept up in the games-as-a-service thing, and I had the foolish chutzpah to assume that it always works out for us.”","qa":"Harvey Smith, co-director of Redfall at Arkane Austin","st":"Arkane veterans Harvey Smith and Ben Horne reunite: 'We're back to the games we love'","su":"https://thegamebusiness.com/p/arkane-veterans-harvey-smith-and#:~:text=We%20got%20swept%20up%20in,always%20works%20out%20for%20us","sd":"Aug 2026","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"renegadeops","n":"Renegade Ops","dv":"Avalanche Studios","y":"2011","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/99300/","ls":"Steam","cv":"renegadeops.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Locked a 16-month plan with one month each for alpha, beta and release","s":"The team's intuition said it was too little. The project slipped 2 months and missed a key promotional event by 2 or 3 weeks.","w":"Avalanche Studios pitched Renegade Ops to Sega late in the Just Cause 2 beta. The initial plan gave 16 months in total, with one month each for alpha, beta and release. Their intuition said it was not enough, but they expected to shift things later. Main production began 7 months in, after a strong vertical slice, with little room left. The team grew from 8 to 16 and felt it was always catching up. The project slipped 2 months and missed a key promotional event by 2 or 3 weeks. Lindberg's June 2012 postmortem called it vanity.","r":"Shipped 2 months late and missed a key promotional event.","re":"The studio said it will never again plan one-month alpha, beta and release phases.","co":"2-month slip; missed a major promotional event by 2 to 3 weeks.","nf":false,"l":"If the team doubts the schedule at kickoff, add time to alpha, beta and release before you lock it.","i":"A plan with no margin leaves the end phases to absorb every slip. The team expected to shift work later, but by main production the plan had no room. A fixed outside date, like a promotional event, turns a slip into lost marketing.","a":"Does the team believe our alpha, beta and release phases are long enough?","q":"“Our intuition warned us that this would not be enough, but we went ahead with the notion that we could shift things around once we got to the main production phase.”","qa":"Axel Lindberg, Avalanche Studios","st":"Postmortem: Avalanche Studios' Renegade Ops","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-avalanche-studios-i-renegade-ops-i-#:~:text=Our%20intuition%20warned%20us%20that,the%20main%20production%20phase","sd":"Jun 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"renownedexplorersinternationalsociety","n":"Renowned Explorers: International Society","dv":"Abbey Games","y":"2015","sc":"Indie","se":"Expanded edition 2016","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/296970/","ls":"Steam","cv":"renownedexplorersinternationalsociety.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Started building an in-house engine one month before concepting the new game","s":"AbbeyCore was far from ready when production began. Learning the new tech on top of that added months of delay to Renowned Explorers.","w":"In September 2013, a few months after Reus launched, Abbey Games began its own engine, AbbeyCore. Development of Renowned Explorers started a month later, in October. Nobody objected, though the team had people who had built engines before. When production started, the engine was far from ready, and learning the new tech added months of delay. Only in the second year was the engine largely release-ready. The game launched on September 2, 2015. The engine cost about 200K on top of the game's 600K budget. In 2016 Jansen wrote that the engine choice was starting to pay off.","r":"Production ran on an unfinished engine and slipped by months.","re":"Kept the engine; by the second year it was largely release-ready.","co":"About 200K for the engine on top of a 600K game budget; months of delay.","nf":false,"l":"Finish and test the engine before content production starts, or plan its delay and cost as a separate project.","i":"Building a new engine and a new game together means content waits on the tech, and the team learns both at once. An own engine can pay off over several games, as Abbey's started to, but only if the studio survives the first one.","a":"Will the engine be ready before content production starts?","q":"“Once we started producing the game, AbbeyCore was far from ready for it.”","qa":"Adriaan Jansen, designer and director at Abbey Games","st":"Post Mortem: Renowned Explorers","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/post-mortem-renowned-explorers#:~:text=Once%20we%20started%20producing%20the,far%20from%20ready%20for%20it","sd":"Sep 2016","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Hired a full team before the concept existed and let 9+ members steer it","s":"Paid staff had no tasks, and every doubt was settled with another compromise. It took up to 8 months to get Renowned Explorers on track.","w":"After Reus became a hit in 2013, Abbey Games chose a flat structure built on trust, where everyone could shape the next project. It hired people to build a company culture rather than for specific tasks, so most had nothing to do at first. Development started in October 2013 with 9+ people brainstorming, each with authority to steer. Renowned Explorers became the idea most people were game for, and doubts were fixed with more compromises. Progress was slow and some of the team grew upset. In 2016 Jansen's first lesson was not to apply democracy to the concept phase.","r":"Up to 8 months passed before the game was on track.","re":"Organised the team properly; the second year of development ran much better.","co":"Up to 8 months; salaries for staff without tasks.","nf":false,"l":"Run concepting with a small group that has clear creative authority, and hire only for work that exists now.","i":"When everyone can steer, the concept that survives is the one nobody objects to. Shared ownership still has value once there is a game to own; giving it before the concept exists turns every brainstorm into a negotiation.","a":"Does one person or a small group have the final say on the concept?","q":"“This of course is recipe for creating a project that is a compromise from the start.”","qa":"Adriaan Jansen, designer and director at Abbey Games","st":"Post Mortem: Renowned Explorers","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/post-mortem-renowned-explorers#:~:text=This%20of%20course%20is%20recipe,compromise%20from%20the%20start","sd":"Sep 2016","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"residentevil2","n":"Resident Evil 2","dv":"Capcom","y":"1998","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.gog.com/en/game/resident_evil_2","ls":"GOG","cv":"residentevil2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Story & world","t":"Scrapped a 70% complete sequel build and restarted the story, setting and cast","s":"The team had to speed up development to finish. The rebuilt game shipped in January 1998 and has sold 4.96 million units on PlayStation.","w":"Capcom's first sequel build, later called Resident Evil 1.5, had a realistic tone and a modern police station. Screenwriter Noboru Sugimura joined and read its finished scenarios. He found them too realistic and the station too modern and sterile, and told the team to rewrite them. With the build about 70% complete, director Hideki Kamiya's team scrapped everything and went back to the drawing board. Development then had to speed up. Resident Evil 2 shipped in January 1998, and that June Sugimura called the restart a very brave move.","r":"Most of a nearly finished build was discarded and the schedule tightened.","re":"Sugimura got the team to agree on one shared story document.","co":"About 70% of a finished build thrown away.","nf":false,"l":"Have the writer review story and tone before the build is far along, while a rewrite is still cheap.","i":"Tone lives in every room, prop and line, so a tone problem found late means rebuilding content, not patching text. The restart paid off at 4.96 million units, but it needed a publisher willing to fund a near-total rebuild.","a":"Has our writer read the scenarios and signed off on the tone?","q":"“Resident Evil 1.5 was actually about 70% complete then… (laughs)”","qa":"Hideki Kamiya, director of Resident Evil 2 at Capcom","st":"Resident Evil 2 – 1998 Developer Interview","su":"https://shmuplations.com/residentevil2/#:~:text=Resident%20Evil%201.5%20was%20actually,about%2070%25%20complete%20then","sd":"Jun 1998, translated Jan 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"residentevil4","n":"Resident Evil 4","dv":"Capcom","y":"2005","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/254700/","ls":"Steam","cv":"residentevil4.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Made a full model change of Resident Evil a premise for RE4","s":"The team overhauled the game midway with no clear picture of the result. The released version was the fourth iteration, and the planned 2004 launch slipped into 2005.","w":"Capcom began RE4 around July 2000, though full work started only after spring 2002. Producer Hiroyuki Kobayashi said a full model change of the series was a premise of the project. The team overhauled the game several times, with no clear picture of the result, and its over 100 Capcom staff went through a stagnant stretch. One version was shown at the Velfarre announcement, one at E3, and one was never shown. The fourth came together after Mikami came in as director. Aimed at 2004, the game slipped into 2005.","r":"Four iterations, and the planned 2004 release slipped into 2005.","re":"Capcom brought in Mikami as director, and the overhaul came together.","co":"4 iterations with over 100 Capcom staff; release slipped from 2004 into 2005.","nf":false,"l":"Before you restart a sequel's design, describe the target in a playable form the whole team agrees on.","i":"A mandate to change everything says what to drop, not what to build, so each person filled the gap with their own Resident Evil. Kobayashi credits the conflict with raising quality: the fault was the missing target, not the reinvention.","a":"Can everyone describe the same finished game we are rebuilding toward?","q":"“Definitely the fact that, although we decided to completely overhaul the game midway through, we didn't have a clear image in our heads about how that would ultimately look.”","qa":"Hiroyuki Kobayashi, producer of Resident Evil 4 at Capcom","st":"Resident Evil 4 – 2005 Developer Interview","su":"https://shmuplations.com/residentevil4/#:~:text=Definitely%20the%20fact%20that%2C%20although,how%20that%20would%20ultimately%20look","sd":"2005, translated Nov 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"residentevil7biohazard","n":"Resident Evil 7 Biohazard","dv":"Capcom","y":"2017","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/418370/","ls":"Steam","cv":"residentevil7biohazard.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Tested VR with a small Kitchen demo before committing the full game to it","s":"Strong reception at E3 2015 and Tokyo Game Show led Capcom to make the whole game playable in VR. Staff said it felt like working on two games.","w":"Capcom started Resident Evil 7 in February 2014, and some of the team were already curious about VR. Instead of planning a full VR game, they built a small first-person demo, Kitchen, and showed it at E3 2015. After positive reception there and at Tokyo Game Show, official VR development began in October 2015. A dedicated VR team, many of them R&D staff, adapted system programming and assets for both modes. Old habits such as still-camera cutscenes no longer worked. The game shipped in January 2017 with the full campaign playable in VR.","r":"Programming and assets had to work in both VR and non-VR.","re":"Capcom formed a dedicated VR team, many drawn from R&D.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you commit a whole game to a new platform, show players a small demo and plan for building two versions.","i":"A short demo answered the question that mattered, whether horror worked in VR, before the full bet. It did not price the bet: the demo needed no camera controls, and the main game still needed controls and cutscenes solved for 2 modes.","a":"Has a small public demo proved this platform bet before we commit?","q":"“Because of this, ‘it feels like working on two games’ was a pretty common sentiment felt by the development staff.”","qa":"Kazuhiro Takahara, lead VR engineer on Resident Evil 7 at Capcom","st":"Adding VR to Resident Evil 7 was 'like working on two games at once'","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/adding-vr-to-i-resident-evil-7-i-was-like-working-on-two-games-at-once-#:~:text=Because%20of%20this%2C,felt%20by%20the%20development%20staff","sd":"Mar 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Cast and costumed real models first, then scanned them into the game's characters","s":"Capcom's in-house scanning rig captured facial expressions from many angles in one shot, which it says sped up development dramatically. Casting, special-effects makeup and costumes now had to be finished before character work began.","w":"Resident Evil 7 began in 2014 with almost everything new to the team, from the RE Engine to VR. Capcom built one of the world's largest 3D scanning systems in-house to chase realism. Instead of designing most characters, the team auditioned models from a list and photographed them in real costumes. Technical artist Makoto Fukui says the workflow was revised so casting, special-effects makeup and costume coordination came before character design. Large objects had to be scanned bit by bit, which was tough. The game launched on 24 January 2017.","r":"Faster expression capture, with casting and costumes moved ahead of character design.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"An in-house scanning system, one of the world's largest.","nf":true,"l":"If you scan real people, lock casting, makeup and costumes before character work starts, and schedule them like a film shoot.","i":"Scanning turns character art into a capture job, so the expensive choices move up to casting and wardrobe. It fits realistic humans best; even on Resident Evil 7, large objects had to be scanned piece by piece.","a":"Are casting, makeup and costumes locked before our scanning sessions start?","q":"“To achieve this, the work flow was revised to complete casting, special-effects makeup and costume coordination in the steps prior to character design.”","qa":"Makoto Fukui, technical artist at Capcom","st":"vol.03 Jun Takeuchi |Developer Interviews 2016","su":"https://www.capcom.co.jp/ir/english/interview/2016/vol03.html#:~:text=the%20work%20flow%20was%20revised,prior%20to%20character%20design","sd":"2016","sy":"Company statement","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Went smaller after Resident Evil 6: first-person horror on a new in-house engine","s":"The game shipped in January 2017 as a single-player horror title. Capcom's 2017 report gave 3.5 million copies sold and a tenfold cut in test time from the RE Engine.","w":"After Resident Evil 6's global-scale production, producer Masachika Kawata said the team did not think going bigger was right and chose an intimate first-person horror game on the new RE Engine. Executive producer Jun Takeuchi recalled that Capcom marketing had pushed for multiplayer, DLC and microtransactions until president Kenzo Tsujimoto asked him to step in; the team cut multiplayer and kept single-player horror. When PSVR became available early on, the Kitchen demo tested first-person VR horror, and its reception led to a VR option. The game launched on 24 January 2017; Capcom's 2017 report put sales at 3.5 million.","r":"Shipped as a single-player horror game; the new engine cut program test time to one-tenth.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Cut the feature list to one focused core and test the riskiest new element with a short demo before you lock it.","i":"A single-player horror game needs fewer systems than a live-service feature list, which freed effort for the engine and camera. Going smaller worked because fans had asked for horror and Revelations and the HD remaster had been well received; without that signal the bet differs.","a":"What is our one risky new element, and has a demo tested it?","q":"“After having gone to a massive global scale with RE6, we didn't think trying to go bigger would be the right way to go.”","qa":"Masachika Kawata, producer of Resident Evil 7 at Capcom","st":"Capcom producer explains Resident Evil 7: Biohazard's return to horror","su":"https://gamesbeat.com/?p=134049#:~:text=After%20having%20gone%20to%20a%20massive,right%20way%20to%20go.","sd":"Jan 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"resogun","n":"Resogun","dv":"Housemarque","y":"2013","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.playstation.com/en-us/games/resogun/","ls":"PlayStation","cv":"resogun.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Started Resogun on the engine of the studio's previous shooter, Super Stardust Delta","s":"Shared sub-systems let the team build the base game right away. Its multithreaded task system later kept CPU load low and carried the automated checks for online play.","w":"After a concept video, Housemarque started Resogun on the Super Stardust Delta engine. Both games were shoot 'em ups mapped onto a 3D surface, so the team reused many sub-systems and began the base game at once. Early work targeted PS Vita. After about a year Resogun became a PS4 exclusive, with devkits arriving roughly 8 months before launch. The Vita-era fiber task system kept CPU load low and carried automated determinism checks for online play. Resogun launched with the PS4 in November 2013 after about 20 months of development.","r":"The team started on the base game at once.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you start a new engine, check whether your last game's engine already shares the new game's core structure.","i":"Both games mapped shooting onto a 3D surface, so the old sub-systems fit as they were. The saving holds only when the structure matches: Resogun still rethought most of its rendering and voxel tech for PS4.","a":"Which systems from our last engine fit this game without rework?","q":"“Since both games shared a similar design approach (being shoot 'em ups mapped onto a 3D surface), we were able to utilize many of the existing sub-systems and start working on the base version of RESOGUN immediately.”","qa":"Harry Krueger, lead programmer at Housemarque","st":"The game is the boss: A Resogun postmortem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/the-game-is-the-boss-a-i-resogun-i-postmortem#:~:text=Since%20both%20games%20shared%20a,base%20version%20of%20RESOGUN%20immediately","sd":"Apr 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"retrograde","n":"Retro/Grade","dv":"24 Caret Games","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/222660/","ls":"Steam","cv":"retrograde.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Scrapped the 6-12 month schedule to remake the art for PS3 without an artist","s":"The PS3 version took about 4 years of 80-hour weeks for its designer. It reviewed at about 80% but has not made a profit, and the custom engine will not be reused.","w":"24 Caret Games started in 2008 with a self-funded XBLA and PSN rhythm shooter planned for 6 to 12 months. A demo built in about 6 weeks earned 2 IGF nominations and interest from Sony. In early 2009 the artist co-founder left, and the 2 remaining founders scrapped the schedule to remake the art for PS3. They chose a pseudo-realistic 3D look, generated procedurally because neither could do art, on an engine meant for future games. The PS3 version shipped in August 2012. At GDC 2013, Gilgenbach said 2D would have looked better and shipped far earlier.","r":"A 6 to 12 month project took about 4 years on PS3.","re":"Gilgenbach ported it to Steam alone, part-time, over 6 months.","co":"About 4 years instead of 6 to 12 months; founders' savings; 750,000 lines of code.","nf":false,"l":"Before you raise the visual bar, check that the people already on the team can produce that art style.","i":"The look was picked for the hardware, not for the team. With no artist, the 2 programmers filled the gap with procedural tech and polish. A funded team can hire for a style, so the check is style against the staff you can afford.","a":"Can the people on our team today produce this art style?","q":"“We scrapped our schedule completely and wanted to try something more ambitious.”","qa":"Matt Gilgenbach, co-founder of 24 Caret Games","st":"Obsessive-Compulsive Development: Retro/Grade Postmortem","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=63UOLVP4dYk&t=340s","sd":"2013, reposted Jul 2018","sy":"Participant talk","w8":5,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=63UOLVP4dYk&t=345s"}]},{"k":"returnoftheobradinn","n":"Return of the Obra Dinn","dv":"3909","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/653530/","ls":"Steam","cv":"returnoftheobradinn.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Art & style","t":"Set a 640x360 one-bit style in 2014 and judged it fullscreen in 2018","s":"With most content built, the visuals were too uncomfortable for long play. Pope fixed it with a new dither and 800x450 resolution, then shipped a couple of months later.","w":"After Papers Please in 2014, Lucas Pope started Obra Dinn in Unity to bring back the 1-bit look of the Macintosh Plus. Early on he saw that the 640x360 image, stretched to fullscreen, fought the dithering, so he used dither as little as possible and carried on. In 2018, with most content built, he looked hard at the visuals on a 27-inch screen. They were too uncomfortable for long play. Stylized dither patterns strayed from the vision, so he built a sphere-mapped dither and raised resolution to 800x450. The game shipped on desktop a couple of months later.","r":"Rendering fixes landed in the final months, after most content was built.","re":"Pope built a sphere-mapped dither and raised resolution to 800x450.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Play a long session of your visual target at full screen size early, and repeat it at every milestone.","i":"Low resolution on a big screen makes each pixel physically huge, and dithering stops blending into gray. Pope saw that risk in 2014, then spent years on non-graphical work. The style survived because he changed the dither, not the art.","a":"Has anyone played our current build fullscreen for a long session?","q":"“At that point, the content and design side of the game was too far along to give up on the whole thing so instead I focused my efforts on solving this fullscreen problem.”","qa":"Lucas Pope, game developer at 3909 LLC","st":"Return of the Obra Dinn – Adventures in One Bit, Out Tomorrow","su":"https://blog.playstation.com/2019/10/17/return-of-the-obra-dinn-adventures-in-one-bit-out-tomorrow/#:~:text=At%20that%20point%2C%20the%20content,solving%20this%20fullscreen%20problem","sd":"Oct 2019","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"returnal","n":"Returnal","dv":"Housemarque","y":"2021","sc":"AAA","se":"Port 2023","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1649240/","ls":"Steam","cv":"returnal.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Chose a third-person AAA game on a new engine and platform over a top-down version","s":"The team grew from 25 to 80 and, by Krueger's account, the game was unplayable for about half of its development. Planned biomes fell from 13 to 6, and multiplayer and photo mode moved to post-launch.","w":"Harry Krueger's November 2014 open-world pitch was practically laughed out of the room. The dark planet concept that became Returnal was camera-agnostic; a top-down game would have been fine. In 2017 a crew of 5 prototyped it. Full development began in August 2017 with 25 people, and Housemarque chose to shoot for the stars: third-person, its first 3D game, first story-driven game, first Unreal Engine 4 project and first PS5 title. The new engine brought instability and regressions; for about half of development the game was unplayable. 13 planned biomes became 6. Returnal released on 30 April 2021.","r":"Half of development spent with a broken game; biomes cut from 13 to 6.","re":"Housemarque used Sony ExDev and CoDev contractors to bring the game to scope.","co":"Team of 25 grew to 80; about half of development time unplayable; money not stated.","nf":false,"l":"Count the firsts in your bet (engine, platform, camera, narrative); more than 2 at once means budgeting a long unplayable stretch.","i":"Each first was a risk the team had no experience to contain, and the unfamiliar engine turned them into constant regressions. Krueger presents the choice as deliberate and the game succeeded; the point is to price the firsts, not to avoid ambition.","a":"How many firsts are we taking on at once, and which can wait?","q":"“It's not an exaggeration to say that about half of the game's development time, the game was actually unplayable.”","qa":"Harry Krueger, Housemarque","st":"A third-person, action, roguelike, bullet-hell arcade thriller: The making of Returnal","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/marketing/a-third-person-action-roguelike-bullet-hell-arcade-thriller-the-making-of-returnal#:~:text=It's%20not%20an%20exaggeration%20to,was%20actually%20unplayable","sd":"Apr 2022","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"riseofnations","n":"Rise of Nations","dv":"Big Huge Games","y":"2003","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/287450/","ls":"Steam","cv":"riseofnations.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Swung the prototype between a classic RTS and a new strategy design for a year","s":"Several months of art and game design work were wasted. Outside events, such as the release of Empire Earth, finally showed a classic RTS was the wrong game.","w":"Big Huge Games formed in early 2000 and signed with Microsoft for a next-generation RTS. A playable solo prototype ran within the first month. Through the first year, studio and publisher went back and forth between a classic RTS, a new kind of strategy game, or something in between, and the prototype swung with them. Empire Earth's release and Microsoft's purchase of Ensemble Studios settled it. By then several months of art and design work were wasted, and the art look took longer to settle. In June 2003 Reynolds listed this second among what went wrong.","r":"Several months of art and game design work were wasted.","re":"For the next game the studio set its style: epic scope, strategic depth, large armies.","co":"Several months of art and game design work.","nf":true,"l":"Agree with the publisher, in writing, which kind of game you are making before art production starts.","i":"Fast prototyping pays only when someone decides what the prototype is testing. With studio and publisher both undecided, each swing pulled art and design along. The loss stayed at months because the team was still small, with just a couple of artists.","a":"Do we and our publisher agree in writing on what game this is?","q":"“In the first year, both we and the publisher waffled back and forth on whether we should be doing a \"classic\" RTS game, a completely new kind of strategy game with some real-time elements, or something in-between.”","qa":"Brian Reynolds, president of Big Huge Games","st":"Postmortem: Big Huge Games' Rise of Nations","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/disciplines/postmortem-big-huge-games-i-rise-of-nations-i-#:~:text=In%20the%20first%20year%2C%20both%20we,or%20something%20in%2Dbetween","sd":"Jun 2003","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Tested 10 to 15 wild RTS ideas in a playable prototype from month one","s":"Weak ideas were thrown out almost as soon as they went in. Multiplayer was fully playable more than 2 years before ship, which left years to refine the core and balance the game.","w":"Big Huge Games formed in early 2000 around veterans of Civilization II and Alpha Centauri, and pitched a new kind of RTS. The team had 10 to 15 wild ideas and expected only a few to work. A playable solo prototype ran within the first month, and every idea went straight into it. Some needed tweaking; others were cut almost at once. The whole company played regularly, and a designer summed up feedback after each daily session. Multiplayer was fully playable more than 2 years before ship, and in June 2003 Reynolds listed the method first under what went right.","r":"Weak ideas died early, and the core had years of refinement and balance.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Daily company-wide play sessions and pushback from staff attached to old RTS habits.","nf":true,"l":"Put every untested design idea into a playable build within the first month, and cut the ones that fail in play.","i":"A playable idea gets judged in days, before art and code pile up on it. That only works with a build the whole team plays daily. The prototype tested mechanics, not direction: the type of game still swung back and forth for a year.","a":"Can we play each of our risky ideas within a month?","q":"“We got a playable solo prototype running within the first month, and a fully playable multiplayer version more than two years prior to ship.”","qa":"Brian Reynolds, president of Big Huge Games","st":"Postmortem: Big Huge Games' Rise of Nations","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/postmortem-big-huge-games-i-rise-of-nations-i-#:~:text=We%20got%20a%20playable%20solo%20prototype,two%20years%20prior%20to%20ship","sd":"Jun 2003","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Hired few programmers early and put multiplayer and matchmaking on the lead","s":"The lead programmer became the bottleneck on critical systems. Coding and revision took longer than planned, and the programming staff ended the project badly overworked.","w":"Big Huge Games began Rise of Nations in 2000 as its first RTS, with fewer than 30 people. It did not hire enough programmers early, and scheduling and technical oversight were thin. The lead programmer carried several critical systems, including multiplayer and matchmaking, and became the bottleneck on them. Coding and revision both took more time than planned, while the team crammed in every feature it could think of. In June 2003, Brian Reynolds traced most of the overwork back to the early hiring.","r":"Programmers were badly overworked, and the lead held up critical systems.","re":"Planned more programmers next time, with the lead kept for management and support.","co":"Extra coding and revision time and an extremely overworked programming staff.","nf":true,"l":"Before production, list every critical system on your lead programmer, and hire so the lead only manages, supports and helps out.","i":"A lead who owns critical systems also reviews, plans and unblocks, so those systems wait on a single calendar. Hiring alone would not have fixed it: Reynolds also named thin scheduling, weak technical oversight and no sense of when the game had enough.","a":"Is our lead programmer the only owner of any critical system?","q":"“Most of this could be traced back to simply not hiring enough programmers early in the project, and was compounded by lack of scheduling and technical oversight.”","qa":"Brian Reynolds, president of Big Huge Games","st":"Postmortem: Big Huge Games' Rise of Nations","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/postmortem-big-huge-games-i-rise-of-nations-i-#:~:text=Most%20of%20this%20could%20be%20traced,scheduling%20and%20technical%20oversight","sd":"Jun 2003","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"riseofthetriad","n":"Rise of the Triad","dv":"Apogee","y":"1994","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/358410/","ls":"Steam","cv":"riseofthetriad.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Started the new in-house team's first game as a licensed Wolfenstein 3-D sequel","s":"id Software cancelled the deal about 6 months in, after tons of art and levels were done. Tom Hall wrote a new story to reuse as much of that work as possible.","w":"Apogee began building its own development team around 1993. Its first project was a Wolfenstein 3-D sequel under a deal with id Software. Tom Hall, an id founder, ran it, and the whole story was laid out. About 6 months in, with tons of art and levels done, John Romero called to say id was cancelling the project, and gave no reason. Hall wrote a new story built to use as many of the assets as possible. In 2009, Scott Miller said much of the shipped game came from the Wolfenstein version, which sold about 110,000 copies.","r":"About 6 months of art and levels had to fit a new story.","re":"Tom Hall wrote a new story built around the existing assets.","co":"A story rewrite to rescue about 6 months of finished art and levels.","nf":false,"l":"Before you build on a partner's IP, check what lets them cancel, and keep your assets usable outside that IP.","i":"A licensed sequel runs on the licensor's plans, and those can change in a single phone call. Apogee recovered because most art and levels could carry a new story. Assets built around a licence's characters and world would not have moved over so easily.","a":"If the licence ends tomorrow, which of our assets can we keep?","q":"“We basically had to come up with a story that would allow us to use as much of these assets as possible.”","qa":"Scott Miller, founder of Apogee","st":"20 Years Of Evolution: Scott Miller And 3D Realms","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/20-years-of-evolution-scott-miller-and-3d-realms#:~:text=We%20basically%20had%20to%20come%20up,these%20assets%20as%20possible","sd":"Aug 2009","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"roboblitz","n":"RoboBlitz","dv":"Naked Sky Entertainment","y":"2006","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/4300/","ls":"Steam","cv":"roboblitz.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Jumped into level building with almost no pre-production on an unfinished engine","s":"Key mechanics and character movement were never locked, so new weapons and controls were still arriving 6 months into an 11-month project. Levels, localised text, sound and art had to be redone weeks before content complete.","w":"RoboBlitz began as an 8-week Unreal Engine 3 tech demo shown at GDC 2005. Naked Sky's team of about 12 entered full production in November 2005 and jumped into level creation with very little pre-production, on a UE3 that was not yet fully functional. Six months in, new weapons and controls were still being added. Mechanics, controls, camera and level features changed weeks before content complete, forcing retranslation, sound and art fixes. The game slipped by multiple months, the money ran out, and the founders borrowed from friends. It shipped in November 2006 on PC, December on Xbox Live Arcade.","r":"Levels reworked across the whole game each time a mechanic changed.","re":"Borrowed from family and close friends to finish; shipped months late.","co":"Delayed multiple months; several hundred thousand dollars spent and more borrowed from friends.","nf":false,"l":"Lock the core mechanics, controls and camera before anyone builds a level.","i":"When a mechanic changes after the levels exist, every level has to be checked against it, and the fix cascades into text, sound and art. Fast design changes are fine in pre-production; the cost appears once the content is built.","a":"Are the controls and camera final before we build levels?","q":"“However, when we were adding new weapons and controls six months into an eleven-month project, it did not work out too well.”","qa":"Naked Sky Entertainment (team-written postmortem)","st":"Postmortem: Naked Sky Entertainment's RoboBlitz","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-naked-sky-entertainment-s-i-roboblitz-i-#:~:text=However%2C%20when%20we%20were%20adding,work%20out%20too%20well","sd":"Jan 2007","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"rocketleague","n":"Rocket League","dv":"Psyonix","y":"2015","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/252950/","ls":"Steam","cv":"rocketleague.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Rebuilt the 2008 car-soccer game with its core unchanged instead of redesigning it","s":"Rocket League launched in July 2015 and demand far outran Psyonix's expectations. Hagewood credited polish of the same concept, and better timing, for the gap with the original.","w":"Psyonix self-published Supersonic Acrobatic Rocket-Powered Battle-Cars on PlayStation Network in late 2008 to mild acclaim. It was too hardcore, and without dedicated servers, laggy connections hurt online play. For Rocket League the team kept the core, slowed the gameplay a little and built its own platform-agnostic online services for PC and PS4. The game launched on July 7, 2015, free for PlayStation Plus members. Demand was far higher than expected, and the team scrambled to keep servers up. Two weeks later founder Dave Hagewood said polish and timing made the difference.","r":"A polished version of the same design drew more demand than the servers handled.","re":"Psyonix scrambled to re-architect its servers for the higher load.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If an earlier game already proved the core loop, keep it and spend the new budget on what held it back.","i":"A loop players already enjoyed removes the biggest pre-production risk, so the budget goes to known weak spots: pace and online play. It works only if the earlier version failed for fixable reasons, which Hagewood names as execution and timing.","a":"What held our earlier version back, and are we fixing that?","q":"“We didn’t want to change the core of what that original Battle-Cars game was; we just wanted to do that game again, the right way.”","qa":"Dave Hagewood, founder of Psyonix","st":"Why some old designs are worth revisiting: A Rocket League story","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/why-some-old-designs-are-worth-revisiting-a-i-rocket-league-i-story#:~:text=We%20didn%E2%80%99t%20want%20to%20change,game%20again%2C%20the%20right%20way","sd":"Jul 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"rocketbowl","n":"RocketBowl","dv":"Large Animal Games","y":"2004","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Started production on an unfamiliar 3D engine with no pre-production phase","s":"The team learned Torque and 3D while building the game. Lead designer Wade Tinney says that inexperience at least doubled production time.","w":"Large Animal made web and casual games, mostly 2D in Flash and Shockwave, and funded its own titles from client work. RocketBowl production began in June 2003 on Torque, the team's first C++ 3D engine, with no pre-production or planning phase. The core team was 5 people, often pulled away to client games, with minimal documentation and no real budget. They learned the tools as they went and found some editor features only later. The game launched in November 2004. In June 2005 Tinney wrote that the new technology had at least doubled production time.","r":"Production time at least doubled because the team was new to 3D.","re":"An associate producer joined for the last 4 months and set up a roadmap.","co":"Production time at least doubled; 17 months from June 2003 to November 2004.","nf":false,"l":"Before production on a new engine, set aside an exploration phase to learn the tools and list the risks.","i":"On new tech, each production task also pays for learning the tools, so the whole schedule stretches. An exploration phase moves that learning to the start. The doubling is Tinney's estimate, and client work also split the team's focus.","a":"Have we planned time to learn this engine before production starts?","q":"“We jumped into production on RocketBowl too quickly, without a pre-production and planning phase.”","qa":"Wade Tinney, lead game designer and producer at Large Animal Games","st":"Indie Postmortem: Large Animal's RocketBowl","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/indie-postmortem-large-animal-s-i-rocketbowl-i-#:~:text=We%20jumped%20into%20production%20on,pre%2Dproduction%20and%20planning%20phase","sd":"Jun 2005","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"roguelegacy","n":"Rogue Legacy","dv":"Cellar Door Games","y":"2013","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/241600/","ls":"Steam","cv":"roguelegacy.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Built Rogue Legacy on a budget-first philosophy: cheap, fast and reusable solutions","s":"Two brothers and friends spent $14,878 over 17 months on a game that is good but not perfect. It recouped its cost within an hour of launch and sold 100,000 copies in a week.","w":"Cellar Door Games had made 8 earlier titles for about $1,000 each. In January 2012 programmer Teddy Lee started Rogue Legacy with the same rules: the design spec comes first, choose for cost and speed, and do not aim for perfection. A friend built the combat from June 2012; Teddy went full time in March 2013. The game launched on Steam on 27 June 2013 after 17 months and $14,878. It recovered its development cost in under an hour and sold 100,000 copies in its first week. Kenny and Teddy Lee presented the approach at GDC in March 2014.","r":"Shipped in 17 months for $14,878; profitable within an hour of launch.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"$14,878 and 17 months, with Teddy Lee full time only from March 2013.","nf":false,"l":"Set the budget ceiling before the design and let cheap, fast and reusable filter every feature.","i":"A hard budget turns every feature into a cost question, so the team ships solutions that are good enough instead of polishing. That works when the core loop carries the game and art can stay modest; Kenny Lee's own framing is good, not perfect.","a":"What is our budget ceiling, and which features fail the cheap-fast-reusable test?","q":"“We're always on the lookout for solutions that are cheap, fast, and reusable.”","qa":"Kenny Lee, co-founder of Cellar Door Games","st":"Video: Developing Rogue Legacy on a budget","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/video-developing-i-rogue-legacy-i-on-a-budget#:~:text=We're%20always%20on%20the%20lookout,fast%2C%20and%20reusable","sd":"Jun 2014","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Switched from a Dark Souls-inspired platformer to a roguelike when costs ran too high","s":"The team reused much of what it had already built, and Rogue Legacy cost just under $15K to make.","w":"Cellar Door Games started with a regular platformer inspired by how Dark Souls handled death: a real consequence that did not end the run. Shortly into development, the team saw the costs were getting too high. It switched to a roguelike, because random levels let it recycle much of what it had already built. Kenny Lee says everything started clicking and the game became much clearer. Rogue Legacy launched on Steam on June 27, 2013. At GDC 2014 Kenny and Teddy Lee said it cost just under $15K to make. Sony later approached them for PlayStation versions.","r":"The design became clearer and existing content could be reused.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Platformer plan dropped; whole game built for just under $15K.","nf":false,"l":"Re-cost the game early in development, and if content costs run too high, pick a structure that reuses what you have.","i":"Hand-built levels cost the same every time, while random assembly turns each piece into many rooms. The switch also changes the genre, so it works only if the new structure is still a game the team wants to make.","a":"Does our structure let us reuse content, or does every level cost new work?","q":"“Shortly into development though we realized the costs were getting too high, and the randomized nature of rogue-likes allowed us to recycle much of what we created.”","qa":"Kenny Lee, programmer and co-founder of Cellar Door Games","st":"Rogue Legacy Interview – Souls Inspiration, Vertigo, and Polygon Counts","su":"https://www.playstationlifestyle.net/2014/08/08/rogue-legacy-interview-souls-inspiration-vertigo-polygon-counts/#:~:text=Shortly%20into%20development%20though%20we,much%20of%20what%20we%20created","sd":"Aug 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"rollercoastertycoon","n":"RollerCoaster Tycoon","dv":"Chris Sawyer","y":"1999","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/285310/","ls":"Steam","cv":"rollercoastertycoon.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Chose pre-rendered isometric bitmaps over real-time 3D to keep the frame rate high","s":"The park ran smoothly on PCs of the day, but the view was locked. Every object needed thousands of bitmaps, which ate memory and Simon Foster's rendering time.","w":"Around 1996 or 1997, Chris Sawyer set Transport Tycoon 2 aside and started a rollercoaster construction set, coding it largely alone. He wanted a big, detailed park at a high frame rate on PCs of the era, so he chose pre-rendered bitmaps over real-time 3D. Simon Foster built the 3D models, tried several rendering packages, and rendered thousands of bitmaps for each object. An 8-bit fixed palette let objects show in many colour schemes at almost no cost. Hasbro Interactive released the game early in 1999. In 2024, Sawyer named the fixed view and the bitmap load as the price.","r":"Smooth play on period PCs, a fixed view, thousands of bitmaps per object.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Thousands of bitmaps per object, plus memory and Simon Foster's rendering time.","nf":false,"l":"Before you choose pre-rendered sprites, count the views and frames each object needs and multiply by the full object list.","i":"Pre-rendering moves the work from the player's CPU to the art pipeline: every angle and frame is a separate image. It pays when runtime speed is the hard limit. On hardware that can draw the scene live, the bitmap count is pure cost.","a":"How many images does each new object need?","q":"“Frame rate was my top priority, which for the graphics meant pre-rendered bitmaps rather than 3D rendering on the fly, but with the downside that the viewpoint was fixed and vast numbers of bitmaps were needed, taking up valuable memory (and Simon Foster’s time while rendering out all the thousands of bitmaps for each object).”","qa":"Chris Sawyer, creator of RollerCoaster Tycoon","st":"Interview with RollerCoaster Tycoon’s Creator, Chris Sawyer","su":"https://medium.com/atari-club/interview-with-rollercoaster-tycoons-creator-chris-sawyer-684a0efb0f13#:~:text=Frame%20rate%20was%20my%20top%20priority,thousands%20of%20bitmaps%20for%20each%20object","sd":"Mar 2024","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"rollersoftherealm","n":"Rollers of the Realm","dv":"Phantom Compass","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/262470/","ls":"Steam","cv":"rollersoftherealm.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Front-loaded staff into full Unity production before the core framework was built","s":"Programming became a bottleneck while designers and artists waited. Design ran ahead of the core scripts and combat, and many workarounds were later redone from scratch.","w":"Phantom Compass and laid-off AAA developers met in November 2011 to make a portfolio game they could build in 3 to 6 months. A 2D XNA prototype was ready for GDC in March 2012. In fall 2012 Canadian funds paid for a larger 3D game, so the team scrapped the prototype and restarted in Unity. Expecting a Unity learning curve, they front-loaded resources. Everyone learned Unity fast, and programming became the bottleneck. Design pushed ahead before the core scripts and combat system were done. The game shipped in November 2014, and the postmortem followed that December.","r":"Programmers became a bottleneck, and many design workarounds were rebuilt from scratch.","re":"The team redid the workarounds from scratch once the core system was complete.","co":"Design and code workarounds redone from scratch.","nf":true,"l":"Before staffing designers and artists on a new engine, finish the core scripts and combat framework they will build on.","i":"Extra people speed things up only when there is a framework to build on. Here the feared Unity learning curve never came, so the extra staff waited on programmers and built workarounds. Front-loading still pays when learning, not code, is the bottleneck.","a":"Is the core framework done before we add designers and artists?","q":"“We were eager to move from our original 6-month prototype into full production -- maybe too eager.”","qa":"David Evans, creative director at Phantom Compass","st":"Postmortem: Pinball-RPG hybrid Rollers of the Realm","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-pinball-rpg-hybrid-i-rollers-of-the-realm-i-#:~:text=We%20were%20eager%20to%20move%20from,maybe%20too%20eager","sd":"Dec 2014","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"rust","n":"Rust","dv":"Facepunch Studios","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/252490/","ls":"Steam","cv":"rust.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built Rust on the code of an unrelated prototype web game","s":"The code made every change slow and fragile. In mid-2014, with 1.6 million copies already sold, Facepunch rebuilt the game from scratch.","w":"Facepunch built Rust on code from Cash4kills, a prototype web game: a Hitman-style title in a GTA-style open world. Rust sold in Steam Early Access and had earned over $30 million by February 2014. Much of the old code had nothing to do with Rust, and changing one system broke others. Earlier in 2014 the team started an experimental branch and re-implemented pretty much everything. In June 2014 Garry Newman said many early decisions were made before the team knew what game it was making. The rebuilt Rust reached 1.0 in February 2018.","r":"Core systems ran 3,000 lines where 100 would do, and changes broke others.","re":"Facepunch rebuilt the game from scratch on an experimental branch.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Once the concept is found, start production on fresh code instead of growing the prototype into the game.","i":"Prototype code carries the rules of a game you were still searching for, so every later system works around them. Early Access sales paid for Facepunch's rewrite; a team without that income pays for it from the schedule.","a":"Is any of our production code still from the prototype?","q":"“There were a lot of decisions made when we didn't know what game we were making.”","qa":"Garry Newman, Facepunch Studios","st":"Rust is rebooting: here's why","su":"https://www.pcgamesn.com/rust/rust-rebooting-heres-why#:~:text=There%20were%20a%20lot%20of%20decisions,what%20game%20we%20were%20making","sd":"Jun 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"stalker2heartofchornobyl","n":"S.T.A.L.K.E.R. 2: Heart of Chornobyl","dv":"GSC Game World","y":"2024","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1643320/","ls":"Steam","cv":"stalker2heartofchornobyl.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Announced S.T.A.L.K.E.R. 2 for 2021 in May 2018, years before the team reached production size","s":"The date slipped six times. The game shipped on 20 November 2024, about three years late, after the team grew from 65 people in 2020 to about 470.","w":"Sergiy Grygorovych had closed GSC in 2011 and shelved the sequel. In 2018 he and Ievgen Grygorovych revived it, and on 16 May 2018 he announced S.T.A.L.K.E.R. 2 while the official site showed the number 2021; StopGame, citing Sergey Galyonkin, reported the project was at the design-document stage. Grygorovych funded development himself until 2020, when that was no longer enough and Maksym Krippa took ownership of a 65-person team. Forbes Ukraine counted six delays before launch on 20 November 2024 with about 470 staff. Krippa put the cost at tens of millions of dollars and said the game was profitable.","r":"Six public delays; release three years after the announced year.","re":"Held the final November 2024 date rather than delay a seventh time.","co":"65 to about 470 staff; tens of millions of dollars; 3 years late.","nf":false,"l":"Do not publish a release year before the team that will build the game exists; count the hires first.","i":"A date published before the team exists turns every later change into a public slip. GSC's CEO described a team too exhausted for another delay, and 270 staff worked in Ukraine through the war, so the early date explains the announcements, not the three years.","a":"How many of the people needed to hit that date are hired today?","q":"“Когда я пришел в компанию в 2020 году, команда состояла из 65 человек” (When I joined the company in 2020, the team consisted of 65 people.)","qa":"Maksym Krippa, owner of GSC Game World","st":"S.T.A.L.K.E.R. 2 уже окупилась, в планах два DLC и сериал на Netflix, — владелец GSC Максим Криппа","su":"https://itc.ua/news/s-t-a-l-k-e-r-2-uzhe-okupylas-v-planah-dva-dlc-y-seryal-na-netflix-vladelets-gsc-maksym-kryppa/amp/#:~:text=%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%B4%D0%B0%20%D1%8F%20%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BB%20%D0%B2%20%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%8E,%D0%B8%D0%B7%2065%20%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%BA","sd":"Dec 2024","sy":"Press report","w8":3},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Adopted an early Unreal Engine 5 and built its own open-world systems on top","s":"Epic later shipped its own versions of those systems, so every engine upgrade meant re-merging everything. Moving the shipped game from UE 5.1 to 5.5.4 for update 2.0 cost an enormous amount of time.","w":"After announcing S.T.A.L.K.E.R. 2 in May 2018, GSC built it on Unreal Engine 4 instead of its own X-Ray engine: 60 Unreal engineers were easier to hire than 60 X-Ray ones. The team then moved to an early version of UE5, whose open-world features did not fit the game's scale, and made many custom changes to make it work. Epic later introduced its own systems doing the same things, so each upgrade meant re-merging everything. The game shipped on 20 November 2024 on UE 5.1. Update 2.0 in 2026 moved it to UE 5.5.4; COO Maria Grygorovych: 'Can I cry?'","r":"Every engine upgrade meant re-merging GSC's custom code with Epic's new systems.","re":"Re-merged everything onto UE 5.5.4 for update 2.0 after launch.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If you adopt an engine before its open-world tools mature, stay close to vanilla and budget for re-merging every custom system.","i":"Custom systems written where the vendor has a gap collide with the vendor's own version of the same thing, and the collision repeats on every upgrade. GSC still calls Unreal the best solution for it, mainly because of hiring.","a":"Which of our custom engine systems will the vendor ship itself next year?","q":"“To upgrade to those newer versions, we had to remerge everything.”","qa":"Ievgen Grygorovych, CEO and head of game direction, GSC Game World","st":"'Can I cry?': Stalker 2 devs say its 2.0 update's engine overhaul was a nightmare—but worth the effort","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/games/fps/can-i-cry-stalker-2-devs-say-its-2-0-updates-engine-overhaul-was-a-nightmare-but-worth-the-effort/#:~:text=To%20upgrade%20to%20those%20newer,had%20to%20remerge%20everything","sd":"Sep 2026","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Dropped the in-house X-Ray engine for Unreal Engine 4 when starting Stalker 2","s":"GSC's CEO says the reason was hiring: about 60 engineers with Unreal experience can be found, while 60 with X-Ray experience cannot. Both studio heads say they do not miss X-Ray.","w":"The earlier Stalker games ran on GSC's in-house X-Ray engine. When GSC started Stalker 2, it built the game on Unreal Engine 4 instead, and later moved to an early version of Unreal Engine 5. The game shipped in November 2024. At Gamescom 2026, CEO Ievgen Grygorovych told PC Gamer the studio needed a lot of engineers, and finding about 60 with X-Ray experience was impossible. COO Maria Grygorovych added that Unreal experience is better for developers' careers than a proprietary engine. Asked if they missed X-Ray, both said no.","r":"Stalker 2 was built on an engine with a large hiring pool.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Count the engineers you will need and check you can hire them with experience in the engine you pick.","i":"A proprietary engine limits hiring to people the studio trains, which caps how fast an engineering team can grow. The trade is control over the tech: heavy changes to a licensed engine bring their own upgrade costs. For a small team, hiring weighs less.","a":"Can we hire the engineers we need with this engine's experience?","q":"“It's much more possible to find 60 engineers who know how to work with Unreal.”","qa":"Ievgen Grygorovych, CEO and head of game direction at GSC Game World","st":"'Can I cry?': Stalker 2 devs say its 2.0 update's engine overhaul was a nightmare—but worth the effort","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/games/fps/can-i-cry-stalker-2-devs-say-its-2-0-updates-engine-overhaul-was-a-nightmare-but-worth-the-effort#:~:text=It%27s%20much%20more%20possible%20to,how%20to%20work%20with%20Unreal","sd":"Sep 2026","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"satisfactory","n":"Satisfactory","dv":"Coffee Stain Studios","y":"2024","sc":"AA","se":"Early Access 2019","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/526870/","ls":"Steam","cv":"satisfactory.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built Satisfactory in Unreal and let artists script their own work in Blueprints","s":"Artists turned their own work around faster, and programmers carried less of the scripting load.","w":"After Coffee Stain stopped work on Goat Simulator, the studio ran a prototyping period. Staff who liked Factorio built a 3D first-person factory prototype, and it convinced the studio to go ahead. The team built it in Unreal Engine 4, an engine its people had used since before the studio was called Coffee Stain. Blueprints let artists write much of their own scripting. The game entered Early Access on the Epic Games Store on 19 March 2019, and that month programmer Jace Varlet credited Blueprints with faster artist turnaround. Version 1.0 reached Steam in September 2024.","r":"Artists scripted more of their own work, freeing programmer time.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Pick an engine your team already knows, and list which scripting jobs artists can take off your programmers.","i":"Visual scripting lets artists change their own content without waiting for a programmer, so each change comes back faster. The gain covers work artists can own; it says nothing about engine fit for huge factories, which still needed LOD and culling work.","a":"Which scripting tasks could our artists do without a programmer?","q":"“In my opinion, Blueprints is fantastic as it is intuitive for our artists to do a lot of scripting themselves, which means faster turnaround on their work and less load on programmers.”","qa":"Jace Varlet, programmer and community manager at Coffee Stain Studios","st":"How Satisfactory is combining crafting, gunplay, and vehicles into one ambitious building simulator","su":"https://www.unrealengine.com/developer-interviews/how-satisfactory-is-combining-crafting-gunplay-and-vehicles-into-one-ambitious-building-simulator?lang=en-US#:~:text=In%20my%20opinion%2C%20Blueprints%20is%20fantastic,less%20load%20on%20programmers","sd":"Mar 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"sayonarawildhearts","n":"Sayonara Wild Hearts","dv":"Simogo","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1122720/","ls":"Steam","cv":"sayonarawildhearts.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Built each stage as one long custom animation synced to the beat","s":"Little of the game could be systemized, and the team had too few tools for the systems it did build. Work became slow and laborious, which led to frustration and stress.","w":"The idea began when Simon Flesser pictured a masked avenger on a motorcycle, and a rough driving prototype set to pop songs turned it into a pop album. Simogo wanted a game that always shifted and felt custom, so each stage became one long animation synced to the beat, edited frame by frame on a timeline. Much of it could not be systemized. The team built many systems but too few tools for them, and work turned slow and stressful. The game shipped in 2019; in March 2025 Flesser said the idea was perhaps too much for a small team.","r":"Work stayed slow and laborious, causing frustration and stress.","re":"Flesser says Lorelei and the Laser Eyes later went much better on daily progress.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If every level must feel hand-made, plan and build the editing tools first, because systems will not carry that load.","i":"When content cannot be systemized, every second of it is hand work, and the tools set how fast that work goes. Simogo built the systems but not the tools, so each change cost more. A game built on reusable rules needs less of this.","a":"Do we have a fast editing tool for every hand-made sequence?","q":"“While we did create a lot of systems, we didn’t create enough tools to interact with them, so work often became laborious and slow, which led to frustration and stress.”","qa":"Simon Flesser, director of Sayonara Wild Hearts at Simogo","st":"Interview Transcript: Sayonara Wild Hearts’ Simon Flesser, March 2025","su":"https://www.lowpolypigeons.com/blog/interview-transcript-sayonara-wild-hearts-simon-flesser-march-2025#:~:text=While%20we%20did%20create%20a,to%20frustration%20and%20stress","sd":"Mar 2025, reprinted Dec 2025","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"scalebound","n":"Scalebound","dv":"PlatinumGames","y":"2017","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2017","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Took on Unreal Engine, online play and a photoreal look, all new to PlatinumGames","s":"The team could not get past the combined hurdles. Microsoft cancelled the Xbox One and PC game in early 2017, the year it was due.","w":"PlatinumGames announced Scalebound for Xbox One at E3 2014 with Microsoft as publisher, and added a PC version at E3 2016. Director Hideki Kamiya wanted a high-end, photorealistic game to raise the studio's graphics skills. The team also worked on Unreal Engine, an environment it was not used to, and lacked the know-how to build a game around online features. Release was planned for 2017, but Microsoft announced its cancellation early that year. In November 2021 Kamiya apologised to players and Microsoft and said the team was not experienced enough to get over that wall.","r":"The team never got past the hurdles, and the game was cancelled.","re":"Kamiya publicly apologised to players and Microsoft in 2021.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"List every first for your team in the plan and prototype each one before greenlight.","i":"A new engine, an online layer and a higher visual bar each need learning time, and on one team they compete for the same people. PlatinumGames' Atsushi Inaba later said both sides failed, so inexperience is Kamiya's account, not the full cause.","a":"How many things in this plan has our team never shipped before?","q":"“We weren’t experienced enough and couldn’t get over that wall, leading to what happened in the end.”","qa":"Hideki Kamiya, director of Scalebound at PlatinumGames","st":"Hideki Kamiya on Scalebound cancellation: 'We weren't experienced enough'","su":"https://www.gematsu.com/2021/11/hideki-kamiya-on-scalebound-cancellation-we-werent-experienced-enough#:~:text=We%20weren%E2%80%99t%20experienced%20enough%20and,what%20happened%20in%20the%20end","sd":"Nov 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"seaofstars","n":"Sea of Stars","dv":"Sabotage Studio","y":"2023","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1244090/","ls":"Steam","cv":"seaofstars.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Staffing","t":"Kept most staff on Messenger DLC while a small group started Sea of Stars","s":"The rest of the studio stayed on established work instead of being laid off, and moved onto the new game once the DLC was out.","w":"In October 2018, about 6 weeks after The Messenger launched, Thierry Boulanger pitched Sea of Stars to the team together with DLC for The Messenger. He started both because Sabotage did not want to lay people off. The bulk of the team worked on the DLC, which built on established work. A smaller team did the groundwork for Sea of Stars, since a whole studio cannot work the first weeks of a new idea. After the DLC came out, everyone moved onto the new game. In August 2023, just before launch, Boulanger said development had run almost 5 years.","r":"The full team stayed busy while the new game was still an idea.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Start a new game with a small team, and keep everyone else on established work, such as DLC, until the concept holds.","i":"The first weeks of a new idea cannot absorb a whole studio, so extra people would sit idle or build on a concept that keeps changing. The bridge needs paid, established work to keep them on, here DLC for a game already out.","a":"What will the rest of the team work on during our first weeks?","q":"“Meanwhile, we do the groundwork for the next big thing, and then DLC comes out, and then we can bring everyone onto the next project.”","qa":"Thierry Boulanger, founder and creative director of Sabotage Studio","st":"\"I Don't Want To Copy\" - Paying Homage To Childhood RPGs With Sea Of Stars","su":"https://www.nintendolife.com/features/i-dont-want-to-copy-paying-homage-to-childhood-rpgs-with-sea-of-stars#:~:text=Meanwhile%2C%20we%20do%20the%20groundwork,onto%20the%20next%20project","sd":"Aug 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"shadowofthecolossus","n":"Shadow of the Colossus","dv":"Team Ico","y":"2005","sc":"AA","se":"Remake 2018","lk":"https://www.playstation.com/en-us/games/shadow-of-the-colossus/","ls":"PlayStation","cv":"shadowofthecolossus.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Halved the planned 48 colossi to 24 before modeling, then kept 16","s":"The team picked the 16 by completion level and by how distinct each fight was, folding ideas from cut colossi into the survivors. The game held its schedule.","w":"When Fumito Ueda planned the game, he had 48 colossi in mind. He judged that many impossible and halved it to 24 before modeling and fight design began. During development, 24 looked doable but hard to finish at quality, so the team settled on 16. They kept colossi by completion level, dropped those beaten in similar ways, and merged elements of 2 colossi into 1. A nearly finished spider colossus was cut, and its defeat idea came back in the ninth. In December 2005, project manager Kenji Kaido said the staff's efforts kept the game on schedule.","r":"The game shipped with 16 colossi and stayed on schedule.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"8 of 24 modeled colossi dropped, including a nearly finished spider.","nf":false,"l":"Count how many bosses you can finish at quality inside the schedule, and cut to that number before modeling starts.","i":"Each colossus needed its own model and its own way to be beaten, so every cut removed a whole block of work. Merging ideas into survivors kept the best parts. Ueda still cut twice: his first estimate was too high.","a":"How many bosses can we finish at full quality by the date?","q":"“Putting in that many would have been impossible, however, so I cut it down to 24, half the original number.”","qa":"Fumito Ueda, director of Shadow of the Colossus","st":"The Making of the Colossi","su":"https://glitterberri.com/extended-developer-interview/making-of-the-colossi/#:~:text=Putting%20in%20that%20many%20would%20have,half%20the%20original%20number","sd":"Dec 2005, translated Oct 2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"shadowtacticsbladesoftheshogun","n":"Shadow Tactics: Blades of the Shogun","dv":"Mimimi Productions","y":"2016","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/418240/","ls":"Steam","cv":"shadowtacticsbladesoftheshogun.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Art & style","t":"Moved from an abstract art style to a richer, more detailed look after prototype feedback","s":"The final style took longer than expected to find and more time to produce. Mimimi spent all its reserves on a 4-month extension and cut all planned DLC levels.","w":"Mimimi pitched Shadow Tactics to Daedalic in 2014, with a style drawn from Japanese ink drawings. Viewers of the first graphical prototypes said it looked like a mobile game. The team moved to a less abstract, more detailed style, which took unexpectedly long to settle and was slower to produce. At Alpha in December 2015, the vertical slice looked much better than first pictured, and the schedule did not cover that bar. Mimimi spent all its reserves on 3 more months, and Daedalic funded a fourth. The game shipped on December 6, 2016, with the studio nearly bankrupt.","r":"Production ran 4 months longer and all planned DLC levels were cut.","re":"Mimimi paid for 3 extra months from reserves; Daedalic funded a fourth.","co":"All company reserves for 3 months, 1 publisher-funded month, DLC levels cut.","nf":false,"l":"Show the graphical prototype to target players, then price the chosen art style per level before you lock the schedule.","i":"Fixing a style problem raises the visual bar, and every level then costs more to build. The team chose its setting carefully but gave the art style less weight, so the schedule kept the pitch's cost. A small team has no slack to absorb that.","a":"Have we priced our final art style per level in the schedule?","q":"“After showing our first graphical prototypes, we got a lot of feedback about the game looking too much like a mobile game.”","qa":"Dominik Abé, creative director and co-founder of Mimimi Productions","st":"Postmortem: Mimimi's Shadow Tactics: Blades of the Shogun","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-mimimi-s-i-shadow-tactics-blades-of-the-shogun-i-#:~:text=After%20showing%20our%20first%20graphical,too%20much%20like%20a%20mobile%20game","sd":"Dec 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"shadowrundragonfall","n":"Shadowrun: Dragonfall – Director’s Cut","dv":"Harebrained Schemes","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/300550/","ls":"Steam","cv":"shadowrundragonfall.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Wrote an in-house AI script language so designers could tune enemies without programmers","s":"Designers iterated on AI behaviour about 10 times faster than before. The Director's Cut shipped with better AI and less engineering time, on a tight schedule.","w":"Shadowrun Returns shipped on 25 July 2013, and the Dragonfall expansion followed 7 months later. When Harebrained Schemes listed what to improve for the Director's Cut, smarter AI was near the top. The old AI was a branching XML tree, and every tuning change needed a programmer and a slow rebuild of assets and code. The team wrote its own small language, GumboScript, instead of adopting Lua. Designers then changed AI behaviour themselves. One cover bug got a brute-force fix before ship. The Director's Cut came out on 18 September 2014.","r":"AI changes came about 10 times faster, with less engineering time.","re":"Fixed the one cover bug with a limited brute-force script.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you build combat content, give designers a way to change AI behaviour without a programmer or a full rebuild.","i":"Every AI change used to wait for a programmer and a rebuild, so designers tried few ideas. GumboScript removed that wait. It was built for a shipped game with known combat rules, so the gain says little about writing a language for an unproven design.","a":"Can our designers change AI behaviour today without asking a programmer?","q":"“Giving the designers the ability to iterate on AI behavior by themselves allowed for updates to be made on an order of magnitude faster than our previous system.”","qa":"Kevin Maloney and Sheridan Thirsk, Harebrained Schemes","st":"Cooking Up Gumbo: The Evolution of Turn-Based AI in Shadowrun","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/cooking-up-gumbo-the-evolution-of-turn-based-ai-in-shadowrun#:~:text=Giving%20the%20designers%20the%20ability,faster%20than%20our%20previous%20system","sd":"Nov 2014","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"shank","n":"Shank","dv":"Klei Entertainment","y":"2010","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/6120/","ls":"Steam","cv":"shank.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Locked the combat feel in 2 months, then built content tools for 9 months","s":"With the tools ready, the team built the last 13 levels in about 3 months and engineers worked only 3 weekends. Designers and artists still took a content crunch at the end.","w":"Klei started Shank in January 2009. Jamie Cheng and Jeff tested the moves in a Flash prototype over one weekend, and within 2 months a demo had the look and feel that shipped. That demo ran on cobbled-together old tools, so the team spent about 9 months on in-house level and FX tools. At PAX East 2010 only 2 levels existed. Between March and June 2010 the team built the other 13. Shank launched in 2010, and Xbox Live Arcade reported 41,000 units in its first week.","r":"13 levels were built in about 3 months once the tools existed.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"About 9 months of tools work, then a content crunch for designers and artists.","nf":false,"l":"Lock the core feel in a quick prototype, then build the content tools before you start making levels.","i":"Once the moves stopped changing, the tools had a fixed target, so level building went fast and engineers avoided crunch. Content still came last: designers and artists took the crunch, made worse by a second project running in parallel.","a":"Is our core feel locked enough to start building content tools?","q":"“We spent the better part of the year building a brand new pipeline for Shank.”","qa":"Jamie Cheng, CEO of Klei Entertainment and executive producer of Shank","st":"The Journey to Creating SHANK","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCntyU1nlpE&t=1253s","sd":"Mar 2011","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"qu":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/pc/road-to-the-igf-klei-entertainment-s-i-shank-i-"}]},{"k":"shenmue","n":"Shenmue","dv":"Sega AM2","y":"1999","sc":"AAA","se":"Remaster 2018","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/758330/","ls":"Steam","cv":"shenmue.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned a 45-hour cinematic open-world RPG for unreleased next-generation hardware","s":"The team grew past 300 people, none of whom had worked on a team that size, and tracked over 10,000 action items in Excel. The game shipped on Dreamcast in 1999, 4 years after the first prototype.","w":"Yu Suzuki, used to 3-minute arcade games, prototyped an RPG on the Saturn in 1995 and in 1996 planned a full-3D game on the Virtua Fighter engine with 45 hours of play. Screenwriters and film directors helped write the scenario. In 1997 the project moved to Sega's next console, before the Dreamcast existed, and in 1998 it became Shenmue. The team passed 300 people, with no project management tools beyond an Excel action list that topped 10,000 items. Data worth 50 to 60 discs had to fit on 3. Shenmue shipped in 1999; Suzuki told the story at GDC 2014.","r":"A 300-person team was managed through paper job sheets and a 10,000-item Excel list.","re":"Compression tech and a QA team filming 24 hours a day shipped it in 1999.","co":"Over 300 people; 4 years from the 1995 prototype to the 1999 release.","nf":false,"l":"When the concept needs a team far larger than anyone on it has run, decide in pre-production how the work will be tracked.","i":"Suzuki went from 3-minute arcade games to 45 hours of open-world content in one step, and team size followed the content. He says the chapter design, with new stages for every story beat, gave poor cost performance; today's engines and tools would change that math.","a":"Who here has managed a team this big, and with what tools?","q":"“It's frightening to think we managed this project basically by pushing around sheets of paper.”","qa":"Yu Suzuki, creator of Shenmue at Sega, speaking at GDC 2014","st":"Yu Suzuki recounts the making of Shenmue","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/yu-suzuki-recounts-the-making-of-i-shenmue-i-#:~:text=frightening%20to%20think%20we%20managed,pushing%20around%20sheets%20of%20paper","sd":"Mar 2014","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Estimated early that the voiced Dreamcast adventure would fit on 1 disc","s":"The voicework needed 20 times the space. The disc plan grew to 2, then 3, while new content kept going in and the team kept growing.","w":"Sega AM2 built Shenmue for the Dreamcast with a large amount of voicework. Early on, the team said fitting the data would be easy, maybe on 1 disc. The estimate was off by an order of magnitude: the game needed 20 times more space. The plan grew to 2 discs, then 3, as new things kept going into the game. Director Keiji Okayasu recalled that work suddenly got busy and the team grew without anyone announcing it. Shenmue shipped in winter 1999, and in a 2000 interview producer Yu Suzuki called memory the biggest struggle.","r":"The game needed 20 times the estimated space and 3 discs.","re":"Suzuki's team built its own compression to keep the disc count down.","co":"20 times the estimated space and 2 extra discs.","nf":false,"l":"Before you size the disc or download, record a representative set of voiced scenes and measure the space they take.","i":"Voice audio grows with every recorded line, so a storage guess made before recording breaks as the script grows. Here new content kept going in at the same time, so the estimate and the scope moved together.","a":"Have we measured the space a full voiced scene takes?","q":"“I don’t know who made that estimation, but they were off by an order of magnitude: we needed a full 20x more space!”","qa":"Yu Suzuki, producer of Shenmue at Sega AM2","st":"Shenmue – 2000 Developer Interview","su":"https://shmuplations.com/shenmue/#:~:text=who%20made%20that%20estimation,a%20full%2020x%20more%20space","sd":"2000, translated Jan 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"shenmueii","n":"Shenmue II","dv":"Sega AM 2","y":"2001","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/758330/","ls":"Steam","cv":"shenmueii.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Drew storyboards for each cutscene before motion capture and showed them to actors","s":"Approvals from director Yu Suzuki started coming through again. Motion capture got more efficient, and operators spent far less time assembling scenes around the captured motion.","w":"On Shenmue II, Sega's authoring team took scripts, directed actors at the motion capture studio, assembled scenes and showed them to director Yu Suzuki. His notes were often abstract, such as make it a bit more powerful, and retakes piled up until nobody knew what to do. Around 1999, anime director Kazuya Murata was seconded to Sega from OLM. His first step was storyboards that showed actors where the camera stood for each moment. Approvals began to pass, and in a 2019 interview Murata said assembly time per operator dropped dramatically.","r":"Stalled cutscene approvals moved again, and scene assembly got much faster.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Get the director to sign off on a storyboard with camera positions before you book the capture studio.","i":"Abstract notes on a finished scene send the team back to capture, the most expensive step. A storyboard moves the disagreement to paper, where a change is cheap. It works only when the director can judge a scene from drawings.","a":"Does the director approve each scene on paper before we capture it?","q":"“More than anything, motion capture became much more efficient, and the amount of time each operator needed to assemble scenes around the captured motion dropped dramatically.”","qa":"Kazuya Murata, cutscene director on Shenmue II, seconded to Sega from OLM","st":"The Director Behind Shenmue II's Cutscenes - Kazuya Murata","su":"https://www.phantomriverstone.com/2026/05/the-director-behind-shenmue-iis.html#:~:text=More%20than%20anything%2C%20motion%20capture,captured%20motion%20dropped%20dramatically","sd":"2019, translated May 2026","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"shovelknight","n":"Shovel Knight","dv":"Yacht Club Games","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"Expanded edition 2019","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/250760/","ls":"Steam","cv":"shovelknight.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Squeezed a 2-year plan into 1 year at half pay to fit $328K","s":"The plan had no slack. Shovel Knight missed its March 31 target, the money ran out on March 1, and the team worked 5 months without pay.","w":"Yacht Club's Kickstarter closed on 13 April 2013, leaving about $328,682 for development. The team's own estimate for 6 people over 2 years, stretch goals included, was 144 man-months, or $1.44 million at $10,000 each. Composer Jake Kaufman agreed to be paid after release. The team moved stretch goals to free post-release updates, cutting the plan to 1 year, then halved each person's budget to $5,000 a month. The game missed its March 31 target, and the money ran out on 1 March 2014. The team worked 5 months unpaid, and Shovel Knight shipped on 26 June 2014.","r":"The release slipped past March 31 after the budget had run out.","re":"The team cut all non-essential spending and kept working unpaid until launch.","co":"5 months without pay; launch slipped from March 31 to June 26.","nf":false,"l":"Budget the base game with room for a slip, and check how many months your cash lasts past the target date.","i":"Halving pay and cutting a year made the budget fit on paper with no margin, so any slip came out of the team's own pockets. Strong launch sales, 75,000 copies in the first week, closed the gap; nothing in the plan did.","a":"How many months can we pay everyone if we miss the date?","q":"“We ended up operating for five months without money or payments to the team here.”","qa":"Yacht Club Games","st":"Sales Breakdown: One month later!","su":"https://old.yachtclubgames.com/2014/08/sales-one-month/#:~:text=We%20ended%20up%20operating%20for,payments%20to%20the%20team%20here","sd":"Aug 2014","sy":"Company report","w8":5},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"External commitments","t":"Promised free stretch-goal campaigns in the Kickstarter at tens of thousands of dollars each","s":"Each stretch goal grew into a full game: the $25,000 King of Cards goal cost $2.5 million and 18 months. The studio spent 6 years and about $9 million on one IP.","w":"Yacht Club's Kickstarter raised $311,502 and promised extra playable campaigns and a 4-player battle mode as free stretch goals. Shovel of Hope shipped in June 2014 after 18 months with 6 people for $1.4 million. Plague of Shadows and Specter of Torment followed, each larger. King of Cards, budgeted at $25,000 in the Kickstarter, took 18 months, 16 people and $2.5 million; Showdown, budgeted at $50,000, took 12 months and $1.5 million. Both shipped in December 2019, 6 years after the campaign. A 2022 studio blog put the Treasure Trove at 110 months and $9 million.","r":"6 years and about $9 million on one IP before new games.","re":"Delivered every goal, then moved to new IP wishing scope had been clearer.","co":"$2.5 million and 18 months for a $25,000 stretch goal; $9 million in total.","nf":false,"l":"Cost every stretch goal as a full production, team times months, before you promise it to backers.","i":"A public promise fixes the deliverable but not its size, so each campaign grew to the studio's current quality bar and team. The boundary: the game sold well enough to carry it; a weaker seller would not have survived the same promises.","a":"What would each stretch goal cost if it became a full game?","q":"“That's one big NES game from a Kickstarter game funded for only $311,502.”","qa":"Yacht Club Games, studio blog (no named author)","st":"Sales Breakdown: A Couple Pocket Months Later","su":"https://www.yachtclubgames.com/blog/sales-breakdown-a-couple-pocket-months-later#:~:text=That%27s%20one%20big%20NES%20game,funded%20for%20only%20%24311%2C502","sd":"2022","sy":"Participant account","w8":5}]},{"k":"simcity","n":"SimCity","dv":"Maxis","y":"2013","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.ea.com/games/simcity/simcity","ls":"Website","cv":"simcity.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Engineered regional play so each player's PC sent all city data to servers","s":"When the team later added offline play, it had to move the server system into the client and rewrite core code. The lead engineer projected over 6½ months of work.","w":"The original vision for SimCity was a game where every action affected the other cities in a region. To build it, the team set up each player's PC to send all of its information to the servers. Without that link, cities could not talk to each other or even save. In August 2013 work began on an offline single-player mode, which meant moving the server system from Java into C++ on the client and reworking saving and loading. By January 2014 the mode had reached alpha, planned for Update 10.","r":"Offline play needed a rewrite of core systems after launch.","re":"Moved regional simulation into the client and removed online-only features.","co":"Over 6½ months of rewriting core code, as projected by the lead engineer.","nf":false,"l":"Before you put core simulation on servers, cost out an offline version and decide now whether you will ever need one.","i":"When saving, loading and regional logic all assume a server, offline play becomes a port of server code into the client. The risk applies when offline play might be needed later, not to games that will always stay online.","a":"If players demand offline play, how many months would it cost us?","q":"“By the time we’re finished we will have spent over 6 ½ months working to write and rewrite core parts of the game to get this to work.”","qa":"Simon Fox, lead engineer on SimCity's Single Player Mode at Maxis","st":"Engineering SimCity for Offline Play","su":"https://community.simtropolis.com/news/engineering-simcity-for-offline-play-r619/#:~:text=By%20the%20time%20we%E2%80%99re%20finished,to%20get%20this%20to%20work","sd":"Jan 2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"singularity","n":"Singularity","dv":"Raven Software","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/42670/","ls":"Steam","cv":"singularity.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Moved Singularity to third person with sticky cover after early focus tests","s":"The game drifted away from its time-manipulation hook. The team cut sticky cover and went back to first person, losing a lot of work.","w":"Raven built a 3-month first-person prototype around the Time Manipulation Device (TMD), and Activision approved the new IP. Early focus testing suggested a third-person shooter, as other popular games had gone that way. Raven and Activision both accepted a third-person prototype with sticky cover. The original prototype still felt better to play. The team listed every feature and checked whether it supported TMD use. It cut sticky cover and returned to first person, losing a lot of work. Singularity shipped on June 29, 2010, after 4 years with a 65-person core team.","r":"Third-person camera, animation and rigging work was dropped.","re":"The team audited every feature against the TMD and cut what did not fit.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Check every proposed feature against the mechanic your prototype proved, and cut the ones that do not support it.","i":"Focus tests reward what players know from other games, and each accepted change pulls the design toward those games. Raven was already handling a new IP, a new engine and a new team, so third person was one risk too many.","a":"Does each feature on our list make the core mechanic stronger?","q":"“We knew it felt better to play our original prototype than our current game, and we began to compare differences.”","qa":"Rob Gee, project lead at Raven Software, with 5 co-authors","st":"Raven Software's Singularity","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_September_2010.pdf#:~:text=We%20knew%20it%20felt%20better,began%20to%20compare%20differences","sd":"Sep 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"sinsofasolarempire","n":"Sins of a Solar Empire","dv":"Ironclad","y":"2008","sc":"AA","se":"Expanded edition 2011","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/201290/","ls":"Steam","cv":"sinsofasolarempire.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Spent a year prototyping real-time 4X before self-funding full development","s":"Early playable builds convinced Ironclad the concept worked and brought pacing, the hardest problem, to the surface early. Self-funded development began in winter 2005.","w":"In fall 2003, former Barking Dog and Rockstar Vancouver developers founded Ironclad Games and began building new technology. The first goal was only to test whether a real-time game could keep the depth of 4X. After a year of almost pure prototyping and wild ideas, Ironclad was convinced, and self-funded development began in winter 2005. Early playable builds exposed many gameplay issues and gave a head start on pacing, which became the hardest problem. Sins shipped in February 2008, and by March it was among the top 3 PC games at retail.","r":"Ironclad started self-funded production sure that the core concept worked.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About 1 year of prototyping before self-funded production began.","nf":false,"l":"Before you fund production of a genre blend, play rough builds until the team is sure the core works.","i":"A genre blend has no reference game to copy, so only play shows which parts survive real time. The year bought confidence, not money: publishers still turned away a new studio with a new IP in a new genre.","a":"Has the team played the core blend enough to know it works?","q":"“After a year of almost pure prototyping, experimentation, and just trying out wild ideas Ironclad was convinced the idea would work, and self-funded development began on Sins in the winter of 2005.”","qa":"Brad Wardell, CEO and president of Stardock","st":"Postmortem: Ironclad/Stardock's Sins of a Solar Empire","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-ironclad-stardock-s-i-sins-of-a-solar-empire-i-#:~:text=After%20a%20year%20of%20almost%20pure%20prototyping,in%20the%20winter%20of%202005","sd":"Apr 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Let publisher Stardock co-design the game and moved the release twice","s":"The design changed at its core, and the game gained features, content and balance. It shipped in February 2008, a year after the first target, into a market with almost no competition.","w":"Stardock met Ironclad in summer 2006, when Ironclad hoped to ship in February 2007. After much discussion, the release moved to August 2007 so the game could be fleshed out. Nearly every design element got input from both teams, including the tech tree and ship roles. In August 2007 Stardock proposed 6 more months, which would push back Ironclad's other projects; Stardock paid advances to help. Sins shipped in February 2008 and was a top 3 PC retail seller by March. Wardell's April 2008 postmortem still named a single-player campaign as lost to budget and time.","r":"The release slipped 12 months, and the original design changed at its core.","re":"Stardock paid advances to cover part of the extra development cost.","co":"12 months of extra development; Ironclad's next projects pushed back.","nf":false,"l":"Before you move the date for a partner, write down what each extra month adds and who pays for it.","i":"A start-up pays for extra months with its next projects, so Stardock's advances helped carry the delay. Timing helped too: February 2008 had almost no major PC releases. The same delay into a crowded season can cost the sales it was meant to win.","a":"Who pays for each extra month, and what does it add?","q":"“Delaying the game six more months was not an easy decision for Ironclad to make.”","qa":"Brad Wardell, CEO and president of Stardock","st":"Postmortem: Ironclad/Stardock's Sins of a Solar Empire","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-ironclad-stardock-s-i-sins-of-a-solar-empire-i-#:~:text=Delaying%20the%20game%20six%20more%20months,decision%20for%20Ironclad%20to%20make","sd":"Apr 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"skullandbones","n":"Skull and Bones","dv":"Ubisoft Singapore","y":"2024","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2853730/","ls":"Steam","cv":"skullandbones.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Turned an Assassin's Creed IV multiplayer expansion into a standalone pirate game","s":"Its core idea then moved from a 5v5 naval arena to an open world to survival play. A late 2018 target became a February 2024 release.","w":"Development began in 2013 as a multiplayer expansion for Assassin's Creed IV: Black Flag, then became a new pirate IP at Ubisoft Singapore. Ubisoft revealed it at E3 2017 as a session-based naval shooter due in late 2018. After E3 2018 the team moved to a multiplayer open world, survival mechanics came in 2019, and direction shifted again in 2020. Staff grew from about 100 in 2015 to about 400 in 2019. Kotaku's 2021 investigation counted three creative directors. The game shipped on 16 February 2024.","r":"The game shipped more than 5 years after its late 2018 target.","re":"Ubisoft announced a new vision in September 2020 and delayed launch again.","co":"More than $120 million by 2021, per Kotaku's sources; team grew to about 400.","nf":false,"l":"Before a feature spins off into its own game, run a fresh concept gate and prove the new core loop.","i":"Naval combat worked as one part of Black Flag, but it did not define a whole game, so the team kept searching for one while it grew to about 400 people. The lesson applies to spin-offs that inherit a mechanic but not a proven loop.","a":"Has the spin-off's own core loop passed a concept gate yet?","q":"“Skull and Bones took time to find the right recipe to deliver our pirate fantasy.”","qa":"Elisabeth Pellen, creative director of Skull and Bones at Ubisoft Singapore","st":"An Interview with Skull and Bones Creative Director Elisabeth Pellen","su":"https://seasonedgaming.com/2022/07/26/an-interview-with-skull-and-bones-creative-director-elisabeth-pellen/#:~:text=Skull%20and%20Bones%20took%20time,deliver%20our%20pirate%20fantasy","sd":"Jul 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"skullgirls","n":"Skullgirls","dv":"Reverge Labs","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/245170/","ls":"Steam","cv":"skullgirls.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Art & style","t":"Hand-animated every Skullgirls fighter at high resolution, about 1,300 frames each","s":"Each core fighter cost $200,000 to $250,000. After the team was laid off, Lab Zero crowdfunded new fighters one at a time: $150,000 for Squigly and $200,000 for Big Band.","w":"Skullgirls was built around 8 fighters, each 100% hand-animated with an average of 1,300 high-resolution frames, which its PlayStation Blog debut called the most of any fighting game. More fighters were planned as DLC. The game launched in April 2012. The team was later laid off and carried on as Lab Zero Games. In February 2013 CEO Peter Bartholow told Siliconera each core character had cost $200,000 to $250,000. Lab Zero asked fans for $150,000 to finish Squigly, about a third done, with staff taking pay cuts. Big Band, the stretch goal, needed $200,000, and both were funded.","r":"Every new fighter needed its own $150,000 to $200,000 budget.","re":"Lab Zero crowdfunded each DLC fighter, with staff on reduced pay.","co":"$200,000 to $250,000 per core character; Squigly budgeted at $150,000.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock the art style, price one finished character at final quality and multiply by the roster you plan.","i":"With hand-drawn frames, cost scales with animation count, so every fighter carries the same large bill. Bartholow calls the price cheap for that much animation, so the issue is roster size against funding, not waste. Without a publisher, each new fighter became its own fundraiser.","a":"What does one finished character cost, times our planned roster?","q":"“For comparison, though, the core Skullgirls characters cost between $200-250k to create, and we have it on pretty good authority that is actually quite cheap, especially for the amount of animation we put into our characters.”","qa":"Peter Bartholow, CEO of Lab Zero Games","st":"Skullgirls Characters Cost $250k Each To Create; Here’s Why Squigly Is Cheaper","su":"https://www.siliconera.com/skullgirls-characters-cost-250k-each-to-create-heres-why-squigly-is-cheaper/#:~:text=For%20comparison%2C%20though%2C%20the%20core,animation%20we%20put%20into%20our%20characters","sd":"Feb 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"skychildrenofthelight","n":"Sky: Children of the Light","dv":"thatgamecompany","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2024","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2325290/","ls":"Steam","cv":"skychildrenofthelight.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Designed Sky's touch controls around console players who hate virtual d-pads","s":"The custom scheme took a year and a half to build. Most mobile players had already adapted to virtual d-pads, and Chen felt much of that R&D was wasted.","w":"After Journey in 2012, thatgamecompany began Sky, its first mobile-first game, projected at 3 years. Its console players hated virtual d-pads, so the team set out to invent a control scheme native to the touchscreen. That work took a year and a half. Meanwhile mobile players had simply adapted to the virtual d-pad, and players who had never touched a controller outnumbered the console audience 10 to 1. Sky took 7 years and launched in 2019. In April 2022 Jenova Chen said a huge amount of time and R&D went into a feature only a fragment of players responded to.","r":"A year and a half went into controls that few players valued.","re":"","co":"A year and a half of control-scheme R&D.","nf":false,"l":"Before you build custom controls, check which controls your target platform's players already use and accept.","i":"The team designed for the players it already had, not the far larger audience on the new platform, who were used to virtual d-pads. The lesson covers controls players already accept; it says nothing against new input where the standard one cannot do the job.","a":"Do players on our target platform already accept the standard controls?","q":"“It felt like we had wasted a huge amount of time and R&D on a feature that only a fragment of our audience would really respond to.”","qa":"Jenova Chen, co-founder and creative director of thatgamecompany","st":"Jenova Chen on Sky’s arduous seven-year development and monetising his way","su":"https://www.pocketgamer.biz/jenova-chen-sky-seven-year-development-monetising-his-way/#:~:text=It%20felt%20like%20we%20had,would%20really%20respond%20to","sd":"Apr 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"skytorn","n":"Skytorn","dv":"Noel Berry","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"Cancelled 2018","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Mixed a story-driven Metroidvania with procedural generation without settling which one led","s":"The two goals kept fighting, and the story drifted linear over several years of work. After a pause for Celeste, finishing meant throwing away much of the code and design, so Skytorn was cancelled in 2018.","w":"Noel Berry went indie in 2012 and worked on Skytorn for several years with a small team. He wanted a Metroidvania with a story, built on a procedurally generated world. The procedural parts kept clashing with the Metroidvania design, and the story and progression drifted linear because the team was unsure how to handle a random open world. In 2016 the team started Celeste as a short side project, and Skytorn development stopped completely. In December 2018 Berry cancelled Skytorn, saying finishing it would mean throwing away much of the code and design.","r":"Skytorn was cancelled after several years of work.","re":"Berry later built Earthblade as a designed world instead of a procedural one.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Prototype the clash between your 2 core pillars first, and pick which one leads before you build content.","i":"Procedural generation pays off only when the design uses the randomness, while an authored story needs fixed places and order. Without a ruling on which leads, each system weakens the other. The two can coexist when one is clearly in charge.","a":"Which of our 2 core pillars wins when they conflict?","q":"“Skytorn just never figured out what it was.”","qa":"Noel Berry, creator of Skytorn","st":"Skytorn is cancelled","su":"https://medium.com/@NoelFB/skytorn-is-cancelled-d40c5d9d35e5#:~:text=Skytorn%20just%20never%20figured,what%20it%20was","sd":"Dec 2018","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"slaythespire","n":"Slay the Spire","dv":"Mega Crit Games","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2017","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/646570/","ls":"Steam","cv":"slaythespire.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Launched into Early Access with weekly patches and a player feedback pipeline","s":"The first 2 weeks brought about 2,200 sales and the founders thought the game was dead. 14 months of weekly patches later, it left Early Access and passed 1.5 million copies.","w":"Anthony Giovannetti and Casey Yano, both from QA jobs, started Slay the Spire in 2015 as a 2-person team. They built a metrics server, daily builds and a Discord feedback bot that logged 2,800 bug and feedback points before launch. Steam Early Access began in November 2017 with an update promised every week. Yano told GDC 2019 that the first 2 weeks brought about 2,200 sales, falling to 90 a day, until a Chinese streamer picked it up in week 3. Sales hit 700,000 by April 2018; the game left Early Access in January 2019 after 57 patches.","r":"Weekly patches took about a day each; Early Access players did the QA.","re":"Yano said he would move to 2-week builds if he did it again.","co":"2 developers plus contractors, 2015 to January 2019; about a day per weekly patch.","nf":false,"l":"If you launch in Early Access, budget the update cadence as production work and instrument balance data from day one.","i":"The metrics server and feedback bot turned Early Access players into the QA and balance team, so 2 people could ship 57 patches. The sales turnaround came from a streamer the team never planned for; the pipeline only kept the game ready for that moment.","a":"Is our build and feedback pipeline ready for weekly patches at launch?","q":"“It's a slower start than we had hoped for, although reception is positive beyond our imagination.”","qa":"Casey Yano, co-lead of Mega Crit Games","st":"Seattle Indies Spotlight: Mega Crit Games","su":"https://seattleindies.org/seattle-indies-spotlight-mega-crit-games/#:~:text=a%20slower%20start%20than%20we,beyond%20our%20imagination","sd":"Dec 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"slimerancher","n":"Slime Rancher","dv":"Monomi Park","y":"2017","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2016","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/433340/","ls":"Steam","cv":"slimerancher.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Prototyped only the core loop and tested it as a finished 15-minute game","s":"The prototype's loop and the final game's loop ended up exactly the same. Features that did not serve that loop were cut early, and some came back during Early Access.","w":"Monomi Park was two people when it began Slime Rancher on Unity with the UFPS asset package. Nick Popovich's first crude prototype sold his partner Mike Thomas on the idea. They then built a second prototype that looked roughly like the final game but covered only the core loop. Testers got it as a finished 15-minute game and were asked if they would buy it. Random worlds, a required vehicle and extra enemy types were cut. After two unfunded years the game was in Steam Early Access by January 2016, and 1.0 followed in August 2017.","r":"The final game kept the prototype's loop without changes.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Prototype the loop players repeat 90% of the time and test it with strangers as a finished game.","i":"Testers can judge only what is in front of them, so a complete small loop gets honest answers that promised features cannot. It fits loop-driven games; Popovich's own advice is to defer crafting or multiplayer unless the game is about them.","a":"Would a stranger want to buy our prototype as it is today?","q":"“Prove this by testing it for what it is not for what you hope it'll become.”","qa":"Nick Popovich, co-founder and CEO of Monomi Park","st":"Slime Rancher: A Preemptive Postmortem","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gyDOrNbvFN8&t=1028s","sd":"2017, reprinted Jan 2018","sy":"Participant talk","w8":3}]},{"k":"smuta","n":"Smuta","dv":"Cyberia Nova","y":"2024","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Accepted a state grant whose contest rules fixed a 2-year production schedule","s":"The release date could not move. Smuta shipped in 2024 unfinished, and the studio has been finishing it with patches since.","w":"Cyberia Nova spent about 1.5 years on self-funded pre-production for Smuta, after dropping a Dynasty Warriors-style concept for a historical game. In 2022 it brought the work to IRI, a state funder, and won a grant. Money came only after each shown result, and the contest rules set 2 years of development with no chance to delay release. The team also chose Unreal Engine 5 and accepted a high risk. The game shipped in 2024 and drew public criticism, including over ready-made assets. In July 2024 CEO Aleksei Koptsev said 2 years was far too little for an AA game.","r":"The fixed date forced release before the game was finished.","re":"The studio kept finishing the game through post-release patches.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you sign funding, get a contract that lets the release date move with the scope.","i":"A funder's fixed schedule makes the release date the one thing that cannot give, so scope and quality give instead. Koptsev blames rules borrowed from other industries; IRI now plans to adjust them, so such terms can be negotiated.","a":"Can our release date move if the scope needs it?","q":"“Два года на разработку AA-игры — очень и очень мало.” (Two years to develop an AA game is very, very little.)","qa":"Aleksei Koptsev, CEO of Cyberia Nova","st":"Алексей Копцев о создании «Смуты» и госфинансировании: «Два года на разработку AA-игры — очень и очень мало»","su":"https://app2top.ru/industry/razrabotka-smuty-gosfinansirovanie-dva-goda-ochen-malo-aleksei-kopcev-219772.html#:~:text=%D0%94%D0%B2%D0%B0%20%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0%20%D0%BD%D0%B0%20%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BA%D1%83%20AA%2D%D0%B8%D0%B3%D1%80%D1%8B,%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%8C%20%D0%B8%20%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%8C%20%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%BE","sd":"Jul 2024","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4}]},{"k":"sneakysasquatch","n":"Sneaky Sasquatch","dv":"RAC7 Games","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/sneaky-sasquatch/id1098342019","ls":"App Store","cv":"sneakysasquatch.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"External commitments","t":"Pitched two games to Apple Arcade expecting only one to be approved","s":"Apple approved both. RAC7 had to switch between two productions at once, which co-founder Jesse Ringrose says pushed the team to its limit.","w":"Sneaky Sasquatch began in a game jam more than 4 years before April 2020, then went on the shelf because it seemed too risky to sell on mobile. When Apple Arcade came along, RAC7 dusted it off and pitched two games, expecting probably only one to get in. Both were approved. The team, which could barely stick to a plan on one game, had to switch back and forth between two. Sneaky Sasquatch launched on Apple Arcade in September 2019. In April 2020 Ringrose called working on two games at once the studio's biggest challenge.","r":"The team ran two Apple Arcade productions at the same time.","re":"","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you pitch several projects, plan how your team would staff all of them if every pitch is approved.","i":"A pitch that might win is a promise the team might have to keep. Two approvals meant two productions for the same people, and switching between them cost focus on top. A studio with a spare team can absorb a second yes.","a":"If every pitch we send gets approved, who builds each one?","q":"“We can barely stick to a plan working on one game, but having to switch back and forth between two pushed us to the limit.”","qa":"Jesse Ringrose, co-founder of RAC7 Games","st":"Indie Spotlight: How RAC7 Games earned two game approvals for Apple Arcade","su":"https://www.pocketgamer.biz/indie-spotlight-rac7-games-jesse-ringrose/#:~:text=We%20can%20barely%20stick%20to,pushed%20us%20to%20the%20limit","sd":"Apr 2020","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"sniperghostwarrior3","n":"Sniper Ghost Warrior 3","dv":"CI Games","y":"2017","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/368070/","ls":"Steam","cv":"sniperghostwarrior3.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Chose a large open world and a AAA position at the start of development","s":"The CEO later said the team and timeframe could not deliver that scale in reasonable time, and called the AAA label a big mistake.","w":"When CI Games began Sniper Ghost Warrior 3, it chose a large open world and aimed the game at the AAA category. The team then spent heavy effort trying to match other AAA games in production values and features. Release moved from the end of 2016 to January 2017, then to April 2017. TheSixthAxis's review criticised the story and the bugs. In June 2017 CEO Marek Tymiñski said the scale was too ambitious and the AAA label a big mistake. In 2018 CI Games booked a PLN 12.2M allowance for unamortised SGW3 development costs.","r":"Release slipped twice, from late 2016 to April 2017.","re":"Planned the next sniper game without open world or the AAA tag.","co":"Two release delays; PLN 12.2M allowance for unamortised development costs in 2018.","nf":false,"l":"Check the world size and the production-value target against your actual team and deadline before development starts.","i":"A AAA label sets the bar by other studios' games, so effort goes into matching their features instead of what the team does best. An open world multiplies the content needed per hour of play, and that math depends on team size.","a":"Does our team size match the world size and the quality bar we promised?","q":"“We simply made the wrong math considering the size of our team and the originally given timeframe.”","qa":"Marek Tymiñski, CEO of CI Games","st":"Labelling Sniper Ghost Warrior 3 As AAA Was “A Mistake” Says CI Games CEO","su":"https://www.thesixthaxis.com/2017/06/27/labelling-sniper-ghost-warrior-3-as-aaa-was-a-mistake-says-ci-games-ceo/#:~:text=We%20simply%20made%20the%20wrong,the%20originally%20given%20timeframe","sd":"Jun 2017","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"sniperghostwarriorcontracts","n":"Sniper Ghost Warrior Contracts","dv":"CI Games","y":"2019","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/973580/","ls":"Steam","cv":"sniperghostwarriorcontracts.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Cut the next Sniper to a Sniper Ghost Warrior 2 budget, without an open world","s":"Contracts shipped in November 2019, and CI Games booked PLN 37.5 million revenue that quarter. Contracts 2, on a lower budget again, passed 1 million units by May 2022.","w":"Sniper Ghost Warrior 3 shipped in 2017 as an open-world game aimed at AAA. In June 2017 CEO Marek Tymiński said that scale had been too ambitious for the team and timeframe, and announced a tactical shooter without an open world. The internal studio fell from over 100 people to about 30 shooter veterans, with outside studios helping on design, art and code. The budget was set comparable to Sniper Ghost Warrior 2. Contracts shipped in November 2019, and Q4 2019 brought PLN 37.5 million revenue and PLN 9.5 million gross profit.","r":"Contracts became the key driver of CI Games' 2019 results.","re":"CI Games gave Contracts 2 a lower budget and aimed for shorter gaps between releases.","co":"Internal studio cut from over 100 people to about 30.","nf":false,"l":"After a game outgrows its team, set the next one's budget at a level your studio has already shipped.","i":"A budget tier the studio has shipped before comes with known costs, so managers can hold cost, schedule and quality. CI Games also shrank its own studio and bought outside help. This fits a series with a proven core, not a new genre.","a":"Have we already shipped a game at this budget and team size?","q":"“We’re planning to make a great tactical shooter where we can focus on exciting gameplay, some key mechanics, and missions that have depth without all of the trappings of a large open-world setting.”","qa":"Marek Tymiński, CEO of CI Games","st":"Labelling Sniper Ghost Warrior 3 As AAA Was \"A Mistake\" Says CI Games CEO","su":"https://www.thesixthaxis.com/2017/06/27/labelling-sniper-ghost-warrior-3-as-aaa-was-a-mistake-says-ci-games-ceo/#:~:text=We%E2%80%99re%20planning%20to%20make%20a%20great,of%20a%20large%20open%2Dworld%20setting","sd":"Jun 2017","sy":"Press report","w8":4}]},{"k":"soldieroffortune","n":"Soldier of Fortune","dv":"Raven Software","y":"2000","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.gog.com/en/game/soldier_of_fortune_platinum_edition","ls":"GOG","cv":"soldieroffortune.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Gave the licensed Quake 2 engine an entire overhaul inside an 18-month plan","s":"Replacing the Quake 2 modelling system with Ghoul meant that for much of early development there was no game to look at, only components. Raven shipped in March 2000 after 23 months instead of 18.","w":"Raven took the Soldier of Fortune licence from Activision with a full team waiting and an 18-month plan. It decided to give the licensed Quake 2 engine an entire overhaul, including Ghoul, a new modelling system tweaked throughout the project. For a substantial part of early development there was no game to look at, only components. In March 1999, expecting 3 or 4 months of core development left, the team judged a skeletal animation system too risky and built vertex compression instead; the deadline then slid another 6 months. The game shipped in March 2000 after 23 months.","r":"No playable game early; animation system chosen under a deadline that then moved.","re":"Raven now designs the whole game before development and gets a green light first.","co":"23 months instead of 18; 20 developers plus 2 contractors; skeletal animation dropped.","nf":false,"l":"If you overhaul a licensed engine, keep a playable build running from month one so the team can see the game.","i":"A component in a viewer tells you nothing about how it plays in the world, so design waits for the engine. Raven's slip also came from Activision moving the date to dodge rival shooters; not all 5 extra months belong to the overhaul.","a":"Can we play the game in the world this month, or only components?","q":"“Unfortunately, this also meant that for a substantial part of the early development, we had no game to look at - only individual components.”","qa":"Eric Biessman, senior designer and project coordinator for Soldier of Fortune, Raven Software","st":"Postmortem: Raven Software's Soldier of Fortune","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/postmortem-raven-software-s-i-soldier-of-fortune-i-#:~:text=Unfortunately%2C%20this%20also%20meant%20that,only%20individual%20components","sd":"Sep 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"sonicadventure","n":"Sonic Adventure","dv":"Sonic Team","y":"1998","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/71250/","ls":"Steam","cv":"sonicadventure.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Built large stages for the new Dreamcast before setting a quality standard","s":"Many stages did not work and were tossed. Once Lost World set the standard, the team remade every earlier stage, and designers threw away all their prior work.","w":"Sonic Team chose the Dreamcast over the Saturn for Sonic Adventure, its first Dreamcast title. Early on there was talk of truly huge levels, while the team was still learning what the hardware could do. Many of the stages built in that period did not work and were tossed. Only while making Lost World did the team see the standard, so it went back and remade every earlier stage. Designers lost all their earlier work, though some programming survived. The game made its promised 23 December release in 1998, with the deadline pushed to the limit.","r":"Every stage built before Lost World had to be remade.","re":"Lost World became the quality standard for all the other stages.","co":"All stage design work done before Lost World; a deadline pushed to the limit.","nf":true,"l":"On new hardware, finish a single stage to final quality before building the rest, and use it as the standard.","i":"Until a team knows what the hardware can draw and how fast the player moves through it, every stage is a guess. A single finished stage turns the guess into a spec. Code carried over; level design work did not.","a":"Do we have a stage at final quality on the target hardware?","q":"“It wasn't until we were making the Lost World stage that we finally started to get a handle on everything and see, OK, this is the standard.”","qa":"Yuji Naka, producer of Sonic Adventure at Sonic Team","st":"Sonic Adventure – 1999 Developer Interview","su":"https://shmuplations.com/sonicadventure/#:~:text=until%20we%20were%20making%20the%20Lost%20World%20stage,OK%2C%20this%20is%20the%20standard","sd":"Dec 1998, translated Feb 2025","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"sonicfrontiers","n":"Sonic Frontiers","dv":"Sonic Team","y":"2022","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1237320/","ls":"Steam","cv":"sonicfrontiers.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Filled an island 3 times the size of Kronos with puzzles after prototyping","s":"The island had plenty to solve but lacked the high-speed action Sonic is known for. The team had to make major changes to battles and puzzles to bring the speed back.","w":"Sonic Frontiers took about 5 years to make. In the first year, a small team set the specifications, and the prototype was not up to par in graphics or gameplay. Once past the prototype phase, the team built an open-zone island 3 times the size of Kronos Island and covered it with puzzles. The puzzles felt fresh, but the game lacked Sonic's high-speed action. A programmer then proposed a way to generate grind rails, and the team made major changes to battles and puzzles around it. The game shipped worldwide in November 2022.","r":"Battles and puzzles had to be reworked to restore Sonic's speed.","re":"Generated grind rails and reworked battles brought speed back into the open zone.","co":"Major rework of battle and puzzle gameplay on an oversized island.","nf":true,"l":"Before you scale up the world, check that the prototype already plays like the series at its core.","i":"Puzzles were new to the series and drew the team's attention, so speed slipped. The bigger the island, the more puzzle content had to change once that showed. A series has a known core to test against; a new IP must define its own.","a":"Does our prototype play like the series at its core?","q":"“We became so engrossed in creating puzzles that we lost sight of Sonic's main draw.”","qa":"Masaya Hirano, sub lead game designer on Sonic Frontiers at Sega","st":"\"Sonic Frontiers\" Interview","su":"https://sega.co.jp/en/recruit/episode/episode15/#:~:text=We%20became%20so%20engrossed%20in%20creating,Sonic%27s%20main%20draw","sd":"","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"sonicthehedgehog","n":"Sonic the Hedgehog","dv":"Sonic Team","y":"2006","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Aimed for a Christmas launch on PS3 and Xbox 360 with a single team","s":"The team had to finish both versions together under heavy pressure. Sonic Team producer Yojiro Ogawa later named this as one reason the game shipped with glitches and control problems.","w":"Sonic Team wanted the new Sonic out around Christmas, with the PS3 launch coming up and an Xbox 360 version due at the same time. Producer Yojiro Ogawa said one team had to deliver both. In January 2006, part of that team split off to start a Wii Sonic game. In February 2007, Kikizo asked Ogawa about the released game's glitches, control problems and loading times. He said there were a lot of reasons, and named the Christmas date and the two-platform workload on one team as one of them.","r":"Both versions shipped under heavy pressure, with glitches and control problems.","re":"","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you lock a holiday date, check that one team can finish and test every platform version by then.","i":"A fixed launch date and a second platform both eat into test time, and one team pays for both at once. Ogawa named several causes, so this explains part of the result.","a":"Can one team finish and test every version by that date?","q":"“It was very hard for the team to try and see how we were going to come out with both versions together with just the one team.”","qa":"Yojiro Ogawa, Sonic Team producer at Sega","st":"Sonic Wii Interview with Yojiro Ogawa","su":"https://games.kikizo.com/features/sega_sonicteam_interview_feb07_p3.asp#:~:text=It%20was%20very%20hard%20for,with%20just%20the%20one%20team","sd":"Feb 2007","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"sorcery","n":"Sorcery!","dv":"inkle","y":"2013","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2016","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/411000/","ls":"Steam","cv":"sorcery.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Wrote a separate data format and editor for combat text instead of using ink","s":"Fights took a huge amount of tedious effort and were hard to polish. Switching later meant rewriting every fight, so inkle put less and less combat into the later parts.","w":"inkle adapted Steve Jackson's Sorcery! gamebooks, and Part 1 shipped in May 2013 on the first version of ink. For combat narrated in live prose, the team wrote a special data format and editor instead of using ink. Getting it to work took a huge amount of tedious effort, and polishing it was hard. By 80 Days, ink could hold all of a game's text, but moving combat over meant rewriting every fight in the first 2 parts. Across 4 games in 4 years, it wrote less and less combat, Jon Ingold said in September 2016.","r":"Later parts got less combat because fights cost too much to write.","re":"inkle kept the old format and wrote fewer fights in later parts.","co":"A huge amount of tedious effort, and less combat in later parts.","nf":true,"l":"Before you build a side tool for a single type of content, check whether your main scripting tool can do the job.","i":"Content written in a side format stays there: each finished fight is another reason not to switch. The cost showed up as less content rather than a slip, which is easy to miss. It matters most for content the game repeats often.","a":"Can our main scripting tool author this content type too?","q":"“Instead, consciously or not, we put less and less combat into the game because it wasn’t worth the effort of authoring it.”","qa":"Jon Ingold, narrative director and co-founder of inkle","st":"Postmortem: Steve Jackson's Sorcery! series by Inkle","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-i-steve-jackson-s-sorcery-i-series-by-inkle#:~:text=Instead%2C%20consciously%20or%20not,the%20effort%20of%20authoring%20it","sd":"Sep 2016","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"sorcery2","n":"Sorcery! 2","dv":"inkle","y":"2013","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/411000/","ls":"Steam","cv":"sorcery2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Carried Part 1's save data and shortcut weapon stats into the sequel","s":"Every weapon upgrade in Part 2 had to undo the old stat change first. The fragile script cost development and debugging time and kept the weapon system small.","w":"Part 1 shipped in May 2013 as only the second game written in ink, and the team worked around the language's limits to finish it. Weapons came only once in that book, so the script changed the player's combat power directly instead of keeping a weapon stat. Part 2, out in November 2013, carried Part 1's data forward and let players buy a new sword. The script had to work out the old bonus from the inventory and undo it. In September 2016 Jon Ingold wrote that the system still broke gameplay in Part 4 testing.","r":"Weapon upgrades needed fragile reverse calculations in every later part.","re":"By Part 3 the team had wrapped the logic in one script file.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before episode one ships, store every reward as its own variable, not as a change to a base stat.","i":"Once saves carry over between episodes, each shortcut in the first save format has to be supported in every sequel. The trap belongs to series with imported saves; a standalone game can drop the shortcut in the next project.","a":"Is every reward in our save data stored as its own variable?","q":"“Each of these mistakes cost us some development time, and debugging time, but more critically they limited our scope.”","qa":"Jon Ingold, narrative director and co-founder of inkle","st":"Postmortem: Steve Jackson's Sorcery! series by Inkle","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-i-steve-jackson-s-sorcery-i-series-by-inkle#:~:text=Each%20of%20these%20mistakes%20cost,critically%20they%20limited%20our%20scope","sd":"Sep 2016","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"spacebasedf9","n":"Spacebase DF-9","dv":"Double Fine Productions","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/246090/","ls":"Steam","cv":"spacebasedf9.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Started Spacebase DF-9 on an open-ended plan funded by Early Access sales","s":"Sales did not cover costs. Production ended after about 1.5 years instead of the 5 or so hoped for, and many planned features never shipped.","w":"Double Fine began Spacebase DF-9 with an open-ended production plan, hoping Early Access sales would fund it like the alpha-funded games that inspired it. Early sales looked promising, but things slowly changed, and new alpha releases did not turn them around. All the money went back into development, and the studio ended up spending more than it made. In September 2014 studio head Tim Schafer moved the game into finishing mode and planned version 1.0, with no new content after it. He said production had run about 1.5 years, not 5 or so, and apologised for poor communication.","r":"Production stopped at about 1.5 years with planned features unbuilt.","re":"Double Fine shipped v1.0 in October 2014 and ended development.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before Early Access, define and budget a 1.0 you can ship with the money you already have.","i":"An open-ended plan has no smallest finished game, so when income drops, cash sets the end date and the cuts fall on promised features. PC Gamer pointed to Double Fine's San Francisco costs; a smaller, cheaper team can ride out the same dip.","a":"If sales stop tomorrow, what finished version can we ship?","q":"“But Spacebase spends more money than it brings in, and that's just not something we can afford to do any more.”","qa":"Tim Schafer, studio head of Double Fine","st":"Tim Schafer explains Spacebase DF-9's unexpected v1.0 release","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/au/tim-schafer-explains-spacebase-df-9s-v1-0-release#:~:text=But%20Spacebase%20spends%20more%20money,can%20afford%20to%20do%20any%20more","sd":"Sep 2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"spacechem","n":"SpaceChem","dv":"Zachtronics Industries","y":"2011","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/92800/","ls":"Steam","cv":"spacechem.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Gave every puzzle a goal and an empty board instead of a set solution","s":"The team designed almost all 50 puzzles without knowing how they would be solved, then fixed the order in playtests. The same open rules made the game hard to learn.","w":"SpaceChem grew out of Zach Barth's Flash game The Codex of Alchemical Engineering, and he started building it in his spare time with a coworker. Each puzzle gave players tools, a clear end condition and an empty area to fill. The designers made almost every puzzle without knowing its solution, then reordered levels, cut logical duplicates and filled gaps after playtests. The game launched in January 2011 with 50 puzzles. In June 2012 Barth called the open puzzles the biggest thing the team did right, though the difficulty they created meant only 2 percent of players reached the end.","r":"Puzzles could be designed without first building their solutions.","re":"The team added tutorial counter-examples and a video explaining the goal.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Write each puzzle as a goal and a toolset, then let playtests set the level order and cut duplicates.","i":"When the designer does not have to build each solution, a puzzle costs only its rules and its goal. Open rules also show players many details at once, so part of the saving moves into onboarding when the audience is broad.","a":"Can we design our puzzles without solving them first?","q":"“In some ways this makes puzzle design easier, as it avoids the chicken-and-egg problems that arise when designing a puzzle and its solution simultaneously.”","qa":"Zach Barth, designer at Zachtronics Industries","st":"Postmortem: Zachtronics Industries' SpaceChem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-zachtronics-industries-i-spacechem-i-#:~:text=In%20some%20ways%20this%20makes,its%20solution%20simultaneously","sd":"Jun 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Wrote the engine, scripting and tools in C#, the language everyone knew","s":"Fast compiles cut the time to try ideas, and the designer could tweak almost anything without a programmer. Later moves to Mac, Linux and iPad reused most of the code.","w":"Every SpaceChem developer, Zach Barth included, was proficient in C#, so the team used it for the engine, in-game scripting and tools. Good tool support and fast compiles cut the time it took to try new ideas, and Barth could tweak almost anything without a developer. The team had feared a managed language would be slow, but it was not a problem. The first plan used XNA for Windows and Xbox; when the targets became Mac and Linux, the team switched to OpenGL, SDL and Mono. SpaceChem launched in January 2011, and a later iPad port rewrote only platform-specific code.","r":"Ideas got tested faster, and later ports reused almost all code.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Pick the language your whole team already knows, and use it for engine, scripts and tools alike.","i":"One known language removes ramp-up time and the handoffs between engine, script and tool code, so the designer can change things directly. The bet rested on modest performance needs: the team had worried a managed language would be too slow.","a":"Does everyone on the team already know the language we picked?","q":"“The excellent tool support and fast compilation times greatly reduced the time it took to try new ideas.”","qa":"Zach Barth, designer at Zachtronics Industries","st":"Postmortem: Zachtronics Industries' SpaceChem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-zachtronics-industries-i-spacechem-i-#:~:text=The%20excellent%20tool%20support%20and,try%20new%20ideas","sd":"Jun 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"spellcraftschoolofmagic","n":"SpellCraft School of Magic","dv":"Appy Entertainment","y":"2011","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Cut the first freemium concept to wizards but kept pets and multiplayer in version 1","s":"Pets cost a lot to design and animate yet shipped in December 2011 half-finished and confusing; the brand director said the effort would have been better spent polishing version 1.0.","w":"Appy committed to SpellCraft in late 2010 as the iOS market shifted to freemium. The first concept was too big and ill-focused, so the 6-person team cut it to wizards and dropped dozens of features before writing code, but kept pets and multiplayer. Pets were designed and animated at what O'Connor calls a lot of money, yet shipped with no way for players to understand them. After 5 months of development the game slipped from 17 November to 8 December 2011 for polish and balancing. In February 2012 O'Connor wrote that pets and multiplayer should have waited for an update.","r":"An expensive pet feature shipped half-finished and needed an update to make sense.","re":"Planned a pets update and kept them, since players liked them.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Cut any version 1 feature you cannot fully bring together, even if its assets are already paid for.","i":"A feature with finished art but no design integration confuses players, whatever the assets cost. O'Connor says pets did not delay the launch: the price was money and a weaker 1.0, and players still liked them, so the lesson is about focus, not about cutting.","a":"Which version 1 feature has assets but no design that ties it together?","q":"“We knew we wanted pets in the game; we knew what they should do; we spent a lot of money designing and animating the pets; but we didn't bring it all together in the form of a fully-cooked feature for the game's release.”","qa":"Paul O'Connor, brand director at Appy Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Appy Entertainment's SpellCraft School of Magic","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-appy-entertainment-s-i-spellcraft-school-of-magic-i-#:~:text=We%20knew%20we%20wanted%20pets,for%20the%20game's%20release.","sd":"Feb 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"splatoon","n":"Splatoon","dv":"Nintendo","y":"2015","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/wiiu/agmj/","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"splatoon.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Prototyped the ink turf game with tofu blocks before choosing a character","s":"A playable 4-vs-4 prototype beat about 70 other ideas. Its flaws later chose the squid design, which fixed hiding and wall inking at once.","w":"After the Wii U launch work ended, producer Hisashi Nogami gathered a team to build a game with a new structure. For about 6 months they pitched ideas almost daily, at least 70 of them. Program director Shintaro Sato's prototype survived: white and black tofu blocks shot ink to claim turf, already playable 4 against 4 online. A humanoid character then broke hiding, and 3D arenas made inked walls pointless. Switching between an ink creature and a human fixed both, and the squid followed. Internal promotion began in November 2013, and Splatoon shipped in May 2015.","r":"The prototype's rules decided the character design, not the other way round.","re":"Co-director Tsubasa Sakaguchi listed every element the character had to satisfy.","co":"About 6 months of ideation and at least 70 ideas.","nf":false,"l":"Prototype the core rule with plain shapes, then pick the character that fixes the prototype's problems.","i":"Plain blocks show whether the rule is fun before any art money is spent. Each fiction choice then has a test: does it fix a known problem? A game sold on its character or story needs a different order.","a":"Can we play our core rule with grey boxes today?","q":"“I think having that prototype to play was a big factor.”","qa":"Yusuke Amano, co-director of Splatoon at Nintendo","st":"Iwata Asks: Splatoon","su":"https://iwataasks.nintendo.com/interviews/wiiu/splatoon/0/1/#:~:text=I%20think%20having%20that%20prototype,was%20a%20big%20factor","sd":"2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"spore","n":"Spore","dv":"Maxis","y":"2008","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/17390/","ls":"Steam","cv":"spore.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built Spore around player-made creatures, with mesh, texture and animation generated at runtime","s":"The player-creation scope ran bigger than Maxis expected. EA granted an extra year, and Spore shipped in 2008 after more than 5 years with over 80 people.","w":"Will Wright's Spore let players build any creature and play it at once. Chris Hecker started on the creature skin in October 2003. The game had to build the mesh on the fly, chart textures in about 10 milliseconds and animate bodies no one had seen before. When Lucy Bradshaw joined at about 10 people, nobody knew if the procedural animation could be done. She later said the schedule stretched because of scope: players had to be able to use their creations. EA granted an extra year, and the game shipped in 2008 with some skin fixes left undone.","r":"The schedule stretched, and EA gave the team an extra year.","re":"Maxis used the extra year to add sharing of player creations.","co":"One extra year; over 80 people and more than 5 years in development.","nf":false,"l":"For a player-authoring mechanic, list every asset step artists normally do by hand and prototype the runtime version of each first.","i":"When players author the content, every hand-made pipeline step becomes code that must run in milliseconds on minimum spec. Each editor feature multiplies into mesh, texture, animation and AI work. A game with fixed assets does not pay this cost.","a":"Which hand-made asset steps does our player editor have to automate?","q":"“We didn’t know if we could get the underlying technology done.”","qa":"Lucy Bradshaw, vice president of production at Maxis","st":"An interview with EA Maxis' Lucy Bradshaw on the making of Spore","su":"https://gamesbeat.com/an-interview-with-ea-maxis-lucy-bradshaw-on-the-making-of-spore/#:~:text=We%20didn%E2%80%99t%20know%20if,the%20underlying%20technology%20done","sd":"Jun 2008","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"squadbusters","n":"Squad Busters","dv":"Supercell","y":"2024","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/squad-busters/id1668983788?uo=4","ls":"App Store","cv":"squadbusters.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Launched to a global audience instead of waiting years in a limited release","s":"Millions joined at launch in May 2024, but the game missed expectations. A big redesign in 2025 did not fix its core problems, and development ended in October 2025.","w":"Supercell applied lessons from previous games with long beta periods: move faster, take bold risks and learn from a global audience instead of waiting years in a limited release. Squad Busters launched worldwide in May 2024 and millions of players jumped in, but the game did not meet expectations. By early 2025 the team faced a crossroads and chose a big creative swing, adding Heroes, the boldest live change of that scale in Supercell's history. It did not solve the core problems. On October 30, 2025 the team ended active development, with closure in 2026.","r":"About 17 months live, then active development ended with core problems unsolved.","re":"Tried the Heroes redesign in 2025, then ended development and planned closure for 2026.","co":"About 17 months of live operation and a full Heroes redesign.","nf":false,"l":"Keep a gate that proves the core loop with real players before you scale to a global launch.","i":"A long beta is slow, but it is where core-loop problems surface while they are cheap to fix; after a global launch every fix is a live redesign. The boundary: Supercell says it got it wrong, not which problems a beta would have caught.","a":"What gate proves our core loop before we go global?","q":"“What we learned was that we had got it wrong.”","qa":"The Squad Busters team, Supercell","st":"Squad Busters Development is Ending, Final Update in December 2025","su":"https://supercell.com/en/news/squad-development-ending/#:~:text=What%20we%20learned%20was%20that,got%20it%20wrong","sd":"Oct 2025","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"squadron42","n":"Squadron 42","dv":"Cloud Imperium Games","y":"","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/4045720/","ls":"Steam","cv":"squadron42.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Shared one development pool between Squadron 42 and the live Star Citizen alpha","s":"Resources earmarked for Squadron 42 kept going to Star Citizen's live issues, at the expense of its plan. In 2023 CIG ringfenced the team, an expensive move that helped push costs up 26%.","w":"Cloud Imperium built its single-player game, Squadron 42, alongside Star Citizen, a live alpha that players already paid for. For years, staff earmarked for Squadron were easy to move onto Star Citizen whenever a release raised live issues. At the start of 2023 the company ringfenced the planned and required resources for Squadron 42 to reach feature complete by year end. Costs including capex rose 26% to $163M, and 2023 closed with a $20M loss after capex. The CFO described the change in May 2025. In October 2026 Steam listed Squadron 42 for the second quarter of 2027.","r":"Squadron 42 kept losing staff time to the live game.","re":"In 2023 CIG ringfenced Squadron 42's planned resources to reach feature complete.","co":"Squadron's plan slipped; ringfencing helped lift 2023 costs 26% to $163M.","nf":false,"l":"When one studio runs a live game and a premium game, ringfence each team's people and budget at greenlight.","i":"Live issues are urgent and visible, so shared staff flow to them, and the unreleased game's plan absorbs the loss. The pull is strongest when paying players wait on fixes. Separate teams with separate leads cost more up front.","a":"Can anyone move people off this project without a formal decision?","q":"“Hitherto it was all too easy to divert resources earmarked for Squadron onto Star Citizen to tackle live issues raised by our customers and community stemming from our latest releases, but this was at the expense of the Squadron development plan.”","qa":"Chief Financial Officer, Cloud Imperium Games","st":"Cloud Imperium Financials for 2023","su":"https://www.cloudimperiumgames.com/blog/corporate/cloud-imperium-financials-for-2023#:~:text=Hitherto%20it%20was%20all%20too,the%20Squadron%20development%20plan","sd":"May 2025","sy":"Company report","w8":3}]},{"k":"squareenixwesternaaaslatefy2013tombraiderhitmanabsolutionsleepingdogspluscancelledreexaminedtitles","n":"Square Enix AAA slate FY2013: Tomb Raider, Hitman: Absolution, Sleeping Dogs","dv":"Square Enix","y":"2013","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/203160/","ls":"Steam","cv":"squareenixwesternaaaslatefy2013tombraiderhitmanabsolutionsleepingdogspluscancelledreexaminedtitles.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Budgeted AAA titles on the assumption that each would sell worldwide","s":"Tomb Raider, Hitman: Absolution and Sleeping Dogs scored well but sold below their 5-6M, 4.5-5M and 2-2.5M potentials; Square Enix booked an ¥11,210 million extraordinary loss and its president stepped down.","w":"Square Enix budgeted its AAA titles to sell worldwide, with little thought for each market's regionality. It put Tomb Raider's potential at 5 to 6 million units, Hitman's at 4.5 to 5 million and Sleeping Dogs' at 2 to 2.5 million. High review scores did not translate to sales; North America sold two thirds of Europe's. On 26 March 2013 it warned of about ¥10 billion in extraordinary losses and president Yoichi Wada said he would leave. On 13 May 2013 Matsuda reported an ¥11,210 million extraordinary loss, tied cancelled and reexamined titles to that thinking, and announced shorter development.","r":"An ¥11,210 million extraordinary loss, titles cancelled or reexamined, and the president gone.","re":"Announced a rethink of long-term, large-scale development, regional portfolios and smart devices.","co":"Extraordinary loss ¥11,210 million; net loss ¥13,714 million for the year to March 2013.","nf":false,"l":"Set each title's budget from sales you can prove per region, not from a worldwide number.","i":"A budget built on a global sales number pays for a long, large-scale single-player production that only a worldwide hit can repay. The reform list targets the model itself, so the lesson is about how budgets are set, not these three games.","a":"Which region's proven sales is this budget built on?","q":"“We created our budgets on the basis that our games would be sold worldwide.”","qa":"Yosuke Matsuda, Senior Executive Managing Director and Representative Director, Square Enix Holdings","st":"Outline of Results Briefing by SQUARE ENIX HOLDINGS held on May 13, 2013","su":"https://www.hd.square-enix.com/eng/ir/library/pdf/20130527_01en.pdf#:~:text=We%20created%20our%20budgets,be%20sold%20worldwide","sd":"May 2013","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"squareenixhdgamepipeline","n":"Square Enix HD game pipeline (unannounced titles)","dv":"Square Enix Holdings","y":"2024","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2024","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Greenlit a broad HD-games pipeline without managing the title portfolio across the company","s":"Titles competed with each other for sales and HD Games lost money every year of the plan. In FY2024/3 the company booked ¥38.8 billion of impairments, including ¥22.0 billion on disposed content.","w":"Under its previous medium-term plan, Square Enix's HD Games sub-segment posted operating losses every year. In May 2023 Takashi Kiryu, then chief strategy officer, called the issue insufficient selection and concentration, with development resources spread too thin; Forspoken's results were disappointing. As president in May 2024, Kiryu said the company had not managed its title portfolio well, causing cannibalization between its own titles. After vetting the content production account it booked ¥38.8 billion in impairments, including ¥22.0 billion on disposal of content, extended part of the pipeline and rethought how to deliver some titles.","r":"¥38.8 billion written off, including ¥22.0 billion of disposed content, and timelines extended.","re":"Shifted the plan from quantity to quality and capped new-IP development investment.","co":"¥38.8 billion impairment in FY2024/3, including ¥22.0 billion loss on disposal of content.","nf":false,"l":"Greenlight against the whole portfolio: check what the new title cannibalises and whether the studio can concentrate on it.","i":"Each title can look fine on its own pitch while together they compete for the same players and the same developers. The boundary: this is a publisher-level fix; the briefing does not say which individual titles were wrong to start.","a":"Which of our own titles does this one compete with?","q":"“In addition to this, we did not manage our title portfolio across the company as well as we could have, which I believe resulted in opportunity losses due to cannibalization between our own titles.”","qa":"Takashi Kiryu, president and representative director of Square Enix Holdings","st":"Outline of Financial Results Briefing by SQUARE ENIX HOLDINGS","su":"https://www.hd.square-enix.com/eng/ir/library/pdf/24q4outline.pdf#:~:text=we%20did%20not%20manage%20our%20title%20portfolio,between%20our%20own%20titles","sd":"May 2024","sy":"Company report","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Revised its HD game policy and cut unannounced titles from the development pipeline","s":"Square Enix booked about ¥22.1 billion in content abandonment losses. Most affected titles were not due to be announced until the year to March 2027 or later.","w":"Square Enix's previous plan launched many HD titles, but some AAA and externally developed games earned less than expected, and overlapping launch dates cannibalized each other. On March 27, 2024, the board resolved to revise HD development policy and concentrate resources. A review of the pipeline followed. On April 30, 2024, the company announced about ¥22.1 billion in content abandonment losses for the year to March 2024. The May 13, 2024 medium-term plan put total valuation and special losses at ¥38.8 billion. It also abolished business units and overhauled title progress management.","r":"Content production assets fell from ¥87.3 billion to ¥48.5 billion.","re":"The company scrapped business units and redefined producer roles and progress tracking.","co":"About ¥22.1 billion abandonment loss; ¥38.8 billion in total valuation and special losses.","nf":false,"l":"At each gate, check every early project against the current portfolio plan, and stop the ones that no longer fit before they ramp.","i":"Projects far from announcement have spent the least, so cutting them costs the least. Square Enix said the hit to profit in its plan period was small for that reason. The filings name no titles, so this is about portfolio gates, not any one game.","a":"Would we still greenlight each early project under today's portfolio plan?","q":"“当社グループを取り巻く多様な環境変化に照らして開発リソースの選択と集中を企図し、2024 年 3 月 27 日開催の取締役会にて、HD ゲームタイトルの開発方針の見直しを決議致しました。”","qa":"Square Enix Holdings, notice signed by President Takashi Kiryu","st":"特別損失の計上に関するお知らせ","su":"https://www.hd.square-enix.com/jpn/ir/pdf/20240430_01.pdf#:~:text=%E5%BD%93%E7%A4%BE%E3%82%B0%E3%83%AB%E3%83%BC%E3%83%97%E3%82%92%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8A%E5%B7%BB%E3%81%8F%E5%A4%9A%E6%A7%98%E3%81%AA,%E9%96%8B%E7%99%BA%E6%96%B9%E9%87%9D%E3%81%AE%E8%A6%8B%E7%9B%B4%E3%81%97%E3%82%92%E6%B1%BA%E8%AD%B0%E8%87%B4%E3%81%97%E3%81%BE%E3%81%97%E3%81%9F","sd":"Apr 2024","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"squareenixnewipprojectscancelledinfy20113andfinalfantasyxiv10context","n":"Square Enix new-IP projects (FY2011)","dv":"Square Enix Holdings","y":"2011","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2011","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Launched as many new-IP projects as possible with relaxed selection standards at inception","s":"Every title that lacked a proper early-stage evaluation failed. In the year to March 2011 Square Enix booked ¥4.4 billion on cancelled projects and content and tightened its planning-stage gate.","w":"Square Enix launched as many new-IP projects as possible across its studios, relaxing its selection standards at inception to do it. By November 2010 Final Fantasy XIV had sold 0.63 million units and its free period was extended to 60 days. In the year to March 2011 the company booked ¥4.4 billion of losses on project cancellations and content disposal, within a ¥12.0 billion net loss. On May 13, 2011 CEO Yoichi Wada said all titles without sufficient early-stage evaluation had failed, and every title would now be evaluated rigorously from early planning.","r":"¥4.4 billion of cancellations and disposals in one year, within a ¥12.0 billion net loss.","re":"Evaluated every title rigorously from early planning and froze new MMO investment.","co":"¥4.4 billion in FY2011/3 on project cancellations, content disposal and related items.","nf":false,"l":"Keep the inception gate strict for new IP: the cheap cancellation is at concept review, not after a year of development.","i":"Lowering the bar at inception moves the selection to the most expensive point, after teams are staffed and content is built. The boundary: Square Enix does not name the titles, so the figure covers a basket of projects, not one game.","a":"Would this project pass a strict early-stage evaluation today?","q":"“We now understand that past failures in creating new IPs were the result of relaxing our selection standards upon project inception with a thought that launching as many projects as possible will lead to having new IPs.”","qa":"Yoichi Wada, president and representative director of Square Enix Holdings","st":"Outline of Results Briefing by SQUARE ENIX HOLDINGS","su":"https://www.hd.square-enix.com/eng/ir/library/pdf/20110513_01E.pdf#:~:text=We%20now%20understand%20that%20past%20failures,having%20new%20IPs","sd":"May 2011","sy":"Company report","w8":4}]},{"k":"costumequeststackingironbrigadesesamestreetonceuponamonster","n":"Stacking","dv":"Double Fine Productions","y":"2011","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/115110/","ls":"Steam","cv":"costumequeststackingironbrigadesesamestreetonceuponamonster.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Started each new game with a two-week playable prototype instead of a concept document","s":"The demo got the whole Stacking team behind one vision and helped sign the game quickly. When the studio lost its big sequel, four such demos became four signed games.","w":"Double Fine president Tim Schafer created Amnesia Fortnight, a two-week jam where small teams each build a game. Lee Petty went into the 2009 edition with a short presentation and a few sketches. Two weeks later his team had a playable Stacking demo with stacking, special abilities and multiple-solution puzzles. In late 2009 the Brütal Legend sequel was cancelled, and the studio pitched four Fortnight demos to publishers. Schafer said all four were signed within two months. Stacking shipped in February 2011 after 11 months with 13 full-time developers.","r":"Stacking signed quickly, and four prototypes became four signed games.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"2 weeks of prototyping; then 13 developers for 11 months.","nf":false,"l":"Open each new concept with a time-boxed playable prototype of its core mechanic, and pitch with that build.","i":"A playable demo settles mood, art style and core mechanics at once, and a publisher can judge it in minutes. It fits small downloadable games; two weeks will not show what a large AAA production costs.","a":"Could we show a publisher a playable core of this game in two weeks?","q":"“This was, by far, the best way to start a project that I have ever experienced.”","qa":"Lee Petty, project lead on Stacking, Double Fine Productions","st":"Postmortem: Stacking","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_February_2012.pdf#:~:text=This%20was%2C%20by%20far%2C%20the,I%20have%20ever%20experienced","sd":"Feb 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4,"sh":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_February_2012.pdf#page=40"}]},{"k":"starcitizen","n":"Star Citizen","dv":"Cloud Imperium Games","y":"","sc":"AAA","se":"Public alpha 2015","lk":"https://robertsspaceindustries.com/en/","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Let crowdfunding stretch goals keep expanding scope and scale the team with the money","s":"The November 2014 target passed, headcount went from 5 at end of 2012 to 1,085 at end of 2023, yearly costs reached $163M, and no full release has shipped.","w":"The 2012 Kickstarter raised $2,134,374, with completion planned for November 2014; Roberts later said the plan then was a much more contained game. In September 2013, 87% of backers voted to keep the counter going and Roberts promised more stretch goals. In August 2014 he called the project several AAA games rolled into one and planned to scale the team with the crowdfunding. Stretch goals stopped at end 2014. By July 2015 headcount was 255, up from 5 in 2012, and Roberts conceded delivery would take longer. By end 2023 CIG had 1,085 staff and $163M of yearly costs.","r":"The November 2014 target passed; Squadron 42 was being driven to feature complete in 2023.","re":"Stopped stretch goals at end 2014; in 2023 focused resources on Squadron 42.","co":"Headcount 5 (end 2012) to 1,085 (end 2023); 2023 costs $163M against $143M income.","nf":false,"l":"Lock the scope you can ship before taking more money; bank extra funding or plan it as a sequel.","i":"The team grew to match the money rather than a plan, so the date moved with the funding. Roberts calls this the whole point and backers kept paying, so the lesson holds only where a date matters more than scope.","a":"If more money arrives, will we add scope or keep the date?","q":"“Our plan is to scale the team based on the crowd funding, with the goal to be able to double down on development wherever it's possible to do so.”","qa":"Chris Roberts, Chairman, Cloud Imperium Games","st":"Letter From The Chairman","su":"https://robertsspaceindustries.com/en/comm-link/transmission/14055-Letter-From-The-Chairman#:~:text=Our%20plan%20is%20to%20scale,possible%20to%20do%20so","sd":"Aug 2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Planned an early public dogfighting build on stock CryEngine netcode","s":"As backer numbers grew, the stock netcode needed lobby and server work that the real backend would make redundant. Roberts pulled the December 2013 build, and the module reached backers in June 2014.","w":"When Chris Roberts started Star Citizen, he planned an early dogfighting build to drum up interest and gather feedback on combat. He accepted that it would run on CryEngine's built-in netcode, not the persistent backend his team was building. Funding surged in 2013, and so did the number of Alpha backers. Serving them on CryEngine netcode meant building a lobby system and session servers, work the new backend would replace. On 16 December 2013, Roberts delayed the end-of-month release by a couple of months. Arena Commander V0.8 launched on 4 June 2014.","r":"The December 2013 release was pushed back by months.","re":"Roberts dropped the throwaway fork and stayed on the planned backend.","co":"Release moved from late December 2013 to 4 June 2014.","nf":false,"l":"Decide whether a public prototype is throwaway before you announce it, and size its tech for the audience it will face.","i":"Temporary tech is cheap only while the audience stays small. Once the Alpha audience grew, the shortcut needed its own lobbies and servers. Polish and an office move also delayed the build, so the netcode explains only part of the slip.","a":"Will our public prototype's tech survive the audience we expect?","q":"“As time has progressed I’ve become more nervous taking the down and dirty route with the initial dogfighting build, especially as our numbers grow.”","qa":"Chris Roberts, chairman of Cloud Imperium Games","st":"Letter from the Chairman: On Dogfighting","su":"https://robertsspaceindustries.com/en/comm-link/transmission/13432-Letter-From-The-Chairman-On-Dogfighting#:~:text=As%20time%20has%20progressed%20I%E2%80%99ve,especially%20as%20our%20numbers%20grow","sd":"Dec 2013","sy":"Participant account","w8":3}]},{"k":"starfoxadventures","n":"Star Fox Adventures","dv":"Rare","y":"2002","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"IP & franchise","t":"Rebranded the original Dinosaur Planet adventure as a Star Fox game mid-development","s":"Players and critics judged it as a Star Fox game, and the team's lead engineer later said the Star Fox elements felt tacked on.","w":"Rare started Dinosaur Planet on the N64 right after Diddy Kong Racing shipped, with two heroes, Sabre and Krystal, and a concept that changed many times early on. Around the time the team moved to GameCube, the game became a Star Fox title, which Tossell heard Miyamoto had suggested. The team was disappointed but saw the licence's potential. For most of development it had only five programmers, and Arwing sections went in late. Star Fox Adventures launched in 2002, and in 2012 Tossell said some criticism unfairly judged it as a Star Fox game.","r":"Late Arwing sections stretched tools built for a different game.","re":"Nintendo's Takaya Imamura stayed at Rare for around a month overseeing the project.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If you move a game into an existing franchise, rerun pre-production on what that franchise's players will expect.","i":"A world built around other heroes keeps its shape after a rebrand, so the franchise parts arrive as add-ons and players judge the game against the franchise. The risk is lower when the rebrand comes before levels and tools are built.","a":"Has the design been checked against what this franchise's players expect?","q":"“It also felt a little too much like the Star Fox elements were tacked on - which of course they were!”","qa":"Phil Tossell, lead software engineer on Star Fox Adventures at Rare","st":"The Making Of Star Fox Adventures - \"Nintendo Was Really Trusting Of Our Ability To Make A Great Game\"","su":"https://www.timeextension.com/features/the-making-of-star-fox-adventures-nintendo-was-really-trusting-of-our-ability-to-make-a-great-game#:~:text=It%20also%20felt%20a%20little,which%20of%20course%20they%20were","sd":"Dec 2012, reprinted Sep 2026","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"starwarsgalaxies","n":"Star Wars Galaxies","dv":"Sony Online Entertainment","y":"2003","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Rejected the shared EverQuest II engine and built all technology from scratch","s":"Content tools came together only just before a fixed launch date. The game shipped in 2003 with one quest and missions from terminals only.","w":"The Austin team started Star Wars Galaxies in 2000 and found the San Diego team planning to share the EverQuest II engine. Executive producer Rich Vogel's team judged it could not deliver their design in the time frame and built their own technology. The tools and content pipeline came together only just before launch. They asked for 6 more months, got 1, and brought in 60 people to finish content. The 2003 launch had one quest and no quest system. Vogel and creative director Raph Koster told the story at GDC in 2021.","r":"The game launched almost empty of content and behind on bug fixes.","re":"After launch they added content slowly; the NGE revamp then lost 25% of active users.","co":"2 years 9 months; 60 extra people; asked for 6 more months, got 1.","nf":false,"l":"If you build your own engine, put finished content tools in the plan as a pre-production deliverable with its own date.","i":"Engine work and content work compete for the same months, and tools come last. With a fixed date, content is what gets cut. A licensed engine helps only if it can run the design; Vogel's team judged EverQuest II's could not.","a":"Does our plan have a date for finished content tools?","q":"“We felt like the engine wouldn't get us to what we really wanted to be and do in the time frame we had to do it in.”","qa":"Rich Vogel, executive producer of Star Wars Galaxies at Sony Online Entertainment","st":"Classic Game Postmortem: 'Star Wars Galaxies'","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=djJO1XSOwuI&t=1579s","sd":"Nov 2021","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=djJO1XSOwuI&t=146s"},{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Showed an award-winning E3 2001 demo and talked features with fans from the start","s":"The hype drove day-one sales and set fan expectations early. Features were then cut to meet a launch date that moved only 1 month.","w":"Development started in 2000. At E3 2001 the team showed a demo that won several awards, and the leads later said they made a lot of promises there. Executive producer Rich Vogel had involved the community from the start, so the team kept talking about what it was making. Hype grew, while the tools and content pipeline came together only just before launch. The team asked for 6 more months, got 1, and cut whole skill trees. At GDC in 2021, Vogel named releasing information way too early as one of his biggest lessons.","r":"Hype peaked while the team knew it was about to hit a wall.","re":"Vogel set up a council of key players to help triage fixes.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Talk about the game in public only when players can act on it, such as a beta sign-up close to the beta.","i":"Every feature shown in public becomes a promise, and a perception set early is hard to change. Early hype still sold copies on day one, so the cost lands when the team cannot finish what it showed.","a":"Is there a beta sign-up or other player action behind our next announcement?","q":"“I think one of the biggest lessons I learned is we release way too early.”","qa":"Rich Vogel, executive producer of Star Wars Galaxies at Sony Online Entertainment","st":"Classic Game Postmortem: 'Star Wars Galaxies'","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=djJO1XSOwuI&t=1481s","sd":"Nov 2021","sy":"Participant talk","w8":3,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=djJO1XSOwuI&t=1468s"}]},{"k":"starwarsragtag","n":"Star Wars Project Ragtag","dv":"Visceral Games","y":"2017","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2017","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Pivoted Visceral's linear story-driven Star Wars game to a broader design after 3 years","s":"EA closed Visceral, handed the project to an EA Vancouver-led team and dropped the fiscal 2019 launch window. The successor project was cancelled in January 2019 and no game shipped.","w":"Amy Hennig announced in April 2014 that she was joining Visceral to make a Star Wars game. The studio had fewer than 100 people and was building a third-person action-adventure on Frostbite. The release slipped from May 2018 to December 2018. On 17 October 2017 Patrick Söderlund, head of EA Worldwide Studios, wrote that after testing the concept with players and tracking the market, EA was pivoting to a broader experience with more variety and player agency, moving development to an EA Vancouver-led team and closing Visceral. In January 2019 the EA Vancouver game was reported cancelled.","r":"Visceral closed, the project restarted elsewhere and was cancelled 15 months later.","re":"Moved the project to an EA Vancouver-led team; that version was cancelled in January 2019.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Lock with your publisher what kind of game it is and whether players must come back, before production starts.","i":"EA's stated reason: players had to want to come back, and a linear story game did not promise that. Kotaku's sources add engine trouble and a team under 100. A pivot this late cost the studio and the schedule, and the restart failed anyway.","a":"Has the publisher agreed what kind of game this is and whether players return?","q":"“It has become clear that to deliver an experience that players will want to come back to and enjoy for a long time to come, we needed to pivot the design.”","qa":"Patrick Söderlund, head of EA Worldwide Studios","st":"An Update on the Visceral Star Wars Project","su":"https://www.ea.com/news/an-update-on-the-visceral-star-wars-project#:~:text=It%20has%20become%20clear%20that,pivot%20the%20design","sd":"Oct 2017","sy":"Company statement","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built a third-person Star Wars adventure on Frostbite, an engine made for first-person shooters","s":"Amy Hennig later called the engine a hurdle the team knew about going in. A former employee told Kotaku that basic third-person features alone meant a year to a year and a half of engine work.","w":"EA had required its studios to use DICE's Frostbite engine for years when Amy Hennig joined Visceral in April 2014 and started Ragtag, a Star Wars adventure, late that year. Frostbite had never run a third-person action-adventure, so Visceral built the basics from scratch; a former employee told Kotaku that meant a year to a year and a half of work. Progress stayed slow, and EA Vancouver, joining in 2017, was described as horrified by how little existed. EA cancelled Ragtag and closed Visceral on 17 October 2017. In 2019 Hennig named the engine as a hurdle.","r":"A year or more of engine work before the game could do basic third-person actions.","re":"EA moved the work to EA Vancouver's new open-world Star Wars game.","co":"A year to a year and a half of engine work (former employee's estimate).","nf":false,"l":"If the engine is mandated, estimate the feature gap for your camera and genre in writing before greenlight and put it in the schedule.","i":"A first-person engine lacks what a third-person cinematic game takes for granted, so the first year goes to foundations, not levels, and a small team cannot absorb that. Hennig says the team knew this going in; the 12 to 18 months is one anonymous estimate.","a":"What does our engine lack for this camera, and how long to build it?","q":"“Obviously trying to create a third-person cinematic traversal action game with an engine that was made to make first-person shooters, that was a hurdle.”","qa":"Amy Hennig, writer and director of Ragtag at Visceral Games (EA)","st":"Amy Hennig interview: Surviving the trauma of making a video game and inspiring newcomers","su":"https://venturebeat.com/2019/02/22/amy-hennig-interview-surviving-the-trauma-of-making-a-video-game-and-inspiring-newcomers/view-all/#:~:text=Obviously%20trying%20to%20create%20a,that%20was%20a%20hurdle","sd":"Feb 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Started a linear Star Wars action game with 30 people and a 160-person plan","s":"Kotaku reported that the planned Motive staff went to Battlefront II instead. Amy Hennig said the team reached the size it needed only late, and EA closed Visceral in October 2017.","w":"Amy Hennig joined Visceral in April 2014 and started the Star Wars game Ragtag late that year. Kotaku reported that about 30 people entered pre-production in mid-2015, under a plan for roughly 160 by 2016. EA's new Montreal studio Motive was to add 70 people, but by early 2016 EA moved it to Battlefront II. Frostbite lacked third-person tools, and Hennig said the team spent much time on foundation work. The release slipped from May 2018 to December 2018. After a gate demo in October 2017, EA closed Visceral, about 80 people, and cancelled the game.","r":"The 160-person team never formed, and progress stayed slow through 2016.","re":"EA added an EA Vancouver team in early 2017, then cancelled the game.","co":"Visceral closed (about 80 staff); one source estimated $100 million to finish.","nf":false,"l":"Tie the greenlight to a staffed plan: check that every team the scope needs is hired or firmly committed.","i":"Scope sized for 160 people does not shrink when 30 show up: the work slows and every review finds less done. Here the engine gap and Lucasfilm approvals added to the load, so staffing was not the only cause.","a":"Is every team our scope needs already hired or firmly committed?","q":"“So it was very hard to get to a point where we felt like we had the foundation we needed, the team size we needed, all the right disciplines we needed, until sort of late in the process.”","qa":"Amy Hennig, creative director of Ragtag at Visceral Games","st":"The Amy Hennig Interview: On What Changed With Uncharted 4, Leaving EA, and What's Next","su":"https://www.usgamer.net/articles/amy-hennig-interview-uncharted-4-leaving-ea-ragtag-star-wars#:~:text=So%20it%20was%20very%20hard,late%20in%20the%20process","sd":"Feb 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"starwarsknightsoftheoldrepublic","n":"Star Wars: Knights of the Old Republic","dv":"BioWare","y":"2003","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/32370/","ls":"Steam","cv":"starwarsknightsoftheoldrepublic.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Counted on designers from a shared staff pool to prototype combat early","s":"The designers did not join in time. The team designed combat after the fact while also meeting story and event-scripting needs.","w":"KOTOR's combat system was unlike anything BioWare had built, so the leads planned lots of early pre-production and prototyping to cut the risk. Designers were meant to join early and prototype gameplay in placeholder areas. BioWare moved staff between projects through a matrix system, and the plan did not work out. The team did a fair amount of retroactive design while balancing story and event scripting. The Xbox version shipped in summer 2003, with PC later that year. In the December 2003 postmortem, the leads said they now plan staffing earlier and sometimes hire or outsource early.","r":"Combat was designed after the fact, alongside story and scripting work.","re":"BioWare began scheduling staff earlier, hiring or outsourcing ahead of need.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Name the people who will build each pre-production prototype, and confirm their start dates before the plan relies on them.","i":"When staff move between projects, a prototype plan is only as good as the date its people become free. If they arrive late, design work slides into production, where it competes with story and scripting for the same hours.","a":"Are the people for each prototype free on its start date?","q":"“We initially planned on putting designers on the project early to prototype gameplay into placeholder areas of the game, but things didn't pan out as expected.”","qa":"Casey Hudson, Ray Muzyka and James Ohlen, project director, joint CEO and lead designer at BioWare","st":"Classic Postmortem: BioWare's Star Wars: Knights of the Old Republic","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-bioware-s-i-star-wars-knights-of-the-old-republic-i-#:~:text=We%20initially%20planned%20on%20putting,pan%20out%20as%20expected","sd":"Dec 2003, reprinted Jul 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"starwarsknightsoftheoldrepubliciithesithlords","n":"Star Wars: Knights of the Old Republic II - The Sith Lords","dv":"Obsidian Entertainment","y":"2004","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/208580/","ls":"Steam","cv":"starwarsknightsoftheoldrepubliciithesithlords.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Set content targets for areas, quests and NPCs on a 14-month sequel schedule","s":"The team saw much too late that the targets were unrealistic. New systems shipped with little polish, and parts of the game felt unfinished.","w":"KOTOR II was Obsidian's first project, built on BioWare's engine and toolset in little more than 14 months. The team of 33 set goals for the number and size of areas, quests and non-player characters. About a third of the team, including 70 percent of the designers, joined in the last 6 months. Most of production ran on overtime, and a full play-through came only shortly before gold. LucasArts released the game in December 2004. In April 2005 senior designer Kevin Saunders wrote that the content goals had been unrealistic.","r":"Parts of the game felt unfinished, and new systems shipped as rough drafts.","re":"Obsidian said it would apply the lessons to Neverwinter Nights 2.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Derive content counts from the months and people you have, and recheck them against real build rates early in production.","i":"Content counts set at the start fix how much work every area needs, and a short schedule leaves no slack when they prove too high. A proven engine and toolset made 14 months possible, but they did not make the counts fit.","a":"Do our content counts fit the months and people we actually have?","q":"“Simply put, we were too ambitious in terms of total content, and this was realized much too late.”","qa":"Kevin Saunders, senior designer at Obsidian Entertainment","st":"Classic Postmortem: Obsidian's Knights of the Old Republic II: The Sith Lords","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-obsidian-s-i-knights-of-the-old-republic-ii-the-sith-lords-i-#:~:text=Simply%20put%2C%20we%20were%20too,realized%20much%20too%20late","sd":"Apr 2005, reprinted Dec 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"starwarstheoldrepublic","n":"Star Wars: The Old Republic","dv":"BioWare Austin","y":"2011","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1286830/","ls":"Steam","cv":"starwarstheoldrepublic.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Built branching cutscenes from shared animation libraries instead of custom animation per scene","s":"BioWare launched with 8 fully voiced class stories plus side, world and companion cutscenes. The same pipeline kept new story content coming for years after launch.","w":"Star Wars: The Old Republic plays each conversation differently by class, choices and romances, so every scene stacks many variants. BioWare could not animate each one by hand. Cinematic designers built scenes from libraries of animations, audio, effects and music, using timeline tools and camera editors. At GDC 2010, BioWare's Ben Cloward presented the same idea for faces: automatic selection from animator-made performances. At launch the team delivered 8 class stories plus side quest, world quest and companion cutscenes. In July 2021, lead cinematic designer Ashley Ruhl said the pipeline had produced new stories for years.","r":"8 class stories with full cutscenes were ready at launch.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Count your scene variants early, and decide which ones reuse library animation and which ones get custom work.","i":"Branching multiplies the number of scenes, and hand animation cost grows with every branch. A shared library keeps the cost per variant low. Every camera is still edited by a cinematic designer, so the library cuts animation work, not designer time.","a":"How many of our scene variants need custom animation?","q":"“The volume and variability of our cutscenes means we can’t create custom animations for every scene.”","qa":"Ashley Ruhl, Lead Cinematic Designer on Star Wars: The Old Republic","st":"Behind the Scenes Look at SWTOR: Cinematics","su":"https://www.swtor.com/info/news/article/20210722#:~:text=The%20volume%20and%20variability%20of,custom%20animations%20for%20every%20scene","sd":"Jul 2021","sy":"Participant account","w8":3}]},{"k":"starcraft","n":"StarCraft","dv":"Blizzard Entertainment","y":"1998","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://starcraft.blizzard.com/en-us/","ls":"Website","cv":"starcraft.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Greenlit StarCraft as a one-year schedule filler on Warcraft II technology","s":"Shown at E3 in June 1996, the 'Orcs in space' build looked dated next to Dominion, the project was rebooted after Diablo, and it shipped in May 1998 after 14 months of crunch.","w":"After Shattered Nations died in 1995, Blizzard president Allen Adham chose a moderately scoped game to fill a schedule hole: a one-year Warcraft II follow-on for Christmas 1996. At E3 in June 1996 Ion Storm's isometric Dominion made the build look dated. Diablo then took priority and developers were moved off StarCraft until no one was left. After Diablo shipped in late 1996 the team restarted with isometric art, Battle.net play, a 1600-unit cap and a C++ engine for Windows. The programmers worked toward shipping in 2 months for the next 14 months; the game released in May 1998.","r":"Christmas 1996 plan became May 1998, after a reboot and 14 months of crunch.","re":"Rebooted the game after Diablo shipped: new art, engine, editor and Battle.net play.","co":"About 17 months late; 14 months of crunch; unit code and engine rewritten.","nf":false,"l":"Do not greenlight a game to fill a revenue gap; a one-year sequel on old tech invites a reboot.","i":"The schedule, not the game, was the goal, so the first build was a Warcraft II lookalike that one trade show exposed. Wyatt admits he did not push back. A modest scope can be right; one chosen for the calendar is not.","a":"Is this scope chosen for the game or for the release calendar?","q":"“In retrospect that decision was a mistake of the first order, and it behooves me try to explain why we, and by that I mean Blizzard’s leadership team, could have allowed that to happen.”","qa":"Patrick Wyatt, Blizzard developer on StarCraft","st":"StarCraft: Orcs in space go down in flames","su":"https://www.codeofhonor.com/blog/starcraft-orcs-in-space-go-down-in-flames#:~:text=In%20retrospect%20that%20decision%20was,allowed%20that%20to%20happen","sd":"Sep 2012","sy":"Participant account","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Rebooted StarCraft with a bigger scope and a C++ rewrite of the Warcraft engine","s":"The game crashed so often that playtesting stayed hard until release. It shipped in Q2 1998 instead of Q4 1996, after more than a year of crunch.","w":"Blizzard first planned StarCraft as a modest one-year game for Christmas 1996, and the June 1996 E3 build was close to orcs in space. Diablo then pulled every developer off the project. After Diablo shipped at the end of 1996, the team restarted StarCraft with far bigger ambitions. Untested programmers joined, and a lead who had never architected a shipping engine rebuilt the Warcraft engine in C++. For 14 months the schedule said launch was 2 months away. StarCraft shipped in Q2 1998 and kept needing patches. Patrick Wyatt wrote this up in 2012.","r":"Crashes made playtesting hard right up to release.","re":"The team stopped adding features long enough to ship, then patched after launch.","co":"About 18 months late; over a year of crunch.","nf":false,"l":"Before a reboot rewrites a working engine, check who on the rewrite team has shipped an engine.","i":"A rewrite throws away lessons the old code had already paid for, and a new team pays for them again. Wyatt's point is about new hands on a rewrite; an experienced team rewriting its own engine carries less of this risk.","a":"Has anyone leading our engine rewrite shipped an engine before?","q":"“And not only were there inexperienced junior members on the team, the leader of the StarCraft programming effort had never architected a shipping game engine.”","qa":"Patrick Wyatt, programmer at Blizzard Entertainment","st":"Tough times on the road to Starcraft","su":"https://www.codeofhonor.com/blog/tough-times-on-the-road-to-starcraft#:~:text=And%20not%20only%20were%20there,architected%20a%20shipping%20game%20engine","sd":"Sep 2012","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"startopia","n":"Startopia","dv":"Mucky Foot Productions","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"Re-release 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/243040/","ls":"Steam","cv":"startopia.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Spent the first year on a graphics demo to win funding, with little gameplay","s":"The demo won Eidos' support. Its code later forced design restrictions and rewrites, and gameplay systems had to fit around it.","w":"Mucky Foot, founded in 1997 by former Bullfrog staff, started Startopia from a broad concept document. For about a year, a small team built what was essentially a graphical demo, because the publisher judged progress on looks. The demo won Eidos' support, and a game designer was hired to write a technical design. Later the demo code forced design restrictions to avoid rewrites, and other parts had to be rewritten. With no editing tools planned, programmers hand-coded animations and models. The team grew from 4 to 15. Imlach's October 2001 postmortem put this first in what went wrong.","r":"Demo code limited the design, and parts had to be rewritten.","re":"After signing, the team hired a game designer to write a technical design.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If you build a pitch demo for looks, plan its code as throwaway and write the technical design before production.","i":"A demo judged on looks gets built around rendering, not gameplay systems, and its code then shapes the design. Rewriting costs time, so teams bend the design instead. The demo did win the deal: the problem was keeping its code.","a":"Which parts of our pitch demo code are we planning to throw away?","q":"“The early development stage of the game focused far too heavily on the graphical look of the game, and almost entirely avoided any thought as to how the technologies used would impact gameplay.”","qa":"Wayne Imlach, Mucky Foot Productions","st":"Postmortem: Startopia","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-i-startopia-i-#:~:text=The%20early%20development%20stage%20of,would%20impact%20gameplay","sd":"Oct 2001","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"stellaris","n":"Stellaris","dv":"Paradox Development Studio","y":"2016","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/281990/","ls":"Steam","cv":"stellaris.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Skipped proper prototyping on the belief that interlocking systems can only be judged together","s":"Fundamental problems such as fleet combat surfaced late, and design iteration ran deep into development. The director says Federations, Alliances and Uplift shipped needing more work.","w":"Henrik Fåhraeus started design drafts for a Paradox space 4X in 2013, and a team of 8 to 15 built it over about 32 months. The studio's habit, formed on tight 24-month cycles, was to skip prototypes and judge systems only once all of them were in. Fleet combat looked awful until programmer Daniel Eriksson built a combat-only test setup. He found almost at once that ships needed different movement rules in combat. Economy rules such as adjacency bonuses and stockpile caps also changed late. Stellaris shipped on 9 May 2016 and broke studio records.","r":"Core systems were still being redesigned late in development.","re":"Paradox made a proper prototyping step mandatory in its development cycle.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Pull the riskiest system, such as combat, into its own test setup and prototype it before full production starts.","i":"Interlocking systems need each other for final balance, but each one's basic feel can be tested alone, as the combat test setup showed. Isolated prototypes will not settle economy balance, which still needs the whole game running.","a":"Which risky system have we not yet tested on its own?","q":"“I’m going to be blunt here; not doing proper prototyping is stupid, plain and simple.”","qa":"Rikard Åslund, project lead of Stellaris at Paradox Development Studio","st":"Postmortem: Paradox Development Studio's Stellaris","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-paradox-development-studio-s-i-stellaris-i-#:~:text=I%E2%80%99m%20going%20to%20be%20blunt,stupid%2C%20plain%20and%20simple","sd":"Jun 2016","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"streetfighterv","n":"Street Fighter V","dv":"Capcom","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/310950/","ls":"Steam","cv":"streetfighterv.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Picked Unreal Engine 4 during prototyping over building alongside Capcom's new engine","s":"Unreal handled the graphics, so Capcom moved more staff and resources onto battle design, game design and network code. Producer Yoshinori Ono said production then ran very smoothly.","w":"Capcom had its own proprietary engine in the works when the team was prototyping Street Fighter V, and the game also needed a new battle system and new online features. During prototyping, producer Yoshinori Ono's team found Unreal Engine 4 had the tools and functions it wanted and ran stable at 60 frames per second. Capcom leaned on Unreal for graphics and put more staff and resources into game design, battle design and network development. The PC version launched in February 2016. A month later, Ono said the choice let production move very smoothly.","r":"More staff went to combat and netcode instead of engine work.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you build an engine together with the game, check whether a licensed engine already meets your frame-rate and tool needs.","i":"A new engine and a new battle system built at the same time compete for the same staff. Licensing frees those people only when rendering is not what sets the game apart; here the edge was combat and online play.","a":"Does a licensed engine already meet our frame-rate and tool needs?","q":"“This proved incredibly fruitful, allowing us to advance production very smoothly.”","qa":"Yoshinori Ono, producer of Street Fighter V at Capcom","st":"How Unreal Engine 4 Provided Street Fighter V's Knockout Punch","su":"https://www.unrealengine.com/developer-interviews/how-unreal-engine-4-provided-street-fighter-vs-knockout-punch#:~:text=This%20proved%20incredibly%20fruitful,production%20very%20smoothly","sd":"Mar 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"stubbsthezombieinrebelwithoutapulse","n":"Stubbs the Zombie in Rebel Without a Pulse","dv":"Wideload Games","y":"2005","sc":"AA","se":"Port 2021","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/7800/","ls":"Steam","cv":"stubbsthezombieinrebelwithoutapulse.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Kept a 12-person core team and bought production assets from fixed-price contractors","s":"Fixed-price contractors carried the schedule risk on a game that shipped four months late. Weak vendor vetting and too few producers still forced 3 months of crunch.","w":"After leaving Bungie in 2002, Alexander Seropian founded Wideload around a small, manageable team. He hired 12 core artists, designers and engineers, licensed the Halo engine, and bought production assets from contractors at fixed prices. He announced the model in early 2004. Some asset classes went into production without a shippable reference, not every contractor made a test asset, and one art house had to be replaced mid-production. The project director doubled as producer, and approvals fell behind. Stubbs shipped on October 18, 2005, four months late, after 3 months of crunch. Seropian called the experiment a resounding success.","r":"A 4-month slip cost about $400,000, against $1.5M for a 45-person team.","re":"Replaced the weak art house mid-production and crunched for 3 months.","co":"4-month slip at $100,000 a month; 3 months of crunch.","nf":false,"l":"Before any vendor starts production, have it match an internal reference asset with a paid test asset.","i":"Fixed prices move schedule risk to vendors, so a slip costs the core team's burn, not a full staff's. The model shifts work into vetting and reviews, and without a producer for that, approvals become the bottleneck.","a":"Has every vendor matched our reference asset with a paid test asset?","q":"“We could have done a way better job of vetting potential suppliers.”","qa":"Alexander Seropian, founder and president of Wideload Games","st":"Postmortem: Wideload Games' Stubbs the Zombie","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-wideload-games-i-stubbs-the-zombie-i-#:~:text=We%20could%20have%20done%20a,job%20of%20vetting%20potential%20suppliers","sd":"Jan 2006, reprinted Aug 2006","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"subnautica","n":"Subnautica","dv":"Unknown Worlds Entertainment","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/264710/","ls":"Steam","cv":"subnautica.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Planned Subnautica as a 4-person, 6-month casual game with no producer","s":"Reality turned it into 25 people, 5 years and $10 million. The studio ran out of money in late 2014 and survived only by putting the unfinished game on Early Access.","w":"In 2013 Unknown Worlds, after the long Natural Selection 2 project, set out to make something small: a casual underwater survival game in Unity, 4 people, 6 months. The studio had no producer and judged progress by gut feeling. When the money ran out it put the game on Steam Early Access in December 2014; sales were slow until streamers found it in spring 2015. Monthly updates followed and 2 million copies sold during Early Access. Version 1.0 shipped on 23 January 2018. At GDC 2019 programmer Jonas Boetel summed it up: 25 people, 5 years, $10 million.","r":"A 6-month plan became 5 years, 25 people and $10 million.","re":"Open development with monthly updates turned near bankruptcy into a growing hit.","co":"$10 million and 5 years against a 6-month plan; team grew from 4 to 25.","nf":false,"l":"Give even a small project a producer and a dated schedule check, so the team learns it is behind before the money runs out.","i":"With no producer, the team only had gut feeling, and Boetel says that was always wrong, so scope grew unchecked until the cash was gone. Early Access rescued this one because the core was already interesting; it is a funding lifeline, not a plan.","a":"Who on this project would know, and say, that we are 6 months behind?","q":"“We made Natural Selection 2 before and that took a long time, so we wanted to make something small.”","qa":"Jonas Boetel, programmer at Unknown Worlds Entertainment","st":"Subnautica Postmortem","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fkjY_R7zQsM&t=256s","sd":"May 2019","sy":"Participant talk","w8":5,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fkjY_R7zQsM&t=215s"},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Took Subnautica into Steam Early Access and opened its whole development to players","s":"Player money in Early Access pulled Unknown Worlds back from the brink of financial disaster. Charlie Cleveland said a closed development would have produced half the game.","w":"By December 2013 Unknown Worlds was building Subnautica in Unity, its first game after Natural Selection 2. The studio spent all its money getting the game to Steam Early Access, which opened on December 16, 2014. The team kept its Trello task board public, patched often and collected hundreds of thousands of in-game feedback tickets. Early Access sales averted a financial disaster, Cleveland said later. Player feedback also led the team to drop vague science elements. Subnautica left Early Access on January 23, 2018, after three years.","r":"Early Access revenue pulled the studio back from financial disaster.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If you choose Early Access, open the work to players from the first build and let their feedback steer design.","i":"Paying players gave the cash and the feedback that a small studio could not get any other way. It rested on a community that already trusted the team from Natural Selection; without one, Early Access revenue and feedback can stay thin.","a":"Can players see and steer our work from the first Early Access build?","q":"“We had no choice: we spent all our money getting into Early Access.”","qa":"Charlie Cleveland, design director at Unknown Worlds","st":"How Subnautica's community helped create an underwater world dying to be explored","su":"https://www.pcgamesn.com/subnautica/subnautica-10-launch-survival-interview#:~:text=We%20had%20no%20choice,money%20getting%20into%20Early%20Access","sd":"Jan 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"subversion","n":"Subversion","dv":"Introversion Software","y":"2011","sc":"Indie","se":"Cancelled 2011","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built procedural city tech for Subversion before proving a core game loop","s":"After about 2 years of full-time work, no mission was playable. Introversion shelved Subversion in October 2011 and switched to Prison Architect.","w":"Introversion worked on Subversion, a procedural bank-heist game, as a background project for years while it shipped Defcon, Multiwinia and Darwinia+. Around 2009 it became the full-time project. The team kept adding more complex simulations, hoping a game would emerge from them, but never reached a playable mission. On a holiday in San Francisco, a tour of Alcatraz gave Chris Delay the idea for Prison Architect. In October 2011 the team put Subversion on hold and built a 6-week first playable of the new game. After those 6 weeks it had more of a game than Subversion ever had.","r":"Years of tech work produced no playable mission.","re":"The team shelved Subversion and built a 6-week first playable of Prison Architect.","co":"About 6 years of development, the last 2 full-time.","nf":false,"l":"Get a playable core action into the build before you add more simulation or tech.","i":"Building the simulation was fun in itself, so the team kept adding to it and missed that no game was forming. Background work hid the problem for years. Tech-first work is safer when the core action is already known, as in a sequel.","a":"Can a player repeat our core action in today's build?","q":"“Eventually we realised there simply wasn't a core game, and no amount of technology or polish would solve that.”","qa":"Chris Delay, designer and co-founder of Introversion Software","st":"Introversion's Chris Delay on shifting from Subversion to Prison Architect: \"I wanted to build Alcatraz\"","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/au/introversions-chris-delay-on-shifting-from-subversion-to-prison-architect-i-wanted-to-build-alcatraz#:~:text=Eventually%20we%20realised%20there%20simply,polish%20would%20solve%20that","sd":"Oct 2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"suicidesquadkillthejusticeleague","n":"Suicide Squad: Kill the Justice League","dv":"Rocksteady Studios","y":"2024","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/315210/","ls":"Steam","cv":"suicidesquadkillthejusticeleague.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Turned a single-player Arkham studio toward a co-op live-service shooter","s":"After about 7 years the game launched in February 2024 and sold short of the company's expectations. Warner Bros. Discovery wrote the title down and took a $200 million hit to quarterly earnings.","w":"Around the end of 2016, Bloomberg reported, Rocksteady co-founders Jamie Walker and Sefton Hill told staff the studio was dropping a multiplayer puzzle prototype for a Suicide Squad live-service game aimed at 2019 or 2020. With no multiplayer experience, the studio grew from about 160 people to more than 250. The game was revealed in 2020 for 2022 and slipped to 2 February 2024. At launch Rocksteady described a 4-player co-op game with seasonal battle passes. On 9 May 2024 WBD reported an impairment and a $200 million hit to first-quarter EBITDA; CEO David Zaslav called the release disappointing.","r":"The title was impaired and cost $200 million of quarterly EBITDA.","re":"WBD said in November 2024 it would focus on core franchises with proven studios.","co":"$200 million EBITDA impact in Q1 2024; about 7 years of development.","nf":false,"l":"Treat a genre and business-model switch as a new concept with its own prototype and gate, not as the studio's next sequel.","i":"A studio's track record is in one genre's craft; a live-service shooter needs different systems, economy and online skills, so the team grows and learns on the job while the schedule runs. The $200 million is the parent's write-down, not a development budget.","a":"Has anyone on this team shipped a game in this genre before?","q":"“Suicide Squad: Kill the Justice League generated significantly lower revenues in the current year quarter.”","qa":"Warner Bros. Discovery, Inc., first quarter 2024 earnings release","st":"Warner Bros. Discovery, Inc. Reports First Quarter 2024 Earnings Results","su":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1437107/000143710724000107/exhibit99-1wbd1q24earnin.htm#:~:text=Kill%20the%20Justice%20League%20generated,current%20year%20quarter","sd":"May 2024","sy":"Company report","w8":3}]},{"k":"sunlesssea","n":"Sunless Sea","dv":"Failbetter Games","y":"2015","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/304650/","ls":"Steam","cv":"sunlesssea.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Cut the design by 30% for a £60K Kickstarter, then grew it with funding","s":"The extra money bought more content and a later release. Sales passed 100,000 copies, against the studio's early estimate of 5,000 to 50,000.","w":"In 2013 Failbetter costed Sunless Sea at about £90K but expected to raise about £60K, so it cut about 30% of the game. The Kickstarter launched on September 3, 2013 and raised £100,803 from 4,271 backers. The team slipped the release date and opened Early Access in June 2014, which brought more money and more scope. Its public roadmap matched the internal one at every step. The game left Early Access after seven months and released on February 6, 2015, on a £250K budget. Sales passed 100,000 copies on February 25.","r":"Development took 15 months instead of the planned 6.","re":"Kept one public roadmap and hired a comms manager with Early Access money.","co":"9 extra months of development; budget reached £250K.","nf":false,"l":"Size the first plan to the money you are sure of, and add scope only in steps as funding arrives.","i":"Cutting to the expected raise kept the project safe if the campaign came in low. Growing scope with each new pot of money then stretched the schedule. That worked because sales beat the studio's own estimate; with average sales it would hurt.","a":"If funding beats the target, have we decided what the extra money buys?","q":"“In the end, our dev time went from 6 months to 15 months, but we had a regularly updated public roadmap at every step of the way.”","qa":"Alexis Kennedy, co-founder and Chief Narrative Officer of Failbetter Games","st":"Postmortem: Failbetter Games' Sunless Sea","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-failbetter-games-i-sunless-sea-i-#:~:text=In%20the%20end%2C%20our%20dev,every%20step%20of%20the%20way","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built combat as a separate semi-turn-based screen to stay inside a 6-month scope","s":"Players and Early Access reviews disliked it. Failbetter delayed release, threw the system out and built real-time combat late, which never got the polish of an early start.","w":"Failbetter's first PC game switched from top-down sailing to an abstract, semi-turn-based combat screen. The team chose it for Fallen London players and because combat physics and AI looked out of scope for a 6-month project. In Early Access in 2014, most players disliked it and every Early Access review flagged combat. Failbetter pushed back release and prototyped a rework, but sailing and combat still clashed. After adding a Unity developer with AI and animation skills, the team threw out the old mechanic and built real-time combat. The game shipped in February 2015, and reviews still said combat could improve.","r":"Core combat was rebuilt late and shipped less polished.","re":"Added a Unity developer with AI skills and rebuilt combat in real time.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When the schedule or team changes, re-check every core system you chose for the old plan.","i":"The turn-based screen was the cheapest combat for 6 months and no experienced Unity developer. Once scope grew, that reason expired but the system stayed. Kennedy still calls the first choice the least bad at the start; the cost came from not revisiting it sooner.","a":"Which of our core systems were chosen for a schedule we no longer have?","q":"“It was probably still the least worst decision to make at the start, when we thought we only had 6+ months dev time and no experienced Unity resources.”","qa":"Alexis Kennedy, co-founder and Chief Narrative Officer of Failbetter Games","st":"Postmortem: Failbetter Games' Sunless Sea","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-failbetter-games-i-sunless-sea-i-#:~:text=It%20was%20probably%20still%20the,no%20experienced%20Unity%20resources","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Promised that saved games from before Early Access would stay playable until release","s":"Veterans with months-old saves led the feedback, so the early game stayed rawer and grindier than it should have. Keeping old saves working also took far more work than expected.","w":"Failbetter made the promise very early, before Sunless Sea entered Early Access. It looked ethical toward early buyers, and the studio was used to keeping years-old Fallen London accounts working. But Sunless Sea had permadeath, and Fallen London did not. Veterans kept months-old saves with long chains of captains and well-equipped ships, and they were the loudest voices on the forums. Compatibility bugs surfaced right before patches and as support work after them. The game left Early Access after 7 months and released on February 6, 2015. In March 2015, Alexis Kennedy called the promise unwise.","r":"Balance tilted toward mid-game veterans, and the early game stayed grindy.","re":"Failbetter kept the promise: old saves still loaded at final release.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you promise save compatibility to early buyers, check how old saves will skew balance and support work.","i":"Old saves keep veterans in the late game, and veterans give most of the feedback, so the early game stops getting attention. The cost is highest with permadeath; Fallen London, which has none, kept years-old player state working routinely.","a":"Can we afford to keep every early save working until launch?","q":"“On top of that… backwards compatibility was just much, much more work than we’d expected.”","qa":"Alexis Kennedy, co-founder and Chief Narrative Officer of Failbetter Games","st":"Postmortem: Failbetter Games' Sunless Sea","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-failbetter-games-i-sunless-sea-i-#:~:text=On%20top%20of%20that,much%2C%20much%20more%20work","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Kept writing in the StoryNexus browser-game CMS and fed it to Unity as JSON","s":"Writers who knew the tool produced complex content fast and iterated quickly. Its old mechanisms and web UI came along too, which hurt the first combat and made the interface clunky.","w":"Failbetter had built browser story games on its StoryNexus CMS for 5 years when it started Sunless Sea, its first actual videogame. The team ported parts of StoryNexus into Unity so the client read a JSON download from the CMS. Writers who knew StoryNexus produced complex content fast and shared work quickly. The first combat reused StoryNexus code built for player-to-player interactions, and its special cases grew until the team reworked combat. The game took about 15 months against a 6-month plan, and in March 2015 Alexis Kennedy called the reuse a big win on balance.","r":"Writers produced and revised complex content quickly.","re":"Failbetter kept StoryNexus for future games but planned a more partitioned architecture.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Keep authoring in the tool your writers already know, even an imperfect one, as long as programmers can tune it.","i":"Writers fluent in a tool move faster than they would in a better tool they have to learn. The win covers authoring: mechanisms built for a browser game, such as social interactions reused as combat, cost Failbetter more than they saved.","a":"Do our writers already know the tool we plan to author in?","q":"“First, even when a tool’s imperfect, there’s a big win from using it when the content creators are comfortable with it and the tech team understand it well enough to tune it.”","qa":"Alexis Kennedy, co-founder of Failbetter Games and creative director of Sunless Sea","st":"Postmortem: Failbetter Games' Sunless Sea","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-failbetter-games-i-sunless-sea-i-#:~:text=First%2C%20even%20when%20a,well%20enough%20to%20tune%20it","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"sunlessskies","n":"Sunless Skies","dv":"Failbetter Games","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"Early Access 2017","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/596970/","ls":"Steam","cv":"sunlessskies.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Took Sunless Skies into Early Access in 2017, as with Sunless Sea in 2014","s":"Early Access sold about 15% as many copies as Sunless Sea had in the same period. Cash fell short, 4 staff were made redundant and the May 2018 launch was dropped.","w":"After a successful Kickstarter, Failbetter Games set Sunless Skies to open in Early Access on 30 August 2017, as it had done with Sunless Sea in 2014. In February 2018 Hannah Flynn reported Early Access sales at about 15% of Sunless Sea's over the same period. The studio said it went in too early and lost a key promotional moment. It also named a changed market and backers who already owned the game. Four staff left after a redundancy process, and the May launch was dropped. Sunless Skies released on 31 January 2019.","r":"The studio had far less cash than planned before launch.","re":"Failbetter restructured and worked on movement, combat and the Albion region.","co":"4 redundancies; May 2018 launch dropped; sales about 15% of Sunless Sea's.","nf":false,"l":"Enter Early Access only when the build is ready for press attention, because the launch spike comes once.","i":"The Early Access launch is when storefronts and press pay attention, and an unready build spends that moment. Here a successful Kickstarter had also pre-sold thousands of copies and the market had changed since 2014, so timing was one cause of three.","a":"Is our Early Access build ready for press attention on day one?","q":"“In hindsight, we went into Early Access too early.”","qa":"Hannah Flynn, Failbetter Games","st":"Failbetter Update: Weathering a Storm","su":"https://www.failbettergames.com/failbetter-update-weathering-a-storm/#:~:text=In%20hindsight%2C%20we%20went%20into,Access%20too%20early","sd":"Feb 2018","sy":"Company statement","w8":3}]},{"k":"supercratebox","n":"Super Crate Box","dv":"Vlambeer","y":"2010","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2012","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/212800/","ls":"Steam","cv":"supercratebox.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Set out to match the PC version's feel on an iOS touchscreen","s":"The port took about 6 months, as long as the original game, and controls ate most of it. Vlambeer and Halfbot recoded the game from scratch in Objective-C.","w":"Super Crate Box began as Vlambeer's free PC and Mac business card: 3 days of prototyping, then 6 months of development in GameMaker 7. Requests for mobile versions poured in, so Vlambeer teamed up with Halfbot and decided to charge for an iOS version. The goal was to match the PC game, not improve it, and the control scheme was the biggest challenge. The team tested it on strangers on trains and tweaked it after each session. The game was recoded from scratch in Objective-C and launched in January 2012, after a port as long as the original.","r":"The port took as long as making the original game.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About 6 months, the same as the original game's development.","nf":false,"l":"Before you estimate a touch port of a precision game, build the touch controls and test them on new players.","i":"A precision game lives in its controls, and a touchscreen removes the buttons that made it precise. That is where the port's time went. A slower game, or one designed for touch from the start, would not carry the same cost.","a":"Have new players tried our touch controls before we estimate the port?","q":"“The iOS port took about the same time as that, with the main timesink being the controls.”","qa":"Rami Ismail (business and development) and Jan Willem Nijman (game designer), Vlambeer","st":"What's in the box? Making of Super Crate Box for iOS","su":"https://www.pocketgamer.biz/whats-in-the-box-making-of-super-crate-box-for-ios/#:~:text=The%20iOS%20port%20took%20about,timesink%20being%20the%20controls","sd":"Mar 2012","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"supermario3dworld","n":"Super Mario 3D World","dv":"Nintendo","y":"2013","sc":"AAA","se":"Expanded edition 2021","lk":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/super-mario-3d-world-plus-bowsers-fury-switch/","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"supermario3dworld.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Cut camera types that clashed with 4-player multiplayer early on","s":"The team knew early which ideas would work. Satoru Iwata summed it up as no waste and no wholesale reset, and Shigeru Miyamoto agreed that everything built went into the game.","w":"After Super Mario 3D Land shipped on 3DS in November 2011, Nintendo's Tokyo team had settled on a parallel camera that keeps a set distance and direction from Mario. For 3D World on Wii U, the team chose multiplayer for up to 4 players and soon dropped several specs that clashed with it, including some camera types. It ruled out split-screen first and quickly found a single-screen format. Shigeru Miyamoto said it became clear early which ideas could work. The game launched in Japan on 21 November 2013 without a late reset.","r":"The team knew early which ideas the camera could support.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Fix the camera rules for your core mode first, and cut every feature that fights them before level work starts.","i":"The camera decides which level ideas can work, so fixing it early stops the team from building content that later fails. The team had already shipped the same camera in 3D Land; a team new to its camera needs prototypes to reach that certainty.","a":"Have we cut every feature that fights our camera rules?","q":"“Deciding on a multiplayer mode immediately eliminated several options.”","qa":"Shigeru Miyamoto, Nintendo","st":"Iwata Asks: Super Mario 3D World","su":"https://iwataasks.nintendo.com/interviews/wiiu/super-mario-3d-world/0/2/#:~:text=Deciding%20on%20a%20multiplayer%20mode,eliminated%20several%20options","sd":"2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"supermariogalaxy","n":"Super Mario Galaxy","dv":"Nintendo EAD Tokyo","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/wii/rmgj/index.html","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"supermariogalaxy.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built a 3-month planet-and-gravity prototype with a limited group before full production","s":"Staff who had called the spherical idea impossible could now play it, and full-scale development started from that build. The game shipped on 1 November 2007.","w":"Koizumi directed the Mario 128 demo at the 2000 GameCube unveiling and spent years doubting its spherical terrain could become a product. After Donkey Kong Jungle Beat shipped in December 2004, Miyamoto asked the Tokyo studio for a Nintendo-character game, and staff proposed making the next Mario themselves. Most then said the recipe Koizumi showed them could not be cooked. So a limited group spent about 3 months on a prototype: a planet in space, with gravity. Full development started from it. At E3 2006 Miyamoto promised release within 6 months of the Wii launch; it came 11 months after.","r":"Full development began on a mechanic the team had already played.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About 3 months of a limited group before full development.","nf":false,"l":"When the team calls your core mechanic impossible, build a small playable prototype before you staff up.","i":"A recipe on paper did not convince the programmers who had to build it; a playable slice did, and it settled the technical question before the studio scaled. The prototype proved feel and feasibility, not schedule: Galaxy still shipped 11 months after the Wii.","a":"Has everyone on the team played our core mechanic yet?","q":"“そこで、試食できるようなものが必要だと思い、限られたメンバーで、3カ月くらいかけてプロトタイプ（試作品）をつくりました。” (So I thought we needed something people could taste, and with a limited group we spent about 3 months building a prototype.)","qa":"Yoshiaki Koizumi, director of Super Mario Galaxy at Nintendo","st":"社長が訊く『スーパーマリオギャラクシー』 Vol.1 プロデューサー・ディレクター 篇","su":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/wii/interview/rmgj/vol1/index.html#:~:text=%E3%81%9D%E3%81%93%E3%81%A7%E3%80%81%E8%A9%A6%E9%A3%9F%E3%81%A7%E3%81%8D%E3%82%8B%E3%82%88%E3%81%86%E3%81%AA%E3%82%82%E3%81%AE%E3%81%8C%E5%BF%85%E8%A6%81%E3%81%A0%E3%81%A8%E6%80%9D%E3%81%84,%E3%83%97%E3%83%AD%E3%83%88%E3%82%BF%E3%82%A4%E3%83%97%EF%BC%88%E8%A9%A6%E4%BD%9C%E5%93%81%EF%BC%89%E3%82%92%E3%81%A4%E3%81%8F%E3%82%8A%E3%81%BE%E3%81%97%E3%81%9F","sd":"2007","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"supermariogalaxy2","n":"Super Mario Galaxy 2","dv":"Nintendo EAD Tokyo","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/wii/sb4j/","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"supermariogalaxy2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Started Galaxy 2 as a one-year Galaxy 1.5 that reused the first game's terrain","s":"Staff kept adding new terrain and play ideas until over 90% of the courses were new. The 1-year plan took 2.5 years.","w":"Super Mario Galaxy shipped in November 2007. Looking again at its spherical terrain, Shigeru Miyamoto saw room for new ideas on the same ground. The sequel started under the working title Super Mario Galaxy 1.5, with a goal to finish in 1 year. Staff kept asking for more terrain and more new play. Before long, over 90% of the courses were new and the reused parts were hard to spot. The 1.5 became a 2, and development took 2.5 years. Since the player controls already existed, Iwata noted, that time went into stages and tuning. It shipped in May 2010.","r":"A planned 1-year reuse sequel became a full 2.5-year sequel.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"1.5 years over the 1-year plan; over 90% of courses built new.","nf":false,"l":"For a reuse sequel, write down what you keep and count every new course against the schedule.","i":"A working title and a 1-year goal set the intent but did not block new work. Staff saw fun in new terrain and kept adding it. Nintendo could afford the extra 1.5 years; a studio with a fixed publisher date may not.","a":"Do we have a written list of what our sequel reuses?","q":"“もともと「１年でつくろう」と言ったのに結果的には２年半もかかってしまいましたから。” (We originally said ‘let’s make it in a year’, but in the end it took as long as two and a half years.)","qa":"Shigeru Miyamoto, Nintendo","st":"社長が訊く『スーパーマリオギャラクシー ２』","su":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/wii/interview/sb4j/vol1/index.html#:~:text=%E3%82%82%E3%81%A8%E3%82%82%E3%81%A8%E3%80%8C%EF%BC%91%E5%B9%B4%E3%81%A7%E3%81%A4%E3%81%8F%E3%82%8D%E3%81%86%E3%80%8D%E3%81%A8%E8%A8%80%E3%81%A3%E3%81%9F%E3%81%AE%E3%81%AB,%EF%BC%92%E5%B9%B4%E5%8D%8A%E3%82%82%E3%81%8B%E3%81%8B%E3%81%A3%E3%81%A6%E3%81%97%E3%81%BE%E3%81%84%E3%81%BE%E3%81%97%E3%81%9F%E3%81%8B%E3%82%89","sd":"2010","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"supermeatboy","n":"Super Meat Boy","dv":"Team Meat","y":"2010","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/40800/","ls":"Steam","cv":"supermeatboy.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built its own engine and a Flash animation exporter instead of using Unity","s":"The programmer could trace any bug down to the hardware, and one export moved animations and sound cues into the engine. Two people reached XBLA certification in 18 months.","w":"Edmund McMillen and Tommy Refenes teamed up in 2008 to turn a Flash prototype into a console game. Refenes wrote the engine and tools himself, although friends at FlashBang kept pushing Unity on him. He built an in-game level editor for McMillen and a Flash Exporter that packed symbols into one texture with animation data and sound cues. The game took 18 months between the first line of engine code and XBLA certification, and it launched on 20 October 2010. In the 2011 postmortem, Refenes credited that speed to knowing every line of the code.","r":"Two developers shipped a content-heavy console game in 18 months.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you pick an engine, check that your programmers can trace a crash in it down to the hardware.","i":"Bugs got fixed fast because the person fixing them had written every line. That depends on one programmer with engine skills on a 2-person team; a bigger team on an in-house engine does not get the same speed.","a":"Can our programmers trace a crash in this engine without outside help?","q":"“I honestly feel the reason we were able to do this is because I was so involved with the code.”","qa":"Tommy Refenes, programmer at Team Meat","st":"Postmortem: Team Meat's Super Meat Boy","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-team-meat-s-i-super-meat-boy-i-#:~:text=I%20honestly%20feel%20the%20reason,so%20involved%20with%20the%20code","sd":"Apr 2011","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"supercellportfolio","n":"Supercell portfolio","dv":"Supercell","y":"2016","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://supercell.com/en/games/clashroyale/","ls":"Website","cv":"supercellportfolio.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Let small game teams grow only with progress and kill their own games","s":"By February 2023 Supercell had launched 5 hit games and killed more than 30. It had also gone over 4 years without a new global launch.","w":"Supercell builds new games in small independent cells, about 5 people each in 2016, that stay small early and grow with progress. Major decisions, including a kill, stay with the team. In 2016 game lead Timur Haussila said the company had 4 published games and 14 killed; Smash Land died after 9 months of work and a beta. In 2019 the Rush Wars team killed its game after beta: the early game was fun but did not carry into the endgame. In February 2023 CEO Ilkka Paananen counted 5 hits, 30+ kills and no global launch since December 12, 2018.","r":"Most new games stopped before global launch; none launched globally for 4 years.","re":"Supercell widened hiring to younger developers and set up North America teams.","co":"30+ games killed; no new global launch from December 2018 to February 2023.","nf":false,"l":"Keep a new game's team small until it shows progress, and let that team make the kill call.","i":"Small early teams keep each kill cheap, so 30+ kills stay affordable. Team-owned calls remove the politics that keep weak projects alive. With a bar this high, the cost moves to time: years can pass without a launch.","a":"Does the team building this game own the decision to kill it?","q":"“We have launched 5 hit games but we have killed 30+, by my latest count.”","qa":"Ilkka Paananen, CEO of Supercell","st":"The Next Chapter of Supercell","su":"https://supercell.com/en/news/next-chapter/#:~:text=We%20have%20launched%205%20hit,by%20my%20latest%20count","sd":"Feb 2023","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"superhot","n":"SUPERHOT","dv":"SUPERHOT Team","y":"2016","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/322500/","ls":"Steam","cv":"superhot.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Built the full game on the 7-day jam mechanic and kept it short","s":"SUPERHOT sold over 150,000 copies in its first 3 weeks. Most negative Steam reviews were about its length for the price.","w":"In 2013 a group of friends in Łódź built SUPERHOT, where time moves only when you move, for the 7 Day FPS jam. The jam build was 3 levels on 3 computers, stitched together as separate Unity players. The team made a better version for the WGK showcase and won it. They paid for everything themselves until a Kickstarter raised $250,798 in mid-2014, which turned them into a real company. The game came out in February 2016 with short, intense levels. Three weeks later it had sold over 150,000 copies, and Piotr Iwanicki defended the length.","r":"Over 150,000 copies sold in 3 weeks, with complaints about length.","re":"The team promised free updates, including an endless arena mode.","co":"7 days for the jam prototype; self-funded until Kickstarter.","nf":false,"l":"Build the full game only around a mechanic a quick prototype has already proven, and size the content to what it sustains.","i":"A mechanic proven in a week tells you the core is fun before real money is spent, and short levels keep it fresh. The price is the length complaint: players who judge value by hours will not accept the trade.","a":"Is our game sized to what our proven core mechanic can sustain?","q":"“We could have made it longer, but decided otherwise to promote multiple playthroughs.”","qa":"Piotr Iwanicki, creative director and CEO of SUPERHOT Team","st":"\"It was supposed to be short and intense\" - creator of Superhot defends the game's length and announces free updates","su":"https://www.gamepressure.com/editorials/it-was-supposed-to-be-short-and-intense-creator-of-superhot-defen/za9a#:~:text=We%20could%20have%20made%20it,promote%20multiple%20playthroughs","sd":"Mar 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"swat3closequartersbattle","n":"SWAT 3: Close Quarters Battle","dv":"Sierra Northwest","y":"1999","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/560370/","ls":"Steam","cv":"swat3closequartersbattle.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Locked an ambitious feature set to a fixed Christmas date without a concrete schedule","s":"8 months in, the first real schedule showed a miss of several months. The team cut features into tiers and postponed multiplayer, and some of it worked heavy overtime to ship on time.","w":"Sierra's SWAT 3 team aimed for an engine like Unreal, animation better than Half-Life, better AI than both, multiplayer and SWAT tactics, all by a fixed Christmas 1999 date. Team members worried about scope had no schedule to argue with. The first 8 months went into the engine, architecture and tools. Only then did a schedule for the remaining 10 months show a miss of several months. Features were split into required, optional and later version, padding was sacrificed and multiplayer was postponed. SWAT 3 shipped in November 1999, and Jim Napier wrote the postmortem in March 2000.","r":"Multiplayer slipped to a later release, and some staff worked overtime for 10 months.","re":"The team tiered features as required, optional and later, and cut to fit.","co":"Multiplayer deferred; overtime for some staff through the last 10 months.","nf":false,"l":"Build a concrete schedule before you lock the feature set, so scope worries can be argued with numbers.","i":"Each feature looked doable alone, but together they exceeded a small team's fixed time. Without a schedule nobody could prove it until 8 months had gone. Here the date could not move, so cuts were the only lever; a movable date changes the trade.","a":"Does a task-level schedule show this feature set fitting our date?","q":"“In design meetings, we would say something like, “um, this is getting a bit complicated here,” but without a concrete schedule, our concerns were difficult to justify.”","qa":"Jim Napier, lead programmer on SWAT 3 at Sierra","st":"Postmortem: Sierra's SWAT3 Close Quarters Battle","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-sierra-s-i-swat3-close-quarters-battle-i-#:~:text=In%20design%20meetings%2C%20we%20would,our%20concerns%20were%20difficult%20to%20justify","sd":"Mar 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"systemshock","n":"System Shock","dv":"Nightdive Studios","y":"2023","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/482400/","ls":"Steam","cv":"systemshock.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Turned a funded remaster into a completely new game and switched from Unity to Unreal","s":"The budget outgrew the crowdfunding and the project went on hiatus in 2018. It shipped in May 2023, more than 5 years after its first target.","w":"In June 2016 Nightdive's Kickstarter for System Shock beat its $900,000 goal, with over 21,000 backers giving more than $1.3M. The first target was late 2017. In early 2017 the team switched from Unity to Unreal Engine 4 and moved to a 2018 date. With the switch it began envisioning more, straying from the original's core, and new hires brought their own vision. As the budget grew, Nightdive looked for publishers. In February 2018 Stephen Kick put the team on hiatus. Nightdive rebuilt the project, signed with Prime Matter in 2021 and released it on May 30, 2023.","r":"Release moved from late 2017 to May 2023.","re":"Paused the team, rebuilt the project and signed with Prime Matter in 2021.","co":"Over 5 years of delay; budget grew past the crowdfunding; team let go.","nf":false,"l":"When crowdfunding beats the goal, lock the pitched scope in writing before adding features or changing engines.","i":"Overfunding feels like permission to build a bigger game, and new hires with their own vision push the same way. Kick does not regret the Unreal switch itself; the cost came from the wider vision that rode along with it.","a":"Are we still building the game we pitched, or a bigger one?","q":"“With the switch we began envisioning doing more, but straying from the core concepts of the original title.”","qa":"Stephen Kick, founder of Nightdive Studios","st":"Sometimes You Need To Take a Step Back In Order To Take Two Steps Forward","su":"https://www.kickstarter.com/projects/1598858095/system-shock/posts/2115044#:~:text=With%20the%20switch%20we%20began,concepts%20of%20the%20original%20title","sd":"Feb 2018","sy":"Participant account","w8":5},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Paused the remake, let the whole team go and restarted with a small one","s":"Nightdive kept the game alive on money from its other projects until Prime Matter signed it in 2021. The remake shipped in May 2023, more than 5 years after the pause.","w":"Nightdive raised over $1 million on Kickstarter in July 2016 for a remake due in late 2017. The team grew the scope, moved from Unity to Unreal and spent the money; it then looked for $5 to 10 million in outside funding. In February 2018 Stephen Kick put the project on hiatus and let everybody go. Nightdive rebooted it with a new team, diverting money from other projects while publishers said no. Prime Matter signed on in 2021, and the game shipped in May 2023. In June 2023 Kick called it the game he had wished to make in 2016.","r":"The project survived, but its team was gone and release slipped to 2023.","re":"Nightdive funded the small team itself until Prime Matter signed in 2021.","co":"Original team let go; about 3 years of studio money from other projects.","nf":false,"l":"Before you restart a stalled project, decide who pays the small team and for how many months while you look for a partner.","i":"A reset removes a team and a plan that outgrew the money, but it does not bring new money. The restart held because the studio had other income to divert; a one-project studio could not carry years of publisher rejections.","a":"If we restart, how many months can we fund the small team ourselves?","q":"“I basically just had to try and remove myself from all that and just dedicate ourselves and the small team to fulfilling this goal.”","qa":"Stephen Kick, founder of Nightdive Studios","st":"Inside Nightdive's 8-Year-Long Odyssey To Remake The Original System Shock","su":"https://www.timeextension.com/features/inside-nightdives-8-year-long-odyssey-to-remake-the-original-system-shock#:~:text=I%20basically%20just%20had%20to,to%20fulfilling%20this%20goal","sd":"Jun 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"systemshock2","n":"System Shock 2","dv":"Irrational Games","y":"1999","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2025","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/238210/","ls":"Steam","cv":"systemshock2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built a first game on Looking Glass's unfinished Thief code under a 1-year plan","s":"Thief slipped more than 6 months, so the shared engine and the promised Looking Glass staff arrived late. Irrational shipped within 2 months of target, but the entire last year was crunch.","w":"Three Looking Glass veterans formed Irrational and prototyped a sci-fi game on the still-unfinished Thief technology. Electronic Arts signed early in 1998, bringing the System Shock licence. With a year for the full team and a small budget, the game shared Thief's live code base rather than a finished engine. Thief slipped more than 6 months. Promised Looking Glass staff arrived only in December 1998, and the full team was complete just 6 months before ship. Engine glitches forced early lock-down of levels; the last year was crunch. The game shipped in 1999 within 2 months of target.","r":"Shared engine and promised staff came late; full team only 6 months before ship.","re":"Subcontracted Looking Glass staff after Thief shipped and worked a year of crunch.","co":"Entire last year in crunch; full team only 6 months before ship.","nf":false,"l":"If you build on another team's unfinished engine, plan for their slip to become yours and budget staff for it.","i":"Sharing a live code base means the other team's deadline sets yours, and their crunch empties the staff pool you were counting on. Chey still rates designing to the technology as what made the ship date; the cost landed on people, not the schedule.","a":"If the engine team slips 6 months, what happens to our date?","q":"“The Thief technology was still under development and months away from completion when our team started working with it.”","qa":"Jonathan Chey, project manager and programmer, Irrational Games","st":"Postmortem: Irrational Games' System Shock 2 (1999)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-irrational-games-i-system-shock-2-i-#:~:text=The%20Thief%20technology%20was%20still,started%20working%20with%20it","sd":"Nov 1999, reprinted Dec 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Staffing","t":"Planned level building with a limited level-designer budget on the unfinished Dark Engine","s":"Level design took far longer than expected and ended up blocking shipment, though the game still shipped within 2 months of its target.","w":"Irrational's brief was a prototype on Looking Glass's Thief technology, which was still months from completion. EA signed on as publisher in early 1998, and the game got roughly one year with its full team. The plan had a limited budget for level designers, and training them on the complex Dark editor took a long time. Editor errors forced the team to lock level architecture early. Level building ended up blocking shipment, and the game shipped within 2 months of its target. In November 1999 Jonathan Chey wrote that the team had seen the same problems on Thief.","r":"Level building became the last item holding up shipment.","re":"The team locked level architecture early to keep the Dark editor stable.","co":"Shipped within 2 months of target; little time left for playtest tuning.","nf":false,"l":"Before you fix the level-designer headcount, build one full sample level in the real editor and time it.","i":"Level work grows with object placement and editor stability, and both were harder than on Thief, where the same problem had already shown up. The schedule held within 2 months, so this is a bottleneck, not a failure.","a":"How long did our sample level take to build in the real editor?","q":"“Despite our understanding from the very beginning that the level building would be a problematic part of the System Shock development process, we didn't quite grasp how difficult and time consuming it would be, nor did we expect that it would eventually block the shipment of the game.”","qa":"Jonathan Chey, project manager on System Shock 2 at Irrational Games","st":"Postmortem: Irrational Games' System Shock 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-irrational-games-i-system-shock-2-i-#:~:text=Despite%20our%20understanding%20from%20the,block%20the%20shipment%20of%20the%20game","sd":"Nov 1999, reprinted Dec 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Chose simple reusable systems that fit the borrowed engine over scripted set pieces","s":"Security cameras built from stripped-down AI gave a lot of gameplay for little code or design work. When the team tried Half-Life style scripted scenes, they became time sinks.","w":"Jonathan Chey, Ken Levine and Rob Fermier left Looking Glass to found Irrational and test their own project ideas. Their first prototype ran on Thief technology that was still in development and focused on simple, reusable gameplay. The rule was to check what the technology could do, then design to fit it. Security cameras were existing AI with most abilities removed; they summoned monsters until players shut the system down. EA signed in early 1998. Half-Life style scripted scenes broke the rule and became time sinks. System Shock 2 shipped in 1999, within 2 months of its target.","r":"One system gave the game a lot of play with minimal design work.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"List what your engine already does well, then build gameplay systems from those parts before you plan any scripted set pieces.","i":"On a borrowed engine, every feature outside its strengths needs new code and debugging, so systems built from existing parts multiply content cheaply. The rule suits systemic games; a story-led game built on scripted scenes needs tools made for them.","a":"Which of our planned features does the engine already support today?","q":"“First, we designed to our technology rather than building technology to fit our design.”","qa":"Jonathan Chey, project manager and co-founder of Irrational Games","st":"Postmortem: Irrational Games' System Shock 2 (1999)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-irrational-games-i-system-shock-2-i-#:~:text=First%2C%20we%20designed%20to%20our,to%20fit%20our%20design","sd":"Nov 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"tabularasa","n":"Tabula Rasa","dv":"Destination Games","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"Closed 2009","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Audience & market","t":"Designed Tabula Rasa as a global IP to please both Asian and Western markets","s":"Korean colleagues kept saying the Asian styling felt like a foreigner's work. After 2 years the idea was abandoned and the game restarted, finishing under corporate discontent; it launched in late 2007 and closed after about 15 months.","w":"Garriott's team started Tabula Rasa for NCsoft with many staff from its Asian partner, aiming at a game popular in Asia. For 2 years the team chased Asian style while Korean counterparts kept saying it never felt domestic. The global-IP idea was then dropped. In July 2004 the lead designer, lead programmer and an artist left, Garriott took over design, and 30 to 40 developers remained with a 2005 target. The 2-year-late restart ran under unusual pressures and corporate discontent. The game launched in October 2007, Garriott resigned in November 2008, and the servers closed on 28 February 2009.","r":"2 years of work thrown out; the game restarted under corporate discontent.","re":"Restarted in 2004 with Garriott as lead designer and a 30-40 person team.","co":"2 years lost; launched late 2007 after a 2005 target; closed after about 15 months.","nf":false,"l":"Pick one primary audience at concept; do not design for two markets at once or staff on that bet.","i":"A Western team imitating Asian style produced work its own Korean partners read as foreign, so 2 years went into a direction later abandoned. Garriott links the failure to the late restart and its pressure; whether a one-market game would have survived is untested.","a":"Which single market is this game for, and who decided?","q":"“After two years of trying we eventually abandoned the idea of trying to create a global intellectual property and decided to turn back on something we ourselves would make for ourselves.”","qa":"Richard Garriott, lead designer of Tabula Rasa, NCsoft","st":"Richard Garriott on What Went Wrong With Tabula Rasa and Ultima VIII","su":"https://www.gamebanshee.com/9xr6#:~:text=After%20two%20years%20of%20trying,make%20for%20ourselves","sd":"Dec 2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"teamfortress2","n":"Team Fortress 2","dv":"Valve","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/440/","ls":"Steam","cv":"teamfortress2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Took the Team Fortress sequel toward realistic military combat with a Commander class","s":"Robin Walker later said the design was not fun. Years of testing refined or removed its mechanics until the game was close to the original Team Fortress again.","w":"Valve hired the original Team Fortress team, Robin Walker among them, in 1998 to make a sequel. Around the turn of the century it revealed Team Fortress 2: Brotherhood of Arms, with realistic graphics, a strategy-driven Commander class, military weapons, tanks and helicopters. Walker later said it was not fun. Valve never dropped one design for another: constant iteration and testing refined or removed mechanics until the core was close to Team Fortress Classic again. In June 2007 Walker and Charlie Brown said the game had been in development for close to 10 years. It shipped in October 2007.","r":"Most of the military design was refined away through years of testing.","re":"Valve iterated toward distinct classes and a stylized 1960s art style.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Prove a new direction is fun in playtests before you build technology and assets around it.","i":"Each added system, from the Commander role to vehicles, had to be fun alongside the rest, and testing kept removing them. Valve could iterate without a restart because it would not ship until ready; a team with a fixed date gets no such slack.","a":"Have players found the new direction fun in a playable build?","q":"“It wasn't fun”","qa":"Robin Walker, Valve","st":"Team Fortress 2 Preview","su":"https://www.shacknews.com/article/47465/team-fortress-2-preview#:~:text=It%20wasn't%20fun","sd":"Jun 2007","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"telltalegamesstudioslate","n":"Telltale Games studio slate","dv":"Telltale Games","y":"","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Ran several licensed episodic series at once with little pre-production","s":"Executive reviews late in each episode forced rework and heavy crunch. In November 2017 Telltale laid off about 90 people, and it closed in September 2018.","w":"After The Walking Dead's success, Telltale moved to a bigger office, and the plan from the top was three Walking Deads at the same time. In 2015 a hiring push aimed to grow from about 200 to about 400 staff. Projects had little pre-production: a writers' room, then about 6 months per episode. Episodes often blew up at executive review, and some took almost a full year. One developer on Michonne worked 70 to 90 hour weeks. In November 2017 about 90 people, 25% of the company, were laid off. The studio closed in September 2018.","r":"Late reviews forced rework; some episodes took almost a full year.","re":"A new CEO cut about 90 jobs and tried to raise funding.","co":"About 90 people laid off in November 2017; studio closed September 2018.","nf":false,"l":"Add a parallel project only when the current ones have finished pre-production with a locked plan.","i":"Without pre-production, the executive review became the first real design check, and it came after the work was built. Each extra project multiplied that late rework. Crunch eased in later years, so the process was not equally bad through the studio's life.","a":"Has every project we run finished pre-production with a locked plan?","q":"“I was hired in the middle of their big hiring push, when they were trying to grow from around 200 something employees to 400 something employees.”","qa":"Emily Grace Buck, narrative designer at Telltale Games","st":"Telltale: The Human Stories Behind The Games","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s-2nNksMBpg&t=422s","sd":"May 2019","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"terrariaotherworld","n":"Terraria: Otherworld","dv":"Re-Logic","y":"2018","sc":"Indie","se":"Cancelled 2018","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Outsourced core development of the Terraria spin-off to external studios","s":"Three years after the 2015 announcement, a status review found the game far from its vision and behind schedule. Re-Logic cancelled it in April 2018.","w":"Re-Logic announced Terraria: Otherworld in 2015 and had external teams build it. In 2017 Engine Software stepped back, and Pipeworks took over with 505. A thorough status review against the intended design then showed both studios the game was much further from the finish line than they had imagined. Finishing it would have pulled Re-Logic away from its other projects. On 12 April 2018 Re-Logic cancelled the game. It said outsourcing such a critical title was the wrong call, and that it had spoken about the game too early.","r":"The game stayed far from its vision and far behind schedule.","re":"Re-Logic moved core development of future titles in-house.","co":"About 3 years of work by 2 external teams, then cancelled.","nf":false,"l":"Keep core development of a title your company depends on with your own people.","i":"The studio that held the vision was not building the game, and the gap stayed hidden until a thorough review. Re-Logic kept 505/Pipeworks on console and mobile work, so outsourcing ports and support carries less of this risk.","a":"Are our own people building the core of our most important title?","q":"“Trying to outsource development of such a critical title for our company – even if just in part – was not the way to go.”","qa":"Re-Logic, statement posted by Loki","st":"This is the End: Otherworld Development Cancelled","su":"https://forums.terraria.org/index.php?threads/this-is-the-end-otherworld-development-cancelled.68594/#:~:text=Trying%20to%20outsource%20development%20of%20such,was%20not%20the%20way%20to%20go","sd":"Apr 2018","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"bannersaga","n":"The Banner Saga","dv":"Stoic Studio","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/237990/","ls":"Steam","cv":"bannersaga.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Spent a sevenfold Kickstarter surplus on design iteration instead of the pitched small game","s":"The game grew from 8 to 10 characters and 5 to 6 hours to 25+ characters and 12+ hours. It took almost exactly 2 years, with the story rewritten at least 5 times.","w":"Three ex-BioWare developers pitched The Banner Saga on Kickstarter in March 2012 as a strategy game of 8 to 10 characters and 5 to 6 hours. The campaign closed on 20 April 2012 with $723,886 from 20,042 backers against a $100,000 goal. Co-founder Alex Thomas says the money let the team run many iterations instead of settling on first ideas: at least 5 major story revisions, over a dozen major changes to combat and usability, a reworked travel game. Game Developer noted the delays. The game shipped on 14 January 2014, almost exactly 2 years in, with 25+ characters.","r":"A game 2 to 3 times the pitched size, shipped after almost exactly 2 years.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"$723,886 raised against a $100,000 goal; almost exactly 2 years of work.","nf":false,"l":"If you raise far more than planned, decide in writing whether the surplus buys iteration or content, and set the new ship date to match.","i":"Stoic prototyped combat on a chessboard before writing code and kept changing systems until they fit, so the surplus went into design rather than headcount. That suits 3 experienced developers with a hand-painted 2D pipeline; a bigger team burns the same surplus on salaries.","a":"What exactly does our extra funding buy: iteration, content or time?","q":"“I'd have to say there's almost nothing left of the original concept, especially in terms of scope and design, and I mean that in a good way.”","qa":"Alex Thomas, co-founder and artist of Stoic","st":"Road to the IGF: Stoic's The Banner Saga","su":"https://gamedeveloper.com/design/road-to-the-igf-stoic-s-i-the-banner-saga-i-#:~:text=I%27d%20have%20to%20say%20there%27s,in%20a%20good%20way","sd":"Feb 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"bannersaga2","n":"The Banner Saga 2","dv":"Stoic Studio","y":"2016","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/281640/","ls":"Steam","cv":"bannersaga2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Kept paying a console port vendor for milestones it had not delivered","s":"The vendor quit after 6 months of staff churn, leaving the ports a year behind and Stoic out about a year's salary, while its only programmer's time went to porting instead of the sequel.","w":"After The Banner Saga shipped in January 2014, technical director John Watson spent most of the next year on localisation and console ports while the sequel sat in slow pre-production. Stoic picked a porting house after 3 two-week prototypes, then watched it miss milestone after milestone as its staff left, and kept paying to keep things moving. After 6 months of churn the vendor abandoned the contract and went bankrupt. Production of Banner Saga 2 started in earnest only with E3 2015 looming; milestones slipped and the 4-person team crunched for months. The game shipped on 19 April 2016.","r":"Ports a year behind schedule, about a year's salary lost, sequel features and tools starved.","re":"Hired Shiny Shoe for PS4 and Xbox One and shelved the Vita port.","co":"About one year's salary lost; console ports a year late.","nf":false,"l":"Pay port vendors only for delivered milestones, and pull the plug at the first missed one.","i":"A 4-person studio had one programmer, so every hour the vendor cost him came straight out of the sequel's features and tools. Watson says fear of restarting the vendor search kept the money flowing; they had to restart anyway, months and dollars later.","a":"Have we paid any vendor for a milestone we have not received?","q":"“In our eternal optimism, we had made the mistake of paying them for more milestones that they had actually delivered, hoping to keep the ball rolling.”","qa":"John Watson, co-owner and technical director of Stoic Studio","st":"Postmortem: Stoic Studio's The Banner Saga 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-stoic-studio-s-i-the-banner-saga-2-i-#:~:text=In%20our%20eternal%20optimism%2C%20we,the%20ball%20rolling","sd":"Jun 2016","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Reused the first game's Adobe AIR engine and tools for the sequel","s":"Once production started in earnest, the 4 full-time developers made the game in under a year. Old tool irritants stayed, and the aging AIR base made console ports harder.","w":"After The Banner Saga shipped, localization and porting took most of technical director John Watson's time for a year, while writer Drew McGee and Arnie Jorgensen ran a slow pre-production. Stoic built the sequel on the same engine and tools as the first game. With E3 2015 approaching, production started in earnest, and the team made the game in less than a year. The tools did not improve, and combat abilities were still written as raw JSON. The Adobe AIR base, which Watson calls slowly dying, pushed the console ports into a ScaleForm wrapper.","r":"Production took under a year, with the first game's tool problems unchanged.","re":"Wrapped the AIR game in ScaleForm to reach consoles.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When you reuse an engine for a sequel, list the tool fixes and platform risks you are carrying over, and budget time for them.","i":"Reuse lets a small team skip engine work and start on content at once, so the saving lands in the schedule. The debt carries over too: tools nobody had time to fix, and a runtime whose vendor had stopped pushing it forward.","a":"Which tool and platform problems are we carrying into the sequel?","q":"“If you slice off the year we spent working on localization, porting, and slow pre-production, we really made the entire game in less than a year using our existing tech.”","qa":"John Watson, co-owner and technical director of Stoic Studio","st":"Postmortem: Stoic Studio's The Banner Saga 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-stoic-studio-s-i-the-banner-saga-2-i-#:~:text=If%20you%20slice%20off%20the,using%20our%20existing%20tech","sd":"Jun 2016","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"bindingofisaac","n":"The Binding of Isaac","dv":"Edmund McMillen & Florian Himsl","y":"2011","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/113200/","ls":"Steam","cv":"bindingofisaac.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Made the next game a week-long jam with one programmer and no commercial plan","s":"About 3 months of part-time work produced a game that sold over 1 million copies on PC and Mac in its first year on Steam.","w":"Super Meat Boy had taken 2 years and all of McMillen's money. While Tommy Refenes was on vacation, McMillen started a week-long jam with Florian Himsl: a roguelike with the Legend of Zelda dungeon structure, about his relationship with religion. After about 3 months of part-time work it shipped on Steam on 28 September 2011 with 100 items and 5 characters, untested. Sales ran at 100 to 200 copies a day, then more than 1,500 a day by month 7. An unplanned expansion followed 6 months later, and in November 2012 McMillen reported over 1 million units in year one.","r":"A finished, released game after about 3 months of part-time work.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"3 months part-time, 2 people; about $40,000 of rating costs avoided on Steam.","nf":false,"l":"After a project that drains your money, make the next one small enough to fail without harm.","i":"With the money risk already spent on Super Meat Boy, McMillen could afford to take a design risk and fail, so the scope stayed at jam size. A small scope allows a risky theme; it does not guarantee a million sales.","a":"Can we afford for this game to fail?","q":"“From any mainstream marketing perspective, I designed Isaac to fail -- and that was my goal from the start.”","qa":"Edmund McMillen, designer and co-creator of The Binding of Isaac","st":"Postmortem: McMillen and Himsl's The Binding of Isaac (2011)","su":"https://gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-mcmillen-and-himsl-s-i-the-binding-of-isaac-i-#:~:text=From%20any%20mainstream%20marketing%20perspective,goal%20from%20the%20start","sd":"Nov 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the game in Flash with ActionScript 2 because that was what the programmer knew","s":"The game ran badly on lower-end PCs, had no controller support or Steam features, and the project file crashed once it passed 300MB.","w":"Isaac started as a week-long jam in 2011, and McMillen expected it to crash and burn, so the tools were whatever Florian Himsl could program in: Flash and ActionScript 2. The game shipped on Steam on 28 September 2011 and could not be made to run well on lower-end PCs, even after Himsl's work. Flash had no controller support and no integrated Steam features. As the game grew, the .FLA file rose above 300MB and the team could not consistently generate an .SWF without crashing. By November 2012 the game had sold over 1 million units despite these problems.","r":"Performance problems on lower-end PCs and a project file too big to build reliably.","re":"Himsl worked around the limits, but lower-end performance stayed poor.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you start, check the engine's hard limits on file size, input and platforms against the game you might end up with.","i":"A throwaway jam project picks tools by who is available, and that is fine until the game grows. Once Isaac was selling, every limit of Flash AS2 became a product problem, and the cheap start was still what made the game exist.","a":"What happens to our tech if this game succeeds?","q":"“Isaac was designed in Flash using ActionScript 2; that's what Florian could program in, so those were the limitations we had to work around.”","qa":"Edmund McMillen, designer and co-creator of The Binding of Isaac","st":"Postmortem: McMillen and Himsl's The Binding of Isaac (2011)","su":"https://gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-mcmillen-and-himsl-s-i-the-binding-of-isaac-i-#:~:text=Isaac%20was%20designed%20in%20Flash,had%20to%20work%20around","sd":"Nov 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"conduit","n":"The Conduit","dv":"High Voltage Software","y":"2009","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Put a large team on the first original IP after a short pre-production","s":"Artists and programmers started levels, characters and objects before designers had defined them. Some of that work was thrown away, and level designers had to build around assets that already existed.","w":"High Voltage had made over 75 licensed titles in 16 years when it self-funded The Conduit, its first major original IP, built for the Wii in 22 months by 30 full-time developers. Early on, content creators joined the team without clear direction or enough work. To show progress and keep them busy, the team started level, character and object work before designers had conceptualized it. Some of it became throw-away work, and art drove design. Sega released the game on June 23, 2009. The postmortem that August called pre-production too short.","r":"Some early level and asset work was thrown away; level design suffered.","re":"Executive management took the publisher role, with one decision-maker approving features.","co":"Throw-away level, character and object work.","nf":true,"l":"Add content creators only when designers have defined work ready for them to build.","i":"Idle artists create pressure to show progress, so assets get built before the design exists, and then levels must fit the assets. The risk is highest on a first original IP, where no licensor supplies style guides or scripts.","a":"Does everyone joining next month have designed work waiting for them?","q":"“THE CONDUIT's team was simply too large during early development to effectively manage.”","qa":"Josh Olson, producer on The Conduit, and Eric Nofsinger, chief creative officer of High Voltage Software","st":"Postmortem: High Voltage's The Conduit","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_August_2009.pdf#:~:text=THE%20CONDUIT%27s%20team%20was%20simply,development%20to%20effectively%20manage","sd":"Aug 2009","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4,"sh":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_August_2009.pdf#page=36"}]},{"k":"crossing","n":"The Crossing","dv":"Arkane Studios","y":"2009","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2009","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Kept the team on payroll while a publisher deal for The Crossing dragged on unsigned","s":"The contract shrank with every two-week round, the deal never closed, and Arkane lost about $1 million before putting the game on hold in 2009.","w":"Between 2006 and 2009 Arkane built The Crossing, a shooter fusing single player with multiplayer, while negotiating a publisher deal. The publisher talked only every week or two, and each round brought a smaller contract. Arkane kept everyone on, expecting to sign; Colantonio says it lost about a million dollars that way. Days before signing, the offer was far below the budget needed, so Arkane took Spielberg's LMNO at EA instead. The 2008-09 crisis killed LMNO; in May 2009 The Crossing went on hold. With 30 or 40 developers idle, Arkane survived on a Bethesda contract.","r":"The Crossing never signed and went on hold; about $1 million was lost.","re":"Took EA's LMNO as a lifeline, then contract work for Bethesda on Dishonored.","co":"About $1 million in unfunded payroll; 30 to 40 developers left without a project.","nf":false,"l":"Do not staff a production against an unsigned publisher deal; set a date when an unsigned contract triggers downsizing or a pivot.","i":"Each short negotiation round kept the deal looking days away, so the studio paid the full team month after month for a shrinking contract. The 2008 crisis hit at the same time, so the lost million was not the only cause of the 2009 squeeze.","a":"On what date do we downsize if the publisher still has not signed?","q":"“And so we lost about a million of our own dollars in this, in this adventure with The Crossing by sustaining the people.”","qa":"Raphaël Colantonio, founder and CEO of Arkane Studios","st":"The Untold History of Arkane: Dishonored / Prey / Ravenholm / LMNO / The Crossing","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h4kdqwdbZZ8&t=934","sd":"May 2020","sy":"Participant documentary","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h4kdqwdbZZ8&t=936s"}]},{"k":"curseofmonkeyisland","n":"The Curse of Monkey Island","dv":"LucasArts","y":"1997","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/730820/","ls":"Steam","cv":"curseofmonkeyisland.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Mapped every room, prop and puzzle step on paper before growing the team","s":"Art cost estimates killed expensive ideas, such as an alligator game, before anyone built them. The production manager said the team was better organized than almost any project she had worked on.","w":"Larry Ahern and Jonathan Ackley brainstormed the sequel from summer 1995 and won approval with a 15-page summary at Christmas. Over the next 2 months they wrote a story outline, a game outline, a prop list and a list of every room. Ackley and 2 writer-programmers built the bare game in SCUMM with programmer art within a month. Meanwhile Ahern priced the art on a legal pad, and costly pieces such as an alligator game were cut. Full production began 4 months in, and the team grew to 50. Planned for summer 1997, the game shipped that fall.","r":"Expensive ideas were cut on paper, before artists or programmers spent time on them.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"2 months of outlining and 1 month of skeleton programming before ramp-up.","nf":false,"l":"Before you hire up, list every room and prop, price the art for each, and cut what you cannot afford.","i":"An adventure game is built from rooms, props and puzzle steps, so a full list prices most of the game. The list did not cover the unknowns: the new character look and a new ink-and-paint pipeline still caused bottlenecks and final crunch.","a":"Do we have a priced list of every room and prop?","q":"“We had the entire game on paper before we started,”","qa":"Jonathan Ackley, programmer-writer and co-team leader at LucasArts","st":"Cruddy Sketches and a Red Pen: Pre-Production on The Curse of Monkey Island at LucasArts","su":"https://www.awn.com/animationworld/cruddy-sketches-and-red-pen-pre-production-curse-monkey-island-lucasarts#:~:text=We%20had%20the%20entire%20game,before%20we%20started","sd":"Mar 1998","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"elderscrollsivoblivion","n":"The Elder Scrolls IV: Oblivion","dv":"Bethesda Game Studios","y":"2006","sc":"AAA","se":"Remaster 2025","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/22330/","ls":"Steam","cv":"elderscrollsivoblivion.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Built the whole game world with a procedural generation system and baked SpeedTree assets","s":"The generated areas were not good enough. Artists went back over the whole world by hand, which took 2 years and forced the environment team to grow.","w":"After Morrowind, Bethesda planned a bigger, more detailed world for a console generation whose spec was unknown. The team baked out SpeedTree assets and generated each area with a procedural system. The output was not good enough, so artists reworked the whole generated world by hand over 2 years, and the environment team had to grow. Content was then trimmed to hit the Xbox 360 launch and fit the disc: arenas planned for every city were cut to one, 800 lines of dialogue with them. The team doubled in size, missed the launch by 4 months, and shipped in 2006.","r":"Two years of hand rework and a 4-month miss of the Xbox 360 launch.","re":"Artists reworked the world by hand and the team doubled to cope.","co":"2 years of hand rework; team doubled; launch missed by 4 months.","nf":false,"l":"Prove a generation pipeline to final quality on one region before you size the world and the team around it.","i":"Procedural output set the floor, and the floor was below the bar, so every area needed a hand pass anyway. Generation only saves time when its raw output is close to shippable; otherwise it adds a step.","a":"Has our generation pipeline produced one shippable-quality area yet?","q":"“So we generated each area of the game and made it and it turned out it wasn't so good.”","qa":"Matt Carofano, lead artist at Bethesda Game Studios (on-screen title: Lead Artist / The Elder Scrolls III: Morrowind)","st":"The History of Bethesda Game Studios","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QKn9yiLVlMM&t=1462s","sd":"Jun 2018","sy":"Participant documentary","w8":4}]},{"k":"incrediblehulk","n":"The Incredible Hulk (Nintendo DS)","dv":"The Fizz Factor","y":"2008","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built fully destructible tile levels with 3 damage states that no studio engine supported","s":"The engine work kept art and design off target hardware, so a solid build came late and the client grew impatient. Polish time shrank, and 2 extra artists joined from alpha.","w":"Producer Paul Benjamin joined The Fizz Factor in 2007 to make The Incredible Hulk for Nintendo DS for SEGA. The team chose fully destructible levels built from tiles with healthy, damaged and destroyed states. None of the studio's DS engines supported that, so 4 engineers reworked the tech while designers prototyped levels in GameMaker. Art and design could not work on target until the engine was ready, and the client saw a solid build late. Environment art took far longer than planned, so 2 more artists joined from alpha. The game still shipped on time.","r":"A solid on-target build came late, and polish time shrank.","re":"The studio stopped building new tech inside projects and now relies on proven engines.","co":"2 extra environment artists from alpha to the end; budget hit.","nf":false,"l":"Keep new engine tech off the critical path; if a feature needs it, build that tech before production starts.","i":"New tech blocks everyone downstream: artists and designers cannot work on target until it lands, and the publisher has nothing solid to see. The GameMaker prototypes kept design moving, but they could not show the client a real DS build.","a":"Does any feature on our critical path need tech we have not built?","q":"“The benefits of achieving this goal did not end up outweighing the costs.”","qa":"Paul Benjamin, producer at The Fizz Factor","st":"Postmortem: Fizz Factor's The Incredible Hulk","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-fizz-factor-s-the-incredible-hulk#:~:text=The%20benefits%20of%20achieving%20this,outweighing%20the%20costs","sd":"Oct 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"lastexpress","n":"The Last Express","dv":"Smoking Car Productions","y":"1997","sc":"Indie","se":"Remaster 2012","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/252710/","ls":"Steam","cv":"lastexpress.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Budgeted a filmed, rotoscoped adventure game at $1 million and two years","s":"The shoot alone cost $400K. The budget rose to $3.5 million, the team to about 30, the ship date slid from Christmas 1995 to March 1997, and the studio closed after release.","w":"In April 1993 Mechner planned to ship for Christmas 1995; by August he reckoned it would cost $1 million. By March 1994 he wrote 'I've created a monster', and in August the shoot alone was priced at $400K. The budget went to $2 million in December 1994, $2.2 million in January 1995 and $3.5 million in October 1995, when the team was 'thirty-odd people'. In July 1996 Smoking Car missed payroll; by October it had two hundred dollars in the bank and the ship had slipped to March. The game shipped in 1997 and the studio closed shortly after.","r":"Shipped about 15 months late at roughly 3.5 times the first budget.","re":"Refinanced repeatedly through Broderbund, a Softbank PlayStation deal and a private loan.","co":"Budget $1 million to $3.5 million; ship slipped Christmas 1995 to March 1997.","nf":false,"l":"Before you fix the budget, run your new pipeline end to end on one scene and price it.","i":"The $1 million was set before the pipeline had been run, so each step of the shoot raised the budget. Mechner's own diagnosis was size: he did not realize it would get this big, and a solo creator's estimate does not scale to 30 people.","a":"Have we priced one finished scene through our new pipeline yet?","q":"“We got too big. I tried to make it a business.”","qa":"Jordan Mechner, founder and director of Smoking Car Productions","st":"Jordan Mechner - The 1993 Journals: Year 4","su":"https://www.jordanmechner.com/en/library/1993-journals/year4/#:~:text=We%20got%20too%20big,it%20a%20business","sd":"Aug 1996","sy":"Participant journal","w8":5}]},{"k":"lastguardian","n":"The Last Guardian","dv":"SIE Japan Studio","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.playstation.com/en-us/games/the-last-guardian/","ls":"PlayStation","cv":"lastguardian.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Showed a sped-up PS3 trailer at E3 2009 before the game could hit frame rate","s":"The team spent years cutting features to reach PS3 frame rate and still failed. Sony moved the game to PS4, where it shipped in December 2016, seven years after the reveal.","w":"The Last Guardian debuted at E3 2009 on PS3. The trailer came from the development system but was taken frame by frame and made to run smoothly. Reaching the required frame rate on PS3 meant compromising features, including the number of characters Fumito Ueda wanted. In June 2015 Shuhei Yoshida said Sony failed and decided it could not be made on PS3, and that this technical issue made the project so long. The game moved to PS4, which Ueda called pretty much a corporate decision by Sony; concept, level design and story were kept. It released on 6 December 2016.","r":"Years lost trying to reach PS3 frame rate; game re-engineered for PS4.","re":"Sony moved the game to PS4 and Japan Studio re-engineered it with central tech help.","co":"7 years from the 2009 reveal to release; years of PS3 optimisation lost.","nf":false,"l":"Do not show a target that only runs when sped up; prove the core scene at frame rate on the launch hardware first.","i":"The trailer promised a density of characters the hardware never delivered, so every year of optimisation chased a bar set by a smoothed video. The design survived intact; the fix was a new platform generation, which only a first-party publisher can wait for.","a":"Does our demo run at target frame rate on real hardware?","q":"“The trailer that we showed at E3 2009 was speeded up.”","qa":"Shuhei Yoshida, head of Sony Worldwide Studios","st":"Sony: 'We failed,' technical issues on PS3 'compromised' The Last Guardian","su":"https://destructoid.com/?p=167080#:~:text=The%20trailer%20that%20we%20showed,was%20speeded%20up","sd":"Jun 2015","sy":"Press report","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Planned the giant AI creature game for PS3 as a project with few technical challenges","s":"On PS3 the game ran far below the frame rate of its 2009 trailer. In 2012 Sony moved it to PS4 and rebuilt much of the code, and it shipped in December 2016.","w":"After Shadow of the Colossus, Fumito Ueda's team began The Last Guardian while the PS3 market was still small. Ueda wanted fewer technical challenges than before and expected a huge creature beside a small boy to be cheap to build. Sony announced it at E3 2009 with a trailer specced up beyond the real build. In 2011 progress became super slow: low frame rate, missing features, cuts to features and characters, and slow SPU porting. In 2012 Sony moved it to PS4, and much SPU-optimised code was thrown away and rebuilt. It shipped in December 2016.","r":"The PS3 build could not reach the speed the trailer had shown.","re":"Sony moved the game to PS4 in 2012 and rebuilt much of the code.","co":"PS3 code thrown away in 2012; release over 7 years after the announcement.","nf":false,"l":"Before locking the platform, run the core creature or AI system at target frame rate on that hardware.","i":"The concept looked cheap on paper because the boy simply follows the creature. Ueda says the hard part was turning AI decisions into believable movement for a giant body, including in tight spaces. A design can be simple and still heavy at runtime.","a":"Does our core system already run at target frame rate on target hardware?","q":"“The game was running at a much lower frame rate.”","qa":"Shuhei Yoshida, head of game development at Sony","st":"What Happened To The Last Guardian","su":"https://kotaku.com/what-happened-to-the-last-guardian-1711835457#:~:text=The%20game%20was%20running%20at,a%20much%20lower%20frame%20rate","sd":"Jun 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"lastofus","n":"The Last of Us","dv":"Naughty Dog","y":"2013","sc":"AAA","se":"PC remake 2023","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1888930/","ls":"Steam","cv":"lastofus.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Story & world","t":"Wrote Ellie's arc so she would never shoot a human until the finale","s":"Early in production, capturing the first scene showed the plan would not work. The team rethought the whole story structure and made Ellie capable from the start.","w":"Naughty Dog spent about 3.5 years on The Last of Us before its June 2013 release. One early idea for Ellie's arc was that she would never shoot a non-Infected person until the very end, when she saved Joel. The team soon felt that was dishonest for someone living in that world, and the game was heading toward an escort mission. A little into production, capturing the first scene showed it would not work. In August 2013, creative director Neil Druckmann said the whole story structure had to be rethought, and Ellie became capable from the start.","r":"The story structure was rebuilt a little into production.","re":"The team rewrote both arcs so Ellie is capable from the start.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before capturing story scenes, test the companion's story role in a playable level and check it does not turn into an escort mission.","i":"A story rule about what a character will not do is also a gameplay rule, and its cost shows only in play. Naughty Dog caught it at the first captured scene, so the rewrite cost story arcs, not finished levels.","a":"Have we played our companion's story role in a level yet?","q":"“Now you have to rethink the entire structure of your story.”","qa":"Neil Druckmann, creative director at Naughty Dog","st":"Bonds Forged in Difficult Times: The Making of The Last of Us","su":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/bonds-forged-in-difficult-times-the-making-of-the-last-of-us/1100-6412499/#:~:text=Now%20you%20have%20to%20rethink,structure%20of%20your%20story","sd":"Aug 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"lastofusonline","n":"The Last of Us Online","dv":"Naughty Dog","y":"2023","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2023","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Cancelled The Last of Us Online while ramping up to full production","s":"Supporting the game after launch would have tied up the whole studio for years. Naughty Dog kept its single-player focus instead.","w":"The multiplayer team began pre-production while Naughty Dog was still making The Last of Us Part II. Over that time the concept became clearer and the gameplay more refined. While ramping up to full production, the studio saw the real scope. Releasing and supporting the game would need all its resources for post-launch content for years, and work on future single-player games would suffer. On December 14, 2023, Naughty Dog announced it had stopped development. It said the technology and lessons would carry into its other projects, including more than one new single-player game.","r":"Development stopped before the studio committed to years of live support.","re":"Naughty Dog carried the technology into its single-player projects.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before full production of a live-service game, cost the years after launch in people and decide if the studio wants that.","i":"Most of a live-service bill comes after launch, so a pre-production plan can hide it. Naughty Dog saw it only when ramping up to full production. A studio whose core business is live service would make the opposite call on the same numbers.","a":"Who staffs this game for the years after launch?","q":"“To release and support The Last of Us Online we’d have to put all our studio resources behind supporting post launch content for years to come, severely impacting development on future single-player games.”","qa":"Naughty Dog, studio statement","st":"An Update on The Last of Us Online","su":"https://www.naughtydog.com/blog/an_update_on_the_last_of_us_online#:~:text=To%20release%20and%20support%20The,on%20future%20single%2Dplayer%20games","sd":"Dec 2023","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"laststory","n":"The Last Story","dv":"Mistwalker","y":"2011","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/wii/slsj/product/index.html","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"laststory.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Prototyped for 1.5 years with square placeholder characters in a bare test dungeon","s":"About 10 times more systems were thrown away than shipped. Wall-climbing still arrived after the first dungeons were built, and all of them had to be rebuilt.","w":"Hironobu Sakaguchi started The Last Story with a prototype phase of about 1.5 years, against about 1 year on Final Fantasy VII. Early builds showed square characters walking around, and the team replayed a bare test dungeon after every system change. About 10 times more systems were discarded than shipped. Later the team added wall-climbing. The early dungeons did not support it, so all were rebuilt, and each new system had to be re-applied to all early terrain data. A replay system was built, then dropped for slowing combat. The game shipped in Japan on January 27, 2011.","r":"Every early dungeon was rebuilt after wall-climbing was added.","re":"The team rebuilt the dungeons and re-applied new systems to all terrain.","co":"About 1.5 years of prototyping; all early dungeons rebuilt.","nf":false,"l":"Before building final dungeons, list the movement verbs the prototype has not tried yet and decide on each one.","i":"A test dungeon proves combat systems, but level geometry depends on how the player moves. A movement verb added later breaks every level already built. A long prototype does not protect you if it never tested traversal.","a":"Is every movement verb in the prototype before we build levels?","q":"“最初の頃につくったダンジョンは対応していなかったから、全部つくり直しだったんですよね。” (The dungeons we made early on did not support it, so we had to rebuild all of them.)","qa":"Hironobu Sakaguchi, director at Mistwalker","st":"社長が訊く『ラストストーリー』","su":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/wii/interview/slsj/vol3/index2.html#:~:text=%E6%9C%80%E5%88%9D%E3%81%AE%E9%A0%83%E3%81%AB%E3%81%A4%E3%81%8F%E3%81%A3%E3%81%9F%E3%83%80%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B8%E3%83%A7%E3%83%B3%E3%81%AF,%E5%85%A8%E9%83%A8%E3%81%A4%E3%81%8F%E3%82%8A%E7%9B%B4%E3%81%97%E3%81%A0%E3%81%A3%E3%81%9F%E3%82%93%E3%81%A7%E3%81%99%E3%82%88%E3%81%AD","sd":"","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"legendofzeldaalinkbetweenworlds","n":"The Legend of Zelda: A Link Between Worlds","dv":"Nintendo EAD","y":"2013","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/3ds/bzlj/detail/index.html","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"legendofzeldaalinkbetweenworlds.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Staffing","t":"Restarted the paused 3DS Zelda with stand-in staff while the core team was away","s":"The stand-in team kept the concept moving until Miyamoto approved it in 2012. The game made its year-end 2013 window in Japan.","w":"After Spirit Tracks shipped in late 2009, 3 people spent about a year looking for a new handheld Zelda. Miyamoto approved a one-day prototype of Link merging into walls. Within half a month, in October 2010, the whole core team moved to Wii U launch titles. In November 2011 producer Eiji Aonuma restarted the project with a programmer from another team and planner Kentaro Tominaga. Miyamoto rejected their pitch of 50 wall dungeons in May 2012, then approved a version on A Link to the Past's terrain two months later. The game shipped in Japan on December 26, 2013.","r":"The concept was approved in 2012, in time for a year-end 2013 release.","re":"Director Shikata and programmer Mouri rejoined about a year after the restart.","co":"A stand-in team for about 1 year until the directors returned.","nf":false,"l":"If the core team gets pulled away, keep a small stand-in team iterating on the prototype so the release window survives.","i":"Aonuma counted back from the date: starting when the core team returned would miss end-2013. A small team kept concept work moving. This works when a prototype has already proven the hook; it does not rescue an unproven concept.","a":"If our core team is reassigned, who keeps the prototype moving?","q":"“でも、そうしないと、彼らが戻ってくる頃につくりはじめたのでは、２０１３年の年末には出せないですから。” (But if we hadn't, and had started making it when they came back, we couldn't have released it at the end of 2013.)","qa":"Eiji Aonuma, producer at Nintendo","st":"社長が訊く『ゼルダの伝説 神々のトライフォース２』","su":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/3ds/interview/bzlj/vol1/index3.html#:~:text=%E3%81%A7%E3%82%82%E3%80%81%E3%81%9D%E3%81%86%E3%81%97%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84%E3%81%A8,%E5%B9%B4%E6%9C%AB%E3%81%AB%E3%81%AF%E5%87%BA%E3%81%9B%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84%E3%81%A7%E3%81%99%E3%81%8B%E3%82%89","sd":"2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"legendofzeldabreathofthewild","n":"The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild","dv":"Nintendo EPD","y":"2017","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.com/jp/zelda/botw/index.html","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"legendofzeldabreathofthewild.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Pitched the open-world Zelda with a 2D NES-style prototype of its multiplying systems","s":"The prototype showed that trees, logs, wind and fire reacting to each other could mass-produce puzzles, so the team built the world on those rules instead of hand-made puzzle objects. The game launched 3 March 2017.","w":"Game director Hidemaro Fujibayashi wanted a Zelda where the player truly experiences freedom. Earlier Zeldas had a fixed sequence of events and impassable walls, and their puzzle objects were built for one puzzle each, which took a lot of resource. His idea was multiplicative gameplay: mechanics that scale across the world, with objects reacting to the player and to each other. To pitch it he built a 2D prototype styled on the NES Zelda, running on a 3D engine: a river, some trees, no puzzles, one rule, reach the goal. The full game shipped on 3 March 2017.","r":"Puzzles came from interacting systems, not from hand-built objects.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before building the world, prove your core systems interact in a throwaway 2D sandbox and pitch with that, not with a document.","i":"A systems-driven design only proves itself when the systems run together, and a tiny 2D build shows that long before the 3D world exists. It fits games whose fun is in rules that multiply; a content-driven game needs a different test.","a":"Can we show our core systems interacting in a 2D sandbox this month?","q":"“This prototype does not include any puzzles.”","qa":"Hidemaro Fujibayashi, game director at Nintendo","st":"Breaking series conventions with Breath of the Wild","su":"https://www.thumbsticks.com/gdc-17-breaking-conventions-breath-of-the-wild#:~:text=This%20prototype%20does%20not%20include,any%20puzzles","sd":"Mar 2017","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built a rough whole-world prototype first, allowing only core features and reused assets","s":"Playtests in that first pass caught a world structure that did not work. The team corrected it before it became a big problem.","w":"Breath of the Wild took about 4 years and 300 people. Wind Waker had been built by finishing one model stage and copying its quality. For an open world, the team built a rough skeleton of the whole world first. Lap 1 took about 1.5 years and banned anything outside the core game; villages, NPCs and the final boss used past-game data. Monitor play then rejected splitting the world into areas built the familiar way. Laps 2 and 3 took just over a year each. It shipped on March 3, 2017, and two leads described the method at CEDEC 2017.","r":"A failed world structure was caught and fixed during the prototype lap.","re":"The team changed its world-building approach during lap 1.","co":"About 1.5 years for lap 1, in a 4-year, 300-person project.","nf":false,"l":"In the first pass, build only the core loop across the whole world with placeholder or reused assets, then playtest it.","i":"An open world works or fails as a whole, so one finished stage cannot test it. Reused assets let the team play the full loop early. For stage-based Wind Waker, the same studio found finishing one model stage first was the right fit.","a":"Can we play the core loop across the whole map yet?","q":"“もちろん、見直しはないに越したことがないが、大事になる前に軌道修正ができたのは、1周目の取り組みの大きな成果” (Of course it is best to have no rework, but being able to correct course before it became serious was a major result of the first lap.)","qa":"Yuichiro Okamura, systems architect at Nintendo","st":"『ゼルダの伝説　BotW』アーティストのクリエイティブな作業を支えたエンジニアの仕事と制作フロー【CEDEC 2017】","su":"https://www.famitsu.com/news/201709/03141030.html#:~:text=%E3%82%82%E3%81%A1%E3%82%8D%E3%82%93%E3%80%81%E8%A6%8B%E7%9B%B4%E3%81%97%E3%81%AF%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84%E3%81%AB%E8%B6%8A%E3%81%97%E3%81%9F%E3%81%93%E3%81%A8%E3%81%8C%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84%E3%81%8C,1%E5%91%A8%E7%9B%AE%E3%81%AE%E5%8F%96%E3%82%8A%E7%B5%84%E3%81%BF%E3%81%AE%E5%A4%A7%E3%81%8D%E3%81%AA%E6%88%90%E6%9E%9C","sd":"Sep 2017","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4}]},{"k":"legendofzeldaechoesofwisdom","n":"The Legend of Zelda: Echoes of Wisdom","dv":"Grezzo","y":"2024","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/the-legend-of-zelda-echoes-of-wisdom-switch/","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"legendofzeldaechoesofwisdom.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Spent a year prototyping a dungeon editor before turning copy and paste into echoes","s":"Producer Eiji Aonuma changed direction after the year because the prototype had shown that copying objects felt fun. The team kept the mechanic, switched from user-made dungeons to a single-player adventure, and shipped on 26 September 2024.","w":"Nintendo first asked every Grezzo employee to pitch ideas for a new Zelda; reviewing them took 3 days. One approach, the edit dungeon, let Link copy and paste doors and candlesticks to build dungeons. Grezzo prototyped it for about a year after the Link's Awakening remake. When Aonuma played it, he proposed using the copied objects as tools to advance the adventure; director Tomomi Sano called it upending the tea table after a year. Aonuma said he had wanted the team to build it first, since potential only shows once the feel is verified. It shipped in September 2024.","r":"The year's prototype became the core mechanic of a shipped single-player adventure.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About 1 year of edit dungeon prototyping before the change of direction.","nf":false,"l":"Prove the feel of your core tool in a playable prototype before you decide what game it belongs to.","i":"A verified feel is portable: once copying objects was fun in hand, the team could change the game around it without losing the year. That holds while the prototype is small and the producer can redirect it; a year of content would not move cheaply.","a":"Has our core mechanic been proven fun before we fixed the genre around it?","q":"“After all, you can't really see the potential for ideas to develop into solid gameplay until you can verify features and their feel, so I wanted them to try making it first.”","qa":"Eiji Aonuma, producer of the Legend of Zelda series, Nintendo","st":"Ask the Developer, The Legend of Zelda: Echoes of Wisdom, Chapter 1: Twice as satisfying","su":"https://www.nintendo.com/sg/interview/bdge/index.html#:~:text=After%20all%2C%20you%20can%27t%20really,try%20making%20it%20first","sd":"Sep 2024","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"legendofzeldamajorasmask","n":"The Legend of Zelda: Majora's Mask","dv":"Nintendo EAD","y":"2000","sc":"AAA","se":"Remake 2015","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Accepted a 1-year deadline for a new Zelda built on Ocarina of Time's models","s":"The deadline shaped the design. A planned week-long time loop shrank to 3 repeating days, and the game shipped in Japan in April 2000.","w":"Ocarina of Time shipped in Japan on November 21, 1998, after 3 years of work. Eiji Aonuma was then asked to rework its dungeons for Master Quest. He secretly built new dungeons instead and asked to make a new game. Shigeru Miyamoto agreed, as long as it was made in a year by reusing Ocarina's 3D models. Yoshiaki Koizumi joined with an idea for a small world played over and over. The loop started at one week and was cut to 3 days, with a week of planned content packed in. Majora's Mask shipped in Japan in April 2000.","r":"A planned week of content was packed into a 3-day loop.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Built to a 1-year target, against 3 years for Ocarina of Time.","nf":false,"l":"For a sequel, set the time box and the list of reused assets at greenlight, then size the design to fit them.","i":"Reused models meant the year could go into new design instead of new assets. The deadline also pushed the team toward a smaller, denser structure. This works when a finished base game exists; a time box without reusable assets only cuts content.","a":"Does our design fit the time box once we list what we reuse?","q":"“He replied by saying it was okay, as long as I could make it in a year.”","qa":"Eiji Aonuma, co-director of Majora's Mask at Nintendo","st":"Iwata Asks | 1. Make it in a Year | Iwata Asks: The Legend of Zelda: Majora’s Mask 3D","su":"https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Iwata-Asks/Iwata-Asks-The-Legend-of-Zelda-Majora-s-Mask-3D/The-Legend-of-Zelda-Majora-s-Mask-3D/1-Make-it-in-a-Year/1-Make-it-in-a-Year-959667.html#:~:text=He%20replied%20by%20saying%20it,could%20make%20it%20in%20a%20year","sd":"2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"legendofzeldaskywardsword","n":"The Legend of Zelda: Skyward Sword","dv":"Nintendo EAD","y":"2011","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.co.jp/wii/souj/index.html","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"legendofzeldaskywardsword.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Dropped Wii MotionPlus for button controls, then reversed after other producers pushed back","s":"Button-based combat basics were already built and had to be set aside. The combat team restarted on MotionPlus with no know-how, and Aonuma called it a huge detour.","w":"Planning began after Twilight Princess shipped in 2006. Fujibayashi pitched a Zelda built around the new Wii MotionPlus and became director. For about half a year the experiments went badly. Producer Aonuma judged that sword, Clawshot, bow and bombs on one field were too hard to make work, and proposed dropping MotionPlus. The team built button-based combat basics and was about to add variations. Other producers then asked why Zelda was not using MotionPlus, and Aonuma reversed course. The combat team started again with no know-how. The game shipped in Japan in November 2011.","r":"Button combat basics were set aside and combat work restarted from scratch.","re":"Aonuma gathered all staff to work out how to make MotionPlus work.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you build combat on a control scheme, get sign-off on the input device from everyone who can overrule it.","i":"Combat is the first system built on the controls, so a late input change throws it away first. The push came from producers outside the team. Aonuma said the detours still let the team pack in more play than earlier Zelda games.","a":"Has everyone who can overrule our control scheme signed off on it?","q":"“We had already made something basic using button controls to fight, and we had taken development to where we were going to start cranking out a bunch of variations.”","qa":"Kobayashi, design director of Skyward Sword at Nintendo","st":"Iwata Asks: The Legend of Zelda: Skyward Sword, Volume One: Wii MotionPlus Inspires New Controls","su":"https://iwataasks.nintendo.com/interviews/wii/zelda-skyward-sword/0/0/#:~:text=We%20had%20already%20made%20something%20basic,a%20bunch%20of%20variations","sd":"2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"legendofzeldatwilightprincess","n":"The Legend of Zelda: Twilight Princess","dv":"Nintendo","y":"2006","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Began Twilight Princess with the director and staff picturing different sizes of game","s":"The world grew past what Aonuma could rein in, and its size caused trouble to the end. Iwata delayed release by a year so the material could be tied together.","w":"Aonuma set out to make the first realistic Zelda since Ocarina of Time, with Link turning into a wolf. He did not expect the scale it reached, but the staff wanted a very big Zelda. A movie shown at E3 in 2004 set the direction. Mid-development Aonuma tried to rein the project in and could not, and Miyamoto said modelling kept expanding the world while content fell behind. Iwata saw the playable E3 2005 demo as great pieces in disarray and proposed a 1-year delay. The game came out in Japan on 2 December 2006.","r":"The size caused trouble to the end, and release slipped a year.","re":"Iwata delayed release a year; Miyamoto and others helped tie it together.","co":"A 1-year delay; extra internal and temporary artists for the art volume.","nf":false,"l":"Before production starts, get the director and leads to agree on the world's size and the content each area must hold.","i":"When leads build toward a bigger game than the director pictures, the world grows faster than the content that fills it, and a grown world is hard to shrink. Iwata also wanted the best Zelda ever, so size alone does not explain the year.","a":"Do our director and leads picture the same size of game?","q":"“At the very least, at the start I didn't envisage the project expanding to the scale it eventually did.”","qa":"Eiji Aonuma, director of Twilight Princess at Nintendo","st":"Iwata Asks: The Legend of Zelda: Twilight Princess, 5. Make it 120% Zelda!","su":"https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Iwata-Asks/Iwata-Asks-Wii/Iwata-Asks-The-Legend-of-Zelda-Twilight-Princess/5-Make-it-120-Zelda-/5-Make-it-120-Zelda--227573.html#:~:text=At%20the%20very%20least%2C%20at,the%20scale%20it%20eventually%20did","sd":"2006","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"lordoftheringsgollum","n":"The Lord of the Rings: Gollum","dv":"Daedalic Entertainment","y":"2023","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1265780/","ls":"Steam","cv":"lordoftheringsgollum.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Built a Lord of the Rings game on a tenth of a AAA budget","s":"Daedalic says the budget ended at about EUR 15M under a time-limited licence, with no public funding or co-investors. The game launched to a Metascore of 38, and internal development closed a month later.","w":"Daedalic's 3 applications for German games funding were all rejected. Its parent Bastei Lübbe said mid-development that it saw no future for the studio, so no co-investors came until Nacon bought Daedalic in 2022. The budget ended at about EUR 15M, a tenth of a AAA game by Daedalic's measure, and the licence had a time limit. Gollum shipped on 25 May 2023 with a Metascore of 38. In June 2023 Daedalic closed internal development and halted a second Lord of the Rings game. In Oct 2023 a report from 30+ former staff drew a written reply.","r":"Metascore 38, internal development closed, a second Tolkien game halted.","re":"Closed internal development in June 2023 and became a publisher only.","co":"About EUR 15M budget; 25 of 90+ staff affected; follow-up game halted.","nf":false,"l":"Price the licence against the budget before you sign: if the IP demands AAA and you have a tenth, change the concept or decline.","i":"A big licence sets player expectations the budget has to meet, and a time limit on the licence removes the option of waiting for more money. Daedalic's account blames money and time; the 30 former staff in the report also point at management and planning.","a":"Does our budget match what players expect from this licence?","q":"“Gollum hatte am Ende ein Entwicklungsbudget von ca. 15 Millionen Euro – also nur ein Zehntel eines Triple-A-Spiels.”","qa":"Daedalic Entertainment, written company statement","st":"Gollum-Reportage: Daedalic reagiert auf Game Two-Recherchen","su":"https://www.gameswirtschaft.de/wirtschaft/gollum-reportage-game-two-daedalic-reaktion/#:~:text=Gollum%20hatte%20am%20Ende%20ein,Zehntel%20eines%20Triple-A-Spiels","sd":"Oct 2023","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"movies","n":"The Movies","dv":"Lionhead","y":"2005","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Expanded Lionhead fast to prepare for a stock market flotation","s":"The float was called off, but the bigger studio stayed. With a burn of over $1 million a month, Lionhead rushed The Movies out alongside two other games for holiday 2005.","w":"After Black & White sold 2.5 million copies in 2001, a City contact told Peter Molyneux that Lionhead could float at half a billion pounds. In 2002 and 2003 the studio grew by 2 or 3 people a week, added a board, and favoured ideas bankers understood, such as The Movies. About 48 hours before the final go or no-go meeting, the bankers called the float off. Lionhead signed The Movies to Activision in March 2003. By late 2004 it had about 220 people, and three teams then rushed to ship within weeks of each other for holiday 2005.","r":"Lionhead needed hit games to cover over $1 million a month.","re":"Lionhead raised venture capital in 2004, then sold to Microsoft in 2006.","co":"Burn over $1 million a month; about 50 layoffs in early 2006.","nf":false,"l":"Before you hire for a funding event, check that the studio can pay its burn if the money never arrives.","i":"Staff hired for a float stay after the float dies, and their burn must come from game sales. Here that pushed three games into one holiday window. The talk does not weigh this against other causes of The Movies' reception.","a":"Can we pay this team if the funding falls through?","q":"“I feel that we rushed them.”","qa":"Peter Molyneux, co-founder of Lionhead Studios","st":"The Secret History of Lionhead: Molyneux, Webley Get Honest","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/the-secret-history-of-lionhead-molyneux-webley-get-honest#:~:text=I%20feel%20that%20we%20rushed%20them","sd":"Oct 2006","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"order1886","n":"The Order: 1886","dv":"Ready at Dawn","y":"2015","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.playstation.com/en-us/games/the-order-1886/","ls":"PlayStation","cv":"order1886.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Set the highest visual target first and planned to work out the technology later","s":"Rendering, materials and simulation became research projects lasting a couple of years, with about 3 years on physics alone. The tech foundation outgrew the game built on it, and many ideas were cut.","w":"Ready at Dawn began The Order: 1886 in 2010 with nothing in hand. CTO Andrea Pessino says the studio told its graphics department to make the game look the best it could and to work out how afterwards. The team went with physically based lighting, complex materials and 3D scanning, in a feedback loop with Sony on the PS4. Rendering, materials, shading and simulation each became research projects of a couple of years; the physics engine took about 3. In February 2015, the week of launch, Pessino said many darlings were killed to fit a finite schedule.","r":"The tech foundation could support far more game than shipped.","re":"The team killed many darlings to fit a finite schedule.","co":"About 3 years on the physics engine; other research ran a couple of years.","nf":false,"l":"Before art production starts, price the research each visual target needs and drop the targets your schedule cannot pay for.","i":"Each open-ended visual goal became a research project with no fixed end, and research years came out of game-building years. Pessino calls that kind of pure research a luxury; it needs a backer willing to fund years of it.","a":"Which of our visual targets still need research before we can build them?","q":"“Especially through something like this because...you know it's kind of like you build this humongous foundation and then you build a little house on top of it.”","qa":"Andrea Pessino, CTO and co-founder of Ready at Dawn","st":"Final Word - The Order: 1886 Interview","su":"https://www.gamereactor.eu/final-word-the-order-1886-interview/#:~:text=Especially%20through%20something%20like%20this,little%20house%20on%20top%20of%20it","sd":"Feb 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"path","n":"The Path","dv":"Tale of Tales","y":"2009","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/27000/","ls":"Steam","cv":"path.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Funding & budget","t":"Designed The Path to fit a budget that arts grants could mostly cover","s":"The 2-person studio finished and self-published the game. Sales did not cover the €300,000 budget, but they repaid the loan and grants covered the rest, so the accounts stayed in the black.","w":"After publishers rejected 8, a game that needed $1.5 million, Tale of Tales sought arts funding for its next project in 2005. Creative Capital gave a first $10,000 grant that year, and Flemish funds added €26,000 in 2006. The 2006 prototype reused a character, an AI engine and a renderer from earlier work, and the game was planned as short and download-only. A €90,000 loan followed in November 2007. The Path launched on March 18, 2009. A year later sales had returned about €135,000 of a €300,000 budget. The loan was repaid, and grants covered the rest.","r":"The game shipped and broke even, with grants covering what sales did not.","re":"Tale of Tales repaid the loan from sales within half a year.","co":"€300,000 budget; sales returned about €135,000 in the first year.","nf":false,"l":"Keep the money you must pay back below what modest sales can return, and design the game to that budget.","i":"Grants need no repayment, so modest sales of almost 50,000 copies cleared the only money that had to come back. The model needs a project that qualifies for arts funding, and the authors say it did not prove such games commercially viable.","a":"Can modest sales repay all the money we must pay back?","q":"“But had we not designed the production to fit into a small budget, it is safe to say that The Path would have followed 8 to the Land of Unfinished Dreams.”","qa":"Auriea Harvey and Michaël Samyn, designers of The Path at Tale of Tales","st":"Postmortem: Tale of Tales' The Path (2009)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-tale-of-tales-i-the-path-i-#:~:text=But%20had%20we%20not%20designed,the%20Land%20of%20Unfinished%20Dreams","sd":"Jul 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built all of The Path's programming in Quest3D's visual flowcharts","s":"A team of artists with no engineers could build and tweak the game in real time, but Quest3D only exported to Windows, so the Mac version came 2 months after launch.","w":"Tale of Tales modeled The Path in Blender and did all of its programming in Quest3D. There you connect flowcharts instead of writing code, and the game changes in real time. Auriea Harvey could paint flora and walk the character through the forest without stopping to compile. The tool's biggest downside was that it only output Windows applications. After the PC launch on March 18, 2009, Transgaming ported it with Cider, helped by the Quest3D team and the studio's own changes. The Mac version arrived in May 2009. Mac sales fell short, partly because that version missed the launch excitement.","r":"Artists built the game alone; the Mac version followed 2 months after PC.","re":"Transgaming ported the game to Mac with Cider, with help from Quest3D staff.","co":"Mac version 2 months after the PC launch, through an outside porting partner.","nf":false,"l":"Before you pick a tool for its ease of use, check that it can export to every platform you plan to launch on.","i":"A single-platform tool turns every extra platform into a separate port. Here the port missed the launch spike, and the first 6 weeks of sales made up a quarter of the year's total.","a":"Can our tools export to every launch platform today?","q":"“The biggest downside to Quest3D is that it can only output applications for Windows.”","qa":"Auriea Harvey and Michaël Samyn, designers of The Path at Tale of Tales","st":"Postmortem: Tale of Tales' The Path (2009)","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-tale-of-tales-i-the-path-i-#:~:text=The%20biggest%20downside%20to%20Quest3D,output%20applications%20for%20Windows","sd":"Jul 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"room","n":"The Room","dv":"Fireproof Games","y":"2012","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/288160/","ls":"Steam","cv":"room.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Budgeted 3 months to test 3 ideas, then committed after the second","s":"The third prototype was never built, and the rest of the coding time went into Puzzle Box. It became The Room, made for less than £80,000 and earned back within a week.","w":"After 4 years of art outsourcing, Fireproof had saved enough to pay a programmer for just 6 months. The team planned to test 3 ideas in Unity in 3 months, then finish the best one. Tiny Planets came first; the second, Puzzle Box, showed potential even in bare form. The team skipped the third prototype and put the remaining coding time into Puzzle Box. The Room launched on 18 September 2012, cost less than £80,000 and earned that back within a week. By June 2013 it had over 2 million paid sales.","r":"The game cost less than £80,000, about 6 months of wages.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"Less than £80,000 in total, basically 6 months of wages.","nf":false,"l":"Before prototyping, fix how much runway the tests may use, and put the rest into the first idea that shows real promise.","i":"With a fixed runway, every month spent on another prototype is a month taken from the game. The shortcut worked because Puzzle Box showed promise in bare form, and 10 artists could polish a small game. A weak second prototype gives no such shortcut.","a":"Will we stop prototyping as soon as one idea shows real promise?","q":"“We had very little money, a tiny games team and a room full of artists next door.”","qa":"Barry Meade, commercial director of Fireproof Games","st":"Thinking outside the box: The making of The Room","su":"https://www.pocketgamer.biz/thinking-outside-the-box-the-making-of-the-room/#:~:text=We%20had%20very%20little%20money,room%20full%20of%20artists%20next%20door","sd":"Jun 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"talosprinciple","n":"The Talos Principle","dv":"Croteam","y":"2014","sc":"AA","se":"Port 2015","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/257510/","ls":"Steam","cv":"talosprinciple.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Turned Serious Sam 4 puzzle prototypes into a separate puzzle game","s":"Croteam built The Talos Principle on puzzles its own team already loved. It took about 2 years with about 25 people and shipped in December 2014.","w":"Croteam started Serious Sam 4 in October 2012. To break up the action, the team prototyped jammer puzzles, and they grew more complex than an action game could hold. Team members liked the hard ones best. After long discussion Croteam changed focus, and in mid 2013 it agreed with Devolver Digital to publish the puzzles as a separate game. About 20 team members played some 200 graybox puzzles, rated each for difficulty and fun, and timed them to set the order. The whole game took about 2 years. It shipped in December 2014.","r":"The puzzles were proven in graybox, so the rest went into content and story.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"About 2 years with about 25 people.","nf":false,"l":"Track which prototypes playtesters ask to replay, and price a game built on the favourite.","i":"The puzzles had already passed the hardest test: players wanted more of them. Croteam owned its engine and had a working visual pipeline, so the new genre added little tech risk. A studio without that base pays more for the same switch.","a":"Which of our prototypes do testers ask to play again?","q":"“So the final result was that after a lot of deliberation and discussions we decided to just change our focus and create a new game out of that.”","qa":"Alen Ladavac, CTO of Croteam","st":"NG16: Making Big Games with Small Teams: The Talos Principle Post-Mortem","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D0ErxdacTT8&t=412s","sd":"May 2016","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D0ErxdacTT8&t=390s"}]},{"k":"witcher","n":"The Witcher","dv":"CD Projekt RED","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"Expanded edition 2008","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/20900/","ls":"Steam","cv":"witcher.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Licensed BioWare's Aurora engine in 2003 and modified most of it for real-time combat","s":"Production only started in earnest after the licence, and the team grew from 4 or 5 people to over 40. By mid-2007, over 70% of the engine had been changed.","w":"CD Projekt spent its first year or two on The Witcher writing the script and building prototypes on its own technology, with 4 or 5 people. In 2003 it licensed BioWare's Aurora engine, which ran Neverwinter Nights, and only then started real work. The team grew to about a dozen, and to over 40 by August 2005. Real-time combat and other features needed engine changes. In May 2007, chief designer Michał Madej said over 70% of the engine had been modified. The game shipped in October 2007. After release, Madej said the inherited engine and controls limited free exploration.","r":"Over 70% of the licensed engine was modified for the game.","re":"The team built a new all-in-one editor, D’jinni, with live preview.","co":"Over 70% of the engine modified: rendering, game mechanics and toolset.","nf":false,"l":"Before you license an engine, list the core features its original game lacks, and budget the engine work for each one.","i":"The licence gave the team a working base and let production start, but Aurora was built for a different game. Every feature it lacked had to be built into it. The saving shrinks as the design moves away from the engine's original game.","a":"Which of our core features does this engine's original game not have?","q":"“Step by step, we have modified over 70 percent of the engine, mainly on the rendering side (which makes use of DirectX 9), game mechanics (combat, character development, and so on), as well as the toolset, which we call D’jinni.”","qa":"Michał Madej, chief designer of The Witcher at CD Projekt RED","st":"The Witcher Q&A – Making a Fantasy Role-Playing Game for Grown-Ups","su":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/the-witcher-qanda-making-a-fantasy-role-playing-game-for-grown-ups/1100-6171783/#:~:text=Step%20by%20step%2C%20we%20have,which%20we%20call%20D%E2%80%99jinni","sd":"May 2007","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"witcher2assassinsofkings","n":"The Witcher 2: Assassins of Kings","dv":"CD Projekt RED","y":"2011","sc":"AA","se":"Port 2012","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/20920/","ls":"Steam","cv":"witcher2assassinsofkings.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Designed the new tech's story tools with designers, talking daily from the start","s":"Designers controlled dialogue and quest features from the beginning. Quest implementers who knew basic scripting could write logic blocks for writers without going to programmers.","w":"CD Projekt RED knew it needed new technology on the day the first Witcher shipped in 2007. For a year to a year and a half, programmers wrote the low-level code and the framework for the tools. They talked with designers every day about features such as the dialogue editor, free cameras and graphical control of gestures. The quest implementation team learned basic scripting, so it could write logic blocks for writers without asking programmers. The Witcher 2 released in May 2011 with dozens of storyline scripting tools. That week, senior producer Tomasz Gop said the approach worked.","r":"Writers got new story functions without waiting on programmers.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"1 to 1.5 years of programmer work on low-level code and tools.","nf":false,"l":"Have programmers agree each story tool's design with the designers who will use it, while the engine is still being written.","i":"The main reason for the new tech was the story tools, so designers set the features. A team on a licensed engine pays for this in custom tools instead; the daily designer and programmer talks still apply.","a":"Have our designers agreed how each story tool should work?","q":"“So, with writing the tech from the very beginning, it was possible to get the idea of how we can do it and make it independent for the designers, and it worked.”","qa":"Tomasz Gop, senior producer at CD Projekt RED","st":"The Complicated Workings of The Witcher 2","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/the-complicated-workings-of-i-the-witcher-2-i-#:~:text=So%2C%20with%20writing%20the%20tech,designers%2C%20and%20it%20worked","sd":"May 2011","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built its own engine for The Witcher 2, with a console game planned after PC","s":"After the first game's outsourced console port failed, the studio released the Xbox 360 edition on its own engine in April 2012. It sold 667,000 copies by the end of that year.","w":"The Witcher 1 ran on BioWare's Aurora Engine, with about 80% of the code rewritten. Its outsourced console port, Rise of the White Wolf, failed, and the company owed Atari a couple of million dollars. For The Witcher 2 the studio built its own engine with a clear goal: a console game after the PC release. The Xbox 360 Enhanced Edition launched on 17 April 2012, the company's debut on that console, and sold 667,000 copies by year end. In 2017 a developer told Noclip they had failed with White Wolf and won with The Witcher 2.","r":"The Xbox 360 version shipped in 2012, and REDengine was named the studio's main engine.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Choose the engine against the full platform plan at concept stage, so the console version is built in, not ported later.","i":"A licensed engine rewritten by 80% is neither portable nor fully yours, so the console port had to be remade by an outside team and failed. Owning the engine let the studio ship the Xbox 360 version on its own technology.","a":"Does our engine choice cover every platform we plan to ship on?","q":"“We failed with the Rise of the White Wolf, but we won with The Witcher 2.”","qa":"CD Projekt RED developer interviewed by Noclip (name not shown in captions)","st":"The Making of The Witcher 1 & 2","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WOYKIxIjLs0&t=1070s","sd":"Oct 2017","sy":"Participant documentary","w8":4}]},{"k":"witcherriseofthewhitewolf","n":"The Witcher: Rise of the White Wolf","dv":"CD Projekt RED","y":"2009","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2009","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Outsourced a full console rebuild of The Witcher to a studio with its own engine","s":"Widescreen Games had to rewrite the game from scratch on its own tech. The project froze in April 2009 and left CD Projekt owing Atari money as its distribution business collapsed.","w":"The Witcher ran on BioWare's Aurora engine, which CD Projekt could not carry to consoles. After trying the conversion itself, the company handed the whole rebuild to Widescreen Games, a French studio that had just lost a cancelled title and needed work. Atari and CD Projekt announced the console version in December 2008. In April 2009 Widescreen froze development over unpaid milestones, which CD Projekt blamed on missed deadlines. The financial crisis hit CD Projekt's distribution business at the same time. In 2017 co-founder Marcin Iwiński said the plan was a bad idea from the start.","r":"Console version cancelled; CD Projekt owed Atari a couple of million dollars.","re":"Built its own engine for The Witcher 2 with consoles planned from the start.","co":"A couple of million dollars owed to Atari; staff cut from 300+ to under 200.","nf":false,"l":"If a port needs a rewrite on another engine, treat it as a new game with its own pre-production and budget.","i":"The engine could not move, so the port was really a second full production, done by a partner that took the job out of need rather than fit. A port that reuses engine and content is a different, cheaper job.","a":"Can our port partner reuse our engine and content, or must they rebuild?","q":"“It was like writing Witcher 1 from scratch.”","qa":"Marcin Iwiński, co-founder of CD Projekt","st":"The Making of The Witcher 1 & 2","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WOYKIxIjLs0&t=794s","sd":"Oct 2017","sy":"Participant interview","w8":5}]},{"k":"witness","n":"The Witness","dv":"Thekla","y":"2016","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/210970/","ls":"Steam","cv":"witness.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Self-funded the game and kept designing until every puzzle idea was explored","s":"A planned 8-hour game grew to 25 to 40 hours over 7 years. Costs rose from an $800,000 budget to close to $6 million, and Blow borrowed money to finish.","w":"Development began in 2009, paid for from Braid's earnings, with an original budget of $800,000. Blow's rule for finishing was to find all the interesting things in each puzzle category. By October 2013 the island was very big, and an internal playthrough took 25 to 40 hours against a planned 8. In February 2015 Blow said Braid income no longer covered the team and he had borrowed money. The game shipped in January 2016 after 7 years and grossed over $5 million in its first week, still short of break-even. The final cost was close to $6 million.","r":"7 years of work and a cost near $6 million.","re":"Blow borrowed money from a friend to finish the game.","co":"Close to $6 million against an $800,000 budget; 7 years.","nf":false,"l":"Set a content cap in hours or puzzle count before production, and review it at each funding checkpoint, even with your own money.","i":"A design-driven stop rule has no date in it: each new idea opens more ideas to explore. It works for a creator who can carry the cost; with outside money or a fixed team budget, the same rule breaks the plan.","a":"What is our content cap, and who decides we have hit it?","q":"“I don’t talk about it as an indie game that much because it’s got a really high budget, in part because we worked on it for seven years.”","qa":"Jonathan Blow, creator of The Witness at Thekla, Inc.","st":"The Witness isn’t an indie game","su":"https://mcvuk.com/?p=64660#:~:text=I%20don%E2%80%99t%20talk%20about%20it,on%20it%20for%20seven%20years","sd":"Feb 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"wonderful101","n":"The Wonderful 101","dv":"PlatinumGames","y":"2013","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2020","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1190400/","ls":"Steam","cv":"wonderful101.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Licensing & IP","t":"Based the first pitch on famous characters owned by Nintendo and others","s":"Nintendo's producer called it impossible on the spot, and PlatinumGames stopped the project. It came back months later as a PC demo with original superheroes.","w":"PlatinumGames president Tatsuya Minami asked Hideki Kamiya for a game that gathered world famous characters, with Nintendo's at the centre. Kamiya's plan put every character on screen at once and already held the core of the final game, including Unite Morph. When it was pitched, Nintendo producer Yamagami said it was impossible. Without permission for the characters, PlatinumGames stopped the project, and Kamiya moved to another one that was later shelved. Several months later, the studio brought Nintendo a PC demo with the same gameplay and superheroes instead. The game launched in Europe on 23 August 2013.","r":"The project stopped for several months before a second pitch.","re":"The team swapped the famous characters for original superheroes in a PC demo.","co":"Several months on hold before the second pitch.","nf":false,"l":"Get the owner's yes for any characters you do not own before you build the pitch around them.","i":"The whole concept hung on a permission the studio did not control, so one no stopped everything. The gameplay core survived because it did not need those characters; a pitch whose core is the licence would not recover this way.","a":"Do we have permission for every character our pitch depends on?","q":"“As far as PlatinumGames was concerned, the project was all about the characters, so we stopped the whole project for a while, because we knew it would be difficult if we couldn’t get permission there.”","qa":"Atsushi Inaba, producer at PlatinumGames","st":"Iwata Asks: The Wonderful 101: Development Staff, 2. We Need to Change This","su":"https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Iwata-Asks/Iwata-Asks-The-Wonderful-101-Development-Staff/The-Wonderful-101-Development-Staff/2-We-Need-to-Change-This/2-We-Need-to-Change-This-798481.html#:~:text=As%20far%20as%20PlatinumGames%20was%20concerned,get%20permission%20there","sd":"2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"xfilesgame","n":"The X-Files Game","dv":"HyperBole Studios","y":"1998","sc":"AA","se":"Port 1999","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Left implementation out of the design document and built tools for more than one game","s":"Hastily sketched concepts took far longer than the design implied in video, graphics and programming. Management kept the original schedule with 8 months of 12 to 20 hour days, which cut code stability.","w":"HyperBole spent nearly a year of pre-production on the story and script. The design document skipped how concepts would be implemented, and the technology was built for more products than this one game, costing unbudgeted time. Building hastily sketched concepts, every department found much more work than the design implied. Instead of re-scoping or running a research phase to reset the schedule, management had everyone work around the clock: 6 to 7 days a week, 12 to 20 hours a day, for 8 months. The PC and Mac game shipped on 22 June 1998 after 4+ years and sold well.","r":"Every department had far more work than the design implied; 8 months of crunch.","re":"An 8-month death march to hold the original date; code stability dropped 3 months in.","co":"8 months of 12 to 20 hour days, 6 to 7 days a week.","nf":false,"l":"Do not sign off a design document until each concept says how it will be built and how long that takes.","i":"A script-complete design hid the real work: each hastily sketched concept grew once video, art and code tried to build it. VandenBerghe believes a normal workload would have finished at about the same time, so the crunch bought nothing.","a":"Does our design document say how each feature gets built, or only what it is?","q":"“Difficult questions like how we were to implement a design concept were not present in the design document.”","qa":"Jason VandenBerghe, programmer on the PC/Mac version, HyperBole Studios","st":"Postmortem: HyperBole Studios' The X-Files","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-hyperbole-studios-i-the-x-files-i-#:~:text=Difficult%20questions%20like%20how%20we,in%20the%20design%20document","sd":"Dec 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Awarded the PlayStation port to a low bidder before the game design was complete","s":"The first 2 port teams, underfunded and on a mythical schedule, produced little usable code or assets. A third team started over after the PC release and needed another full year.","w":"About 18 months before the PC and Mac version shipped, an outside developer won the PlayStation port on a low bid made before the game design was complete. From the start the port was underfunded, and HyperBole was focused almost entirely on shipping PC and Mac. The port went through 3 full staff turnovers; the first 2 teams delivered very little usable code or assets. After the PC and Mac release on 22 June 1998, a third team with VandenBerghe as producer chose to start over. After another full year the port shipped on 13 October 1999.","r":"2 wasted port teams; a from-scratch restart added another full year.","re":"A third team, formed after the PC release, threw everything out and started over.","co":"3 staff turnovers and 1 extra year; the port shipped 16 months after PC.","nf":false,"l":"Never award a port on a bid made before the design is complete; fund and staff it like a project of its own.","i":"A bid made before the design existed became the port's budget, so it was underfunded from the outset. VandenBerghe's own read is optimistic scheduling: once someone made a realistic assessment of what shipping required, development went fairly smoothly.","a":"Was any outsourced work bid before its design was complete?","q":"“Many of these problems stemmed from one initial difficulty: an outside developer made a low-bid to develop the port before the game design was even complete, and they were hired on the basis of that bid.”","qa":"Jason VandenBerghe, producer of the PlayStation port, HyperBole Studios","st":"Postmortem: HyperBole Studios' The X-Files","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-hyperbole-studios-i-the-x-files-i-#:~:text=Many%20of%20these%20problems%20stemmed,basis%20of%20that%20bid","sd":"Dec 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"thiefthedarkproject","n":"Thief: The Dark Project","dv":"Looking Glass Studios","y":"1998","sc":"AA","se":"Expanded edition 1999","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/211600/","ls":"Steam","cv":"thiefthedarkproject.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the game on an after-hours renderer and a demo-grade DOS editor","s":"When its author left at the end of 1996 the renderer lost its owner and never reached 1998's state of the art, and the undocumented editor cost the design team time.","w":"Thief's renderer core was written in fall 1995 as an after-hours experiment by Sean Barrett, and the project, begun in spring 1996, was built on it. Barrett left at the end of 1996 and contracted back at times, but the renderer never got the attention needed to reach 1998's state of the art. By the time licensing was viable, game and renderer were too tightly integrated to change. The Dromed editor began as a DOS demo editor, never got formal specifications and was poorly documented; in 1999 Leonard reckoned the time lost cleaning it up would have been saved later.","r":"The renderer lagged 1998's state of the art and the editor ate design time.","re":"Kept Barrett on contract and spread renderer knowledge across the team; shipped on it.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Give the renderer and the editor an owner and a spec from day one, and decide early whether to license.","i":"Tech that starts as a side experiment has no owner, no spec and no exit: once the game grew into it, swapping it for a licensed engine was no longer possible. The lock-in, not the first choice, is what cost them.","a":"Who owns our renderer and editor, and when do we decide on licensing?","q":"“By the time licensing was a viable choice, the game and the renderer were too tightly integrated for us to consider changing.”","qa":"Tom Leonard, lead programmer, Looking Glass Studios","st":"Postmortem: Thief: The Dark Project","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-i-thief-the-dark-project-i-#:~:text=By%20the%20time%20licensing,to%20consider%20changing","sd":"Jul 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Staffed a full content team before the stealth design and core tech were mature","s":"Without specs, the team built content that was wrong for the game, threw away four-fifths of the AI in March 1998, and saw the core systems gel only 3 months before ship.","w":"The project began in spring 1996 as Dark Camelot, a sword-combat game due in 1997. In early 1997 it became a thievery game; full development began in May 1997 with a mostly new team, staffed before the design and technology were mature. Six months in, the project director and overseer of the AI system left. Without proper specifications the team built content wrong for the game; new AI work began only in mid-March 1998 after four-fifths of the code was thrown away. The core systems gelled only in mid-summer 1998, 3 months before the early-November gold master.","r":"Content built wrong, AI rewritten from mid-March 1998, systems gelled 3 months before ship.","re":"Rewrote the AI, cut missions needing absent tech and reworked missions off the core loop.","co":"Four-fifths of the AI code thrown away; AI usable 3 to 5 months late.","nf":false,"l":"Do not ramp the content team until the core mechanic, AI and tools are proven; schedule from evidence.","i":"A large team with no specs rushes to finish a design it does not understand, so the content it produces fits the old concept, not the new one. Leonard's own estimate is that an earlier AI rewrite alone would have bought 3 to 5 months.","a":"Are our specs and core systems ready for the people we are hiring?","q":"“During \"Dark Camelot,\" and continuing through the first half of Thief, we staffed the team before the design and technology was sufficiently mature.”","qa":"Tom Leonard, lead programmer, Looking Glass Studios","st":"Postmortem: Thief: The Dark Project","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-i-thief-the-dark-project-i-#:~:text=we%20staffed%20the%20team,sufficiently%20mature","sd":"Jul 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the guard AI before the stealth design requirements were specified","s":"The AI suited an action fighting game, not stealth. With 6 months left to the scheduled ship date, the team threw away four-fifths of it.","w":"Another programmer designed Thief's original AI before the revised stealth design was fully specified. Six months in, the project director overseeing it left, and the AI carried on without oversight or a firm design. It was geared toward an action fighting game. Lead programmer Tom Leonard had a new design by November 1997, but a string of make-or-break demos through February 1998 stopped the work. The rewrite began in mid-March 1998, with the scheduled ship date 6 months away. After 12 weeks of grueling hours the AI was ready for testing.","r":"Four-fifths of the AI code was thrown away and rewritten late.","re":"Leonard rewrote the AI in a 12-week push of grueling hours.","co":"Four-fifths of the AI code discarded; 12-week rewrite 6 months before ship.","nf":false,"l":"Write down what a core system must do for the current design before anyone builds it.","i":"The AI was built for an older idea of the game, and nobody owned it after its overseer left. Leonard said an earlier rewrite would have saved 3 to 5 months. Early systems work pays only when someone checks it against the current design.","a":"Does someone own checking our core systems against the current design?","q":"“Despite the fact that our scheduled ship date was just six months away, we threw away four-fifths of our existing AI code and started over.”","qa":"Tom Leonard, lead programmer at Looking Glass Studios","st":"Postmortem: Thief: The Dark Project","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-i-thief-the-dark-project-i-#:~:text=Despite%20the%20fact%20that%20our%20scheduled,AI%20code%20and%20started%20over","sd":"Jul 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"threes","n":"Threes!","dv":"Sirvo","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"Port 2024","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1818570/","ls":"Steam","cv":"threes.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Shelved Threes in early 2013 instead of polishing a core loop that plateaued","s":"In June 2013 a new batch of prototypes found the doubling rule behind the final game. Threes shipped in February 2014, 14 months after the first prototype.","w":"Asher Vollmer sent the first Threes prototype to Greg Wohlwend in December 2012. Over email they tried holes, walls, a hungry monster and other twists. On 17 February 2013 Asher wrote that the game was not worth releasing: once learned, it left nothing to achieve, and menu polish would not fix that. After one last try in March, they tabled it. In June 2013 Asher sent a flurry of new prototypes, and the 20 June build showed the doubling mechanic. Threes shipped in February 2014 after 570 emails. Within weeks, 1024 and 2048 followed with similar systems.","r":"The final doubling mechanic arrived about 7 months after the first prototype.","re":"After the clones, the two published 45,000 words of their emails.","co":"14 months and 570 emails between 2 developers.","nf":false,"l":"If the core loop plateaus once players learn it, stop polishing and go back to prototyping rules.","i":"Polish improves how a game feels, not whether players have a reason to keep playing. The break ended with a burst of cheap prototypes, and one of them found the fix. Long hidden iteration has a price: simpler copies reached players within weeks.","a":"Do players still have something to chase after an hour of play?","q":"“Threes, in its current state, is not worth releasing.”","qa":"Asher Vollmer, co-developer of Threes","st":"The Rip-offs & Making Our Original Game","su":"http://asherv.com/threes/threemails/#:~:text=Threes%2C%20in%20its%20current%20state,is%20not%20worth%20releasing","sd":"2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"thumper","n":"Thumper","dv":"Drool","y":"2016","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/356400/","ls":"Steam","cv":"thumper.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Made building a custom engine the first goal of a 2-person rhythm game","s":"Thumper took over 7 years and 100,000 lines of engine code while the team kept promising itself a one-year release. The engine later paid off in tools and VR performance.","w":"In 2009 Marc Flury and Brian Gibson set their goals: first make an engine, then make their own game, released within a year. Flury had never written a shader, and neither had designed a whole game. They kept saying 'within a year' for the first few years, and spent a long time with a game that was not fun yet. The custom engine later let them build editor tools for working across a 14-hour time difference and tune performance for PSVR. Thumper shipped in October 2016. In his 2017 GDC talk Flury said the engine was worth it.","r":"Development ran over 7 years against repeated one-year estimates.","re":"Flury went full-time on savings for the last 5 years.","co":"Over 7 years for 2 people, 100,000+ lines of engine code.","nf":false,"l":"Decide whether to build an engine from what the game needs, and put the years it adds into the plan.","i":"An engine built while learning 3D graphics adds years before the game is fun, and most of that work shows nothing on screen. It paid back here because Thumper needed low-level control for VR. Without that need, you pay the cost and get no payoff.","a":"Have we put the years a custom engine adds into our schedule?","q":"“Part of me just felt like I'd never be a real game developer unless I made my own engine.”","qa":"Marc Flury, programmer and co-founder of Drool","st":"Seven Years in Alpha: The Thumper Postmortem","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ckm8_SEIXQM&t=326s","sd":"2017","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ckm8_SEIXQM&t=328s"}]},{"k":"timeshift","n":"TimeShift","dv":"Saber Interactive","y":"2007","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/10130/","ls":"Steam","cv":"timeshift.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Schedule & milestones","t":"Added a year and a PS3 version to a nearly finished game at gold","s":"Story, FMVs, voice acting and the opening and closing levels were thrown away and redone with no proper pre-production pass. The 2006 build was a mishmash of visions and the story came out weak.","w":"Saber built a proof-of-concept demo in 2003 with 25 people Atari signed it a year later for PC and Xbox, then moved it to Xbox 360. Two years in, Atari had cash-flow trouble and sold the project to Sierra, which first planned 6 more months of polish. Then, on the eve of gold master in September 2006, Sierra gave TimeShift another year and asked for a PS3 version. In that year the FMVs, voice acting, story, opening and ending levels, characters, vehicles and weapons were all redone, with no proper pre-production phase. TimeShift shipped in fall 2007 after 4 years.","r":"A year of rework on a game that had been days from gold.","re":"Redid story, FMVs, voice acting and the opening and closing levels in a year.","co":"1 extra year; team grew from 25 to 92 full-time plus 50 contractors.","nf":false,"l":"If a publisher reboots a near-finished game, run a real pre-production pass before rework starts; piecemeal extensions produce a mishmash.","i":"Extensions of 2 to 4 months never gave enough room to either finish or overhaul properly, so each one layered another creative vision onto the last. The publisher paid for the extra year; the hidden cost was a game the press had already seen.","a":"If we get more time, will we re-plan or just keep patching?","q":"“Literally on the eve of GMC, Sierra made the rather unorthodox decision to give TimeShift another year of development.”","qa":"Matthew Karch, Saber Interactive","st":"Postmortem: Saber Interactive's TimeShift","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-saber-interactive-s-i-timeshift-i-#:~:text=Literally%20on%20the%20eve%20of%20GMC,another%20year%20of%20development","sd":"Apr 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Produced art assets in parallel with the technology meant to support them","s":"Shadow mapping worked only at alpha, so lighting artists had little time for the levels, and the team kept hitting unforeseen issues.","w":"Saber began TimeShift in 2003 as a small proof-of-concept demo, with a studio of 25 people. Minimal time went into preproduction and feature testing, so assets were built while the technology to support them came online. Two years in, Sierra acquired the game from Atari. In September 2006 the build was a mishmash of creative visions, and Sierra gave the project an extra year. Shadow mapping only became functional at alpha, which left lighting artists little time. TimeShift shipped in fall 2007 after 4 years, three extensions and three voice re-recordings.","r":"Lighting was set up in a rush once shadow maps worked at alpha.","re":"Sierra's extra year let the team redo story, levels, characters and weapons.","co":"4 years with three extensions; peak of 92 staff plus 50+ contractors.","nf":false,"l":"Get core rendering features such as shadows working before artists build the final assets that depend on them.","i":"Assets made before the tech settles are built against guesses, and every late tech change lands on finished content. Here the lighting pass got squeezed into the short time after alpha. The extra year came from a split creative vision, a separate problem.","a":"Which tech features must work before artists start final assets?","q":"“We had minimal time to spend on preproduction and feature testing in the early stages.”","qa":"Matthew Karch, Saber Interactive","st":"Postmortem: Saber Interactive's TimeShift","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-saber-interactive-s-i-timeshift-i-#:~:text=We%20had%20minimal%20time%20to,in%20the%20early%20stages","sd":"Apr 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"titanquest","n":"Titan Quest","dv":"Iron Lore Entertainment","y":"2006","sc":"AA","se":"Remaster 2016","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/475150/","ls":"Steam","cv":"titanquest.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Started production with one systems designer and no detailed design documents","s":"First-cut design docs were still being written a year in. Programmers worked without specs, some systems had to be redone, and tech tasks piled up.","w":"Iron Lore was founded in October 2000 and spent nearly 3.5 years pitching Titan Quest with a demo. Contract talks with THQ ran 6 months over plan, and the deal was signed in January 2004. Production began with one systems designer; the second and third joined 10 and 12 months in. First-cut design documents were still in progress a year after the start, so the technical team flew blind and some systems were redone. The game shipped in June 2006 after 30 months. Producer Jeff Goodsill's October 2006 postmortem listed this first under what went wrong.","r":"Programmers started without specs, and some systems had to be redone.","re":"Four leads, including THQ's creative manager, had to sign off every design document before coding.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Have the core system designs written and the systems designers hired before the contract starts the production clock.","i":"Programmers need specs to build a system once. Without them they wait or guess, and guesses get redone or built too broad. A long pitch phase builds a demo, not design documents, so it does not replace pre-production.","a":"Are our core system design documents done before production starts?","q":"“We were still working on first cut design documentation more than one year after the start of the project, so the technical team had to fly blind in the beginning and some systems had to be redone.”","qa":"Jeff Goodsill, producer at Iron Lore Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Iron Lore's Titan Quest","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_October_2006.pdf#:~:text=We%20were%20still%20working%20on%20first,some%20systems%20had%20to%20be%20redone","sd":"Oct 2006","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"tomclancysrainbow6patriots","n":"Tom Clancy's Rainbow Six Siege (restarted from Rainbow 6: Patriots)","dv":"Ubisoft Montreal","y":"2015","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/359550/","ls":"Steam","cv":"tomclancysrainbow6patriots.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Wrote off the Patriots campaign and restarted as a 60 fps multiplayer-first game","s":"Patriots, shown in 2011 and 2012, was dropped and the team rebuilt the game around multiplayer. Siege shipped in December 2015 and passed 30 million registered players by April 2018.","w":"Ubisoft announced Rainbow 6 Patriots in November 2011 and showed it again at E3 2012. Until then Ubisoft built single-player first and took its systems into multiplayer. Watching the market, CEO Yves Guillemot concluded the team had to lead with multiplayer and target 60 frames per second. Ubisoft took the write-off and restarted the game. Patriots was officially cancelled on 9 June 2014 and Siege shipped on 1 December 2015. By April 2018 Ubisoft reported more than 30 million registered players.","r":"Patriots work written off; the restarted game shipped 4 years after the first announcement.","re":"Rebuilt the game multiplayer-first and shipped it as Siege in December 2015.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When the market or platform shifts under your project, decide early whether to write it off and restart; waiting makes the write-off bigger.","i":"Guillemot's point: a game below the quality bar no longer sells a couple of million units, so an early write-off beats finishing the wrong game. That holds for a publisher that can fund a restart; a smaller studio has to re-scope instead.","a":"If the market shifted today, would we restart or keep porting the old plan?","q":"“The longer you wait, the bigger the mistake is.”","qa":"Yves Guillemot, CEO of Ubisoft","st":"Ubisoft president explains the untimely death of Rainbow Six: Patriots","su":"https://www.gamewatcher.com/2014-18-06-ubisoft-president-explains-the-untimely-death-of-rainbow-six-patriots#:~:text=The%20longer%20you%20wait,the%20mistake%20is","sd":"Jun 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"tomclancyssplintercell","n":"Tom Clancy's Splinter Cell","dv":"Ubi Soft Shanghai","y":"2002","sc":"AAA","se":"Port 2003","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/13560/","ls":"Steam","cv":"tomclancyssplintercell.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Prototyped the PS2 port with a small team to cost one map before staffing up","s":"Prototype data set the schedule, the headcount and pessimistic memory budgets. Production took no more than 4 months, and the PS2 version shipped inside Ubi Soft's fiscal year.","w":"Ubi Soft Shanghai started the PS2 port in April 2002 with 5 people, including producer Wu Dong Hao. After 2 months, 4 more engineers and an animator joined. The prototype proved the port was technically possible, set pessimistic memory budgets for each part of the game, and split the work of porting one map into steps to estimate resource costs. Pre-production began 3 months before full production, which started in October 2002. The team grew to about 90 people. The PS2 version shipped on March 27, 2003, and Wu's postmortem followed that July.","r":"Schedule and headcount came from prototype data, not guesses.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"5 to 10 people from April to October 2002, before full production.","nf":false,"l":"Before you staff up a port, cost one map through every step with a small team.","i":"A port has a known target, so one map is a fair sample of the whole job. The cost of one map turns straight into headcount and dates. The method fits new designs less well, where the first map is rarely typical.","a":"Have we costed one full unit of content before setting headcount?","q":"“The effort required to port one map was split into several steps, and we used our experience creating the prototype to accurately define the data flow between steps and estimate the overall resource costs.”","qa":"Wu Dong Hao, producer at Ubi Soft Shanghai","st":"Postmortem: Tom Clancy's Splinter Cell","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/production/postmortem-i-tom-clancy-s-splinter-cell-i-#:~:text=The%20effort%20required%20to%20port,estimate%20the%20overall%20resource%20costs","sd":"Jul 2003","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"tomclancyssplintercellconviction","n":"Tom Clancy's Splinter Cell: Conviction","dv":"Ubisoft Montreal","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/33220/","ls":"Steam","cv":"tomclancyssplintercellconviction.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Replaced the series' light-and-shadow stealth with disguise and crowd concealment","s":"After more than two years the game fell short of Ubisoft's flagship bar. The work was binned, the team was restructured and core stealth systems were recoded from scratch.","w":"After Double Agent shipped in 2006, the team set out to rebuild Splinter Cell around disguise, crowd concealment, improvisation and dynamic environmental action. The plan dropped dynamic lighting for ambient occlusion and static bounce light. Ubisoft revealed the game at UbiDays 2007. By early 2008 the game was so far from the series' core mechanics that Ubisoft saw a key franchise at risk, and over two years of work was binned. The new leads had to recode dynamic lighting, two-handed firing and gadget management. In 2010 the authors called the original direction unworkable as a full game.","r":"Over two years of work binned and the team restructured.","re":"Ubisoft brought in a new producer and creative director to restore shadow stealth.","co":"Over 2 years of work binned; team restructured.","nf":false,"l":"Before a sequel drops its core mechanic, play the replacement for a full mission and compare it with the old one.","i":"Light and shadow was the base most Splinter Cell features sat on, so going back meant recoding it all. The risk is specific to a core mechanic; a new story or setting is far cheaper to reverse.","a":"Have we played the new core mechanic for a full mission yet?","q":"“When the results fell short of the publisher’s standards for its flagship titles, it meant the binning of over two years worth of work and the restructuring of the team.”","qa":"Patrick Redding (co-op game director), Alex Parizeau (producer) and Maxime Beland (creative director), Ubisoft Montreal","st":"Postmortem: Ubisoft Montreal's Splinter Cell Conviction","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_December_2010.pdf#:~:text=When%20the%20results%20fell%20short,the%20restructuring%20of%20the%20team","sd":"Dec 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Locked one vision and built a vertical slice holding every shipping feature","s":"Once pre-production began, the direction never changed. The pitched walkthrough of the whole game differed from what shipped in April 2010 by a single scripted event.","w":"Producer Alex Parizeau and creative director Maxime Beland took over at the start of 2008. Beland set one target for the whole team: Sam moves and behaves like a panther. The team ran an accelerated pre-production, with a top-down blueprint for levels and Feature Sign-Offs that wrote each feature as testable requirements. In November 2008 it delivered a vertical slice with every feature that later shipped, and pitched a walkthrough of the full game. The team grew to 200 developers in Montreal, Bucharest and Paris. The E3 2009 demo ran on real systems, and the game shipped in April 2010.","r":"The November 2008 slice held every feature the final game shipped with.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"200 developers at peak across 3 studios.","nf":false,"l":"After a restart, build a vertical slice that holds every feature you plan to ship before the team reaches full size.","i":"The slice could hold every feature because the original crew had spent a year prototyping and many of them stayed on, engine veterans first. An accelerated pre-production without that groundwork is a much bigger gamble.","a":"Does our vertical slice contain every feature we plan to ship?","q":"“We delivered a publishable vertical slice halfway through the new production schedule, which contained all the features that shipped in the final game, in part because the team had already spent a year of rapid prototyping and knew how to plan and what to include.”","qa":"Patrick Redding (co-op game director), Alex Parizeau (producer) and Maxime Beland (creative director), Ubisoft Montreal","st":"Postmortem: Ubisoft Montreal's Splinter Cell Conviction","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_December_2010.pdf#:~:text=We%20delivered%20a%20publishable%20vertical,and%20what%20to%20include","sd":"Dec 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"tombraider","n":"Tomb Raider","dv":"Crystal Dynamics","y":"2013","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/203160/","ls":"Steam","cv":"tombraider.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built several finished vertical slices instead of a single slice at the start","s":"Each finished chunk showed the real quality, content and build effort, and became a blueprint for the rest. The reboot shipped in March 2013 with a Metacritic score of 86.","w":"After Tomb Raider: Underworld, around 2008, Crystal Dynamics spent years prototyping a reboot with twin axes, demons, a horse, a motorcycle and a companion girl. The team then settled on survival as the lens for all gameplay. Studio head Darrell Gallagher pushed for an 85+ Metacritic game, which this team had never made. Instead of grey-boxing everything and polishing in the final year, the team finished 30 and 90 minute chunks early, user-tested them and handed them to the team as blueprints. The game shipped on 4 March 2013, and Steam lists its Metacritic score at 86.","r":"The team knew early what finished content took to build.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"Constant shipping of finished chunks, which the team found exhausting.","nf":true,"l":"Finish one 30 to 90 minute chunk to ship quality early, user-test it, and plan the rest from what it took.","i":"Grey-box gyms hide what happens when lighting, weather, stealth and AI run together. A finished chunk shows those clashes and the real effort early. It needs the core pillars set first; during open prototyping, finishing chunks polishes ideas you may throw away.","a":"Have we finished any chunk of our game to ship quality yet?","q":"“We actually, instead, made multiple vertical slices.”","qa":"Scot Amos, executive producer at Crystal Dynamics","st":"The 30 Year History of Crystal Dynamics - Noclip Documentary","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DsByUubJkk8&t=4370s","sd":"Nov 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"tombraiderlegend","n":"Tomb Raider: Legend","dv":"Crystal Dynamics","y":"2006","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/7000/","ls":"Steam","cv":"tombraiderlegend.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Started the Tomb Raider reboot with about 45 people before core mechanics were set","s":"The team struggled to move in one direction while story locations and core play were still open. The lead designer called the result wasted energy and stress.","w":"When Crystal Dynamics got the next Tomb Raider, it was eager to start, and talented people were free from other projects. The initial team was about 45 people, while story locations and core mechanics were not yet hammered out. An aggressive schedule fixed dates for level designs, so levels were designed and arted before they could be played. About halfway through, Eidos was sold and the new owner SCi gave more time, which also meant rework. Legend shipped in April 2006 after 2 years with about 80 people. Lead designer Riley Cooper wrote it up that August.","r":"Effort and stress were wasted getting 45 people aligned on an open design.","re":"Crystal Dynamics became much more careful about how many people start a project.","co":"Initial team of about 45 people before the design was settled.","nf":false,"l":"Keep the starting team small until story locations and core mechanics are locked.","i":"A large team needs direction from day one. With the big decisions still open, people pulled in different directions and work was redone. The risk is smaller on a sequel where the mechanics already exist.","a":"Are our core mechanics locked enough for the team we have now?","q":"“This combination led to an initial team size that was simply too big.”","qa":"Riley Cooper, lead designer of Tomb Raider: Legend at Crystal Dynamics","st":"Reestablishing an Icon: The Peaks and Pitfalls of Tomb Raider: Legend","su":"https://media.gdcvault.com/GD_Mag_Archives/GDM_August_2006.pdf#page=30","sd":"Aug 2006","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"tombraiderunderworld","n":"Tomb Raider: Underworld","dv":"Crystal Dynamics","y":"2008","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/8140/","ls":"Steam","cv":"tombraiderunderworld.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built Underworld on a studio-wide shared code base owned by a separate engine group","s":"The engine group did not report to the game team, which could only influence it by petition. An early refactor of the player code took the game offline for the window designers had planned for exploratory layout work.","w":"At the start of Underworld, Crystal Dynamics decided to build a shared code base for all future games, maintained by dedicated engineers, and to base Underworld on it. The shared technology group served several teams, did not report to the game's lead engineer, and gave limited visibility into its progress. An early refactor of the player code took the engine apart and made it unplayable for the duration; slips stretched that period into production and Alpha. The design team lost its exploratory window. The game shipped in November 2008 after 2.5 years; Lindstrom later called the refactor an irreversible miscommunication.","r":"Designers built layouts without playable mechanics; the effects lasted to ship.","re":"Nothing could reverse it; the team pushed into production without core player functions.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Keep the engine team accountable to the game team in pre-production, and never take the player offline while designers need it.","i":"A dependency you can only petition cannot be rescheduled when it slips, and a refactor nobody understood as 'engine in pieces' ate the design window. Lindstrom calls it miscommunication, not a bad idea: the shared base was meant to pay off on later games.","a":"Does our engine team report to us, and when is the player playable?","q":"“But the shared technology group was not on the team.”","qa":"Eric Lindstrom, creative director of Tomb Raider: Underworld, Crystal Dynamics","st":"Postmortem: Crystal Dynamics' Tomb Raider: Underworld","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-crystal-dynamics-i-tomb-raider-underworld-i-#:~:text=But%20the%20shared%20technology%20group,not%20on%20the%20team","sd":"Sep 2009","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Ended preproduction on a fixed date before art needs and outsourcing were understood","s":"The team had to schedule an unusually long alpha to clear unresolved preproduction issues, and cut content again whenever the game kept coming in too big.","w":"Concept work on Underworld began while Tomb Raider: Legend was in final QA. Crystal Dynamics bet on a shared engine owned by another group, and an early refactor left core mechanics unplayable, so designers drew layouts from paper metrics. The ship date could not move. The team ended preproduction before it understood its art needs or had a pipeline for outsourcing art, with some slots still held for people finishing Tomb Raider: Anniversary. A long alpha with repeated cuts followed. The game shipped in November 2008, and creative director Eric Lindstrom's 2009 postmortem called it a repeated mistake.","r":"Production started without measured art costs or an outsourcing pipeline.","re":"The team scheduled a long alpha and cut content in repeated passes.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When the ship date is fixed, cut scope instead of cutting preproduction short.","i":"With both the date and the scope held fixed, preproduction was the only thing left to squeeze. The art costs and outsourcing needs it should have measured showed up later, as cuts during alpha.","a":"Are we cutting preproduction to hold the date instead of cutting scope?","q":"“At the time, and indeed throughout the project, we knew it was risky to move on to the next phase of production before the previous one had completely finished, yet we did so anyway.”","qa":"Eric Lindstrom, creative director of Tomb Raider: Underworld at Crystal Dynamics","st":"Postmortem: Crystal Dynamics' Tomb Raider: Underworld","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-crystal-dynamics-i-tomb-raider-underworld-i-#:~:text=At%20the%20time%2C%20and%20indeed,yet%20we%20did%20so%20anyway","sd":"2009, reprinted Sep 2009","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"toohuman","n":"Too Human","dv":"Silicon Knights","y":"2008","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Licensed Unreal Engine 3 in May 2005 to build Too Human on Xbox 360","s":"Silicon Knights later said engine problems forced it to write its own engine and delayed the game by nearly two years. A jury sided with Epic instead.","w":"On May 10, 2005, Silicon Knights signed a license for Epic's Unreal Engine 3 to make Too Human for Microsoft. The Xbox 360 launched that November. MCV reported that work started on incomplete engine versions and that the E3 2006 demo drew a largely negative reaction. Silicon Knights rewrote parts of the engine into its own Silicon Knights Engine and sued Epic in 2007. Planned for November 2006, the game shipped in 2008 and sold 640,076 units by June 2009. In May 2012 a jury awarded Epic $4.45 million, and the court later ordered unsold copies destroyed.","r":"Release slipped from a November 2006 plan to 2008.","re":"Silicon Knights built its own engine and sued Epic in 2007.","co":"Release nearly 2 years late; $4.45M jury award to Epic, over $9M with fees.","nf":false,"l":"Before you sign an engine license, build a playable slice of your own game on the version you will actually receive.","i":"A new console and an engine still in development put two moving targets under one schedule. Silicon Knights' own expert put about half of the delay down to normal slippage, so the engine was not the only cause.","a":"Have we built our own content on the engine version we are licensing?","q":"“SK contends that problems with UE3 eventually forced SK to develop its own game engine (\"SKE\"), delaying the release of Too Human by nearly two years.”","qa":"US District Court for the Eastern District of North Carolina, summarising Silicon Knights' claim","st":"Silicon Knights, Inc. v. Epic Games, Inc., No. 5:07-CV-275-D, Order (Doc. 697)","su":"https://cases.justia.com/federal/district-courts/north-carolina/ncedce/5:2007cv00275/89570/697/0.pdf#:~:text=SK%20contends%20that%20problems%20with,by%20nearly%20two%20years","sd":"Dec 2011","sy":"Court filing","w8":5}]},{"k":"torchlight","n":"Torchlight","dv":"Runic Games","y":"2009","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/41500/","ls":"Steam","cv":"torchlight.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Used the months of publisher deal-making to build a prototype and production tools","s":"The team avoided the Mythos pattern of laying track in front of a moving train. Its art director reported a far better balance between design and production.","w":"Flagship Studios closed in July 2008, and the Mythos team lost its game. A month later it formed Runic Games, with initial funding for a prototype and a pitch. Several deal options opened up, and the team knew its deadline to close one. It spent that time on preproduction: a prototype, concept work and the tools to make production efficient. Runic kept a playable build at all times and picked the Ogre 3D engine along the way. After signing with Perfect World, it shipped Torchlight in October 2009.","r":"Design no longer ran just ahead of production, as on Mythos.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"While a publisher deal is pending, build the prototype and the production tools you will need after signing.","i":"The deal deadline gave a fixed window, and the team spent it on tools and a prototype, not content. Runic had initial funding and veterans of Diablo, Fate and Mythos; a team without that runway gets less from the wait.","a":"Which production tools can we build before the deal is signed?","q":"“With Torchlight, we have been able to strike a far better balance between design and production.”","qa":"Jason Beck, art director at Runic Games","st":"From the Ashes of Mythos: The Art of Torchlight","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/from-the-ashes-of-i-mythos-i-the-art-of-i-torchlight-i-#:~:text=With%20Torchlight%2C%20we%20have%20been%20able,between%20design%20and%20production","sd":"Sep 2009","sy":"Participant account","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Gave each of 3 classes a unique character and left customization to the MMO","s":"The art team dropped a shared-body shortcut it disliked. Morale, which had dipped during the debate, recovered once the call was made.","w":"During preproduction, after Runic formed in August 2008, the team tested a unibody plan: one body mesh with swappable heads for all races and genders. It promised schedule savings and a simple art pipeline. A prototype model showed the problem: the males did not look heroic and the females did not look feminine. Morale dipped as artists felt art was being sacrificed for schedule. Once the team accepted it needed at least 2 meshes, 3 seemed manageable. It chose a single-player game before the MMO, with 3 unique characters for 3 classes, and shipped in October 2009.","r":"Morale recovered, and character art went ahead with 3 meshes.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Prototype the cheap character setup in preproduction; if it fails the look, cut the class count until unique meshes fit.","i":"The shortcut saved schedule but broke the proportions the artists cared about, and the debate cost morale. Limiting players to 3 classes made unique meshes affordable. This fits a game with fixed classes; a game sold on deep customization cannot defer it.","a":"How many unique character meshes fit our schedule, and do our classes match?","q":"“Once we made the call for unique characters, the mood changed considerably and we were back on track.”","qa":"Jason Beck, art director at Runic Games","st":"From the Ashes of Mythos: The Art of Torchlight","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/from-the-ashes-of-i-mythos-i-the-art-of-i-torchlight-i-#:~:text=Once%20we%20made%20the%20call%20for,we%20were%20back%20on%20track","sd":"Sep 2009","sy":"Participant account","w8":4}]},{"k":"tormenttidesofnumenera","n":"Torment: Tides of Numenera","dv":"inXile Entertainment","y":"2017","sc":"AA","se":"Early Access 2016","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/272270/","ls":"Steam","cv":"tormenttidesofnumenera.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"External commitments","t":"Added stretch-goal companions, a second city and crafting during the live Kickstarter","s":"About a month before launch, inXile cut crafting, shrank the companion roster and the Oasis city, and dropped Italian. Backers heard late, and the creative lead apologized.","w":"inXile ran the Torment Kickstarter from 6 March to 5 April 2013 and raised $4,188,927 against a $900,000 goal. The extra money unlocked stretch goals, among them new companions, the Oasis as a second major city, and crafting. In November 2015 the release slipped from 2015 to 2016 and project lead Kevin Saunders left; inXile gave quality as the reason. About a month before the February 2017 launch, creative lead Colin McComb wrote that some campaign decisions had failed in practice. Crafting was cut, 6 companions shipped, the Bloom replaced the Oasis as the second hub, and Italian was dropped.","r":"Promised crafting, companions and the Oasis hub were cut or reduced before launch.","re":"inXile apologized to backers and promised them free DLC and expansions.","co":"Crafting cut, companion roster cut to 6, Italian localization dropped.","nf":false,"l":"Cost every stretch goal in people and months before the campaign starts, and offer only the ones your schedule can absorb.","i":"A live campaign pushes a team to promise content before anyone has built it. McComb also named at least seven story revisions and companion work that took longer than planned, so stretch goals are one cause of the cuts, not the only one.","a":"Have we costed and scheduled every stretch goal before the campaign goes live?","q":"“We had to make decisions and add content on the fly.”","qa":"Colin McComb, Creative Lead at inXile Entertainment","st":"Updated Our Journal (63): A Look Back on Development","su":"https://www.kickstarter.com/projects/inxile/torment-tides-of-numenera/posts/1770943#:~:text=We%20had%20to%20make%20decisions,content%20on%20the%20fly","sd":"Jan 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"transistor","n":"Transistor","dv":"Supergiant Games","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/237930/","ls":"Steam","cv":"transistor.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Staffing","t":"Put the full team on Transistor from the first day of pre-production","s":"With more voices in the room, problems that took weeks on Bastion took months. Pre-production alone ran longer than Bastion's whole 18-month development.","w":"After Bastion, Supergiant started Transistor with a full team for the first time, while some staff still brought Bastion to mobile. The studio grew to 12 people and hired roles, such as a 3D artist, it had once given to freelancers. The team agreed on a sci-fi love story but struggled to find the game's direction, and studio director Amir Rao and creative director Greg Kasavin clashed. Audio director Darren Korb experimented for 6 months to find the sound. Transistor shipped in May 2014, and in 2021 several team members still recalled the process as hard.","r":"Pre-production alone outlasted Bastion's full 18-month development.","re":"The team pushed on and announced at PAX East 2013 despite mixed playtests.","co":"Pre-production over 18 months; 6 months of music experiments.","nf":false,"l":"Before the whole team joins, have the leads agree on the game's direction and on each person's role.","i":"A full team brought more opinions before anyone owned the direction, and roles like creative director were still being defined. Sequel pressure added to it. The art director valued the extra iteration time, so the cost falls on decisions, not on craft.","a":"Do the leads agree on direction and roles before everyone joins?","q":"“We were starting the game with a full team – including more voices and perspectives and learning how to do that.”","qa":"Amir Rao, studio director of Supergiant Games","st":"The Story Behind Supergiant Games’ Transistor","su":"https://gameinformer.com/2021/05/20/the-story-behind-supergiant-games-transistor#:~:text=We%20were%20starting%20the%20game%20with,learning%20how%20to%20do%20that","sd":"May 2021","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"trespasser","n":"Trespasser","dv":"DreamWorks Interactive","y":"1998","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Bet on a from-scratch engine, unseen rendering tech and full physics, without a design spec","s":"The 3D card wave arrived mid-project, forcing a mixed renderer that drew bump-mapped objects in software; the physics interpenetrated, nearly every stacking puzzle was cut, and the game slipped by more than a year.","w":"Trespasser was begun before the 3dfx Voodoo 1 started the 3D hardware wave. The engineers implemented unseen rendering technologies, ignoring compatibility with hardware cards, and chose penalty-force physics for physics-modeled dinosaurs. There was no design spec, only a prose walkthrough and a short proposal, and no level editor. The result was a mixed-mode renderer that drew bump-mapped objects in software. The first quadruped went in around early summer 1998, 4 months before the intended ship; nearly every stacking puzzle was cut. The game shipped in October 1998, more than a year late, as 'at best a deeply flawed beta'.","r":"Shipped more than a year late as a deeply flawed beta; the engine was abandoned.","re":"Cut stacking puzzles, froze unreliable objects, moved DreamWorks Interactive to licensed technology.","co":"Budget $6 to 7 million (estimate); 32 to 36 months; over a year late.","nf":false,"l":"Do not bet a game on unproven rendering and physics without a design spec and a fallback for shipping hardware.","i":"The engine was designed for the hardware of its start date, and without a spec or editor the design could only follow what the engineering delivered. When physics could not be made reliable, the puzzles built on it went too.","a":"What is our fallback if the shipping hardware changes mid-project?","q":"“As such, the Trespasser engineers set about to create an engine in the old-school manner: they picked some previously-unseen rendering technologies and implemented them, ignoring any issues of compatibility with hardware cards.”","qa":"Richard Wyckoff, designer on Trespasser, DreamWorks Interactive","st":"Postmortem: DreamWorks Interactive's Trespasser","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/postmortem-dreamworks-interactive-s-i-trespasser-i-#:~:text=As%20such%2C%20the%20Trespasser%20engineers,compatibility%20with%20hardware%20cards","sd":"May 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Entered production with only a prose walkthrough instead of a design spec","s":"Art and code had run for a year or more before levels were planned, so gameplay followed the engineering. The dinosaur AI went in directions unsuited to play.","w":"For a long time Trespasser's only gameplay documents were a prose walkthrough and a short proposal listing keys and rough ideas. Both were written before any playable technology existed. When the full team entered production, the walkthrough became level maps and puzzle descriptions. By then artists had built assets for nearly a year and programmers had coded for longer. Gameplay became engineering-driven. Without a spec calling for movie-like behaviours, the dinosaur AI ended up unsuitable for play, found only months before ship. Trespasser shipped in October 1998, and its designer called it at best a deeply flawed beta.","r":"Gameplay was shaped by what the code did, not by design.","re":"The team worked around the AI because there was no time to rewrite it.","co":"Nearly a year of art built before levels were designed.","nf":false,"l":"Write a design spec before full production, and rewrite it as the tech changes rather than skip it.","i":"Without a spec, each department builds to its own picture of the game, and the engine's picture wins by default. Wyckoff notes an out-of-date spec still beats none. Engineering-led work only suits games whose gameplay needs are light and flexible.","a":"Do we have a current design spec that programmers and artists build to?","q":"“The biggest indication that Trespasser had game design problems was the fact that it never had a proper design spec.”","qa":"Richard Wyckoff, designer at DreamWorks Interactive","st":"Postmortem: DreamWorks Interactive's Trespasser","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/postmortem-dreamworks-interactive-s-i-trespasser-i-#:~:text=The%20biggest%20indication%20that%20Trespasser,a%20proper%20design%20spec","sd":"May 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Used 3D Studio Max as the whole level editor instead of building a custom tool","s":"Levels of 20,000 to 25,000 meshes pushed Max far past its limit of about 5,000 objects. Selecting one object could take 30 to 60 seconds, and level building fell behind.","w":"Trespasser's levels were built entirely in 3D Studio Max. Designers placed 20,000 to 25,000 meshes per level and typed object behavior as code into Max's properties buffers. Only when it was too late to change did the team learn that Max could not handle more than about 5,000 objects. Level files averaged 40 MB, took minutes to load, and one click could take 30 to 60 seconds to select. The game shipped in October 1998, and in May 1999 designer Richard Wyckoff wrote that slow level building thwarted late attempts to find the fun.","r":"Level building was slow, and late gameplay fixes were hard to make.","re":"The technical artist wrote MaxScripts as design tools and crash warnings.","co":"Selecting one object took 30 to 60 seconds; levels took minutes to load.","nf":false,"l":"Before level production starts, build one level at the final object count in your editor and time select, load and save.","i":"A modeling package works on one asset at a time, so it slows down as object counts grow and has no proper place for gameplay data. Wyckoff still saw Max as right for geometry; the mistake was making it replace the game editor.","a":"Have we timed our editor on a level with the final object count?","q":"“The mistake Trespasser made was assuming that a modeling program like Max could replace all the functions of a custom game editor.”","qa":"Richard Wyckoff, designer on Trespasser at DreamWorks Interactive","st":"Postmortem: DreamWorks Interactive's Trespasser","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/postmortem-dreamworks-interactive-s-i-trespasser-i-#:~:text=The%20mistake%20Trespasser%20made%20was,of%20a%20custom%20game%20editor","sd":"May 1999","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"trine","n":"Trine","dv":"Frozenbyte","y":"2009","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/35700/","ls":"Steam","cv":"trine.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Pitched a $5-10 million multiplatform AAA concept to publishers for almost two years","s":"No publisher signed: those with money did not believe Frozenbyte could deliver, and those who believed had no money. The company shrank, and the leftover team moved onto the one-man Trine project.","w":"Frozenbyte, founded in 2001, shipped its first game, Shadowgrounds, in 2005. In 2006 it developed an AAA concept, and most of the company spent almost two years pitching it, asking publishers for 5 to 10 million dollars while planning to go multiplatform and hire many people. Smaller teams did prototypes and outsourcing work to pay for it; only Shadowgrounds Survivor (2007) shipped. Publishers with money did not believe the studio could deliver, and those with faith had no money. The project was shut down and headcount fell. In 2010 the Trine postmortem called the ask a missed warning.","r":"Almost two years of most of the company produced no signed deal.","re":"The remaining developers moved onto Trine, then a one-man pre-production project.","co":"Almost two years of most of the company; staff lost; debts carried.","nf":false,"l":"Before you pitch, check that the budget, platform list and hiring plan match what your studio has already shipped.","i":"Publishers weighed the pitch against the studio's record, and a PC team with one shipped game asking for AAA money did not pass. Kinnunen's hindsight is that the leap itself was the warning. The lesson is about the gap between pitch and record, not ambition.","a":"Does our pitch budget match what we have shipped before?","q":"“In hindsight, asking for five to 10 million dollars, going from PC-only to multiplatform overnight, and hiring a lot of new people should've set off our own alarm bells too.”","qa":"Joel Kinnunen, associate producer at Frozenbyte","st":"Postmortem: Frozenbyte's Trine","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-frozenbyte-s-i-trine-i-#:~:text=In%20hindsight%2C%20asking%20for%20five,our%20own%20alarm%20bells%20too","sd":"Jun 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Shipped Trine on the studio engine that had skipped networking in 2002","s":"Trine launched with local co-op only, and the missing online play became the main complaint. Each project had made networking more expensive to add, so a rewrite stayed out of reach.","w":"In 2002 Frozenbyte was working on a real-time strategy concept and chose to skip online and LAN features to develop faster. Every later game ran on that code base, Trine included. Each project added layers of code, and the estimated cost of adding online play grew each time. The studio could not raise money for both an engine rewrite and the game in production. Trine shipped on PC on 2 July 2009 with offline co-op for up to 3 players. Players sent hundreds of complaints that co-op was not online. By June 2010 a full code rewrite was under way.","r":"Trine shipped without online co-op, the studio's most criticized gap.","re":"Frozenbyte started a full code rewrite for its next project.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before prototype code becomes your engine, list the features later games will need, such as online play, and price adding them.","i":"Every layer built on a shortcut raises the price of undoing it, and a cheap prototype stays in use because it is cheaper and faster. The same shortcut let a small studio ship games at all; the trap is leaving its price unchecked.","a":"What would it cost to add online play to this code later?","q":"“A little bit more forward-planning in 2002 would have gone a long way.”","qa":"Joel Kinnunen, associate producer at Frozenbyte","st":"Postmortem: Frozenbyte's Trine","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-frozenbyte-s-i-trine-i-#:~:text=A%20little%20bit%20more%20forward-planning,gone%20a%20long%20way","sd":"Jun 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Scaled a hobby platformer up and kept adding people instead of cutting scope","s":"The budget grew from 30,000 to about 850,000 euros and the team from 3 to 16. Frozenbyte paid for two-thirds of it from its own pocket.","w":"Trine spent almost two years in pre-production as one programmer's hobby project, planned at 30,000 euros and under 12 months. After Frozenbyte shut down a larger AAA project that found no publisher, the freed team joined Trine. The game was redesigned over a weekend and greenlit the next Monday with a 300,000-euro budget. Instead of cutting design, the studio kept adding people, and the budget nearly doubled again when PS3 was added. The final story was written two days before voice recording in March 2009. The PC version shipped on July 2, 2009, after 18 months and about 850,000 euros.","r":"Work was redone often, and features were cut a month before gold master.","re":"An empty bank account stopped further hiring; late features were cut in May 2009.","co":"Budget 30,000 to 850,000 euros; team 3 to 16; 18 months.","nf":false,"l":"When a prototype becomes a real project, set its scope and budget once and cut design there before adding people.","i":"Each budget step raised the quality bar and threw away earlier work, so new money bought rework as well as content. The game still succeeded; the lesson is about cost control, not about refusing to grow a promising project.","a":"Have we cut scope since the budget last went up?","q":"“Instead of cutting down design and scope in the early stages, we pumped up production resources with a \"yes we can\" attitude and gradually added more people to the project.”","qa":"Joel Kinnunen, associate producer at Frozenbyte","st":"Postmortem: Frozenbyte's Trine","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/postmortem-frozenbyte-s-i-trine-i-#:~:text=Instead%20of%20cutting%20down%20design,more%20people%20to%20the%20project","sd":"Jun 2010","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"tron20","n":"TRON 2.0","dv":"Monolith Productions","y":"2003","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/327740/","ls":"Steam","cv":"tron20.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Started preproduction with 4 or 5 people and no fleshed-out prototype","s":"Light cycle racing, disc combat and the subroutine system stayed unproven for too long. The first full playable level came very late, leaving little time to build on what worked.","w":"Most of Monolith's TRON 2.0 team was still finishing other projects, so a core of about 4 or 5 people ran preproduction. It built no fleshed-out prototype. When production started and the full team arrived, quite a few months passed with minimal output, and design documents had to explain the concept. Light cycles, disc combat and subroutines stayed unknowns, and the team played a full level only very late in the schedule. The gameplay came together, with very little time left to expand it. Rooke's September 2003 postmortem traced the slow start to an underused preproduction period.","r":"Months of minimal output and a very late first playable level.","re":"Added weekly and sometimes daily reviews in the final third of production.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before the full team arrives, build one playable level that runs every core mechanic.","i":"A small core can write documents, but a document cannot show whether light cycles or disc combat play well. Until something is playable, the full team has nothing concrete to build toward. A small preproduction team is common; the missing prototype was the gap.","a":"Can the full team play every core mechanic when it arrives?","q":"“In addition, TRON 2.0 pre-production did not include a fleshed-out prototype.”","qa":"Frank Rooke, Monolith Productions","st":"Postmortem: Monolith's TRON 2.0","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-monolith-s-i-tron-2-0-i-#:~:text=In%20addition%2C%20TRON%202.0%20pre%2Dproduction,a%20fleshed%2Dout%20prototype","sd":"Sep 2003","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"tropico","n":"Tropico","dv":"PopTop Software","y":"2001","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/33520/","ls":"Steam","cv":"tropico.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Started an original game without a shared design, working as on Railroad Tycoon II","s":"Each developer built toward a different game. Building art varied wildly in scale and detail, artists had to rework it, and the project became just a job for many.","w":"In spring 1999 PopTop, then 4 artists and 2 programmers, began Tropico after Railroad Tycoon II. On RT2 the team had re-created an existing game; Tropico was an original idea. The team worked the same way anyway: no unified design, and each person built what he thought the game would be. Halfway through, building art varied wildly in scale and detail. The studio appointed an art producer, but artists still had to sift through and rework art. Tropico shipped after about 2 years with 10 staff. Smith's October 2001 postmortem called it essentially bypassing the design phase.","r":"Team members had to sift through and rework the building art.","re":"Appointed an art producer halfway through to align the artists' work.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Agree on one written picture of the final game before anyone starts building.","i":"On a sequel, the original game is the shared design. An original idea has nothing in that role, so each person fills the gap with his own picture. A working habit from a sequel stops working once the game is new.","a":"Would everyone on the team describe the final game the same way?","q":"“Unfortunately, we failed to realize at the time that everybody was carrying a slightly different picture in his head of what the final game would be.”","qa":"Brent Smith, PopTop Software","st":"Postmortem: Poptop Software's Tropico","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/postmortem-poptop-software-s-i-tropico-i-#:~:text=Unfortunately%2C%20we%20failed%20to%20realize,the%20final%20game%20would%20be","sd":"Oct 2001","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"tropico5","n":"Tropico 5","dv":"Haemimont","y":"2014","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/245620/","ls":"Steam","cv":"tropico5.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Dropped individually simulated citizens from the core of the Tropico 5 redesign","s":"The working prototype 2 months later did not feel like Tropico to the team or to Kalypso. The studio scrapped almost all of it and brought simulated citizens back.","w":"Haemimont chose to build Tropico 5 from scratch in both game code and game design. Early in development the team moved the core mechanic away from individually simulated citizens, to make the game feel fresh, free the economy from single citizens and allow realistic population numbers. Kalypso Media approved. After 2 months the team had a working prototype, but neither the team nor the publisher felt it was Tropico. The team scrapped almost everything and brought back simulated citizens, built smarter. The game shipped on Windows on May 23, 2014. Spasov's 2015 postmortem filed it under what went wrong.","r":"Almost all of the 2-month prototype was scrapped.","re":"Reverted to individually simulated citizens, implemented smarter than before.","co":"2 months of prototype work, almost all scrapped.","nf":false,"l":"On a sequel, prototype any change to the core mechanic and check it still feels like the series.","i":"Players know a series by its core mechanic, so changing it changes what the game is. The loss stayed at about 2 months because a working prototype reached the team and the publisher early.","a":"Does the new core prototype still feel like the series?","q":"“There was a lot of good work thrown away, but when you are making a sequel you can’t afford to make a product that doesn’t feel true to its predecessors.”","qa":"Boian Spasov, Haemimont Games","st":"Starting From Scratch: Haemimont Games’ Tropico 5 Postmortem","su":"https://www.haemimontgames.com/starting-from-scratch-haemimont-games-tropico-5-postmortem/#:~:text=There%20was%20a%20lot%20of,true%20to%20its%20predecessors","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Planned the studio's first PS4 version from its Xbox 360 experience, starting with 2 devkits","s":"Learning the new platform's code, documentation and certification rules took far longer than expected. The PS4 version shipped last, 11 months after Windows.","w":"Tropico 5 targeted Windows, Linux, OS X, Xbox 360 and PlayStation 4, the studio's first PS4 game. On Xbox 360 the problems were familiar: frame rate and loading times. Because the game already ran there, the team expected PS4 to be easier than it was. Fitting its code to foreign code, checking documentation at every step and learning new certification rules all took far more time than planned. The team started with only 2 devkits. Windows shipped on 23 May 2014, Xbox 360 on 11 November 2014 and PS4 on 28 April 2015.","r":"PS4 work took far longer than the team had planned.","re":"The team kept going and shipped PS4 last, on 28 April 2015.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Plan a first-time console from its certification rules, platform code and devkit count, not from how well the game runs elsewhere.","i":"Performance work carries over between consoles. Platform knowledge does not: certification rules and platform code are learned per platform, and 2 devkits limit how many people can learn at once. A platform the team has already shipped on carries far less of this risk.","a":"Have we estimated certification and platform learning separately for each new console?","q":"“Knowing that the game already runs on Xbox 360, we severely underestimated the challenges of going next-next gen.”","qa":"Boian Spasov, lead designer of Tropico 5 at Haemimont Games","st":"Starting From Scratch: Haemimont Games' Tropico 5 postmortem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/starting-from-scratch-haemimont-games-i-tropico-5-i-postmortem#:~:text=Knowing%20that%20the%20game%20already,challenges%20of%20going%20next%2Dnext%20gen","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built the Tropico 5 alpha with art-free UI mockups and placeholder buildings","s":"Designers settled how menus and gamepad controls should work before any UI art existed. The final interface took less time and fewer resources than on any similar project the studio had made.","w":"Tropico 5 has many complex dialogues and supports both gamepad and mouse. On earlier projects, that UI work had eaten design, art and code time. For the alpha, Haemimont built every dialogue as a quick mockup by a designer and a programmer, with full function and no art. Most of the roughly 100 buildings were coloured boxes that still worked in game. The team changed screens on the fly as mechanics moved. Final UI art started late, once mechanics settled. The game shipped on Windows on 23 May 2014.","r":"The final UI took less time and fewer resources than on similar projects.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Build each screen as a working art-free mockup first, and start final UI art only when the mechanics behind it stop changing.","i":"UI art made on top of unsettled mechanics gets redrawn every time a mechanic changes. A mockup from one designer and one programmer is cheap to throw away. A game whose screens must sell its look at early demos needs some final art sooner.","a":"Are we drawing final UI art for mechanics that might still change?","q":"“We created this interface faster and using less resources than in any other project of similar scope and we are very happy with the end result.”","qa":"Boian Spasov, lead designer of Tropico 5 at Haemimont Games","st":"Starting From Scratch: Haemimont Games' Tropico 5 postmortem","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/starting-from-scratch-haemimont-games-i-tropico-5-i-postmortem#:~:text=We%20created%20this%20interface%20faster,very%20happy%20with%20the%20end%20result","sd":"Mar 2015","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"udrawgametabletandudrawsoftwareline","n":"uDraw GameTablet (Xbox 360 / PlayStation 3) and uDraw software line","dv":"THQ","y":"2011","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Planned uDraw tablets for Xbox 360 and PS3 as a top-3 driver of holiday 2011","s":"Sales were far weaker than anticipated, costing about $33M of operating income in the December 2011 quarter. THQ stopped making uDraw, quit kids' licensed games and lost $239.9M for the fiscal year.","w":"On 2 November 2011 THQ said it was planning for the largest quarter in its history, driven by Saints Row: The Third, the uDraw GameTablet and WWE '12, with uDraw on Xbox 360, PlayStation 3 and Wii. On 2 February 2012 CEO Brian Farrell reported that uDraw and other kids and casual titles had sold far weaker than anticipated. Lower uDraw revenue, inventory reserves, price protection and retail concessions cost about $33.0M of operating income; the quarter's net loss was $55.9M. THQ dropped uDraw hardware and kids' licensed games. On 15 May 2012 it reported a $239.9M fiscal-year loss.","r":"About $33M off operating income in one quarter; uDraw hardware line ended.","re":"Stopped uDraw manufacturing, exited kids' licensed games and cut about $160M in annual costs.","co":"About $33.0M operating income impact; $55.9M quarterly net loss.","nf":false,"l":"Before porting a hit peripheral to a new platform, test whether that platform's audience wants it at all.","i":"Betting a record quarter on a peripheral never tried on those consoles left no room for a miss; the hardware inventory, price protection and retail concessions turned weak sales into a $33M hit. THQ itself never said which part of the plan failed.","a":"Have we tested this product with the new platform's audience?","q":"“Sales of the uDraw GameTablet and related software, and other titles in the kids, family and casual category were far weaker than anticipated, substantially reducing our financial results for the quarter.”","qa":"Brian Farrell, President and Chief Executive Officer of THQ","st":"THQ REPORTS FISCAL 2012 THIRD QUARTER RESULTS","su":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/865570/000086557012000007/a12312011ex991.htm#:~:text=Sales%20of%20the%20uDraw%20GameTablet,results%20for%20the%20quarter","sd":"Feb 2012","sy":"Company report","w8":4}]},{"k":"ultimaix","n":"Ultima IX","dv":"Origin","y":"1999","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.gog.com/en/game/ultima_9_ascension","ls":"GOG","cv":"ultimaix.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Built the 3D Ultima IX on the old Crusader and Ultima 8 code","s":"The restarted team carried heavy tech debt in a codebase too big to manage, and features nobody knew about surfaced only in QA.","w":"Ultima IX first shared a refined Ultima 8 engine with Crusader and used 2D bitmaps. At some point Origin switched it to full 3D and dropped the bitmap version, but kept building on the Crusader and Ultima 8 code. The project was then cancelled and its staff moved to Ultima Online. A 3dfx demo revived it, and Bill Randolph joined as lead programmer around summer 1997. Direct3D support took just over a year, and old features turned up only in QA. The game shipped in 1999. In 2014, Randolph said they should have started from scratch.","r":"The team worked in code too big for anyone to fully manage.","re":"The team cut features such as the party system and ship combat.","co":"Just over a year of Direct3D work; party system and ship combat cut.","nf":false,"l":"At a restart, decide on purpose whether to keep the old code, and write down the tech debt you take on with it.","i":"Each milestone added to code that already worked, so no single step justified a rewrite, and the debt grew. Randolph says the team lacked the clout to ask for a clean start. Old code pays off more when the camera and renderer stay the same.","a":"If we started today, would we choose this codebase again?","q":"“Well…I will say that the short answer is: absolutely yes, we should have started from scratch.”","qa":"Bill Randolph, lead programmer on Ultima IX at Origin Systems","st":"“An Inertia of Legend” — An Interview with Bill Randolph","su":"https://ultimacodex.com/interviews/an-inertia-of-legend-an-interview-with-bill-randolph/#:~:text=I%20will%20say%20that%20the%20short,should%20have%20started%20from%20scratch","sd":"Nov 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"ultimaonline","n":"Ultima Online","dv":"Origin Systems","y":"1997","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://www.ea.com/games/ultima/ultima-online","ls":"Website","cv":"ultimaonline.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Sized the world for 300,000 players, 10 times EA's sales forecast","s":"The game sold over 1 million units in its first month or two. Players had to be split across shards, and systems borrowed from text MUDs broke at that scale.","w":"In 1995 EA's sales team projected 15,000 and then 30,000 lifetime units. On the third pitch, a year after the first, Origin got approval to go about $250,000 over budget for a prototype that let about 100 people play at once. The team built the world 10 times bigger than EA's number, expecting at least 300,000 sales. After the September 1997 launch it sold over 1 million units in the first month or two. One world became many shards. Open player killing cost well over half the users, and 7 complete redesigns of the PvP systems followed.","r":"Real sales were over 3 times what the world was built for.","re":"Split players across shards and redesigned the PvP systems 7 times.","co":"Well over half the users lost to open PvP; 7 PvP system redesigns.","nf":false,"l":"Decide in pre-production what happens when players exceed your most optimistic forecast: how you split the world and how griefing rules scale.","i":"Social rules from small text MUDs relied on peer pressure, and that fell apart with a million strangers. The risk is specific to shared worlds: in a single-player game, more buyers than planned costs nothing in design.","a":"What breaks first if 3 times more players show up than planned?","q":"“I didn't want this fiction where the two worlds were unrelated to each other, so I invented the term 'shards.'”","qa":"Richard Garriott, co-founder of Origin Systems","st":"Classic Game Postmortem: Ultima Online","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lnnsDi7Sxq0&t=1511s","sd":"Mar 2018","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"qu":"https://gamersnexus.net/gg/1891-where-mmorpgs-come-richard-garriott-interview"},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Designed each server for about 250 concurrent players, like the MUDs of the day","s":"When 50,000 people signed up to test, the team was asked to make servers far bigger. The whole game was rearchitected around new multiserver tech between 1996 and 1997.","w":"Rick Delashmit built an early prototype from February to September 1995, and the core team arrived that autumn. Like Meridian 59 and other MUD-style games of the time, UO was designed for about 250 players at once, with a lifetime forecast of about 30,000 units. Multiple servers were planned from the start. After 50,000 people signed up to test, the team was asked to make servers much bigger. Delashmit invented multiserver tech that shared the map across clusters of machines, and the whole game was rearchitected for it between 1996 and 1997. UO launched on 26 September 1997.","r":"The whole game was rearchitected mid-development for clustered servers.","re":"Rick Delashmit built multiserver tech that shared the map across machine clusters.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before production, test whether the server design can scale well past the forecast, so a demand surprise does not force a rewrite.","i":"Players per server decides how the map, objects and network are split, so raising it later touches every system. Here the warning came from tester sign-ups, not the forecast, and it came early enough to rebuild before launch.","a":"Could our server design handle far more players than we forecast?","q":"“UO was originally designed for a concurrency around 250, much like Meridian 59 and other MUD heirs of the day.”","qa":"Raph Koster, original lead designer on Ultima Online","st":"Did players destroy the UO ecology?","su":"https://www.raphkoster.com/games/snippets/did-players-destroy-the-uo-ecology/#:~:text=UO%20was%20originally%20designed%20for,MUD%20heirs%20of%20the%20day","sd":"Aug 2024","sy":"Participant account","w8":4},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Built the online game with no plan for accounts, billing or keycodes","s":"When the $5 paid beta drew 50,000 sign-ups, the live producer had to improvise keycodes, borrowed billing and prepaid game-time cards before launch.","w":"A core team of 8 to 12 people built Ultima Online in 2 years and 3 months. To pay for beta discs, Origin charged testers $5, and 50,000 people signed up within days. EA then moved the Ultima 9 team and its own middle managers onto the game, which grew to about 50 people. Live producer Rich Vogel found no service component at all. He generated keycodes by moving a mouse, used Mplayer's registration and billing, and sold 60 and 90 day game-time cards for Europe. It launched in September 1997. At GDC 2018 Vogel called it very rushed.","r":"Keycodes, billing and game-time cards were improvised under EA's launch deadline.","re":"Vogel used Mplayer's registration and billing until Origin's own systems were running.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"List every service system your online game needs to sell and run, such as accounts, billing and keys, and schedule each in pre-production.","i":"In the mid 1990s boxed games had no customer accounts, so the service side had no owner until the beta proved demand. The gap matters for any game that sells access or runs live billing; a boxed single-player game of that era had none.","a":"Who owns accounts, billing and key generation in our schedule?","q":"“So one of the big things that when I came on board I realized is they had no they had a game but not the infrastructure to launch it.”","qa":"Rich Vogel, live producer of Ultima Online at Origin Systems","st":"Classic Game Postmortem: Ultima Online","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lnnsDi7Sxq0&t=1239s","sd":"Mar 2018","sy":"Participant talk","w8":3,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lnnsDi7Sxq0&t=1232s"}]},{"k":"uncharted4","n":"Uncharted 4","dv":"Naughty Dog","y":"2016","sc":"AAA","se":"Port 2022","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1659420/","ls":"Steam","cv":"uncharted4.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Kept late story sequences in Uncharted 4 and moved the release past 2015","s":"Naughty Dog shipped its longest and most location-varied Uncharted on 10 May 2016. Cutting the unfinished sequences would have put the pacing and story at risk.","w":"Naughty Dog built Uncharted 4 as Nathan Drake's last chapter: the longest game in the series, with the most varied locations. When the team realised it would miss 2015, several sequences were only just coming online. Cutting them and stitching the game together was an option, but it risked hurting pacing and story. In September 2015, creative director Neil Druckmann told OPM that meeting the fall 2015 date would have meant cutting scope and ambition. The release moved to March 2016, then slipped again. The game came out on 10 May 2016.","r":"The release slipped from fall 2015 to May 2016.","re":"Naughty Dog finished the late sequences instead of cutting them.","co":"Release moved from fall 2015 to 10 May 2016.","nf":false,"l":"Decide early which sequences the story cannot lose, and plan the date around finishing those first.","i":"Sequences that come online last are the hardest to cut, because the story around them is already built. Druckmann treated the series finale as a one-time chance and accepted the delay. A team that cannot move its date has to set these priorities sooner.","a":"Can our story survive losing the sequences that come online last?","q":"“Everything we put in there we felt was necessary to tell the story.”","qa":"Neil Druckmann, creative director at Naughty Dog","st":"Uncharted 4 Interview: Naughty Dog on Crafting a Legendary Ending","su":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/uncharted-4-interview-naughty-dog-on-crafting-a-le/1100-6437139/#:~:text=Everything%20we%20put%20in%20there,necessary%20to%20tell%20the%20story","sd":"Apr 2016","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"uncharted","n":"Uncharted: Drake's Fortune","dv":"Naughty Dog","y":"2007","sc":"AAA","se":"Remaster 2015","lk":"https://www.playstation.com/en-us/games/uncharted-the-nathan-drake-collection/","ls":"PlayStation","cv":"uncharted.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Built an all-new asset system, build tool and shader editor for the first PS3 game","s":"The tools tried to fix every old problem, and a way to play levels in a build came late. The June 2006 E3 teaser exposed the problems, and production ran behind from then on.","w":"Naughty Dog began its first PlayStation 3 game at the end of 2004 and focused early on the engine, rendering pipeline and tools. Partly to share technology, it built a new asset-management system, its first GUI build tool and a shader and material editor. The tools tried to solve every problem anyone had had, and a pipeline to run and play levels came very late. In June 2006, halfway through, making the first E3 teaser exposed the problems. The team returned to its Jak and Daxter methods for many tools. Uncharted shipped in November 2007, after production played catch-up throughout.","r":"Levels became playable late, and production played catch-up to the end.","re":"The team went back to its Jak and Daxter methods for many tools.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Get one level running and playable in a build first, and add new tool features only after that pipeline works.","i":"Tools that try to fix every past complaint take the longest to finish, and level builders wait on them. The lesson is about order, not ambition: an ambitious shared toolset can work once people can already play levels.","a":"Can our designers run and play a level in today's build?","q":"“To make it worse, we'd gotten distracted by our lofty aspirations, and had left it very late to implement a build pipeline that let people actually run and play levels.”","qa":"Neil Druckmann, game designer at Naughty Dog","st":"Postmortem: Naughty Dog's Uncharted: Drake's Fortune","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-naughty-dog-s-i-uncharted-drake-s-fortune-i-#:~:text=To%20make%20it%20worse%2C%20we'd,run%20and%20play%20levels","sd":"Oct 2008","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"unchartedgoldenabyss","n":"Uncharted: Golden Abyss","dv":"Sony Bend Studio","y":"2011","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Story & world","t":"Locked the script before core production, after spending all of 2010 on story drafts","s":"The locked script let Bend capture over 200 pages in 12 days. The story turned out too big, the lock left little room to cut it, and the team crunched through 2011.","w":"Bend began story work in 2009. A Drake origin story was scrapped after a few months, partly because Naughty Dog was uneasy about filling in Drake's backstory. An open-world hub draft in January 2010 lasted a couple of months. The team spent all of 2010 iterating and started casting in December. With the script locked, 6 shoots from January to May 2011 captured over 200 pages. The game proved too big, and the script left little to cut. The team crunched from January to December 2011. Garvin later wished the story had been locked a year earlier.","r":"Performance capture took 12 days, but the scope could not be trimmed later.","re":"Bend took steps to protect the team's work and life balance after 2011.","co":"Crunch from January to December 2011; over 158 overtime meals ordered.","nf":false,"l":"Before you lock the script, cost the levels and cinematics it needs against your team, because a locked script is hard to trim.","i":"A fixed script lets mocap, asset and support teams plan ahead, which is how capture fit into 12 days. The same lock ties every level to story beats, so cutting scope after it breaks pacing.","a":"Can our team build every level this script needs by the date?","q":"“I wonder, with some regret, how much easier development would have been had we locked the story a year earlier.”","qa":"John Garvin, writer and director at Sony Bend Studio","st":"Postmortem: Sony Bend Studio's Uncharted: Golden Abyss","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-sony-bend-studio-s-i-uncharted-golden-abyss-i-#:~:text=I%20wonder%2C%20with%20some%20regret,story%20a%20year%20earlier","sd":"Nov 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Kept the Vita Uncharted to a few locations instead of a globetrotting tour","s":"Fewer new texture sets and models let a small team aim at the Uncharted 2 visual bar on Vita. The team later called its environment process one of the big successes.","w":"Sony Bend entered preproduction in 2009, just as Uncharted 2 hit beta and set a new visual bar. Every new locale would need its own texture sets and models, and the team lacked the time and resources. Principal artist Francois Gilbert's team focused on a few locations, with Uncharted 2 as the quality bar and Drake's Fortune as the location reference. Naughty Dog shared its full Uncharted texture library, and levels were built about 80% from reusable blocks. The game shipped on schedule with the PS Vita; the November 2012 postmortem called environments a big success.","r":"Environment production became one of the project's big success stories.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you approve each new location, count the texture sets and models it needs, and cut locations to fit.","i":"Each new locale needs its own texture sets and models, so locations multiply art cost faster than levels do. The cap did not stop scope growth: the game still had 34 levels and the rainforest world nearly doubled in production.","a":"How many unique texture sets does our location list need?","q":"“We quickly realized we couldn't be a globetrotting Uncharted.”","qa":"Francois Gilbert, principal artist at Sony Bend","st":"Postmortem: Sony Bend Studio's Uncharted: Golden Abyss","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/postmortem-sony-bend-studio-s-i-uncharted-golden-abyss-i-#:~:text=We%20quickly%20realized%20we%20couldn%27t,be%20a%20globetrotting%20Uncharted","sd":"Nov 2012","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"undeadclashmagicaldistrictfishingmasters","n":"Undead Clash, Magical District and Fishing Masters (Ten Square Games)","dv":"Ten Square Games","y":"2023","sc":"AA","se":"Cancelled 2023","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Greenlit new mobile titles outside the company's hobby and simulation niche","s":"All 3 projects were closed within 6 weeks in 2023. Ten Square Games wrote off PLN 40.2 million of capitalised development costs and cut about 120 jobs, a quarter of its people.","w":"Ten Square Games had 5 titles in production by March 2022, with Undead Clash and Magical District in testing in the American market. Marketing tests in late 2022 led to a gameplay change in Undead Clash that discarded earlier work. On 9 March 2023 the board wrote off PLN 9.2 million on Magical District and PLN 5.3 million on Undead Clash. On 17 April 2023 it suspended Undead Clash and Fishing Masters, wrote off another PLN 25.7 million and began laying off about 120 people, about 25% of its workforce, to refocus on its 3 core titles.","r":"PLN 40.2 million written off and about 120 jobs cut, 25% of the company.","re":"Refocused on Fishing Clash, Hunting Clash and Wings of Heroes in the hobby niche.","co":"PLN 40.2 million of capitalised development written off; about 120 jobs.","nf":false,"l":"Before greenlighting a title outside your proven niche, agree the test results that would kill it and the spend cap.","i":"The board's own reading: its strength is the hobby and simulation niche, and the dropped titles were attempts to leave it. The report also blames the weak economy and an unstable mobile market, so genre alone does not explain the write-off.","a":"Which of our projects is outside our proven niche, and what result kills it?","q":"“The Undead Clash project was an attempt to go beyond the niche in which the Company specializes.”","qa":"Ten Square Games management board, signed by Maciej Zużałek, CEO, and Magdalena Jurewicz, board member","st":"TEN SQUARE GAMES S.A.: Decyzja o skupieniu się na rozwoju głównych produktów Grupy i dopasowaniu struktury zatrudnienia do obecnej sytuacji i planów Spółki","su":"https://www.bankier.pl/wiadomosc/TEN-SQUARE-GAMES-S-A-Decyzja-o-skupieniu-sie-na-rozwoju-glownych-produktow-Grupy-i-dopasowaniu-struktury-zatrudnienia-do-obecnej-sytuacji-i-planow-Spolki-8524062.html#:~:text=The%20Undead%20Clash%20project%20was,which%20the%20Company%20specializes","sd":"Apr 2023","sy":"Company report","w8":4}]},{"k":"unpacking","n":"Unpacking","dv":"Witch Beam","y":"2021","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1135690/","ls":"Steam","cv":"unpacking.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Spent the 2-month Stugan accelerator polishing one kitchen instead of 3 or 4 rough levels","s":"The kitchen became the first demo for publishers and Day of the Devs. It also let Wren Brier estimate 2.5 years of art for the full game.","w":"Wren Brier and Tim Dawson arrived at the Stugan accelerator in Sweden in 2018 with a kitchen prototype of about a dozen items. Seven of the final 8 stages were already planned, and the idea was to rough out 3 or 4 of them there. They spent all 2 months on the kitchen instead and left with about 70 items. A GIF of it went viral and brought publishers, including Humble. The kitchen became the first demo, and Brier used it to estimate the art. Unpacking shipped in November 2021, after slipping past a mid 2020 target.","r":"The team got a publisher demo and a 2.5-year art estimate.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"2 months on 1 room instead of 3 or 4 rough levels.","nf":false,"l":"Finish one level to shipping quality early, and time it to estimate the art for the whole game.","i":"A finished room shows the real cost per item and per space; rough levels hide it. Unpacking repeats one kind of space many times, so one room scales well. A game with very different levels needs a finished slice of each kind.","a":"Have we timed one finished level to estimate the full art schedule?","q":"“We went from wanting to make half the game to a rough point to doing one room to vertical-slice level.”","qa":"Wren Brier, Witch Beam","st":"The making of Unpacking: Developer Witch Beam on bringing the moving indie hit to life","su":"https://www.gamesradar.com/the-making-of-unpacking-developer-witch-beam-on-bringing-the-moving-indie-hit-to-life/#:~:text=We%20went%20from%20wanting%20to%20make,one%20room%20to%20vertical%2Dslice%20level","sd":"Jan 2023","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"unreal","n":"Unreal","dv":"Epic","y":"1998","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/13250/","ls":"Steam","cv":"unreal.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Started a full 3D game in 1994 after years of 1 to 3 person projects","s":"The team grew to about 20 people, and Epic and Digital Extremes spent all their past profits plus credit card balances. Sweeney said 6 more months of delay would probably have ended Epic.","w":"Around 1994 Tim Sweeney decided Epic could compete with id's 3D games. Unreal began with James Schmalz writing a 3D game and Sweeney its editor. Epic's games had been made by 1 to 3 people, and Unreal grew to about 20 by the time it shipped, with no one experienced in managing a project that size. In 1997 progress lagged because the team was spread out, so everyone moved to Waterloo, Canada, for a year to finish. In 2009 Sweeney said the game took all profits from earlier games, and 6 more months of delay would probably have ended Epic.","r":"Both studios spent all their earlier profits on one game.","re":"In 1997 the spread-out team moved to Waterloo for a year to finish.","co":"All profits from earlier games plus credit card balances; team grew to about 20.","nf":false,"l":"Before you greenlight a project several times bigger than anything you shipped, work out how many months of delay your cash can absorb.","i":"A jump from 3-person to 20-person projects adds management work nobody on the team had done, and it ties all the cash to one release date. It paid off here; the same bet with a 6-month slip would probably have closed Epic.","a":"How many months of slip can our cash cover?","q":"“If the game had been delayed by another six months, Epic probably wouldn't be here now.”","qa":"Tim Sweeney, founder of Epic Games","st":"From The Past To The Future: Tim Sweeney Talks","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/from-the-past-to-the-future-tim-sweeney-talks#:~:text=If%20the%20game%20had%20been%20delayed,be%20here%20now","sd":"May 2009","sy":"Participant interview","w8":5}]},{"k":"unrealtournament","n":"Unreal Tournament","dv":"Epic Games","y":"1999","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Turned an Unreal multiplayer add-on on the existing engine into a standalone game","s":"With no engine to write, about 16 developers shipped Unreal Tournament in 18 months for $2M and it won more than 5 Game of the Year awards. It still launched in November 1999, close to Quake 3.","w":"In early summer 1998 Epic began an official add-on to Unreal on the existing engine. In November 1998, after meetings with publisher GT Interactive, Mark Rein suggested making it a separate game; the team at first opposed the idea. With no engine to write, the team spent its time on improvements, and 90% of the gameplay code was UnrealScript. There was no strong initial design: features came from spur-of-the-moment decisions, while contract renegotiations with GT dragged on. The game hit shelves in November 1999, close to Quake 3, after 18 months, $2M and about 16 developers.","r":"Shipped in 18 months for $2M with about 16 developers.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"$2M, 18 months, about 16 developers.","nf":false,"l":"Build a new product on the engine you already have, but still write the design document before features pile up.","i":"Reusing a shipped engine turned 18 months into pure gameplay work, which is why so small a team could ship. The missing design document and the contract fights still cost time: the author believes an October release would have sold much more.","a":"Could this ship on our current engine, with a design document?","q":"“We didn't have to focus on writing an engine from scratch, so we were free to focus entirely on improvements.”","qa":"Brandon Reinhart, programmer at Epic Games","st":"Postmortem: Epic Games' Unreal Tournament","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/postmortem-epic-games-i-unreal-tournament-i-#:~:text=We%20didn%27t%20have%20to%20focus,entirely%20on%20improvements","sd":"Jun 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"valkyriachronicles","n":"Valkyria Chronicles","dv":"Sega","y":"2008","sc":"AA","se":"Port 2014","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/294860/","ls":"Steam","cv":"valkyriachronicles.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Scrapped the first top-down tactics build for third-person action with 3D maps","s":"Playing the first build showed it did not make players identify with the soldiers. The small team changed the battle system and made every map 3D with verticality in one go.","w":"Sega's team chose guns over swords and magic early, so it rethought the whole logic of tactical combat. The first build was a regular tactical RPG on a top-down map, without a grid. When the team played it, no one was happy: it did not make players identify with the characters or feel their loss. Producer Ryutaro Nonaka's team moved to a system close to third-person action. All maps had to become 3D with verticality, and every change went in at once. By November 2008 the PS3 game had a very positive critical reception.","r":"The battle system and all maps were redone in one change.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Play the first build against the feeling you want players to have, and change the core before map work multiplies.","i":"The camera and combat model decide what every map must support, so changing them later means redoing every map. Catching it in the first build kept the rework to one change; a small team that knew the whole game made that possible.","a":"Does our first build already give players the feeling we promised?","q":"“But then after we played that initial build we realized that it didn't express what we wanted to communicate in the game, which was having the player identify with the characters in the game.”","qa":"Ryutaro Nonaka, producer of Valkyria Chronicles at Sega","st":"Emotions And War: The Valkyria Chronicles Interview","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/emotions-and-war-the-valkyria-chronicles-interview#:~:text=But%20then%20after%20we%20played,the%20characters%20in%20the%20game","sd":"Nov 2008","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"vampiresurvivors","n":"Vampire Survivors","dv":"poncle","y":"2022","sc":"Solo","se":"Early Access 2021","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1794680/","ls":"Steam","cv":"vampiresurvivors.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Prototyped a Magic Survival clone with an old asset pack and no set vision","s":"Total spend on illustrations and sounds stayed at £1,100, so Galante priced the game at $2.99. He hoped for up to 200 players; by March 2022 it averaged about 25,000 a day.","w":"Around the start of 2021, Luca Galante, who made games in his spare time, built a Phaser prototype that played exactly like the Android game Magic Survival. He was ready to drop it, then swapped the placeholder sprites for a Castlevania-style asset pack he had bought years before. He kept building on weekends, adding whatever made it fun. It went to itch.io in March 2021 and to Steam Early Access on 17 December 2021 at $2.99. Peak concurrent players jumped from 12 in December to 50,847 in January 2022, and Galante left a new job after a week.","r":"Asset spending stayed at £1,100 for illustrations and sounds.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"£1,100 on illustrations and sounds, plus months of spare-time weekends.","nf":false,"l":"Before you commission any art, prove the fun with a cheap prototype built on assets you already own.","i":"The bought assets gave the prototype a look Galante loved, so he kept going, and low costs let him price at $2.99. This fits a small arcade loop; a story or IP game needs its own art direction tested much earlier.","a":"Can we test our core loop with assets we already own?","q":"“I just put elements in to try and make things fun.”","qa":"Luca Galante, developer at poncle","st":"Vampire Survivors creator 'didn't have a vision' when he started making the game that allowed him to quit his job","su":"https://www.pcgamer.com/uk/vampire-survivors-creator-didnt-have-a-vision-when-he-started-making-the-game-that-allowed-him-to-quit-his-job/#:~:text=I%20just%20put%20elements%20in,make%20things%20fun","sd":"Mar 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"vampirethemasqueradebloodlines2","n":"Vampire: The Masquerade - Bloodlines 2","dv":"The Chinese Room","y":"2025","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/532790/","ls":"Steam","cv":"vampirethemasqueradebloodlines2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Greenlit a role-playing sequel outside Paradox's core areas, built by an outside studio","s":"The first developer was replaced, the game shipped more than 5 years after its March 2020 date, and weak sales forced a MSEK 355 write-down.","w":"Bloodlines 2 was first planned for March 2020, with Hardsuit Labs developing. Paradox announced a delay in Q3 2020. In February 2021 it said Hardsuit would no longer lead development and the game would not launch that year. CEO Fredrik Wester later said Paradox ran a long review and was prepared to close production, until a new partner's pitch convinced it. The Chinese Room rebuilt the game on a new code base and reused a significant amount of art and level design. It launched on 21 October 2025. A month later sales missed projections and Paradox wrote down MSEK 355.","r":"Shipped more than 5 years late, and sales missed projections.","re":"Paradox wrote down MSEK 355 and moved capital to its core segments.","co":"MSEK 355 written down; more than 5 years of delay.","nf":false,"l":"Before you greenlight a genre you have never published, get comparable titles and an outside expert to check the sales forecast.","i":"A publisher forecasts sales from the games it knows best, and this one sat outside them. Wester tied the genre only to the forecast; Paradox never said why the first developer's version failed.","a":"Which comparable titles back the sales forecast for this game?","q":"“The game is outside of our core areas, in hindsight it is clear that this has made it difficult for us to gauge sales.”","qa":"Fredrik Wester, CEO of Paradox Interactive","st":"Paradox Interactive AB writes down MSEK 355 of capitalised development costs for Vampire: The Masquerade - Bloodlines 2","su":"https://www.paradoxinteractive.com/media/press-releases/press-release/paradox-interactive-ab-writes-down-msek-355-of-capitalised-development-costs-for-vampire-the-masquerade-bloodlines-2#:~:text=The%20game%20is%20outside%20of,us%20to%20gauge%20sales","sd":"Nov 2025","sy":"Company statement","w8":4}]},{"k":"vampirethemasquerade","n":"Vampire: The Masquerade - Redemption","dv":"Nihilistic","y":"2000","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/559680/","ls":"Steam","cv":"vampirethemasquerade.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Let artists and level designers pick their own tools over one shared package","s":"Level designers built levels in QERadiant before an engine existed, and artists used Maya. Three designers made more than 100 levels and four artists nearly 1,500 models in 24 months.","w":"Nihilistic started Vampire around April 1998, with a staff that grew to 12 and a $1.8 million budget. When mapping the project, the programmers wanted 3D Studio Max for both art and levels, to share assets and pay for one cheap tool. The art and design leads objected, and each department got its own tool. Level designers who knew QERadiant from Quake mission packs started building levels at once, and artists used Maya. The game shipped in June 2000, and lead programmer Robert Huebner called this choice the biggest time saver of the project.","r":"Level building started at once instead of waiting months for an editor.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Let each department lead pick the tool that gets their people building on day one, and price their time against the licence.","i":"A tool the team already knows produces content before the engine exists, and its files re-export as the engine changes. The gain came from that fit: these designers knew QERadiant and its data stayed generic, so more tools are not better by themselves.","a":"Can every department start building content on day one with its tools?","q":"“This single decision probably accounted for more time saved than any other.”","qa":"Robert Huebner, co-founder and lead programmer at Nihilistic Software","st":"Nihilistic Software's Vampire: The Masquerade -- Redemption","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/nihilistic-software-s-i-vampire-the-masquerade----redemption-i-#:~:text=This%20single%20decision%20probably%20accounted,than%20any%20other","sd":"Aug 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Prototyped the engine on 3dfx Glide and put off Direct3D support","s":"The game was still Glide-only at E3 1999. The late switch to Direct3D threw away most of a custom texture manager and forced re-exports of hundreds of levels and models.","w":"When Nihilistic began its Nod engine in 1998, the programmers picked 3dfx's Glide as a first API, planning to move to a general one once the engine was stable. Once it ran, attention turned to gameplay, and at E3 1999 the game was still basically Glide-only. Glide's low-level access led the team to write its own texture manager, and most of it was discarded in the switch to Direct3D. Data structures changed too, so hundreds of levels and models were re-exported. In August 2000 Huebner wrote that the switch should have come several months earlier.","r":"Engine architecture changed late, when it should have been locked.","re":"Switched to Direct3D late and re-exported the affected levels and models.","co":"Most of the texture manager discarded; hundreds of levels and models re-exported.","nf":false,"l":"Move to the final graphics API before content production starts, and test it on the hardware you plan to ship on.","i":"A prototype API that works removes any pressure to switch, while code and data formats keep adapting to its quirks. Prototyping on a narrow API was fine; leaving the switch without a date is what made it expensive.","a":"Have we set a date to move off the prototype API?","q":"“In retrospect, we should have switched to Direct3D or OpenGL several months earlier in the development schedule.”","qa":"Robert Huebner, co-founder and lead programmer at Nihilistic Software","st":"Nihilistic Software's Vampire: The Masquerade -- Redemption","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/nihilistic-software-s-i-vampire-the-masquerade----redemption-i-#:~:text=In%20retrospect%2C%20we%20should%20have%20switched,earlier%20in%20the%20development%20schedule","sd":"Aug 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5},{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Made pathfinding good enough for demos and left the real fix for later","s":"By the time the team came back to it, more than 100 levels were done and a proper fix could not be retrofitted. Weeks of small patches only downgraded the worst bugs.","w":"Early in development, Huebner flagged pathfinding for characters of different sizes in free-form 3D levels as one of the hardest problems he had faced. He made it good enough for early milestones and demos and planned to deal with it later. Level designers had only walkable floor flags, with no marking of walls, cliffs or other hazards. When the problem came back, walk boxes or walk zones would have taken too long to fit into more than 100 finished levels. The team spent weeks on small fixes, and the game shipped in June 2000.","r":"The game shipped with pathfinding bugs reduced, not fixed.","re":"Spent weeks on small fixes that hid the worst pathfinding errors.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before level production, agree navigation rules and editor tags with level designers, and test pathfinding on a finished level.","i":"Navigation depends on how levels are built, so every finished level locks in the current solution. Putting off a hard system is safe only when its fix does not touch content; pathfinding touches every level.","a":"Have level designers got navigation rules before they build final levels?","q":"“We should have tackled this problem much earlier, before the levels were near completion.”","qa":"Robert Huebner, co-founder and lead programmer at Nihilistic Software","st":"Nihilistic Software's Vampire: The Masquerade -- Redemption","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/nihilistic-software-s-i-vampire-the-masquerade----redemption-i-#:~:text=We%20should%20have%20tackled%20this,levels%20were%20near%20completion","sd":"Aug 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":4}]},{"k":"vampirethemasqueradebloodlines","n":"Vampire: The Masquerade – Bloodlines","dv":"Troika","y":"2004","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2600/","ls":"Steam","cv":"vampirethemasqueradebloodlines.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Added a multiplayer component early to a game conceived as single-player","s":"Half-Life 2's multiplayer code was still early and took more work than Troika expected. The team dropped the mode and shipped a single-player game.","w":"Troika conceived Bloodlines as a single-player RPG on Valve's Half-Life 2 engine. Early in development, the team decided a multiplayer component would be cool to add. Half-Life 2's multiplayer code was still in its early stages, and working with it took more effort than Troika had planned. The team eventually dropped multiplayer and focused only on the single-player game. Bloodlines shipped on 18 November 2004. On 30 November 2004, CEO Leonard Boyarsky explained the cut in a post-release interview with GameBanshee.","r":"Multiplayer was cut, and the game shipped as single-player only.","re":"The team put all its effort into the single-player game.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you promise a multiplayer mode, get a small networked build running on the engine version you actually have.","i":"Troika planned the mode on another studio's multiplayer code while that code was still early, so the work could not be estimated. On an engine whose multiplayer has already shipped in other games, the risk is much smaller.","a":"Does multiplayer run in our engine build today?","q":"“However, working with HL-2's multiplayer code in its early stages proved to be more work than we had anticipated, so we eventually decided to focus solely on creating the best single player experience.”","qa":"Leonard Boyarsky, CEO and Art Director of Troika Games","st":"Vampire: The Masquerade - Bloodlines Interview","su":"https://www.gamebanshee.com/interviews/28341-vampire-the-masquerade-bloodlines-interview-v15-28341.html#:~:text=However%2C%20working%20with%20HL%2D2%27s%20multiplayer,the%20best%20single%20player%20experience","sd":"Nov 2004","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"vvvvvv","n":"VVVVVV","dv":"Terry Cavanagh","y":"2010","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/70300/","ls":"Steam","cv":"vvvvvv.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Started VVVVVV as a short Flash side game planned to take a few weeks","s":"By the second level it was too big for a Flash sponsor. Cavanagh turned it into his first paid game, and donations kept him afloat before release.","w":"Terry Cavanagh had quit his bank job before he had saved enough. In the second half of 2009 he started VVVVVV as a short side game for the Flash sponsorship model, expecting a few weeks of work. He thought it might be his last game, so he let it grow. By the second level, a couple of months in, it was far too big for a Flash site, and he decided to sell it. Donations of over $1,000 in the first week covered his IGF fees, rent and loan repayments. VVVVVV launched in January 2010.","r":"A few weeks of planned work became months and a paid release.","re":"Cavanagh raised donations for IGF fees, then sold the game.","co":"Months instead of weeks; he was 1 to 2 weeks from borrowing money.","nf":false,"l":"When a side project outgrows its plan, recheck how it will earn money and how long your savings last.","i":"The Flash sponsor model paid for small games, so the business plan broke as soon as the game grew. Thin savings turned a scope change into a cash problem; a funded developer would have faced only the switch to selling.","a":"Does our release model still fit the game we are now building?","q":"“Really, after I'd started working on the second level it was clear that the game was way too big for a flash site.”","qa":"Terry Cavanagh, independent developer, Distractionware","st":"Intervvvvvview: VVVVVV Developer Terry Cavanagh","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/intervvvvvview-i-vvvvvv-i-developer-terry-cavanagh#:~:text=Really%2C%20after%20I%27d%20started%20working,too%20big%20for%20a%20flash%20site","sd":"Jan 2010","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"warcraftiii","n":"Warcraft III","dv":"Blizzard","y":"2002","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Brought the strategy camera down to a near third-person view for the first 3D game","s":"Units were hard to select and the battlefield was hard to read. Progress came only after the team went back to a traditional isometric view.","w":"After StarCraft, Blizzard's Team One wanted Warcraft III to feel different: its first 3D game, with many RPG elements. The team brought the camera down low, and the game became a pseudo-third-person experience. Moving around the map was disorienting. In battle, players could not see enough of the field to select their units. The early prototype tested technology and art rather than gameplay, so the camera was tried once the real 3D engine ran. The team pulled the camera back to an isometric view, and progress started there. Warcraft III shipped in July 2002.","r":"Real design progress began only after the camera returned to isometric.","re":"The team switched to a traditional isometric camera.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you build content, play a large battle with your new camera and check that players can see and select their units.","i":"A strategy game depends on seeing and selecting many units at once, and a low camera takes that away. The team found out only once the real engine and art were running, because its prototype tested technology, not gameplay.","a":"Can players select units in a full battle with this camera?","q":"“Eventually we pulled the camera into a more traditional isometric view and that's when we really started making progress.”","qa":"Rob Pardo, Director of Game Design at Blizzard Entertainment","st":"A Conversation With Rob Pardo","su":"https://flylib.com/books/en/2.489.1.80/1/#:~:text=Eventually%20we%20pulled%20the%20camera,really%20started%20making%20progress","sd":"2003","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"warframe","n":"Warframe","dv":"Digital Extremes","y":"2013","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/230410/","ls":"Steam","cv":"warframe.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Gave a small team 9 months to ship a minimum free-to-play co-op game","s":"The open beta launched in March 2013 with 8 frames. After the Steam launch, the game earned enough to support the 250-person studio, which later stopped taking work-for-hire contracts.","w":"In 2012 Digital Extremes' work-for-hire deals were drying up, and the 250-person studio had a couple million dollars to spend. About a dozen people built a space-ninja demo from old Dark Sector ideas in under 2 months. Free-to-play publishers turned it down, so the studio gave itself 9 months to self-publish. The team built a minimum set of warframes, a back end, payments and support. On 10 January 2013 it removed pay-to-win items after player complaints. The open beta launched in March 2013, and the Steam launch made the game able to carry the studio.","r":"Shipped a playable minimum game and grew it from player feedback.","re":"The team kept adding content in small updates after launch.","co":"A couple million dollars of studio reserves and 9 months.","nf":false,"l":"When money is short, cut the first release to the smallest version players will play and pay for, and fix the date.","i":"A small first release brings real players and real money sooner, and their feedback picks what to build next. It works for a game built to live on updates: DE also built procedural levels so new content could keep coming.","a":"What is the smallest version players would pay for, and when can it ship?","q":"“So that nine months was trying to build as many of the beginning warframes as possible so that we could a minimum set, and then trying to invent an infrastructure for this kind of so-called \"back end,\" because we have MMO lite features and count persistence.”","qa":"Steve Sinclair, who led development on Warframe at Digital Extremes","st":"The Rise of Warframe  (Warframe Doc Part 2)","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NA5vT1LooXk&t=96s","sd":"Mar 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"wasteland2","n":"Wasteland 2","dv":"inXile Entertainment","y":"2014","sc":"AA","se":"Early Access 2013","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/240760/","ls":"Steam","cv":"wasteland2.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Chose Unity over Unreal and bought Asset Store content to start prototyping at once","s":"The team skipped months of placeholder art and started prototyping right after the Kickstarter. MCV reported that the store saved inXile $200,000 in its first few months.","w":"inXile raised $2,933,252 on Kickstarter between March and April 2012. The engine was its first decision: after a tech evaluation, project lead Chris Keenan picked Unity over Unreal. inXile had used Unreal for years, but this team was a quarter of that size, and no large PC game was then being built on Unity. The Asset Store swung the choice, and the team bought items and scripts in bulk. By June 2012 Keenan said the base assets were already in place. Wasteland 2 reached Steam Early Access in December 2013 and shipped in September 2014.","r":"Prototyping started at once instead of after months of placeholder asset work.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Size the engine to the team you have, and buy placeholder assets so prototyping starts in the first weeks.","i":"Bought assets remove the slowest early step: making placeholder art before anything can be played. That holds when store art is good enough to test with; inXile still took a risk on an engine no large PC game had shipped on.","a":"Could we buy our placeholder art and start prototyping this month?","q":"“We could hit the ground running, purchasing assets in bulk that would allow our team to start prototyping immediately.”","qa":"Chris Keenan, project lead at inXile Entertainment","st":"Unity Focus: Bringing life to the Wasteland","su":"https://mcvuk.com/development-news/unity-focus-bringing-life-to-the-wasteland/#:~:text=We%20could%20hit%20the%20ground,start%20prototyping%20immediately","sd":"Oct 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Made a bigger game after Kickstarter brought in triple the planned money","s":"The release date set before the campaign no longer fit the larger plan. Wasteland 2 ran a year late, and Fargo defended the delay as funded scope.","w":"inXile ran the Wasteland 2 Kickstarter between 13 March and 17 April 2012, with a $900,000 goal. Kickstarter asked for a release date up front, before the team knew its budget or the game's size. The campaign raised $2,933,252 from 61,290 backers. Brian Fargo chose to make a bigger, more ambitious game rather than the one planned, and the original date slipped by a year. In an August 2014 interview, weeks before the planned September release, he said he never worried about the extra year.","r":"Release slipped a year past the date promised to backers.","re":"inXile pushed the release back a year to fit the bigger game.","co":"About 12 extra months of development.","nf":false,"l":"When funding grows past plan, set a new date for the bigger scope before you start building it.","i":"Kickstarter asks for a date before anyone knows the budget, so the first date fits a smaller game. Extra money bought scope, and the schedule had to move with it. This works only when the extra money also pays for the extra months.","a":"Does our public date still match the scope we are now funding?","q":"“So I didn't worry about that extra year, I knew we were going to create something twice as big and crazy as what we had originally.”","qa":"Brian Fargo, director and founder of inXile","st":"Wasteland 2 PC Interview","su":"https://www.gamewatcher.com/interviews/wasteland-2-interview/11192#:~:text=So%20I%20didn%27t%20worry%20about%20that,what%20we%20had%20originally","sd":"Aug 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":5}]},{"k":"watchdogs","n":"Watch Dogs","dv":"Ubisoft Montreal","y":"2014","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/243470/","ls":"Steam","cv":"watchdogs.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"External commitments","t":"Revealed Watch Dogs at E3 2012 on a powerful PC before next-gen specs were known","s":"The demo set a visual bar the final console versions had to meet. At launch players accused the game of a graphics downgrade, which a producer later blamed on missing hardware information.","w":"Ubisoft Montreal built Watch Dogs on seventh-generation consoles from the start, since the next consoles were still unknown. CEO Yves Guillemot wanted it revealed at E3 2012, though creative director Jonathan Morin felt that was a bit early. The demo ran on a very powerful PC. The game was due in November 2013 with the new consoles, then slipped to 27 May 2014. Players said lighting, fog and depth of field looked worse than in early videos. In 2016 producer Dominic Guay blamed the gap on missing PS4 and Xbox One information at the reveal.","r":"The public visual bar was set before target hardware was known.","re":"For Watch Dogs 2, the team developed on target platforms from the start.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before a public reveal, label the demo's platform and show only what the minimum target hardware can run.","i":"A reveal fixes a visual bar in players' memory, and the final hardware decides whether you can meet it. Morin still thinks the early reveal was right, so the lesson is about what the demo shows rather than the timing.","a":"Can our minimum target hardware run what this reveal shows?","q":"“Yves was the one who wanted us to go at that E3, even though we felt it was a bit early, and in the end I think he was right.”","qa":"Jonathan Morin, creative director of Watch Dogs at Ubisoft Montreal","st":"Ubisoft Montreal believed E3 2012 was too early to reveal Watch Dogs","su":"https://www.videogamer.com/?p=110668#:~:text=Yves%20was%20the%20one%20who,I%20think%20he%20was%20right","sd":"Mar 2014","sy":"Press report","w8":3}]},{"k":"weedcraftinc","n":"Weedcraft Inc","dv":"Vile Monarch","y":"2019","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/622720/","ls":"Steam","cv":"weedcraftinc.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Closed pre-production with a prototype that had systems but no game loop","s":"Playtesters could not play without a developer beside them. The team simplified every system, hired a UX designer and added scenario goals during production.","w":"Vile Monarch's earlier games were pixel-art titles made in about a year by 3 to 5 people, without a producer. Weedcraft Inc, a 3D cannabis business sim for Devolver Digital, took 2.5 years with a core team of about 15 to 20. Its vision held legislation, logistics, smuggling, police and competition. The prototype that closed pre-production had those systems but no goal or progression, and Devolver accepted it. In playtests, players could not follow the game without a developer helping. The team simplified the systems, hired a UX designer and added scenario-driven goals before the April 2019 release.","r":"Core systems were simplified and progression was designed during production.","re":"The team hired a UX designer and built scenario-based objectives.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Do not exit pre-production until the prototype has a player goal and a progression a playtester can follow alone.","i":"Systems without a goal make a sandbox: testers cannot tell what to do, and the team cannot judge which systems matter. A publisher accepting the prototype is not proof that the loop works.","a":"Can a playtester tell what to aim for without us in the room?","q":"“After the prototype we realized that our game loop it's not really there.”","qa":"Mateusz Osuch, lead programmer at Vile Monarch","st":"Mateusz Osuch - Venture into the Unknown. Weedcraft Inc Production Post-mortem","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-2XssS3pCW4&t=910s","sd":"Jul 2019","sy":"Participant talk","w8":3,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-2XssS3pCW4&t=911s"}]},{"k":"whatremainsofedithfinch","n":"What Remains of Edith Finch","dv":"Giant Sparrow","y":"2017","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/501300/","ls":"Steam","cv":"whatremainsofedithfinch.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Built the game as a collection of short stories so any one could be cut","s":"Stories cut at the prototype stage dropped out cleanly. As the family grew interconnected, later cuts needed repair work across the remaining stories.","w":"Giant Sparrow set What Remains of Edith Finch up as short stories about one family, partly so stories could be dropped to lighten the load. Each story began as a mechanics prototype, with story and characters added later. In December 2015, with 16 people on the team, Ian Dallas said the family's interconnections made any cut need deft repair. Late in development the team cut a whole generation and moved or merged its members into earlier ones. The game shipped in April 2017, and the stories cut at prototype stage had never reached character work.","r":"Late cuts meant moving and merging characters across the family.","re":"Cut family members were moved to earlier generations or merged.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Make the cut decision on a story while it is still a mechanics prototype, before other stories reference it.","i":"A story is cheap to cut while it is only a mechanic; each link to other characters adds repair work. A modular structure stays a scope buffer only while the links are few or the cuts come early.","a":"Which stories can we still cut without rewriting others?","q":"“The feeling was that we could toss things off the back of the bus, if needed, to lighten the load.”","qa":"Ian Dallas, creative director of Giant Sparrow","st":"What Remains of Edith Fitch creators don’t know the destination for their ‘sublime horror’ game","su":"https://gamesbeat.com/sony-game-creator-tells-how-a-haunting-tale-came-about-and-is-still-being-rewritten/#:~:text=The%20feeling%20was%20that%20we%20could,to%20lighten%20the%20load","sd":"Dec 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"wiifit","n":"Wii Fit","dv":"Nintendo","y":"2007","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built a balance game prototype from 2 bathroom scales before designing the board","s":"The test showed that store scales report only 4 or 5 times a second, too slow for a game. A version sending 60 signals a second became a playable prototype within a week.","w":"After the Shigureden museum project, which opened in January 2006, Takao Sawano's group was asked for an accessory like bathroom scales. Inspired by sumo wrestlers who weigh in on two scales, Sawano had two computer-linked scales made into a prototype. Testing showed that store scales measure weight only 4 or 5 times a second, while games process 60 images a second. The software team modified the setup to send 60 signals a second, and a week later had the prototype of the Basic Balance Test. Nintendo then asked its partners to switch the design to two sensors at once.","r":"A playable balance prototype in a week, and a two-sensor board design.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"About 1 week of software work for the first playable prototype.","nf":false,"l":"Measure your input device's update rate against the game's frame rate before you design the hardware or the games.","i":"A bathroom scale only has to settle on one number, so 4 or 5 readings a second is plenty for it. A game needs a fresh reading every frame. Testing the real input exposed the gap before any hardware was built around it.","a":"Does our input hardware report as fast as the game updates?","q":"“We asked our software team to modify the accessory to make it send out 60 signals a second, and a week later they came back with the prototype for the Basic Balance Test that comes up in the beginning of Wii Fit’s Body Test.”","qa":"Takao Sawano, producer of the Wii Balance Board at Nintendo","st":"Iwata Asks: Wii Fit Vol 2, 2. Circles, Octagons and Saucepan Lids","su":"https://nintendo.com/en-za/Iwata-Asks/Iwata-Asks-Wii-Fit/Iwata-Asks-Wii-Fit-Vol-2/2-Circles-Octagons-and-Saucepan-Lids/2-Circles-Octagons-and-Saucepan-Lids-237687.html#:~:text=We%20asked%20our%20software%20team,of%20Wii%20Fit%E2%80%99s%20Body%20Test","sd":"","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"wiisports","n":"Wii Sports","dv":"Nintendo","y":"2006","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Kept Wii Sports to five small sports instead of building a full licensed tennis game","s":"The team needed the game ready with the Wii hardware, so it skipped extra modes, a sports licence and Mario, and spent the time polishing each sport.","w":"Wii Sports began as Wii Remote prototypes at Nintendo EAD: Ota's Tennis, Yamashita's Baseball and a putting game that grew into Golf. The demos piled up unfinished until Shigeru Miyamoto sorted them on one sheet of paper into sports games and family games. That sheet became the real start of Wii Sports. Producer Eguchi chose not to grow Tennis into a stand-alone game with many modes, a Tennis Association licence and Mario. The team wanted the game out with the hardware, so it kept the scale small. Wii Sports came out in Europe on 8 December 2006.","r":"Scope stayed small enough to aim at the hardware launch.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When someone else fixes your date, cut modes and licences first and spend the saved time on polish.","i":"The hardware set the date, so scope was the only thing the team could move. The lesson fits a product whose job is to show one idea fast; a game sold on depth in one mode needs a different plan.","a":"Which modes or licences can we drop to hit the fixed date?","q":"“For that reason, we decided we had to prepare it for release as efficiently as we could, without allowing it to grow excessively large in scale.”","qa":"Katsuya Eguchi, producer of Wii Sports at Nintendo EAD","st":"Iwata Asks: Wii Sports, 1. Ground-breaking Games","su":"https://www.nintendo.co.uk/Iwata-Asks-Wii/Iwata-Asks-Wii-Sports/1-Ground-breaking-Games/1-Ground-breaking-Games-217775.html#:~:text=For%20that%20reason%2C%20we%20decided,grow%20excessively%20large%20in%20scale","sd":"2006","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"wikandthefableofsouls","n":"Wik & The Fable of Souls","dv":"Reflexive Entertainment","y":"2004","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Greenlit a quick prototype whose fun came from its 8-player party mode","s":"The single-player mechanic carried over from it was still not fun at month 3 of a 6-month schedule. The team redesigned it from scratch, scrapped every level built so far and cut all 4 bosses.","w":"Reflexive builds one-day-quick prototypes, and BugEater was picked from about 6 of them for a 6-month project. Its standout was MouseParty, an 8-player mode on one PC. The single-player mechanic, a straight-line jump to snatch bugs, stayed in the full game. By the end of month 3 it was still not fun, so the team redesigned it around gravity and a swinging tongue. Every level built so far was scrapped, balancing took almost 6 weeks, and 4 planned bosses were cut. Wik shipped in September 2004, and producer Simon Hallam wrote the postmortem in February 2005.","r":"Half the schedule went into a weaker game, and its levels were scrapped.","re":"The team rebuilt the mechanic around gravity and tongue swinging, then rebuilt the levels.","co":"Over 3 of 6 months on a discarded mechanic; 4 bosses cut.","nf":false,"l":"Before greenlight, check that the prototype proves the mode you will ship, not only its most fun mode.","i":"Eight people crowding one PC make a simple mechanic fun, so the party mode hid a weak single-player core. The prototype's creator still disputes this. When the party mode is what ships, the same prototype proves what it needs to.","a":"Does our prototype prove the mode we are actually shipping?","q":"“Thanks to the ingenuity and creativity of the team, Wik turned into a product that we are all very proud of, but for more than half of its originally planned development time it was a completely different and much less fun game.”","qa":"Simon Hallam, producer and lead programmer at Reflexive Entertainment","st":"Indie Postmortem: Reflexive's Wik & The Fable Of Souls","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/indie-postmortem-reflexive-s-i-wik-the-fable-of-souls-i-#:~:text=Thanks%20to%20the%20ingenuity%20and,much%20less%20fun%20game","sd":"Feb 2005","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":5}]},{"k":"wild9","n":"Wild 9","dv":"Shiny","y":"1998","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Planned Wild 9 for PlayStation and Saturn with one shared export pipeline","s":"When Shiny dropped the Saturn version, half of the data in the export scripts became obsolete. The two-platform plan still shaped the game's tech to the end.","w":"Wild 9 was first planned for both Sony PlayStation and Sega Saturn. Characters and levels were modeled in 3D Studio Max and exported with Malachy Duffin's in-house plug-in, which he wrote to output both formats, and the export scripts held lines for both consoles. Shiny later dropped the Saturn version. Half of the data in those scripts became obsolete, and only the PlayStation side kept evolving. Wild 9 shipped on PlayStation in October 1998, and programmer Didier Malenfant wrote that the original plan shaped the tech throughout development.","r":"Half of the export-script data became obsolete when Saturn was cut.","re":"Kept developing only the PlayStation side of the export pipeline.","co":"Half of the export-script data became obsolete.","nf":false,"l":"When a second platform is uncertain, keep its export code separate so dropping it removes code instead of leaving dead data in shared scripts.","i":"Shared tools for 2 platforms carry both sets of data until one is cut, and the dead half then sits in every script. The cost here stayed inside the pipeline; the lesson matters most when the second platform is a long shot.","a":"If we drop a platform, can we remove its code cleanly?","q":"“When we dropped the Saturn version, half of the data in these scripts became obsolete; from that point on, only the PlayStation side continued to evolve.”","qa":"Didier Malenfant, programmer at Shiny Entertainment","st":"Postmortem: Shiny Entertainment's Wild 9","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/game-platforms/postmortem-shiny-entertainment-s-i-wild-9-i-#:~:text=When%20we%20dropped%20the%20Saturn,PlayStation%20side%20continued%20to%20evolve","sd":"Jan 2000","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"wildstar","n":"WildStar","dv":"Carbine","y":"2014","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/376570/","ls":"Steam","cv":"wildstar.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Pipeline & tools","t":"Prototyped each WildStar content type before turning it into an in-game template","s":"With templates in place, producers could estimate how long each quest, path mission or challenge took to build, and designers delivered content on a reliable schedule.","w":"Carbine wanted levelling to offer more than kill-ten-rats quests, so it built several content systems: quests, paths for 4 player styles, and timed challenges. Each type was first prototyped, then refined, then turned into in-game templates and editors for faster implementation. The early high-level schedule stayed a guide, and new fun systems sometimes forced cuts elsewhere. Once templates existed, design producer Stephan Frost and the content design lead estimated build time per system. Milestones then included planning, implementation, iteration, polish and bug fixes, with weekly reviews. Frost wrote up the process in January 2014.","r":"Designers implemented content on a regular, predictable schedule.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Prototype a content type until it plays well, then build its template and time one unit before you schedule the rest.","i":"A template turns a content type into a repeatable unit with a known build time, so milestones rest on real numbers. Order matters: a template for an unproven type repeats its flaws in every unit. A steady pipeline does not show players will stay.","a":"Do we know the build time per unit for each content type?","q":"“Once we found the mechanics and features we wanted to use, we’d then systemize the content and make implementation easier on the designer so we could regularly start implementing content on a reliable schedule.”","qa":"Stephan Frost, design producer at Carbine Studios","st":"The WildStar Creation Process and Maintaining Deadlines","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/the-wildstar-creation-process-and-maintaining-deadlines#:~:text=Once%20we%20found%20the%20mechanics,content%20on%20a%20reliable%20schedule","sd":"Jan 2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":3}]},{"k":"wonderwoman","n":"Wonder Woman","dv":"Monolith Productions","y":"2025","sc":"AAA","se":"Cancelled 2025","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Built Wonder Woman around a reverse Nemesis System that turned enemies into allies","s":"Bloomberg's Jason Schreier reported the system was not coming together. Around early 2024 the game was rebooted as a traditional action game, and in 2025 it was cancelled along with the studio.","w":"In spring 2021 Warner Bros. cancelled Monolith's new IP, and the studio's entire leadership team quit. Monolith started Wonder Woman around April to June 2021 while it rebuilt the studio. The game was announced at The Game Awards in December 2021, about 6 months later. According to Bloomberg's Jason Schreier, the core idea was a reverse Nemesis System in which people Diana befriended became allies, and it was not coming together. Around early 2024 the game was rebooted with new directors as a God of War style action game. Warner Bros. closed Monolith and cancelled it in February 2025.","r":"The core mechanic was dropped in a reboot around early 2024.","re":"Monolith changed directors and rebuilt it as a traditional action game.","co":"About 2.5 years of work before the reboot; game and studio closed in 2025.","nf":false,"l":"Before you announce, prove in a playable prototype that your signature system still works when you flip its purpose.","i":"The Nemesis System was built to make enemies memorable, and flipping it to make allies changed the job it had to do. Schreier also reports a lost leadership team and a reveal 6 months in, so the mechanic was one problem among several.","a":"Has a prototype proven our flipped core system is fun?","q":"“The Nemesis System raised the bar for player-driven storytelling and we’re excited to push the open-world genre forward, combining cutting-edge action with inventive narratives to create a Wonder Woman game everyone will want to play.”","qa":"David Hewitt, vice president and studio head of Monolith Productions","st":"Popular Nemesis System ‘wasn’t coming together’ in cancelled Wonder Woman game","su":"https://www.videogameschronicle.com/news/popular-nemesis-system-wasnt-coming-together-cancelled-wonder-woman-game/#:~:text=The%20game%20was%20rebooted%20around,the%20beginning%20of%202024","sd":"Feb 2025","sy":"Press report","w8":3,"qu":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/press-release/warner-bros-games-and-dc-announce-wonder-woman#:~:text=The%20Nemesis%20System%20raised%20the%20bar"}]},{"k":"worldoftanks","n":"World of Tanks","dv":"Wargaming.net","y":"2010","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1407200/","ls":"Steam","cv":"worldoftanks.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Business model & genre","t":"Dropped a fantasy MMO after 10 months and bet the independent studio on WWII tanks","s":"The CEO took designer Piotr Bitiukov's case for tanks over elves and cut the work-for-hire contracts to go fully digital. World of Tanks released in 2010 and had 100 million registered players by 2014.","w":"Wargaming, a 60-person work-for-hire strategy studio, cancelled those contracts and went fully independent and digital, which CEO Victor Kislyi called burning all the bridges. For its first 10 months the new MMO was a completely different game, a fantasy one. Then designer Piotr Bitiukov proposed World of Tanks; the team gave Kislyi a very detailed explanation of why tanks rather than elves would work, and he trusted them. The fantasy prototype's engine carried over. The game was free-to-play from the start and released in 2010. By September 2014 it had 100 million registered players and Wargaming had 2,500 staff.","r":"10 months of fantasy MMO work set aside for a new concept.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"10 months of fantasy MMO pre-production.","nf":false,"l":"Be willing to throw away months of pre-production when a stronger concept appears, and switch before the team scales.","i":"A small, self-funded team could switch concepts in month 10 because little content existed yet and the engine carried over. The pitch won on expertise: WWII hardware was what the studio knew. Once production starts, the same switch costs a full rebuild.","a":"Is there a stronger concept we should switch to before we scale?","q":"“They gave me a very detailed explanation of why it would work, and I trusted them.”","qa":"Victor Kislyi, CEO of Wargaming","st":"The chance meeting that made World of Tanks","su":"https://mcvuk.com/?p=73981#:~:text=They%20gave%20me%20a%20very,I%20trusted%20them","sd":"Sep 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"xrebirth","n":"X Rebirth","dv":"Egosoft","y":"2013","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2870/","ls":"Steam","cv":"xrebirth.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Engine & technology","t":"Rebuilt the space sim from scratch, engine included, under contracts set before Early Access","s":"After 7 years the game launched on 15 November 2013 with crashes and trade-blocking bugs and no way to switch to a staged release. Metacritic stood at 34 three weeks later; Egosoft patched monthly through 2014.","w":"Egosoft built X Rebirth from the ground up, new design and new engine, under Deep Silver contracts drawn up when there was no trace of Early Access. The game released on 15 November 2013 after 7 years of work. Players hit crashes from many causes and a trade bug that left freighters stuck at stations. Egosoft apologised within days and shipped 8 patches in under 3 weeks. In December 2013 Lehahn said the team had definitely underestimated multithreading and should have planned differently for a title this large; Moriz said the contracts had left no room for Early Access.","r":"Launch build shipped broken; Metacritic 34 and a year of patching.","re":"Apologised, shipped 8 patches in 3 weeks, then monthly updates through 2014.","co":"7 years of development; 8 patches in 3 weeks; a year of monthly updates.","nf":false,"l":"When you rebuild the engine from scratch, keep the release model open in the publisher contract so a staged release stays possible.","i":"A from-scratch engine breeds bug classes nobody has seen, yet Lehahn said testing used the same team and scope as for the predecessors. Contracts fixed years earlier left no room for a staged release. With a proven engine, a fixed release model costs little.","a":"Can our contract switch to Early Access if the new engine slips?","q":"“Wir haben definitiv die mit dem Multithreading verbundene Komplexität unterschätzt.”","qa":"Bernd Lehahn, studio head of Egosoft","st":"X Rebirth: \"Die schlechten Bewertungen haben wir uns selbst zuzuschreiben\" - Interview mit Egosoft","su":"https://www.pcgameshardware.de/X-Rebirth-Spiel-43387/Specials/X-Rebirth-Interview-schlechte-Bewertungen-1100888/#:~:text=Wir%20haben%20definitiv%20die%20mit,verbundene%20Komplexit%C3%A4t%20untersch%C3%A4tzt","sd":"Dec 2013","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4}]},{"k":"xcomufodefense","n":"X-COM: UFO Defense","dv":"Mythos Games","y":"1994","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/7760/","ls":"Steam","cv":"xcomufodefense.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Accepted the publisher's call to make it a big game on an 18-month contract","s":"The game took nearly 2.5 years and ended in 3 months of 7-day weeks. It sold 470,000 copies by 2000.","w":"In 1991 Julian and Nick Gollop showed MicroProse UK a Laser Squad 2 demo. The publisher asked them to make it a big game: set it on Earth, add a research tree and a Civilopedia-style database, and theme it on UFOs. The Geoscape came out of that. They signed MicroProse's standard 18-month contract, with the two brothers as the only programmers. In mid-1993 new owner Spectrum HoloByte cancelled the project, and MicroProse UK kept it going quietly. At Christmas 1993 the game was unplayable, so the brothers moved in-house and crunched to ship in March 1994.","r":"Nearly 2.5 years against an 18-month contract, finished in crunch.","re":"The Gollops moved in-house for the final 3 months to finish.","co":"Nearly a year over the 18-month contract; 3 months of 7-day weeks.","nf":false,"l":"When greenlight adds systems to a demo, re-plan the team and schedule, and keep the lead designer off production programming.","i":"The publisher's extra systems made the game, but the team stayed at two programmers, so the designer coded to the end. The 18-month figure was a standard contract term, not an estimate, so part of the slip reflects contract practice.","a":"Did the team and schedule change when the scope grew at greenlight?","q":"“But another problem we had was that we signed a contract to make the game in 18 months.”","qa":"Julian Gollop, designer and programmer at Mythos Games","st":"Classic Games Postmortem - XCOM: UFO Defense","su":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LP7VjbuNEzg&t=1030s","sd":"Mar 2013","sy":"Participant talk","w8":4,"sh":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LP7VjbuNEzg&t=1236s"}]},{"k":"xcomenemyunknown","n":"XCOM: Enemy Unknown","dv":"Firaxis Games","y":"2012","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/200510/","ls":"Steam","cv":"xcomenemyunknown.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Design & prototyping","t":"Threw away the 12-month checklist prototype and restarted from the simplest combat","s":"The first prototype copied the original X-COM feature by feature and played like a soulless golem. Firaxis rebuilt combat from a tile grid, cover and one number per soldier, then added mechanics one at a time.","w":"Firaxis shelved a 2003 XCOM attempt and tried again in 2008. The first prototype rebuilt the original from a checklist of its features: time units, no cover, a large squad. After 12 months it was demoed to the company, and once people played it the excitement faded. Solomon called it a soulless golem and asked the team to start over from the simplest version, a tile grid, cover and one number per soldier, then built mechanics back up piece by piece. About 12 months before ship the game was on target. It released in Oct 2012 after almost 5 years.","r":"A year of prototype art and gameplay systems was scrapped before the real design emerged.","re":"Rebuilt combat from the simplest version and added one mechanic at a time.","co":"About 12 months of prototype work discarded; almost 5 years in total.","nf":false,"l":"Judge a prototype by people playing it, not by the demo applause, and be ready to throw away the year.","i":"Recreating a classic feature by feature copies the parts, not the feel, and only play reveals that. The restart worked because 2K gave Firaxis almost 5 years; with a year less, Solomon says, they would have shipped the first prototype.","a":"Have outsiders played our prototype, or only watched the demo?","q":"“If we'd had a year less, we would have stuck with our original prototype and made a 60 Metacritic game.”","qa":"Jake Solomon, lead designer of XCOM: Enemy Unknown, Firaxis Games","st":"The Making Of…","su":"https://www.pressreader.com/australia/edge/20140703/283094182206454#:~:text=If%20we'd%20had%20a%20year,a%2060%20Metacritic%20game","sd":"Jul 2014","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Left high-risk design systems unlocked as pre-production wrapped up","s":"The strategy layer stagnated for months while combat got the attention, then needed a late daily design cabal. Late design squeezed cinematics, audio and UI, and the team crunched, but shipped within weeks of target.","w":"Jake Solomon first raised an XCOM remake inside Firaxis in 2004, and the project started after Civilization Revolution shipped. Sightlines, fog of war, destruction and both gameplay layers were still being reworked in production. By midproduction the strategy layer had been a turn-based card system for months and stagnated while the team improved combat. A daily design cabal of DeAngelis, Solomon and others then set a plan for each part. Narrative, audio and UI waited on the late design, and the team crunched. XCOM shipped in October 2012, within weeks of its original target.","r":"Narrative and cinematics were finished under heavy pressure, and the team crunched.","re":"Firaxis met daily in a design cabal until each strategy component had a plan.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before pre-production ends, list your high-risk systems and lock each one, or give it an owner and a dated plan.","i":"Narrative, audio and UI cannot finish until design locks, so every open system pushes their work later. DeAngelis also says the late iteration made the game better, so locking early trades some design quality for a calmer schedule.","a":"Which high-risk systems are still unlocked as we leave pre-production?","q":"“Practices like the design cabal helped the team focus on areas of the game that weren’t fun, but in a perfect world, we would have locked down as many high-risk systems as possible as preproduction wrapped up.”","qa":"Garth DeAngelis, lead producer of XCOM: Enemy Unknown at Firaxis Games","st":"Classic Postmortem: XCOM: Enemy Unknown, which turns 5 today","su":"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/classic-postmortem-i-xcom-enemy-unknown-i-which-turns-5-today#:~:text=Practices%20like%20the%20design%20cabal,as%20preproduction%20wrapped%20up","sd":"Jan 2013, reprinted Oct 2017","sy":"Participant-written postmortem","w8":3}]},{"k":"xenobladechronicles","n":"Xenoblade Chronicles","dv":"Monolith Soft","y":"2010","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Gates & greenlight","t":"Required one chapter at near-final quality before the team built out the rest","s":"The team finished a single map to final standard. Staff saw the target clearly, and the studio learned how long one section takes to make.","w":"Monolith Soft usually built games from a broad, thin base and added detail over time. Nintendo usually proved the game system and the world on Wii before making the actual game. On Xenoblade, Nintendo producer Yamagami could not tell how long the broad approach would take at this size, so he asked for one chapter at near-final quality. Director Koh Kojima first said it would be tough, then agreed to try. The team finished a single map, saw its target clearly and learned the time per section. The game reached Europe on 19 August 2011.","r":"The team got a shared quality target and a time estimate per section.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before full production on a large game, finish one section to final quality and time how long it took.","i":"A finished slice turns a vague target into something staff can see, and its build time gives a real rate for the rest. The rate holds only if later sections look like the sample; unique set pieces need their own estimate.","a":"Have we finished one section at final quality and timed it?","q":"“It also made us aware of how much time we’d need to spend to produce one section, which was really great.”","qa":"Koh Kojima, director at Monolith Soft","st":"Iwata Asks: Xenoblade Chronicles, Vol. 3: The Development Process, 3. Like a Writer and an Editor","su":"https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Iwata-Asks/Iwata-Asks-Xenoblade-Chronicles/Vol-3-The-Development-Process/3-Like-a-Writer-and-an-Editor/3-Like-a-Writer-and-an-Editor-839496.html#:~:text=It%20also%20made%20us%20aware,which%20was%20really%20great","sd":"2010","sy":"Participant interview","w8":4},{"o":"Paid off","c":"Staffing","t":"Brought in an anime scenario writer as the director's script partner","s":"Anime habits gave a fixed ratio: about 15,000 Japanese characters made 22 minutes of story. Planning the structure, schedule and cutscene costs became much easier.","w":"Tetsuya Takahashi was executive director on Xenoblade and expected a heavy workload. To make development more efficient, he brought in Yuichiro Takeda, an anime scenario writer he had known for ten years. In anime, a 22-minute episode script runs about 15,000 Japanese characters. So when Takeda wrote a section of that length, the team knew it held about 22 minutes of story. Pages three and four counted as about five minutes, which helped the team space out cutscenes to keep costs down. The game reached Europe on 19 August 2011.","r":"Script length gave the team a direct read on schedule and cutscene costs.","re":"No rescue needed","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you schedule cutscenes, agree how many minutes of finished scene one page of script makes.","i":"A writer trained on fixed broadcast lengths writes to a steady pace, so script volume converts to screen time. The ratio comes from that writer's habits; a new writer or a branching script needs its own measure.","a":"How many minutes of cutscene does one page of our script make?","q":"“This made it much easier to plan the overall structure and schedule.”","qa":"Tetsuya Takahashi, executive director at Monolith Soft","st":"Iwata Asks: Xenoblade Chronicles, Vol. 2: Story, 2. Working on a Weekly Cycle","su":"https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Iwata-Asks/Iwata-Asks-Xenoblade-Chronicles/Vol-2-Story/2-Working-on-a-Weekly-Cycle/2-Working-on-a-Weekly-Cycle-206748.html#:~:text=This%20made%20it%20much%20easier,overall%20structure%20and%20schedule","sd":"2010","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"xenobladechronicles2","n":"Xenoblade Chronicles 2","dv":"Monolith Soft","y":"2017","sc":"AA","se":"","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Outsourcing & partners","t":"Built Xenoblade 2 around 40-plus core staff and outsourced most of the assets","s":"More than half of Monolith Tokyo was helping Nintendo with Zelda. Outside studios made most character models, the core team corrected them in house, and event design and scenario stayed internal.","w":"Monolith Soft's Tokyo studio had just under 100 developers, and 50 to 60 percent of them were helping Nintendo with Zelda. A few more did R&D, which left just over 40 people on Xenoblade Chronicles 2. That was not enough to build the game, so the team outsourced work and corrected what came back. Most character models were made outside and adjusted in house, while Rex, Pyra and a few others were built entirely by founding members. The game shipped in December 2017. In February 2018, Tetsuya Takahashi said 3 programmers collapsed mid-development and the UI fell short.","r":"Outsourced assets came back for in-house correction by a 40-plus core.","re":"The remaining staff covered for 3 collapsed programmers until the very end.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"If most of your studio is on another project, assign named reviewers and time to fix every outsourced asset.","i":"Outsourcing adds hands for making assets, but review and correction still land on the core team, here about 40 people who also owned events and story. A core that thin has no slack when a few programmers drop out.","a":"Who on the core team fixes outsourced assets, and when?","q":"“ただ、このメンバーだけでは作れないので、アウトソースに出して、上がってきたものを修正するというのが主な流れですね。” (But we could not build it with these members alone, so the main flow was to outsource and correct what came back.)","qa":"Tetsuya Takahashi, general director at Monolith Soft","st":"「ゼノブレイド」高橋哲哉 ×「ペルソナ」橋野桂：対談──作家性とは何か？ 世界で評価されるJRPGの旗手が掲げる美学をめぐって","su":"https://news.denfaminicogamer.jp/interview/180202#:~:text=%E3%81%9F%E3%81%A0%E3%80%81%E3%81%93%E3%81%AE%E3%83%A1%E3%83%B3%E3%83%90%E3%83%BC%E3%81%A0%E3%81%91%E3%81%A7%E3%81%AF%E4%BD%9C%E3%82%8C%E3%81%AA%E3%81%84,%E4%BF%AE%E6%AD%A3%E3%81%99%E3%82%8B%E3%81%A8%E3%81%84%E3%81%86%E3%81%AE%E3%81%8C%E4%B8%BB%E3%81%AA%E6%B5%81%E3%82%8C%E3%81%A7%E3%81%99%E3%81%AD","sd":"Feb 2018","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"xenobladechronicles3","n":"Xenoblade Chronicles 3","dv":"Monolith Soft","y":"2022","sc":"AAA","se":"","lk":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/xenoblade-chronicles-3-switch/","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"xenobladechronicles3.webp","cs":[{"o":"Paid off","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Spent map effort on towns and quests instead of landscape texture variety","s":"The walkable area came out over 5 times larger than in Xenoblade Chronicles 2. The world is built from a limited set of landscapes, with many colonies full of quests.","w":"Executive director Tetsuya Takahashi says an RPG needs its content volume set at the start: story hours, characters, maps. On Xenoblade Chronicles 3, the team also wanted more towns from the beginning. It chose not to fill the world with many textures and detailed variations such as wall patterns. Instead it built a large area from a limited set of landscapes and put the effort into colonies with quests and exploration. The game came out on 29 July 2022. That day, producer Koh Kojima said the walkable area was over 5 times that of the second game.","r":"A walkable world over 5 times the size of the previous game.","re":"No rescue needed.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Cut the decorative variants players stop noticing after 30 minutes of play, and spend that art time on places they use.","i":"Artist time spent on variants that players stop seeing is time taken from towns and quests, which keep paying back. The trade holds in a long game where players cross the same ground for hours; a short game has less ground to fill.","a":"Which of our planned art variants will players still notice after 30 minutes?","q":"“In fact, there are many colonies where you can explore and enjoy quests, and if we put too much effort into adding a lot of detailed variations such as wall patterns, I think we would not have been able to provide as much gameplay as we did.”","qa":"Koh Kojima, producer and director at Monolith Soft","st":"Ask the Developer Vol. 6, Xenoblade Chronicles 3 – Chapter 3","su":"https://www.nintendo.com/au/news-and-articles/ask-the-developer-vol-6-xenoblade-chronicles-3-chapter-3/#:~:text=In%20fact%2C%20there%20are%20many,as%20much%20gameplay%20as%20we%20did","sd":"Jul 2022","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"xenobladechroniclesx","n":"Xenoblade Chronicles X","dv":"Monolith Soft","y":"2015","sc":"AAA","se":"Expanded edition 2025","lk":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/xenoblade-chronicles-x-definitive-edition-switch/","ls":"Nintendo","cv":"xenobladechroniclesx.webp","cs":[{"o":"Tradeoff","c":"Scope & ambition","t":"Kept philosophical themes out of the story on the studio's first HD game","s":"The team put its effort into HD technology, exploration and battle builds. Players later asked for a richer story, and Takahashi promised one for the next game.","w":"Monolith Soft had never made an HD game before Xenoblade Chronicles X on Wii U. Tetsuya Takahashi set the goal as trying out and building the tech skills HD work needs. Pairing that with a theme-heavy epic looked too hard. So the team kept philosophical themes out of the core story and focused on map exploration and battle builds. In July 2015 Siliconera reported a guidebook comment: Takahashi agreed with players who wanted a richer story, citing the studio's late start in HD and its size. In December 2015 he told TIME the plan was core systems first, story later.","r":"Players criticised the lack of a rich story after release.","re":"Takahashi said the next project would be rich in story.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"When the team is learning a new technology, write down which ambitions you are deferring to the next game, and why.","i":"A team learning HD tools has little room left for a demanding story, so Takahashi cut the risk to one front. The cut still shows: players judge the game they get, not the plan for the sequel.","a":"Which ambition are we deferring while the team learns this technology?","q":"“We deliberately distanced ourselves from having philosophical themes intertwined with the core of the story.”","qa":"Tetsuya Takahashi, general director of Xenoblade Chronicles X at Monolith Soft","st":"5 Things Xenoblade Chronicles X Director Tetsuya Takahashi Told Us","su":"https://time.com/4149809/xenoblade-chronicles-x-director-tetsuya-takahashi/#:~:text=We%20deliberately%20distanced%20ourselves%20from,the%20core%20of%20the%20story","sd":"Dec 2015","sy":"Participant interview","w8":3}]},{"k":"yogventures","n":"Yogventures","dv":"Winterkewl Games","y":"2014","sc":"Indie","se":"Cancelled 2014","lk":"","ls":"Website","cv":null,"cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Staffing","t":"Budgeted the Kickstarter money in $35,000 lump sums with the lead programmer still unhired","s":"One artist took his $35,000, worked about 2 weeks and left; the programmer was never hired. Two years later the game was unfinished and the studio handed $150,000 to the Yogscast.","w":"In 2012 Winterkewl raised $567,665 on Kickstarter for a Yogscast-branded game, about $415,000 after fees. The plan split it into $35,000 lump sums for 5 senior artists plus the creator's own programming, and $100,000 for a main programmer still to be found. The matte painter took his $35,000, worked about 2 weeks and left for LucasArts. The team worked 18 more months without a main programmer and no funds to hire one. After the Yogscast lost confidence, $150,000 went to them for rewards. On July 19, 2014 Winterkewl posted its last update: the game was never finished.","r":"Two years of work, no finished game, and the Kickstarter money spent.","re":"The Yogscast took $150,000 to ship the physical rewards; the game was abandoned.","co":"$567,665 raised ($415,000 net); $35,000 lost on one artist; about 2 years.","nf":false,"l":"Do not start spending the money until the lead programmer is on the team.","i":"The budget was a list of hires, not a team. With the lead programmer missing, every design change landed on a first-time lead who was also coding. Lump-sum payments made the plan fragile: one departure cost $35,000 and could not be undone.","a":"Is our lead programmer hired before we start spending?","q":"“Unfortunately, too many design changes and my in-experience as a project lead and programmer were what's to blame for our company never really making what it was we set out to make.”","qa":"Kris Vale, lead developer and project lead, Winterkewl Games","st":"One last update from Winterkewl.","su":"https://www.kickstarter.com/projects/winterkewlgames/yogventures/posts/919100#:~:text=Unfortunately%2C%20too%20many%20design%20changes,set%20out%20to%20make.","sd":"Jul 2014","sy":"Participant account","w8":5}]},{"k":"yookalaylee","n":"Yooka-Laylee","dv":"Playtonic Games","y":"2017","sc":"Indie","se":"","lk":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/360830/","ls":"Steam","cv":"yookalaylee.webp","cs":[{"o":"Backfired","c":"Platforms & ports","t":"Promised day-one Wii U, Xbox One and PS4 versions as a Kickstarter stretch goal","s":"The campaign raised £2,090,104 and unlocked the goal. After many months of work on technical issues, Playtonic dropped the Wii U version in December 2016 and offered those backers other platforms.","w":"Playtonic launched its Kickstarter on 1 May 2015 with a £175,000 goal for PC, Mac and Linux, with consoles to follow. A £1,000,000 stretch goal promised simultaneous day-one releases on Wii U, Xbox One and PS4. The campaign closed on 16 June 2015 with £2,090,104 from 73,206 backers. In December 2016 Playtonic cancelled the Wii U version, after its developers had spent many months on technical issues. Wii U backers could move their pledge to other platforms or to Switch. Studio head Gavin Price said the cause was simply unforeseen technical difficulties.","r":"The Wii U version was cancelled after many months of work.","re":"Playtonic let Wii U backers move pledges to other platforms or Switch.","co":"","nf":true,"l":"Before you promise a platform to backers, get a build of the game running on that hardware.","i":"A stretch goal turns a platform into a public promise before anyone has run the game on it. Playtonic called the issues unforeseen, which is the risk on hardware the build has never run on.","a":"Have we run a build on every platform we have promised?","q":"“Our developers worked tirelessly over many months in attempt to solve our technical issues, but unfortunately it’s not been possible to bring Yooka-Laylee to Wii U as originally planned.”","qa":"Playtonic Games, statement on its Yooka-Laylee FAQ page","st":"Playtonic Admit Why They Axed The Wii U Version Of Yooka-Laylee","su":"https://mynintendonews.com/2016/12/18/playtonic-admit-why-they-axed-the-wii-u-version-of-yooka-laylee/#:~:text=Our%20developers%20worked%20tirelessly%20over,Wii%20U%20as%20originally%20planned","sd":"Dec 2016","sy":"Company statement","w8":3}]}]};
